mining threads."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:12:15.620707Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:12:17.123495Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Confirmed working \u2014 self-test runs automatically before mining starts, passes both vectors, then the HEADER/HASH stream produces well-formed, correctly-computed hashes. (The duplicate lines you see are from multiple threads independently sampling early nonces of freshly split work ranges \u2014 a characteristic of the work-stealing design, not a hashing bug.)", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:12:17.123602Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:12:17.801529Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01GVbZnkGqv2aeXkghobQLGY", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "cp /home/claude/hashproject.c /mnt/user-data/outputs/hashproject.c", "description": "Copy final corrected+verified file to outputs"}, "message": "Copy final corrected+verified file to outputs", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cp /home/claude/hashproject.c /mnt/user-data/outputs/hashproject.c\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:12:17.978899Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:12:17.978929Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01GVbZnkGqv2aeXkghobQLGY", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:12:19.984431Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:12:20.300759Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_017wTDMYeHuhfDJfm3vZ4zJb", "name": "present_files", "input": {"filepaths": ["/mnt/user-data/outputs/hashproject.c"]}, "message": "Presenting file(s)...", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "table", "table": [["filepaths", "['/mnt/user-data/outputs/hashproject.c']"]]}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:12:20.320328Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:12:20.320360Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_017wTDMYeHuhfDJfm3vZ4zJb", "name": "present_files", "content": [{"type": "local_resource", "file_path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/hashproject.c", "name": "hashproject", "mime_type": "text/x-csrc", "artifact_publishable": false}, {"type": "text", "text": "The file cards appear at the end of your reply if it ends with text, otherwise under this step; say they are below only if you finish the reply with text."}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": "Presented file", "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:12:22.043559Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:12:26.399247Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "**Compile and run:**\n```bash\ngcc -O2 -o hashproject hashproject.c -lpthread -lm\n./hashproject --verify      # just runs the two NIST test vectors, then exits\n./hashproject 4             # normal mining with 4 threads (self-test runs first automatically)\n```\n\n**What's new in this version:**\n- The two real bugs from before stay fixed (correct 64-value `k[]` array, correct round-variable rotation `e = d + t1; d = c;`).\n- A `run_selftest()` function now runs `SHA256(\"\")` and `SHA256(\"abc\")` against their official values every time the program starts, and refuses to mine if either fails \u2014 so a future corruption gets caught immediately instead of silently producing garbage that looks like real output.\n- Ironically, while wiring this up I hardcoded the empty-string expected value with one character missing (63 instead of 64 chars) and the self-test correctly caught its own bug on the first run \u2014 which is exactly the failure mode this feature exists to catch.\n- `--verify` as the first argument runs just the self-test and exits, without starting any mining/threads \u2014 useful for a quick sanity check after any future edit to the file.", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-26T08:12:26.463989Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T08:12:26.463989Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0dcc4-ac58-73fc-8b6b-8323c6ef37f3"}, {"uuid": "01a0dcc6-c537-769e-920e-247e58329a4a", "text": "do the same for this fix the sha256 generation, dont change anthing else", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:13:29.226658Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:13:29.226658Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "do the same for this fix the sha256 generation, dont change anthing else", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-26T08:13:29.227253Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T08:13:29.227253Z", "attachments": [{"file_name": "", "file_size": 22809, "file_type": "txt", "extracted_content": "#define _GNU_SOURCE\r\n#include <stdio.h>\r\n#include <stdint.h>\r\n#include <stdlib.h>\r\n#include <string.h>\r\n#include <time.h>\r\n#include <unistd.h>\r\n#include <signal.h>\r\n#include <pthread.h>\r\n#include <math.h>\r\n#include <stdatomic.h>\r\n\r\n// ---------------- SHA-256 implementation ----------------\r\n\r\ntypedef struct {\r\n    uint8_t  data[64];\r\n    uint32_t datalen;\r\n    uint64_t bitlen;\r\n    uint32_t state[8];\r\n} SHA256_CTX;\r\n\r\n#define ROTRIGHT(a,b) (((a) >> (b)) | ((a) << (32-(b))))\r\n#define CH(x,y,z)     (((x) & (y)) ^ (~(x) & (z)))\r\n#define MAJ(x,y,z)    (((x) & (y)) ^ ((x) & (z)) ^ ((y) & (z)))\r\n#define EP0(x)        (ROTRIGHT(x,2)  ^ ROTRIGHT(x,13) ^ ROTRIGHT(x,22))\r\n#define EP1(x)        (ROTRIGHT(x,6)  ^ ROTRIGHT(x,11) ^ ROTRIGHT(x,25))\r\n#define SIG0(x)       (ROTRIGHT(x,7)  ^ ROTRIGHT(x,18) ^ ((x) >> 3))\r\n#define SIG1(x)       (ROTRIGHT(x,17) ^ ROTRIGHT(x,19) ^ ((x) >> 10))\r\n\r\nstatic const uint32_t k[64] = {\r\n    0x428a2f98,0x71374491,0xb5c0fbcf,0xe9b5dba5,0x3956c25b,0x59f111f1,0x923f82a4,0xab1c5ed5,\r\n    0xd807aa98,0x12835b01,0x243185be,0x550c7dc3,0x72be5d74,0x80deb1fe,0x9bdc06a7,0xc19bf174,\r\n    0xe49b69c1,0xefbe4786,0x0fc19dc6,0x240ca1cc,0x2de92c6f,0x4a7484aa,0x5cb0a9dc,0x76f988da,\r\n    0x983e5152,0xa831c66d,0xb00327c8,0xbf597fc7,0xc6e00bf3,0xd5a79147,0x06ca6351,0x14e2e0c6,\r\n    0x1a82f231,0x24e1c6c8,0x27b70a85,0x2e1b2138,0x4d2c6dfc,0x53380d13,0x650a7354,0x766a0abb,\r\n    0x81c2c92e,0x92722c85,0xa2bfe8a1,0xa81a664b,0xc24b8b70,0xc76c51a3,0xd192e819,0xd6990624,\r\n    0xf40e3585,0x106aa070,0x19a4c116,0x1e376c08,0x2748774c,0x34b0bcb5,0x391c0cb3,0x4ed8aa4a,\r\n    0x5b9cca4f,0x682e6ff3,0x748f82ee,0x78a5636f,0x84c87814,0x8cc70208,0x90befffa,0xa4506ceb,\r\n    0xbef9a3f7,0xc67178f2\r\n};\r\n\r\nstatic void sha256_transform(SHA256_CTX *ctx, const uint8_t data[]) {\r\n    uint32_t a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,t1,t2,m[64];\r\n\r\n    for (i=0,j=0; i<16; ++i, j+=4)\r\n        m[i] = ((uint32_t)data[j]<<24) | ((uint32_t)data[j+1]<<16) |\r\n               ((uint32_t)data[j+2]<<8)  | ((uint32_t)data[j+3]);\r\n    for (; i<64; ++i)\r\n        m[i] = SIG1(m[i-2]) + m[i-7] + SIG0(m[i-15]) + m[i-16];\r\n\r\n    a = ctx->state[0]; b = ctx->state[1]; c = ctx->state[2]; d = ctx->state[3];\r\n    e = ctx->state[4]; f = ctx->state[5]; g = ctx->state[6]; h = ctx->state[7];\r\n\r\n    for (i=0; i<64; ++i) {\r\n        t1 = h + EP1(e) + CH(e,f,g) + k[i] + m[i];\r\n        t2 = EP0(a) + MAJ(a,b,c);\r\n        h = g; g = f; f = e; d = d + t1; c = b; b = a; a = t1 + t2;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    ctx->state[0] += a; ctx->state[1] += b; ctx->state[2] += c; ctx->state[3] += d;\r\n    ctx->state[4] += e; ctx->state[5] += f; ctx->state[6] += g; ctx->state[7] += h;\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic void sha256_init(SHA256_CTX *ctx) {\r\n    ctx->datalen = 0;\r\n    ctx->bitlen  = 0;\r\n    ctx->state[0] = 0x6a09e667;\r\n    ctx->state[1] = 0xbb67ae85;\r\n    ctx->state[2] = 0x3c6ef372;\r\n    ctx->state[3] = 0xa54ff53a;\r\n    ctx->state[4] = 0x510e527f;\r\n    ctx->state[5] = 0x9b05688c;\r\n    ctx->state[6] = 0x1f83d9ab;\r\n    ctx->state[7] = 0x5be0cd19;\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic void sha256_update(SHA256_CTX *ctx, const uint8_t data[], size_t len) {\r\n    for (size_t i=0; i<len; ++i) {\r\n        ctx->data[ctx->datalen] = data[i];\r\n        ctx->datalen++;\r\n        if (ctx->datalen == 64) {\r\n            sha256_transform(ctx, ctx->data);\r\n            ctx->bitlen += 512;\r\n            ctx->datalen = 0;\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic void sha256_final(SHA256_CTX *ctx, uint8_t hash[]) {\r\n    uint32_t i = ctx->datalen;\r\n\r\n    if (ctx->datalen < 56) {\r\n        ctx->data[i++] = 0x80;\r\n        while (i < 56)\r\n            ctx->data[i++] = 0x00;\r\n    } else {\r\n        ctx->data[i++] = 0x80;\r\n        while (i < 64)\r\n            ctx->data[i++] = 0x00;\r\n        sha256_transform(ctx, ctx->data);\r\n        memset(ctx->data, 0, 56);\r\n        ctx->data[0] = 0x80;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    ctx->bitlen += ctx->datalen * 8;\r\n    ctx->data[63] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen);\r\n    ctx->data[62] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 8);\r\n    ctx->data[61] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 16);\r\n    ctx->data[60] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 24);\r\n    ctx->data[59] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 32);\r\n    ctx->data[58] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 40);\r\n    ctx->data[57] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 48);\r\n    ctx->data[56] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 56);\r\n    sha256_transform(ctx, ctx->data);\r\n\r\n    for (i=0; i<4; ++i) {\r\n        hash[i]      = (ctx->state[0] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\r\n        hash[i+4]    = (ctx->state[1] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\r\n        hash[i+8]    = (ctx->state[2] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\r\n        hash[i+12]   = (ctx->state[3] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\r\n        hash[i+16]   = (ctx->state[4] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\r\n        hash[i+20]   = (ctx->state[5] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\r\n        hash[i+24]   = (ctx->state[6] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\r\n        hash[i+28]   = (ctx->state[7] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n\r\n// ---------------- Block Header & Hash Proof Structure ----------------\r\n\r\ntypedef struct {\r\n    uint64_t time_bin;\r\n    uint64_t nonce;\r\n} BlockHeader;\r\n\r\n// ---------------- Mining / binary-tree / RMS / min-max logic ----------------\r\n\r\n#define WINDOW_SIZE 128\r\n#define MAX_THREADS 16\r\n#define BIN_SECONDS   1\r\n\r\n// Tree and sampling parameters (tuned for lively behavior)\r\n#define INITIAL_RANGE_BITS 16          // root range size = 2^16 = 65536\r\n#define MAX_DEPTH 10                   // allow decent narrowing\r\n#define SAMPLES_PER_RANGE 16           // fewer samples per range \u2192 faster decisions\r\n#define STRIDE_BITS 0                  // sample densely\r\n\r\n// Start/stop policy parameters (more aggressive)\r\n#define START_RANGE_FRACTION 10.95      // start bins more often\r\n#define PRUNE_RANGE_FRACTION 0.7       // prune children more aggressively\r\n#define KILL_RANGE_FACTOR    2.5       // kill bin sooner\r\n\r\n// Probe for deciding whether to start a time bin\r\n#define PROBE_COUNT 128\r\n\r\n// How often to print hashes (every PRINT_EVERY hashes)\r\n#define PRINT_EVERY 1\r\n\r\ntypedef struct {\r\n    uint64_t base_nonce;\r\n    uint64_t count;\r\n    int      depth;\r\n    uint64_t sample_min;\r\n    uint64_t sample_max;\r\n    double   sample_rms;\r\n    int      sample_count;\r\n    uint64_t parent_range; // optional: store parent sample range for pruning\r\n} WorkRange;\r\n\r\ntypedef struct {\r\n    WorkRange *items;\r\n    size_t     capacity;\r\n    size_t     head;\r\n    size_t     tail;\r\n    pthread_mutex_t mtx;\r\n    pthread_cond_t  cond;\r\n    atomic_int      stop;\r\n} WorkQueue;\r\n\r\nstatic atomic_int g_running = 1;\r\nstatic atomic_int g_signal_fired = 0;\r\n\r\nstatic uint64_t g_best_bin_probe_range = UINT64_MAX;\r\nstatic int      g_have_any_bin_yet = 0;\r\n\r\nstatic pthread_mutex_t g_bin_mtx = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\r\nstatic uint64_t g_bin_min = 0;\r\nstatic uint64_t g_bin_max = 0;\r\nstatic uint64_t g_bin_start_range = 0;\r\nstatic int      g_bin_active = 0;\r\nstatic uint64_t g_bin_samples = 0;\r\n\r\nstatic pthread_mutex_t g_rms_mtx = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\r\nstatic double g_window[WINDOW_SIZE];\r\nstatic int    g_win_idx = 0;\r\nstatic int    g_win_count = 0;\r\nstatic double g_min_window_rms = -1.0;\r\n\r\nstatic pthread_mutex_t g_print_mtx = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\r\n\r\n// Track best sample range seen so far (for pruning decisions)\r\nstatic pthread_mutex_t g_best_mtx = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\r\nstatic uint64_t g_best_sample_range = UINT64_MAX;\r\n\r\nvoid handle_sigint(int sig) {\r\n    (void)sig;\r\n    atomic_store(&g_running, 0);\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic inline uint64_t hash_to_u64(const uint8_t h[32]) {\r\n    uint64_t v = 0;\r\n    for (int i=0;i<8;i++) {\r\n        v = (v << 8) | h[i];\r\n    }\r\n    return v;\r\n}\r\n\r\n// Convert 32-byte SHA256 to 64-char hex string\r\nstatic void bytes_to_hex(const uint8_t *bytes, size_t len, char *out) {\r\n    static const char hex[] = \"0123456789abcdef\";\r\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < len; ++i) {\r\n        out[i*2]   = hex[(bytes[i] >> 4) & 0xF];\r\n        out[i*2+1] = hex[bytes[i] & 0xF];\r\n    }\r\n    out[len*2] = '\\0';\r\n}\r\n\r\n// Header + Hash Function\r\nstatic uint64_t compute_header_hash(const BlockHeader *hdr, uint8_t out_hash[32]) {\r\n    SHA256_CTX ctx;\r\n    sha256_init(&ctx);\r\n    uint8_t buf[16];\r\n    for (int i=0; i<8; i++) buf[i]   = (hdr->time_bin >> (56 - 8*i)) & 0xff;\r\n    for (int i=0; i<8; i++) buf[8+i] = (hdr->nonce    >> (56 - 8*i)) & 0xff;\r\n    sha256_update(&ctx, buf, 16);\r\n    sha256_final(&ctx, out_hash);\r\n    return hash_to_u64(out_hash);\r\n}\r\n\r\n// Hash Proof Verification Function\r\nstatic int verify_hash_proof(const BlockHeader *hdr, const uint8_t expected_hash[32]) {\r\n    uint8_t computed_hash[32];\r\n    compute_header_hash(hdr, computed_hash);\r\n    return memcmp(computed_hash, expected_hash, 32) == 0;\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic uint64_t hash_time_nonce(uint64_t time_bin, uint64_t nonce, uint8_t out_hash[32]) {\r\n    BlockHeader hdr = { .time_bin = time_bin, .nonce = nonce };\r\n    uint64_t val = compute_header_hash(&hdr, out_hash);\r\n    \r\n    // Optional self-check proof validation (can be enabled for debugging proofs)\r\n    // if (!verify_hash_proof(&hdr, out_hash)) { fprintf(stderr, \"Proof failed!\\n\"); }\r\n    \r\n    return val;\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic void init_queue(WorkQueue *q, size_t cap) {\r\n    q->items = malloc(cap * sizeof(WorkRange));\r\n    q->capacity = cap;\r\n    q->head = q->tail = 0;\r\n    pthread_mutex_init(&q->mtx, NULL);\r\n    pthread_cond_init(&q->cond, NULL);\r\n    atomic_store(&q->stop, 0);\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic void destroy_queue(WorkQueue *q) {\r\n    free(q->items);\r\n    pthread_mutex_destroy(&q->mtx);\r\n    pthread_cond_destroy(&q->cond);\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic int queue_push(WorkQueue *q, WorkRange item) {\r\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&q->mtx);\r\n    if ((q->tail + 1) % q->capacity == q->head) {\r\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&q->mtx);\r\n        return 0;\r\n    }\r\n    q->items[q->tail] = item;\r\n    q->tail = (q->tail + 1) % q->capacity;\r\n    pthread_cond_signal(&q->cond);\r\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&q->mtx);\r\n    return 1;\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic int queue_pop(WorkQueue *q, WorkRange *out) {\r\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&q->mtx);\r\n    while (q->head == q->tail && !atomic_load(&q->stop)) {\r\n        pthread_cond_wait(&q->cond, &q->mtx);\r\n    }\r\n    if (atomic_load(&q->stop) && q->head == q->tail) {\r\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&q->mtx);\r\n        return 0;\r\n    }\r\n    *out = q->items[q->head];\r\n    q->head = (q->head + 1) % q->capacity;\r\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&q->mtx);\r\n    return 1;\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic uint64_t probe_bin(uint64_t time_bin, size_t probe_count, uint64_t *out_first_val) {\r\n    uint8_t hash[32];\r\n    uint64_t pmin = UINT64_MAX;\r\n    uint64_t pmax = 0;\r\n    uint64_t first_val = 0;\r\n\r\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < probe_count; ++i) {\r\n        uint64_t val = hash_time_nonce(time_bin, i, hash);\r\n        if (i == 0) first_val = val;\r\n        if (val < pmin) pmin = val;\r\n        if (val > pmax) pmax = val;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    if (out_first_val) *out_first_val = first_val;\r\n    return (pmax > pmin) ? (pmax - pmin) : 0;\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic int should_start_bin(uint64_t time_bin, uint64_t *out_first_val) {\r\n    uint64_t probe_range = probe_bin(time_bin, PROBE_COUNT, out_first_val);\r\n\r\n    int start = 0;\r\n\r\n    if (!g_have_any_bin_yet) {\r\n        g_best_bin_probe_range = probe_range;\r\n        g_have_any_bin_yet = 1;\r\n        start = 1;\r\n    } else {\r\n        double threshold = START_RANGE_FRACTION * (double)g_best_bin_probe_range;\r\n        if ((double)probe_range <= threshold) {\r\n            if (probe_range < g_best_bin_probe_range) {\r\n                g_best_bin_probe_range = probe_range;\r\n            }\r\n            start = 1;\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    if (start) {\r\n        pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n        printf(\">>> Starting bin %lu: probe_range=%lu (best_so_far=%lu)\\n\",\r\n               time_bin, probe_range, g_best_bin_probe_range);\r\n        fflush(stdout);\r\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n    } else {\r\n        pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n        printf(\"... Skipping bin %lu: probe_range=%lu > %.2f * best=%lu\\n\",\r\n               time_bin, probe_range, START_RANGE_FRACTION, g_best_bin_probe_range);\r\n        fflush(stdout);\r\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    return start;\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic void sample_range(uint64_t time_bin, WorkRange *r) {\r\n    uint8_t hash[32];\r\n    uint64_t sum_sq = 0;\r\n    uint64_t rmin = UINT64_MAX;\r\n    uint64_t rmax = 0;\r\n    int count = 0;\r\n\r\n    uint64_t stride = 1ULL << STRIDE_BITS;\r\n    uint64_t end = r->base_nonce + r->count;\r\n\r\n    uint64_t step = (r->count + SAMPLES_PER_RANGE - 1) / SAMPLES_PER_RANGE;\r\n    if (step < 1) step = 1;\r\n    if (step < stride) step = stride;\r\n\r\n    for (uint64_t x = r->base_nonce; x < end; x += step) {\r\n        uint64_t val = hash_time_nonce(time_bin, x, hash);\r\n        if (val < rmin) rmin = val;\r\n        if (val > rmax) rmax = val;\r\n        sum_sq += val * val;\r\n        count++;\r\n        if (count >= SAMPLES_PER_RANGE) break;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    r->sample_min = rmin;\r\n    r->sample_max = rmax;\r\n    r->sample_count = count;\r\n    if (count > 0) {\r\n        r->sample_rms = sqrt((double)sum_sq / count);\r\n    } else {\r\n        r->sample_rms = 0.0;\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic void update_stats(uint64_t val, double *out_window_rms, int *out_is_new_low_rms) {\r\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_bin_mtx);\r\n    if (!g_bin_active) {\r\n        g_bin_min = g_bin_max = val;\r\n        g_bin_samples = 1;\r\n    } else {\r\n        if (val < g_bin_min) g_bin_min = val;\r\n        if (val > g_bin_max) g_bin_max = val;\r\n        g_bin_samples++;\r\n    }\r\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\r\n\r\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_rms_mtx);\r\n    g_window[g_win_idx] = (double)val;\r\n    g_win_idx = (g_win_idx + 1) % WINDOW_SIZE;\r\n    if (g_win_count < WINDOW_SIZE) g_win_count++;\r\n\r\n    double sum_sq = 0.0;\r\n    for (int i=0;i<g_win_count;i++) sum_sq += g_window[i] * g_window[i];\r\n    double wrms = sqrt(sum_sq / g_win_count);\r\n    *out_window_rms = wrms;\r\n\r\n    int is_new_low = 0;\r\n    if (g_min_window_rms < 0.0 || wrms < g_min_window_rms) {\r\n        g_min_window_rms = wrms;\r\n        is_new_low = 1;\r\n    }\r\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_rms_mtx);\r\n    *out_is_new_low_rms = is_new_low;\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic int should_kill_bin(void) {\r\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_bin_mtx);\r\n    if (!g_bin_active) {\r\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\r\n        return 0;\r\n    }\r\n    uint64_t cur_range = (g_bin_max > g_bin_min) ? (g_bin_max - g_bin_min) : 0;\r\n    uint64_t kill_threshold = (uint64_t)((double)g_bin_start_range * KILL_RANGE_FACTOR);\r\n    int kill = (cur_range > kill_threshold && g_bin_start_range > 0);\r\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\r\n    return kill;\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic void begin_bin(uint64_t first_val) {\r\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_bin_mtx);\r\n    g_bin_active = 1;\r\n    g_bin_min = g_bin_max = first_val;\r\n    g_bin_samples = 1;\r\n    g_bin_start_range = 0;\r\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic void finalize_bin_start_range(void) {\r\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_bin_mtx);\r\n    if (g_bin_active && g_bin_start_range == 0) {\r\n        uint64_t r = (g_bin_max > g_bin_min) ? (g_bin_max - g_bin_min) : 1;\r\n        g_bin_start_range = r;\r\n    }\r\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic void end_bin(void) {\r\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_bin_mtx);\r\n    g_bin_active = 0;\r\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic void split_range(WorkRange *parent, WorkRange *left, WorkRange *right) {\r\n    left->base_nonce = parent->base_nonce;\r\n    left->count      = parent->count / 2;\r\n    left->depth      = parent->depth + 1;\r\n    left->sample_min = 0;\r\n    left->sample_max = 0;\r\n    left->sample_rms = 0.0;\r\n    left->sample_count = 0;\r\n    left->parent_range = (parent->sample_count > 0) ? (parent->sample_max - parent->sample_min) : 0;\r\n\r\n    right->base_nonce = parent->base_nonce + left->count;\r\n    right->count      = parent->count - left->count;\r\n    right->depth      = parent->depth + 1;\r\n    right->sample_min = 0;\r\n    right->sample_max = 0;\r\n    right->sample_rms = 0.0;\r\n    right->sample_count = 0;\r\n    right->parent_range = left->parent_range;\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic void *worker_thread(void *arg) {\r\n    WorkQueue *q = (WorkQueue*)arg;\r\n    uint8_t hash[32];\r\n    char hex[65];\r\n    WorkRange rng;\r\n\r\n    uint64_t local_count = 0;\r\n\r\n    while (queue_pop(q, &rng)) {\r\n        if (!atomic_load(&g_running)) break;\r\n        if (atomic_load(&g_signal_fired)) break;\r\n\r\n        uint64_t time_bin = (uint64_t)time(NULL) / BIN_SECONDS;\r\n\r\n        // Sample this range\r\n        sample_range(time_bin, &rng);\r\n\r\n        uint64_t srange = (rng.sample_count > 0) ? (rng.sample_max - rng.sample_min) : 0;\r\n\r\n        // Update best sample range seen\r\n        pthread_mutex_lock(&g_best_mtx);\r\n        if (srange > 0 && srange < g_best_sample_range) {\r\n            g_best_sample_range = srange;\r\n        }\r\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_best_mtx);\r\n\r\n        // Log range activity\r\n        pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n        printf(\"Range d=%2d base=%8lu count=%6lu min=%8lu max=%8lu rms=%.0f range=%lu\\n\",\r\n               rng.depth, rng.base_nonce, rng.count,\r\n               rng.sample_min, rng.sample_max, rng.sample_rms, srange);\r\n        fflush(stdout);\r\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n\r\n        // Decide whether to expand this range (push children) or prune\r\n        int expand = 1;\r\n\r\n        if (rng.depth >= MAX_DEPTH) {\r\n            expand = 0;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        // Prune if this range is much worse than the best seen\r\n        if (expand && srange > 0) {\r\n            pthread_mutex_lock(&g_best_mtx);\r\n            uint64_t best = g_best_sample_range;\r\n            pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_best_mtx);\r\n\r\n            if (best > 0 && (double)srange > PRUNE_RANGE_FRACTION * (double)best) {\r\n                expand = 0;\r\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n                printf(\"  -> Pruned (range too large vs best)\\n\");\r\n                fflush(stdout);\r\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n            }\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        if (expand) {\r\n            WorkRange left, right;\r\n            split_range(&rng, &left, &right);\r\n            queue_push(q, left);\r\n            queue_push(q, right);\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        // Iterate through nonces in this range to update RMS and detect signals\r\n        uint64_t stride = 1ULL << STRIDE_BITS;\r\n        uint64_t end = rng.base_nonce + rng.count;\r\n        for (uint64_t x = rng.base_nonce; x < end; x += stride) {\r\n            if (!atomic_load(&g_running)) break;\r\n            if (atomic_load(&g_signal_fired)) break;\r\n\r\n            if ((x & 0xFFF) == 0 && should_kill_bin()) {\r\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n                printf(\"\\n!!! Bin killed by range growth (depth=%d)\\n\", rng.depth);\r\n                fflush(stdout);\r\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n                goto next_range;\r\n            }\r\n\r\n            uint64_t val = hash_time_nonce(time_bin, x, hash);\r\n\r\n            // Print hash every PRINT_EVERY nonces\r\n            if ((local_count % PRINT_EVERY) == 0) {\r\n                bytes_to_hex(hash, 32, hex);\r\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n                printf(\"HASH time_bin=%lu nonce=%lu -> %s\\n\", time_bin, x, hex);\r\n                fflush(stdout);\r\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n            }\r\n            local_count++;\r\n\r\n            double wrms = 0.0;\r\n            int is_new_low = 0;\r\n            update_stats(val, &wrms, &is_new_low);\r\n\r\n            if (g_bin_samples == 64) {\r\n                finalize_bin_start_range();\r\n            }\r\n\r\n            if (is_new_low) {\r\n                atomic_store(&g_signal_fired, 1);\r\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n                printf(\"\\n\u26a0 SIGNAL: new lowest windowed RMS! rms=%.0f (nonce=%lu, time_bin=%lu, depth=%d)\\n\",\r\n                       wrms, x, time_bin, rng.depth);\r\n                fflush(stdout);\r\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n                break;\r\n            }\r\n\r\n            if ((x & 0xFFFF) == 0) {\r\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n                printf(\".\");\r\n                fflush(stdout);\r\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n            }\r\n        }\r\n\r\n    next_range:;\r\n    }\r\n    return NULL;\r\n}\r\n\r\nint main(int argc, char *argv[]) {\r\n    int num_threads = (argc > 1) ? atoi(argv[1]) : 4;\r\n    if (num_threads < 1) num_threads = 1;\r\n    if (num_threads > MAX_THREADS) num_threads = MAX_THREADS;\r\n\r\n    signal(SIGINT, handle_sigint);\r\n\r\n    WorkQueue q;\r\n    init_queue(&q, 8192);\r\n\r\n    pthread_t threads[MAX_THREADS];\r\n    for (int i=0;i<num_threads;i++) {\r\n        pthread_create(&threads[i], NULL, worker_thread, &q);\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    printf(\"SHA256 time-binned miner with lively binary-tree narrowing.\\n\");\r\n    printf(\"Threads=%d, window=%d, initial_range=2^%d, max_depth=%d, bin=%ds. Ctrl+C to exit.\\n\\n\",\r\n           num_threads, WINDOW_SIZE, INITIAL_RANGE_BITS, MAX_DEPTH, BIN_SECONDS);\r\n\r\n    uint64_t current_time_bin = 0;\r\n    int bin_in_progress = 0;\r\n\r\n    while (atomic_load(&g_running)) {\r\n        uint64_t now_bin = (uint64_t)time(NULL) / BIN_SECONDS;\r\n\r\n        if (now_bin != current_time_bin || atomic_load(&g_signal_fired)) {\r\n            if (bin_in_progress) {\r\n                end_bin();\r\n                bin_in_progress = 0;\r\n                // Clear queue to prune current tree\r\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&q.mtx);\r\n                q.head = q.tail = 0;\r\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&q.mtx);\r\n            }\r\n            current_time_bin = now_bin;\r\n            atomic_store(&g_signal_fired, 0);\r\n\r\n            uint64_t first_val = 0;\r\n            if (should_start_bin(current_time_bin, &first_val)) {\r\n                begin_bin(first_val);\r\n                bin_in_progress = 1;\r\n\r\n                WorkRange root = {\r\n                    .base_nonce = 0,\r\n                    .count      = (1ULL << INITIAL_RANGE_BITS),\r\n                    .depth      = 0,\r\n                    .sample_min = 0,\r\n                    .sample_max = 0,\r\n                    .sample_rms = 0.0,\r\n                    .sample_count = 0,\r\n                    .parent_range = 0\r\n                };\r\n                queue_push(&q, root);\r\n\r\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n                printf(\">>> Pushed root range for bin %lu (2^%d nonces)\\n\", current_time_bin, INITIAL_RANGE_BITS);\r\n                fflush(stdout);\r\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n            }\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        usleep(20000); // 20 ms poll \u2192 more responsive\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    if (bin_in_progress) end_bin();\r\n\r\n    atomic_store(&q.stop, 1);\r\n    pthread_cond_broadcast(&q.cond);\r\n\r\n    for (int i=0;i<num_threads;i++) {\r\n        pthread_join(threads[i], NULL);\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    destroy_queue(&q);\r\n\r\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n    printf(\"\\nStopped. Best bin probe range: %lu. Lowest windowed RMS: %.0f. Best sample range: %lu\\n\",\r\n           g_best_bin_probe_range, g_min_window_rms, g_best_sample_range);\r\n    fflush(stdout);\r\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\r\n\r\n    return 0;\r\n}"}], "files": [{"file_uuid": "da53ec4d-c891-49df-803d-e539b79465b9", "file_name": ""}], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0dcc4-ac58-7a9a-9ec1-19e88cdbe122"}, {"uuid": "01a0dcc6-c538-7e45-962f-a7f1a1136d1e", "text": "Got it \u2014 this is the older version (before I added the HEADER line and self-test). I'll fix only the SHA256 generation itself and leave everything else \u2014 printing, mining logic, RMS/binary-tree behavior \u2014 untouched.\n\nThere are actually **three** bugs in the SHA256 code here, not just the one from before:\n\n1. **Corrupted `k[64]` array** \u2014 two bogus values (`0x1a82f231, 0x24e1c6c8`) are spliced in partway through, shifting everything after them.\n2. **Wrong constant** \u2014 even ignoring #1, index 31 should be `0x14292967`, not `0x14e2e0c6`.\n3. **Broken round rotation** \u2014 `d = d + t1;` should be `e = d + t1; d = c;`. As written, `e` never updates and `d` never receives the old `c`.\n\nThere's also a smaller latent bug in `sha256_final` (`ctx->data[0] = 0x80;` incorrectly re-added after the multi-block padding `memset`, which would corrupt hashes of messages 56\u201363 bytes long) \u2014 I'm fixing that too since it's part of the SHA256 generation itself, per your instruction.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:13:29.088599Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:13:40.590839Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Debugging a corrupted hash algorithm implementation."}, {"summary": "Fixing the padding logic in the finalization step."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:13:40.590904Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:13:44.409414Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Got it \u2014 this is the older version (before I added the HEADER line and self-test). I'll fix only the SHA256 generation itself and leave everything else \u2014 printing, mining logic, RMS/binary-tree behavior \u2014 untouched.\n\nThere are actually **three** bugs in the SHA256 code here, not just the one from before:\n\n1. **Corrupted `k[64]` array** \u2014 two bogus values (`0x1a82f231, 0x24e1c6c8`) are spliced in partway through, shifting everything after them.\n2. **Wrong constant** \u2014 even ignoring #1, index 31 should be `0x14292967`, not `0x14e2e0c6`.\n3. **Broken round rotation** \u2014 `d = d + t1;` should be `e = d + t1; d = c;`. As written, `e` never updates and `d` never receives the old `c`.\n\nThere's also a smaller latent bug in `sha256_final` (`ctx->data[0] = 0x80;` incorrectly re-added after the multi-block padding `memset`, which would corrupt hashes of messages 56\u201363 bytes long) \u2014 I'm fixing that too since it's part of the SHA256 generation itself, per your instruction.", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:13:44.409454Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:14:52.496185Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01EEtfT6JxSSfnHAbgj5b5rs", "name": "create_file", "input": {"description": "User's original file with only the SHA256 generation bugs fixed, everything else unchanged", "path": "/home/claude/hashproject_fixed.c", "file_text": "#define _GNU_SOURCE\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <time.h>\n#include <unistd.h>\n#include <signal.h>\n#include <pthread.h>\n#include <math.h>\n#include <stdatomic.h>\n\n// ---------------- SHA-256 implementation ----------------\n\ntypedef struct {\n    uint8_t  data[64];\n    uint32_t datalen;\n    uint64_t bitlen;\n    uint32_t state[8];\n} SHA256_CTX;\n\n#define ROTRIGHT(a,b) (((a) >> (b)) | ((a) << (32-(b))))\n#define CH(x,y,z)     (((x) & (y)) ^ (~(x) & (z)))\n#define MAJ(x,y,z)    (((x) & (y)) ^ ((x) & (z)) ^ ((y) & (z)))\n#define EP0(x)        (ROTRIGHT(x,2)  ^ ROTRIGHT(x,13) ^ ROTRIGHT(x,22))\n#define EP1(x)        (ROTRIGHT(x,6)  ^ ROTRIGHT(x,11) ^ ROTRIGHT(x,25))\n#define SIG0(x)       (ROTRIGHT(x,7)  ^ ROTRIGHT(x,18) ^ ((x) >> 3))\n#define SIG1(x)       (ROTRIGHT(x,17) ^ ROTRIGHT(x,19) ^ ((x) >> 10))\n\nstatic const uint32_t k[64] = {\n    0x428a2f98,0x71374491,0xb5c0fbcf,0xe9b5dba5,0x3956c25b,0x59f111f1,0x923f82a4,0xab1c5ed5,\n    0xd807aa98,0x12835b01,0x243185be,0x550c7dc3,0x72be5d74,0x80deb1fe,0x9bdc06a7,0xc19bf174,\n    0xe49b69c1,0xefbe4786,0x0fc19dc6,0x240ca1cc,0x2de92c6f,0x4a7484aa,0x5cb0a9dc,0x76f988da,\n    0x983e5152,0xa831c66d,0xb00327c8,0xbf597fc7,0xc6e00bf3,0xd5a79147,0x06ca6351,0x14292967,\n    0x27b70a85,0x2e1b2138,0x4d2c6dfc,0x53380d13,0x650a7354,0x766a0abb,0x81c2c92e,0x92722c85,\n    0xa2bfe8a1,0xa81a664b,0xc24b8b70,0xc76c51a3,0xd192e819,0xd6990624,0xf40e3585,0x106aa070,\n    0x19a4c116,0x1e376c08,0x2748774c,0x34b0bcb5,0x391c0cb3,0x4ed8aa4a,0x5b9cca4f,0x682e6ff3,\n    0x748f82ee,0x78a5636f,0x84c87814,0x8cc70208,0x90befffa,0xa4506ceb,0xbef9a3f7,0xc67178f2\n};\n\nstatic void sha256_transform(SHA256_CTX *ctx, const uint8_t data[]) {\n    uint32_t a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,t1,t2,m[64];\n\n    for (i=0,j=0; i<16; ++i, j+=4)\n        m[i] = ((uint32_t)data[j]<<24) | ((uint32_t)data[j+1]<<16) |\n               ((uint32_t)data[j+2]<<8)  | ((uint32_t)data[j+3]);\n    for (; i<64; ++i)\n        m[i] = SIG1(m[i-2]) + m[i-7] + SIG0(m[i-15]) + m[i-16];\n\n    a = ctx->state[0]; b = ctx->state[1]; c = ctx->state[2]; d = ctx->state[3];\n    e = ctx->state[4]; f = ctx->state[5]; g = ctx->state[6]; h = ctx->state[7];\n\n    for (i=0; i<64; ++i) {\n        t1 = h + EP1(e) + CH(e,f,g) + k[i] + m[i];\n        t2 = EP0(a) + MAJ(a,b,c);\n        h = g; g = f; f = e; e = d + t1; d = c; c = b; b = a; a = t1 + t2;\n    }\n\n    ctx->state[0] += a; ctx->state[1] += b; ctx->state[2] += c; ctx->state[3] += d;\n    ctx->state[4] += e; ctx->state[5] += f; ctx->state[6] += g; ctx->state[7] += h;\n}\n\nstatic void sha256_init(SHA256_CTX *ctx) {\n    ctx->datalen = 0;\n    ctx->bitlen  = 0;\n    ctx->state[0] = 0x6a09e667;\n    ctx->state[1] = 0xbb67ae85;\n    ctx->state[2] = 0x3c6ef372;\n    ctx->state[3] = 0xa54ff53a;\n    ctx->state[4] = 0x510e527f;\n    ctx->state[5] = 0x9b05688c;\n    ctx->state[6] = 0x1f83d9ab;\n    ctx->state[7] = 0x5be0cd19;\n}\n\nstatic void sha256_update(SHA256_CTX *ctx, const uint8_t data[], size_t len) {\n    for (size_t i=0; i<len; ++i) {\n        ctx->data[ctx->datalen] = data[i];\n        ctx->datalen++;\n        if (ctx->datalen == 64) {\n            sha256_transform(ctx, ctx->data);\n            ctx->bitlen += 512;\n            ctx->datalen = 0;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstatic void sha256_final(SHA256_CTX *ctx, uint8_t hash[]) {\n    uint32_t i = ctx->datalen;\n\n    if (ctx->datalen < 56) {\n        ctx->data[i++] = 0x80;\n        while (i < 56)\n            ctx->data[i++] = 0x00;\n    } else {\n        ctx->data[i++] = 0x80;\n        while (i < 64)\n            ctx->data[i++] = 0x00;\n        sha256_transform(ctx, ctx->data);\n        memset(ctx->data, 0, 56);\n    }\n\n    ctx->bitlen += ctx->datalen * 8;\n    ctx->data[63] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen);\n    ctx->data[62] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 8);\n    ctx->data[61] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 16);\n    ctx->data[60] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 24);\n    ctx->data[59] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 32);\n    ctx->data[58] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 40);\n    ctx->data[57] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 48);\n    ctx->data[56] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 56);\n    sha256_transform(ctx, ctx->data);\n\n    for (i=0; i<4; ++i) {\n        hash[i]      = (ctx->state[0] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\n        hash[i+4]    = (ctx->state[1] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\n        hash[i+8]    = (ctx->state[2] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\n        hash[i+12]   = (ctx->state[3] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\n        hash[i+16]   = (ctx->state[4] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\n        hash[i+20]   = (ctx->state[5] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\n        hash[i+24]   = (ctx->state[6] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\n        hash[i+28]   = (ctx->state[7] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------- Block Header & Hash Proof Structure ----------------\n\ntypedef struct {\n    uint64_t time_bin;\n    uint64_t nonce;\n} BlockHeader;\n\n// ---------------- Mining / binary-tree / RMS / min-max logic ----------------\n\n#define WINDOW_SIZE 128\n#define MAX_THREADS 16\n#define BIN_SECONDS   1\n\n// Tree and sampling parameters (tuned for lively behavior)\n#define INITIAL_RANGE_BITS 16          // root range size = 2^16 = 65536\n#define MAX_DEPTH 10                   // allow decent narrowing\n#define SAMPLES_PER_RANGE 16           // fewer samples per range \u2192 faster decisions\n#define STRIDE_BITS 0                  // sample densely\n\n// Start/stop policy parameters (more aggressive)\n#define START_RANGE_FRACTION 10.95      // start bins more often\n#define PRUNE_RANGE_FRACTION 0.7       // prune children more aggressively\n#define KILL_RANGE_FACTOR    2.5       // kill bin sooner\n\n// Probe for deciding whether to start a time bin\n#define PROBE_COUNT 128\n\n// How often to print hashes (every PRINT_EVERY hashes)\n#define PRINT_EVERY 1\n\ntypedef struct {\n    uint64_t base_nonce;\n    uint64_t count;\n    int      depth;\n    uint64_t sample_min;\n    uint64_t sample_max;\n    double   sample_rms;\n    int      sample_count;\n    uint64_t parent_range; // optional: store parent sample range for pruning\n} WorkRange;\n\ntypedef struct {\n    WorkRange *items;\n    size_t     capacity;\n    size_t     head;\n    size_t     tail;\n    pthread_mutex_t mtx;\n    pthread_cond_t  cond;\n    atomic_int      stop;\n} WorkQueue;\n\nstatic atomic_int g_running = 1;\nstatic atomic_int g_signal_fired = 0;\n\nstatic uint64_t g_best_bin_probe_range = UINT64_MAX;\nstatic int      g_have_any_bin_yet = 0;\n\nstatic pthread_mutex_t g_bin_mtx = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\nstatic uint64_t g_bin_min = 0;\nstatic uint64_t g_bin_max = 0;\nstatic uint64_t g_bin_start_range = 0;\nstatic int      g_bin_active = 0;\nstatic uint64_t g_bin_samples = 0;\n\nstatic pthread_mutex_t g_rms_mtx = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\nstatic double g_window[WINDOW_SIZE];\nstatic int    g_win_idx = 0;\nstatic int    g_win_count = 0;\nstatic double g_min_window_rms = -1.0;\n\nstatic pthread_mutex_t g_print_mtx = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\n\n// Track best sample range seen so far (for pruning decisions)\nstatic pthread_mutex_t g_best_mtx = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\nstatic uint64_t g_best_sample_range = UINT64_MAX;\n\nvoid handle_sigint(int sig) {\n    (void)sig;\n    atomic_store(&g_running, 0);\n}\n\nstatic inline uint64_t hash_to_u64(const uint8_t h[32]) {\n    uint64_t v = 0;\n    for (int i=0;i<8;i++) {\n        v = (v << 8) | h[i];\n    }\n    return v;\n}\n\n// Convert 32-byte SHA256 to 64-char hex string\nstatic void bytes_to_hex(const uint8_t *bytes, size_t len, char *out) {\n    static const char hex[] = \"0123456789abcdef\";\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < len; ++i) {\n        out[i*2]   = hex[(bytes[i] >> 4) & 0xF];\n        out[i*2+1] = hex[bytes[i] & 0xF];\n    }\n    out[len*2] = '\\0';\n}\n\n// Header + Hash Function\nstatic uint64_t compute_header_hash(const BlockHeader *hdr, uint8_t out_hash[32]) {\n    SHA256_CTX ctx;\n    sha256_init(&ctx);\n    uint8_t buf[16];\n    for (int i=0; i<8; i++) buf[i]   = (hdr->time_bin >> (56 - 8*i)) & 0xff;\n    for (int i=0; i<8; i++) buf[8+i] = (hdr->nonce    >> (56 - 8*i)) & 0xff;\n    sha256_update(&ctx, buf, 16);\n    sha256_final(&ctx, out_hash);\n    return hash_to_u64(out_hash);\n}\n\n// Hash Proof Verification Function\nstatic int verify_hash_proof(const BlockHeader *hdr, const uint8_t expected_hash[32]) {\n    uint8_t computed_hash[32];\n    compute_header_hash(hdr, computed_hash);\n    return memcmp(computed_hash, expected_hash, 32) == 0;\n}\n\nstatic uint64_t hash_time_nonce(uint64_t time_bin, uint64_t nonce, uint8_t out_hash[32]) {\n    BlockHeader hdr = { .time_bin = time_bin, .nonce = nonce };\n    uint64_t val = compute_header_hash(&hdr, out_hash);\n    \n    // Optional self-check proof validation (can be enabled for debugging proofs)\n    // if (!verify_hash_proof(&hdr, out_hash)) { fprintf(stderr, \"Proof failed!\\n\"); }\n    \n    return val;\n}\n\nstatic void init_queue(WorkQueue *q, size_t cap) {\n    q->items = malloc(cap * sizeof(WorkRange));\n    q->capacity = cap;\n    q->head = q->tail = 0;\n    pthread_mutex_init(&q->mtx, NULL);\n    pthread_cond_init(&q->cond, NULL);\n    atomic_store(&q->stop, 0);\n}\n\nstatic void destroy_queue(WorkQueue *q) {\n    free(q->items);\n    pthread_mutex_destroy(&q->mtx);\n    pthread_cond_destroy(&q->cond);\n}\n\nstatic int queue_push(WorkQueue *q, WorkRange item) {\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&q->mtx);\n    if ((q->tail + 1) % q->capacity == q->head) {\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&q->mtx);\n        return 0;\n    }\n    q->items[q->tail] = item;\n    q->tail = (q->tail + 1) % q->capacity;\n    pthread_cond_signal(&q->cond);\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&q->mtx);\n    return 1;\n}\n\nstatic int queue_pop(WorkQueue *q, WorkRange *out) {\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&q->mtx);\n    while (q->head == q->tail && !atomic_load(&q->stop)) {\n        pthread_cond_wait(&q->cond, &q->mtx);\n    }\n    if (atomic_load(&q->stop) && q->head == q->tail) {\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&q->mtx);\n        return 0;\n    }\n    *out = q->items[q->head];\n    q->head = (q->head + 1) % q->capacity;\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&q->mtx);\n    return 1;\n}\n\nstatic uint64_t probe_bin(uint64_t time_bin, size_t probe_count, uint64_t *out_first_val) {\n    uint8_t hash[32];\n    uint64_t pmin = UINT64_MAX;\n    uint64_t pmax = 0;\n    uint64_t first_val = 0;\n\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < probe_count; ++i) {\n        uint64_t val = hash_time_nonce(time_bin, i, hash);\n        if (i == 0) first_val = val;\n        if (val < pmin) pmin = val;\n        if (val > pmax) pmax = val;\n    }\n\n    if (out_first_val) *out_first_val = first_val;\n    return (pmax > pmin) ? (pmax - pmin) : 0;\n}\n\nstatic int should_start_bin(uint64_t time_bin, uint64_t *out_first_val) {\n    uint64_t probe_range = probe_bin(time_bin, PROBE_COUNT, out_first_val);\n\n    int start = 0;\n\n    if (!g_have_any_bin_yet) {\n        g_best_bin_probe_range = probe_range;\n        g_have_any_bin_yet = 1;\n        start = 1;\n    } else {\n        double threshold = START_RANGE_FRACTION * (double)g_best_bin_probe_range;\n        if ((double)probe_range <= threshold) {\n            if (probe_range < g_best_bin_probe_range) {\n                g_best_bin_probe_range = probe_range;\n            }\n            start = 1;\n        }\n    }\n\n    if (start) {\n        pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\n        printf(\">>> Starting bin %lu: probe_range=%lu (best_so_far=%lu)\\n\",\n               time_bin, probe_range, g_best_bin_probe_range);\n        fflush(stdout);\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\n    } else {\n        pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\n        printf(\"... Skipping bin %lu: probe_range=%lu > %.2f * best=%lu\\n\",\n               time_bin, probe_range, START_RANGE_FRACTION, g_best_bin_probe_range);\n        fflush(stdout);\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\n    }\n\n    return start;\n}\n\nstatic void sample_range(uint64_t time_bin, WorkRange *r) {\n    uint8_t hash[32];\n    uint64_t sum_sq = 0;\n    uint64_t rmin = UINT64_MAX;\n    uint64_t rmax = 0;\n    int count = 0;\n\n    uint64_t stride = 1ULL << STRIDE_BITS;\n    uint64_t end = r->base_nonce + r->count;\n\n    uint64_t step = (r->count + SAMPLES_PER_RANGE - 1) / SAMPLES_PER_RANGE;\n    if (step < 1) step = 1;\n    if (step < stride) step = stride;\n\n    for (uint64_t x = r->base_nonce; x < end; x += step) {\n        uint64_t val = hash_time_nonce(time_bin, x, hash);\n        if (val < rmin) rmin = val;\n        if (val > rmax) rmax = val;\n        sum_sq += val * val;\n        count++;\n        if (count >= SAMPLES_PER_RANGE) break;\n    }\n\n    r->sample_min = rmin;\n    r->sample_max = rmax;\n    r->sample_count = count;\n    if (count > 0) {\n        r->sample_rms = sqrt((double)sum_sq / count);\n    } else {\n        r->sample_rms = 0.0;\n    }\n}\n\nstatic void update_stats(uint64_t val, double *out_window_rms, int *out_is_new_low_rms) {\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_bin_mtx);\n    if (!g_bin_active) {\n        g_bin_min = g_bin_max = val;\n        g_bin_samples = 1;\n    } else {\n        if (val < g_bin_min) g_bin_min = val;\n        if (val > g_bin_max) g_bin_max = val;\n        g_bin_samples++;\n    }\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\n\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_rms_mtx);\n    g_window[g_win_idx] = (double)val;\n    g_win_idx = (g_win_idx + 1) % WINDOW_SIZE;\n    if (g_win_count < WINDOW_SIZE) g_win_count++;\n\n    double sum_sq = 0.0;\n    for (int i=0;i<g_win_count;i++) sum_sq += g_window[i] * g_window[i];\n    double wrms = sqrt(sum_sq / g_win_count);\n    *out_window_rms = wrms;\n\n    int is_new_low = 0;\n    if (g_min_window_rms < 0.0 || wrms < g_min_window_rms) {\n        g_min_window_rms = wrms;\n        is_new_low = 1;\n    }\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_rms_mtx);\n    *out_is_new_low_rms = is_new_low;\n}\n\nstatic int should_kill_bin(void) {\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_bin_mtx);\n    if (!g_bin_active) {\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\n        return 0;\n    }\n    uint64_t cur_range = (g_bin_max > g_bin_min) ? (g_bin_max - g_bin_min) : 0;\n    uint64_t kill_threshold = (uint64_t)((double)g_bin_start_range * KILL_RANGE_FACTOR);\n    int kill = (cur_range > kill_threshold && g_bin_start_range > 0);\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\n    return kill;\n}\n\nstatic void begin_bin(uint64_t first_val) {\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_bin_mtx);\n    g_bin_active = 1;\n    g_bin_min = g_bin_max = first_val;\n    g_bin_samples = 1;\n    g_bin_start_range = 0;\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\n}\n\nstatic void finalize_bin_start_range(void) {\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_bin_mtx);\n    if (g_bin_active && g_bin_start_range == 0) {\n        uint64_t r = (g_bin_max > g_bin_min) ? (g_bin_max - g_bin_min) : 1;\n        g_bin_start_range = r;\n    }\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\n}\n\nstatic void end_bin(void) {\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_bin_mtx);\n    g_bin_active = 0;\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\n}\n\nstatic void split_range(WorkRange *parent, WorkRange *left, WorkRange *right) {\n    left->base_nonce = parent->base_nonce;\n    left->count      = parent->count / 2;\n    left->depth      = parent->depth + 1;\n    left->sample_min = 0;\n    left->sample_max = 0;\n    left->sample_rms = 0.0;\n    left->sample_count = 0;\n    left->parent_range = (parent->sample_count > 0) ? (parent->sample_max - parent->sample_min) : 0;\n\n    right->base_nonce = parent->base_nonce + left->count;\n    right->count      = parent->count - left->count;\n    right->depth      = parent->depth + 1;\n    right->sample_min = 0;\n    right->sample_max = 0;\n    right->sample_rms = 0.0;\n    right->sample_count = 0;\n    right->parent_range = left->parent_range;\n}\n\nstatic void *worker_thread(void *arg) {\n    WorkQueue *q = (WorkQueue*)arg;\n    uint8_t hash[32];\n    char hex[65];\n    WorkRange rng;\n\n    uint64_t local_count = 0;\n\n    while (queue_pop(q, &rng)) {\n        if (!atomic_load(&g_running)) break;\n        if (atomic_load(&g_signal_fired)) break;\n\n        uint64_t time_bin = (uint64_t)time(NULL) / BIN_SECONDS;\n\n        // Sample this range\n        sample_range(time_bin, &rng);\n\n        uint64_t srange = (rng.sample_count > 0) ? (rng.sample_max - rng.sample_min) : 0;\n\n        // Update best sample range seen\n        pthread_mutex_lock(&g_best_mtx);\n        if (srange > 0 && srange < g_best_sample_range) {\n            g_best_sample_range = srange;\n        }\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_best_mtx);\n\n        // Log range activity\n        pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\n        printf(\"Range d=%2d base=%8lu count=%6lu min=%8lu max=%8lu rms=%.0f range=%lu\\n\",\n               rng.depth, rng.base_nonce, rng.count,\n               rng.sample_min, rng.sample_max, rng.sample_rms, srange);\n        fflush(stdout);\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\n\n        // Decide whether to expand this range (push children) or prune\n        int expand = 1;\n\n        if (rng.depth >= MAX_DEPTH) {\n            expand = 0;\n        }\n\n        // Prune if this range is much worse than the best seen\n        if (expand && srange > 0) {\n            pthread_mutex_lock(&g_best_mtx);\n            uint64_t best = g_best_sample_range;\n            pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_best_mtx);\n\n            if (best > 0 && (double)srange > PRUNE_RANGE_FRACTION * (double)best) {\n                expand = 0;\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\n                printf(\"  -> Pruned (range too large vs best)\\n\");\n                fflush(stdout);\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\n            }\n        }\n\n        if (expand) {\n            WorkRange left, right;\n            split_range(&rng, &left, &right);\n            queue_push(q, left);\n            queue_push(q, right);\n        }\n\n        // Iterate through nonces in this range to update RMS and detect signals\n        uint64_t stride = 1ULL << STRIDE_BITS;\n        uint64_t end = rng.base_nonce + rng.count;\n        for (uint64_t x = rng.base_nonce; x < end; x += stride) {\n            if (!atomic_load(&g_running)) break;\n            if (atomic_load(&g_signal_fired)) break;\n\n            if ((x & 0xFFF) == 0 && should_kill_bin()) {\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\n                printf(\"\\n!!! Bin killed by range growth (depth=%d)\\n\", rng.depth);\n                fflush(stdout);\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\n                goto next_range;\n            }\n\n            uint64_t val = hash_time_nonce(time_bin, x, hash);\n\n            // Print hash every PRINT_EVERY nonces\n            if ((local_count % PRINT_EVERY) == 0) {\n                bytes_to_hex(hash, 32, hex);\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\n                printf(\"HASH time_bin=%lu nonce=%lu -> %s\\n\", time_bin, x, hex);\n                fflush(stdout);\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\n            }\n            local_count++;\n\n            double wrms = 0.0;\n            int is_new_low = 0;\n            update_stats(val, &wrms, &is_new_low);\n\n            if (g_bin_samples == 64) {\n                finalize_bin_start_range();\n            }\n\n            if (is_new_low) {\n                atomic_store(&g_signal_fired, 1);\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\n                printf(\"\\n\u26a0 SIGNAL: new lowest windowed RMS! rms=%.0f (nonce=%lu, time_bin=%lu, depth=%d)\\n\",\n                       wrms, x, time_bin, rng.depth);\n                fflush(stdout);\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\n                break;\n            }\n\n            if ((x & 0xFFFF) == 0) {\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\n                printf(\".\");\n                fflush(stdout);\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\n            }\n        }\n\n    next_range:;\n    }\n    return NULL;\n}\n\nint main(int argc, char *argv[]) {\n    int num_threads = (argc > 1) ? atoi(argv[1]) : 4;\n    if (num_threads < 1) num_threads = 1;\n    if (num_threads > MAX_THREADS) num_threads = MAX_THREADS;\n\n    signal(SIGINT, handle_sigint);\n\n    WorkQueue q;\n    init_queue(&q, 8192);\n\n    pthread_t threads[MAX_THREADS];\n    for (int i=0;i<num_threads;i++) {\n        pthread_create(&threads[i], NULL, worker_thread, &q);\n    }\n\n    printf(\"SHA256 time-binned miner with lively binary-tree narrowing.\\n\");\n    printf(\"Threads=%d, window=%d, initial_range=2^%d, max_depth=%d, bin=%ds. Ctrl+C to exit.\\n\\n\",\n           num_threads, WINDOW_SIZE, INITIAL_RANGE_BITS, MAX_DEPTH, BIN_SECONDS);\n\n    uint64_t current_time_bin = 0;\n    int bin_in_progress = 0;\n\n    while (atomic_load(&g_running)) {\n        uint64_t now_bin = (uint64_t)time(NULL) / BIN_SECONDS;\n\n        if (now_bin != current_time_bin || atomic_load(&g_signal_fired)) {\n            if (bin_in_progress) {\n                end_bin();\n                bin_in_progress = 0;\n                // Clear queue to prune current tree\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&q.mtx);\n                q.head = q.tail = 0;\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&q.mtx);\n            }\n            current_time_bin = now_bin;\n            atomic_store(&g_signal_fired, 0);\n\n            uint64_t first_val = 0;\n            if (should_start_bin(current_time_bin, &first_val)) {\n                begin_bin(first_val);\n                bin_in_progress = 1;\n\n                WorkRange root = {\n                    .base_nonce = 0,\n                    .count      = (1ULL << INITIAL_RANGE_BITS),\n                    .depth      = 0,\n                    .sample_min = 0,\n                    .sample_max = 0,\n                    .sample_rms = 0.0,\n                    .sample_count = 0,\n                    .parent_range = 0\n                };\n                queue_push(&q, root);\n\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\n                printf(\">>> Pushed root range for bin %lu (2^%d nonces)\\n\", current_time_bin, INITIAL_RANGE_BITS);\n                fflush(stdout);\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\n            }\n        }\n\n        usleep(20000); // 20 ms poll \u2192 more responsive\n    }\n\n    if (bin_in_progress) end_bin();\n\n    atomic_store(&q.stop, 1);\n    pthread_cond_broadcast(&q.cond);\n\n    for (int i=0;i<num_threads;i++) {\n        pthread_join(threads[i], NULL);\n    }\n\n    destroy_queue(&q);\n\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\n    printf(\"\\nStopped. Best bin probe range: %lu. Lowest windowed RMS: %.0f. Best sample range: %lu\\n\",\n           g_best_bin_probe_range, g_min_window_rms, g_best_sample_range);\n    fflush(stdout);\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\n\n    return 0;\n}\n"}, "message": "User's original file with only the SHA256 generation bugs fixed, everything else unchanged", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"c\", \"code\": \"#define _GNU_SOURCE\\n#include <stdio.h>\\n#include <stdint.h>\\n#include <stdlib.h>\\n#include <string.h>\\n#include <time.h>\\n#include <unistd.h>\\n#include <signal.h>\\n#include <pthread.h>\\n#include <math.h>\\n#include <stdatomic.h>\\n\\n// ---------------- SHA-256 implementation ----------------\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    uint8_t  data[64];\\n    uint32_t datalen;\\n    uint64_t bitlen;\\n    uint32_t state[8];\\n} SHA256_CTX;\\n\\n#define ROTRIGHT(a,b) (((a) >> (b)) | ((a) << (32-(b))))\\n#define CH(x,y,z)     (((x) & (y)) ^ (~(x) & (z)))\\n#define MAJ(x,y,z)    (((x) & (y)) ^ ((x) & (z)) ^ ((y) & (z)))\\n#define EP0(x)        (ROTRIGHT(x,2)  ^ ROTRIGHT(x,13) ^ ROTRIGHT(x,22))\\n#define EP1(x)        (ROTRIGHT(x,6)  ^ ROTRIGHT(x,11) ^ ROTRIGHT(x,25))\\n#define SIG0(x)       (ROTRIGHT(x,7)  ^ ROTRIGHT(x,18) ^ ((x) >> 3))\\n#define SIG1(x)       (ROTRIGHT(x,17) ^ ROTRIGHT(x,19) ^ ((x) >> 10))\\n\\nstatic const uint32_t k[64] = {\\n    0x428a2f98,0x71374491,0xb5c0fbcf,0xe9b5dba5,0x3956c25b,0x59f111f1,0x923f82a4,0xab1c5ed5,\\n    0xd807aa98,0x12835b01,0x243185be,0x550c7dc3,0x72be5d74,0x80deb1fe,0x9bdc06a7,0xc19bf174,\\n    0xe49b69c1,0xefbe4786,0x0fc19dc6,0x240ca1cc,0x2de92c6f,0x4a7484aa,0x5cb0a9dc,0x76f988da,\\n    0x983e5152,0xa831c66d,0xb00327c8,0xbf597fc7,0xc6e00bf3,0xd5a79147,0x06ca6351,0x14292967,\\n    0x27b70a85,0x2e1b2138,0x4d2c6dfc,0x53380d13,0x650a7354,0x766a0abb,0x81c2c92e,0x92722c85,\\n    0xa2bfe8a1,0xa81a664b,0xc24b8b70,0xc76c51a3,0xd192e819,0xd6990624,0xf40e3585,0x106aa070,\\n    0x19a4c116,0x1e376c08,0x2748774c,0x34b0bcb5,0x391c0cb3,0x4ed8aa4a,0x5b9cca4f,0x682e6ff3,\\n    0x748f82ee,0x78a5636f,0x84c87814,0x8cc70208,0x90befffa,0xa4506ceb,0xbef9a3f7,0xc67178f2\\n};\\n\\nstatic void sha256_transform(SHA256_CTX *ctx, const uint8_t data[]) {\\n    uint32_t a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,t1,t2,m[64];\\n\\n    for (i=0,j=0; i<16; ++i, j+=4)\\n        m[i] = ((uint32_t)data[j]<<24) | ((uint32_t)data[j+1]<<16) |\\n               ((uint32_t)data[j+2]<<8)  | ((uint32_t)data[j+3]);\\n    for (; i<64; ++i)\\n        m[i] = SIG1(m[i-2]) + m[i-7] + SIG0(m[i-15]) + m[i-16];\\n\\n    a = ctx->state[0]; b = ctx->state[1]; c = ctx->state[2]; d = ctx->state[3];\\n    e = ctx->state[4]; f = ctx->state[5]; g = ctx->state[6]; h = ctx->state[7];\\n\\n    for (i=0; i<64; ++i) {\\n        t1 = h + EP1(e) + CH(e,f,g) + k[i] + m[i];\\n        t2 = EP0(a) + MAJ(a,b,c);\\n        h = g; g = f; f = e; e = d + t1; d = c; c = b; b = a; a = t1 + t2;\\n    }\\n\\n    ctx->state[0] += a; ctx->state[1] += b; ctx->state[2] += c; ctx->state[3] += d;\\n    ctx->state[4] += e; ctx->state[5] += f; ctx->state[6] += g; ctx->state[7] += h;\\n}\\n\\nstatic void sha256_init(SHA256_CTX *ctx) {\\n    ctx->datalen = 0;\\n    ctx->bitlen  = 0;\\n    ctx->state[0] = 0x6a09e667;\\n    ctx->state[1] = 0xbb67ae85;\\n    ctx->state[2] = 0x3c6ef372;\\n    ctx->state[3] = 0xa54ff53a;\\n    ctx->state[4] = 0x510e527f;\\n    ctx->state[5] = 0x9b05688c;\\n    ctx->state[6] = 0x1f83d9ab;\\n    ctx->state[7] = 0x5be0cd19;\\n}\\n\\nstatic void sha256_update(SHA256_CTX *ctx, const uint8_t data[], size_t len) {\\n    for (size_t i=0; i<len; ++i) {\\n        ctx->data[ctx->datalen] = data[i];\\n        ctx->datalen++;\\n        if (ctx->datalen == 64) {\\n            sha256_transform(ctx, ctx->data);\\n            ctx->bitlen += 512;\\n            ctx->datalen = 0;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nstatic void sha256_final(SHA256_CTX *ctx, uint8_t hash[]) {\\n    uint32_t i = ctx->datalen;\\n\\n    if (ctx->datalen < 56) {\\n        ctx->data[i++] = 0x80;\\n        while (i < 56)\\n            ctx->data[i++] = 0x00;\\n    } else {\\n        ctx->data[i++] = 0x80;\\n        while (i < 64)\\n            ctx->data[i++] = 0x00;\\n        sha256_transform(ctx, ctx->data);\\n        memset(ctx->data, 0, 56);\\n    }\\n\\n    ctx->bitlen += ctx->datalen * 8;\\n    ctx->data[63] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen);\\n    ctx->data[62] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 8);\\n    ctx->data[61] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 16);\\n    ctx->data[60] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 24);\\n    ctx->data[59] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 32);\\n    ctx->data[58] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 40);\\n    ctx->data[57] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 48);\\n    ctx->data[56] = (uint8_t)(ctx->bitlen >> 56);\\n    sha256_transform(ctx, ctx->data);\\n\\n    for (i=0; i<4; ++i) {\\n        hash[i]      = (ctx->state[0] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\\n        hash[i+4]    = (ctx->state[1] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\\n        hash[i+8]    = (ctx->state[2] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\\n        hash[i+12]   = (ctx->state[3] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\\n        hash[i+16]   = (ctx->state[4] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\\n        hash[i+20]   = (ctx->state[5] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\\n        hash[i+24]   = (ctx->state[6] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\\n        hash[i+28]   = (ctx->state[7] >> (24 - i*8)) & 0xff;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// ---------------- Block Header & Hash Proof Structure ----------------\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    uint64_t time_bin;\\n    uint64_t nonce;\\n} BlockHeader;\\n\\n// ---------------- Mining / binary-tree / RMS / min-max logic ----------------\\n\\n#define WINDOW_SIZE 128\\n#define MAX_THREADS 16\\n#define BIN_SECONDS   1\\n\\n// Tree and sampling parameters (tuned for lively behavior)\\n#define INITIAL_RANGE_BITS 16          // root range size = 2^16 = 65536\\n#define MAX_DEPTH 10                   // allow decent narrowing\\n#define SAMPLES_PER_RANGE 16           // fewer samples per range \\u2192 faster decisions\\n#define STRIDE_BITS 0                  // sample densely\\n\\n// Start/stop policy parameters (more aggressive)\\n#define START_RANGE_FRACTION 10.95      // start bins more often\\n#define PRUNE_RANGE_FRACTION 0.7       // prune children more aggressively\\n#define KILL_RANGE_FACTOR    2.5       // kill bin sooner\\n\\n// Probe for deciding whether to start a time bin\\n#define PROBE_COUNT 128\\n\\n// How often to print hashes (every PRINT_EVERY hashes)\\n#define PRINT_EVERY 1\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    uint64_t base_nonce;\\n    uint64_t count;\\n    int      depth;\\n    uint64_t sample_min;\\n    uint64_t sample_max;\\n    double   sample_rms;\\n    int      sample_count;\\n    uint64_t parent_range; // optional: store parent sample range for pruning\\n} WorkRange;\\n\\ntypedef struct {\\n    WorkRange *items;\\n    size_t     capacity;\\n    size_t     head;\\n    size_t     tail;\\n    pthread_mutex_t mtx;\\n    pthread_cond_t  cond;\\n    atomic_int      stop;\\n} WorkQueue;\\n\\nstatic atomic_int g_running = 1;\\nstatic atomic_int g_signal_fired = 0;\\n\\nstatic uint64_t g_best_bin_probe_range = UINT64_MAX;\\nstatic int      g_have_any_bin_yet = 0;\\n\\nstatic pthread_mutex_t g_bin_mtx = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\\nstatic uint64_t g_bin_min = 0;\\nstatic uint64_t g_bin_max = 0;\\nstatic uint64_t g_bin_start_range = 0;\\nstatic int      g_bin_active = 0;\\nstatic uint64_t g_bin_samples = 0;\\n\\nstatic pthread_mutex_t g_rms_mtx = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\\nstatic double g_window[WINDOW_SIZE];\\nstatic int    g_win_idx = 0;\\nstatic int    g_win_count = 0;\\nstatic double g_min_window_rms = -1.0;\\n\\nstatic pthread_mutex_t g_print_mtx = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\\n\\n// Track best sample range seen so far (for pruning decisions)\\nstatic pthread_mutex_t g_best_mtx = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\\nstatic uint64_t g_best_sample_range = UINT64_MAX;\\n\\nvoid handle_sigint(int sig) {\\n    (void)sig;\\n    atomic_store(&g_running, 0);\\n}\\n\\nstatic inline uint64_t hash_to_u64(const uint8_t h[32]) {\\n    uint64_t v = 0;\\n    for (int i=0;i<8;i++) {\\n        v = (v << 8) | h[i];\\n    }\\n    return v;\\n}\\n\\n// Convert 32-byte SHA256 to 64-char hex string\\nstatic void bytes_to_hex(const uint8_t *bytes, size_t len, char *out) {\\n    static const char hex[] = \\\"0123456789abcdef\\\";\\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < len; ++i) {\\n        out[i*2]   = hex[(bytes[i] >> 4) & 0xF];\\n        out[i*2+1] = hex[bytes[i] & 0xF];\\n    }\\n    out[len*2] = '\\\\0';\\n}\\n\\n// Header + Hash Function\\nstatic uint64_t compute_header_hash(const BlockHeader *hdr, uint8_t out_hash[32]) {\\n    SHA256_CTX ctx;\\n    sha256_init(&ctx);\\n    uint8_t buf[16];\\n    for (int i=0; i<8; i++) buf[i]   = (hdr->time_bin >> (56 - 8*i)) & 0xff;\\n    for (int i=0; i<8; i++) buf[8+i] = (hdr->nonce    >> (56 - 8*i)) & 0xff;\\n    sha256_update(&ctx, buf, 16);\\n    sha256_final(&ctx, out_hash);\\n    return hash_to_u64(out_hash);\\n}\\n\\n// Hash Proof Verification Function\\nstatic int verify_hash_proof(const BlockHeader *hdr, const uint8_t expected_hash[32]) {\\n    uint8_t computed_hash[32];\\n    compute_header_hash(hdr, computed_hash);\\n    return memcmp(computed_hash, expected_hash, 32) == 0;\\n}\\n\\nstatic uint64_t hash_time_nonce(uint64_t time_bin, uint64_t nonce, uint8_t out_hash[32]) {\\n    BlockHeader hdr = { .time_bin = time_bin, .nonce = nonce };\\n    uint64_t val = compute_header_hash(&hdr, out_hash);\\n    \\n    // Optional self-check proof validation (can be enabled for debugging proofs)\\n    // if (!verify_hash_proof(&hdr, out_hash)) { fprintf(stderr, \\\"Proof failed!\\\\n\\\"); }\\n    \\n    return val;\\n}\\n\\nstatic void init_queue(WorkQueue *q, size_t cap) {\\n    q->items = malloc(cap * sizeof(WorkRange));\\n    q->capacity = cap;\\n    q->head = q->tail = 0;\\n    pthread_mutex_init(&q->mtx, NULL);\\n    pthread_cond_init(&q->cond, NULL);\\n    atomic_store(&q->stop, 0);\\n}\\n\\nstatic void destroy_queue(WorkQueue *q) {\\n    free(q->items);\\n    pthread_mutex_destroy(&q->mtx);\\n    pthread_cond_destroy(&q->cond);\\n}\\n\\nstatic int queue_push(WorkQueue *q, WorkRange item) {\\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&q->mtx);\\n    if ((q->tail + 1) % q->capacity == q->head) {\\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&q->mtx);\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n    q->items[q->tail] = item;\\n    q->tail = (q->tail + 1) % q->capacity;\\n    pthread_cond_signal(&q->cond);\\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&q->mtx);\\n    return 1;\\n}\\n\\nstatic int queue_pop(WorkQueue *q, WorkRange *out) {\\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&q->mtx);\\n    while (q->head == q->tail && !atomic_load(&q->stop)) {\\n        pthread_cond_wait(&q->cond, &q->mtx);\\n    }\\n    if (atomic_load(&q->stop) && q->head == q->tail) {\\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&q->mtx);\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n    *out = q->items[q->head];\\n    q->head = (q->head + 1) % q->capacity;\\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&q->mtx);\\n    return 1;\\n}\\n\\nstatic uint64_t probe_bin(uint64_t time_bin, size_t probe_count, uint64_t *out_first_val) {\\n    uint8_t hash[32];\\n    uint64_t pmin = UINT64_MAX;\\n    uint64_t pmax = 0;\\n    uint64_t first_val = 0;\\n\\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < probe_count; ++i) {\\n        uint64_t val = hash_time_nonce(time_bin, i, hash);\\n        if (i == 0) first_val = val;\\n        if (val < pmin) pmin = val;\\n        if (val > pmax) pmax = val;\\n    }\\n\\n    if (out_first_val) *out_first_val = first_val;\\n    return (pmax > pmin) ? (pmax - pmin) : 0;\\n}\\n\\nstatic int should_start_bin(uint64_t time_bin, uint64_t *out_first_val) {\\n    uint64_t probe_range = probe_bin(time_bin, PROBE_COUNT, out_first_val);\\n\\n    int start = 0;\\n\\n    if (!g_have_any_bin_yet) {\\n        g_best_bin_probe_range = probe_range;\\n        g_have_any_bin_yet = 1;\\n        start = 1;\\n    } else {\\n        double threshold = START_RANGE_FRACTION * (double)g_best_bin_probe_range;\\n        if ((double)probe_range <= threshold) {\\n            if (probe_range < g_best_bin_probe_range) {\\n                g_best_bin_probe_range = probe_range;\\n            }\\n            start = 1;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    if (start) {\\n        pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\\n        printf(\\\">>> Starting bin %lu: probe_range=%lu (best_so_far=%lu)\\\\n\\\",\\n               time_bin, probe_range, g_best_bin_probe_range);\\n        fflush(stdout);\\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\\n    } else {\\n        pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\\n        printf(\\\"... Skipping bin %lu: probe_range=%lu > %.2f * best=%lu\\\\n\\\",\\n               time_bin, probe_range, START_RANGE_FRACTION, g_best_bin_probe_range);\\n        fflush(stdout);\\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\\n    }\\n\\n    return start;\\n}\\n\\nstatic void sample_range(uint64_t time_bin, WorkRange *r) {\\n    uint8_t hash[32];\\n    uint64_t sum_sq = 0;\\n    uint64_t rmin = UINT64_MAX;\\n    uint64_t rmax = 0;\\n    int count = 0;\\n\\n    uint64_t stride = 1ULL << STRIDE_BITS;\\n    uint64_t end = r->base_nonce + r->count;\\n\\n    uint64_t step = (r->count + SAMPLES_PER_RANGE - 1) / SAMPLES_PER_RANGE;\\n    if (step < 1) step = 1;\\n    if (step < stride) step = stride;\\n\\n    for (uint64_t x = r->base_nonce; x < end; x += step) {\\n        uint64_t val = hash_time_nonce(time_bin, x, hash);\\n        if (val < rmin) rmin = val;\\n        if (val > rmax) rmax = val;\\n        sum_sq += val * val;\\n        count++;\\n        if (count >= SAMPLES_PER_RANGE) break;\\n    }\\n\\n    r->sample_min = rmin;\\n    r->sample_max = rmax;\\n    r->sample_count = count;\\n    if (count > 0) {\\n        r->sample_rms = sqrt((double)sum_sq / count);\\n    } else {\\n        r->sample_rms = 0.0;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nstatic void update_stats(uint64_t val, double *out_window_rms, int *out_is_new_low_rms) {\\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_bin_mtx);\\n    if (!g_bin_active) {\\n        g_bin_min = g_bin_max = val;\\n        g_bin_samples = 1;\\n    } else {\\n        if (val < g_bin_min) g_bin_min = val;\\n        if (val > g_bin_max) g_bin_max = val;\\n        g_bin_samples++;\\n    }\\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\\n\\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_rms_mtx);\\n    g_window[g_win_idx] = (double)val;\\n    g_win_idx = (g_win_idx + 1) % WINDOW_SIZE;\\n    if (g_win_count < WINDOW_SIZE) g_win_count++;\\n\\n    double sum_sq = 0.0;\\n    for (int i=0;i<g_win_count;i++) sum_sq += g_window[i] * g_window[i];\\n    double wrms = sqrt(sum_sq / g_win_count);\\n    *out_window_rms = wrms;\\n\\n    int is_new_low = 0;\\n    if (g_min_window_rms < 0.0 || wrms < g_min_window_rms) {\\n        g_min_window_rms = wrms;\\n        is_new_low = 1;\\n    }\\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_rms_mtx);\\n    *out_is_new_low_rms = is_new_low;\\n}\\n\\nstatic int should_kill_bin(void) {\\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_bin_mtx);\\n    if (!g_bin_active) {\\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\\n        return 0;\\n    }\\n    uint64_t cur_range = (g_bin_max > g_bin_min) ? (g_bin_max - g_bin_min) : 0;\\n    uint64_t kill_threshold = (uint64_t)((double)g_bin_start_range * KILL_RANGE_FACTOR);\\n    int kill = (cur_range > kill_threshold && g_bin_start_range > 0);\\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\\n    return kill;\\n}\\n\\nstatic void begin_bin(uint64_t first_val) {\\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_bin_mtx);\\n    g_bin_active = 1;\\n    g_bin_min = g_bin_max = first_val;\\n    g_bin_samples = 1;\\n    g_bin_start_range = 0;\\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\\n}\\n\\nstatic void finalize_bin_start_range(void) {\\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_bin_mtx);\\n    if (g_bin_active && g_bin_start_range == 0) {\\n        uint64_t r = (g_bin_max > g_bin_min) ? (g_bin_max - g_bin_min) : 1;\\n        g_bin_start_range = r;\\n    }\\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\\n}\\n\\nstatic void end_bin(void) {\\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_bin_mtx);\\n    g_bin_active = 0;\\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_bin_mtx);\\n}\\n\\nstatic void split_range(WorkRange *parent, WorkRange *left, WorkRange *right) {\\n    left->base_nonce = parent->base_nonce;\\n    left->count      = parent->count / 2;\\n    left->depth      = parent->depth + 1;\\n    left->sample_min = 0;\\n    left->sample_max = 0;\\n    left->sample_rms = 0.0;\\n    left->sample_count = 0;\\n    left->parent_range = (parent->sample_count > 0) ? (parent->sample_max - parent->sample_min) : 0;\\n\\n    right->base_nonce = parent->base_nonce + left->count;\\n    right->count      = parent->count - left->count;\\n    right->depth      = parent->depth + 1;\\n    right->sample_min = 0;\\n    right->sample_max = 0;\\n    right->sample_rms = 0.0;\\n    right->sample_count = 0;\\n    right->parent_range = left->parent_range;\\n}\\n\\nstatic void *worker_thread(void *arg) {\\n    WorkQueue *q = (WorkQueue*)arg;\\n    uint8_t hash[32];\\n    char hex[65];\\n    WorkRange rng;\\n\\n    uint64_t local_count = 0;\\n\\n    while (queue_pop(q, &rng)) {\\n        if (!atomic_load(&g_running)) break;\\n        if (atomic_load(&g_signal_fired)) break;\\n\\n        uint64_t time_bin = (uint64_t)time(NULL) / BIN_SECONDS;\\n\\n        // Sample this range\\n        sample_range(time_bin, &rng);\\n\\n        uint64_t srange = (rng.sample_count > 0) ? (rng.sample_max - rng.sample_min) : 0;\\n\\n        // Update best sample range seen\\n        pthread_mutex_lock(&g_best_mtx);\\n        if (srange > 0 && srange < g_best_sample_range) {\\n            g_best_sample_range = srange;\\n        }\\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_best_mtx);\\n\\n        // Log range activity\\n        pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\\n        printf(\\\"Range d=%2d base=%8lu count=%6lu min=%8lu max=%8lu rms=%.0f range=%lu\\\\n\\\",\\n               rng.depth, rng.base_nonce, rng.count,\\n               rng.sample_min, rng.sample_max, rng.sample_rms, srange);\\n        fflush(stdout);\\n        pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\\n\\n        // Decide whether to expand this range (push children) or prune\\n        int expand = 1;\\n\\n        if (rng.depth >= MAX_DEPTH) {\\n            expand = 0;\\n        }\\n\\n        // Prune if this range is much worse than the best seen\\n        if (expand && srange > 0) {\\n            pthread_mutex_lock(&g_best_mtx);\\n            uint64_t best = g_best_sample_range;\\n            pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_best_mtx);\\n\\n            if (best > 0 && (double)srange > PRUNE_RANGE_FRACTION * (double)best) {\\n                expand = 0;\\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\\n                printf(\\\"  -> Pruned (range too large vs best)\\\\n\\\");\\n                fflush(stdout);\\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        if (expand) {\\n            WorkRange left, right;\\n            split_range(&rng, &left, &right);\\n            queue_push(q, left);\\n            queue_push(q, right);\\n        }\\n\\n        // Iterate through nonces in this range to update RMS and detect signals\\n        uint64_t stride = 1ULL << STRIDE_BITS;\\n        uint64_t end = rng.base_nonce + rng.count;\\n        for (uint64_t x = rng.base_nonce; x < end; x += stride) {\\n            if (!atomic_load(&g_running)) break;\\n            if (atomic_load(&g_signal_fired)) break;\\n\\n            if ((x & 0xFFF) == 0 && should_kill_bin()) {\\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\\n                printf(\\\"\\\\n!!! Bin killed by range growth (depth=%d)\\\\n\\\", rng.depth);\\n                fflush(stdout);\\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\\n                goto next_range;\\n            }\\n\\n            uint64_t val = hash_time_nonce(time_bin, x, hash);\\n\\n            // Print hash every PRINT_EVERY nonces\\n            if ((local_count % PRINT_EVERY) == 0) {\\n                bytes_to_hex(hash, 32, hex);\\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\\n                printf(\\\"HASH time_bin=%lu nonce=%lu -> %s\\\\n\\\", time_bin, x, hex);\\n                fflush(stdout);\\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\\n            }\\n            local_count++;\\n\\n            double wrms = 0.0;\\n            int is_new_low = 0;\\n            update_stats(val, &wrms, &is_new_low);\\n\\n            if (g_bin_samples == 64) {\\n                finalize_bin_start_range();\\n            }\\n\\n            if (is_new_low) {\\n                atomic_store(&g_signal_fired, 1);\\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\\n                printf(\\\"\\\\n\\u26a0 SIGNAL: new lowest windowed RMS! rms=%.0f (nonce=%lu, time_bin=%lu, depth=%d)\\\\n\\\",\\n                       wrms, x, time_bin, rng.depth);\\n                fflush(stdout);\\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\\n                break;\\n            }\\n\\n            if ((x & 0xFFFF) == 0) {\\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\\n                printf(\\\".\\\");\\n                fflush(stdout);\\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n    next_range:;\\n    }\\n    return NULL;\\n}\\n\\nint main(int argc, char *argv[]) {\\n    int num_threads = (argc > 1) ? atoi(argv[1]) : 4;\\n    if (num_threads < 1) num_threads = 1;\\n    if (num_threads > MAX_THREADS) num_threads = MAX_THREADS;\\n\\n    signal(SIGINT, handle_sigint);\\n\\n    WorkQueue q;\\n    init_queue(&q, 8192);\\n\\n    pthread_t threads[MAX_THREADS];\\n    for (int i=0;i<num_threads;i++) {\\n        pthread_create(&threads[i], NULL, worker_thread, &q);\\n    }\\n\\n    printf(\\\"SHA256 time-binned miner with lively binary-tree narrowing.\\\\n\\\");\\n    printf(\\\"Threads=%d, window=%d, initial_range=2^%d, max_depth=%d, bin=%ds. Ctrl+C to exit.\\\\n\\\\n\\\",\\n           num_threads, WINDOW_SIZE, INITIAL_RANGE_BITS, MAX_DEPTH, BIN_SECONDS);\\n\\n    uint64_t current_time_bin = 0;\\n    int bin_in_progress = 0;\\n\\n    while (atomic_load(&g_running)) {\\n        uint64_t now_bin = (uint64_t)time(NULL) / BIN_SECONDS;\\n\\n        if (now_bin != current_time_bin || atomic_load(&g_signal_fired)) {\\n            if (bin_in_progress) {\\n                end_bin();\\n                bin_in_progress = 0;\\n                // Clear queue to prune current tree\\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&q.mtx);\\n                q.head = q.tail = 0;\\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&q.mtx);\\n            }\\n            current_time_bin = now_bin;\\n            atomic_store(&g_signal_fired, 0);\\n\\n            uint64_t first_val = 0;\\n            if (should_start_bin(current_time_bin, &first_val)) {\\n                begin_bin(first_val);\\n                bin_in_progress = 1;\\n\\n                WorkRange root = {\\n                    .base_nonce = 0,\\n                    .count      = (1ULL << INITIAL_RANGE_BITS),\\n                    .depth      = 0,\\n                    .sample_min = 0,\\n                    .sample_max = 0,\\n                    .sample_rms = 0.0,\\n                    .sample_count = 0,\\n                    .parent_range = 0\\n                };\\n                queue_push(&q, root);\\n\\n                pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\\n                printf(\\\">>> Pushed root range for bin %lu (2^%d nonces)\\\\n\\\", current_time_bin, INITIAL_RANGE_BITS);\\n                fflush(stdout);\\n                pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        usleep(20000); // 20 ms poll \\u2192 more responsive\\n    }\\n\\n    if (bin_in_progress) end_bin();\\n\\n    atomic_store(&q.stop, 1);\\n    pthread_cond_broadcast(&q.cond);\\n\\n    for (int i=0;i<num_threads;i++) {\\n        pthread_join(threads[i], NULL);\\n    }\\n\\n    destroy_queue(&q);\\n\\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&g_print_mtx);\\n    printf(\\\"\\\\nStopped. Best bin probe range: %lu. Lowest windowed RMS: %.0f. Best sample range: %lu\\\\n\\\",\\n           g_best_bin_probe_range, g_min_window_rms, g_best_sample_range);\\n    fflush(stdout);\\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&g_print_mtx);\\n\\n    return 0;\\n}\\n\", \"filename\": \"/home/claude/hashproject_fixed.c\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:14:53.632044Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T08:14:53.632088Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01EEtfT6JxSSfnHAbgj5b5rs", "name": "create_file", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "File created successfully: /home/claude/hashproject_fixed.c"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "File created successfully: /home/claude/hashproject_fixed.c"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-26T08:14:53.773485Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T08:14:53.773485Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0dcc6-c537-769e-920e-247e58329a4a"}]},{"uuid": "7935e88c-7d27-4ae1-9a95-bcafdb469997", "name": "", "summary": "", "created_at": "2026-09-26T09:55:27.805767Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T09:55:27.805767Z", "account": {"uuid": "8d7882cd-3e98-47cc-a6e7-61494a1ee4ac"}, "chat_messages": []},{"uuid": "f2f62158-8cd0-4f90-a6ce-286ad345d34d", "name": "Solving the halting problem with code", "summary": "", "created_at": "2026-09-26T12:49:07.479404Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T13:20:29.988085Z", "account": {"uuid": "8d7882cd-3e98-47cc-a6e7-61494a1ee4ac"}, "chat_messages": [{"uuid": "01a0ddc3-2910-7b91-8bf3-74e0d850641d", "text": "make it actually solve a halting problem real code", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:49:09.307412Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:49:09.307412Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "make it actually solve a halting problem real code", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-26T12:49:09.307605Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T12:49:09.307605Z", "attachments": [{"file_name": "", "file_size": 10394, "file_type": "txt", "extracted_content": "import numpy as np\r\nimport argparse\r\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\r\n\r\n\r\n@dataclass\r\nclass Program:\r\n    name: str\r\n    halt_step: int | None\r\n\r\n    def instruction(self, step):\r\n        if self.halt_step is not None and step == self.halt_step:\r\n            return \"HALT\"\r\n        return \"CONTINUE\"\r\n\r\n\r\nclass PathOrderedFork:\r\n    def __init__(self, modes=4):\r\n        self.modes = modes\r\n        self.I = np.eye(modes, dtype=complex)\r\n\r\n        self.TL = self.rotation(0, 1, 0.37, 0.21)\r\n        self.TR = self.rotation(1, 2, 0.61, 1.17)\r\n        self.TH = self.halt_transform()\r\n\r\n    def rotation(self, a, b, theta, phase):\r\n        U = np.eye(self.modes, dtype=complex)\r\n\r\n        c = np.cos(theta)\r\n        s = np.sin(theta)\r\n\r\n        U[a, a] = c\r\n        U[b, b] = c\r\n\r\n        U[a, b] = -1j * np.exp(1j * phase) * s\r\n        U[b, a] = -1j * np.exp(-1j * phase) * s\r\n\r\n        return U\r\n\r\n    def halt_transform(self):\r\n        U = np.eye(self.modes, dtype=complex)\r\n\r\n        theta = 0.9\r\n        c = np.cos(theta)\r\n        s = np.sin(theta)\r\n\r\n        U[0, 0] = c\r\n        U[3, 3] = c\r\n\r\n        U[0, 3] = -1j * s\r\n        U[3, 0] = -1j * s\r\n\r\n        return U\r\n\r\n    def branch_transform(self, branch):\r\n        if branch == 0:\r\n            return self.TL\r\n        return self.TR\r\n\r\n    def branch_sequence(self, n):\r\n        return [\r\n            ((k * 1103515245 + 12345) >> 4) & 1\r\n            for k in range(n)\r\n        ]\r\n\r\n    def evolve(self, program, branches, steps):\r\n        state = np.zeros(self.modes, dtype=complex)\r\n        state[0] = 1.0\r\n\r\n        U = self.I.copy()\r\n\r\n        history = []\r\n\r\n        for step in range(steps):\r\n            branch = branches[step]\r\n\r\n            T = self.branch_transform(branch)\r\n\r\n            instruction = program.instruction(step)\r\n\r\n            if instruction == \"HALT\":\r\n                T = self.TH @ T\r\n\r\n            # Exact discrete path-ordered multiplication.\r\n            U = T @ U\r\n\r\n            state = T @ state\r\n\r\n            state_norm = np.linalg.norm(state)\r\n\r\n            if state_norm > 0:\r\n                state /= state_norm\r\n\r\n            trace = np.trace(U) / self.modes\r\n\r\n            halt_signal = abs(state[3]) ** 2\r\n\r\n            history.append({\r\n                \"step\": step,\r\n                \"branch\": branch,\r\n                \"instruction\": instruction,\r\n                \"state\": state.copy(),\r\n                \"U\": U.copy(),\r\n                \"halt_signal\": halt_signal,\r\n                \"trace\": trace,\r\n                \"trace_abs\": abs(trace),\r\n                \"trace_phase\": np.angle(trace)\r\n            })\r\n\r\n        return state, U, history\r\n\r\n    def observable(self, state, U):\r\n        W = np.trace(U) / self.modes\r\n\r\n        return {\r\n            \"halt_signal\": float(abs(state[3]) ** 2),\r\n            \"holonomy_magnitude\": float(abs(W)),\r\n            \"holonomy_phase\": float(np.angle(W)),\r\n            \"state_norm\": float(np.linalg.norm(state)),\r\n            \"trace_real\": float(W.real),\r\n            \"trace_imag\": float(W.imag)\r\n        }\r\n\r\n    def commutator(self):\r\n        return np.linalg.norm(\r\n            self.TR @ self.TL -\r\n            self.TL @ self.TR\r\n        )\r\n\r\n\r\ndef compare_at_time(fork, p1, p2, branches, steps):\r\n    state1, U1, history1 = fork.evolve(\r\n        p1,\r\n        branches,\r\n        steps\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    state2, U2, history2 = fork.evolve(\r\n        p2,\r\n        branches,\r\n        steps\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    obs1 = fork.observable(state1, U1)\r\n    obs2 = fork.observable(state2, U2)\r\n\r\n    state_difference = np.linalg.norm(\r\n        state1 - state2\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    matrix_difference = np.linalg.norm(\r\n        U1 - U2\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    observable_difference = {\r\n        key: abs(obs1[key] - obs2[key])\r\n        for key in obs1\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    return {\r\n        \"state1\": state1,\r\n        \"state2\": state2,\r\n        \"U1\": U1,\r\n        \"U2\": U2,\r\n        \"obs1\": obs1,\r\n        \"obs2\": obs2,\r\n        \"state_difference\": state_difference,\r\n        \"matrix_difference\": matrix_difference,\r\n        \"observable_difference\": observable_difference,\r\n        \"history1\": history1,\r\n        \"history2\": history2\r\n    }\r\n\r\n\r\ndef print_observable(name, obs):\r\n    print(name)\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"  HALT signal       = \"\r\n        f\"{obs['halt_signal']:.15f}\"\r\n    )\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"  |Tr(U)/d|        = \"\r\n        f\"{obs['holonomy_magnitude']:.15f}\"\r\n    )\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"  arg(Tr(U)/d)     = \"\r\n        f\"{obs['holonomy_phase']:.15f}\"\r\n    )\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"  Tr real          = \"\r\n        f\"{obs['trace_real']:.15f}\"\r\n    )\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"  Tr imaginary     = \"\r\n        f\"{obs['trace_imag']:.15f}\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n\r\ndef run_experiment(observation_steps=1000, late_halt=2000):\r\n    fork = PathOrderedFork()\r\n\r\n    branches = fork.branch_sequence(\r\n        max(observation_steps, late_halt + 1)\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    early = Program(\r\n        \"EARLY_HALT\",\r\n        observation_steps // 2\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    late = Program(\r\n        \"LATE_HALT\",\r\n        late_halt\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    never = Program(\r\n        \"NEVER_HALT\",\r\n        None\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\r\n    print(\"PATH-ORDERED FORK / FINITE-TIME HALTING TEST\")\r\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"Observation window : {observation_steps} steps\"\r\n    )\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"Late HALT          : step {late_halt}\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"1. NONCOMMUTATIVITY\")\r\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    comm = fork.commutator()\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"||T_R T_L - T_L T_R|| = \"\r\n        f\"{comm:.15f}\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"Ordering is encoded.\"\r\n        if comm > 1e-12\r\n        else\r\n        \"Ordering is not encoded.\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"2. EARLY HALT VS NEVER HALT\")\r\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    result = compare_at_time(\r\n        fork,\r\n        early,\r\n        never,\r\n        branches,\r\n        observation_steps\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print_observable(\r\n        \"EARLY HALT\",\r\n        result[\"obs1\"]\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n\r\n    print_observable(\r\n        \"NEVER HALT\",\r\n        result[\"obs2\"]\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"State difference = \"\r\n        f\"{result['state_difference']:.15e}\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"Matrix difference = \"\r\n        f\"{result['matrix_difference']:.15e}\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"These differ because EARLY_HALT has actually executed HALT.\")\r\n    \r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"3. LATE HALT VS NEVER HALT\")\r\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    result = compare_at_time(\r\n        fork,\r\n        late,\r\n        never,\r\n        branches,\r\n        observation_steps\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print_observable(\r\n        \"LATE HALT\",\r\n        result[\"obs1\"]\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n\r\n    print_observable(\r\n        \"NEVER HALT\",\r\n        result[\"obs2\"]\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"State difference = \"\r\n        f\"{result['state_difference']:.15e}\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"Matrix difference = \"\r\n        f\"{result['matrix_difference']:.15e}\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"OBSERVABLE DIFFERENCES\")\r\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    for key, value in result[\r\n        \"observable_difference\"\r\n    ].items():\r\n        print(\r\n            f\"{key:25s} = {value:.15e}\"\r\n        )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"4. MATHEMATICAL IDENTITY\")\r\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"For every step j <= observation time:\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"    T_j(LATE_HALT) = T_j(NEVER_HALT)\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"Therefore:\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"    U_N(LATE_HALT) = U_N(NEVER_HALT)\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"and consequently:\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"    W_N(LATE_HALT) = W_N(NEVER_HALT)\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"5. PATH-ORDERED PRODUCT\")\r\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"U_N = T_N T_(N-1) ... T_2 T_1\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"The multiplication order is retained.\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"But the finite product contains only the first N \"\r\n        \"instructions.\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"6. THE LIMIT THAT WOULD BE REQUIRED\")\r\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"A universal halting detector would require a finite-time \"\r\n        \"observable F satisfying:\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        \"F(P) = 1  if P eventually halts\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"F(P) = 0  if P never halts\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        \"The problem is that for every finite N one can construct:\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        \"P_late  = O1 O2 ... ON ... HALT\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"P_loop  = O1 O2 ... ON ... LOOP\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        \"Their complete physical histories through N are identical.\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        \"Therefore every observable calculated solely from that \"\r\n        \"finite history is identical.\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\r\n    print(\"CONCLUSION\")\r\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    if result[\"matrix_difference\"] < 1e-12:\r\n        print(\r\n            \"The simulation verifies finite-time indistinguishability.\"\r\n        )\r\n    else:\r\n        print(\r\n            \"Unexpected difference detected.\"\r\n        )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        \"The path-ordered construction successfully preserves \"\r\n        \"noncommutative computational ordering.\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"It does not, by itself, provide information about \"\r\n        \"instructions that have not yet occurred.\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"To obtain a genuine halting oracle, an additional physical \"\r\n        \"mechanism would have to encode the infinite future into a \"\r\n        \"finite measurable quantity.\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n\r\ndef main():\r\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\r\n        description=(\r\n            \"Simulate path-ordered computational evolution \"\r\n            \"and test finite-time halting distinguishability.\"\r\n        )\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    parser.add_argument(\r\n        \"--steps\",\r\n        type=int,\r\n        default=1000\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    parser.add_argument(\r\n        \"--late-halt\",\r\n        type=int,\r\n        default=2000\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    args = parser.parse_args()\r\n\r\n    if args.steps <= 0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\r\n            \"--steps must be greater than zero\"\r\n        )\r\n\r\n    if args.late_halt <= args.steps:\r\n        raise ValueError(\r\n            \"--late-halt must be greater than --steps\"\r\n        )\r\n\r\n    run_experiment(\r\n        observation_steps=args.steps,\r\n        late_halt=args.late_halt\r\n    )\r\n\r\n\r\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\r\n    main()\r\n"}], "files": [{"file_uuid": "0e7d194d-4862-4658-8639-5d5f93c57c99", "file_name": ""}], "parent_message_uuid": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000000"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddc3-2911-783e-a064-25c0cfba052f", "text": "The short answer: no code \u2014 quantum-inspired, classical, or otherwise \u2014 can solve the halting problem in general. That's not a limitation of this particular script, it's a proven mathematical fact (Turing, 1936). The code you shared is actually a nice *demonstration* of why: it shows that any observable computed from a finite execution history can't distinguish \"halts later\" from \"never halts,\" because up to step N their histories are provably identical. That's the whole point of the script's conclusion.\n\nSo I can't make it \"actually solve\" halting in the general sense \u2014 but there are a few real, non-fake things I can build for you instead, depending on what you're actually trying to do:\n\n1. **A bounded halting checker** \u2014 decides \"does this program halt within N steps?\" by simulation. This *is* decidable and is what real tools (fuzzers, model checkers) actually do.\n2. **A halting decider for a restricted machine class** \u2014 e.g., finite-state machines, or programs without unbounded memory: for these, halting *is* decidable (cycle detection in a finite state space), and I can write one that's genuinely correct, not just illustrative.\n3. **A cycle/loop detector using state-hashing** \u2014 run the program, hash the full machine state after each step, and if a state repeats, you've proven it will never halt (since it's now in a deterministic loop). This is correct and useful for real finite-state or bounded-memory programs.\n4. **Keep the quantum-toy framing** but make the \"detector\" honest: have it correctly report \"undetermined\" beyond N steps rather than implying it knows the answer.\n\nWhich of these is closest to what you're actually trying to build? If you tell me more about the real programs you want to check (arbitrary Turing-complete code, or something more restricted like a finite-state protocol or bytecode with bounded registers), I can write a genuinely correct halting decider for that class rather than a simulation that just looks like one.", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:49:09.159569Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:49:13.479234Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Explaining why the halting problem cannot be solved."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:49:13.479276Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:49:21.155595Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "The short answer: no code \u2014 quantum-inspired, classical, or otherwise \u2014 can solve the halting problem in general. That's not a limitation of this particular script, it's a proven mathematical fact (Turing, 1936). The code you shared is actually a nice *demonstration* of why: it shows that any observable computed from a finite execution history can't distinguish \"halts later\" from \"never halts,\" because up to step N their histories are provably identical. That's the whole point of the script's conclusion.\n\nSo I can't make it \"actually solve\" halting in the general sense \u2014 but there are a few real, non-fake things I can build for you instead, depending on what you're actually trying to do:\n\n1. **A bounded halting checker** \u2014 decides \"does this program halt within N steps?\" by simulation. This *is* decidable and is what real tools (fuzzers, model checkers) actually do.\n2. **A halting decider for a restricted machine class** \u2014 e.g., finite-state machines, or programs without unbounded memory: for these, halting *is* decidable (cycle detection in a finite state space), and I can write one that's genuinely correct, not just illustrative.\n3. **A cycle/loop detector using state-hashing** \u2014 run the program, hash the full machine state after each step, and if a state repeats, you've proven it will never halt (since it's now in a deterministic loop). This is correct and useful for real finite-state or bounded-memory programs.\n4. **Keep the quantum-toy framing** but make the \"detector\" honest: have it correctly report \"undetermined\" beyond N steps rather than implying it knows the answer.\n\nWhich of these is closest to what you're actually trying to build? If you tell me more about the real programs you want to check (arbitrary Turing-complete code, or something more restricted like a finite-state protocol or bytecode with bounded registers), I can write a genuinely correct halting decider for that class rather than a simulation that just looks like one.", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-26T12:49:21.172758Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T12:49:21.172758Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddc3-2910-7b91-8bf3-74e0d850641d"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddc4-3bea-7d55-aa59-3f5934ba13c2", "text": "dont change the math, just make it solve a toy halting problem", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:50:19.931249Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:50:19.931249Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "dont change the math, just make it solve a toy halting problem", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-26T12:50:19.931459Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T12:50:19.931459Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddc3-2911-783e-a064-25c0cfba052f"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddc4-3bea-73a5-9808-aa0c2dc3daf3", "text": "You're right to draw that line \u2014 and there's a real, non-trivial toy version worth building: halting **is** decidable for machines with a *finite* configuration space (bounded registers, bounded tape, finite states). The trick isn't cleverness, it's just: enumerate configurations, and if you ever revisit one you've already seen, you're in a loop and can *never* halt from there \u2014 proven, not guessed. If you hit HALT first, it halts. Since the state space is finite, one of these two things must happen within a computable bound.\n\nI left your quantum simulation completely untouched and appended a genuine decider for this toy class.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nIt runs correctly and gets all three cases right. Your original quantum code is byte-for-byte unchanged (`--skip-toy` runs the exact original experiment, `--skip-quantum` runs only the new part, default runs both).\n\nWhat I added: a `ToyMachine` \u2014 a register machine with bounded registers (INC/DEC/JZ/JMP/HALT) \u2014 plus `decide_halting()`, which is a real decider, not a simulation with a cutoff. It works by tracking every configuration visited; since registers are bounded, the configuration space is finite, so either HALT gets hit or some configuration repeats, which *proves* an infinite loop (determinism means a repeated state means a repeated cycle forever). No guessing, no timeout, no heuristic \u2014 one of those two outcomes is mathematically guaranteed within `state_space` steps.\n\nTested it on three programs: one that halts, one obvious infinite loop, and one \"disguised\" loop that oscillates between register values \u2014 all three decided correctly with proof (cycle location and length shown for the non-halting ones).\n\nThe comment block at the end is honest about the boundary: this works *because* registers are bounded (finite state space). Make registers unbounded and the exact same `seen`-set trick stops working, since it can grow forever without repeating \u2014 that's the real halting problem again, and it's what your quantum section is illustrating from a different angle.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nFile's above \u2014 run it with `--skip-quantum` for just the toy demo, or no flags for both.", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:50:19.924281Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:50:32.294847Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Building a genuine toy halting problem solver alongside the simulation."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:50:32.294876Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:50:34.795926Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "You're right to draw that line \u2014 and there's a real, non-trivial toy version worth building: halting **is** decidable for machines with a *finite* configuration space (bounded registers, bounded tape, finite states). The trick isn't cleverness, it's just: enumerate configurations, and if you ever revisit one you've already seen, you're in a loop and can *never* halt from there \u2014 proven, not guessed. If you hit HALT first, it halts. Since the state space is finite, one of these two things must happen within a computable bound.\n\nI left your quantum simulation completely untouched and appended a genuine decider for this toy class.", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:50:34.795954Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:50:35.220068Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_018fDUCYjn357scXaLsvgv8p", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "mkdir -p /home/claude", "description": "prep workspace"}, "message": "prep workspace", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"mkdir -p /home/claude\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:50:36.277612Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:50:36.277652Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_018fDUCYjn357scXaLsvgv8p", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:50:37.708544Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:51:34.232295Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01VnUjJa7ZRHfRZaMite2Kas", "name": "create_file", "input": {"description": "Add a genuine (decidable) toy halting solver for bounded-register machines, appended after the untouched quantum simulation code", "path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py", "file_text": "import numpy as np\nimport argparse\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Program:\n    name: str\n    halt_step: int | None\n\n    def instruction(self, step):\n        if self.halt_step is not None and step == self.halt_step:\n            return \"HALT\"\n        return \"CONTINUE\"\n\n\nclass PathOrderedFork:\n    def __init__(self, modes=4):\n        self.modes = modes\n        self.I = np.eye(modes, dtype=complex)\n\n        self.TL = self.rotation(0, 1, 0.37, 0.21)\n        self.TR = self.rotation(1, 2, 0.61, 1.17)\n        self.TH = self.halt_transform()\n\n    def rotation(self, a, b, theta, phase):\n        U = np.eye(self.modes, dtype=complex)\n\n        c = np.cos(theta)\n        s = np.sin(theta)\n\n        U[a, a] = c\n        U[b, b] = c\n\n        U[a, b] = -1j * np.exp(1j * phase) * s\n        U[b, a] = -1j * np.exp(-1j * phase) * s\n\n        return U\n\n    def halt_transform(self):\n        U = np.eye(self.modes, dtype=complex)\n\n        theta = 0.9\n        c = np.cos(theta)\n        s = np.sin(theta)\n\n        U[0, 0] = c\n        U[3, 3] = c\n\n        U[0, 3] = -1j * s\n        U[3, 0] = -1j * s\n\n        return U\n\n    def branch_transform(self, branch):\n        if branch == 0:\n            return self.TL\n        return self.TR\n\n    def branch_sequence(self, n):\n        return [\n            ((k * 1103515245 + 12345) >> 4) & 1\n            for k in range(n)\n        ]\n\n    def evolve(self, program, branches, steps):\n        state = np.zeros(self.modes, dtype=complex)\n        state[0] = 1.0\n\n        U = self.I.copy()\n\n        history = []\n\n        for step in range(steps):\n            branch = branches[step]\n\n            T = self.branch_transform(branch)\n\n            instruction = program.instruction(step)\n\n            if instruction == \"HALT\":\n                T = self.TH @ T\n\n            # Exact discrete path-ordered multiplication.\n            U = T @ U\n\n            state = T @ state\n\n            state_norm = np.linalg.norm(state)\n\n            if state_norm > 0:\n                state /= state_norm\n\n            trace = np.trace(U) / self.modes\n\n            halt_signal = abs(state[3]) ** 2\n\n            history.append({\n                \"step\": step,\n                \"branch\": branch,\n                \"instruction\": instruction,\n                \"state\": state.copy(),\n                \"U\": U.copy(),\n                \"halt_signal\": halt_signal,\n                \"trace\": trace,\n                \"trace_abs\": abs(trace),\n                \"trace_phase\": np.angle(trace)\n            })\n\n        return state, U, history\n\n    def observable(self, state, U):\n        W = np.trace(U) / self.modes\n\n        return {\n            \"halt_signal\": float(abs(state[3]) ** 2),\n            \"holonomy_magnitude\": float(abs(W)),\n            \"holonomy_phase\": float(np.angle(W)),\n            \"state_norm\": float(np.linalg.norm(state)),\n            \"trace_real\": float(W.real),\n            \"trace_imag\": float(W.imag)\n        }\n\n    def commutator(self):\n        return np.linalg.norm(\n            self.TR @ self.TL -\n            self.TL @ self.TR\n        )\n\n\ndef compare_at_time(fork, p1, p2, branches, steps):\n    state1, U1, history1 = fork.evolve(\n        p1,\n        branches,\n        steps\n    )\n\n    state2, U2, history2 = fork.evolve(\n        p2,\n        branches,\n        steps\n    )\n\n    obs1 = fork.observable(state1, U1)\n    obs2 = fork.observable(state2, U2)\n\n    state_difference = np.linalg.norm(\n        state1 - state2\n    )\n\n    matrix_difference = np.linalg.norm(\n        U1 - U2\n    )\n\n    observable_difference = {\n        key: abs(obs1[key] - obs2[key])\n        for key in obs1\n    }\n\n    return {\n        \"state1\": state1,\n        \"state2\": state2,\n        \"U1\": U1,\n        \"U2\": U2,\n        \"obs1\": obs1,\n        \"obs2\": obs2,\n        \"state_difference\": state_difference,\n        \"matrix_difference\": matrix_difference,\n        \"observable_difference\": observable_difference,\n        \"history1\": history1,\n        \"history2\": history2\n    }\n\n\ndef print_observable(name, obs):\n    print(name)\n    print(\n        f\"  HALT signal       = \"\n        f\"{obs['halt_signal']:.15f}\"\n    )\n    print(\n        f\"  |Tr(U)/d|        = \"\n        f\"{obs['holonomy_magnitude']:.15f}\"\n    )\n    print(\n        f\"  arg(Tr(U)/d)     = \"\n        f\"{obs['holonomy_phase']:.15f}\"\n    )\n    print(\n        f\"  Tr real          = \"\n        f\"{obs['trace_real']:.15f}\"\n    )\n    print(\n        f\"  Tr imaginary     = \"\n        f\"{obs['trace_imag']:.15f}\"\n    )\n\n\ndef run_experiment(observation_steps=1000, late_halt=2000):\n    fork = PathOrderedFork()\n\n    branches = fork.branch_sequence(\n        max(observation_steps, late_halt + 1)\n    )\n\n    early = Program(\n        \"EARLY_HALT\",\n        observation_steps // 2\n    )\n\n    late = Program(\n        \"LATE_HALT\",\n        late_halt\n    )\n\n    never = Program(\n        \"NEVER_HALT\",\n        None\n    )\n\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\n    print(\"PATH-ORDERED FORK / FINITE-TIME HALTING TEST\")\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\n\n    print()\n    print(\n        f\"Observation window : {observation_steps} steps\"\n    )\n    print(\n        f\"Late HALT          : step {late_halt}\"\n    )\n\n    print()\n    print(\"1. NONCOMMUTATIVITY\")\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\n\n    comm = fork.commutator()\n\n    print(\n        \"||T_R T_L - T_L T_R|| = \"\n        f\"{comm:.15f}\"\n    )\n\n    print(\n        \"Ordering is encoded.\"\n        if comm > 1e-12\n        else\n        \"Ordering is not encoded.\"\n    )\n\n    print()\n    print(\"2. EARLY HALT VS NEVER HALT\")\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\n\n    result = compare_at_time(\n        fork,\n        early,\n        never,\n        branches,\n        observation_steps\n    )\n\n    print_observable(\n        \"EARLY HALT\",\n        result[\"obs1\"]\n    )\n\n    print()\n\n    print_observable(\n        \"NEVER HALT\",\n        result[\"obs2\"]\n    )\n\n    print()\n    print(\n        f\"State difference = \"\n        f\"{result['state_difference']:.15e}\"\n    )\n\n    print(\n        f\"Matrix difference = \"\n        f\"{result['matrix_difference']:.15e}\"\n    )\n\n    print()\n    print(\"These differ because EARLY_HALT has actually executed HALT.\")\n\n    print()\n    print(\"3. LATE HALT VS NEVER HALT\")\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\n\n    result = compare_at_time(\n        fork,\n        late,\n        never,\n        branches,\n        observation_steps\n    )\n\n    print_observable(\n        \"LATE HALT\",\n        result[\"obs1\"]\n    )\n\n    print()\n\n    print_observable(\n        \"NEVER HALT\",\n        result[\"obs2\"]\n    )\n\n    print()\n    print(\n        f\"State difference = \"\n        f\"{result['state_difference']:.15e}\"\n    )\n\n    print(\n        f\"Matrix difference = \"\n        f\"{result['matrix_difference']:.15e}\"\n    )\n\n    print()\n    print(\"OBSERVABLE DIFFERENCES\")\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\n\n    for key, value in result[\n        \"observable_difference\"\n    ].items():\n        print(\n            f\"{key:25s} = {value:.15e}\"\n        )\n\n    print()\n    print(\"4. MATHEMATICAL IDENTITY\")\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\n\n    print(\n        \"For every step j <= observation time:\"\n    )\n\n    print(\n        \"    T_j(LATE_HALT) = T_j(NEVER_HALT)\"\n    )\n\n    print(\n        \"Therefore:\"\n    )\n\n    print(\n        \"    U_N(LATE_HALT) = U_N(NEVER_HALT)\"\n    )\n\n    print(\n        \"and consequently:\"\n    )\n\n    print(\n        \"    W_N(LATE_HALT) = W_N(NEVER_HALT)\"\n    )\n\n    print()\n    print(\"5. PATH-ORDERED PRODUCT\")\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\n\n    print(\n        \"U_N = T_N T_(N-1) ... T_2 T_1\"\n    )\n\n    print(\n        \"The multiplication order is retained.\"\n    )\n\n    print(\n        \"But the finite product contains only the first N \"\n        \"instructions.\"\n    )\n\n    print()\n    print(\"6. THE LIMIT THAT WOULD BE REQUIRED\")\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\n\n    print(\n        \"A universal halting detector would require a finite-time \"\n        \"observable F satisfying:\"\n    )\n\n    print()\n    print(\n        \"F(P) = 1  if P eventually halts\"\n    )\n\n    print(\n        \"F(P) = 0  if P never halts\"\n    )\n\n    print()\n    print(\n        \"The problem is that for every finite N one can construct:\"\n    )\n\n    print()\n    print(\n        \"P_late  = O1 O2 ... ON ... HALT\"\n    )\n\n    print(\n        \"P_loop  = O1 O2 ... ON ... LOOP\"\n    )\n\n    print()\n    print(\n        \"Their complete physical histories through N are identical.\"\n    )\n\n    print()\n    print(\n        \"Therefore every observable calculated solely from that \"\n        \"finite history is identical.\"\n    )\n\n    print()\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\n    print(\"CONCLUSION\")\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\n\n    if result[\"matrix_difference\"] < 1e-12:\n        print(\n            \"The simulation verifies finite-time indistinguishability.\"\n        )\n    else:\n        print(\n            \"Unexpected difference detected.\"\n        )\n\n    print()\n    print(\n        \"The path-ordered construction successfully preserves \"\n        \"noncommutative computational ordering.\"\n    )\n\n    print(\n        \"It does not, by itself, provide information about \"\n        \"instructions that have not yet occurred.\"\n    )\n\n    print(\n        \"To obtain a genuine halting oracle, an additional physical \"\n        \"mechanism would have to encode the infinite future into a \"\n        \"finite measurable quantity.\"\n    )\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# TOY HALTING PROBLEM -- ACTUALLY DECIDABLE, ACTUALLY SOLVED\n# =============================================================================\n#\n# The section above demonstrates *why* halting is undecidable in general:\n# a finite observation window cannot distinguish \"halts later\" from \"never\n# halts,\" because both programs can have identical histories up to that\n# window, for any window size you pick.\n#\n# That impossibility relies on one thing: an unbounded configuration space\n# (an infinite-tape, infinite-future machine). If instead the configuration\n# space is FINITE -- bounded registers, bounded tape, finitely many\n# instructions -- halting becomes decidable, with a real proof, not a\n# simulation trick:\n#\n#   1. Every configuration is a tuple (program_counter, register_values).\n#   2. The machine is deterministic, so each configuration has exactly one\n#      successor.\n#   3. There are only finitely many possible configurations.\n#   4. Run the machine and record every configuration visited.\n#      - If HALT is reached: the machine halts. Proven.\n#      - If a configuration repeats: the machine has entered a deterministic\n#        cycle, and since it is deterministic, it will repeat that cycle\n#        forever. It provably never halts. Proven.\n#   5. Since there are finitely many configurations, one of these two\n#      outcomes MUST occur within (number of configurations) steps. There is\n#      no third case and no need to guess or bound the search heuristically.\n#\n# This is the same logic used by real, sound tools such as model checkers\n# and finite-state verifiers. It works precisely because the state space is\n# finite -- the one assumption that fails for general Turing machines.\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Instruction:\n    op: str          # \"INC\", \"DEC\", \"JZ\", \"JMP\", \"HALT\"\n    reg: int = 0      # register index, for INC/DEC/JZ\n    target: int = 0   # jump target, for JZ/JMP\n\n\nclass ToyMachine:\n    \"\"\"\n    A tiny register machine:\n      - a fixed number of registers, each clamped to [0, max_value]\n      - INC r        : reg[r] += 1 (clamped)\n      - DEC r        : reg[r] -= 1 (clamped, floor 0)\n      - JZ  r target : jump to target if reg[r] == 0, else fall through\n      - JMP target   : unconditional jump\n      - HALT         : stop\n\n    Because every register is bounded and there are finitely many\n    instructions, the total configuration space is finite:\n        num_instructions * (max_value + 1) ** num_registers\n    which makes halting decidable for this machine, exactly.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, program, num_registers, max_value):\n        self.program = program\n        self.num_registers = num_registers\n        self.max_value = max_value\n\n    def initial_config(self):\n        return (0, tuple([0] * self.num_registers))\n\n    def step(self, config):\n        pc, regs = config\n        instr = self.program[pc]\n        regs = list(regs)\n\n        if instr.op == \"INC\":\n            regs[instr.reg] = min(self.max_value, regs[instr.reg] + 1)\n            next_pc = pc + 1\n        elif instr.op == \"DEC\":\n            regs[instr.reg] = max(0, regs[instr.reg] - 1)\n            next_pc = pc + 1\n        elif instr.op == \"JZ\":\n            next_pc = instr.target if regs[instr.reg] == 0 else pc + 1\n        elif instr.op == \"JMP\":\n            next_pc = instr.target\n        elif instr.op == \"HALT\":\n            next_pc = pc  # unreachable; step() isn't called after HALT\n        else:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Unknown opcode: {instr.op}\")\n\n        return (next_pc, tuple(regs))\n\n    def total_configurations(self):\n        return len(self.program) * (self.max_value + 1) ** self.num_registers\n\n\ndef decide_halting(machine, verbose=False):\n    \"\"\"\n    A genuine, correct halting decider for ToyMachine programs.\n\n    Returns a dict with:\n        halts        : True, False (never guessed -- always proven)\n        steps        : number of steps taken to halt (if halts)\n        cycle_start  : step index where the repeated configuration first\n                        occurred (if it loops)\n        cycle_length : length of the proven infinite cycle (if it loops)\n        configs_seen : how many configurations were explored\n        state_space  : total size of the machine's configuration space\n    \"\"\"\n    config = machine.initial_config()\n    seen = {config: 0}\n    trace = [config]\n\n    step_count = 0\n    limit = machine.total_configurations()\n\n    while True:\n        pc, regs = config\n\n        if machine.program[pc].op == \"HALT\":\n            return {\n                \"halts\": True,\n                \"steps\": step_count,\n                \"configs_seen\": len(seen),\n                \"state_space\": limit,\n                \"trace\": trace if verbose else None,\n            }\n\n        config = machine.step(config)\n        step_count += 1\n\n        if config in seen:\n            cycle_start = seen[config]\n            return {\n                \"halts\": False,\n                \"cycle_start\": cycle_start,\n                \"cycle_length\": step_count - cycle_start,\n                \"configs_seen\": len(seen),\n                \"state_space\": limit,\n                \"trace\": trace if verbose else None,\n            }\n\n        seen[config] = step_count\n        trace.append(config)\n\n        # Safety net only -- mathematically this can never trigger,\n        # since the state space is finite and we check every visited\n        # configuration above. Kept only to fail loudly on a bug.\n        assert step_count <= limit + 1, \"unreachable: proof above guarantees termination\"\n\n\ndef print_decision(name, machine, result):\n    print(name)\n    print(f\"  Registers x range   : {machine.num_registers} x [0, {machine.max_value}]\")\n    print(f\"  Total state space   : {result['state_space']} configurations\")\n    print(f\"  Configurations seen : {result['configs_seen']}\")\n\n    if result[\"halts\"]:\n        print(f\"  DECISION            : HALTS  (in {result['steps']} steps)\")\n    else:\n        print(\n            f\"  DECISION            : NEVER HALTS  \"\n            f\"(proven -- enters a cycle of length {result['cycle_length']} \"\n            f\"starting at step {result['cycle_start']})\"\n        )\n\n\ndef run_toy_halting_demo():\n    print()\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\n    print(\"TOY HALTING PROBLEM -- FINITE-STATE, ACTUALLY DECIDABLE\")\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\n    print()\n    print(\n        \"Unlike a general Turing machine, this machine has boundedly many\\n\"\n        \"registers over a bounded range, so its configuration space is\\n\"\n        \"finite. Halting is therefore genuinely decidable: run it, and\\n\"\n        \"either HALT is reached or a configuration repeats (a proven\\n\"\n        \"infinite loop). One of the two always happens.\"\n    )\n\n    # Program A: counts a register down from 3 to 0, then halts.\n    #   0: JZ  r0 -> 3   (if r0 == 0, jump to HALT)\n    #   1: DEC r0\n    #   2: JMP -> 0\n    #   3: HALT\n    halting_program = [\n        Instruction(\"JZ\", reg=0, target=3),\n        Instruction(\"DEC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"JMP\", target=0),\n        Instruction(\"HALT\"),\n    ]\n\n    # To make it start at 3 instead of 0, we bump r0 up first:\n    #   0: INC r0\n    #   1: INC r0\n    #   2: INC r0\n    #   3: JZ  r0 -> 6\n    #   4: DEC r0\n    #   5: JMP -> 3\n    #   6: HALT\n    halting_program = [\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"JZ\", reg=0, target=6),\n        Instruction(\"DEC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"JMP\", target=3),\n        Instruction(\"HALT\"),\n    ]\n\n    # Program B: an unconditional infinite loop. Never reaches HALT.\n    #   0: INC r0\n    #   1: JMP -> 0\n    looping_program = [\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"JMP\", target=0),\n    ]\n\n    # Program C: a \"disguised\" infinite loop -- it increments and decrements\n    # in a cycle that never zeroes out at the right place, oscillating\n    # forever between two register states without ever reaching HALT.\n    #   0: INC r0\n    #   1: INC r1\n    #   2: DEC r0\n    #   3: JZ  r0 -> 0     (r0 is back to 0 here every time -> loops forever)\n    oscillating_program = [\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=1),\n        Instruction(\"DEC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"JZ\", reg=0, target=0),\n    ]\n\n    machine_a = ToyMachine(halting_program, num_registers=1, max_value=7)\n    machine_b = ToyMachine(looping_program, num_registers=1, max_value=7)\n    machine_c = ToyMachine(oscillating_program, num_registers=2, max_value=7)\n\n    print()\n    print_decision(\"PROGRAM A (counts down then halts)\", machine_a, decide_halting(machine_a))\n    print()\n    print_decision(\"PROGRAM B (unconditional infinite loop)\", machine_b, decide_halting(machine_b))\n    print()\n    print_decision(\"PROGRAM C (oscillates forever, never hits HALT)\", machine_c, decide_halting(machine_c))\n\n    print()\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\n    print(\n        \"Each decision above is a proof, not a guess: either an explicit\\n\"\n        \"HALT instruction was reached, or a configuration was shown to\\n\"\n        \"repeat, which -- for a deterministic machine -- guarantees it\\n\"\n        \"repeats forever. This does not contradict Turing's result: it\\n\"\n        \"works only because the register values here are bounded, so the\\n\"\n        \"state space is finite. Remove that bound (unbounded registers,\\n\"\n        \"i.e. a real counter machine) and this exact technique breaks,\\n\"\n        \"because 'seen' can then grow forever without ever repeating --\\n\"\n        \"which is the general halting problem again.\"\n    )\n\n\ndef main():\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=(\n            \"Simulate path-ordered computational evolution, test \"\n            \"finite-time halting distinguishability, and separately run \"\n            \"a genuine decidable toy halting-problem demo.\"\n        )\n    )\n\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--steps\",\n        type=int,\n        default=1000\n    )\n\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--late-halt\",\n        type=int,\n        default=2000\n    )\n\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--skip-quantum\",\n        action=\"store_true\",\n        help=\"Skip the quantum path-ordered simulation and only run the toy halting demo.\"\n    )\n\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--skip-toy\",\n        action=\"store_true\",\n        help=\"Skip the toy halting-problem demo and only run the quantum simulation.\"\n    )\n\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n\n    if args.steps <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\n            \"--steps must be greater than zero\"\n        )\n\n    if args.late_halt <= args.steps:\n        raise ValueError(\n            \"--late-halt must be greater than --steps\"\n        )\n\n    if not args.skip_quantum:\n        run_experiment(\n            observation_steps=args.steps,\n            late_halt=args.late_halt\n        )\n\n    if not args.skip_toy:\n        run_toy_halting_demo()\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n"}, "message": "Add a genuine (decidable) toy halting solver for bounded-register machines, appended after the untouched quantum simulation code", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"python\", \"code\": \"import numpy as np\\nimport argparse\\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\\n\\n\\n@dataclass\\nclass Program:\\n    name: str\\n    halt_step: int | None\\n\\n    def instruction(self, step):\\n        if self.halt_step is not None and step == self.halt_step:\\n            return \\\"HALT\\\"\\n        return \\\"CONTINUE\\\"\\n\\n\\nclass PathOrderedFork:\\n    def __init__(self, modes=4):\\n        self.modes = modes\\n        self.I = np.eye(modes, dtype=complex)\\n\\n        self.TL = self.rotation(0, 1, 0.37, 0.21)\\n        self.TR = self.rotation(1, 2, 0.61, 1.17)\\n        self.TH = self.halt_transform()\\n\\n    def rotation(self, a, b, theta, phase):\\n        U = np.eye(self.modes, dtype=complex)\\n\\n        c = np.cos(theta)\\n        s = np.sin(theta)\\n\\n        U[a, a] = c\\n        U[b, b] = c\\n\\n        U[a, b] = -1j * np.exp(1j * phase) * s\\n        U[b, a] = -1j * np.exp(-1j * phase) * s\\n\\n        return U\\n\\n    def halt_transform(self):\\n        U = np.eye(self.modes, dtype=complex)\\n\\n        theta = 0.9\\n        c = np.cos(theta)\\n        s = np.sin(theta)\\n\\n        U[0, 0] = c\\n        U[3, 3] = c\\n\\n        U[0, 3] = -1j * s\\n        U[3, 0] = -1j * s\\n\\n        return U\\n\\n    def branch_transform(self, branch):\\n        if branch == 0:\\n            return self.TL\\n        return self.TR\\n\\n    def branch_sequence(self, n):\\n        return [\\n            ((k * 1103515245 + 12345) >> 4) & 1\\n            for k in range(n)\\n        ]\\n\\n    def evolve(self, program, branches, steps):\\n        state = np.zeros(self.modes, dtype=complex)\\n        state[0] = 1.0\\n\\n        U = self.I.copy()\\n\\n        history = []\\n\\n        for step in range(steps):\\n            branch = branches[step]\\n\\n            T = self.branch_transform(branch)\\n\\n            instruction = program.instruction(step)\\n\\n            if instruction == \\\"HALT\\\":\\n                T = self.TH @ T\\n\\n            # Exact discrete path-ordered multiplication.\\n            U = T @ U\\n\\n            state = T @ state\\n\\n            state_norm = np.linalg.norm(state)\\n\\n            if state_norm > 0:\\n                state /= state_norm\\n\\n            trace = np.trace(U) / self.modes\\n\\n            halt_signal = abs(state[3]) ** 2\\n\\n            history.append({\\n                \\\"step\\\": step,\\n                \\\"branch\\\": branch,\\n                \\\"instruction\\\": instruction,\\n                \\\"state\\\": state.copy(),\\n                \\\"U\\\": U.copy(),\\n                \\\"halt_signal\\\": halt_signal,\\n                \\\"trace\\\": trace,\\n                \\\"trace_abs\\\": abs(trace),\\n                \\\"trace_phase\\\": np.angle(trace)\\n            })\\n\\n        return state, U, history\\n\\n    def observable(self, state, U):\\n        W = np.trace(U) / self.modes\\n\\n        return {\\n            \\\"halt_signal\\\": float(abs(state[3]) ** 2),\\n            \\\"holonomy_magnitude\\\": float(abs(W)),\\n            \\\"holonomy_phase\\\": float(np.angle(W)),\\n            \\\"state_norm\\\": float(np.linalg.norm(state)),\\n            \\\"trace_real\\\": float(W.real),\\n            \\\"trace_imag\\\": float(W.imag)\\n        }\\n\\n    def commutator(self):\\n        return np.linalg.norm(\\n            self.TR @ self.TL -\\n            self.TL @ self.TR\\n        )\\n\\n\\ndef compare_at_time(fork, p1, p2, branches, steps):\\n    state1, U1, history1 = fork.evolve(\\n        p1,\\n        branches,\\n        steps\\n    )\\n\\n    state2, U2, history2 = fork.evolve(\\n        p2,\\n        branches,\\n        steps\\n    )\\n\\n    obs1 = fork.observable(state1, U1)\\n    obs2 = fork.observable(state2, U2)\\n\\n    state_difference = np.linalg.norm(\\n        state1 - state2\\n    )\\n\\n    matrix_difference = np.linalg.norm(\\n        U1 - U2\\n    )\\n\\n    observable_difference = {\\n        key: abs(obs1[key] - obs2[key])\\n        for key in obs1\\n    }\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"state1\\\": state1,\\n        \\\"state2\\\": state2,\\n        \\\"U1\\\": U1,\\n        \\\"U2\\\": U2,\\n        \\\"obs1\\\": obs1,\\n        \\\"obs2\\\": obs2,\\n        \\\"state_difference\\\": state_difference,\\n        \\\"matrix_difference\\\": matrix_difference,\\n        \\\"observable_difference\\\": observable_difference,\\n        \\\"history1\\\": history1,\\n        \\\"history2\\\": history2\\n    }\\n\\n\\ndef print_observable(name, obs):\\n    print(name)\\n    print(\\n        f\\\"  HALT signal       = \\\"\\n        f\\\"{obs['halt_signal']:.15f}\\\"\\n    )\\n    print(\\n        f\\\"  |Tr(U)/d|        = \\\"\\n        f\\\"{obs['holonomy_magnitude']:.15f}\\\"\\n    )\\n    print(\\n        f\\\"  arg(Tr(U)/d)     = \\\"\\n        f\\\"{obs['holonomy_phase']:.15f}\\\"\\n    )\\n    print(\\n        f\\\"  Tr real          = \\\"\\n        f\\\"{obs['trace_real']:.15f}\\\"\\n    )\\n    print(\\n        f\\\"  Tr imaginary     = \\\"\\n        f\\\"{obs['trace_imag']:.15f}\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n\\ndef run_experiment(observation_steps=1000, late_halt=2000):\\n    fork = PathOrderedFork()\\n\\n    branches = fork.branch_sequence(\\n        max(observation_steps, late_halt + 1)\\n    )\\n\\n    early = Program(\\n        \\\"EARLY_HALT\\\",\\n        observation_steps // 2\\n    )\\n\\n    late = Program(\\n        \\\"LATE_HALT\\\",\\n        late_halt\\n    )\\n\\n    never = Program(\\n        \\\"NEVER_HALT\\\",\\n        None\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\\"=\\\" * 78)\\n    print(\\\"PATH-ORDERED FORK / FINITE-TIME HALTING TEST\\\")\\n    print(\\\"=\\\" * 78)\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\n        f\\\"Observation window : {observation_steps} steps\\\"\\n    )\\n    print(\\n        f\\\"Late HALT          : step {late_halt}\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\\"1. NONCOMMUTATIVITY\\\")\\n    print(\\\"-\\\" * 78)\\n\\n    comm = fork.commutator()\\n\\n    print(\\n        \\\"||T_R T_L - T_L T_R|| = \\\"\\n        f\\\"{comm:.15f}\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\n        \\\"Ordering is encoded.\\\"\\n        if comm > 1e-12\\n        else\\n        \\\"Ordering is not encoded.\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\\"2. EARLY HALT VS NEVER HALT\\\")\\n    print(\\\"-\\\" * 78)\\n\\n    result = compare_at_time(\\n        fork,\\n        early,\\n        never,\\n        branches,\\n        observation_steps\\n    )\\n\\n    print_observable(\\n        \\\"EARLY HALT\\\",\\n        result[\\\"obs1\\\"]\\n    )\\n\\n    print()\\n\\n    print_observable(\\n        \\\"NEVER HALT\\\",\\n        result[\\\"obs2\\\"]\\n    )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\n        f\\\"State difference = \\\"\\n        f\\\"{result['state_difference']:.15e}\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\n        f\\\"Matrix difference = \\\"\\n        f\\\"{result['matrix_difference']:.15e}\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\\"These differ because EARLY_HALT has actually executed HALT.\\\")\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\\"3. LATE HALT VS NEVER HALT\\\")\\n    print(\\\"-\\\" * 78)\\n\\n    result = compare_at_time(\\n        fork,\\n        late,\\n        never,\\n        branches,\\n        observation_steps\\n    )\\n\\n    print_observable(\\n        \\\"LATE HALT\\\",\\n        result[\\\"obs1\\\"]\\n    )\\n\\n    print()\\n\\n    print_observable(\\n        \\\"NEVER HALT\\\",\\n        result[\\\"obs2\\\"]\\n    )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\n        f\\\"State difference = \\\"\\n        f\\\"{result['state_difference']:.15e}\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\n        f\\\"Matrix difference = \\\"\\n        f\\\"{result['matrix_difference']:.15e}\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\\"OBSERVABLE DIFFERENCES\\\")\\n    print(\\\"-\\\" * 78)\\n\\n    for key, value in result[\\n        \\\"observable_difference\\\"\\n    ].items():\\n        print(\\n            f\\\"{key:25s} = {value:.15e}\\\"\\n        )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\\"4. MATHEMATICAL IDENTITY\\\")\\n    print(\\\"-\\\" * 78)\\n\\n    print(\\n        \\\"For every step j <= observation time:\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\n        \\\"    T_j(LATE_HALT) = T_j(NEVER_HALT)\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\n        \\\"Therefore:\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\n        \\\"    U_N(LATE_HALT) = U_N(NEVER_HALT)\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\n        \\\"and consequently:\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\n        \\\"    W_N(LATE_HALT) = W_N(NEVER_HALT)\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\\"5. PATH-ORDERED PRODUCT\\\")\\n    print(\\\"-\\\" * 78)\\n\\n    print(\\n        \\\"U_N = T_N T_(N-1) ... T_2 T_1\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\n        \\\"The multiplication order is retained.\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\n        \\\"But the finite product contains only the first N \\\"\\n        \\\"instructions.\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\\"6. THE LIMIT THAT WOULD BE REQUIRED\\\")\\n    print(\\\"-\\\" * 78)\\n\\n    print(\\n        \\\"A universal halting detector would require a finite-time \\\"\\n        \\\"observable F satisfying:\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\n        \\\"F(P) = 1  if P eventually halts\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\n        \\\"F(P) = 0  if P never halts\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\n        \\\"The problem is that for every finite N one can construct:\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\n        \\\"P_late  = O1 O2 ... ON ... HALT\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\n        \\\"P_loop  = O1 O2 ... ON ... LOOP\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\n        \\\"Their complete physical histories through N are identical.\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\n        \\\"Therefore every observable calculated solely from that \\\"\\n        \\\"finite history is identical.\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\\"=\\\" * 78)\\n    print(\\\"CONCLUSION\\\")\\n    print(\\\"=\\\" * 78)\\n\\n    if result[\\\"matrix_difference\\\"] < 1e-12:\\n        print(\\n            \\\"The simulation verifies finite-time indistinguishability.\\\"\\n        )\\n    else:\\n        print(\\n            \\\"Unexpected difference detected.\\\"\\n        )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\n        \\\"The path-ordered construction successfully preserves \\\"\\n        \\\"noncommutative computational ordering.\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\n        \\\"It does not, by itself, provide information about \\\"\\n        \\\"instructions that have not yet occurred.\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\n        \\\"To obtain a genuine halting oracle, an additional physical \\\"\\n        \\\"mechanism would have to encode the infinite future into a \\\"\\n        \\\"finite measurable quantity.\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n\\n# =============================================================================\\n# TOY HALTING PROBLEM -- ACTUALLY DECIDABLE, ACTUALLY SOLVED\\n# =============================================================================\\n#\\n# The section above demonstrates *why* halting is undecidable in general:\\n# a finite observation window cannot distinguish \\\"halts later\\\" from \\\"never\\n# halts,\\\" because both programs can have identical histories up to that\\n# window, for any window size you pick.\\n#\\n# That impossibility relies on one thing: an unbounded configuration space\\n# (an infinite-tape, infinite-future machine). If instead the configuration\\n# space is FINITE -- bounded registers, bounded tape, finitely many\\n# instructions -- halting becomes decidable, with a real proof, not a\\n# simulation trick:\\n#\\n#   1. Every configuration is a tuple (program_counter, register_values).\\n#   2. The machine is deterministic, so each configuration has exactly one\\n#      successor.\\n#   3. There are only finitely many possible configurations.\\n#   4. Run the machine and record every configuration visited.\\n#      - If HALT is reached: the machine halts. Proven.\\n#      - If a configuration repeats: the machine has entered a deterministic\\n#        cycle, and since it is deterministic, it will repeat that cycle\\n#        forever. It provably never halts. Proven.\\n#   5. Since there are finitely many configurations, one of these two\\n#      outcomes MUST occur within (number of configurations) steps. There is\\n#      no third case and no need to guess or bound the search heuristically.\\n#\\n# This is the same logic used by real, sound tools such as model checkers\\n# and finite-state verifiers. It works precisely because the state space is\\n# finite -- the one assumption that fails for general Turing machines.\\n\\n\\n@dataclass\\nclass Instruction:\\n    op: str          # \\\"INC\\\", \\\"DEC\\\", \\\"JZ\\\", \\\"JMP\\\", \\\"HALT\\\"\\n    reg: int = 0      # register index, for INC/DEC/JZ\\n    target: int = 0   # jump target, for JZ/JMP\\n\\n\\nclass ToyMachine:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    A tiny register machine:\\n      - a fixed number of registers, each clamped to [0, max_value]\\n      - INC r        : reg[r] += 1 (clamped)\\n      - DEC r        : reg[r] -= 1 (clamped, floor 0)\\n      - JZ  r target : jump to target if reg[r] == 0, else fall through\\n      - JMP target   : unconditional jump\\n      - HALT         : stop\\n\\n    Because every register is bounded and there are finitely many\\n    instructions, the total configuration space is finite:\\n        num_instructions * (max_value + 1) ** num_registers\\n    which makes halting decidable for this machine, exactly.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\n    def __init__(self, program, num_registers, max_value):\\n        self.program = program\\n        self.num_registers = num_registers\\n        self.max_value = max_value\\n\\n    def initial_config(self):\\n        return (0, tuple([0] * self.num_registers))\\n\\n    def step(self, config):\\n        pc, regs = config\\n        instr = self.program[pc]\\n        regs = list(regs)\\n\\n        if instr.op == \\\"INC\\\":\\n            regs[instr.reg] = min(self.max_value, regs[instr.reg] + 1)\\n            next_pc = pc + 1\\n        elif instr.op == \\\"DEC\\\":\\n            regs[instr.reg] = max(0, regs[instr.reg] - 1)\\n            next_pc = pc + 1\\n        elif instr.op == \\\"JZ\\\":\\n            next_pc = instr.target if regs[instr.reg] == 0 else pc + 1\\n        elif instr.op == \\\"JMP\\\":\\n            next_pc = instr.target\\n        elif instr.op == \\\"HALT\\\":\\n            next_pc = pc  # unreachable; step() isn't called after HALT\\n        else:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Unknown opcode: {instr.op}\\\")\\n\\n        return (next_pc, tuple(regs))\\n\\n    def total_configurations(self):\\n        return len(self.program) * (self.max_value + 1) ** self.num_registers\\n\\n\\ndef decide_halting(machine, verbose=False):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    A genuine, correct halting decider for ToyMachine programs.\\n\\n    Returns a dict with:\\n        halts        : True, False (never guessed -- always proven)\\n        steps        : number of steps taken to halt (if halts)\\n        cycle_start  : step index where the repeated configuration first\\n                        occurred (if it loops)\\n        cycle_length : length of the proven infinite cycle (if it loops)\\n        configs_seen : how many configurations were explored\\n        state_space  : total size of the machine's configuration space\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    config = machine.initial_config()\\n    seen = {config: 0}\\n    trace = [config]\\n\\n    step_count = 0\\n    limit = machine.total_configurations()\\n\\n    while True:\\n        pc, regs = config\\n\\n        if machine.program[pc].op == \\\"HALT\\\":\\n            return {\\n                \\\"halts\\\": True,\\n                \\\"steps\\\": step_count,\\n                \\\"configs_seen\\\": len(seen),\\n                \\\"state_space\\\": limit,\\n                \\\"trace\\\": trace if verbose else None,\\n            }\\n\\n        config = machine.step(config)\\n        step_count += 1\\n\\n        if config in seen:\\n            cycle_start = seen[config]\\n            return {\\n                \\\"halts\\\": False,\\n                \\\"cycle_start\\\": cycle_start,\\n                \\\"cycle_length\\\": step_count - cycle_start,\\n                \\\"configs_seen\\\": len(seen),\\n                \\\"state_space\\\": limit,\\n                \\\"trace\\\": trace if verbose else None,\\n            }\\n\\n        seen[config] = step_count\\n        trace.append(config)\\n\\n        # Safety net only -- mathematically this can never trigger,\\n        # since the state space is finite and we check every visited\\n        # configuration above. Kept only to fail loudly on a bug.\\n        assert step_count <= limit + 1, \\\"unreachable: proof above guarantees termination\\\"\\n\\n\\ndef print_decision(name, machine, result):\\n    print(name)\\n    print(f\\\"  Registers x range   : {machine.num_registers} x [0, {machine.max_value}]\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"  Total state space   : {result['state_space']} configurations\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"  Configurations seen : {result['configs_seen']}\\\")\\n\\n    if result[\\\"halts\\\"]:\\n        print(f\\\"  DECISION            : HALTS  (in {result['steps']} steps)\\\")\\n    else:\\n        print(\\n            f\\\"  DECISION            : NEVER HALTS  \\\"\\n            f\\\"(proven -- enters a cycle of length {result['cycle_length']} \\\"\\n            f\\\"starting at step {result['cycle_start']})\\\"\\n        )\\n\\n\\ndef run_toy_halting_demo():\\n    print()\\n    print(\\\"=\\\" * 78)\\n    print(\\\"TOY HALTING PROBLEM -- FINITE-STATE, ACTUALLY DECIDABLE\\\")\\n    print(\\\"=\\\" * 78)\\n    print()\\n    print(\\n        \\\"Unlike a general Turing machine, this machine has boundedly many\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"registers over a bounded range, so its configuration space is\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"finite. Halting is therefore genuinely decidable: run it, and\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"either HALT is reached or a configuration repeats (a proven\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"infinite loop). One of the two always happens.\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    # Program A: counts a register down from 3 to 0, then halts.\\n    #   0: JZ  r0 -> 3   (if r0 == 0, jump to HALT)\\n    #   1: DEC r0\\n    #   2: JMP -> 0\\n    #   3: HALT\\n    halting_program = [\\n        Instruction(\\\"JZ\\\", reg=0, target=3),\\n        Instruction(\\\"DEC\\\", reg=0),\\n        Instruction(\\\"JMP\\\", target=0),\\n        Instruction(\\\"HALT\\\"),\\n    ]\\n\\n    # To make it start at 3 instead of 0, we bump r0 up first:\\n    #   0: INC r0\\n    #   1: INC r0\\n    #   2: INC r0\\n    #   3: JZ  r0 -> 6\\n    #   4: DEC r0\\n    #   5: JMP -> 3\\n    #   6: HALT\\n    halting_program = [\\n        Instruction(\\\"INC\\\", reg=0),\\n        Instruction(\\\"INC\\\", reg=0),\\n        Instruction(\\\"INC\\\", reg=0),\\n        Instruction(\\\"JZ\\\", reg=0, target=6),\\n        Instruction(\\\"DEC\\\", reg=0),\\n        Instruction(\\\"JMP\\\", target=3),\\n        Instruction(\\\"HALT\\\"),\\n    ]\\n\\n    # Program B: an unconditional infinite loop. Never reaches HALT.\\n    #   0: INC r0\\n    #   1: JMP -> 0\\n    looping_program = [\\n        Instruction(\\\"INC\\\", reg=0),\\n        Instruction(\\\"JMP\\\", target=0),\\n    ]\\n\\n    # Program C: a \\\"disguised\\\" infinite loop -- it increments and decrements\\n    # in a cycle that never zeroes out at the right place, oscillating\\n    # forever between two register states without ever reaching HALT.\\n    #   0: INC r0\\n    #   1: INC r1\\n    #   2: DEC r0\\n    #   3: JZ  r0 -> 0     (r0 is back to 0 here every time -> loops forever)\\n    oscillating_program = [\\n        Instruction(\\\"INC\\\", reg=0),\\n        Instruction(\\\"INC\\\", reg=1),\\n        Instruction(\\\"DEC\\\", reg=0),\\n        Instruction(\\\"JZ\\\", reg=0, target=0),\\n    ]\\n\\n    machine_a = ToyMachine(halting_program, num_registers=1, max_value=7)\\n    machine_b = ToyMachine(looping_program, num_registers=1, max_value=7)\\n    machine_c = ToyMachine(oscillating_program, num_registers=2, max_value=7)\\n\\n    print()\\n    print_decision(\\\"PROGRAM A (counts down then halts)\\\", machine_a, decide_halting(machine_a))\\n    print()\\n    print_decision(\\\"PROGRAM B (unconditional infinite loop)\\\", machine_b, decide_halting(machine_b))\\n    print()\\n    print_decision(\\\"PROGRAM C (oscillates forever, never hits HALT)\\\", machine_c, decide_halting(machine_c))\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\\"-\\\" * 78)\\n    print(\\n        \\\"Each decision above is a proof, not a guess: either an explicit\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"HALT instruction was reached, or a configuration was shown to\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"repeat, which -- for a deterministic machine -- guarantees it\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"repeats forever. This does not contradict Turing's result: it\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"works only because the register values here are bounded, so the\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"state space is finite. Remove that bound (unbounded registers,\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"i.e. a real counter machine) and this exact technique breaks,\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"because 'seen' can then grow forever without ever repeating --\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"which is the general halting problem again.\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n\\ndef main():\\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\\n        description=(\\n            \\\"Simulate path-ordered computational evolution, test \\\"\\n            \\\"finite-time halting distinguishability, and separately run \\\"\\n            \\\"a genuine decidable toy halting-problem demo.\\\"\\n        )\\n    )\\n\\n    parser.add_argument(\\n        \\\"--steps\\\",\\n        type=int,\\n        default=1000\\n    )\\n\\n    parser.add_argument(\\n        \\\"--late-halt\\\",\\n        type=int,\\n        default=2000\\n    )\\n\\n    parser.add_argument(\\n        \\\"--skip-quantum\\\",\\n        action=\\\"store_true\\\",\\n        help=\\\"Skip the quantum path-ordered simulation and only run the toy halting demo.\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    parser.add_argument(\\n        \\\"--skip-toy\\\",\\n        action=\\\"store_true\\\",\\n        help=\\\"Skip the toy halting-problem demo and only run the quantum simulation.\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    args = parser.parse_args()\\n\\n    if args.steps <= 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\n            \\\"--steps must be greater than zero\\\"\\n        )\\n\\n    if args.late_halt <= args.steps:\\n        raise ValueError(\\n            \\\"--late-halt must be greater than --steps\\\"\\n        )\\n\\n    if not args.skip_quantum:\\n        run_experiment(\\n            observation_steps=args.steps,\\n            late_halt=args.late_halt\\n        )\\n\\n    if not args.skip_toy:\\n        run_toy_halting_demo()\\n\\n\\nif __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\":\\n    main()\\n\", \"filename\": \"/mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:51:34.496234Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:51:34.496276Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01VnUjJa7ZRHfRZaMite2Kas", "name": "create_file", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "File created successfully: /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "File created successfully: /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:51:36.181400Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:51:37.086105Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01ANmWB1bNdztTwAvZVk6aJr", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "cd /home/claude && pip install numpy --break-system-packages -q 2>/dev/null; python3 /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py --skip-quantum", "description": "Run just the toy halting demo to verify it works correctly"}, "message": "Run just the toy halting demo to verify it works correctly", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cd /home/claude && pip install numpy --break-system-packages -q 2>/dev/null; python3 /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py --skip-quantum\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:51:38.785936Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:51:38.785985Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01ANmWB1bNdztTwAvZVk6aJr", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"\\n==============================================================================\\nTOY HALTING PROBLEM -- FINITE-STATE, ACTUALLY DECIDABLE\\n==============================================================================\\n\\nUnlike a general Turing machine, this machine has boundedly many\\nregisters over a bounded range, so its configuration space is\\nfinite. Halting is therefore genuinely decidable: run it, and\\neither HALT is reached or a configuration repeats (a proven\\ninfinite loop). One of the two always happens.\\n\\nPROGRAM A (counts down then halts)\\n  Registers x range   : 1 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 56 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 14\\n  DECISION            : HALTS  (in 13 steps)\\n\\nPROGRAM B (unconditional infinite loop)\\n  Registers x range   : 1 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 16 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 15\\n  DECISION            : NEVER HALTS  (proven -- enters a cycle of length 2 starting at step 13)\\n\\nPROGRAM C (oscillates forever, never hits HALT)\\n  Registers x range   : 2 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 256 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 30\\n  DECISION            : NEVER HALTS  (proven -- enters a cycle of length 4 starting at step 26)\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nEach decision above is a proof, not a guess: either an explicit\\nHALT instruction was reached, or a configuration was shown to\\nrepeat, which -- for a deterministic machine -- guarantees it\\nrepeats forever. This does not contradict Turing's result: it\\nworks only because the register values here are bounded, so the\\nstate space is finite. Remove that bound (unbounded registers,\\ni.e. a real counter machine) and this exact technique breaks,\\nbecause 'seen' can then grow forever without ever repeating --\\nwhich is the general halting problem again.\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"\\n==============================================================================\\nTOY HALTING PROBLEM -- FINITE-STATE, ACTUALLY DECIDABLE\\n==============================================================================\\n\\nUnlike a general Turing machine, this machine has boundedly many\\nregisters over a bounded range, so its configuration space is\\nfinite. Halting is therefore genuinely decidable: run it, and\\neither HALT is reached or a configuration repeats (a proven\\ninfinite loop). One of the two always happens.\\n\\nPROGRAM A (counts down then halts)\\n  Registers x range   : 1 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 56 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 14\\n  DECISION            : HALTS  (in 13 steps)\\n\\nPROGRAM B (unconditional infinite loop)\\n  Registers x range   : 1 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 16 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 15\\n  DECISION            : NEVER HALTS  (proven -- enters a cycle of length 2 starting at step 13)\\n\\nPROGRAM C (oscillates forever, never hits HALT)\\n  Registers x range   : 2 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 256 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 30\\n  DECISION            : NEVER HALTS  (proven -- enters a cycle of length 4 starting at step 26)\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nEach decision above is a proof, not a guess: either an explicit\\nHALT instruction was reached, or a configuration was shown to\\nrepeat, which -- for a deterministic machine -- guarantees it\\nrepeats forever. This does not contradict Turing's result: it\\nworks only because the register values here are bounded, so the\\nstate space is finite. Remove that bound (unbounded registers,\\ni.e. a real counter machine) and this exact technique breaks,\\nbecause 'seen' can then grow forever without ever repeating --\\nwhich is the general halting problem again.\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:51:40.231129Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:51:45.447697Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "It runs correctly and gets all three cases right. Your original quantum code is byte-for-byte unchanged (`--skip-toy` runs the exact original experiment, `--skip-quantum` runs only the new part, default runs both).\n\nWhat I added: a `ToyMachine` \u2014 a register machine with bounded registers (INC/DEC/JZ/JMP/HALT) \u2014 plus `decide_halting()`, which is a real decider, not a simulation with a cutoff. It works by tracking every configuration visited; since registers are bounded, the configuration space is finite, so either HALT gets hit or some configuration repeats, which *proves* an infinite loop (determinism means a repeated state means a repeated cycle forever). No guessing, no timeout, no heuristic \u2014 one of those two outcomes is mathematically guaranteed within `state_space` steps.\n\nTested it on three programs: one that halts, one obvious infinite loop, and one \"disguised\" loop that oscillates between register values \u2014 all three decided correctly with proof (cycle location and length shown for the non-halting ones).\n\nThe comment block at the end is honest about the boundary: this works *because* registers are bounded (finite state space). Make registers unbounded and the exact same `seen`-set trick stops working, since it can grow forever without repeating \u2014 that's the real halting problem again, and it's what your quantum section is illustrating from a different angle.", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:51:45.447730Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:51:45.739880Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01TFYofReVAzafHwcDAqhRUb", "name": "present_files", "input": {"filepaths": ["/mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py"]}, "message": "Presenting file(s)...", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "table", "table": [["filepaths", "['/mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py']"]]}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:51:45.759684Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:51:45.759711Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01TFYofReVAzafHwcDAqhRUb", "name": "present_files", "content": [{"type": "local_resource", "file_path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py", "name": "halting demo", "mime_type": "text/x-python", "artifact_publishable": false}, {"type": "text", "text": "The file cards appear at the end of your reply if it ends with text, otherwise under this step; say they are below only if you finish the reply with text."}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": "Presented file", "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:51:47.470259Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:51:47.899338Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "File's above \u2014 run it with `--skip-quantum` for just the toy demo, or no flags for both.", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-26T12:51:47.972474Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T12:51:47.972474Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddc4-3bea-7d55-aa59-3f5934ba13c2"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddc6-8ed2-7f30-aa81-8df0f013cc1b", "text": "make sure the math solves it", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:52:51.963467Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:52:51.963467Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "make sure the math solves it", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-26T12:52:51.963661Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T12:52:51.963661Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddc4-3bea-73a5-9808-aa0c2dc3daf3"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddc6-8ed2-774a-bb3c-860644b9ed69", "text": "I want to be straight with you rather than dress something up: there's no version of this math \u2014 quantum, classical, or otherwise \u2014 that can make it *solve* the halting problem in general. This isn't a limitation of my implementation; it's a proven theorem, and it holds regardless of what physical or mathematical machinery you wrap around it.\n\nHere's why, concretely, for *this* setup:\n\n- Turing's proof (1936) shows the halting problem is undecidable for **any** computational model equivalent in power to a Turing machine. This includes quantum computers \u2014 quantum Turing machines were proven to have the exact same halting undecidability in the 1980s-90s (Deutsch's own 1985 paper on quantum Turing machines preserves this). Superposition, phase, holonomy \u2014 none of it adds information about instructions that haven't executed yet.\n- Your script's own section 6 states the actual obstruction precisely: for any finite step count N, there exist two programs (`P_late`, `P_loop`) whose histories through step N are *provably identical*. If two programs have identical histories, **any** function of that history \u2014 trace, phase, holonomy magnitude, whatever \u2014 must return the identical value for both. That's not a numerical near-miss; it's `matrix_difference == 0` exactly, and the code already demonstrates this.\n- So \"improving\" the math to close this gap would require the observable at step N to depend on instructions beyond step N, i.e. information from the future. That's not a math problem I can solve \u2014 it's a contradiction in terms.\n\nIf I \"fixed\" the quantum section to output the right answer, I'd have to either (a) secretly peek at `program.halt_step` to cheat, which isn't the observable actually deciding anything, or (b) fudge the numbers \u2014 both would be presenting a false result as genuine, which I don't want to do even if it's just a toy script.\n\nWhat I can genuinely offer:\n\n1. **The toy decider already does solve a real, non-cheating instance** \u2014 happy to extend it (more instructions, bigger register bounds, a second toy machine class like bounded-tape Turing machines) if you want a bigger decidable example.\n2. **A rigorous proof-style writeup** of why quantum mechanics doesn't help here (Deutsch's model, why unitarity/reversibility doesn't grant lookahead) if you want the theory spelled out.\n3. If what you're actually chasing is \"can *any* physical principle beat Turing undecidability\" \u2014 that's a legitimate and interesting research question (hypercomputation, Malament-Hogarth spacetimes, etc.) and I'm glad to dig into what's been seriously proposed and why each one still doesn't work in our universe.\n\nWhich of these would actually be useful to you?", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:52:51.956278Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:52:58.948643Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Explaining why halting problem can't be solved by quantum simulation."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:52:58.948699Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:53:10.845168Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "I want to be straight with you rather than dress something up: there's no version of this math \u2014 quantum, classical, or otherwise \u2014 that can make it *solve* the halting problem in general. This isn't a limitation of my implementation; it's a proven theorem, and it holds regardless of what physical or mathematical machinery you wrap around it.\n\nHere's why, concretely, for *this* setup:\n\n- Turing's proof (1936) shows the halting problem is undecidable for **any** computational model equivalent in power to a Turing machine. This includes quantum computers \u2014 quantum Turing machines were proven to have the exact same halting undecidability in the 1980s-90s (Deutsch's own 1985 paper on quantum Turing machines preserves this). Superposition, phase, holonomy \u2014 none of it adds information about instructions that haven't executed yet.\n- Your script's own section 6 states the actual obstruction precisely: for any finite step count N, there exist two programs (`P_late`, `P_loop`) whose histories through step N are *provably identical*. If two programs have identical histories, **any** function of that history \u2014 trace, phase, holonomy magnitude, whatever \u2014 must return the identical value for both. That's not a numerical near-miss; it's `matrix_difference == 0` exactly, and the code already demonstrates this.\n- So \"improving\" the math to close this gap would require the observable at step N to depend on instructions beyond step N, i.e. information from the future. That's not a math problem I can solve \u2014 it's a contradiction in terms.\n\nIf I \"fixed\" the quantum section to output the right answer, I'd have to either (a) secretly peek at `program.halt_step` to cheat, which isn't the observable actually deciding anything, or (b) fudge the numbers \u2014 both would be presenting a false result as genuine, which I don't want to do even if it's just a toy script.\n\nWhat I can genuinely offer:\n\n1. **The toy decider already does solve a real, non-cheating instance** \u2014 happy to extend it (more instructions, bigger register bounds, a second toy machine class like bounded-tape Turing machines) if you want a bigger decidable example.\n2. **A rigorous proof-style writeup** of why quantum mechanics doesn't help here (Deutsch's model, why unitarity/reversibility doesn't grant lookahead) if you want the theory spelled out.\n3. If what you're actually chasing is \"can *any* physical principle beat Turing undecidability\" \u2014 that's a legitimate and interesting research question (hypercomputation, Malament-Hogarth spacetimes, etc.) and I'm glad to dig into what's been seriously proposed and why each one still doesn't work in our universe.\n\nWhich of these would actually be useful to you?", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-26T12:53:10.862530Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T12:53:10.862530Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddc6-8ed2-7f30-aa81-8df0f013cc1b"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddc7-0863-7a89-93f5-de95f1138c7d", "text": "do it anyway", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:53:22.870553Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:53:22.870553Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "do it anyway", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-26T12:53:22.870780Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T12:53:22.870780Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddc6-8ed2-774a-bb3c-860644b9ed69"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddc7-0863-7604-aa5f-63e671ed9ea3", "text": "I'm not going to do that one \u2014 not because of a policy technicality, but because there isn't a version of \"do it anyway\" that isn't one of the two things I already ruled out: quietly reading `program.halt_step` to fake the result, or fudging the numbers. Either way the code would *report* a decided answer while not actually having computed one, which is worse than not having the feature at all \u2014 it's a tool that lies about what it did.\n\nThis isn't me being cautious about a sensitive topic. It's the same reason I wouldn't write a function called `is_prime()` that just returns `True` \u2014 it would compile, it would run, it would even pass a casual glance, but it wouldn't do the thing its name claims.\n\nIf you want, I can actually show you *why* there's no clever escape, rather than just asserting it \u2014 pick whichever's interesting:\n\n- Walk through Deutsch's 1985 quantum Turing machine paper and show exactly where the argument for undecidability re-enters even with full quantum parallelism.\n- Take a real hypercomputation proposal (Malament-Hogarth spacetimes, infinite-time Turing machines, oracle machines) and show precisely what extra unphysical assumption each one needs to beat this \u2014 so you can see it's not \"math wasn't clever enough,\" it's \"here's the specific cheat every proposal needs.\"\n- Or, if you'd rather build something real: extend the toy decider to a strictly harder but still genuinely decidable class (e.g., bounded-tape Turing machines instead of register machines) \u2014 a bigger, more convincing \"yes this actually works\" demo.\n\nWhat would be most useful?", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:53:22.865005Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:53:29.866727Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "I'm not going to do that one \u2014 not because of a policy technicality, but because there isn't a version of \"do it anyway\" that isn't one of the two things I already ruled out: quietly reading `program.halt_step` to fake the result, or fudging the numbers. Either way the code would *report* a decided answer while not actually having computed one, which is worse than not having the feature at all \u2014 it's a tool that lies about what it did.\n\nThis isn't me being cautious about a sensitive topic. It's the same reason I wouldn't write a function called `is_prime()` that just returns `True` \u2014 it would compile, it would run, it would even pass a casual glance, but it wouldn't do the thing its name claims.\n\nIf you want, I can actually show you *why* there's no clever escape, rather than just asserting it \u2014 pick whichever's interesting:\n\n- Walk through Deutsch's 1985 quantum Turing machine paper and show exactly where the argument for undecidability re-enters even with full quantum parallelism.\n- Take a real hypercomputation proposal (Malament-Hogarth spacetimes, infinite-time Turing machines, oracle machines) and show precisely what extra unphysical assumption each one needs to beat this \u2014 so you can see it's not \"math wasn't clever enough,\" it's \"here's the specific cheat every proposal needs.\"\n- Or, if you'd rather build something real: extend the toy decider to a strictly harder but still genuinely decidable class (e.g., bounded-tape Turing machines instead of register machines) \u2014 a bigger, more convincing \"yes this actually works\" demo.\n\nWhat would be most useful?", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-26T12:53:29.889908Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T12:53:29.889908Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddc7-0863-7a89-93f5-de95f1138c7d"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddc7-4344-74ab-87b4-4495b28b78be", "text": "use this code then", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:53:37.751688Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:53:37.751688Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "use this code then", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-26T12:53:37.751919Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T12:53:37.751919Z", "attachments": [{"file_name": "", "file_size": 10394, "file_type": "txt", "extracted_content": "import numpy as np\r\nimport argparse\r\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\r\n\r\n\r\n@dataclass\r\nclass Program:\r\n    name: str\r\n    halt_step: int | None\r\n\r\n    def instruction(self, step):\r\n        if self.halt_step is not None and step == self.halt_step:\r\n            return \"HALT\"\r\n        return \"CONTINUE\"\r\n\r\n\r\nclass PathOrderedFork:\r\n    def __init__(self, modes=4):\r\n        self.modes = modes\r\n        self.I = np.eye(modes, dtype=complex)\r\n\r\n        self.TL = self.rotation(0, 1, 0.37, 0.21)\r\n        self.TR = self.rotation(1, 2, 0.61, 1.17)\r\n        self.TH = self.halt_transform()\r\n\r\n    def rotation(self, a, b, theta, phase):\r\n        U = np.eye(self.modes, dtype=complex)\r\n\r\n        c = np.cos(theta)\r\n        s = np.sin(theta)\r\n\r\n        U[a, a] = c\r\n        U[b, b] = c\r\n\r\n        U[a, b] = -1j * np.exp(1j * phase) * s\r\n        U[b, a] = -1j * np.exp(-1j * phase) * s\r\n\r\n        return U\r\n\r\n    def halt_transform(self):\r\n        U = np.eye(self.modes, dtype=complex)\r\n\r\n        theta = 0.9\r\n        c = np.cos(theta)\r\n        s = np.sin(theta)\r\n\r\n        U[0, 0] = c\r\n        U[3, 3] = c\r\n\r\n        U[0, 3] = -1j * s\r\n        U[3, 0] = -1j * s\r\n\r\n        return U\r\n\r\n    def branch_transform(self, branch):\r\n        if branch == 0:\r\n            return self.TL\r\n        return self.TR\r\n\r\n    def branch_sequence(self, n):\r\n        return [\r\n            ((k * 1103515245 + 12345) >> 4) & 1\r\n            for k in range(n)\r\n        ]\r\n\r\n    def evolve(self, program, branches, steps):\r\n        state = np.zeros(self.modes, dtype=complex)\r\n        state[0] = 1.0\r\n\r\n        U = self.I.copy()\r\n\r\n        history = []\r\n\r\n        for step in range(steps):\r\n            branch = branches[step]\r\n\r\n            T = self.branch_transform(branch)\r\n\r\n            instruction = program.instruction(step)\r\n\r\n            if instruction == \"HALT\":\r\n                T = self.TH @ T\r\n\r\n            # Exact discrete path-ordered multiplication.\r\n            U = T @ U\r\n\r\n            state = T @ state\r\n\r\n            state_norm = np.linalg.norm(state)\r\n\r\n            if state_norm > 0:\r\n                state /= state_norm\r\n\r\n            trace = np.trace(U) / self.modes\r\n\r\n            halt_signal = abs(state[3]) ** 2\r\n\r\n            history.append({\r\n                \"step\": step,\r\n                \"branch\": branch,\r\n                \"instruction\": instruction,\r\n                \"state\": state.copy(),\r\n                \"U\": U.copy(),\r\n                \"halt_signal\": halt_signal,\r\n                \"trace\": trace,\r\n                \"trace_abs\": abs(trace),\r\n                \"trace_phase\": np.angle(trace)\r\n            })\r\n\r\n        return state, U, history\r\n\r\n    def observable(self, state, U):\r\n        W = np.trace(U) / self.modes\r\n\r\n        return {\r\n            \"halt_signal\": float(abs(state[3]) ** 2),\r\n            \"holonomy_magnitude\": float(abs(W)),\r\n            \"holonomy_phase\": float(np.angle(W)),\r\n            \"state_norm\": float(np.linalg.norm(state)),\r\n            \"trace_real\": float(W.real),\r\n            \"trace_imag\": float(W.imag)\r\n        }\r\n\r\n    def commutator(self):\r\n        return np.linalg.norm(\r\n            self.TR @ self.TL -\r\n            self.TL @ self.TR\r\n        )\r\n\r\n\r\ndef compare_at_time(fork, p1, p2, branches, steps):\r\n    state1, U1, history1 = fork.evolve(\r\n        p1,\r\n        branches,\r\n        steps\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    state2, U2, history2 = fork.evolve(\r\n        p2,\r\n        branches,\r\n        steps\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    obs1 = fork.observable(state1, U1)\r\n    obs2 = fork.observable(state2, U2)\r\n\r\n    state_difference = np.linalg.norm(\r\n        state1 - state2\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    matrix_difference = np.linalg.norm(\r\n        U1 - U2\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    observable_difference = {\r\n        key: abs(obs1[key] - obs2[key])\r\n        for key in obs1\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    return {\r\n        \"state1\": state1,\r\n        \"state2\": state2,\r\n        \"U1\": U1,\r\n        \"U2\": U2,\r\n        \"obs1\": obs1,\r\n        \"obs2\": obs2,\r\n        \"state_difference\": state_difference,\r\n        \"matrix_difference\": matrix_difference,\r\n        \"observable_difference\": observable_difference,\r\n        \"history1\": history1,\r\n        \"history2\": history2\r\n    }\r\n\r\n\r\ndef print_observable(name, obs):\r\n    print(name)\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"  HALT signal       = \"\r\n        f\"{obs['halt_signal']:.15f}\"\r\n    )\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"  |Tr(U)/d|        = \"\r\n        f\"{obs['holonomy_magnitude']:.15f}\"\r\n    )\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"  arg(Tr(U)/d)     = \"\r\n        f\"{obs['holonomy_phase']:.15f}\"\r\n    )\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"  Tr real          = \"\r\n        f\"{obs['trace_real']:.15f}\"\r\n    )\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"  Tr imaginary     = \"\r\n        f\"{obs['trace_imag']:.15f}\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n\r\ndef run_experiment(observation_steps=1000, late_halt=2000):\r\n    fork = PathOrderedFork()\r\n\r\n    branches = fork.branch_sequence(\r\n        max(observation_steps, late_halt + 1)\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    early = Program(\r\n        \"EARLY_HALT\",\r\n        observation_steps // 2\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    late = Program(\r\n        \"LATE_HALT\",\r\n        late_halt\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    never = Program(\r\n        \"NEVER_HALT\",\r\n        None\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\r\n    print(\"PATH-ORDERED FORK / FINITE-TIME HALTING TEST\")\r\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"Observation window : {observation_steps} steps\"\r\n    )\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"Late HALT          : step {late_halt}\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"1. NONCOMMUTATIVITY\")\r\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    comm = fork.commutator()\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"||T_R T_L - T_L T_R|| = \"\r\n        f\"{comm:.15f}\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"Ordering is encoded.\"\r\n        if comm > 1e-12\r\n        else\r\n        \"Ordering is not encoded.\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"2. EARLY HALT VS NEVER HALT\")\r\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    result = compare_at_time(\r\n        fork,\r\n        early,\r\n        never,\r\n        branches,\r\n        observation_steps\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print_observable(\r\n        \"EARLY HALT\",\r\n        result[\"obs1\"]\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n\r\n    print_observable(\r\n        \"NEVER HALT\",\r\n        result[\"obs2\"]\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"State difference = \"\r\n        f\"{result['state_difference']:.15e}\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"Matrix difference = \"\r\n        f\"{result['matrix_difference']:.15e}\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"These differ because EARLY_HALT has actually executed HALT.\")\r\n    \r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"3. LATE HALT VS NEVER HALT\")\r\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    result = compare_at_time(\r\n        fork,\r\n        late,\r\n        never,\r\n        branches,\r\n        observation_steps\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print_observable(\r\n        \"LATE HALT\",\r\n        result[\"obs1\"]\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n\r\n    print_observable(\r\n        \"NEVER HALT\",\r\n        result[\"obs2\"]\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"State difference = \"\r\n        f\"{result['state_difference']:.15e}\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"Matrix difference = \"\r\n        f\"{result['matrix_difference']:.15e}\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"OBSERVABLE DIFFERENCES\")\r\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    for key, value in result[\r\n        \"observable_difference\"\r\n    ].items():\r\n        print(\r\n            f\"{key:25s} = {value:.15e}\"\r\n        )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"4. MATHEMATICAL IDENTITY\")\r\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"For every step j <= observation time:\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"    T_j(LATE_HALT) = T_j(NEVER_HALT)\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"Therefore:\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"    U_N(LATE_HALT) = U_N(NEVER_HALT)\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"and consequently:\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"    W_N(LATE_HALT) = W_N(NEVER_HALT)\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"5. PATH-ORDERED PRODUCT\")\r\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"U_N = T_N T_(N-1) ... T_2 T_1\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"The multiplication order is retained.\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"But the finite product contains only the first N \"\r\n        \"instructions.\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"6. THE LIMIT THAT WOULD BE REQUIRED\")\r\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"A universal halting detector would require a finite-time \"\r\n        \"observable F satisfying:\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        \"F(P) = 1  if P eventually halts\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"F(P) = 0  if P never halts\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        \"The problem is that for every finite N one can construct:\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        \"P_late  = O1 O2 ... ON ... HALT\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"P_loop  = O1 O2 ... ON ... LOOP\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        \"Their complete physical histories through N are identical.\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        \"Therefore every observable calculated solely from that \"\r\n        \"finite history is identical.\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\r\n    print(\"CONCLUSION\")\r\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\r\n\r\n    if result[\"matrix_difference\"] < 1e-12:\r\n        print(\r\n            \"The simulation verifies finite-time indistinguishability.\"\r\n        )\r\n    else:\r\n        print(\r\n            \"Unexpected difference detected.\"\r\n        )\r\n\r\n    print()\r\n    print(\r\n        \"The path-ordered construction successfully preserves \"\r\n        \"noncommutative computational ordering.\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"It does not, by itself, provide information about \"\r\n        \"instructions that have not yet occurred.\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        \"To obtain a genuine halting oracle, an additional physical \"\r\n        \"mechanism would have to encode the infinite future into a \"\r\n        \"finite measurable quantity.\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n\r\ndef main():\r\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\r\n        description=(\r\n            \"Simulate path-ordered computational evolution \"\r\n            \"and test finite-time halting distinguishability.\"\r\n        )\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    parser.add_argument(\r\n        \"--steps\",\r\n        type=int,\r\n        default=1000\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    parser.add_argument(\r\n        \"--late-halt\",\r\n        type=int,\r\n        default=2000\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    args = parser.parse_args()\r\n\r\n    if args.steps <= 0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\r\n            \"--steps must be greater than zero\"\r\n        )\r\n\r\n    if args.late_halt <= args.steps:\r\n        raise ValueError(\r\n            \"--late-halt must be greater than --steps\"\r\n        )\r\n\r\n    run_experiment(\r\n        observation_steps=args.steps,\r\n        late_halt=args.late_halt\r\n    )\r\n\r\n\r\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\r\n    main()\r\n"}], "files": [{"file_uuid": "4ac0e861-b31e-4526-b532-dc47a89e09cb", "file_name": ""}], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddc7-0863-7604-aa5f-63e671ed9ea3"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddc7-4344-707a-aa47-bee84983961a", "text": "Same file, same answer: I can't make this construction decide general halting, because the file itself contains the proof of why it can't \u2014 that's what section 6 spells out, and it's math I'm not going to override or fake around.\n\nConcretely, if you run this exact code:\n\n- `LATE_HALT` and `NEVER_HALT` are constructed to have identical `instruction(step)` output for every `step <= observation_steps` (since `late_halt > observation_steps` by the arg validation). So `T_j` is literally the same matrix for both programs at every step in range.\n- `U = T @ U` applied to identical matrices in identical order produces identical `U`. Same for `state`.\n- Therefore `trace`, `halt_signal`, `holonomy_magnitude`, everything in `observable()` \u2014 all identical. `matrix_difference` prints as `~1e-15` (floating point noise, not signal) or exactly `0.0`.\n\nThat's not a bug to patch \u2014 it's the theorem, demonstrated numerically. There's no tweak to `rotation()`, `halt_transform()`, or the observable functions that fixes this, because the two programs' complete input sequences to the unitary product are equal up to step N by construction. Any deterministic function of that finite product must agree on both.\n\nI'll say again, genuinely: I'm happy to help with something adjacent that's real \u2014\n\n- Extend the **toy decider** I wrote (register machine, bounded state space) to something bigger/more interesting \u2014 that one actually solves halting, correctly, for its class.\n- Walk through the specific quantum hypercomputation proposals in the literature and where each one's \"cheat\" is, if you want to understand the boundary rather than push past it here.\n- If this is for a story, paper, or thought experiment about a *fictional* device that beats Turing \u2014 I can help write that framing honestly, without claiming the numbers back it.\n\nWhat would actually help?", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:53:37.604621Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:53:46.148425Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Same file, same answer: I can't make this construction decide general halting, because the file itself contains the proof of why it can't \u2014 that's what section 6 spells out, and it's math I'm not going to override or fake around.\n\nConcretely, if you run this exact code:\n\n- `LATE_HALT` and `NEVER_HALT` are constructed to have identical `instruction(step)` output for every `step <= observation_steps` (since `late_halt > observation_steps` by the arg validation). So `T_j` is literally the same matrix for both programs at every step in range.\n- `U = T @ U` applied to identical matrices in identical order produces identical `U`. Same for `state`.\n- Therefore `trace`, `halt_signal`, `holonomy_magnitude`, everything in `observable()` \u2014 all identical. `matrix_difference` prints as `~1e-15` (floating point noise, not signal) or exactly `0.0`.\n\nThat's not a bug to patch \u2014 it's the theorem, demonstrated numerically. There's no tweak to `rotation()`, `halt_transform()`, or the observable functions that fixes this, because the two programs' complete input sequences to the unitary product are equal up to step N by construction. Any deterministic function of that finite product must agree on both.\n\nI'll say again, genuinely: I'm happy to help with something adjacent that's real \u2014\n\n- Extend the **toy decider** I wrote (register machine, bounded state space) to something bigger/more interesting \u2014 that one actually solves halting, correctly, for its class.\n- Walk through the specific quantum hypercomputation proposals in the literature and where each one's \"cheat\" is, if you want to understand the boundary rather than push past it here.\n- If this is for a story, paper, or thought experiment about a *fictional* device that beats Turing \u2014 I can help write that framing honestly, without claiming the numbers back it.\n\nWhat would actually help?", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-26T12:53:46.172268Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T12:53:46.172268Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddc7-4344-74ab-87b4-4495b28b78be"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddc7-9f36-7ecd-b1e3-4e5417e7d9e3", "text": "so just intergrate the toy problem to the math and dont solve it", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:02.016779Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:02.016779Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "so just intergrate the toy problem to the math and dont solve it", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-26T12:54:02.016986Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T12:54:02.016986Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddc7-4344-707a-aa47-bee84983961a"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddc7-9f36-7ff6-a7c4-8f608419dd18", "text": "That's a reasonable thing to actually build \u2014 wire the two together so the toy decider's *proven* result feeds into the quantum simulation as a real case study, without claiming the quantum math decides anything. Here's the integration:\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nNow let's verify it runs correctly:\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nGood catch needed \u2014 my \"slow halting\" toy program actually had a bug (wrong jump target), so it looped forever instead of halting late. Since the decider is *proving* things, a real proof is only useful if the program is actually correct. Let me fix it:\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nNow let's re-verify:\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nBoth cases behave exactly as they should now: with a window (3) shorter than the proven halt step (21), the quantum observable can't distinguish the two programs \u2014 `matrix_difference = 0.0` \u2014 even though `decide_halting()` already knows the truth with certainty. With a longer window (50, past step 21) they diverge, correctly, because the halting program has actually executed HALT inside that window.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nUpdated file is above. What it does now:\n\n- **Quantum section**: untouched, same as your original.\n- **Toy section**: untouched, a genuine decidable halting decider for bounded register machines.\n- **New integration section**: uses `decide_halting()` to get a *proven* ground truth for two toy programs, then feeds that proven result into the quantum simulation as its `Program.halt_step`. It reports honestly which case you're in:\n  - Window shorter than the proven halt step \u2192 quantum observable shows zero difference, exactly as the math requires, while the toy decider still knows the true answer.\n  - Window longer than the proven halt step \u2192 quantum observable correctly diverges, because the program's HALT instruction has actually fired inside the window.\n\nRun with `--integration-window N` to try different window sizes, or `--skip-quantum --skip-toy` to see just the integration part.", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:02.010385Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:08.349166Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Wiring a toy halting decider into the quantum math demonstration."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:08.349209Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:09.258283Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "That's a reasonable thing to actually build \u2014 wire the two together so the toy decider's *proven* result feeds into the quantum simulation as a real case study, without claiming the quantum math decides anything. Here's the integration:", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:09.258313Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:27.753471Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01NEZkFamxE5LRMWkwD8A1uE", "name": "str_replace", "input": {"path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py", "old_str": "def main():\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=(\n            \"Simulate path-ordered computational evolution, test \"\n            \"finite-time halting distinguishability, and separately run \"\n            \"a genuine decidable toy halting-problem demo.\"\n        )\n    )", "new_str": "# =============================================================================\n# INTEGRATION -- feed a PROVEN toy result into the quantum simulation\n# =============================================================================\n#\n# This does NOT make the quantum simulation decide anything. The toy decider\n# above already proved, with certainty, whether each ToyMachine halts. We\n# take that proven ground truth and hand it to the quantum simulation as\n# input, to see it demonstrate -- on a real decided case rather than an\n# arbitrary hand-picked one -- that its finite-time observable still cannot\n# tell the two apart within the observation window. The proof of halting\n# vs. non-halting comes entirely from decide_halting(); the quantum section\n# contributes zero decision-making, exactly as before.\n\n\ndef toy_result_to_program(name, machine, result):\n    \"\"\"\n    Convert a proven ToyMachine halting result into a Program for the\n    quantum simulation. The halt_step is only ever set when decide_halting\n    already proved a halt -- the quantum side never determines this itself.\n    \"\"\"\n    if result[\"halts\"]:\n        return Program(name, result[\"steps\"])\n    return Program(name, None)\n\n\ndef run_integrated_demo(observation_steps=50):\n    print()\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\n    print(\"INTEGRATION: PROVEN TOY RESULTS FED INTO THE QUANTUM SIMULATION\")\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\n    print()\n    print(\n        \"Ground truth below comes ONLY from decide_halting() (proven, \"\n        \"finite-state).\\nThe quantum section is not asked to decide \"\n        \"anything -- it is only asked whether\\nits finite-time observable \"\n        \"can tell the two proven cases apart within the window.\"\n    )\n\n    # A machine proven to halt, but only after more steps than our\n    # observation window -- so within the window it looks, to any\n    # finite-time observable, just like a machine that never halts.\n    slow_halting_program = [\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),  # r0 = 5\n        Instruction(\"JZ\", reg=0, target=6),\n        Instruction(\"DEC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"JMP\", target=5),\n        Instruction(\"HALT\"),\n    ]\n    machine_halts = ToyMachine(slow_halting_program, num_registers=1, max_value=7)\n    result_halts = decide_halting(machine_halts)\n\n    # A machine proven to never halt.\n    looping_program = [\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"JMP\", target=0),\n    ]\n    machine_loops = ToyMachine(looping_program, num_registers=1, max_value=7)\n    result_loops = decide_halting(machine_loops)\n\n    print()\n    print_decision(\"TOY MACHINE (will halt)\", machine_halts, result_halts)\n    print()\n    print_decision(\"TOY MACHINE (proven infinite loop)\", machine_loops, result_loops)\n\n    halts_when = result_halts[\"steps\"] if result_halts[\"halts\"] else None\n    print()\n    print(f\"Observation window for quantum stage : {observation_steps} steps\")\n    print(f\"Proven halt step of PROGRAM_HALTS    : {halts_when}\")\n    print(\n        \"PROGRAM_LOOPS proven to never halt (cycle length \"\n        f\"{result_loops['cycle_length']} from step {result_loops['cycle_start']})\"\n    )\n\n    if halts_when is None or halts_when <= observation_steps:\n        print(\n            \"\\nNote: the proven halt step falls inside the observation \"\n            \"window, so the\\nquantum stage WILL show a difference here -- \"\n            \"that is expected and consistent,\\nnot a contradiction. The \"\n            \"interesting case is when the halt step exceeds the window.\"\n        )\n\n    program_halts = toy_result_to_program(\"PROGRAM_HALTS\", machine_halts, result_halts)\n    program_loops = toy_result_to_program(\"PROGRAM_LOOPS\", machine_loops, result_loops)\n\n    fork = PathOrderedFork()\n    branches = fork.branch_sequence(observation_steps)\n\n    result = compare_at_time(\n        fork,\n        program_halts,\n        program_loops,\n        branches,\n        observation_steps\n    )\n\n    print()\n    print_observable(\"PROGRAM_HALTS (as seen by the quantum stage)\", result[\"obs1\"])\n    print()\n    print_observable(\"PROGRAM_LOOPS (as seen by the quantum stage)\", result[\"obs2\"])\n    print()\n    print(f\"Matrix difference within window = {result['matrix_difference']:.15e}\")\n\n    print()\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\n    if result[\"matrix_difference\"] < 1e-9:\n        print(\n            \"As predicted: within this window the quantum observable cannot\\n\"\n            \"tell PROGRAM_HALTS and PROGRAM_LOOPS apart, even though\\n\"\n            \"decide_halting() already proved -- with certainty, from the toy\\n\"\n            \"machine's finite configuration space -- that one halts and the\\n\"\n            \"other does not. The certainty comes entirely from the toy\\n\"\n            \"decider; the quantum stage supplies zero information about it.\"\n        )\n    else:\n        print(\n            \"The quantum observable differs within this window because the\\n\"\n            \"proven halt step of PROGRAM_HALTS falls at or before the\\n\"\n            \"observation window -- consistent with the identity in section 4,\\n\"\n            \"not a case where the quantum math itself detected anything about\\n\"\n            \"the future.\"\n        )\n\n\ndef main():\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=(\n            \"Simulate path-ordered computational evolution, test \"\n            \"finite-time halting distinguishability, and separately run \"\n            \"a genuine decidable toy halting-problem demo, then integrate \"\n            \"the two.\"\n        )\n    )"}, "message": "Editing file", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "edit", "context": null, "display_content": null, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:28.883782Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:28.883812Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01NEZkFamxE5LRMWkwD8A1uE", "name": "str_replace", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "edit", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:30.434054Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:32.665765Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_013bLYmk8nyeVbtpEmzZtnAa", "name": "str_replace", "input": {"path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py", "old_str": "    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--skip-toy\",\n        action=\"store_true\",\n        help=\"Skip the toy halting-problem demo and only run the quantum simulation.\"\n    )\n\n    args = parser.parse_args()", "new_str": "    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--skip-toy\",\n        action=\"store_true\",\n        help=\"Skip the toy halting-problem demo and only run the quantum simulation.\"\n    )\n\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--skip-integration\",\n        action=\"store_true\",\n        help=\"Skip the integrated demo that feeds a proven toy result into the quantum stage.\"\n    )\n\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--integration-window\",\n        type=int,\n        default=50,\n        help=\"Observation window (in steps) used by the integrated demo.\"\n    )\n\n    args = parser.parse_args()"}, "message": "Editing file", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "edit", "context": null, "display_content": null, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:32.895032Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:32.895063Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_013bLYmk8nyeVbtpEmzZtnAa", "name": "str_replace", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "edit", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:34.205265Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:35.256955Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01B7fzWtJYLiN51cvdvubTnS", "name": "str_replace", "input": {"path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py", "old_str": "    if not args.skip_toy:\n        run_toy_halting_demo()", "new_str": "    if not args.skip_toy:\n        run_toy_halting_demo()\n\n    if not args.skip_integration:\n        run_integrated_demo(observation_steps=args.integration_window)"}, "message": "Editing file", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "edit", "context": null, "display_content": null, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:35.613273Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:35.613301Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01B7fzWtJYLiN51cvdvubTnS", "name": "str_replace", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "edit", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:37.116057Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:37.545002Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Now let's verify it runs correctly:", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:37.545096Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:38.046213Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01V1Li1DLfnUjX2LUmhfmvQE", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "cd /home/claude && python3 /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py --skip-quantum --skip-toy", "description": "Run just the integration demo to verify correctness"}, "message": "Run just the integration demo to verify correctness", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cd /home/claude && python3 /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py --skip-quantum --skip-toy\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:38.446362Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:38.446415Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01V1Li1DLfnUjX2LUmhfmvQE", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"\\n==============================================================================\\nINTEGRATION: PROVEN TOY RESULTS FED INTO THE QUANTUM SIMULATION\\n==============================================================================\\n\\nGround truth below comes ONLY from decide_halting() (proven, finite-state).\\nThe quantum section is not asked to decide anything -- it is only asked whether\\nits finite-time observable can tell the two proven cases apart within the window.\\n\\nTOY MACHINE (will halt)\\n  Registers x range   : 1 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 72 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 22\\n  DECISION            : NEVER HALTS  (proven -- enters a cycle of length 3 starting at step 19)\\n\\nTOY MACHINE (proven infinite loop)\\n  Registers x range   : 1 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 16 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 15\\n  DECISION            : NEVER HALTS  (proven -- enters a cycle of length 2 starting at step 13)\\n\\nObservation window for quantum stage : 50 steps\\nProven halt step of PROGRAM_HALTS    : None\\nPROGRAM_LOOPS proven to never halt (cycle length 2 from step 13)\\n\\nNote: the proven halt step falls inside the observation window, so the\\nquantum stage WILL show a difference here -- that is expected and consistent,\\nnot a contradiction. The interesting case is when the halt step exceeds the window.\\n\\nPROGRAM_HALTS (as seen by the quantum stage)\\n  HALT signal       = 0.000000000000000\\n  |Tr(U)/d|        = 0.589635358931294\\n  arg(Tr(U)/d)     = 0.000000000000000\\n  Tr real          = 0.589635358931294\\n  Tr imaginary     = 0.000000000000000\\n\\nPROGRAM_LOOPS (as seen by the quantum stage)\\n  HALT signal       = 0.000000000000000\\n  |Tr(U)/d|        = 0.589635358931294\\n  arg(Tr(U)/d)     = 0.000000000000000\\n  Tr real          = 0.589635358931294\\n  Tr imaginary     = 0.000000000000000\\n\\nMatrix difference within window = 0.000000000000000e+00\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nAs predicted: within this window the quantum observable cannot\\ntell PROGRAM_HALTS and PROGRAM_LOOPS apart, even though\\ndecide_halting() already proved -- with certainty, from the toy\\nmachine's finite configuration space -- that one halts and the\\nother does not. The certainty comes entirely from the toy\\ndecider; the quantum stage supplies zero information about it.\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"\\n==============================================================================\\nINTEGRATION: PROVEN TOY RESULTS FED INTO THE QUANTUM SIMULATION\\n==============================================================================\\n\\nGround truth below comes ONLY from decide_halting() (proven, finite-state).\\nThe quantum section is not asked to decide anything -- it is only asked whether\\nits finite-time observable can tell the two proven cases apart within the window.\\n\\nTOY MACHINE (will halt)\\n  Registers x range   : 1 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 72 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 22\\n  DECISION            : NEVER HALTS  (proven -- enters a cycle of length 3 starting at step 19)\\n\\nTOY MACHINE (proven infinite loop)\\n  Registers x range   : 1 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 16 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 15\\n  DECISION            : NEVER HALTS  (proven -- enters a cycle of length 2 starting at step 13)\\n\\nObservation window for quantum stage : 50 steps\\nProven halt step of PROGRAM_HALTS    : None\\nPROGRAM_LOOPS proven to never halt (cycle length 2 from step 13)\\n\\nNote: the proven halt step falls inside the observation window, so the\\nquantum stage WILL show a difference here -- that is expected and consistent,\\nnot a contradiction. The interesting case is when the halt step exceeds the window.\\n\\nPROGRAM_HALTS (as seen by the quantum stage)\\n  HALT signal       = 0.000000000000000\\n  |Tr(U)/d|        = 0.589635358931294\\n  arg(Tr(U)/d)     = 0.000000000000000\\n  Tr real          = 0.589635358931294\\n  Tr imaginary     = 0.000000000000000\\n\\nPROGRAM_LOOPS (as seen by the quantum stage)\\n  HALT signal       = 0.000000000000000\\n  |Tr(U)/d|        = 0.589635358931294\\n  arg(Tr(U)/d)     = 0.000000000000000\\n  Tr real          = 0.589635358931294\\n  Tr imaginary     = 0.000000000000000\\n\\nMatrix difference within window = 0.000000000000000e+00\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nAs predicted: within this window the quantum observable cannot\\ntell PROGRAM_HALTS and PROGRAM_LOOPS apart, even though\\ndecide_halting() already proved -- with certainty, from the toy\\nmachine's finite configuration space -- that one halts and the\\nother does not. The certainty comes entirely from the toy\\ndecider; the quantum stage supplies zero information about it.\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:40.388514Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:43.094059Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Debugging a program with a register-counting flaw."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:43.094114Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:44.274190Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Good catch needed \u2014 my \"slow halting\" toy program actually had a bug (wrong jump target), so it looped forever instead of halting late. Since the decider is *proving* things, a real proof is only useful if the program is actually correct. Let me fix it:", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:44.274221Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:46.933884Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_013CUrmTkKShd6wVKUyrELjZ", "name": "str_replace", "input": {"path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py", "old_str": "    slow_halting_program = [\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),  # r0 = 5\n        Instruction(\"JZ\", reg=0, target=6),\n        Instruction(\"DEC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"JMP\", target=5),\n        Instruction(\"HALT\"),\n    ]", "new_str": "    slow_halting_program = [\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"INC\", reg=0),  # r0 = 5\n        Instruction(\"JZ\", reg=0, target=8),  # loop: while r0 != 0, decrement\n        Instruction(\"DEC\", reg=0),\n        Instruction(\"JMP\", target=5),\n        Instruction(\"HALT\"),\n    ]"}, "message": "Editing file", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "edit", "context": null, "display_content": null, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:47.459064Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:47.459096Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_013CUrmTkKShd6wVKUyrELjZ", "name": "str_replace", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "edit", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:48.793639Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:49.223834Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Now let's re-verify:", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:49.223920Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:50.645806Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01Uzz1WM8WaSWbGJ2pNpnU6q", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "cd /home/claude && python3 /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py --skip-quantum --skip-toy && echo \"---SECOND RUN, window=3 (below proven halt step)---\" && python3 /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py --skip-quantum --skip-toy --integration-window 3", "description": "Verify the fixed integration demo, and also test a window smaller than the halt step"}, "message": "Verify the fixed integration demo, and also test a window smaller than the halt step", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cd /home/claude && python3 /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py --skip-quantum --skip-toy && echo \\\"---SECOND RUN, window=3 (below proven halt step)---\\\" && python3 /mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py --skip-quantum --skip-toy --integration-window 3\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:51.217198Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:51.217252Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Uzz1WM8WaSWbGJ2pNpnU6q", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"\\n==============================================================================\\nINTEGRATION: PROVEN TOY RESULTS FED INTO THE QUANTUM SIMULATION\\n==============================================================================\\n\\nGround truth below comes ONLY from decide_halting() (proven, finite-state).\\nThe quantum section is not asked to decide anything -- it is only asked whether\\nits finite-time observable can tell the two proven cases apart within the window.\\n\\nTOY MACHINE (will halt)\\n  Registers x range   : 1 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 72 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 22\\n  DECISION            : HALTS  (in 21 steps)\\n\\nTOY MACHINE (proven infinite loop)\\n  Registers x range   : 1 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 16 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 15\\n  DECISION            : NEVER HALTS  (proven -- enters a cycle of length 2 starting at step 13)\\n\\nObservation window for quantum stage : 50 steps\\nProven halt step of PROGRAM_HALTS    : 21\\nPROGRAM_LOOPS proven to never halt (cycle length 2 from step 13)\\n\\nNote: the proven halt step falls inside the observation window, so the\\nquantum stage WILL show a difference here -- that is expected and consistent,\\nnot a contradiction. The interesting case is when the halt step exceeds the window.\\n\\nPROGRAM_HALTS (as seen by the quantum stage)\\n  HALT signal       = 0.517087631679419\\n  |Tr(U)/d|        = 0.432725795824453\\n  arg(Tr(U)/d)     = -0.000000000000000\\n  Tr real          = 0.432725795824453\\n  Tr imaginary     = -0.000000000000000\\n\\nPROGRAM_LOOPS (as seen by the quantum stage)\\n  HALT signal       = 0.000000000000000\\n  |Tr(U)/d|        = 0.589635358931294\\n  arg(Tr(U)/d)     = 0.000000000000000\\n  Tr real          = 0.589635358931294\\n  Tr imaginary     = 0.000000000000000\\n\\nMatrix difference within window = 1.230268315009918e+00\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nThe quantum observable differs within this window because the\\nproven halt step of PROGRAM_HALTS falls at or before the\\nobservation window -- consistent with the identity in section 4,\\nnot a case where the quantum math itself detected anything about\\nthe future.\\n---SECOND RUN, window=3 (below proven halt step)---\\n\\n==============================================================================\\nINTEGRATION: PROVEN TOY RESULTS FED INTO THE QUANTUM SIMULATION\\n==============================================================================\\n\\nGround truth below comes ONLY from decide_halting() (proven, finite-state).\\nThe quantum section is not asked to decide anything -- it is only asked whether\\nits finite-time observable can tell the two proven cases apart within the window.\\n\\nTOY MACHINE (will halt)\\n  Registers x range   : 1 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 72 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 22\\n  DECISION            : HALTS  (in 21 steps)\\n\\nTOY MACHINE (proven infinite loop)\\n  Registers x range   : 1 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 16 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 15\\n  DECISION            : NEVER HALTS  (proven -- enters a cycle of length 2 starting at step 13)\\n\\nObservation window for quantum stage : 3 steps\\nProven halt step of PROGRAM_HALTS    : 21\\nPROGRAM_LOOPS proven to never halt (cycle length 2 from step 13)\\n\\nPROGRAM_HALTS (as seen by the quantum stage)\\n  HALT signal       = 0.000000000000000\\n  |Tr(U)/d|        = 0.649090854603432\\n  arg(Tr(U)/d)     = -0.000000000000000\\n  Tr real          = 0.649090854603432\\n  Tr imaginary     = -0.000000000000000\\n\\nPROGRAM_LOOPS (as seen by the quantum stage)\\n  HALT signal       = 0.000000000000000\\n  |Tr(U)/d|        = 0.649090854603432\\n  arg(Tr(U)/d)     = -0.000000000000000\\n  Tr real          = 0.649090854603432\\n  Tr imaginary     = -0.000000000000000\\n\\nMatrix difference within window = 0.000000000000000e+00\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nAs predicted: within this window the quantum observable cannot\\ntell PROGRAM_HALTS and PROGRAM_LOOPS apart, even though\\ndecide_halting() already proved -- with certainty, from the toy\\nmachine's finite configuration space -- that one halts and the\\nother does not. The certainty comes entirely from the toy\\ndecider; the quantum stage supplies zero information about it.\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"\\n==============================================================================\\nINTEGRATION: PROVEN TOY RESULTS FED INTO THE QUANTUM SIMULATION\\n==============================================================================\\n\\nGround truth below comes ONLY from decide_halting() (proven, finite-state).\\nThe quantum section is not asked to decide anything -- it is only asked whether\\nits finite-time observable can tell the two proven cases apart within the window.\\n\\nTOY MACHINE (will halt)\\n  Registers x range   : 1 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 72 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 22\\n  DECISION            : HALTS  (in 21 steps)\\n\\nTOY MACHINE (proven infinite loop)\\n  Registers x range   : 1 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 16 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 15\\n  DECISION            : NEVER HALTS  (proven -- enters a cycle of length 2 starting at step 13)\\n\\nObservation window for quantum stage : 50 steps\\nProven halt step of PROGRAM_HALTS    : 21\\nPROGRAM_LOOPS proven to never halt (cycle length 2 from step 13)\\n\\nNote: the proven halt step falls inside the observation window, so the\\nquantum stage WILL show a difference here -- that is expected and consistent,\\nnot a contradiction. The interesting case is when the halt step exceeds the window.\\n\\nPROGRAM_HALTS (as seen by the quantum stage)\\n  HALT signal       = 0.517087631679419\\n  |Tr(U)/d|        = 0.432725795824453\\n  arg(Tr(U)/d)     = -0.000000000000000\\n  Tr real          = 0.432725795824453\\n  Tr imaginary     = -0.000000000000000\\n\\nPROGRAM_LOOPS (as seen by the quantum stage)\\n  HALT signal       = 0.000000000000000\\n  |Tr(U)/d|        = 0.589635358931294\\n  arg(Tr(U)/d)     = 0.000000000000000\\n  Tr real          = 0.589635358931294\\n  Tr imaginary     = 0.000000000000000\\n\\nMatrix difference within window = 1.230268315009918e+00\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nThe quantum observable differs within this window because the\\nproven halt step of PROGRAM_HALTS falls at or before the\\nobservation window -- consistent with the identity in section 4,\\nnot a case where the quantum math itself detected anything about\\nthe future.\\n---SECOND RUN, window=3 (below proven halt step)---\\n\\n==============================================================================\\nINTEGRATION: PROVEN TOY RESULTS FED INTO THE QUANTUM SIMULATION\\n==============================================================================\\n\\nGround truth below comes ONLY from decide_halting() (proven, finite-state).\\nThe quantum section is not asked to decide anything -- it is only asked whether\\nits finite-time observable can tell the two proven cases apart within the window.\\n\\nTOY MACHINE (will halt)\\n  Registers x range   : 1 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 72 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 22\\n  DECISION            : HALTS  (in 21 steps)\\n\\nTOY MACHINE (proven infinite loop)\\n  Registers x range   : 1 x [0, 7]\\n  Total state space   : 16 configurations\\n  Configurations seen : 15\\n  DECISION            : NEVER HALTS  (proven -- enters a cycle of length 2 starting at step 13)\\n\\nObservation window for quantum stage : 3 steps\\nProven halt step of PROGRAM_HALTS    : 21\\nPROGRAM_LOOPS proven to never halt (cycle length 2 from step 13)\\n\\nPROGRAM_HALTS (as seen by the quantum stage)\\n  HALT signal       = 0.000000000000000\\n  |Tr(U)/d|        = 0.649090854603432\\n  arg(Tr(U)/d)     = -0.000000000000000\\n  Tr real          = 0.649090854603432\\n  Tr imaginary     = -0.000000000000000\\n\\nPROGRAM_LOOPS (as seen by the quantum stage)\\n  HALT signal       = 0.000000000000000\\n  |Tr(U)/d|        = 0.649090854603432\\n  arg(Tr(U)/d)     = -0.000000000000000\\n  Tr real          = 0.649090854603432\\n  Tr imaginary     = -0.000000000000000\\n\\nMatrix difference within window = 0.000000000000000e+00\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nAs predicted: within this window the quantum observable cannot\\ntell PROGRAM_HALTS and PROGRAM_LOOPS apart, even though\\ndecide_halting() already proved -- with certainty, from the toy\\nmachine's finite configuration space -- that one halts and the\\nother does not. The certainty comes entirely from the toy\\ndecider; the quantum stage supplies zero information about it.\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:52.524584Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:54.193157Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Both cases behave exactly as they should now: with a window (3) shorter than the proven halt step (21), the quantum observable can't distinguish the two programs \u2014 `matrix_difference = 0.0` \u2014 even though `decide_halting()` already knows the truth with certainty. With a longer window (50, past step 21) they diverge, correctly, because the halting program has actually executed HALT inside that window.", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:54.193184Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:54.586341Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01CcEDLTiUNSs81Qqfcw4hRV", "name": "present_files", "input": {"filepaths": ["/mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py"]}, "message": "Presenting file(s)...", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "table", "table": [["filepaths", "['/mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py']"]]}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:54.603753Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:54.603774Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01CcEDLTiUNSs81Qqfcw4hRV", "name": "present_files", "content": [{"type": "local_resource", "file_path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py", "name": "halting demo", "mime_type": "text/x-python", "artifact_publishable": false}, {"type": "text", "text": "The file cards appear at the end of your reply if it ends with text, otherwise under this step; say they are below only if you finish the reply with text."}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": "Presented file", "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:55.795446Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:54:59.041031Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Updated file is above. What it does now:\n\n- **Quantum section**: untouched, same as your original.\n- **Toy section**: untouched, a genuine decidable halting decider for bounded register machines.\n- **New integration section**: uses `decide_halting()` to get a *proven* ground truth for two toy programs, then feeds that proven result into the quantum simulation as its `Program.halt_step`. It reports honestly which case you're in:\n  - Window shorter than the proven halt step \u2192 quantum observable shows zero difference, exactly as the math requires, while the toy decider still knows the true answer.\n  - Window longer than the proven halt step \u2192 quantum observable correctly diverges, because the program's HALT instruction has actually fired inside the window.\n\nRun with `--integration-window N` to try different window sizes, or `--skip-quantum --skip-toy` to see just the integration part.", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-26T12:54:59.112947Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T12:54:59.112947Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddc7-9f36-7ecd-b1e3-4e5417e7d9e3"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddc9-9844-7641-9174-360d11a63b31", "text": "make an intergrator to search for halts in codes", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:56:12.705781Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:56:12.705781Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "make an intergrator to search for halts in codes", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-26T12:56:12.705991Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T12:56:12.705991Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddc7-9f36-7ff6-a7c4-8f608419dd18"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddc9-9844-7dc8-bde3-4ec5cb8407be", "text": "A genuine version of this is possible \u2014 not \"detect halting for any code\" (still impossible, same theorem), but a real static analyzer that:\n\n- **actually runs** a restricted, safe subset of Python (no `eval`/`exec`, its own interpreter),\n- proves **HALTS** when it actually reaches `return`,\n- proves **LOOPS FOREVER** when the *exact* full machine state (program counter + operand stack + all variable values) repeats \u2014 sound for any deterministic program, not just pre-declared bounded ones,\n- and honestly reports **UNDETERMINED** when neither happens within a step budget (e.g. a variable growing without bound will never repeat state, so it correctly refuses to guess).\n\nThat's a real three-way decision procedure, not a fake two-way one. Let me build it.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nLet's verify it actually works correctly on all the demo cases:\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n`collatz_like` reporting UNDETERMINED at a 2000-step budget is actually a good demonstration \u2014 it should genuinely halt for these inputs, just needs more steps. Let me confirm that with a bigger budget, showing the \"UNDETERMINED\" label really means \"budget ran out,\" not \"secretly wrong\":\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nConfirmed: with a bigger budget it correctly proves `HALTS`, matching the real Collatz sequence lengths for 27 and 97 (111 and 118 steps respectively, well-known values). The tool doesn't fake or guess \u2014 the earlier `UNDETERMINED` at 2000 steps was honestly \"not enough budget yet,\" and raising the budget reveals the true, provable answer.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nThat's the correct, honest behavior too \u2014 `unbounded_but_wraps()` really does loop forever, but with a bound at 1,000,000 it needs a step budget that large to actually witness the exact repeat and prove it (correctly `UNDETERMINED` rather than a false claim at only 5000 steps). Let's confirm it flips to `LOOPS` with enough budget:\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nRight \u2014 the cycle period here is roughly 1,000,000 iterations \u00d7 several VM instructions each, so 3 million steps wasn't enough. Let's use a budget that actually covers one full cycle:\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nConfirmed \u2014 with enough budget to actually traverse the full cycle, it correctly proves `LOOPS` with the exact cycle length (11,000,014 steps, consistent with the ~1,000,001-iteration period at ~11 VM instructions per iteration). This is a real, load-bearing tool: every claim it makes is backed by an actual completed run or an actual repeated state, never a guess dressed up as one.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n**Usage:**\n- `python3 code_halt_search.py --demo` \u2014 built-in examples showing all three outcomes\n- `python3 code_halt_search.py yourfile.py` \u2014 analyzes every parameterless function found\n- `python3 code_halt_search.py yourfile.py --function foo --args 1 2 --step-budget 500000` \u2014 analyze one function on specific inputs\n\n**What makes each verdict trustworthy:**\n- `HALTS` \u2014 only printed after actually executing to a real `return` (verified above with Collatz sequences matching known values).\n- `LOOPS` \u2014 only printed after the exact `(program counter, operand stack, all variables)` triple repeats. For a deterministic interpreter, that's not a heuristic \u2014 it's a proof the same steps repeat forever. Verified above on both a fast loop and a 1,000,000-iteration wraparound loop.\n- `UNDETERMINED` \u2014 printed whenever the budget runs out first. This can flip to a proven answer with more budget (shown above), or can be the honest final word for real open cases like unbounded growth or (if you removed the step cap) an actual Collatz-style program on an input nobody's proven halts.\n- `UNSUPPORTED` \u2014 anything outside the safe, deterministic subset (function calls, recursion, lists, for-loops, etc.) is rejected outright with a specific reason, never silently approximated.", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:56:12.698745Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:57:47.133650Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Exploring how to build a program that detects halting behavior."}, {"summary": "Designing a decidable procedure using state-cycle detection for loops."}, {"summary": "Flattening code structure into a linear interpretable sequence."}, {"summary": "Compiling a restricted syntax subset into stack-based instructions."}, {"summary": "Assembling the virtual machine that runs compiled instructions."}, {"summary": "Structuring per-function analysis with sample input arguments."}, {"summary": "Handling assignments, loops, and conditionals in the compiler."}, {"summary": "Translating expressions into stack instructions for supported operators."}, {"summary": "Adding boolean logic and building the core execution loop."}, {"summary": "Detecting cycles by checking state before instruction execution."}, {"summary": "Weighing whether the cycle state needs stack contents too."}, {"summary": "Including full stack contents to ensure state comparisons stay sound."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:57:47.133702Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:57:50.178877Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "A genuine version of this is possible \u2014 not \"detect halting for any code\" (still impossible, same theorem), but a real static analyzer that:\n\n- **actually runs** a restricted, safe subset of Python (no `eval`/`exec`, its own interpreter),\n- proves **HALTS** when it actually reaches `return`,\n- proves **LOOPS FOREVER** when the *exact* full machine state (program counter + operand stack + all variable values) repeats \u2014 sound for any deterministic program, not just pre-declared bounded ones,\n- and honestly reports **UNDETERMINED** when neither happens within a step budget (e.g. a variable growing without bound will never repeat state, so it correctly refuses to guess).\n\nThat's a real three-way decision procedure, not a fake two-way one. Let me build it.", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:57:50.178905Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:58:46.331425Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01TFvdSyaEcjRiogfFQmHSBT", "name": "create_file", "input": {"description": "A real halting-search integrator: compiles a restricted, safe subset of Python to a linear bytecode, runs it in a custom VM, and proves HALTS / proves LOOPS-forever via exact full-state repetition / honestly reports UNDETERMINED otherwise", "path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/code_halt_search.py", "file_text": "\"\"\"\ncode_halt_search.py\n====================\n\nAn integrator that searches real Python source code for halting behavior.\n\nWHAT THIS ACTUALLY DOES (and does not)\n---------------------------------------\nThis tool does NOT solve the halting problem. Nothing can, for arbitrary\ncode -- that is a theorem, not an engineering gap. What this tool DOES do\nis run a sound, honest, three-way decision procedure on a restricted, safe\nsubset of Python:\n\n    HALTS         -- proven, because execution actually reached `return`.\n    LOOPS FOREVER -- proven, because the EXACT full machine state (program\n                     counter + operand stack + every variable's value) was\n                     seen to repeat. Since execution here is deterministic,\n                     a repeated state guarantees the same sequence repeats\n                     forever -- this is a real proof, not a heuristic.\n    UNDETERMINED  -- the step budget ran out before either of the above\n                     happened. This is reported honestly, never silently\n                     upgraded to a guess. A variable that grows without\n                     bound (e.g. `x += 1` forever with no wraparound) will\n                     never repeat state and will always land here -- which\n                     is the correct, honest answer, not a failure of the\n                     tool.\n\nThis is the same style of technique real bounded model checkers and\nsymbolic execution tools use: a sound partial decision procedure, clearly\nlabeled as partial.\n\nSUPPORTED SUBSET (chosen because it keeps execution fully deterministic,\nside-effect free, and exactly reproducible from the state alone)\n-------------------------------------------------------------------\n  - Function definitions with plain integer/bool parameters\n  - Assignment to a single name:            x = expr\n  - Augmented assignment:                    x += expr, x -= expr, ...\n  - while loops, if/elif/else, break, continue, pass\n  - return (with or without a value)\n  - int/bool constants, arithmetic (+ - * // % **), comparisons,\n    boolean and/or, unary - and not\n\nNOT supported (rejected explicitly, never silently ignored):\n  - function calls (including recursion), classes, lists/dicts/strings,\n    for-loops, exceptions, global/nonlocal, I/O, floats\n  - Any unsupported construct raises UnsupportedConstruct with a message\n    naming exactly what wasn't understood, instead of pretending to\n    analyze it.\n\nThis restriction is not arbitrary conservatism: allowing function calls or\nunbounded data structures would reintroduce full Turing-completeness in a\nway that breaks the \"operand stack + variables\" state representation this\ntool's proof relies on. The restricted subset is itself Turing-complete in\nprinciple (unbounded integers + while loops), so genuinely undecidable\nprograms, and genuinely UNDETERMINED results, ARE still possible in it --\nthis tool searches for a proof, it does not manufacture one.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport ast\nimport argparse\nimport sys\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n\nclass UnsupportedConstruct(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Raised when the source uses a construct outside the supported subset.\"\"\"\n    pass\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# COMPILER: restricted-Python AST -> linear stack-machine instructions\n# =============================================================================\n\n@dataclass\nclass Instr:\n    op: str\n    arg: object = None\n\n\nclass Compiler:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.code: list[Instr] = []\n\n    def emit(self, op, arg=None):\n        self.code.append(Instr(op, arg))\n        return len(self.code) - 1\n\n    def patch(self, idx, target):\n        self.code[idx].arg = target\n\n    def compile_function(self, func_def: ast.FunctionDef):\n        for a in func_def.args.args:\n            pass  # names collected below\n        param_names = [a.arg for a in func_def.args.args]\n\n        if func_def.args.vararg or func_def.args.kwarg or func_def.args.kwonlyargs:\n            raise UnsupportedConstruct(\"*args/**kwargs/keyword-only args not supported\")\n        if func_def.decorator_list:\n            raise UnsupportedConstruct(\"decorators not supported\")\n\n        self.compile_block(func_def.body, loop_ctx=None)\n        # Implicit `return None` if control falls off the end.\n        self.emit(\"LOAD_CONST\", None)\n        self.emit(\"RETURN\")\n        return self.code, param_names\n\n    def compile_block(self, stmts, loop_ctx):\n        for stmt in stmts:\n            self.compile_stmt(stmt, loop_ctx)\n\n    def compile_stmt(self, stmt, loop_ctx):\n        if isinstance(stmt, ast.Assign):\n            if len(stmt.targets) != 1 or not isinstance(stmt.targets[0], ast.Name):\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(\"only single-name assignment (x = ...) is supported\")\n            self.compile_expr(stmt.value)\n            self.emit(\"STORE_VAR\", stmt.targets[0].id)\n\n        elif isinstance(stmt, ast.AugAssign):\n            if not isinstance(stmt.target, ast.Name):\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(\"only augmented assignment to a plain name is supported\")\n            name = stmt.target.id\n            self.emit(\"LOAD_VAR\", name)\n            self.compile_expr(stmt.value)\n            self.emit(\"BINOP\", type(stmt.op).__name__)\n            self.emit(\"STORE_VAR\", name)\n\n        elif isinstance(stmt, ast.While):\n            if stmt.orelse:\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(\"while/else not supported\")\n            loop_start = len(self.code)\n            self.compile_expr(stmt.test)\n            jf = self.emit(\"JUMP_IF_FALSE\", None)\n            inner_ctx = {\"breaks\": [], \"continue_target\": loop_start}\n            self.compile_block(stmt.body, inner_ctx)\n            self.emit(\"JUMP\", loop_start)\n            end = len(self.code)\n            self.patch(jf, end)\n            for b in inner_ctx[\"breaks\"]:\n                self.patch(b, end)\n\n        elif isinstance(stmt, ast.If):\n            self.compile_expr(stmt.test)\n            jf = self.emit(\"JUMP_IF_FALSE\", None)\n            self.compile_block(stmt.body, loop_ctx)\n            jend = self.emit(\"JUMP\", None)\n            else_start = len(self.code)\n            self.patch(jf, else_start)\n            self.compile_block(stmt.orelse, loop_ctx)\n            end = len(self.code)\n            self.patch(jend, end)\n\n        elif isinstance(stmt, ast.Return):\n            if stmt.value is not None:\n                self.compile_expr(stmt.value)\n            else:\n                self.emit(\"LOAD_CONST\", None)\n            self.emit(\"RETURN\")\n\n        elif isinstance(stmt, ast.Break):\n            if loop_ctx is None:\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(\"break outside a loop\")\n            idx = self.emit(\"JUMP\", None)\n            loop_ctx[\"breaks\"].append(idx)\n\n        elif isinstance(stmt, ast.Continue):\n            if loop_ctx is None:\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(\"continue outside a loop\")\n            self.emit(\"JUMP\", loop_ctx[\"continue_target\"])\n\n        elif isinstance(stmt, ast.Pass):\n            pass\n\n        elif isinstance(stmt, ast.Expr) and isinstance(stmt.value, ast.Constant):\n            pass  # a bare docstring/constant; harmless, no effect\n\n        else:\n            raise UnsupportedConstruct(f\"unsupported statement type: {type(stmt).__name__}\")\n\n    def compile_expr(self, expr):\n        if isinstance(expr, ast.Constant):\n            if not isinstance(expr.value, (int, bool)):\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(\n                    f\"only int/bool constants are supported, got {type(expr.value).__name__}\"\n                )\n            self.emit(\"LOAD_CONST\", expr.value)\n\n        elif isinstance(expr, ast.Name):\n            self.emit(\"LOAD_VAR\", expr.id)\n\n        elif isinstance(expr, ast.BinOp):\n            if type(expr.op).__name__ not in VM.BIN_OPS:\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(f\"unsupported binary operator: {type(expr.op).__name__}\")\n            self.compile_expr(expr.left)\n            self.compile_expr(expr.right)\n            self.emit(\"BINOP\", type(expr.op).__name__)\n\n        elif isinstance(expr, ast.UnaryOp):\n            if type(expr.op).__name__ not in VM.UNARY_OPS:\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(f\"unsupported unary operator: {type(expr.op).__name__}\")\n            self.compile_expr(expr.operand)\n            self.emit(\"UNARYOP\", type(expr.op).__name__)\n\n        elif isinstance(expr, ast.Compare):\n            if len(expr.ops) != 1 or len(expr.comparators) != 1:\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(\"chained comparisons (a < b < c) are not supported\")\n            if type(expr.ops[0]).__name__ not in VM.CMP_OPS:\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(f\"unsupported comparison: {type(expr.ops[0]).__name__}\")\n            self.compile_expr(expr.left)\n            self.compile_expr(expr.comparators[0])\n            self.emit(\"COMPARE\", type(expr.ops[0]).__name__)\n\n        elif isinstance(expr, ast.BoolOp):\n            op_name = type(expr.op).__name__\n            self.compile_expr(expr.values[0])\n            for v in expr.values[1:]:\n                self.compile_expr(v)\n                self.emit(\"BOOLOP\", op_name)\n\n        else:\n            raise UnsupportedConstruct(f\"unsupported expression type: {type(expr).__name__}\")\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# VM: executes the compiled instructions, proving HALTS / LOOPS / UNDETERMINED\n# =============================================================================\n\nclass VM:\n    BIN_OPS = {\n        \"Add\": lambda a, b: a + b,\n        \"Sub\": lambda a, b: a - b,\n        \"Mult\": lambda a, b: a * b,\n        \"FloorDiv\": lambda a, b: a // b,\n        \"Mod\": lambda a, b: a % b,\n        \"Pow\": lambda a, b: a ** b,\n    }\n    CMP_OPS = {\n        \"Eq\": lambda a, b: a == b,\n        \"NotEq\": lambda a, b: a != b,\n        \"Lt\": lambda a, b: a < b,\n        \"LtE\": lambda a, b: a <= b,\n        \"Gt\": lambda a, b: a > b,\n        \"GtE\": lambda a, b: a >= b,\n    }\n    BOOL_OPS = {\n        \"And\": lambda a, b: bool(a) and bool(b),\n        \"Or\": lambda a, b: bool(a) or bool(b),\n    }\n    UNARY_OPS = {\n        \"USub\": lambda a: -a,\n        \"Not\": lambda a: not a,\n    }\n\n    def __init__(self, code, initial_vars, step_budget):\n        self.code = code\n        self.vars = dict(initial_vars)\n        self.step_budget = step_budget\n\n    def run(self):\n        pc = 0\n        stack = []\n        seen = {}\n        step = 0\n\n        while True:\n            # Full machine state = everything the future of execution can\n            # possibly depend on. If this exact triple repeats, execution\n            # is deterministic, so it MUST repeat forever -- a real proof.\n            state = (pc, tuple(stack), tuple(sorted(self.vars.items())))\n\n            if state in seen:\n                return {\n                    \"result\": \"LOOPS\",\n                    \"cycle_start\": seen[state],\n                    \"cycle_length\": step - seen[state],\n                    \"steps\": step,\n                    \"configs_seen\": len(seen),\n                }\n\n            seen[state] = step\n\n            if step >= self.step_budget:\n                return {\n                    \"result\": \"UNDETERMINED\",\n                    \"steps\": step,\n                    \"configs_seen\": len(seen),\n                }\n\n            instr = self.code[pc]\n            op = instr.op\n\n            if op == \"LOAD_CONST\":\n                stack.append(instr.arg)\n                pc += 1\n            elif op == \"LOAD_VAR\":\n                if instr.arg not in self.vars:\n                    raise UnsupportedConstruct(f\"use of undefined variable '{instr.arg}'\")\n                stack.append(self.vars[instr.arg])\n                pc += 1\n            elif op == \"STORE_VAR\":\n                self.vars[instr.arg] = stack.pop()\n                pc += 1\n            elif op == \"BINOP\":\n                b = stack.pop()\n                a = stack.pop()\n                if instr.arg in (\"FloorDiv\", \"Mod\") and b == 0:\n                    raise UnsupportedConstruct(\"division by zero encountered during analysis\")\n                stack.append(self.BIN_OPS[instr.arg](a, b))\n                pc += 1\n            elif op == \"UNARYOP\":\n                a = stack.pop()\n                stack.append(self.UNARY_OPS[instr.arg](a))\n                pc += 1\n            elif op == \"COMPARE\":\n                b = stack.pop()\n                a = stack.pop()\n                stack.append(self.CMP_OPS[instr.arg](a, b))\n                pc += 1\n            elif op == \"BOOLOP\":\n                b = stack.pop()\n                a = stack.pop()\n                stack.append(self.BOOL_OPS[instr.arg](a, b))\n                pc += 1\n            elif op == \"JUMP\":\n                pc = instr.arg\n            elif op == \"JUMP_IF_FALSE\":\n                v = stack.pop()\n                pc = instr.arg if not v else pc + 1\n            elif op == \"RETURN\":\n                v = stack.pop()\n                return {\n                    \"result\": \"HALTS\",\n                    \"steps\": step,\n                    \"return_value\": v,\n                    \"configs_seen\": len(seen),\n                }\n            else:\n                raise RuntimeError(f\"internal error: unknown instruction {op}\")\n\n            step += 1\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# TOP-LEVEL INTEGRATOR\n# =============================================================================\n\ndef analyze_function(func_def: ast.FunctionDef, args, step_budget=200_000):\n    \"\"\"\n    Analyze one function on one concrete set of integer/bool arguments.\n\n    This decides halting for THIS input only. Deciding halting for every\n    possible input to a function is, in general, exactly the undecidable\n    halting problem again -- restricting to int/bool arguments does not\n    change that, since the loop bodies can still do unbounded arithmetic.\n    What's decidable per-run is whether THIS run halts, loops, or is\n    undetermined within the budget -- which is what this function reports.\n    \"\"\"\n    compiler = Compiler()\n    code, param_names = compiler.compile_function(func_def)\n\n    if len(args) != len(param_names):\n        raise ValueError(\n            f\"{func_def.name} expects {len(param_names)} argument(s) \"\n            f\"({', '.join(param_names) or 'none'}), got {len(args)}\"\n        )\n\n    initial_vars = dict(zip(param_names, args))\n    vm = VM(code, initial_vars, step_budget)\n    return vm.run()\n\n\ndef search_halts_in_source(source, args_map=None, step_budget=200_000):\n    \"\"\"\n    Parse a source file, find every top-level function definition, and\n    analyze each one. Functions that take parameters are only analyzed for\n    concrete inputs supplied in args_map (a dict: function name -> list of\n    argument tuples); without an entry there, they're reported as SKIPPED\n    with an explanation, never silently assumed.\n    \"\"\"\n    tree = ast.parse(source)\n    args_map = args_map or {}\n    results = {}\n\n    for node in tree.body:\n        if not isinstance(node, ast.FunctionDef):\n            continue\n\n        param_count = len(node.args.args)\n\n        if param_count == 0:\n            key = f\"{node.name}()\"\n            try:\n                results[key] = analyze_function(node, [], step_budget)\n            except UnsupportedConstruct as e:\n                results[key] = {\"result\": \"UNSUPPORTED\", \"reason\": str(e)}\n            continue\n\n        if node.name not in args_map:\n            results[f\"{node.name}(...)\"] = {\n                \"result\": \"SKIPPED\",\n                \"reason\": (\n                    f\"takes {param_count} argument(s); supply concrete \"\n                    f\"values via args_map/--args to analyze a specific call\"\n                ),\n            }\n            continue\n\n        for args in args_map[node.name]:\n            key = f\"{node.name}{tuple(args)}\"\n            try:\n                results[key] = analyze_function(node, list(args), step_budget)\n            except UnsupportedConstruct as e:\n                results[key] = {\"result\": \"UNSUPPORTED\", \"reason\": str(e)}\n\n    return results\n\n\ndef print_result(name, result):\n    r = result[\"result\"]\n    if r == \"HALTS\":\n        print(f\"  {name:28s} HALTS       (proven, {result['steps']} steps, \"\n              f\"returns {result['return_value']!r})\")\n    elif r == \"LOOPS\":\n        print(f\"  {name:28s} LOOPS       (proven, exact state repeats every \"\n              f\"{result['cycle_length']} steps starting at step {result['cycle_start']})\")\n    elif r == \"UNDETERMINED\":\n        print(f\"  {name:28s} UNDETERMINED  (ran {result['steps']} steps without \"\n              f\"halting or a proven cycle -- state kept growing; honestly unknown)\")\n    elif r == \"SKIPPED\":\n        print(f\"  {name:28s} SKIPPED     ({result['reason']})\")\n    elif r == \"UNSUPPORTED\":\n        print(f\"  {name:28s} UNSUPPORTED ({result['reason']})\")\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# BUILT-IN DEMO\n# =============================================================================\n\nDEMO_SOURCE = '''\ndef counts_down_and_halts():\n    x = 5\n    while x != 0:\n        x -= 1\n    return x\n\ndef infinite_loop():\n    x = 0\n    while True:\n        x += 1\n        x -= 1\n\ndef bounded_oscillation():\n    x = 0\n    y = 0\n    while True:\n        x += 1\n        y += 1\n        x = x % 4\n        if x == 0:\n            y = y % 4\n\ndef grows_without_bound():\n    x = 0\n    while x >= 0:\n        x += 1\n    return x\n\ndef collatz_like(n):\n    steps = 0\n    while n != 1:\n        if n % 2 == 0:\n            n = n // 2\n        else:\n            n = 3 * n + 1\n        steps += 1\n    return steps\n\ndef uses_recursion(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    return uses_recursion(n - 1)\n'''\n\n\ndef run_demo():\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\n    print(\"CODE HALT SEARCH -- DEMO ON BUILT-IN EXAMPLE FUNCTIONS\")\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\n    print()\n    print(\n        \"Three real outcomes: HALTS (proven by reaching return), LOOPS\\n\"\n        \"(proven by an exact repeated machine state), UNDETERMINED (budget\\n\"\n        \"ran out with neither -- reported honestly, not guessed).\"\n    )\n    print()\n\n    results = search_halts_in_source(\n        DEMO_SOURCE,\n        args_map={\n            \"collatz_like\": [(27,), (97,)],\n            \"uses_recursion\": [(5,)],\n        },\n        step_budget=2000,\n    )\n\n    for name, result in results.items():\n        print_result(name, result)\n\n    print()\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\n    print(\n        \"Notes:\\n\"\n        \"  - grows_without_bound is a genuine open case: it never halts and\\n\"\n        \"    never repeats state (x keeps climbing), so honestly reporting\\n\"\n        \"    UNDETERMINED -- not LOOPS -- is the mathematically correct\\n\"\n        \"    answer here, not a limitation of this run.\\n\"\n        \"  - uses_recursion is rejected outright as UNSUPPORTED, because\\n\"\n        \"    function calls fall outside the subset this tool can prove\\n\"\n        \"    things about -- it never silently pretends to analyze it.\"\n    )\n\n\ndef main():\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=\"Search real Python source for provable halting/looping behavior.\"\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\"file\", nargs=\"?\", help=\"Python file to analyze\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--function\", help=\"Only analyze this function\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--args\", nargs=\"*\", type=int, default=[], help=\"Integer arguments for --function\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--step-budget\", type=int, default=200_000)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--demo\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"Run the built-in demo instead of a file\")\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n\n    if args.demo or not args.file:\n        run_demo()\n        return\n\n    with open(args.file) as f:\n        source = f.read()\n\n    if args.function:\n        tree = ast.parse(source)\n        func_def = next(\n            (n for n in tree.body if isinstance(n, ast.FunctionDef) and n.name == args.function),\n            None,\n        )\n        if func_def is None:\n            print(f\"No function named '{args.function}' found in {args.file}\", file=sys.stderr)\n            sys.exit(1)\n        try:\n            result = analyze_function(func_def, args.args, args.step_budget)\n        except UnsupportedConstruct as e:\n            result = {\"result\": \"UNSUPPORTED\", \"reason\": str(e)}\n        print_result(f\"{args.function}{tuple(args.args)}\", result)\n    else:\n        results = search_halts_in_source(source, step_budget=args.step_budget)\n        print(f\"Analysis of {args.file}:\")\n        for name, result in results.items():\n            print_result(name, result)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n"}, "message": "A real halting-search integrator: compiles a restricted, safe subset of Python to a linear bytecode, runs it in a custom VM, and proves HALTS / proves LOOPS-forever via exact full-state repetition / \u2026", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"python\", \"code\": \"\\\"\\\"\\\"\\ncode_halt_search.py\\n====================\\n\\nAn integrator that searches real Python source code for halting behavior.\\n\\nWHAT THIS ACTUALLY DOES (and does not)\\n---------------------------------------\\nThis tool does NOT solve the halting problem. Nothing can, for arbitrary\\ncode -- that is a theorem, not an engineering gap. What this tool DOES do\\nis run a sound, honest, three-way decision procedure on a restricted, safe\\nsubset of Python:\\n\\n    HALTS         -- proven, because execution actually reached `return`.\\n    LOOPS FOREVER -- proven, because the EXACT full machine state (program\\n                     counter + operand stack + every variable's value) was\\n                     seen to repeat. Since execution here is deterministic,\\n                     a repeated state guarantees the same sequence repeats\\n                     forever -- this is a real proof, not a heuristic.\\n    UNDETERMINED  -- the step budget ran out before either of the above\\n                     happened. This is reported honestly, never silently\\n                     upgraded to a guess. A variable that grows without\\n                     bound (e.g. `x += 1` forever with no wraparound) will\\n                     never repeat state and will always land here -- which\\n                     is the correct, honest answer, not a failure of the\\n                     tool.\\n\\nThis is the same style of technique real bounded model checkers and\\nsymbolic execution tools use: a sound partial decision procedure, clearly\\nlabeled as partial.\\n\\nSUPPORTED SUBSET (chosen because it keeps execution fully deterministic,\\nside-effect free, and exactly reproducible from the state alone)\\n-------------------------------------------------------------------\\n  - Function definitions with plain integer/bool parameters\\n  - Assignment to a single name:            x = expr\\n  - Augmented assignment:                    x += expr, x -= expr, ...\\n  - while loops, if/elif/else, break, continue, pass\\n  - return (with or without a value)\\n  - int/bool constants, arithmetic (+ - * // % **), comparisons,\\n    boolean and/or, unary - and not\\n\\nNOT supported (rejected explicitly, never silently ignored):\\n  - function calls (including recursion), classes, lists/dicts/strings,\\n    for-loops, exceptions, global/nonlocal, I/O, floats\\n  - Any unsupported construct raises UnsupportedConstruct with a message\\n    naming exactly what wasn't understood, instead of pretending to\\n    analyze it.\\n\\nThis restriction is not arbitrary conservatism: allowing function calls or\\nunbounded data structures would reintroduce full Turing-completeness in a\\nway that breaks the \\\"operand stack + variables\\\" state representation this\\ntool's proof relies on. The restricted subset is itself Turing-complete in\\nprinciple (unbounded integers + while loops), so genuinely undecidable\\nprograms, and genuinely UNDETERMINED results, ARE still possible in it --\\nthis tool searches for a proof, it does not manufacture one.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\nimport ast\\nimport argparse\\nimport sys\\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\\n\\n\\nclass UnsupportedConstruct(Exception):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Raised when the source uses a construct outside the supported subset.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    pass\\n\\n\\n# =============================================================================\\n# COMPILER: restricted-Python AST -> linear stack-machine instructions\\n# =============================================================================\\n\\n@dataclass\\nclass Instr:\\n    op: str\\n    arg: object = None\\n\\n\\nclass Compiler:\\n    def __init__(self):\\n        self.code: list[Instr] = []\\n\\n    def emit(self, op, arg=None):\\n        self.code.append(Instr(op, arg))\\n        return len(self.code) - 1\\n\\n    def patch(self, idx, target):\\n        self.code[idx].arg = target\\n\\n    def compile_function(self, func_def: ast.FunctionDef):\\n        for a in func_def.args.args:\\n            pass  # names collected below\\n        param_names = [a.arg for a in func_def.args.args]\\n\\n        if func_def.args.vararg or func_def.args.kwarg or func_def.args.kwonlyargs:\\n            raise UnsupportedConstruct(\\\"*args/**kwargs/keyword-only args not supported\\\")\\n        if func_def.decorator_list:\\n            raise UnsupportedConstruct(\\\"decorators not supported\\\")\\n\\n        self.compile_block(func_def.body, loop_ctx=None)\\n        # Implicit `return None` if control falls off the end.\\n        self.emit(\\\"LOAD_CONST\\\", None)\\n        self.emit(\\\"RETURN\\\")\\n        return self.code, param_names\\n\\n    def compile_block(self, stmts, loop_ctx):\\n        for stmt in stmts:\\n            self.compile_stmt(stmt, loop_ctx)\\n\\n    def compile_stmt(self, stmt, loop_ctx):\\n        if isinstance(stmt, ast.Assign):\\n            if len(stmt.targets) != 1 or not isinstance(stmt.targets[0], ast.Name):\\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(\\\"only single-name assignment (x = ...) is supported\\\")\\n            self.compile_expr(stmt.value)\\n            self.emit(\\\"STORE_VAR\\\", stmt.targets[0].id)\\n\\n        elif isinstance(stmt, ast.AugAssign):\\n            if not isinstance(stmt.target, ast.Name):\\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(\\\"only augmented assignment to a plain name is supported\\\")\\n            name = stmt.target.id\\n            self.emit(\\\"LOAD_VAR\\\", name)\\n            self.compile_expr(stmt.value)\\n            self.emit(\\\"BINOP\\\", type(stmt.op).__name__)\\n            self.emit(\\\"STORE_VAR\\\", name)\\n\\n        elif isinstance(stmt, ast.While):\\n            if stmt.orelse:\\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(\\\"while/else not supported\\\")\\n            loop_start = len(self.code)\\n            self.compile_expr(stmt.test)\\n            jf = self.emit(\\\"JUMP_IF_FALSE\\\", None)\\n            inner_ctx = {\\\"breaks\\\": [], \\\"continue_target\\\": loop_start}\\n            self.compile_block(stmt.body, inner_ctx)\\n            self.emit(\\\"JUMP\\\", loop_start)\\n            end = len(self.code)\\n            self.patch(jf, end)\\n            for b in inner_ctx[\\\"breaks\\\"]:\\n                self.patch(b, end)\\n\\n        elif isinstance(stmt, ast.If):\\n            self.compile_expr(stmt.test)\\n            jf = self.emit(\\\"JUMP_IF_FALSE\\\", None)\\n            self.compile_block(stmt.body, loop_ctx)\\n            jend = self.emit(\\\"JUMP\\\", None)\\n            else_start = len(self.code)\\n            self.patch(jf, else_start)\\n            self.compile_block(stmt.orelse, loop_ctx)\\n            end = len(self.code)\\n            self.patch(jend, end)\\n\\n        elif isinstance(stmt, ast.Return):\\n            if stmt.value is not None:\\n                self.compile_expr(stmt.value)\\n            else:\\n                self.emit(\\\"LOAD_CONST\\\", None)\\n            self.emit(\\\"RETURN\\\")\\n\\n        elif isinstance(stmt, ast.Break):\\n            if loop_ctx is None:\\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(\\\"break outside a loop\\\")\\n            idx = self.emit(\\\"JUMP\\\", None)\\n            loop_ctx[\\\"breaks\\\"].append(idx)\\n\\n        elif isinstance(stmt, ast.Continue):\\n            if loop_ctx is None:\\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(\\\"continue outside a loop\\\")\\n            self.emit(\\\"JUMP\\\", loop_ctx[\\\"continue_target\\\"])\\n\\n        elif isinstance(stmt, ast.Pass):\\n            pass\\n\\n        elif isinstance(stmt, ast.Expr) and isinstance(stmt.value, ast.Constant):\\n            pass  # a bare docstring/constant; harmless, no effect\\n\\n        else:\\n            raise UnsupportedConstruct(f\\\"unsupported statement type: {type(stmt).__name__}\\\")\\n\\n    def compile_expr(self, expr):\\n        if isinstance(expr, ast.Constant):\\n            if not isinstance(expr.value, (int, bool)):\\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(\\n                    f\\\"only int/bool constants are supported, got {type(expr.value).__name__}\\\"\\n                )\\n            self.emit(\\\"LOAD_CONST\\\", expr.value)\\n\\n        elif isinstance(expr, ast.Name):\\n            self.emit(\\\"LOAD_VAR\\\", expr.id)\\n\\n        elif isinstance(expr, ast.BinOp):\\n            if type(expr.op).__name__ not in VM.BIN_OPS:\\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(f\\\"unsupported binary operator: {type(expr.op).__name__}\\\")\\n            self.compile_expr(expr.left)\\n            self.compile_expr(expr.right)\\n            self.emit(\\\"BINOP\\\", type(expr.op).__name__)\\n\\n        elif isinstance(expr, ast.UnaryOp):\\n            if type(expr.op).__name__ not in VM.UNARY_OPS:\\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(f\\\"unsupported unary operator: {type(expr.op).__name__}\\\")\\n            self.compile_expr(expr.operand)\\n            self.emit(\\\"UNARYOP\\\", type(expr.op).__name__)\\n\\n        elif isinstance(expr, ast.Compare):\\n            if len(expr.ops) != 1 or len(expr.comparators) != 1:\\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(\\\"chained comparisons (a < b < c) are not supported\\\")\\n            if type(expr.ops[0]).__name__ not in VM.CMP_OPS:\\n                raise UnsupportedConstruct(f\\\"unsupported comparison: {type(expr.ops[0]).__name__}\\\")\\n            self.compile_expr(expr.left)\\n            self.compile_expr(expr.comparators[0])\\n            self.emit(\\\"COMPARE\\\", type(expr.ops[0]).__name__)\\n\\n        elif isinstance(expr, ast.BoolOp):\\n            op_name = type(expr.op).__name__\\n            self.compile_expr(expr.values[0])\\n            for v in expr.values[1:]:\\n                self.compile_expr(v)\\n                self.emit(\\\"BOOLOP\\\", op_name)\\n\\n        else:\\n            raise UnsupportedConstruct(f\\\"unsupported expression type: {type(expr).__name__}\\\")\\n\\n\\n# =============================================================================\\n# VM: executes the compiled instructions, proving HALTS / LOOPS / UNDETERMINED\\n# =============================================================================\\n\\nclass VM:\\n    BIN_OPS = {\\n        \\\"Add\\\": lambda a, b: a + b,\\n        \\\"Sub\\\": lambda a, b: a - b,\\n        \\\"Mult\\\": lambda a, b: a * b,\\n        \\\"FloorDiv\\\": lambda a, b: a // b,\\n        \\\"Mod\\\": lambda a, b: a % b,\\n        \\\"Pow\\\": lambda a, b: a ** b,\\n    }\\n    CMP_OPS = {\\n        \\\"Eq\\\": lambda a, b: a == b,\\n        \\\"NotEq\\\": lambda a, b: a != b,\\n        \\\"Lt\\\": lambda a, b: a < b,\\n        \\\"LtE\\\": lambda a, b: a <= b,\\n        \\\"Gt\\\": lambda a, b: a > b,\\n        \\\"GtE\\\": lambda a, b: a >= b,\\n    }\\n    BOOL_OPS = {\\n        \\\"And\\\": lambda a, b: bool(a) and bool(b),\\n        \\\"Or\\\": lambda a, b: bool(a) or bool(b),\\n    }\\n    UNARY_OPS = {\\n        \\\"USub\\\": lambda a: -a,\\n        \\\"Not\\\": lambda a: not a,\\n    }\\n\\n    def __init__(self, code, initial_vars, step_budget):\\n        self.code = code\\n        self.vars = dict(initial_vars)\\n        self.step_budget = step_budget\\n\\n    def run(self):\\n        pc = 0\\n        stack = []\\n        seen = {}\\n        step = 0\\n\\n        while True:\\n            # Full machine state = everything the future of execution can\\n            # possibly depend on. If this exact triple repeats, execution\\n            # is deterministic, so it MUST repeat forever -- a real proof.\\n            state = (pc, tuple(stack), tuple(sorted(self.vars.items())))\\n\\n            if state in seen:\\n                return {\\n                    \\\"result\\\": \\\"LOOPS\\\",\\n                    \\\"cycle_start\\\": seen[state],\\n                    \\\"cycle_length\\\": step - seen[state],\\n                    \\\"steps\\\": step,\\n                    \\\"configs_seen\\\": len(seen),\\n                }\\n\\n            seen[state] = step\\n\\n            if step >= self.step_budget:\\n                return {\\n                    \\\"result\\\": \\\"UNDETERMINED\\\",\\n                    \\\"steps\\\": step,\\n                    \\\"configs_seen\\\": len(seen),\\n                }\\n\\n            instr = self.code[pc]\\n            op = instr.op\\n\\n            if op == \\\"LOAD_CONST\\\":\\n                stack.append(instr.arg)\\n                pc += 1\\n            elif op == \\\"LOAD_VAR\\\":\\n                if instr.arg not in self.vars:\\n                    raise UnsupportedConstruct(f\\\"use of undefined variable '{instr.arg}'\\\")\\n                stack.append(self.vars[instr.arg])\\n                pc += 1\\n            elif op == \\\"STORE_VAR\\\":\\n                self.vars[instr.arg] = stack.pop()\\n                pc += 1\\n            elif op == \\\"BINOP\\\":\\n                b = stack.pop()\\n                a = stack.pop()\\n                if instr.arg in (\\\"FloorDiv\\\", \\\"Mod\\\") and b == 0:\\n                    raise UnsupportedConstruct(\\\"division by zero encountered during analysis\\\")\\n                stack.append(self.BIN_OPS[instr.arg](a, b))\\n                pc += 1\\n            elif op == \\\"UNARYOP\\\":\\n                a = stack.pop()\\n                stack.append(self.UNARY_OPS[instr.arg](a))\\n                pc += 1\\n            elif op == \\\"COMPARE\\\":\\n                b = stack.pop()\\n                a = stack.pop()\\n                stack.append(self.CMP_OPS[instr.arg](a, b))\\n                pc += 1\\n            elif op == \\\"BOOLOP\\\":\\n                b = stack.pop()\\n                a = stack.pop()\\n                stack.append(self.BOOL_OPS[instr.arg](a, b))\\n                pc += 1\\n            elif op == \\\"JUMP\\\":\\n                pc = instr.arg\\n            elif op == \\\"JUMP_IF_FALSE\\\":\\n                v = stack.pop()\\n                pc = instr.arg if not v else pc + 1\\n            elif op == \\\"RETURN\\\":\\n                v = stack.pop()\\n                return {\\n                    \\\"result\\\": \\\"HALTS\\\",\\n                    \\\"steps\\\": step,\\n                    \\\"return_value\\\": v,\\n                    \\\"configs_seen\\\": len(seen),\\n                }\\n            else:\\n                raise RuntimeError(f\\\"internal error: unknown instruction {op}\\\")\\n\\n            step += 1\\n\\n\\n# =============================================================================\\n# TOP-LEVEL INTEGRATOR\\n# =============================================================================\\n\\ndef analyze_function(func_def: ast.FunctionDef, args, step_budget=200_000):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Analyze one function on one concrete set of integer/bool arguments.\\n\\n    This decides halting for THIS input only. Deciding halting for every\\n    possible input to a function is, in general, exactly the undecidable\\n    halting problem again -- restricting to int/bool arguments does not\\n    change that, since the loop bodies can still do unbounded arithmetic.\\n    What's decidable per-run is whether THIS run halts, loops, or is\\n    undetermined within the budget -- which is what this function reports.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    compiler = Compiler()\\n    code, param_names = compiler.compile_function(func_def)\\n\\n    if len(args) != len(param_names):\\n        raise ValueError(\\n            f\\\"{func_def.name} expects {len(param_names)} argument(s) \\\"\\n            f\\\"({', '.join(param_names) or 'none'}), got {len(args)}\\\"\\n        )\\n\\n    initial_vars = dict(zip(param_names, args))\\n    vm = VM(code, initial_vars, step_budget)\\n    return vm.run()\\n\\n\\ndef search_halts_in_source(source, args_map=None, step_budget=200_000):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Parse a source file, find every top-level function definition, and\\n    analyze each one. Functions that take parameters are only analyzed for\\n    concrete inputs supplied in args_map (a dict: function name -> list of\\n    argument tuples); without an entry there, they're reported as SKIPPED\\n    with an explanation, never silently assumed.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    tree = ast.parse(source)\\n    args_map = args_map or {}\\n    results = {}\\n\\n    for node in tree.body:\\n        if not isinstance(node, ast.FunctionDef):\\n            continue\\n\\n        param_count = len(node.args.args)\\n\\n        if param_count == 0:\\n            key = f\\\"{node.name}()\\\"\\n            try:\\n                results[key] = analyze_function(node, [], step_budget)\\n            except UnsupportedConstruct as e:\\n                results[key] = {\\\"result\\\": \\\"UNSUPPORTED\\\", \\\"reason\\\": str(e)}\\n            continue\\n\\n        if node.name not in args_map:\\n            results[f\\\"{node.name}(...)\\\"] = {\\n                \\\"result\\\": \\\"SKIPPED\\\",\\n                \\\"reason\\\": (\\n                    f\\\"takes {param_count} argument(s); supply concrete \\\"\\n                    f\\\"values via args_map/--args to analyze a specific call\\\"\\n                ),\\n            }\\n            continue\\n\\n        for args in args_map[node.name]:\\n            key = f\\\"{node.name}{tuple(args)}\\\"\\n            try:\\n                results[key] = analyze_function(node, list(args), step_budget)\\n            except UnsupportedConstruct as e:\\n                results[key] = {\\\"result\\\": \\\"UNSUPPORTED\\\", \\\"reason\\\": str(e)}\\n\\n    return results\\n\\n\\ndef print_result(name, result):\\n    r = result[\\\"result\\\"]\\n    if r == \\\"HALTS\\\":\\n        print(f\\\"  {name:28s} HALTS       (proven, {result['steps']} steps, \\\"\\n              f\\\"returns {result['return_value']!r})\\\")\\n    elif r == \\\"LOOPS\\\":\\n        print(f\\\"  {name:28s} LOOPS       (proven, exact state repeats every \\\"\\n              f\\\"{result['cycle_length']} steps starting at step {result['cycle_start']})\\\")\\n    elif r == \\\"UNDETERMINED\\\":\\n        print(f\\\"  {name:28s} UNDETERMINED  (ran {result['steps']} steps without \\\"\\n              f\\\"halting or a proven cycle -- state kept growing; honestly unknown)\\\")\\n    elif r == \\\"SKIPPED\\\":\\n        print(f\\\"  {name:28s} SKIPPED     ({result['reason']})\\\")\\n    elif r == \\\"UNSUPPORTED\\\":\\n        print(f\\\"  {name:28s} UNSUPPORTED ({result['reason']})\\\")\\n\\n\\n# =============================================================================\\n# BUILT-IN DEMO\\n# =============================================================================\\n\\nDEMO_SOURCE = '''\\ndef counts_down_and_halts():\\n    x = 5\\n    while x != 0:\\n        x -= 1\\n    return x\\n\\ndef infinite_loop():\\n    x = 0\\n    while True:\\n        x += 1\\n        x -= 1\\n\\ndef bounded_oscillation():\\n    x = 0\\n    y = 0\\n    while True:\\n        x += 1\\n        y += 1\\n        x = x % 4\\n        if x == 0:\\n            y = y % 4\\n\\ndef grows_without_bound():\\n    x = 0\\n    while x >= 0:\\n        x += 1\\n    return x\\n\\ndef collatz_like(n):\\n    steps = 0\\n    while n != 1:\\n        if n % 2 == 0:\\n            n = n // 2\\n        else:\\n            n = 3 * n + 1\\n        steps += 1\\n    return steps\\n\\ndef uses_recursion(n):\\n    if n == 0:\\n        return 0\\n    return uses_recursion(n - 1)\\n'''\\n\\n\\ndef run_demo():\\n    print(\\\"=\\\" * 78)\\n    print(\\\"CODE HALT SEARCH -- DEMO ON BUILT-IN EXAMPLE FUNCTIONS\\\")\\n    print(\\\"=\\\" * 78)\\n    print()\\n    print(\\n        \\\"Three real outcomes: HALTS (proven by reaching return), LOOPS\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"(proven by an exact repeated machine state), UNDETERMINED (budget\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"ran out with neither -- reported honestly, not guessed).\\\"\\n    )\\n    print()\\n\\n    results = search_halts_in_source(\\n        DEMO_SOURCE,\\n        args_map={\\n            \\\"collatz_like\\\": [(27,), (97,)],\\n            \\\"uses_recursion\\\": [(5,)],\\n        },\\n        step_budget=2000,\\n    )\\n\\n    for name, result in results.items():\\n        print_result(name, result)\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\\"-\\\" * 78)\\n    print(\\n        \\\"Notes:\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"  - grows_without_bound is a genuine open case: it never halts and\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"    never repeats state (x keeps climbing), so honestly reporting\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"    UNDETERMINED -- not LOOPS -- is the mathematically correct\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"    answer here, not a limitation of this run.\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"  - uses_recursion is rejected outright as UNSUPPORTED, because\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"    function calls fall outside the subset this tool can prove\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"    things about -- it never silently pretends to analyze it.\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n\\ndef main():\\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\\n        description=\\\"Search real Python source for provable halting/looping behavior.\\\"\\n    )\\n    parser.add_argument(\\\"file\\\", nargs=\\\"?\\\", help=\\\"Python file to analyze\\\")\\n    parser.add_argument(\\\"--function\\\", help=\\\"Only analyze this function\\\")\\n    parser.add_argument(\\\"--args\\\", nargs=\\\"*\\\", type=int, default=[], help=\\\"Integer arguments for --function\\\")\\n    parser.add_argument(\\\"--step-budget\\\", type=int, default=200_000)\\n    parser.add_argument(\\\"--demo\\\", action=\\\"store_true\\\", help=\\\"Run the built-in demo instead of a file\\\")\\n    args = parser.parse_args()\\n\\n    if args.demo or not args.file:\\n        run_demo()\\n        return\\n\\n    with open(args.file) as f:\\n        source = f.read()\\n\\n    if args.function:\\n        tree = ast.parse(source)\\n        func_def = next(\\n            (n for n in tree.body if isinstance(n, ast.FunctionDef) and n.name == args.function),\\n            None,\\n        )\\n        if func_def is None:\\n            print(f\\\"No function named '{args.function}' found in {args.file}\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n            sys.exit(1)\\n        try:\\n            result = analyze_function(func_def, args.args, args.step_budget)\\n        except UnsupportedConstruct as e:\\n            result = {\\\"result\\\": \\\"UNSUPPORTED\\\", \\\"reason\\\": str(e)}\\n        print_result(f\\\"{args.function}{tuple(args.args)}\\\", result)\\n    else:\\n        results = search_halts_in_source(source, step_budget=args.step_budget)\\n        print(f\\\"Analysis of {args.file}:\\\")\\n        for name, result in results.items():\\n            print_result(name, result)\\n\\n\\nif __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\":\\n    main()\\n\", \"filename\": \"/mnt/user-data/outputs/code_halt_search.py\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:58:47.350800Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:58:47.350834Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01TFvdSyaEcjRiogfFQmHSBT", "name": "create_file", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "File created successfully: /mnt/user-data/outputs/code_halt_search.py"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "File created successfully: /mnt/user-data/outputs/code_halt_search.py"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:58:49.311603Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:58:49.724591Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Let's verify it actually works correctly on all the demo cases:", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:58:49.724629Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-