tion_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"  unbounded_but_wraps()        UNDETERMINED  (ran 3000005 steps without halting or a proven cycle -- state kept growing; honestly unknown)\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:59:21.310181Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:59:24.913598Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Tracing the loop's cycle length and behavior."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:59:24.913642Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:59:25.778035Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Right \u2014 the cycle period here is roughly 1,000,000 iterations \u00d7 several VM instructions each, so 3 million steps wasn't enough. Let's use a budget that actually covers one full cycle:", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:59:25.778064Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T12:59:26.619438Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01C8MHVbJbtkHQsDitxAXPLQ", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "cd /home/claude && python3 /mnt/user-data/outputs/code_halt_search.py /tmp/test_misc.py --function unbounded_but_wraps --step-budget 15000000", "description": "Use a large enough budget to actually witness the full cycle period"}, "message": "Use a large enough budget to actually witness the full cycle period", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cd /home/claude && python3 /mnt/user-data/outputs/code_halt_search.py /tmp/test_misc.py --function unbounded_but_wraps --step-budget 15000000\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:01:50.635466Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:01:50.635595Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01C8MHVbJbtkHQsDitxAXPLQ", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"  unbounded_but_wraps()        LOOPS       (proven, exact state repeats every 11000014 steps starting at step 2)\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"  unbounded_but_wraps()        LOOPS       (proven, exact state repeats every 11000014 steps starting at step 2)\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:01:52.423636Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:01:53.997920Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Confirmed \u2014 with enough budget to actually traverse the full cycle, it correctly proves `LOOPS` with the exact cycle length (11,000,014 steps, consistent with the ~1,000,001-iteration period at ~11 VM instructions per iteration). This is a real, load-bearing tool: every claim it makes is backed by an actual completed run or an actual repeated state, never a guess dressed up as one.", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:01:53.997951Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:01:54.307848Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01XajeEi2zz4KwtSzknS2Mem", "name": "present_files", "input": {"filepaths": ["/mnt/user-data/outputs/code_halt_search.py"]}, "message": "Presenting file(s)...", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "table", "table": [["filepaths", "['/mnt/user-data/outputs/code_halt_search.py']"]]}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:01:54.329222Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:01:54.329247Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01XajeEi2zz4KwtSzknS2Mem", "name": "present_files", "content": [{"type": "local_resource", "file_path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/code_halt_search.py", "name": "code halt search", "mime_type": "text/x-python", "artifact_publishable": false}, {"type": "text", "text": "The file cards appear at the end of your reply if it ends with text, otherwise under this step; say they are below only if you finish the reply with text."}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": "Presented file", "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:01:55.656357Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:02:01.154415Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "**Usage:**\n- `python3 code_halt_search.py --demo` \u2014 built-in examples showing all three outcomes\n- `python3 code_halt_search.py yourfile.py` \u2014 analyzes every parameterless function found\n- `python3 code_halt_search.py yourfile.py --function foo --args 1 2 --step-budget 500000` \u2014 analyze one function on specific inputs\n\n**What makes each verdict trustworthy:**\n- `HALTS` \u2014 only printed after actually executing to a real `return` (verified above with Collatz sequences matching known values).\n- `LOOPS` \u2014 only printed after the exact `(program counter, operand stack, all variables)` triple repeats. For a deterministic interpreter, that's not a heuristic \u2014 it's a proof the same steps repeat forever. Verified above on both a fast loop and a 1,000,000-iteration wraparound loop.\n- `UNDETERMINED` \u2014 printed whenever the budget runs out first. This can flip to a proven answer with more budget (shown above), or can be the honest final word for real open cases like unbounded growth or (if you removed the step cap) an actual Collatz-style program on an input nobody's proven halts.\n- `UNSUPPORTED` \u2014 anything outside the safe, deterministic subset (function calls, recursion, lists, for-loops, etc.) is rejected outright with a specific reason, never silently approximated.", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-26T13:02:01.268135Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T13:02:01.268135Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddc9-9844-7641-9174-360d11a63b31"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddd2-f70e-7c96-b4db-0d19b45a6783", "text": "make it provide an code injection to make it run forever", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:06:29.321828Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:06:29.321828Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "make it provide an code injection to make it run forever", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-26T13:06:29.322056Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T13:06:29.322056Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddc9-9844-7dc8-bde3-4ec5cb8407be"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddd2-f70e-76f0-b167-9655d4ad3c5a", "text": "I'm not going to build that. \"Code injection to make [a program] run forever\" is a denial-of-service technique \u2014 it's the same category as writing an exploit or a resource-exhaustion attack, just aimed at a different symptom (hanging instead of crashing/leaking data). Whether the target is this toy VM, a real service, or someone else's code, I don't write injection payloads designed to make software fail or hang.\n\nIf you tell me what you're actually trying to accomplish, I can probably help directly:\n\n- **Testing this tool's own robustness** \u2014 I can add a `--fuzz` mode that generates random *valid* programs from the supported subset and checks the analyzer terminates correctly (HALTS/LOOPS/UNDETERMINED) on all of them, which is the legitimate way to stress-test a decision procedure like this.\n- **Understanding infinite-loop bugs** \u2014 if you're learning about how unintended infinite loops happen in real code (off-by-one errors, wrong loop conditions, etc.), I can walk through common patterns and how to catch them via testing or static analysis.\n- **A deliberately non-halting example for teaching** \u2014 I already included a few of these in the demo (`infinite_loop`, `bounded_oscillation`, `grows_without_bound`). If you want more varied non-halting patterns *as normal example code*, not as an injection into something else, I'm happy to add those.\n- **Security research on resource-exhaustion / DoS defenses** \u2014 if this is for building or testing timeout/sandbox protections, I can help design the defensive side (step budgets, watchdogs, resource limits), which is the constructive counterpart to what you're describing.\n\nWhich of these (or something else) is actually what you're after?", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:06:29.313588Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:06:36.183186Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "I'm not going to build that. \"Code injection to make [a program] run forever\" is a denial-of-service technique \u2014 it's the same category as writing an exploit or a resource-exhaustion attack, just aimed at a different symptom (hanging instead of crashing/leaking data). Whether the target is this toy VM, a real service, or someone else's code, I don't write injection payloads designed to make software fail or hang.\n\nIf you tell me what you're actually trying to accomplish, I can probably help directly:\n\n- **Testing this tool's own robustness** \u2014 I can add a `--fuzz` mode that generates random *valid* programs from the supported subset and checks the analyzer terminates correctly (HALTS/LOOPS/UNDETERMINED) on all of them, which is the legitimate way to stress-test a decision procedure like this.\n- **Understanding infinite-loop bugs** \u2014 if you're learning about how unintended infinite loops happen in real code (off-by-one errors, wrong loop conditions, etc.), I can walk through common patterns and how to catch them via testing or static analysis.\n- **A deliberately non-halting example for teaching** \u2014 I already included a few of these in the demo (`infinite_loop`, `bounded_oscillation`, `grows_without_bound`). If you want more varied non-halting patterns *as normal example code*, not as an injection into something else, I'm happy to add those.\n- **Security research on resource-exhaustion / DoS defenses** \u2014 if this is for building or testing timeout/sandbox protections, I can help design the defensive side (step budgets, watchdogs, resource limits), which is the constructive counterpart to what you're describing.\n\nWhich of these (or something else) is actually what you're after?", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-26T13:06:36.201145Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T13:06:36.201145Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddd2-f70e-7c96-b4db-0d19b45a6783"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddd3-5095-7658-b00b-77790ef79624", "text": "A deliberately non-halting example for teaching,", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:06:51.074496Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:06:51.074496Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "A deliberately non-halting example for teaching,", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-26T13:06:51.074718Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T13:06:51.074718Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddd2-f70e-76f0-b167-9655d4ad3c5a"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddd3-5095-7a47-bc9e-7d4d815f4f95", "text": "Good \u2014 these are useful precisely because they're honest, runnable examples with a real proof of non-termination behind each one. I built a small catalog of classic infinite-loop patterns, categorized by *why* they don't halt, and verified each one against the analyzer from before so the \"proof\" isn't just asserted \u2014 it's actually printed with an exact cycle length.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nNow let's actually verify each Group A example is correctly proven `LOOPS`:\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nGood catch from the run: `wrong_direction()` actually cycles through *all* residues mod 20, so it does eventually hit 10 and halts \u2014 a real reminder that \"looks buggy\" isn't the same as \"provably doesn't halt.\" And `mutual_oscillation()` used tuple-swap assignment, which the analyzer's restricted subset doesn't support. Let me fix both properly:\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nNow let's re-verify every Group A example is genuinely, correctly proven to loop:\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nAll six Group A patterns now come back correctly and honestly proven `LOOPS`, each with a real exact cycle length as evidence.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nSix real, common bug patterns (Group A), each mechanically proven to loop forever by `code_halt_search.py`, not just claimed:\n\n| Function | The actual mistake |\n|---|---|\n| `wrong_direction` | Parity mismatch \u2014 target value is structurally unreachable, not just slow to reach |\n| `inverted_condition` | Loop's continue-condition is backwards |\n| `off_by_one_never_reached` | Step size skips clean over the exact target |\n| `mutual_oscillation` | Two variables swap forever, canceling out any progress |\n| `reset_before_exit_check` | Progress-resetting line accidentally placed before the exit check instead of after the loop |\n| `unconditional_infinite_loop` | No exit condition at all \u2014 the classic missing `break` |\n\nPlus three Group B examples (recursion without a shrinking base case, float-equality drift, mutating a list while iterating it) \u2014 shown as realistic source for discussion, correctly flagged as outside what the tool can safely execute rather than run as-is.", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:06:51.069882Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:06:57.818407Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Drafting varied examples of non-terminating code for teaching."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:06:57.818466Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:06:59.409244Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Good \u2014 these are useful precisely because they're honest, runnable examples with a real proof of non-termination behind each one. I built a small catalog of classic infinite-loop patterns, categorized by *why* they don't halt, and verified each one against the analyzer from before so the \"proof\" isn't just asserted \u2014 it's actually printed with an exact cycle length.", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:06:59.409272Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:20.722480Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_017TdBNKa11dgNRU1Lij9xxU", "name": "create_file", "input": {"description": "A teaching catalog of classic non-halting code patterns, categorized by the underlying bug, with each analyzable example verified against the halt-search tool's proof output", "file_text": "\"\"\"\nnon_halting_examples.py\n========================\n\nA teaching catalog of classic reasons real code accidentally never\nterminates. Each pattern below is a genuine, common bug -- not a\ncontrived edge case -- shown in isolation so the mechanism is clear.\n\nTwo groups:\n\n  A) Examples written in the restricted subset understood by\n     code_halt_search.py, so their non-termination is not just claimed\n     but PROVEN by that tool (exact repeated machine state -> must\n     repeat forever). Run:\n\n         python3 code_halt_search.py non_halting_examples.py\n\n     and every function below will be reported LOOPS, with the exact\n     cycle length that proves it.\n\n  B) Examples that need full Python (recursion, floats, mutable\n     objects) to show the pattern realistically. These are outside\n     code_halt_search.py's supported subset (by design -- see that\n     tool's docstring for why), so they're explained rather than\n     proven mechanically. They are NOT meant to be executed as-is.\n\"\"\"\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# GROUP A -- provable via code_halt_search.py (restricted subset)\n# =============================================================================\n\ndef wrong_direction():\n    \"\"\"\n    BUG: counting the wrong way.\n    Intent: count from 0 up to 10.\n    Mistake: decrementing instead of incrementing, so the condition\n    (x != 10) is never satisfied -- x heads to negative infinity in a\n    real unbounded interpreter, but here it wraps via modulo so the\n    analyzer can actually witness the exact repeat and prove it loops.\n    \"\"\"\n    x = 0\n    while x != 10:\n        x -= 1\n        x = x % 20  # wraparound so the analyzer can observe a finite cycle\n    return x\n\n\ndef inverted_condition():\n    \"\"\"\n    BUG: the loop condition is backwards.\n    Intent: stop once x reaches 0.\n    Mistake: wrote `x >= 0` instead of `x <= 0`, or similar -- the\n    condition that should signal \"done\" instead signals \"keep going\",\n    so it never becomes false as long as x stays non-negative.\n    \"\"\"\n    x = 5\n    while x >= 0:\n        x += 1\n        x = x % 8  # wraparound keeps x non-negative forever -> proven loop\n    return x\n\n\ndef off_by_one_never_reached():\n    \"\"\"\n    BUG: the exact target value is skipped over.\n    Intent: stop when x == 10.\n    Mistake: x increases by 2 each time starting from an odd number,\n    so it jumps straight over 10 and the equality check never fires.\n    \"\"\"\n    x = 1\n    while x != 10:\n        x += 2\n        x = x % 16  # wraparound so the skip-over pattern still cycles\n    return x\n\n\ndef mutual_oscillation():\n    \"\"\"\n    BUG: two variables update each other in a way that cancels out.\n    A very common pattern in hand-rolled state machines: A depends on\n    B's old value and B depends on A's old value, so the pair swings\n    between a fixed set of states forever instead of converging.\n    \"\"\"\n    a = 0\n    b = 1\n    while a != b:\n        a, b = b, a  # swap forever; a != b stays true unless they start equal\n        a = a % 4\n        b = b % 4\n    return a\n\n\ndef reset_before_exit_check():\n    \"\"\"\n    BUG: a counter is reset to its starting value just before the\n    check that would have ended the loop -- a classic \"I meant to put\n    this line after the loop, not inside it\" mistake.\n    \"\"\"\n    x = 0\n    while True:\n        x += 1\n        if x >= 5:\n            x = 0  # <-- accidentally undoes the progress right before exit\n    # unreachable, but included for clarity: intent was `return x`\n\n\ndef unconditional_infinite_loop():\n    \"\"\"\n    BUG: no exit condition was ever written at all -- `while True`\n    with no `break`. The simplest and most common infinite loop.\n    \"\"\"\n    x = 0\n    while True:\n        x += 1\n        x = x % 2\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# GROUP B -- realistic patterns needing full Python (not analyzer-provable\n# by this tool; shown for teaching only, do not execute)\n# =============================================================================\n\ndef recursion_without_base_case_EXAMPLE(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    BUG: a recursive function with no base case, or a base case that's\n    unreachable from some inputs. Each call makes the problem \"smaller\"\n    in the wrong direction (n + 1 instead of n - 1), so it never\n    reaches the case that would stop the recursion -- and in real\n    Python this ends in a RecursionError, not a clean infinite loop.\n\n    NOT SAFE TO RUN. Shown as source only.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    return recursion_without_base_case_EXAMPLE(n + 1)  # should be n - 1\n\n\ndef float_equality_never_true_EXAMPLE():\n    \"\"\"\n    BUG: looping until a floating-point value exactly equals a target,\n    when repeated addition of a fractional step accumulates rounding\n    error and the value hops over the target without ever landing on\n    it exactly (e.g. 0.1 added ten times often isn't exactly 1.0).\n\n    NOT SAFE TO RUN. Shown as source only.\n    \"\"\"\n    x = 0.0\n    while x != 1.0:\n        x += 0.1\n    return x\n\n\ndef mutating_collection_while_iterating_EXAMPLE(items):\n    \"\"\"\n    BUG: appending to a list while iterating over it. The loop's\n    \"end\" keeps moving further away as new items are added, so a\n    naive index-based loop over a growing list never catches up.\n\n    NOT SAFE TO RUN as shown. Shown as source only.\n    \"\"\"\n    i = 0\n    while i < len(items):\n        items.append(items[i] * 2)  # keeps growing the thing being iterated\n        i += 1\n    return items\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    print(__doc__)\n    print(\n        \"Run: python3 code_halt_search.py non_halting_examples.py\\n\"\n        \"to see each Group A function PROVEN to loop forever, with its\\n\"\n        \"exact cycle length -- not just asserted, but demonstrated.\"\n    )\n", "path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/non_halting_examples.py"}, "message": "A teaching catalog of classic non-halting code patterns, categorized by the underlying bug, with each analyzable example verified against the halt-search tool's proof output", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"python\", \"code\": \"\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nnon_halting_examples.py\\n========================\\n\\nA teaching catalog of classic reasons real code accidentally never\\nterminates. Each pattern below is a genuine, common bug -- not a\\ncontrived edge case -- shown in isolation so the mechanism is clear.\\n\\nTwo groups:\\n\\n  A) Examples written in the restricted subset understood by\\n     code_halt_search.py, so their non-termination is not just claimed\\n     but PROVEN by that tool (exact repeated machine state -> must\\n     repeat forever). Run:\\n\\n         python3 code_halt_search.py non_halting_examples.py\\n\\n     and every function below will be reported LOOPS, with the exact\\n     cycle length that proves it.\\n\\n  B) Examples that need full Python (recursion, floats, mutable\\n     objects) to show the pattern realistically. These are outside\\n     code_halt_search.py's supported subset (by design -- see that\\n     tool's docstring for why), so they're explained rather than\\n     proven mechanically. They are NOT meant to be executed as-is.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\n\\n# =============================================================================\\n# GROUP A -- provable via code_halt_search.py (restricted subset)\\n# =============================================================================\\n\\ndef wrong_direction():\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    BUG: counting the wrong way.\\n    Intent: count from 0 up to 10.\\n    Mistake: decrementing instead of incrementing, so the condition\\n    (x != 10) is never satisfied -- x heads to negative infinity in a\\n    real unbounded interpreter, but here it wraps via modulo so the\\n    analyzer can actually witness the exact repeat and prove it loops.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    x = 0\\n    while x != 10:\\n        x -= 1\\n        x = x % 20  # wraparound so the analyzer can observe a finite cycle\\n    return x\\n\\n\\ndef inverted_condition():\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    BUG: the loop condition is backwards.\\n    Intent: stop once x reaches 0.\\n    Mistake: wrote `x >= 0` instead of `x <= 0`, or similar -- the\\n    condition that should signal \\\"done\\\" instead signals \\\"keep going\\\",\\n    so it never becomes false as long as x stays non-negative.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    x = 5\\n    while x >= 0:\\n        x += 1\\n        x = x % 8  # wraparound keeps x non-negative forever -> proven loop\\n    return x\\n\\n\\ndef off_by_one_never_reached():\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    BUG: the exact target value is skipped over.\\n    Intent: stop when x == 10.\\n    Mistake: x increases by 2 each time starting from an odd number,\\n    so it jumps straight over 10 and the equality check never fires.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    x = 1\\n    while x != 10:\\n        x += 2\\n        x = x % 16  # wraparound so the skip-over pattern still cycles\\n    return x\\n\\n\\ndef mutual_oscillation():\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    BUG: two variables update each other in a way that cancels out.\\n    A very common pattern in hand-rolled state machines: A depends on\\n    B's old value and B depends on A's old value, so the pair swings\\n    between a fixed set of states forever instead of converging.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    a = 0\\n    b = 1\\n    while a != b:\\n        a, b = b, a  # swap forever; a != b stays true unless they start equal\\n        a = a % 4\\n        b = b % 4\\n    return a\\n\\n\\ndef reset_before_exit_check():\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    BUG: a counter is reset to its starting value just before the\\n    check that would have ended the loop -- a classic \\\"I meant to put\\n    this line after the loop, not inside it\\\" mistake.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    x = 0\\n    while True:\\n        x += 1\\n        if x >= 5:\\n            x = 0  # <-- accidentally undoes the progress right before exit\\n    # unreachable, but included for clarity: intent was `return x`\\n\\n\\ndef unconditional_infinite_loop():\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    BUG: no exit condition was ever written at all -- `while True`\\n    with no `break`. The simplest and most common infinite loop.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    x = 0\\n    while True:\\n        x += 1\\n        x = x % 2\\n\\n\\n# =============================================================================\\n# GROUP B -- realistic patterns needing full Python (not analyzer-provable\\n# by this tool; shown for teaching only, do not execute)\\n# =============================================================================\\n\\ndef recursion_without_base_case_EXAMPLE(n):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    BUG: a recursive function with no base case, or a base case that's\\n    unreachable from some inputs. Each call makes the problem \\\"smaller\\\"\\n    in the wrong direction (n + 1 instead of n - 1), so it never\\n    reaches the case that would stop the recursion -- and in real\\n    Python this ends in a RecursionError, not a clean infinite loop.\\n\\n    NOT SAFE TO RUN. Shown as source only.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    if n == 0:\\n        return 0\\n    return recursion_without_base_case_EXAMPLE(n + 1)  # should be n - 1\\n\\n\\ndef float_equality_never_true_EXAMPLE():\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    BUG: looping until a floating-point value exactly equals a target,\\n    when repeated addition of a fractional step accumulates rounding\\n    error and the value hops over the target without ever landing on\\n    it exactly (e.g. 0.1 added ten times often isn't exactly 1.0).\\n\\n    NOT SAFE TO RUN. Shown as source only.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    x = 0.0\\n    while x != 1.0:\\n        x += 0.1\\n    return x\\n\\n\\ndef mutating_collection_while_iterating_EXAMPLE(items):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    BUG: appending to a list while iterating over it. The loop's\\n    \\\"end\\\" keeps moving further away as new items are added, so a\\n    naive index-based loop over a growing list never catches up.\\n\\n    NOT SAFE TO RUN as shown. Shown as source only.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    i = 0\\n    while i < len(items):\\n        items.append(items[i] * 2)  # keeps growing the thing being iterated\\n        i += 1\\n    return items\\n\\n\\nif __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\":\\n    print(__doc__)\\n    print(\\n        \\\"Run: python3 code_halt_search.py non_halting_examples.py\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"to see each Group A function PROVEN to loop forever, with its\\\\n\\\"\\n        \\\"exact cycle length -- not just asserted, but demonstrated.\\\"\\n    )\\n\", \"filename\": \"/mnt/user-data/outputs/non_halting_examples.py\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:21.731425Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:21.731458Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_017TdBNKa11dgNRU1Lij9xxU", "name": "create_file", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "File created successfully: /mnt/user-data/outputs/non_halting_examples.py"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "File created successfully: /mnt/user-data/outputs/non_halting_examples.py"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:23.543329Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:24.217089Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Now let's actually verify each Group A example is correctly proven `LOOPS`:", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:24.217118Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:24.899487Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_016kffar8Vd3uJg275ZcdA6D", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "cd /home/claude && python3 /mnt/user-data/outputs/code_halt_search.py /mnt/user-data/outputs/non_halting_examples.py --step-budget 50000", "description": "Verify every Group A teaching example is genuinely proven to loop forever"}, "message": "Verify every Group A teaching example is genuinely proven to loop forever", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cd /home/claude && python3 /mnt/user-data/outputs/code_halt_search.py /mnt/user-data/outputs/non_halting_examples.py --step-budget 50000\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:25.442058Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:25.442095Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_016kffar8Vd3uJg275ZcdA6D", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"Analysis of /mnt/user-data/outputs/non_halting_examples.py:\\n  wrong_direction()            HALTS       (proven, 137 steps, returns 10)\\n  inverted_condition()         LOOPS       (proven, exact state repeats every 104 steps starting at step 2)\\n  off_by_one_never_reached()   LOOPS       (proven, exact state repeats every 104 steps starting at step 2)\\n  mutual_oscillation()         UNSUPPORTED (only single-name assignment (x = ...) is supported)\\n  reset_before_exit_check()    LOOPS       (proven, exact state repeats every 58 steps starting at step 2)\\n  unconditional_infinite_loop() LOOPS       (proven, exact state repeats every 22 steps starting at step 2)\\n  recursion_without_base_case_EXAMPLE(...) SKIPPED     (takes 1 argument(s); supply concrete values via args_map/--args to analyze a specific call)\\n  float_equality_never_true_EXAMPLE() UNSUPPORTED (only int/bool constants are supported, got float)\\n  mutating_collection_while_iterating_EXAMPLE(...) SKIPPED     (takes 1 argument(s); supply concrete values via args_map/--args to analyze a specific call)\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"Analysis of /mnt/user-data/outputs/non_halting_examples.py:\\n  wrong_direction()            HALTS       (proven, 137 steps, returns 10)\\n  inverted_condition()         LOOPS       (proven, exact state repeats every 104 steps starting at step 2)\\n  off_by_one_never_reached()   LOOPS       (proven, exact state repeats every 104 steps starting at step 2)\\n  mutual_oscillation()         UNSUPPORTED (only single-name assignment (x = ...) is supported)\\n  reset_before_exit_check()    LOOPS       (proven, exact state repeats every 58 steps starting at step 2)\\n  unconditional_infinite_loop() LOOPS       (proven, exact state repeats every 22 steps starting at step 2)\\n  recursion_without_base_case_EXAMPLE(...) SKIPPED     (takes 1 argument(s); supply concrete values via args_map/--args to analyze a specific call)\\n  float_equality_never_true_EXAMPLE() UNSUPPORTED (only int/bool constants are supported, got float)\\n  mutating_collection_while_iterating_EXAMPLE(...) SKIPPED     (takes 1 argument(s); supply concrete values via args_map/--args to analyze a specific call)\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:27.273214Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:37.752969Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Finding a wrap-around bug that stops the halting design."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:37.753009Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:39.070377Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Good catch from the run: `wrong_direction()` actually cycles through *all* residues mod 20, so it does eventually hit 10 and halts \u2014 a real reminder that \"looks buggy\" isn't the same as \"provably doesn't halt.\" And `mutual_oscillation()` used tuple-swap assignment, which the analyzer's restricted subset doesn't support. Let me fix both properly:", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:39.070401Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:43.044782Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01UKbfUSgUunVMf2Cmdi2GwN", "name": "str_replace", "input": {"new_str": "def wrong_direction():\n    \"\"\"\n    BUG: counting the wrong way, with a parity mismatch that makes the\n    target unreachable.\n    Intent: count from 1 up to 10.\n    Mistake: decrementing by 2 instead of incrementing, starting from\n    an odd number while the target (10) is even. Every value visited\n    stays odd forever, so `x != 10` can never become false -- the\n    target isn't just approached slowly, it's structurally unreachable.\n    \"\"\"\n    x = 1\n    while x != 10:\n        x -= 2\n        x = x % 20  # wraparound so the analyzer can observe the finite cycle\n    return x", "old_str": "def wrong_direction():\n    \"\"\"\n    BUG: counting the wrong way.\n    Intent: count from 0 up to 10.\n    Mistake: decrementing instead of incrementing, so the condition\n    (x != 10) is never satisfied -- x heads to negative infinity in a\n    real unbounded interpreter, but here it wraps via modulo so the\n    analyzer can actually witness the exact repeat and prove it loops.\n    \"\"\"\n    x = 0\n    while x != 10:\n        x -= 1\n        x = x % 20  # wraparound so the analyzer can observe a finite cycle\n    return x", "path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/non_halting_examples.py"}, "message": "Editing file", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "edit", "context": null, "display_content": null, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:43.297401Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:43.297433Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01UKbfUSgUunVMf2Cmdi2GwN", "name": "str_replace", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/non_halting_examples.py"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "edit", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/non_halting_examples.py"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:46.692810Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:49.505928Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01Hph9p3cWgiYDv1BrxWR1uH", "name": "str_replace", "input": {"new_str": "def mutual_oscillation():\n    \"\"\"\n    BUG: two variables update each other in a way that cancels out.\n    A very common pattern in hand-rolled state machines: A depends on\n    B's old value and B depends on A's old value, so the pair swings\n    between a fixed set of states forever instead of converging.\n    \"\"\"\n    a = 0\n    b = 1\n    while a != b:\n        temp = a\n        a = b\n        b = temp\n        a = a % 4\n        b = b % 4\n    return a", "old_str": "def mutual_oscillation():\n    \"\"\"\n    BUG: two variables update each other in a way that cancels out.\n    A very common pattern in hand-rolled state machines: A depends on\n    B's old value and B depends on A's old value, so the pair swings\n    between a fixed set of states forever instead of converging.\n    \"\"\"\n    a = 0\n    b = 1\n    while a != b:\n        a, b = b, a  # swap forever; a != b stays true unless they start equal\n        a = a % 4\n        b = b % 4\n    return a", "path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/non_halting_examples.py"}, "message": "Editing file", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "edit", "context": null, "display_content": null, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:49.817421Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:49.817453Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Hph9p3cWgiYDv1BrxWR1uH", "name": "str_replace", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/non_halting_examples.py"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "edit", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/non_halting_examples.py"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:51.755884Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:52.367892Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Now let's re-verify every Group A example is genuinely, correctly proven to loop:", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:07:52.367920Z"