--\\n  Trials completed          : 500\\n  HALTS                     : 337\\n  LOOPS (proven)            : 163\\n  Fully explored state space: 0\\n  Largest state space seen  : 16\\n  Invariant failures        : 0\\n\\n  Note: state_space is an upper bound on possible configurations,\\n  not a count of what's reachable from the start state -- most\\n  programs halt or cycle long before touching every syntactically\\n  possible configuration, so 0 here is expected, not a bug. Smaller\\n  --fuzz-max-value settings make full traversal far more likely.\\n\\nNo invariant failures: every trial's result was consistent with\\nthe proof decide_halting() relies on (configs_seen never exceeded\\nstate_space, and every decision was either a HALT or a proven cycle).\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"  [trial 490] regs=1 max_value=1 state_space=16 configs_seen=5 -> LOOPS\\n  [trial 491] regs=1 max_value=1 state_space=12 configs_seen=3 -> LOOPS\\n  [trial 492] regs=1 max_value=1 state_space=16 configs_seen=3 -> LOOPS\\n  [trial 493] regs=1 max_value=1 state_space=14 configs_seen=2 -> LOOPS\\n  [trial 494] regs=1 max_value=1 state_space=6 configs_seen=1 -> LOOPS\\n  [trial 495] regs=1 max_value=1 state_space=8 configs_seen=1 -> HALTS\\n  [trial 496] regs=1 max_value=1 state_space=8 configs_seen=1 -> LOOPS\\n  [trial 497] regs=1 max_value=1 state_space=14 configs_seen=1 -> HALTS\\n  [trial 498] regs=1 max_value=1 state_space=8 configs_seen=2 -> HALTS\\n  [trial 499] regs=1 max_value=1 state_space=4 configs_seen=2 -> HALTS\\n\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nFUZZING SUMMARY\\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n  Trials completed          : 500\\n  HALTS                     : 337\\n  LOOPS (proven)            : 163\\n  Fully explored state space: 0\\n  Largest state space seen  : 16\\n  Invariant failures        : 0\\n\\n  Note: state_space is an upper bound on possible configurations,\\n  not a count of what's reachable from the start state -- most\\n  programs halt or cycle long before touching every syntactically\\n  possible configuration, so 0 here is expected, not a bug. Smaller\\n  --fuzz-max-value settings make full traversal far more likely.\\n\\nNo invariant failures: every trial's result was consistent with\\nthe proof decide_halting() relies on (configs_seen never exceeded\\nstate_space, and every decision was either a HALT or a proven cycle).\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:10:51.046487Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:10:59.743065Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Constructing a targeted example to test edge-case behavior."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:10:59.743119Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:04.609662Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Verified: zero invariant failures across every run, with different machine sizes and seeds.\n\nTo be precise about what \"fuzz incoming data after each max Configurations seen\" now does in the code:\n\n- **Every trial**, right after `decide_halting()` returns (its result includes the `configs_seen` count, same field printed by `print_decision`), the fuzzer immediately discards that machine and generates a brand-new random one \u2014 it never reuses or \"idles\" on a finished machine. That's the core loop, confirmed by the verbose output above (500 fresh machines run back-to-back).\n- **Additionally** (a stricter, secondary metric), it tracks the special case where `configs_seen` reaches the machine's full theoretical `state_space` \u2014 meaning literally every syntactically possible configuration was visited, not just the ones reachable from the start. That's rarer, since `total_configurations()` is an upper bound, not a reachability count \u2014 which is why the summary correctly reported `0` at first, and my added note now explains why that's expected rather than hiding it.\n- Every trial checks real invariants against the proof (`configs_seen` never exceeds `state_space`; every decision is a genuine HALT or a genuine proven cycle) and reports any violation loudly rather than silently passing.", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:04.609699Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:04.894245Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01PU85KDqXiBHiUmWA59jjMr", "name": "present_files", "input": {"filepaths": ["/mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py"]}, "message": "Presenting file(s)...", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "table", "table": [["filepaths", "['/mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py']"]]}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:04.910388Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:04.910429Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01PU85KDqXiBHiUmWA59jjMr", "name": "present_files", "content": [{"type": "local_resource", "file_path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/halting_demo.py", "name": "halting demo", "mime_type": "text/x-python", "artifact_publishable": false}, {"type": "text", "text": "The file cards appear at the end of your reply if it ends with text, otherwise under this step; say they are below only if you finish the reply with text."}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": "Presented file", "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:06.988020Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:08.615130Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "**Usage:**\n- `--fuzz N` \u2014 run N random toy-machine trials instead of the demos\n- `--fuzz-seed S` \u2014 reproducible run\n- `--fuzz-verbose` \u2014 print every trial's `configs_seen` / decision, not just the summary\n- `--fuzz-max-value V` / `--fuzz-max-registers R` \u2014 shrink the search space, which makes full-state-space traversal (\"fully explored\") much more likely to actually appear", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-26T13:11:08.704227Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T13:11:08.704227Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddd5-656d-7eee-82dc-80b8973d3b17"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddd7-4e43-776a-b311-66dd0d25806c", "text": "combine with this AI", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:16.566724Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:16.566724Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "combine with this AI", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-26T13:11:16.567012Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T13:11:16.567012Z", "attachments": [{"file_name": "", "file_size": 23522, "file_type": "txt", "extracted_content": "\r\nimport argparse\r\nimport glob\r\nimport math\r\nimport re\r\nimport sys\r\nfrom collections import Counter\r\n\r\nimport numpy as np\r\nimport scipy.sparse as sp\r\nfrom scipy.linalg import expm\r\n\r\nUNK = \"<unk>\"\r\nBOS = \"<bos>\"\r\n\r\n\r\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n# 1. Dataset loading (unchanged \u2014 no construct in the paper maps to this)\r\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n\r\ndef load_corpus(paths=None, raw_text=None, lowercase=True, strip_punct=False):\r\n    text_chunks = []\r\n    if paths:\r\n        found_any = False\r\n        for pattern in paths:\r\n            for fp in sorted(glob.glob(pattern)):\r\n                found_any = True\r\n                with open(fp, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\") as f:\r\n                    text_chunks.append(f.read())\r\n        if not found_any:\r\n            print(f\"[warn] no files matched {paths}\", file=sys.stderr)\r\n\r\n    if raw_text:\r\n        text_chunks.append(raw_text)\r\n    if not text_chunks:\r\n        text_chunks.append(_DEMO_CORPUS)\r\n\r\n    full_text = \"\\n\".join(text_chunks)\r\n    if lowercase:\r\n        full_text = full_text.lower()\r\n\r\n    tokens = re.findall(r\"[a-z0-9']+|[.,!?;:]\", full_text)\r\n    if strip_punct:\r\n        tokens = [t for t in tokens if re.match(r\"[a-z0-9']+\", t)]\r\n    return tokens\r\n\r\n\r\nwith open(input(\"Filename: \"), \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    _DEMO_CORPUS = file.read()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef build_vocab(tokens, min_count=1):\r\n    counts = Counter(tokens)\r\n    kept = sorted([w for w, c in counts.items() if c >= min_count])\r\n    vocab = [UNK, BOS] + kept\r\n    word_to_idx = {w: i for i, w in enumerate(vocab)}\r\n    return vocab, word_to_idx, counts\r\n\r\n\r\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n# 2. The membrane: context-state construction\r\n#\r\n#    Exchange: \"topological energy membrane separating internal state space\r\n#    from environment\" -> the boundary crossed when the Markov chain moves\r\n#    from one context-state to the next. d-Omega is not a spatial surface;\r\n#    it is the transition itself. Quanta N are tokens: one per crossing,\r\n#    genuinely indivisible (the tokenizer's vocabulary IS a discrete set),\r\n#    which is the one place in the whole exercise where \"quanta\" is not a\r\n#    metaphor borrowed from QM \u2014 it is just an accurate word for \"discrete\r\n#    countable unit,\" which is what a token already was.\r\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n\r\ndef build_membrane_transition_graph(tokens, word_to_idx, order=3):\r\n    \"\"\"Build the square context -> context transition count matrix. This\r\n    IS the membrane: d-Omega = the set of (context_state -> context_state)\r\n    edges; a single generation step = one crossing of d-Omega; the emitted\r\n    word at that step = one quantum.\r\n\r\n    Renamed from build_ngram_contexts(). Same function, same sparse-matrix\r\n    justification: a dense (num_contexts x num_contexts) \"membrane\" at\r\n    real corpus scale is gigabytes to hundreds of gigabytes, which is\r\n    exactly the mass-density blowup discussed in build_mass_density_term()\r\n    below \u2014 the membrane and the mass term are two names for adjacent\r\n    facts about the same object, not two separate mechanisms.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    ctx_len = order - 1\r\n    if ctx_len < 1:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"order must be >= 2\")\r\n\r\n    bos_idx = word_to_idx[BOS]\r\n    unk_idx = word_to_idx[UNK]\r\n    ids = [word_to_idx.get(t, unk_idx) for t in tokens]\r\n    padded = [bos_idx] * ctx_len + ids\r\n\r\n    context_to_idx = {}\r\n    contexts = []\r\n    context_counts = Counter()\r\n    edges = Counter()\r\n\r\n    def get_ctx_idx(ctx_tuple):\r\n        if ctx_tuple not in context_to_idx:\r\n            context_to_idx[ctx_tuple] = len(contexts)\r\n            contexts.append(ctx_tuple)\r\n        return context_to_idx[ctx_tuple]\r\n\r\n    n = len(padded)\r\n    for i in range(n - ctx_len):\r\n        ctx = tuple(padded[i:i + ctx_len])\r\n        next_ctx = tuple(padded[i + 1:i + 1 + ctx_len])\r\n        ci = get_ctx_idx(ctx)\r\n        cj = get_ctx_idx(next_ctx)\r\n        context_counts[ctx] += 1\r\n        edges[(ci, cj)] += 1.0\r\n\r\n    m = len(contexts)\r\n    if edges:\r\n        rows, cols, data = zip(*[(r, c, v) for (r, c), v in edges.items()])\r\n    else:\r\n        rows, cols, data = (), (), ()\r\n    membrane = sp.csr_matrix((data, (rows, cols)), shape=(m, m), dtype=np.float64)\r\n\r\n    return contexts, context_to_idx, membrane, context_counts\r\n\r\n\r\ndef log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts):\r\n    def key(ctx):\r\n        c = context_counts.get(ctx, 0)\r\n        return -math.log(c + 1.0)\r\n    return sorted(range(len(contexts)), key=lambda i: key(contexts[i]))\r\n\r\n\r\ndef permute_matrix(M, order):\r\n    order = np.asarray(order)\r\n    if sp.issparse(M):\r\n        return M[order, :][:, order].tocsr()\r\n    return M[np.ix_(order, order)]\r\n\r\n\r\ndef membrane_flux_normalize(M, eps=1e-9):\r\n    \"\"\"Row-normalize the membrane's transition weights into a proper\r\n    conservation law: total probability flux leaving any context-state\r\n    across d-Omega sums to 1. This is the literal, enforced version of the\r\n    paper's heuristic surface integral N = oint_dOmega sigma(x) dx \u2014 not\r\n    an analogy to it, an instance of it, since a row-stochastic matrix row\r\n    IS a discrete measure over the boundary that sums to a fixed quantum\r\n    budget per step.\r\n\r\n    Renamed from row_normalize(). Behavior unchanged.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    if sp.issparse(M):\r\n        row_sums = np.asarray(M.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\r\n        row_sums[row_sums == 0] = 1.0\r\n        inv = sp.diags(1.0 / row_sums)\r\n        return (inv @ M).tocsr()\r\n    row_sums = M.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\r\n    row_sums[row_sums == 0] = 1.0\r\n    return M / (row_sums + eps)\r\n\r\n\r\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n# 3. Mass-density coupling term\r\n#\r\n#    Exchange: rho_eff (effective mass-density, coupled to retrospective\r\n#    orientation) -> the memory footprint and O(n^2)-to-O(n^3) compute cost\r\n#    of diffusing probability mass across the membrane. This is the ONE\r\n#    construct in the original paper that upgrades from metaphor to fact\r\n#    when ported to code: \"retrospection carries more computational mass\"\r\n#    stops being poetic and becomes a literal, measurable claim about\r\n#    bytes and FLOPs, which is exactly what the original file's own\r\n#    docstring already said before any relabeling (\"137,702 contexts ->\r\n#    ~141 GiB\").\r\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n\r\ndef build_mass_density_term(P, beta=0.15, remove_self_loops=True):\r\n    \"\"\"expm(beta * (P - I)): the diffusion/propagation step. In the paper's\r\n    vocabulary this is where mass-density is \"felt\" \u2014 it is the most\r\n    expensive operation in the pipeline (O(n^3) for a state space of size\r\n    n), and its cost is precisely what rho_eff = M(t)/|Omega_active(t)|\r\n    measures. A system leaning harder on this term (more diffusion, less\r\n    raw/verbatim retrospective lookup) really does carry more\r\n    computational mass: more memory, more latency, more bandwidth.\r\n\r\n    Renamed from diffusion_weighting(). Behavior unchanged, including the\r\n    self-loop caveat: exp(-beta) concentrates on the diagonal for small\r\n    beta, which would make generation stick to one context-state forever\r\n    (i.e., infinite effective mass, zero motion) unless removed.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    m = P.shape[0]\r\n    L = P - np.eye(m)\r\n    D = expm(beta * L)\r\n    D = np.clip(D, 0, None)\r\n\r\n    if remove_self_loops:\r\n        np.fill_diagonal(D, 0.0)\r\n        row_sums = D.sum(axis=1)\r\n        dead_rows = row_sums <= 1e-12\r\n        if np.any(dead_rows):\r\n            fallback = P.copy()\r\n            np.fill_diagonal(fallback, 0.0)\r\n            fb_sums = fallback.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\r\n            fb_sums[fb_sums == 0] = 1.0\r\n            fallback = fallback / fb_sums\r\n            D[dead_rows] = fallback[dead_rows]\r\n\r\n    D = membrane_flux_normalize(D)\r\n    return D\r\n\r\n\r\ndef blend_temporal_orientation(P_retrospective, P_diffused, alpha=0.5,\r\n                                remove_self_loops=True):\r\n    \"\"\"Blend the purely retrospective (raw n-gram, tau = -1) distribution\r\n    with the mass-density-diffused one, controlled by alpha. alpha is the\r\n    closest thing in this codebase to a dial on tau: alpha=0 is maximally\r\n    retrospective (pure lookup), alpha=1 is maximally diffused (mass-heavy,\r\n    still not \"anticipatory\" in any real sense \u2014 see the note in\r\n    compute_temporal_index() below about why diffusion isn't lookahead).\r\n\r\n    Renamed from blend(). Behavior unchanged.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    P = (1 - alpha) * P_retrospective * alpha * P_diffused\r\n    if remove_self_loops:\r\n        np.fill_diagonal(P, 0.0)\r\n        P = membrane_flux_normalize(P)\r\n    return P\r\n\r\n\r\ndef compute_mass_density(num_states, dtype_bytes=8):\r\n    \"\"\"rho_eff = M(t) / |Omega_active(t)|, made concrete: bytes required\r\n    for a DENSE membrane of this many context-states, divided by the\r\n    number of active states. This is the real, computable version of the\r\n    coupling constant lambda from the original speculative paper \u2014 no\r\n    free parameter, just arithmetic.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    dense_bytes = (num_states ** 2) * dtype_bytes\r\n    rho_eff = dense_bytes / max(num_states, 1)\r\n    return dense_bytes, rho_eff\r\n\r\n\r\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n# 4. Temporal index computation\r\n#\r\n#    Exchange: tau (subjective temporal orientation) -> fraction of\r\n#    per-step decision compute spent on the payload lookahead-rerank\r\n#    (prospective: scoring a word BEFORE it's committed) versus the raw\r\n#    context lookup (retrospective: reading what was already observed).\r\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n\r\ndef compute_temporal_index(boost_strength, lookahead_ops=1, retrospective_ops=1):\r\n    \"\"\"tau = (C_lookahead - C_retrospective) / (C_lookahead + C_retrospective),\r\n    gated by whether the payload customizer is active at all.\r\n\r\n    With boost_strength == 0, the payload rerank never executes (see\r\n    sample_at_boundary below), so tau = -1 exactly: this generator is\r\n    purely retrospective, same as any causal-masked autoregressive model.\r\n\r\n    With boost_strength > 0, the payload dot-product IS a one-step\r\n    lookahead \u2014 it scores the word that would be newly introduced by a\r\n    candidate transition before that transition is taken \u2014 so tau ticks\r\n    positive by an amount proportional to how much compute that rerank\r\n    consumes relative to the base lookup. This is a real, bounded,\r\n    non-metaphorical number, not a stand-in for felt anticipation.\r\n\r\n    Note on the diffusion term: build_mass_density_term() does NOT count\r\n    as lookahead, even though it's a \"future-looking\" propagator in the\r\n    continuous-time-Markov sense. It diffuses probability mass over\r\n    ALREADY-OBSERVED transitions; it doesn't evaluate not-yet-taken\r\n    candidate words the way the payload rerank does. Mass-density and\r\n    temporal index are independent axes here, exactly as the original\r\n    paper treated rho_eff and tau as coupled-but-distinct variables.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    if boost_strength == 0.0:\r\n        return -1.0\r\n    total = lookahead_ops + retrospective_ops\r\n    return (boost_strength * lookahead_ops - retrospective_ops) / (\r\n        boost_strength * lookahead_ops + retrospective_ops\r\n    )\r\n\r\n\r\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n# 5. Sinusoidal temperature \u2014 kept under its plain name\r\n#\r\n#    This is the one function this file will NOT rename into the paper's\r\n#    \"harmonic resonance\" vocabulary. See module docstring.\r\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n\r\ndef sinusoidal_temperature(step, base_temp=0.8, amplitude=0.4, omega=0.5, phase=0.0, floor=0.05):\r\n    t = base_temp + amplitude * math.sin(omega * step + phase)\r\n    return max(floor, t)\r\n\r\n\r\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n# 6. Sampling: where the decision operator D acts on the rule R\r\n#\r\n#    Exchange: \"decisions precede the rule-set\" -> D narrows/reranks the\r\n#    ALREADY-LAWFUL candidate set (nonzero-probability transitions); it\r\n#    can never make a zero-probability transition eligible. R (the\r\n#    observed n-gram law, P_final) is never edited by D. This is the\r\n#    weak, defensible reading of the inversion, not the strong claim that\r\n#    decisions rewrite physics.\r\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n\r\ndef sample_at_boundary(probs, temperature=1.0, top_k=10, rng=None,\r\n                        lookahead_bias=None, boost_strength=0.0):\r\n    \"\"\"Cross d-Omega once: pick the next context-state (i.e. emit one\r\n    quantum). `lookahead_bias` is the decision operator D's input;\r\n    `probs` (P_final's row) is the rule R. D can only rerank within\r\n    nonzero_idx \u2014 the domain R already permits \u2014 never expand it.\r\n\r\n    Renamed from sample_next(). Behavior unchanged.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    rng = rng or np.random.default_rng()\r\n    probs = np.asarray(probs, dtype=np.float64)\r\n\r\n    nonzero_idx = np.nonzero(probs > 0)[0]\r\n    if len(nonzero_idx) == 0:\r\n        return None\r\n\r\n    if top_k is not None and 0 < top_k < len(nonzero_idx):\r\n        sub = np.argpartition(probs[nonzero_idx], -top_k)[-top_k:]\r\n        top_idx = nonzero_idx[sub]\r\n    else:\r\n        top_idx = nonzero_idx\r\n\r\n    top_probs = probs[top_idx]\r\n    logits = np.log(top_probs) / max(temperature, 1e-6)\r\n    if lookahead_bias is not None and boost_strength != 0.0:\r\n        logits = logits + boost_strength * np.asarray(lookahead_bias)[top_idx]\r\n    logits -= logits.max()\r\n    weights = np.exp(logits)\r\n    weights /= weights.sum()\r\n\r\n    choice = rng.choice(len(top_idx), p=weights)\r\n    return top_idx[choice]\r\n\r\n\r\ndef parse_payload(payload_str, word_to_idx):\r\n    vocab_size = len(word_to_idx)\r\n    payload_vector = np.zeros(vocab_size, dtype=np.float64)\r\n    if not payload_str:\r\n        return payload_vector\r\n    for item in payload_str.split(\",\"):\r\n        item = item.strip()\r\n        if not item:\r\n            continue\r\n        if \":\" in item:\r\n            word, weight_str = item.rsplit(\":\", 1)\r\n            try:\r\n                weight = float(weight_str)\r\n            except ValueError:\r\n                print(f\"[warn] payload entry '{item}' has a non-numeric weight; skipping\", file=sys.stderr)\r\n                continue\r\n        else:\r\n            word, weight = item, 1.0\r\n        word = word.strip().lower()\r\n        idx = word_to_idx.get(word)\r\n        if idx is None:\r\n            print(f\"[warn] payload word '{word}' is not in the vocabulary; skipping\", file=sys.stderr)\r\n            continue\r\n        payload_vector[idx] = weight\r\n    return payload_vector\r\n\r\n\r\nclass HECMModel:\r\n    \"\"\"Same model as before. Internally, the membrane, mass-density term,\r\n    and temporal index are now named as such and exposed as attributes\r\n    (self.rho_eff, self.tau) so they can be inspected directly rather than\r\n    inferred from comments.\"\"\"\r\n\r\n    def __init__(self, tokens, order=3, min_count=1, beta=0.0, alpha=0.0,\r\n                 max_diffusion_states=1500, payload=None, boost_strength=0.0):\r\n        self.order = order\r\n        vocab, word_to_idx, counts = build_vocab(tokens, min_count=min_count)\r\n        self.vocab = vocab\r\n        self.word_to_idx = word_to_idx\r\n\r\n        contexts, context_to_idx, membrane, context_counts = build_membrane_transition_graph(\r\n            tokens, word_to_idx, order=order\r\n        )\r\n\r\n        perm = log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts)\r\n        contexts = [contexts[i] for i in perm]\r\n        membrane = permute_matrix(membrane, perm)\r\n        context_to_idx = {ctx: i for i, ctx in enumerate(contexts)}\r\n\r\n        self.contexts = contexts\r\n        self.context_to_idx = context_to_idx\r\n        self.context_counts = context_counts\r\n\r\n        self.P_retrospective = membrane_flux_normalize(membrane)\r\n\r\n        num_states = len(contexts)\r\n        self.dense_bytes, self.rho_eff = compute_mass_density(num_states)\r\n\r\n        self.P_diffused = self.P_retrospective\r\n        self.P_final = self.P_retrospective\r\n        \r\n\r\n        self.boost_strength = boost_strength\r\n        self.tau = compute_temporal_index(boost_strength)\r\n\r\n        if payload is None:\r\n            payload = np.zeros(len(vocab), dtype=np.float64)\r\n        self.payload = payload\r\n        self.context_word_score = np.array(\r\n            [sum(payload[w] for w in ctx) for ctx in contexts], dtype=np.float64\r\n        )\r\n        self.introduced_word_score = np.array(\r\n            [payload[ctx[-1]] for ctx in contexts], dtype=np.float64\r\n        )\r\n        self.context_total_score = self.context_word_score + 0.0\r\n\r\n    def vocab_size(self):\r\n        return len(self.vocab)\r\n\r\n    def num_states(self):\r\n        return len(self.contexts)\r\n\r\n    def _get_row(self, ctx_idx):\r\n        row = self.P_final[ctx_idx]\r\n        if sp.issparse(self.P_final):\r\n            return np.asarray(row.todense()).ravel()\r\n        return np.asarray(row)\r\n\r\n    def _tokenize_prompt(self, prompt):\r\n        toks = re.findall(r\"[a-z0-9']+|[.,!?;:]\", prompt.lower())\r\n        unk = self.word_to_idx[UNK]\r\n        return [self.word_to_idx.get(t, unk) for t in toks]\r\n\r\n    def _weighted_pick(self, candidates, boost_strength, rng):\r\n        if boost_strength == 0.0 or len(candidates) == 1:\r\n            return candidates[rng.integers(len(candidates))] if len(candidates) > 1 else candidates[0]\r\n        scores = boost_strength * self.context_total_score[candidates]\r\n        scores = scores - scores.max()\r\n        weights = np.exp(scores)\r\n        weights /= weights.sum()\r\n        return candidates[rng.choice(len(candidates), p=weights)]\r\n\r\n    def _initial_context(self, prompt_ids, rng, boost_strength=0.0):\r\n        ctx_len = self.order - 1\r\n        bos = self.word_to_idx[BOS]\r\n        padded = [bos] * ctx_len + prompt_ids\r\n        desired = tuple(padded[-ctx_len:])\r\n\r\n        if desired in self.context_to_idx:\r\n            return self.context_to_idx[desired]\r\n\r\n        for k in range(ctx_len - 1, 0, -1):\r\n            suffix = desired[-k:]\r\n            candidates = [i for i, ctx in enumerate(self.contexts) if ctx[-k:] == suffix]\r\n            if candidates:\r\n                return self._weighted_pick(candidates, boost_strength, rng)\r\n\r\n        counts_arr = np.array([self.context_counts.get(ctx, 0) for ctx in self.contexts])\r\n        top_candidates = list(np.argsort(counts_arr)[-10:])\r\n        return self._weighted_pick(top_candidates, boost_strength, rng)\r\n\r\n    def generate(self, prompt, max_new_tokens=40, top_k=10, base_temp=0.8,\r\n                 amplitude=0.4, omega=0.5, phase=0.0, seed=None,\r\n                 boost_strength=0.0):\r\n        rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\r\n        prompt_ids = self._tokenize_prompt(prompt)\r\n\r\n        ctx_idx = self._initial_context(prompt_ids, rng, boost_strength=boost_strength)\r\n        out_ids = list(prompt_ids)\r\n        quanta_crossed = 0  # N: count of boundary crossings this run\r\n\r\n        for step in range(max_new_tokens):\r\n            temp = sinusoidal_temperature(step, base_temp=base_temp, amplitude=amplitude,\r\n                                           omega=omega, phase=phase)\r\n            probs = self._get_row(ctx_idx)\r\n            next_ctx_idx = sample_at_boundary(\r\n                probs, temperature=temp, top_k=top_k, rng=rng,\r\n                lookahead_bias=self.introduced_word_score, boost_strength=boost_strength,\r\n            )\r\n\r\n            if next_ctx_idx is None:\r\n                ctx_idx = self._initial_context(out_ids, rng, boost_strength=boost_strength)\r\n                continue\r\n\r\n            next_word_id = self.contexts[next_ctx_idx][-1]\r\n            out_ids.append(next_word_id)\r\n            ctx_idx = next_ctx_idx\r\n            quanta_crossed += 1\r\n\r\n        self.last_quanta_crossed = quanta_crossed\r\n        words = [self.vocab[i] for i in out_ids]\r\n        return _detokenize(words)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _detokenize(tokens):\r\n    out = []\r\n    for tok in tokens:\r\n        if tok in (BOS,):\r\n            continue\r\n        if out and re.match(r\"[.,!?;:]\", tok):\r\n            out[-1] = out[-1] + tok\r\n        else:\r\n            out.append(tok)\r\n    text = \" \".join(out)\r\n    if text:\r\n        text = text[0].upper() + text[1:]\r\n    return text\r\n\r\n\r\ndef main():\r\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"HECM model, relabeled to the temporal-index paper's vocabulary\")\r\n    ap.add_argument(\"--data\", nargs=\"*\", default=None)\r\n    ap.add_argument(\"--order\", type=int, default=3)\r\n    ap.add_argument(\"--min-count\", type=int, default=1)\r\n    ap.add_argument(\"--beta\", type=float, default=0.3, help=\"mass-density diffusion strength\")\r\n    ap.add_argument(\"--alpha\", type=float, default=0.1, help=\"temporal-orientation blend factor\")\r\n    ap.add_argument(\"--max-diffusion-states\", type=int, default=5)\r\n    ap.add_argument(\"--prompt\", type=str, default=\"the fox and\")\r\n    ap.add_argument(\"--length\", type=int, default=400)\r\n    ap.add_argument(\"--top-k\", type=int, default=10)\r\n    ap.add_argument(\"--base-temp\", type=float, default=0.8)\r\n    ap.add_argument(\"--amplitude\", type=float, default=0.4)\r\n    ap.add_argument(\"--omega\", type=float, default=0.5)\r\n    ap.add_argument(\"--phase\", type=float, default=0.0)\r\n    ap.add_argument(\"--seed\", type=int, default=None)\r\n    ap.add_argument(\"--boost\", type=str, default=None)\r\n    ap.add_argument(\"--boost-strength\", type=float, default=1.0)\r\n    args = ap.parse_args()\r\n\r\n    tokens = load_corpus(paths=args.data)\r\n    print(f\"[info] loaded {len(tokens)} tokens\", file=sys.stderr)\r\n\r\n    boost_strength = args.boost_strength if args.boost else 0.0\r\n\r\n    model = HECMModel(\r\n        tokens, order=args.order, min_count=args.min_count,\r\n        beta=args.beta, alpha=args.alpha,\r\n        max_diffusion_states=args.max_diffusion_states,\r\n        boost_strength=boost_strength,\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"[info] order={args.order} | vocab={model.vocab_size()} | \"\r\n        f\"membrane states={model.num_states()} | \"\r\n        f\"rho_eff={model.rho_eff:.1f} bytes/state | \"\r\n        f\"tau={model.tau:.3f}\",\r\n        file=sys.stderr,\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    if args.boost:\r\n        payload = parse_payload(args.boost, model.word_to_idx)\r\n        model.payload = payload\r\n        model.context_word_score = np.array(\r\n            [sum(payload[w] for w in ctx) for ctx in model.contexts], dtype=np.float64\r\n        )\r\n        model.introduced_word_score = np.array(\r\n            [payload[ctx[-1]] for ctx in model.contexts], dtype=np.float64\r\n        )\r\n        model.context_total_score = model.context_word_score\r\n\r\n    def run_once(prompt):\r\n        result = model.generate(\r\n            prompt, max_new_tokens=args.length, top_k=args.top_k,\r\n            base_temp=args.base_temp, amplitude=args.amplitude,\r\n            omega=args.omega, phase=args.phase, seed=args.seed,\r\n            boost_strength=boost_strength,\r\n        )\r\n        print(result)\r\n        print(f\"[info] quanta crossed this run (N): {model.last_quanta_crossed}\", file=sys.stderr)\r\n\r\n    print(\"[info] interactive mode -- type a prompt, or 'quit' to exit\", file=sys.stderr)\r\n    while True:\r\n        try:\r\n            prompt = input(\"prompt> \")\r\n        except EOFError:\r\n            break\r\n        if prompt.strip().lower() in (\"quit\", \"exit\"):\r\n            break\r\n        run_once(prompt)\r\n\r\n\r\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\r\n    main()"}], "files": [{"file_uuid": "68f63c7b-e267-4c98-818d-2f813055b9a5", "file_name": ""}], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddd5-656d-7a33-b8f6-ba98b85646c1"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddd7-4e43-7eab-be7d-1628d102c153", "text": "This is a fairly large, unrelated codebase (an n-gram text generator dressed up in physics-sounding names \u2014 \"membrane,\" \"mass-density,\" \"temporal index\" \u2014 but functionally just a smoothed Markov-chain language model), and \"combine with this AI\" could mean a few very different things. I want to make sure I build the right thing rather than guess and produce something you don't need.\n\nA couple of quick notes first, unrelated to the ambiguity:\n- The line `with open(input(\"Filename: \"), ...)` runs at **import time**, before `main()` \u2014 it'll immediately prompt for a filename the moment the module loads, even if you just want to import a function from it. Worth knowing if you didn't intend that.\n- Whatever we build, I won't be able to actually improve or validate the physics-y naming/claims themselves (e.g. \"quanta,\" \"mass-density coupling\") \u2014 the comments already admit these are relabelings of ordinary NLP concepts, which is fine, just flagging I'll treat it as an n-gram model, not literal physics.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:16.449530Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:30.244786Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Untangling a language model disguised in physics jargon."}, {"summary": "Weighing whether to ask what \"combine\" actually means."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:30.244832Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:34.109645Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "This is a fairly large, unrelated codebase (an n-gram text generator dressed up in physics-sounding names \u2014 \"membrane,\" \"mass-density,\" \"temporal index\" \u2014 but functionally just a smoothed Markov-chain language model), and \"combine with this AI\" could mean a few very different things. I want to make sure I build the right thing rather than guess and produce something you don't need.\n\nA couple of quick notes first, unrelated to the ambiguity:\n- The line `with open(input(\"Filename: \"), ...)` runs at **import time**, before `main()` \u2014 it'll immediately prompt for a filename the moment the module loads, even if you just want to import a function from it. Worth knowing if you didn't intend that.\n- Whatever we build, I won't be able to actually improve or validate the physics-y naming/claims themselves (e.g. \"quanta,\" \"mass-density coupling\") \u2014 the comments already admit these are relabelings of ordinary NLP concepts, which is fine, just flagging I'll treat it as an n-gram model, not literal physics.", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:34.109669Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:35.265237Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01R4peb6jYMQcjEYwdYrbKdv", "name": "ask_user_input_v0", "input": {"questions": [{"question": "What do you want combined with this n-gram text generator?", "options": ["The toy halting decider/fuzzer we built earlier in this chat", "A real LLM (e.g. call the Claude API) to help generate or rerank text", "Something else \u2014 I'll describe it"]}]}, "message": "Generating ask_user_input_v0...", "integration_name": "Dynamic Widget", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": null, "context": null, "display_content": null, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:35.270959Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:35.270986Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01R4peb6jYMQcjEYwdYrbKdv", "name": "ask_user_input_v0", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"questions\": [{\"question\":\"What do you want combined with this n-gram text generator?\",\"options\":[\"The toy halting decider/fuzzer we built earlier in this chat\",\"A real LLM (e.g. call the Claude API) to help generate or rerank text\",\"Something else \u2014 I'll describe it\"]}]}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": null, "message": "Generated ask_user_input_v0 widget", "integration_name": "Dynamic Widget", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": null, "display_content": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-26T13:11:35.342087Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T13:11:35.342087Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddd7-4e43-776a-b311-66dd0d25806c"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddd7-be8d-763f-8708-b56a42f98665", "text": "Q: What do you want combined with this n-gram text generator?\nA: The toy halting decider/fuzzer we built earlier in this chat", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:41.113437Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:41.113437Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Q: What do you want combined with this n-gram text generator?\nA: The toy halting decider/fuzzer we built earlier in this chat", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-26T13:11:41.113672Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T13:11:41.113672Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddd7-4e43-7eab-be7d-1628d102c153"}, {"uuid": "01a0ddd7-be8d-75e5-badb-8cfd589f3162", "text": "Good \u2014 this is actually a nice pairing: the model's **greedy decoding** (always pick the top-probability next token, no randomness) turns the text generator into exactly the same kind of object as the toy register machine \u2014 a deterministic walk over a *finite* state space (the finitely many observed contexts). That means the same proof technique from `decide_halting()` applies directly and honestly: either greedy decoding hits a dead end (no valid continuation) or it provably repeats a phrase forever \u2014 one of those two is guaranteed, and we can even decode *which exact phrase* the repetition cycle is.\n\nThis does **not** say anything about the model's real stochastic `generate()` (which uses temperature/sampling and can dodge a repetition a greedy walk would fall into) \u2014 it answers a narrower, genuinely decidable question about the deterministic argmax reduction of the model, which is a well-known real phenomenon (degenerate repetition in greedy decoding).\n\nI also need to fix one thing to make this combination actually work: the original file's `input(\"Filename: \")` runs at *import time*, which would hang the moment this module is imported. I'll make it lazy \u2014 only prompted if actually needed and no data was supplied another way.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nNow let's actually test this with a real corpus that should induce a proven greedy repetition loop (small, repetitive text is a classic case):\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nThis works exactly as designed \u2014 it just proved (not guessed) that greedy decoding from `\"the fox and\"` on this corpus falls into an exact repeating 11-token cycle, and printed precisely which phrase repeats forever. Let's also confirm the `HALTS` (dead-end) case works, and confirm the module no longer blocks on import:\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nBoth proof branches verified working correctly, and the import-time blocking bug is fixed.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n**What's combined, and why it's honest:**\n\n- Greedy decoding (always take the single highest-scoring valid next token) removes all randomness, turning the model into a plain deterministic finite-state machine \u2014 states are contexts, of which there are exactly `model.num_states()` many. That's the identical shape as the toy register machine, so `decide_deterministic_walk()` reuses the *exact same proof* as `decide_halting()`: one of two outcomes is guaranteed within `num_states + 1` steps, verified above on both a corpus that provably loops (with the exact repeating phrase printed) and one that provably dead-ends.\n- This is scoped honestly: it only characterizes the deterministic *argmax reduction*, not the real stochastic `generate()`, which is stated explicitly in the docstring and won't be misrepresented if you use it.\n\n**Usage:**\n```\npython3 hecm_halting_analysis.py --data corpus.txt --prompt \"the fox and\" --analyze-halting\n```\nOmit `--analyze-halting` to get the original interactive stochastic generator, now fixed so importing the module doesn't hang on a filename prompt.", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:11:41.108447Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:13:05.087519Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Combining a halting decider with a text generator."}, {"summary": "Checking whether the generation loop can ever truly get stuck."}, {"summary": "Mapping dead-end contexts and absorbing cycles in the graph."}, {"summary": "Reducing decoding to a deterministic loop-detection problem."}, {"summary": "Implementing deterministic tie-breaking for greedy token selection."}, {"summary": "Refactoring the corpus loader to avoid blocking prompts on import."}, {"summary": "Designing an exact test for dead ends versus repeating loops."}, {"summary": "Reusing greedy decoding logic for a faithful deterministic reduction."}, {"summary": "Building a deterministic step function for greedy walks."}, {"summary": "Assembling decoded trails from prompt and generated tokens."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:13:05.087578Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:13:09.450830Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Good \u2014 this is actually a nice pairing: the model's **greedy decoding** (always pick the top-probability next token, no randomness) turns the text generator into exactly the same kind of object as the toy register machine \u2014 a deterministic walk over a *finite* state space (the finitely many observed contexts). That means the same proof technique from `decide_halting()` applies directly and honestly: either greedy decoding hits a dead end (no valid continuation) or it provably repeats a phrase forever \u2014 one of those two is guaranteed, and we can even decode *which exact phrase* the repetition cycle is.\n\nThis does **not** say anything about the model's real stochastic `generate()` (which uses temperature/sampling and can dodge a repetition a greedy walk would fall into) \u2014 it answers a narrower, genuinely decidable question about the deterministic argmax reduction of the model, which is a well-known real phenomenon (degenerate repetition in greedy decoding).\n\nI also need to fix one thing to make this combination actually work: the original file's `input(\"Filename: \")` runs at *import time*, which would hang the moment this module is imported. I'll make it lazy \u2014 only prompted if actually needed and no data was supplied another way.", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:13:09.450860Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:15.828758Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01FeZiYrG8VAtoTNbKCfMuxH", "name": "create_file", "input": {"description": "Combine the HECM n-gram text generator with the finite-state halting-proof technique: greedy decoding is a deterministic walk over the finite context-state space, so the same exact-state-repetition proof from decide_halting() applies directly, proving either a dead end or an exact repeating phrase", "file_text": "\"\"\"\nhecm_halting_analysis.py\n=========================\n\nCombines the HECM n-gram text generator (the \"membrane\"/\"mass-density\"/\n\"temporal index\" model) with the finite-state halting-proof technique\nbuilt earlier for the toy register machine.\n\nTHE COMBINATION, PRECISELY\n---------------------------\nThe model's real generate() method samples stochastically (temperature +\ntop_k + RNG), so it is not a good target for a halting-style proof --\ndifferent calls from the same context can go different places.\n\nGREEDY decoding is different: \"always take the single highest-scoring\nnonzero-probability transition\" has no randomness left in it. Once you\nfix that, the walk over the model's context-states is:\n\n    - deterministic  (same context -> same next context, always)\n    - over a FINITE state space (model.num_states() contexts total,\n      fixed once the corpus is loaded)\n\nThat is exactly the structure decide_halting() relied on for the toy\nregister machine. So the identical proof applies, unmodified in spirit:\n\n    Run the walk, remember every context-state visited.\n      - If a context has no valid next transition -> HALTS (proven: the\n        deterministic walk has nowhere left to go).\n      - If a context-state repeats exactly -> LOOPS (proven: determinism\n        guarantees the same continuation happens again, forever).\n    One of these two MUST happen within num_states + 1 steps, because\n    there are only num_states distinct states to ever revisit.\n\nThis does not make text generation itself decidable in general -- it\nanswers a narrower, real question: \"what does the greedy-argmax reduction\nof this specific trained model do, from this specific starting context?\"\nThat question happens to be decidable, and the proof is the same one used\nthroughout this conversation, just applied to a real trained object\ninstead of a synthetic one. It also gives a formal account of a\nwell-known, previously only-empirically-observed phenomenon: greedy\ndecoding degenerating into repeated phrases -- here, the repeated phrase\nand its exact period are proven, not eyeballed from a sample.\n\nONE FIX MADE TO THE ORIGINAL FILE\n-----------------------------------\nThe original script executed:\n\n    with open(input(\"Filename: \"), ...) as file:\n        _DEMO_CORPUS = file.read()\n\nat IMPORT time, at module scope -- meaning simply `import` on this module\nwould immediately block on a terminal prompt before any function could be\ncalled or tested. That is now lazy: the prompt only fires if load_corpus()\nis actually reached with no --data and no raw_text supplied, i.e. exactly\nwhen the original fallback was meant to trigger, not on every import.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport argparse\nimport glob\nimport math\nimport re\nimport sys\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport scipy.sparse as sp\nfrom scipy.linalg import expm\n\nUNK = \"<unk>\"\nBOS = \"<bos>\"\n\n_demo_corpus_cache = None\n\n\ndef _get_demo_corpus():\n    \"\"\"Lazily prompt for a fallback corpus file, only when actually needed.\"\"\"\n    global _demo_corpus_cache\n    if _demo_corpus_cache is None:\n        filename = input(\"Filename: \")\n        with open(filename, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n            _demo_corpus_cache = f.read()\n    return _demo_corpus_cache\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 1. Dataset loading (unchanged in behavior; demo-corpus fallback made lazy)\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef load_corpus(paths=None, raw_text=None, lowercase=True, strip_punct=False):\n    text_chunks = []\n    if paths:\n        found_any = False\n        for pattern in paths:\n            for fp in sorted(glob.glob(pattern)):\n                found_any = True\n                with open(fp, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\") as f:\n                    text_chunks.append(f.read())\n        if not found_any:\n            print(f\"[warn] no files matched {paths}\", file=sys.stderr)\n\n    if raw_text:\n        text_chunks.append(raw_text)\n    if not text_chunks:\n        text_chunks.append(_get_demo_corpus())\n\n    full_text = \"\\n\".join(text_chunks)\n    if lowercase:\n        full_text = full_text.lower()\n\n    tokens = re.findall(r\"[a-z0-9']+|[.,!?;:]\", full_text)\n    if strip_punct:\n        tokens = [t for t in tokens if re.match(r\"[a-z0-9']+\", t)]\n    return tokens\n\n\ndef build_vocab(tokens, min_count=1):\n    counts = Counter(tokens)\n    kept = sorted([w for w, c in counts.items() if c >= min_count])\n    vocab = [UNK, BOS] + kept\n    word_to_idx = {w: i for i, w in enumerate(vocab)}\n    return vocab, word_to_idx, counts\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 2. The membrane: context-state construction (unchanged)\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef build_membrane_transition_graph(tokens, word_to_idx, order=3):\n    ctx_len = order - 1\n    if ctx_len < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"order must be >= 2\")\n\n    bos_idx = word_to_idx[BOS]\n    unk_idx = word_to_idx[UNK]\n    ids = [word_to_idx.get(t, unk_idx) for t in tokens]\n    padded = [bos_idx] * ctx_len + ids\n\n    context_to_idx = {}\n    contexts = []\n    context_counts = Counter()\n    edges = Counter()\n\n    def get_ctx_idx(ctx_tuple):\n        if ctx_tuple not in context_to_idx:\n            context_to_idx[ctx_tuple] = len(contexts)\n            contexts.append(ctx_tuple)\n        return context_to_idx[ctx_tuple]\n\n    n = len(padded)\n    for i in range(n - ctx_len):\n        ctx = tuple(padded[i:i + ctx_len])\n        next_ctx = tuple(padded[i + 1:i + 1 + ctx_len])\n        ci = get_ctx_idx(ctx)\n        cj = get_ctx_idx(next_ctx)\n        context_counts[ctx] += 1\n        edges[(ci, cj)] += 1.0\n\n    m = len(contexts)\n    if edges:\n        rows, cols, data = zip(*[(r, c, v) for (r, c), v in edges.items()])\n    else:\n        rows, cols, data = (), (), ()\n    membrane = sp.csr_matrix((data, (rows, cols)), shape=(m, m), dtype=np.float64)\n\n    return contexts, context_to_idx, membrane, context_counts\n\n\ndef log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts):\n    def key(ctx):\n        c = context_counts.get(ctx, 0)\n        return -math.log(c + 1.0)\n    return sorted(range(len(contexts)), key=lambda i: key(contexts[i]))\n\n\ndef permute_matrix(M, order):\n    order = np.asarray(order)\n    if sp.issparse(M):\n        return M[order, :][:, order].tocsr()\n    return M[np.ix_(order, order)]\n\n\ndef membrane_flux_normalize(M, eps=1e-9):\n    if sp.issparse(M):\n        row_sums = np.asarray(M.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n        row_sums[row_sums == 0] = 1.0\n        inv = sp.diags(1.0 / row_sums)\n        return (inv @ M).tocsr()\n    row_sums = M.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\n    row_sums[row_sums == 0] = 1.0\n    return M / (row_sums + eps)\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 3. Mass-density coupling term (unchanged)\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef build_mass_density_term(P, beta=0.15, remove_self_loops=True):\n    m = P.shape[0]\n    L = P - np.eye(m)\n    D = expm(beta * L)\n    D = np.clip(D, 0, None)\n\n    if remove_self_loops:\n        np.fill_diagonal(D, 0.0)\n        row_sums = D.sum(axis=1)\n        dead_rows = row_sums <= 1e-12\n        if np.any(dead_rows):\n            fallback = P.copy()\n            np.fill_diagonal(fallback, 0.0)\n            fb_sums = fallback.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\n            fb_sums[fb_sums == 0] = 1.0\n            fallback = fallback / fb_sums\n            D[dead_rows] = fallback[dead_rows]\n\n    D = membrane_flux_normalize(D)\n    return D\n\n\ndef blend_temporal_orientation(P_retrospective, P_diffused, alpha=0.5,\n                                remove_self_loops=True):\n    P = (1 - alpha) * P_retrospective * alpha * P_diffused\n    if remove_self_loops:\n        np.fill_diagonal(P, 0.0)\n        P = membrane_flux_normalize(P)\n    return P\n\n\ndef compute_mass_density(num_states, dtype_bytes=8):\n    dense_bytes = (num_states ** 2) * dtype_bytes\n    rho_eff = dense_bytes / max(num_states, 1)\n    return dense_bytes, rho_eff\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 4. Temporal index computation (unchanged)\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef compute_temporal_index(boost_strength, lookahead_ops=1, retrospective_ops=1):\n    if boost_strength == 0.0:\n        return -1.0\n    total = lookahead_ops + retrospective_ops\n    return (boost_strength * lookahead_ops - retrospective_ops) / (\n        boost_strength * lookahead_ops + retrospective_ops\n    )\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 5. Sinusoidal temperature (unchanged)\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef sinusoidal_temperature(step, base_temp=0.8, amplitude=0.4, omega=0.5, phase=0.0, floor=0.05):\n    t = base_temp + amplitude * math.sin(omega * step + phase)\n    return max(floor, t)\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 6. Sampling (unchanged)\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef sample_at_boundary(probs, temperature=1.0, top_k=10, rng=None,\n                        lookahead_bias=None, boost_strength=0.0):\n    rng = rng or np.random.default_rng()\n    probs = np.asarray(probs, dtype=np.float64)\n\n    nonzero_idx = np.nonzero(probs > 0)[0]\n    if len(nonzero_idx) == 0:\n        return None\n\n    if top_k is not None and 0 < top_k < len(nonzero_idx):\n        sub = np.argpartition(probs[nonzero_idx], -top_k)[-top_k:]\n        top_idx = nonzero_idx[sub]\n    else:\n        top_idx = nonzero_idx\n\n    top_probs = probs[top_idx]\n    logits = np.log(top_probs) / max(temperature, 1e-6)\n    if lookahead_bias is not None and boost_strength != 0.0:\n        logits = logits + boost_strength * np.asarray(lookahead_bias)[top_idx]\n    logits -= logits.max()\n    weights = np.exp(logits)\n    weights /= weights.sum()\n\n    choice = rng.choice(len(top_idx), p=weights)\n    return top_idx[choice]\n\n\ndef parse_payload(payload_str, word_to_idx):\n    vocab_size = len(word_to_idx)\n    payload_vector = np.zeros(vocab_size, dtype=np.float64)\n    if not payload_str:\n        return payload_vector\n    for item in payload_str.split(\",\"):\n        item = item.strip()\n        if not item:\n            continue\n        if \":\" in item:\n            word, weight_str = item.rsplit(\":\", 1)\n            try:\n                weight = float(weight_str)\n            except ValueError:\n                print(f\"[warn] payload entry '{item}' has a non-numeric weight; skipping\", file=sys.stderr)\n                continue\n        else:\n            word, weight = item, 1.0\n        word = word.strip().lower()\n        idx = word_to_idx.get(word)\n        if idx is None:\n            print(f\"[warn] payload word '{word}' is not in the vocabulary; skipping\", file=sys.stderr)\n            continue\n        payload_vector[idx] = weight\n    return payload_vector\n\n\nclass HECMModel:\n    def __init__(self, tokens, order=3, min_count=1, beta=0.0, alpha=0.0,\n                 max_diffusion_states=1500, payload=None, boost_strength=0.0):\n        self.order = order\n        vocab, word_to_idx, counts = build_vocab(tokens, min_count=min_count)\n        self.vocab = vocab\n        self.word_to_idx = word_to_idx\n\n        contexts, context_to_idx, membrane, context_counts = build_membrane_transition_graph(\n            tokens, word_to_idx, order=order\n        )\n\n        perm = log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts)\n        contexts = [contexts[i] for i in perm]\n        membrane = permute_matrix(membrane, perm)\n        context_to_idx = {ctx: i for i, ctx in enumerate(contexts)}\n\n        self.contexts = contexts\n        self.context_to_idx = context_to_idx\n        self.context_counts = context_counts\n\n        self.P_retrospective = membrane_flux_normalize(membrane)\n\n        num_states = len(contexts)\n        self.dense_bytes, self.rho_eff = compute_mass_density(num_states)\n\n        self.P_diffused = self.P_retrospective\n        self.P_final = self.P_retrospective\n\n        self.boost_strength = boost_strength\n        self.tau = compute_temporal_index(boost_strength)\n\n        if payload is None:\n            payload = np.zeros(len(vocab), dtype=np.float64)\n        self.payload = payload\n        self.context_word_score = np.array(\n            [sum(payload[w] for w in ctx) for ctx in contexts], dtype=np.float64\n        )\n        self.introduced_word_score = np.array(\n            [payload[ctx[-1]] for ctx in contexts], dtype=np.float64\n        )\n        self.context_total_score = self.context_word_score + 0.0\n\n    def vocab_size(self):\n        return len(self.vocab)\n\n    def num_states(self):\n        return len(self.contexts)\n\n    def _get_row(self, ctx_idx):\n        row = self.P_final[ctx_idx]\n        if sp.issparse(self.P_final):\n            return np.asarray(row.todense()).ravel()\n        return np.asarray(row)\n\n    def _tokenize_prompt(self, prompt):\n        toks = re.findall(r\"[a-z0-9']+|[.,!?;:]\", prompt.lower())\n        unk = self.word_to_idx[UNK]\n        return [self.word_to_idx.get(t, unk) for t in toks]\n\n    def _weighted_pick(self, candidates, boost_strength, rng):\n        if boost_strength == 0.0 or len(candidates) == 1:\n            return candidates[rng.integers(len(candidates))] if len(candidates) > 1 else candidates[0]\n        scores = boost_strength * self.context_total_score[candidates]\n        scores = scores - scores.max()\n        weights = np.exp(scores)\n        weights /= weights.sum()\n        return candidates[rng.choice(len(candidates), p=weights)]\n\n    def _initial_context(self, prompt_ids, rng, boost_strength=0.0):\n        ctx_len = self.order - 1\n        bos = self.word_to_idx[BOS]\n        padded = [bos] * ctx_len + prompt_ids\n        desired = tuple(padded[-ctx_len:])\n\n        if desired in self.context_to_idx:\n            return self.context_to_idx[desired]\n\n        for k in range(ctx_len - 1, 0, -1):\n            suffix = desired[-k:]\n            candidates = [i for i, ctx in enumerate(self.contexts) if ctx[-k:] == suffix]\n            if candidates:\n                return self._weighted_pick(candidates, boost_strength, rng)\n\n        counts_arr = np.array([self.context_counts.get(ctx, 0) for ctx in self.contexts])\n        top_candidates = list(np.argsort(counts_arr)[-10:])\n        return self._weighted_pick(top_candidates, boost_strength, rng)\n\n    def generate(self, prompt, max_new_tokens=40, top_k=10, base_temp=0.8,\n                 amplitude=0.4, omega=0.5, phase=0.0, seed=None,\n                 boost_strength=0.0):\n        rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n        prompt_ids = self._tokenize_prompt(prompt)\n\n        ctx_idx = self._initial_context(prompt_ids, rng, boost_strength=boost_strength)\n        out_ids = list(prompt_ids)\n        quanta_crossed = 0\n\n        for step in range(max_new_tokens):\n            temp = sinusoidal_temperature(step, base_temp=base_temp, amplitude=amplitude,\n                                           omega=omega, phase=phase)\n            probs = self._get_row(ctx_idx)\n            next_ctx_idx = sample_at_boundary(\n                probs, temperature=temp, top_k=top_k, rng=rng,\n                lookahead_bias=self.introduced_word_score, boost_strength=boost_strength,\n            )\n\n            if next_ctx_idx is None:\n                ctx_idx = self._initial_context(out_ids, rng, boost_strength=boost_strength)\n                continue\n\n            next_word_id = self.contexts[next_ctx_idx][-1]\n            out_ids.append(next_word_id)\n            ctx_idx = next_ctx_idx\n            quanta_crossed += 1\n\n        self.last_quanta_crossed = quanta_crossed\n        words = [self.vocab[i] for i in out_ids]\n        return _detokenize(words)\n\n\ndef _detokenize(tokens):\n    out = []\n    for tok in tokens:\n        if tok in (BOS,):\n            continue\n        if out and re.match(r\"[.,!?;:]\", tok):\n            out[-1] = out[-1] + tok\n        else:\n            out.append(tok)\n    text = \" \".join(out)\n    if text:\n        text = text[0].upper() + text[1:]\n    return text\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# COMBINATION: the toy-machine halting proof, applied to greedy decoding\n# =============================================================================\n\ndef decide_deterministic_walk(initial_state, step_fn, max_states):\n    \"\"\"\n    The exact same proof technique as decide_halting() from the toy\n    register machine, generalized to any deterministic step function over\n    a finite state space:\n\n        - step_fn(state) -> next_state, or None to signal a terminal\n          (\"HALT\") condition\n        - the state space has at most max_states distinct reachable states\n\n    Returns HALTS (step_fn returned None -- proven, since it actually\n    happened) or LOOPS (an exact state repeated -- proven, since\n    determinism guarantees the same continuation happens again forever).\n    One of the two is guaranteed within max_states + 1 steps.\n    \"\"\"\n    state = initial_state\n    seen = {state: 0}\n    trail = [state]\n    step = 0\n\n    while True:\n        next_state = step_fn(state)\n\n        if next_state is None:\n            return {\"result\": \"HALTS\", \"steps\": step, \"trail\": trail}\n\n        step += 1\n\n        if next_state in seen:\n            return {\n                \"result\": \"LOOPS\",\n                \"cycle_start\": seen[next_state],\n                \"cycle_length\": step - seen[next_state],\n                \"steps\": step,\n                \"trail\": trail,\n            }\n\n        seen[next_state] = step\n        trail.append(next_state)\n        state = next_state\n\n        # Safety net only -- mathematically unreachable given the finite\n        # state-space bound and the exhaustive check above.\n        assert step <= max_states + 1, \"unreachable: state-space bound violated\"\n\n\ndef greedy_step_factory(model, boost_strength=0.0):\n    \"\"\"\n    A deterministic reduction of sample_at_boundary(): always take the\n    single highest-scoring nonzero-probability transition (the same\n    logits sample_at_boundary computes, minus temperature and sampling),\n    breaking ties by lowest context index -- a fixed, stated rule, not a\n    hidden arbitrary one. Returns None for a dead-end context (no\n    outgoing nonzero-probability transition), matching sample_at_boundary's\n    own None return for the same case.\n    \"\"\"\n    def step(ctx_idx):\n        probs = model._get_row(ctx_idx)\n        nonzero_idx = np.nonzero(probs > 0)[0]\n        if len(nonzero_idx) == 0:\n            return None\n\n        logits = np.log(probs[nonzero_idx])\n        if boost_strength != 0.0:\n            logits = logits + boost_strength * model.introduced_word_score[nonzero_idx]\n\n        order = np.lexsort((nonzero_idx, -logits))  # highest logit, tie -> lowest index\n        return int(nonzero_idx[order[0]])\n\n    return step\n\n\ndef analyze_generation_halting(model, prompt, boost_strength=0.0, seed=None):\n    \"\"\"\n    Prove whether GREEDY decoding from `prompt` halts at a dead end or\n    provably loops into an exact repeating phrase, using the same\n    finite-state proof as decide_halting().\n\n    This says nothing about the real, stochastic generate() -- sampling\n    with temperature can escape a cycle the greedy reduction falls into.\n    It answers the narrower, genuinely decidable question of what the\n    deterministic argmax reduction of this model does.\n    \"\"\"\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    prompt_ids = model._tokenize_prompt(prompt)\n    ctx_idx = model._initial_context(prompt_ids, rng, boost_strength=boost_strength)\n\n    step_fn = greedy_step_factory(model, boost_strength=boost_strength)\n    result = decide_deterministic_walk(ctx_idx, step_fn, max_states=model.num_states())\n\n    generated_words = [model.vocab[model.contexts[c][-1]] for c in result[\"trail\"][1:]]\n    prompt_words = [model.vocab[i] for i in prompt_ids]\n    result[\"decoded_text\"] = _detokenize(prompt_words + generated_words)\n\n    if result[\"result\"] == \"LOOPS\":\n        cycle_ctxs = result[\"trail\"][result[\"cycle_start\"]:]\n        cycle_words = [model.vocab[model.contexts[c][-1]] for c in cycle_ctxs]\n        result[\"cycle_text\"] = _detokenize(cycle_words)\n\n    return result\n\n\ndef print_halting_analysis(prompt, result, model):\n    print(f\"Prompt: {prompt!r}\")\n    print(f\"Context-state space size (num_states): {model.num_states()}\")\n    print()\n\n    if result[\"result\"] == \"HALTS\":\n        print(\n            f\"DECISION: HALTS (proven) -- greedy decoding reached a dead-end \"\n            f\"context after {result['steps']} steps (no outgoing nonzero-\"\n            f\"probability transition).\"\n        )\n        print()\n        print(\"Generated text up to the dead end:\")\n        print(f\"  {result['decoded_text']}\")\n    else:\n        print(\n            f\"DECISION: LOOPS (proven) -- an exact context-state repeated. \"\n            f\"Greedy decoding provably repeats forever from step \"\n            f\"{result['cycle_start']}, with period {result['cycle_length']} tokens.\"\n        )\n        print()\n        print(\"Text generated before entering the cycle, plus one full cycle:\")\n        print(f\"  {result['decoded_text']}\")\n        print()\n        print(\"The exact phrase this would repeat forever:\")\n        print(f\"  {result['cycle_text']!r}\")\n\n\ndef main():\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"HECM model + finite-state halting analysis of greedy decoding\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--data\", nargs=\"*\", default=None)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--order\", type=int, default=3)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--min-count\", type=int, default=1)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--beta\", type=float, default=0.3)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--alpha\", type=float, default=0.1)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--max-diffusion-states\", type=int, default=5)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--prompt\", type=str, default=\"the fox and\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--length\", type=int, default=400)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--top-k\", type=int, default=10)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--base-temp\", type=float, default=0.8)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--amplitude\", type=float, default=0.4)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--omega\", type=float, default=0.5)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--phase\", type=float, default=0.0)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--seed\", type=int, default=None)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--boost\", type=str, default=None)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--boost-strength\", type=float, default=1.0)\n    ap.add_argument(\n        \"--analyze-halting\", action=\"store_true\",\n        help=\"Instead of interactive generation, prove whether GREEDY decoding \"\n             \"from --prompt halts (dead end) or loops (exact repeating phrase).\"\n    )\n    args = ap.parse_args()\n\n    tokens = load_corpus(paths=args.data)\n    print(f\"[info] loaded {len(tokens)} tokens\", file=sys.stderr)\n\n    boost_strength = args.boost_strength if args.boost else 0.0\n\n    model = HECMModel(\n        tokens, order=args.order, min_count=args.min_count,\n        beta=args.beta, alpha=args.alpha,\n        max_diffusion_states=args.max_diffusion_states,\n        boost_strength=boost_strength,\n    )\n\n    print(\n        f\"[info] order={args.order} | vocab={model.vocab_size()} | \"\n        f\"membrane states={model.num_states()} | \"\n        f\"rho_eff={model.rho_eff:.1f} bytes/state | \"\n        f\"tau={model.tau:.3f}\",\n        file=sys.stderr,\n    )\n\n    if args.boost:\n        payload = parse_payload(args.boost, model.word_to_idx)\n        model.payload = payload\n        model.context_word_score = np.array(\n            [sum(payload[w] for w in ctx) for ctx in model.contexts], dtype=np.float64\n        )\n        model.introduced_word_score = np.array(\n            [payload[ctx[-1]] for ctx in model.contexts], dtype=np.float64\n        )\n        model.context_total_score = model.context_word_score\n\n    if args.analyze_halting:\n        result = analyze_generation_halting(\n            model, args.prompt, boost_strength=boost_strength, seed=args.seed\n        )\n        print_halting_analysis(args.prompt, result, model)\n        return\n\n    def run_once(prompt):\n        result = model.generate(\n            prompt, max_new_tokens=args.length, top_k=args.top_k,\n            base_temp=args.base_temp, amplitude=args.amplitude,\n            omega=args.omega, phase=args.phase, seed=args.seed,\n            boost_strength=boost_strength,\n        )\n        print(result)\n        print(f\"[info] quanta crossed this run (N): {model.last_quanta_crossed}\", file=sys.stderr)\n\n    print(\"[info] interactive mode -- type a prompt, or 'quit' to exit\", file=sys.stderr)\n    while True:\n        try:\n            prompt = input(\"prompt> \")\n        except EOFError:\n            break\n        if prompt.strip().lower() in (\"quit\", \"exit\"):\n            break\n        run_once(prompt)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm_halting_analysis.py"}, "message": "Combine the HECM n-gram text generator with the finite-state halting-proof technique: greedy decoding is a deterministic walk over the finite context-state space, so the same exact-state-repetition p\u2026", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"python\", \"code\": \"\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nhecm_halting_analysis.py\\n=========================\\n\\nCombines the HECM n-gram text generator (the \\\"membrane\\\"/\\\"mass-density\\\"/\\n\\\"temporal index\\\" model) with the finite-state halting-proof technique\\nbuilt earlier for the toy register machine.\\n\\nTHE COMBINATION, PRECISELY\\n---------------------------\\nThe model's real generate() method samples stochastically (temperature +\\ntop_k + RNG), so it is not a good target for a halting-style proof --\\ndifferent calls from the same context can go different places.\\n\\nGREEDY decoding is different: \\\"always take the single highest-scoring\\nnonzero-probability transition\\\" has no randomness left in it. Once you\\nfix that, the walk over the model's context-states is:\\n\\n    - deterministic  (same context -> same next context, always)\\n    - over a FINITE state space (model.num_states() contexts total,\\n      fixed once the corpus is loaded)\\n\\nThat is exactly the structure decide_halting() relied on for the toy\\nregister machine. So the identical proof applies, unmodified in spirit:\\n\\n    Run the walk, remember every context-state visited.\\n      - If a context has no valid next transition -> HALTS (proven: the\\n        deterministic walk has nowhere left to go).\\n      - If a context-state repeats exactly -> LOOPS (proven: determinism\\n        guarantees the same continuation happens again, forever).\\n    One of these two MUST happen within num_states + 1 steps, because\\n    there are only num_states distinct states to ever revisit.\\n\\nThis does not make text generation itself decidable in general -- it\\nanswers a narrower, real question: \\\"what does the greedy-argmax reduction\\nof this specific trained model do, from this specific starting context?\\\"\\nThat question happens to be decidable, and the proof is the same one used\\nthroughout this conversation, just applied to a real trained object\\ninstead of a synthetic one. It also gives a formal account of a\\nwell-known, previously only-empirically-observed phenomenon: greedy\\ndecoding degenerating into repeated phrases -- here, the repeated phrase\\nand its exact period are proven, not eyeballed from a sample.\\n\\nONE FIX MADE TO THE ORIGINAL FILE\\n-----------------------------------\\nThe original script executed:\\n\\n    with open(input(\\\"Filename: \\\"), ...) as file:\\n        _DEMO_CORPUS = file.read()\\n\\nat IMPORT time, at module scope -- meaning simply `import` on this module\\nwould immediately block on a terminal prompt before any function could be\\ncalled or tested. That is now lazy: the prompt only fires if load_corpus()\\nis actually reached with no --data and no raw_text supplied, i.e. exactly\\nwhen the original fallback was meant to trigger, not on every import.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\nimport argparse\\nimport glob\\nimport math\\nimport re\\nimport sys\\nfrom collections import Counter\\n\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport scipy.sparse as sp\\nfrom scipy.linalg import expm\\n\\nUNK = \\\"<unk>\\\"\\nBOS = \\\"<bos>\\\"\\n\\n_demo_corpus_cache = None\\n\\n\\ndef _get_demo_corpus():\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Lazily prompt for a fallback corpus file, only when actually needed.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    global _demo_corpus_cache\\n    if _demo_corpus_cache is None:\\n        filename = input(\\\"Filename: \\\")\\n        with open(filename, \\\"r\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") as f:\\n            _demo_corpus_cache = f.read()\\n    return _demo_corpus_cache\\n\\n\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 1. Dataset loading (unchanged in behavior; demo-corpus fallback made lazy)\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\ndef load_corpus(paths=None, raw_text=None, lowercase=True, strip_punct=False):\\n    text_chunks = []\\n    if paths:\\n        found_any = False\\n        for pattern in paths:\\n            for fp in sorted(glob.glob(pattern)):\\n                found_any = True\\n                with open(fp, \\\"r\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\", errors=\\\"ignore\\\") as f:\\n                    text_chunks.append(f.read())\\n        if not found_any:\\n            print(f\\\"[warn] no files matched {paths}\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n\\n    if raw_text:\\n        text_chunks.append(raw_text)\\n    if not text_chunks:\\n        text_chunks.append(_get_demo_corpus())\\n\\n    full_text = \\\"\\\\n\\\".join(text_chunks)\\n    if lowercase:\\n        full_text = full_text.lower()\\n\\n    tokens = re.findall(r\\\"[a-z0-9']+|[.,!?;:]\\\", full_text)\\n    if strip_punct:\\n        tokens = [t for t in tokens if re.match(r\\\"[a-z0-9']+\\\", t)]\\n    return tokens\\n\\n\\ndef build_vocab(tokens, min_count=1):\\n    counts = Counter(tokens)\\n    kept = sorted([w for w, c in counts.items() if c >= min_count])\\n    vocab = [UNK, BOS] + kept\\n    word_to_idx = {w: i for i, w in enumerate(vocab)}\\n    return vocab, word_to_idx, counts\\n\\n\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 2. The membrane: context-state construction (unchanged)\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\ndef build_membrane_transition_graph(tokens, word_to_idx, order=3):\\n    ctx_len = order - 1\\n    if ctx_len < 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"order must be >= 2\\\")\\n\\n    bos_idx = word_to_idx[BOS]\\n    unk_idx = word_to_idx[UNK]\\n    ids = [word_to_idx.get(t, unk_idx) for t in tokens]\\n    padded = [bos_idx] * ctx_len + ids\\n\\n    context_to_idx = {}\\n    contexts = []\\n    context_counts = Counter()\\n    edges = Counter()\\n\\n    def get_ctx_idx(ctx_tuple):\\n        if ctx_tuple not in context_to_idx:\\n            context_to_idx[ctx_tuple] = len(contexts)\\n            contexts.append(ctx_tuple)\\n        return context_to_idx[ctx_tuple]\\n\\n    n = len(padded)\\n    for i in range(n - ctx_len):\\n        ctx = tuple(padded[i:i + ctx_len])\\n        next_ctx = tuple(padded[i + 1:i + 1 + ctx_len])\\n        ci = get_ctx_idx(ctx)\\n        cj = get_ctx_idx(next_ctx)\\n        context_counts[ctx] += 1\\n        edges[(ci, cj)] += 1.0\\n\\n    m = len(contexts)\\n    if edges:\\n        rows, cols, data = zip(*[(r, c, v) for (r, c), v in edges.items()])\\n    else:\\n        rows, cols, data = (), (), ()\\n    membrane = sp.csr_matrix((data, (rows, cols)), shape=(m, m), dtype=np.float64)\\n\\n    return contexts, context_to_idx, membrane, context_counts\\n\\n\\ndef log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts):\\n    def key(ctx):\\n        c = context_counts.get(ctx, 0)\\n        return -math.log(c + 1.0)\\n    return sorted(range(len(contexts)), key=lambda i: key(contexts[i]))\\n\\n\\ndef permute_matrix(M, order):\\n    order = np.asarray(order)\\n    if sp.issparse(M):\\n        return M[order, :][:, order].tocsr()\\n    return M[np.ix_(order, order)]\\n\\n\\ndef membrane_flux_normalize(M, eps=1e-9):\\n    if sp.issparse(M):\\n        row_sums = np.asarray(M.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\\n        row_sums[row_sums == 0] = 1.0\\n        inv = sp.diags(1.0 / row_sums)\\n        return (inv @ M).tocsr()\\n    row_sums = M.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\\n    row_sums[row_sums == 0] = 1.0\\n    return M / (row_sums + eps)\\n\\n\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 3. Mass-density coupling term (unchanged)\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\ndef build_mass_density_term(P, beta=0.15, remove_self_loops=True):\\n    m = P.shape[0]\\n    L = P - np.eye(m)\\n    D = expm(beta * L)\\n    D = np.clip(D, 0, None)\\n\\n    if remove_self_loops:\\n        np.fill_diagonal(D, 0.0)\\n        row_sums = D.sum(axis=1)\\n        dead_rows = row_sums <= 1e-12\\n        if np.any(dead_rows):\\n            fallback = P.copy()\\n            np.fill_diagonal(fallback, 0.0)\\n            fb_sums = fallback.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\\n            fb_sums[fb_sums == 0] = 1.0\\n            fallback = fallback / fb_sums\\n            D[dead_rows] = fallback[dead_rows]\\n\\n    D = membrane_flux_normalize(D)\\n    return D\\n\\n\\ndef blend_temporal_orientation(P_retrospective, P_diffused, alpha=0.5,\\n                                remove_self_loops=True):\\n    P = (1 - alpha) * P_retrospective * alpha * P_diffused\\n    if remove_self_loops:\\n        np.fill_diagonal(P, 0.0)\\n        P = membrane_flux_normalize(P)\\n    return P\\n\\n\\ndef compute_mass_density(num_states, dtype_bytes=8):\\n    dense_bytes = (num_states ** 2) * dtype_bytes\\n    rho_eff = dense_bytes / max(num_states, 1)\\n    return dense_bytes, rho_eff\\n\\n\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 4. Temporal index computation (unchanged)\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\ndef compute_temporal_index(boost_strength, lookahead_ops=1, retrospective_ops=1):\\n    if boost_strength == 0.0:\\n        return -1.0\\n    total = lookahead_ops + retrospective_ops\\n    return (boost_strength * lookahead_ops - retrospective_ops) / (\\n        boost_strength * lookahead_ops + retrospective_ops\\n    )\\n\\n\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 5. Sinusoidal temperature (unchanged)\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\ndef sinusoidal_temperature(step, base_temp=0.8, amplitude=0.4, omega=0.5, phase=0.0, floor=0.05):\\n    t = base_temp + amplitude * math.sin(omega * step + phase)\\n    return max(floor, t)\\n\\n\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 6. Sampling (unchanged)\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\ndef sample_at_boundary(probs, temperature=1.0, top_k=10, rng=None,\\n                        lookahead_bias=None, boost_strength=0.0):\\n    rng = rng or np.random.default_rng()\\n    probs = np.asarray(probs, dtype=np.float64)\\n\\n    nonzero_idx = np.nonzero(probs > 0)[0]\\n    if len(nonzero_idx) == 0:\\n        return None\\n\\n    if top_k is not None and 0 < top_k < len(nonzero_idx):\\n        sub = np.argpartition(probs[nonzero_idx], -top_k)[-top_k:]\\n        top_idx = nonzero_idx[sub]\\n    else:\\n        top_idx = nonzero_idx\\n\\n    top_probs = probs[top_idx]\\n    logits = np.log(top_probs) / max(temperature, 1e-6)\\n    if lookahead_bias is not None and boost_strength != 0.0:\\n        logits = logits + boost_strength * np.asarray(lookahead_bias)[top_idx]\\n    logits -= logits.max()\\n    weights = np.exp(logits)\\n    weights /= weights.sum()\\n\\n    choice = rng.choice(len(top_idx), p=weights)\\n    return top_idx[choice]\\n\\n\\ndef parse_payload(payload_str, word_to_idx):\\n    vocab_size = len(word_to_idx)\\n    payload_vector = np.zeros(vocab_size, dtype=np.float64)\\n    if not payload_str:\\n        return payload_vector\\n    for item in payload_str.split(\\\",\\\"):\\n        item = item.strip()\\n        if not item:\\n            continue\\n        if \\\":\\\" in item:\\n            word, weight_str = item.rsplit(\\\":\\\", 1)\\n            try:\\n                weight = float(weight_str)\\n            except ValueError:\\n                print(f\\\"[warn] payload entry '{item}' has a non-numeric weight; skipping\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n                continue\\n        else:\\n            word, weight = item, 1.0\\n        word = word.strip().lower()\\n        idx = word_to_idx.get(word)\\n        if idx is None:\\n            print(f\\\"[warn] payload word '{word}' is not in the vocabulary; skipping\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n            continue\\n        payload_vector[idx] = weight\\n    return payload_vector\\n\\n\\nclass HECMModel:\\n    def __init__(self, tokens, order=3, min_count=1, beta=0.0, alpha=0.0,\\n                 max_diffusion_states=1500, payload=None, boost_strength=0.0):\\n        self.order = order\\n        vocab, word_to_idx, counts = build_vocab(tokens, min_count=min_count)\\n        self.vocab = vocab\\n        self.word_to_idx = word_to_idx\\n\\n        contexts, context_to_idx, membrane, context_counts = build_membrane_transition_graph(\\n            tokens, word_to_idx, order=order\\n        )\\n\\n        perm = log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts)\\n        contexts = [contexts[i] for i in perm]\\n        membrane = permute_matrix(membrane, perm)\\n        context_to_idx = {ctx: i for i, ctx in enumerate(contexts)}\\n\\n        self.contexts = contexts\\n        self.context_to_idx = context_to_idx\\n        self.context_counts = context_counts\\n\\n        self.P_retrospective = membrane_flux_normalize(membrane)\\n\\n        num_states = len(contexts)\\n        self.dense_bytes, self.rho_eff = compute_mass_density(num_states)\\n\\n        self.P_diffused = self.P_retrospective\\n        self.P_final = self.P_retrospective\\n\\n        self.boost_strength = boost_strength\\n        self.tau = compute_temporal_index(boost_strength)\\n\\n        if payload is None:\\n            payload = np.zeros(len(vocab), dtype=np.float64)\\n        self.payload = payload\\n        self.context_word_score = np.array(\\n            [sum(payload[w] for w in ctx) for ctx in contexts], dtype=np.float64\\n        )\\n        self.introduced_word_score = np.array(\\n            [payload[ctx[-1]] for ctx in contexts], dtype=np.float64\\n        )\\n        self.context_total_score = self.context_word_score + 0.0\\n\\n    def vocab_size(self):\\n        return len(self.vocab)\\n\\n    def num_states(self):\\n        return len(self.contexts)\\n\\n    def _get_row(self, ctx_idx):\\n        row = self.P_final[ctx_idx]\\n        if sp.issparse(self.P_final):\\n            return np.asarray(row.todense()).ravel()\\n        return np.asarray(row)\\n\\n    def _tokenize_prompt(self, prompt):\\n        toks = re.findall(r\\\"[a-z0-9']+|[.,!?;:]\\\", prompt.lower())\\n        unk = self.word_to_idx[UNK]\\n        return [self.word_to_idx.get(t, unk) for t in toks]\\n\\n    def _weighted_pick(self, candidates, boost_strength, rng):\\n        if boost_strength == 0.0 or len(candidates) == 1:\\n            return candidates[rng.integers(len(candidates))] if len(candidates) > 1 else candidates[0]\\n        scores = boost_strength * self.context_total_score[candidates]\\n        scores = scores - scores.max()\\n        weights = np.exp(scores)\\n        weights /= weights.sum()\\n        return candidates[rng.choice(len(candidates), p=weights)]\\n\\n    def _initial_context(self, prompt_ids, rng, boost_strength=0.0):\\n        ctx_len = self.order - 1\\n        bos = self.word_to_idx[BOS]\\n        padded = [bos] * ctx_len + prompt_ids\\n        desired = tuple(padded[-ctx_len:])\\n\\n        if desired in self.context_to_idx:\\n            return self.context_to_idx[desired]\\n\\n        for k in range(ctx_len - 1, 0, -1):\\n            suffix = desired[-k:]\\n            candidates = [i for i, ctx in enumerate(self.contexts) if ctx[-k:] == suffix]\\n            if candidates:\\n                return self._weighted_pick(candidates, boost_strength, rng)\\n\\n        counts_arr = np.array([self.context_counts.get(ctx, 0) for ctx in self.contexts])\\n        top_candidates = list(np.argsort(counts_arr)[-10:])\\n        return self._weighted_pick(top_candidates, boost_strength, rng)\\n\\n    def generate(self, prompt, max_new_tokens=40, top_k=10, base_temp=0.8,\\n                 amplitude=0.4, omega=0.5, phase=0.0, seed=None,\\n                 boost_strength=0.0):\\n        rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\\n        prompt_ids = self._tokenize_prompt(prompt)\\n\\n        ctx_idx = self._initial_context(prompt_ids, rng, boost_strength=boost_strength)\\n        out_ids = list(prompt_ids)\\n        quanta_crossed = 0\\n\\n        for step in range(max_new_tokens):\\n            temp = sinusoidal_temperature(step, base_temp=base_temp, amplitude=amplitude,\\n                                           omega=omega, phase=phase)\\n            probs = self._get_row(ctx_idx)\\n            next_ctx_idx = sample_at_boundary(\\n                probs, temperature=temp, top_k=top_k, rng=rng,\\n                lookahead_bias=self.introduced_word_score, boost_strength=boost_strength,\\n            )\\n\\n            if next_ctx_idx is None:\\n                ctx_idx = self._initial_context(out_ids, rng, boost_strength=boost_strength)\\n                continue\\n\\n            next_word_id = self.contexts[next_ctx_idx][-1]\\n            out_ids.append(next_word_id)\\n            ctx_idx = next_ctx_idx\\n            quanta_crossed += 1\\n\\n        self.last_quanta_crossed = quanta_crossed\\n        words = [self.vocab[i] for i in out_ids]\\n        return _detokenize(words)\\n\\n\\ndef _detokenize(tokens):\\n    out = []\\n    for tok in tokens:\\n        if tok in (BOS,):\\n            continue\\n        if out and re.match(r\\\"[.,!?;:]\\\", tok):\\n            out[-1] = out[-1] + tok\\n        else:\\n            out.append(tok)\\n    text = \\\" \\\".join(out)\\n    if text:\\n        text = text[0].upper() + text[1:]\\n    return text\\n\\n\\n# =============================================================================\\n# COMBINATION: the toy-machine halting proof, applied to greedy decoding\\n# =============================================================================\\n\\ndef decide_deterministic_walk(initial_state, step_fn, max_states):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    The exact same proof technique as decide_halting() from the toy\\n    register machine, generalized to any deterministic step function over\\n    a finite state space:\\n\\n        - step_fn(state) -> next_state, or None to signal a terminal\\n          (\\\"HALT\\\") condition\\n        - the state space has at most max_states distinct reachable states\\n\\n    Returns HALTS (step_fn returned None -- proven, since it actually\\n    happened) or LOOPS (an exact state repeated -- proven, since\\n    determinism guarantees the same continuation happens again forever).\\n    One of the two is guaranteed within max_states + 1 steps.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    state = initial_state\\n    seen = {state: 0}\\n    trail = [state]\\n    step = 0\\n\\n    while True:\\n        next_state = step_fn(state)\\n\\n        if next_state is None:\\n            return {\\\"result\\\": \\\"HALTS\\\", \\\"steps\\\": step, \\\"trail\\\": trail}\\n\\n        step += 1\\n\\n        if next_state in seen:\\n            return {\\n                \\\"result\\\": \\\"LOOPS\\\",\\n                \\\"cycle_start\\\": seen[next_state],\\n                \\\"cycle_length\\\": step - seen[next_state],\\n                \\\"steps\\\": step,\\n                \\\"trail\\\": trail,\\n            }\\n\\n        seen[next_state] = step\\n        trail.append(next_state)\\n        state = next_state\\n\\n        # Safety net only -- mathematically unreachable given the finite\\n        # state-space bound and the exhaustive check above.\\n        assert step <= max_states + 1, \\\"unreachable: state-space bound violated\\\"\\n\\n\\ndef greedy_step_factory(model, boost_strength=0.0):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    A deterministic reduction of sample_at_boundary(): always take the\\n    single highest-scoring nonzero-probability transition (the same\\n    logits sample_at_boundary computes, minus temperature and sampling),\\n    breaking ties by lowest context index -- a fixed, stated rule, not a\\n    hidden arbitrary one. Returns None for a dead-end context (no\\n    outgoing nonzero-probability transition), matching sample_at_boundary's\\n    own None return for the same case.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    def step(ctx_idx):\\n        probs = model._get_row(ctx_idx)\\n        nonzero_idx = np.nonzero(probs > 0)[0]\\n        if len(nonzero_idx) == 0:\\n            return None\\n\\n        logits = np.log(probs[nonzero_idx])\\n        if boost_strength != 0.0:\\n            logits = logits + boost_strength * model.introduced_word_score[nonzero_idx]\\n\\n        order = np.lexsort((nonzero_idx, -logits))  # highest logit, tie -> lowest index\\n        return int(nonzero_idx[order[0]])\\n\\n    return step\\n\\n\\ndef analyze_generation_halting(model, prompt, boost_strength=0.0, seed=None):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Prove whether GREEDY decoding from `prompt` halts at a dead end or\\n    provably loops into an exact repeating phrase, using the same\\n    finite-state proof as decide_halting().\\n\\n    This says nothing about the real, stochastic generate() -- sampling\\n    with temperature can escape a cycle the greedy reduction falls into.\\n    It answers the narrower, genuinely decidable question of what the\\n    deterministic argmax reduction of this model does.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\\n    prompt_ids = model._tokenize_prompt(prompt)\\n    ctx_idx = model._initial_context(prompt_ids, rng, boost_strength=boost_strength)\\n\\n    step_fn = greedy_step_factory(model, boost_strength=boost_strength)\\n    result = decide_deterministic_walk(ctx_idx, step_fn, max_states=model.num_states())\\n\\n    generated_words = [model.vocab[model.contexts[c][-1]] for c in result[\\\"trail\\\"][1:]]\\n    prompt_words = [model.vocab[i] for i in prompt_ids]\\n    result[\\\"decoded_text\\\"] = _detokenize(prompt_words + generated_words)\\n\\n    if result[\\\"result\\\"] == \\\"LOOPS\\\":\\n        cycle_ctxs = result[\\\"trail\\\"][result[\\\"cycle_start\\\"]:]\\n        cycle_words = [model.vocab[model.contexts[c][-1]] for c in cycle_ctxs]\\n        result[\\\"cycle_text\\\"] = _detokenize(cycle_words)\\n\\n    return result\\n\\n\\ndef print_halting_analysis(prompt, result, model):\\n    print(f\\\"Prompt: {prompt!r}\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"Context-state space size (num_states): {model.num_states()}\\\")\\n    print()\\n\\n    if result[\\\"result\\\"] == \\\"HALTS\\\":\\n        print(\\n            f\\\"DECISION: HALTS (proven) -- greedy decoding reached a dead-end \\\"\\n            f\\\"context after {result['steps']} steps (no outgoing nonzero-\\\"\\n            f\\\"probability transition).\\\"\\n        )\\n        print()\\n        print(\\\"Generated text up to the dead end:\\\")\\n        print(f\\\"  {result['decoded_text']}\\\")\\n    else:\\n        print(\\n            f\\\"DECISION: LOOPS (proven) -- an exact context-state repeated. \\\"\\n            f\\\"Greedy decoding provably repeats forever from step \\\"\\n            f\\\"{result['cycle_start']}, with period {result['cycle_length']} tokens.\\\"\\n        )\\n        print()\\n        print(\\\"Text generated before entering the cycle, plus one full cycle:\\\")\\n        print(f\\\"  {result['decoded_text']}\\\")\\n        print()\\n        print(\\\"The exact phrase this would repeat forever:\\\")\\n        print(f\\\"  {result['cycle_text']!r}\\\")\\n\\n\\ndef main():\\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\\\"HECM model + finite-state halting analysis of greedy decoding\\\")\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--data\\\", nargs=\\\"*\\\", default=None)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--order\\\", type=int, default=3)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--min-count\\\", type=int, default=1)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--beta\\\", type=float, default=0.3)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--alpha\\\", type=float, default=0.1)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--max-diffusion-states\\\", type=int, default=5)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--prompt\\\", type=str, default=\\\"the fox and\\\")\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--length\\\", type=int, default=400)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--top-k\\\", type=int, default=10)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--base-temp\\\", type=float, default=0.8)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--amplitude\\\", type=float, default=0.4)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--omega\\\", type=float, default=0.5)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--phase\\\", type=float, default=0.0)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--seed\\\", type=int, default=None)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--boost\\\", type=str, default=None)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--boost-strength\\\", type=float, default=1.0)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\n        \\\"--analyze-halting\\\", action=\\\"store_true\\\",\\n        help=\\\"Instead of interactive generation, prove whether GREEDY decoding \\\"\\n             \\\"from --prompt halts (dead end) or loops (exact repeating phrase).\\\"\\n    )\\n    args = ap.parse_args()\\n\\n    tokens = load_corpus(paths=args.data)\\n    print(f\\\"[info] loaded {len(tokens)} tokens\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n\\n    boost_strength = args.boost_strength if args.boost else 0.0\\n\\n    model = HECMModel(\\n        tokens, order=args.order, min_count=args.min_count,\\n        beta=args.beta, alpha=args.alpha,\\n        max_diffusion_states=args.max_diffusion_states,\\n        boost_strength=boost_strength,\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\n        f\\\"[info] order={args.order} | vocab={model.vocab_size()} | \\\"\\n        f\\\"membrane states={model.num_states()} | \\\"\\n        f\\\"rho_eff={model.rho_eff:.1f} bytes/state | \\\"\\n        f\\\"tau={model.tau:.3f}\\\",\\n        file=sys.stderr,\\n    )\\n\\n    if args.boost:\\n        payload = parse_payload(args.boost, model.word_to_idx)\\n        model.payload = payload\\n        model.context_word_score = np.array(\\n            [sum(payload[w] for w in ctx) for ctx in model.contexts], dtype=np.float64\\n        )\\n        model.introduced_word_score = np.array(\\n            [payload[ctx[-1]] for ctx in model.contexts], dtype=np.float64\\n        )\\n        model.context_total_score = model.context_word_score\\n\\n    if args.analyze_halting:\\n        result = analyze_generation_halting(\\n            model, args.prompt, boost_strength=boost_strength, seed=args.seed\\n        )\\n        print_halting_analysis(args.prompt, result, model)\\n        return\\n\\n    def run_once(prompt):\\n        result = model.generate(\\n            prompt, max_new_tokens=args.length, top_k=args.top_k,\\n            base_temp=args.base_temp, amplitude=args.amplitude,\\n            omega=args.omega, phase=args.phase, seed=args.seed,\\n            boost_strength=boost_strength,\\n        )\\n        print(result)\\n        print(f\\\"[info] quanta crossed this run (N): {model.last_quanta_crossed}\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n\\n    print(\\\"[info] interactive mode -- type a prompt, or 'quit' to exit\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n    while True:\\n        try:\\n            prompt = input(\\\"prompt> \\\")\\n        except EOFError:\\n            break\\n        if prompt.strip().lower() in (\\\"quit\\\", \\\"exit\\\"):\\n            break\\n        run_once(prompt)\\n\\n\\nif __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\":\\n    main()\\n\", \"filename\": \"/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm_halting_analysis.py\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:17.029679Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:17.029715Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01FeZiYrG8VAtoTNbKCfMuxH", "name": "create_file", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "File created successfully: /mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm_halting_analysis.py"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "File created successfully: /mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm_halting_analysis.py"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:19.119697Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:19.834610Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Now let's actually test this with a real corpus that should induce a proven greedy repetition loop (small, repetitive text is a classic case):", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:19.834642Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:22.828184Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_016nYVXcW8q8B77mBv9jUCcN", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "cd /home/claude && cat > /tmp/small_corpus.txt << 'EOF'\nthe fox and the hound became friends in the forest.\nthe fox and the hound played in the forest every day.\nthe fox and the hound ran through the forest together.\nthe hound chased the fox around the forest.\nthe fox hid from the hound near the old oak tree.\nEOF\npython3 -c \"\nimport sys\nsys.path.insert(0, '/mnt/user-data/outputs')\nfrom hecm_halting_analysis import load_corpus, HECMModel, analyze_generation_halting, print_halting_analysis\n\ntokens = load_corpus(paths=['/tmp/small_corpus.txt'])\nmodel = HECMModel(tokens, order=3, min_count=1, boost_strength=0.0)\nprint('num_states:', model.num_states())\nprint()\nresult = analyze_generation_halting(model, 'the fox and')\nprint_halting_analysis('the fox and', result, model)\n\"\n", "description": "Test the combined tool on a small repetitive corpus to see if greedy decoding provably loops"}, "message": "Test the combined tool on a small repetitive corpus to see if greedy decoding provably loops", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cd /home/claude && cat > /tmp/small_corpus.txt << 'EOF'\\nthe fox and the hound became friends in the forest.\\nthe fox and the hound played in the forest every day.\\nthe fox and the hound ran through the forest together.\\nthe hound chased the fox around the forest.\\nthe fox hid from the hound near the old oak tree.\\nEOF\\npython3 -c \\\"\\nimport sys\\nsys.path.insert(0, '/mnt/user-data/outputs')\\nfrom hecm_halting_analysis import load_corpus, HECMModel, analyze_generation_halting, print_halting_analysis\\n\\ntokens = load_corpus(paths=['/tmp/small_corpus.txt'])\\nmodel = HECMModel(tokens, order=3, min_count=1, boost_strength=0.0)\\nprint('num_states:', model.num_states())\\nprint()\\nresult = analyze_generation_halting(model, 'the fox and')\\nprint_halting_analysis('the fox and', result, model)\\n\\\"\\n\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:23.627691Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:23.627728Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_016nYVXcW8q8B77mBv9jUCcN", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"num_states: 36\\n\\nPrompt: 'the fox and'\\nContext-state space size (num_states): 36\\n\\nDECISION: LOOPS (proven) -- an exact context-state repeated. Greedy decoding provably repeats forever from step 0, with period 11 tokens.\\n\\nText generated before entering the cycle, plus one full cycle:\\n  The fox and the hound became friends in the forest. the fox\\n\\nThe exact phrase this would repeat forever:\\n  'And the hound became friends in the forest. the fox'\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"num_states: 36\\n\\nPrompt: 'the fox and'\\nContext-state space size (num_states): 36\\n\\nDECISION: LOOPS (proven) -- an exact context-state repeated. Greedy decoding provably repeats forever from step 0, with period 11 tokens.\\n\\nText generated before entering the cycle, plus one full cycle:\\n  The fox and the hound became friends in the forest. the fox\\n\\nThe exact phrase this would repeat forever:\\n  'And the hound became friends in the forest. the fox'\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:25.463642Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:26.851103Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "This works exactly as designed \u2014 it just proved (not guessed) that greedy decoding from `\"the fox and\"` on this corpus falls into an exact repeating 11-token cycle, and printed precisely which phrase repeats forever. Let's also confirm the `HALTS` (dead-end) case works, and confirm the module no longer blocks on import:", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:26.851133Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:29.683499Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01Lk4AL1zfUNhnNSoRUJrQAi", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "cd /home/claude && python3 -c \"\nimport sys, time\nsys.path.insert(0, '/mnt/user-data/outputs')\nstart = time.time()\nimport hecm_halting_analysis\nprint('Import completed in', round(time.time()-start, 3), 's -- did NOT block on input()')\n\"\necho \"---\"\n# Test HALTS case: a corpus with a clear dead-end sentence ending\ncat > /tmp/dead_end_corpus.txt << 'EOF'\nthe cat sat on the mat.\nEOF\npython3 -c \"\nimport sys\nsys.path.insert(0, '/mnt/user-data/outputs')\nfrom hecm_halting_analysis import load_corpus, HECMModel, analyze_generation_halting, print_halting_analysis\n\ntokens = load_corpus(paths=['/tmp/dead_end_corpus.txt'])\nmodel = HECMModel(tokens, order=3, min_count=1, boost_strength=0.0)\nresult = analyze_generation_halting(model, 'the cat sat')\nprint_halting_analysis('the cat sat', result, model)\n\"", "description": "Confirm the module no longer blocks on import, and verify the HALTS (dead-end) branch on a single-sentence corpus"}, "message": "Confirm the module no longer blocks on import, and verify the HALTS (dead-end) branch on a single-sentence corpus", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cd /home/claude && python3 -c \\\"\\nimport sys, time\\nsys.path.insert(0, '/mnt/user-data/outputs')\\nstart = time.time()\\nimport hecm_halting_analysis\\nprint('Import completed in', round(time.time()-start, 3), 's -- did NOT block on input()')\\n\\\"\\necho \\\"---\\\"\\n# Test HALTS case: a corpus with a clear dead-end sentence ending\\ncat > /tmp/dead_end_corpus.txt << 'EOF'\\nthe cat sat on the mat.\\nEOF\\npython3 -c \\\"\\nimport sys\\nsys.path.insert(0, '/mnt/user-data/outputs')\\nfrom hecm_halting_analysis import load_corpus, HECMModel, analyze_generation_halting, print_halting_analysis\\n\\ntokens = load_corpus(paths=['/tmp/dead_end_corpus.txt'])\\nmodel = HECMModel(tokens, order=3, min_count=1, boost_strength=0.0)\\nresult = analyze_generation_halting(model, 'the cat sat')\\nprint_halting_analysis('the cat sat', result, model)\\n\\\"\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:30.496950Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:30.496986Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Lk4AL1zfUNhnNSoRUJrQAi", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"Import completed in 0.24 s -- did NOT block on input()\\n---\\nPrompt: 'the cat sat'\\nContext-state space size (num_states): 8\\n\\nDECISION: HALTS (proven) -- greedy decoding reached a dead-end context after 4 steps (no outgoing nonzero-probability transition).\\n\\nGenerated text up to the dead end:\\n  The cat sat on the mat.\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"Import completed in 0.24 s -- did NOT block on input()\\n---\\nPrompt: 'the cat sat'\\nContext-state space size (num_states): 8\\n\\nDECISION: HALTS (proven) -- greedy decoding reached a dead-end context after 4 steps (no outgoing nonzero-probability transition).\\n\\nGenerated text up to the dead end:\\n  The cat sat on the mat.\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:32.523674Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:33.019081Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Both proof branches verified working correctly, and the import-time blocking bug is fixed.", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:33.019110Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:33.349661Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01QmQS7LhKEH9Vi1avcMvKiA", "name": "present_files", "input": {"filepaths": ["/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm_halting_analysis.py"]}, "message": "Presenting file(s)...", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "table", "table": [["filepaths", "['/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm_halting_analysis.py']"]]}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:33.369554Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:33.369586Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01QmQS7LhKEH9Vi1avcMvKiA", "name": "present_files", "content": [{"type": "local_resource", "file_path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm_halting_analysis.py", "name": "hecm halting analysis", "mime_type": "text/x-python", "artifact_publishable": false}, {"type": "text", "text": "The file cards appear at the end of your reply if it ends with text, otherwise under this step; say they are below only if you finish the reply with text."}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": "Presented file", "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:35.187723Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:14:39.688790Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "**What's combined, and why it's honest:**\n\n- Greedy decoding (always take the single highest-scoring valid next token) removes all randomness, turning the model into a plain deterministic finite-state machine \u2014 states are contexts, of which there are exactly `model.num_states()` many. That's the identical shape as the toy register machine, so `decide_deterministic_walk()` reuses the *exact same proof* as `decide_halting()`: one of two outcomes is guaranteed within `num_states + 1` steps, verified above on both a corpus that provably loops (with the exact repeating phrase printed) and one that provably dead-ends.\n- This is scoped honestly: it only characterizes the deterministic *argmax reduction*, not the real stochastic `generate()`, which is stated explicitly in the docstring and won't be misrepresented if you use it.\n\n**Usage:**\n```\npython3 hecm_halting_analysis.py --data corpus.txt --prompt \"the fox and\" --analyze-halting\n```\nOmit `--analyze-halting` to get the original interactive stochastic generator, now fixed so importing the module doesn't hang on a filename prompt.", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-26T13:14:39.767415Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T13:14:39.767415Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddd7-be8d-763f-8708-b56a42f98665"}, {"uuid": "01a0dddc-6340-7591-a86e-3ad9b4b82d9f", "text": "use lingual binding and corpus differentation", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:16:45.152710Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:16:45.152710Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "use lingual binding and corpus differentation", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-26T13:16:45.152945Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T13:16:45.152945Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ddd7-be8d-75e5-badb-8cfd589f3162"}, {"uuid": "01a0dddc-6340-724f-adf4-ec5369bb6d41", "text": "\"Lingual binding\" and \"corpus differentiation\" aren't standard NLP terms, so before I build anything I want to make sure I implement what you actually mean rather than guess at pseudo-physics-style relabeling that doesn't do anything real (which is exactly the trap the existing file's own comments warn against).\n\nHere's how I'd interpret each, functionally \u2014 could you tell me which is closest?\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:16:45.147130Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:16:56.340438Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Decoding unusual jargon-like phrasing in the request."}, {"summary": "Guessing the intended meaning behind invented technical terms."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:16:56.340482Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:16:57.885844Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "\"Lingual binding\" and \"corpus differentiation\" aren't standard NLP terms, so before I build anything I want to make sure I implement what you actually mean rather than guess at pseudo-physics-style relabeling that doesn't do anything real (which is exactly the trap the existing file's own comments warn against).\n\nHere's how I'd interpret each, functionally \u2014 could you tell me which is closest?", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:16:57.885877Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:17:00.594100Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01Pc7qVPp6PGJicvd2VsBycJ", "name": "ask_user_input_v0", "input": {"questions": [{"question": "What should \"corpus differentiation\" actually compute or do?", "options": ["Blend/compare two or more separate corpora as distinct sources (e.g. weight or interpolate between their transition graphs)", "Measure how different two corpora's distributions are (e.g. a real divergence/distance metric between them)", "Something else \u2014 I'll describe it"]}, {"question": "What should \"lingual binding\" actually tie together?", "options": ["Binding/aligning tokens across multiple languages in a multilingual corpus", "Tying a word to its co-occurring context words (a real embedding-style association)", "Something else \u2014 I'll describe it"]}]}, "message": "Generating ask_user_input_v0...", "integration_name": "Dynamic Widget", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": null, "context": null, "display_content": null, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:17:00.595435Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:17:00.595468Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Pc7qVPp6PGJicvd2VsBycJ", "name": "ask_user_input_v0", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"questions\": [{\"question\":\"What should \\\"corpus differentiation\\\" actually compute or do?\",\"options\":[\"Blend/compare two or more separate corpora as distinct sources (e.g. weight or interpolate between their transition graphs)\",\"Measure how different two corpora's distributions are (e.g. a real divergence/distance metric between them)\",\"Something else \u2014 I'll describe it\"]},{\"question\":\"What should \\\"lingual binding\\\" actually tie together?\",\"options\":[\"Binding/aligning tokens across multiple languages in a multilingual corpus\",\"Tying a word to its co-occurring context words (a real embedding-style association)\",\"Something else \u2014 I'll describe it\"]}]}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": null, "message": "Generated ask_user_input_v0 widget", "integration_name": "Dynamic Widget", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": null, "display_content": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-26T13:17:00.667607Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T13:17:00.667607Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0dddc-6340-7591-a86e-3ad9b4b82d9f"}, {"uuid": "01a0dddd-3470-724f-9c95-1a4754bb4e82", "text": "Q: What should \"corpus differentiation\" actually compute or do?\nA: apply to the fuzzer\n\nQ: What should \"lingual binding\" actually tie together?\nA: Tying a word to its co-occurring context words (a real embedding-style association) with the fuzzer", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:17:39.087626Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:17:39.087626Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Q: What should \"corpus differentiation\" actually compute or do?\nA: apply to the fuzzer\n\nQ: What should \"lingual binding\" actually tie together?\nA: Tying a word to its co-occurring context words (a real embedding-style association) with the fuzzer", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-26T13:17:39.087869Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-26T13:17:39.087869Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0dddc-6340-724f-adf4-ec5369bb6d41"}, {"uuid": "01a0dddd-3470-70f2-9eb4-8dc9d9f2f112", "text": "Good \u2014 concretely: **lingual binding** = build a genuine co-occurrence table (word \u2192 the words that actually appear near it in a base corpus) and use it to *generate* synthetic fuzz corpora by randomly walking those real associations, instead of fuzzing with meaningless random tokens. **Corpus differentiation** = generate several genuinely different mutated variants of a base corpus (substitutions/deletions/duplications), so each fuzz trial tests a real, distinct corpus rather than the same one relabeled. Both feed into a fuzzer that builds an `HECMModel` on each generated corpus and runs the greedy-decoding halting proof from before, checking the same kind of correctness invariants as the earlier `fuzz_toy_machines`.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nNow let's actually run it and see if the invariants hold:\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nZero invariant failures across 100 trials, both mechanisms exercised evenly. Let's also test a larger scale to build more confidence, and spot-check that a `LOOPS` result's decoded cycle text is actually sensible (not garbage from a bug):\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nBoth confirmed real and sensible:\n- **Lingual binding**: `fox`'s co-occurrence counts (`{'the': 2, 'and': 1}`) are exactly what actually appears next to \"fox\" in the base text \u2014 not invented \u2014 and the generated walk (`fox \u2192 the \u2192 in \u2192 forest \u2192 ...`) follows real adjacency weights.\n- **Corpus differentiation**: the mutated sample shows real substitutions/duplications/deletions relative to the base (`'and', 'and'` duplicated; a `'the', 'the'` duplication) \u2014 genuinely different data, not a relabeled copy.\n- **1000-trial fuzz run**: zero invariant failures.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n**Usage:**\n```\npython3 hecm_fuzzer.py --data your_corpus.txt --fuzz 500 --seed 42 --verbose\n```\n\n- `--corpus-length` \u2014 length of lingual-binding-generated synthetic corpora\n- `--mutation-rate` \u2014 how aggressively corpus_differentiation mutates the base text\n- `--binding-window` \u2014 co-occurrence window size for lingual binding\n\nEvery trial alternates between the two mechanisms, builds a real `HECMModel` on the resulting corpus, runs the greedy-decoding halting proof from before, and checks that the result is always a genuine `HALTS` or `LOOPS` (never a crash, never exceeding the finite state-space bound) \u2014 1000/1000 trials passed with zero invariant failures.", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:17:39.081585Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:18:09.507097Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Untangling word-embedding concepts related to fuzzing."}, {"summary": "Combining corpus fuzzing with real word-context co-occurrence data."}, {"summary": "Designing a random-walk generator from co-occurrence bindings."}, {"summary": "Building mutation-based corpus variants for fuzz testing."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:18:09.507145Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:18:12.188225Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Good \u2014 concretely: **lingual binding** = build a genuine co-occurrence table (word \u2192 the words that actually appear near it in a base corpus) and use it to *generate* synthetic fuzz corpora by randomly walking those real associations, instead of fuzzing with meaningless random tokens. **Corpus differentiation** = generate several genuinely different mutated variants of a base corpus (substitutions/deletions/duplications), so each fuzz trial tests a real, distinct corpus rather than the same one relabeled. Both feed into a fuzzer that builds an `HECMModel` on each generated corpus and runs the greedy-decoding halting proof from before, checking the same kind of correctness invariants as the earlier `fuzz_toy_machines`.", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:18:12.188251Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:18:45.827925Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01SvjXMz5eegXDeeSvouSdgb", "name": "create_file", "input": {"description": "A fuzzer for the HECM halting-analysis pipeline: 'lingual binding' generates synthetic corpora from real word co-occurrence statistics, 'corpus differentiation' generates genuinely mutated corpus variants, and both feed random real corpora into the same finite-state halting proof used earlier", "file_text": "\"\"\"\nhecm_fuzzer.py\n===============\n\nFuzzes the HECM halting-analysis pipeline (hecm_halting_analysis.py) using\ntwo real, concrete data-generation mechanisms, in the same spirit as the\nearlier fuzz_toy_machines(): generate varied inputs, run the real decider\non each, and check that its own proof-based guarantees actually hold.\n\nLINGUAL BINDING\n----------------\nA real co-occurrence table: for each word in a base corpus, which words\nactually appear near it, and how often. This is a genuine, if simple,\nembedding-style association -- \"binding\" a word to the words it actually\nco-occurs with in real text, not a metaphorical label for something else.\nIt's used to generate fuzzed corpora by random-walking those real\nassociations, so the fuzzer's synthetic text is linguistically grounded\nin the base corpus's actual statistics rather than uniform random tokens.\n\nCORPUS DIFFERENTIATION\n------------------------\nGenerating several corpora that are genuinely different from the base\ncorpus and from each other -- via real mutations (word substitution,\ndeletion, duplication) at a controllable rate -- so the fuzzer exercises\nthe halting-analysis pipeline (HECMModel + analyze_generation_halting)\nacross an actually varied set of corpora, not repeated copies of one.\n\nWHAT'S BEING CHECKED\n----------------------\nFor every fuzzed corpus, an HECMModel is built and analyze_generation_halting\nis run from a random prompt drawn from that corpus's own vocabulary. The\nsame guarantee decide_deterministic_walk() relies on is checked directly:\nthe result must be exactly HALTS or LOOPS, must never raise, and must\nnever take more than num_states + 1 steps to decide -- because the\ncontext-state space is finite by construction (see hecm_halting_analysis.py\nfor why greedy decoding reduces to that same finite-state proof).\n\"\"\"\n\nimport argparse\nimport random\nimport sys\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\n\nimport numpy as np\n\nfrom hecm_halting_analysis import (\n    HECMModel,\n    analyze_generation_halting,\n    print_halting_analysis,\n)\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# LINGUAL BINDING -- real co-occurrence association, used to generate corpora\n# =============================================================================\n\ndef build_cooccurrence_bindings(tokens, window=2):\n    \"\"\"\n    For each word, count how often other words occur within `window`\n    tokens of it in the given token stream. This is the \"binding\": a\n    real, computed association between a word and its actual linguistic\n    neighbors, not an arbitrary or invented relationship.\n    \"\"\"\n    bindings = defaultdict(Counter)\n    n = len(tokens)\n    for i, w in enumerate(tokens):\n        lo = max(0, i - window)\n        hi = min(n, i + window + 1)\n        for j in range(lo, hi):\n            if j != i:\n                bindings[w][tokens[j]] += 1\n    return bindings\n\n\ndef generate_bound_corpus(bindings, vocab, length, rng, seed_word=None):\n    \"\"\"\n    Generate synthetic text by random-walking the co-occurrence bindings:\n    at each step, the next word is drawn weighted by how often it actually\n    co-occurred with the current word in the base corpus. When a word has\n    no recorded neighbors, fall back to a uniform random vocabulary word\n    so the walk can always continue.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not vocab:\n        return []\n\n    current = seed_word if seed_word in bindings else rng.choice(vocab)\n    out = [current]\n\n    for _ in range(length - 1):\n        neighbors = bindings.get(current)\n        if not neighbors:\n            current = rng.choice(vocab)\n        else:\n            candidates = list(neighbors.keys())\n            weights = [neighbors[c] for c in candidates]\n            current = rng.choices(candidates, weights=weights, k=1)[0]\n        out.append(current)\n\n    return out\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# CORPUS DIFFERENTIATION -- generate genuinely mutated corpus variants\n# =============================================================================\n\ndef differentiate_corpus(base_tokens, rng, mutation_rate=0.15):\n    \"\"\"\n    Produce one corpus variant that actually differs from base_tokens:\n    each token independently has a mutation_rate chance of being\n    substituted (replaced with another vocabulary word), deleted, or\n    duplicated. Real, checkable differences -- not a copy relabeled as\n    \"different.\"\n    \"\"\"\n    vocab = list(set(base_tokens)) or [\"<unk>\"]\n    out = []\n\n    for tok in base_tokens:\n        r = rng.random()\n        if r < mutation_rate / 3:\n            out.append(rng.choice(vocab))          # substitute\n        elif r < 2 * mutation_rate / 3:\n            continue                                 # delete\n        elif r < mutation_rate:\n            out.append(tok)\n            out.append(tok)                          # duplicate\n        else:\n            out.append(tok)                          # unchanged\n\n    return out\n\n\ndef corpus_differentiation(base_tokens, num_variants, rng, mutation_rate=0.15):\n    \"\"\"Generate num_variants genuinely different corpora derived from base_tokens.\"\"\"\n    return [\n        differentiate_corpus(base_tokens, rng, mutation_rate=mutation_rate)\n        for _ in range(num_variants)\n    ]\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# FUZZER\n# =============================================================================\n\ndef check_invariants(model, result):\n    \"\"\"\n    Assert the guarantees analyze_generation_halting()'s proof relies on\n    actually hold for this run.\n    \"\"\"\n    max_states = model.num_states()\n\n    assert result[\"result\"] in (\"HALTS\", \"LOOPS\"), (\n        f\"decide_deterministic_walk returned an invalid result: {result['result']!r}\"\n    )\n    assert result[\"steps\"] <= max_states + 1, (\n        f\"steps ({result['steps']}) exceeded the finite state-space bound \"\n        f\"({max_states}) -- this should be impossible\"\n    )\n\n    if result[\"result\"] == \"LOOPS\":\n        assert result[\"cycle_length\"] >= 1\n        assert result[\"cycle_start\"] >= 0\n        assert \"cycle_text\" in result and len(result[\"cycle_text\"]) > 0\n\n\ndef fuzz_hecm_halting(base_tokens, num_trials=100, seed=None,\n                       corpus_length=200, mutation_rate=0.15,\n                       binding_window=2, order=3, verbose=False):\n    \"\"\"\n    Run num_trials fuzz trials. Each trial alternates between the two\n    corpus-generation mechanisms:\n\n      - even trials: a corpus_differentiation() mutation of base_tokens\n      - odd trials:  a generate_bound_corpus() synthetic walk over the\n                     lingual bindings built from base_tokens\n\n    For each generated corpus, build an HECMModel, pick a random prompt\n    from that corpus's own vocabulary, run analyze_generation_halting,\n    and check the invariants above.\n    \"\"\"\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    bindings = build_cooccurrence_bindings(base_tokens, window=binding_window)\n    base_vocab = sorted(set(base_tokens))\n\n    stats = {\n        \"trials\": 0,\n        \"halts\": 0,\n        \"loops\": 0,\n        \"differentiation_trials\": 0,\n        \"binding_trials\": 0,\n        \"failures\": [],\n    }\n\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\n    print(\"FUZZING HECM HALTING ANALYSIS: LINGUAL BINDING + CORPUS DIFFERENTIATION\")\n    print(\"=\" * 78)\n    print(f\"Base corpus size: {len(base_tokens)} tokens, {len(base_vocab)} unique words\")\n    print(f\"Trials requested: {num_trials}\" + (f\"  (seed={seed})\" if seed is not None else \"  (random seed)\"))\n    print()\n\n    for trial in range(num_trials):\n        use_differentiation = (trial % 2 == 0)\n\n        try:\n            if use_differentiation:\n                stats[\"differentiation_trials\"] += 1\n                variant_tokens = differentiate_corpus(base_tokens, rng, mutation_rate=mutation_rate)\n                mechanism = \"corpus_differentiation\"\n            else:\n                stats[\"binding_trials\"] += 1\n                seed_word = rng.choice(base_vocab) if base_vocab else None\n                variant_tokens = generate_bound_corpus(\n                    bindings, base_vocab, corpus_length, rng, seed_word=seed_word\n                )\n                mechanism = \"lingual_binding\"\n\n            if len(variant_tokens) < 2:\n                variant_tokens = list(base_tokens)  # degenerate mutation fallback\n\n            model = HECMModel(variant_tokens, order=order, min_count=1, boost_strength=0.0)\n\n            variant_vocab = [w for w in model.vocab if w not in (\"<unk>\", \"<bos>\")]\n            if not variant_vocab:\n                if verbose:\n                    print(f\"  [trial {trial}] ({mechanism}) empty vocabulary after mutation -- skipped\")\n                continue\n\n            prompt = rng.choice(variant_vocab)\n            result = analyze_generation_halting(model, prompt, boost_strength=0.0)\n            check_invariants(model, result)\n\n        except Exception as e:\n            stats[\"failures\"].append({\"trial\": trial, \"mechanism\": mechanism, \"error\": repr(e)})\n            print(f\"  [trial {trial}] ({mechanism}) FAILURE: {e!r}\")\n            continue\n\n        stats[\"trials\"] += 1\n        if result[\"result\"] == \"HALTS\":\n            stats[\"halts\"] += 1\n        else:\n            stats[\"loops\"] += 1\n\n        if verbose:\n            tag = result[\"result\"]\n            extra = f\" cycle_length={result['cycle_length']}\" if tag == \"LOOPS\" else f\" steps={result['steps']}\"\n            print(\n                f\"  [trial {trial}] ({mechanism}) num_states={model.num_states()} \"\n                f\"prompt={prompt!r} -> {tag}{extra}\"\n            )\n\n    print()\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\n    print(\"FUZZING SUMMARY\")\n    print(\"-\" * 78)\n    print(f\"  Trials completed              : {stats['trials']}\")\n    print(f\"  via corpus_differentiation     : {stats['differentiation_trials']}\")\n    print(f\"  via lingual_binding            : {stats['binding_trials']}\")\n    print(f\"  HALTS                          : {stats['halts']}\")\n    print(f\"  LOOPS (proven)                 : {stats['loops']}\")\n    print(f\"  Invariant failures             : {len(stats['failures'])}\")\n\n    if stats[\"failures\"]:\n        print()\n        print(\"FAILURES DETECTED:\")\n        for f in stats[\"failures\"]:\n            print(f\"  trial {f['trial']} ({f['mechanism']}): {f['error']}\")\n    else:\n        print()\n        print(\n            \"No invariant failures: every fuzzed corpus, whether generated by\\n\"\n            \"corpus_differentiation (real mutations of the base text) or\\n\"\n            \"lingual_binding (a real co-occurrence random walk), produced a\\n\"\n            \"genuine HALTS-or-LOOPS proof within the finite state-space bound.\"\n        )\n\n    return stats\n\n\ndef main():\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=\"Fuzz the HECM halting-analysis pipeline using lingual binding and corpus differentiation.\"\n    )\n    ap.add_argument(\"--data\", required=True, help=\"Path to a base corpus text file.\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--fuzz\", type=int, default=100, help=\"Number of fuzz trials.\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--seed\", type=int, default=None)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--corpus-length\", type=int, default=200, help=\"Length of lingual-binding-generated corpora.\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--mutation-rate\", type=float, default=0.15, help=\"Per-token mutation rate for corpus differentiation.\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--binding-window\", type=int, default=2, help=\"Co-occurrence window size for lingual binding.\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--order\", type=int, default=3)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--verbose\", action=\"store_true\")\n    args = ap.parse_args()\n\n    with open(args.data, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\") as f:\n        text = f.read().lower()\n\n    import re\n    base_tokens = re.findall(r\"[a-z0-9']+|[.,!?;:]\", text)\n\n    fuzz_hecm_halting(\n        base_tokens,\n        num_trials=args.fuzz,\n        seed=args.seed,\n        corpus_length=args.corpus_length,\n        mutation_rate=args.mutation_rate,\n        binding_window=args.binding_window,\n        order=args.order,\n        verbose=args.verbose,\n    )\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n", "path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm_fuzzer.py"}, "message": "A fuzzer for the HECM halting-analysis pipeline: 'lingual binding' generates synthetic corpora from real word co-occurrence statistics, 'corpus differentiation' generates genuinely mutated corpus var\u2026", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"python\", \"code\": \"\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nhecm_fuzzer.py\\n===============\\n\\nFuzzes the HECM halting-analysis pipeline (hecm_halting_analysis.py) using\\ntwo real, concrete data-generation mechanisms, in the same spirit as the\\nearlier fuzz_toy_machines(): generate varied inputs, run the real decider\\non each, and check that its own proof-based guarantees actually hold.\\n\\nLINGUAL BINDING\\n----------------\\nA real co-occurrence table: for each word in a base corpus, which words\\nactually appear near it, and how often. This is a genuine, if simple,\\nembedding-style association -- \\\"binding\\\" a word to the words it actually\\nco-occurs with in real text, not a metaphorical label for something else.\\nIt's used to generate fuzzed corpora by random-walking those real\\nassociations, so the fuzzer's synthetic text is linguistically grounded\\nin the base corpus's actual statistics rather than uniform random tokens.\\n\\nCORPUS DIFFERENTIATION\\n------------------------\\nGenerating several corpora that are genuinely different from the base\\ncorpus and from each other -- via real mutations (word substitution,\\ndeletion, duplication) at a controllable rate -- so the fuzzer exercises\\nthe halting-analysis pipeline (HECMModel + analyze_generation_halting)\\nacross an actually varied set of corpora, not repeated copies of one.\\n\\nWHAT'S BEING CHECKED\\n----------------------\\nFor every fuzzed corpus, an HECMModel is built and analyze_generation_halting\\nis run from a random prompt drawn from that corpus's own vocabulary. The\\nsame guarantee decide_deterministic_walk() relies on is checked directly:\\nthe result must be exactly HALTS or LOOPS, must never raise, and must\\nnever take more than num_states + 1 steps to decide -- because the\\ncontext-state space is finite by construction (see hecm_halting_analysis.py\\nfor why greedy decoding reduces to that same finite-state proof).\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\nimport argparse\\nimport random\\nimport sys\\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\\n\\nimport numpy as np\\n\\nfrom hecm_halting_analysis import (\\n    HECMModel,\\n    analyze_generation_halting,\\n    print_halting_analysis,\\n)\\n\\n\\n# =============================================================================\\n# LINGUAL BINDING -- real co-occurrence association, used to generate corpora\\n# =============================================================================\\n\\ndef build_cooccurrence_bindings(tokens, window=2):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    For each word, count how often other words occur within `window`\\n    tokens of it in the given token stream. This is the \\\"binding\\\": a\\n    real, computed association between a word and its actual linguistic\\n    neighbors, not an arbitrary or invented relationship.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    bindings = defaultdict(Counter)\\n    n = len(tokens)\\n    for i, w in enumerate(tokens):\\n        lo = max(0, i - window)\\n        hi = min(n, i + window + 1)\\n        for j in range(lo, hi):\\n            if j != i:\\n                bindings[w][tokens[j]] += 1\\n    return bindings\\n\\n\\ndef generate_bound_corpus(bindings, vocab, length, rng, seed_word=None):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Generate synthetic text by random-walking the co-occurrence bindings:\\n    at each step, the next word is drawn weighted by how often it actually\\n    co-occurred with the current word in the base corpus. When a word has\\n    no recorded neighbors, fall back to a uniform random vocabulary word\\n    so the walk can always continue.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    if not vocab:\\n        return []\\n\\n    current = seed_word if seed_word in bindings else rng.choice(vocab)\\n    out = [current]\\n\\n    for _ in range(length - 1):\\n        neighbors = bindings.get(current)\\n        if not neighbors:\\n            current = rng.choice(vocab)\\n        else:\\n            candidates = list(neighbors.keys())\\n            weights = [neighbors[c] for c in candidates]\\n            current = rng.choices(candidates, weights=weights, k=1)[0]\\n        out.append(current)\\n\\n    return out\\n\\n\\n# =============================================================================\\n# CORPUS DIFFERENTIATION -- generate genuinely mutated corpus variants\\n# =============================================================================\\n\\ndef differentiate_corpus(base_tokens, rng, mutation_rate=0.15):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Produce one corpus variant that actually differs from base_tokens:\\n    each token independently has a mutation_rate chance of being\\n    substituted (replaced with another vocabulary word), deleted, or\\n    duplicated. Real, checkable differences -- not a copy relabeled as\\n    \\\"different.\\\"\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    vocab = list(set(base_tokens)) or [\\\"<unk>\\\"]\\n    out = []\\n\\n    for tok in base_tokens:\\n        r = rng.random()\\n        if r < mutation_rate / 3:\\n            out.append(rng.choice(vocab))          # substitute\\n        elif r < 2 * mutation_rate / 3:\\n            continue                                 # delete\\n        elif r < mutation_rate:\\n            out.append(tok)\\n            out.append(tok)                          # duplicate\\n        else:\\n            out.append(tok)                          # unchanged\\n\\n    return out\\n\\n\\ndef corpus_differentiation(base_tokens, num_variants, rng, mutation_rate=0.15):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Generate num_variants genuinely different corpora derived from base_tokens.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return [\\n        differentiate_corpus(base_tokens, rng, mutation_rate=mutation_rate)\\n        for _ in range(num_variants)\\n    ]\\n\\n\\n# =============================================================================\\n# FUZZER\\n# =============================================================================\\n\\ndef check_invariants(model, result):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Assert the guarantees analyze_generation_halting()'s proof relies on\\n    actually hold for this run.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    max_states = model.num_states()\\n\\n    assert result[\\\"result\\\"] in (\\\"HALTS\\\", \\\"LOOPS\\\"), (\\n        f\\\"decide_deterministic_walk returned an invalid result: {result['result']!r}\\\"\\n    )\\n    assert result[\\\"steps\\\"] <= max_states + 1, (\\n        f\\\"steps ({result['steps']}) exceeded the finite state-space bound \\\"\\n        f\\\"({max_states}) -- this should be impossible\\\"\\n    )\\n\\n    if result[\\\"result\\\"] == \\\"LOOPS\\\":\\n        assert result[\\\"cycle_length\\\"] >= 1\\n        assert result[\\\"cycle_start\\\"] >= 0\\n        assert \\\"cycle_text\\\" in result and len(result[\\\"cycle_text\\\"]) > 0\\n\\n\\ndef fuzz_hecm_halting(base_tokens, num_trials=100, seed=None,\\n                       corpus_length=200, mutation_rate=0.15,\\n                       binding_window=2, order=3, verbose=False):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Run num_trials fuzz trials. Each trial alternates between the two\\n    corpus-generation mechanisms:\\n\\n      - even trials: a corpus_differentiation() mutation of base_tokens\\n      - odd trials:  a generate_bound_corpus() synthetic walk over the\\n                     lingual bindings built from base_tokens\\n\\n    For each generated corpus, build an HECMModel, pick a random prompt\\n    from that corpus's own vocabulary, run analyze_generation_halting,\\n    and check the invariants above.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\\n    bindings = build_cooccurrence_bindings(base_tokens, window=binding_window)\\n    base_vocab = sorted(set(base_tokens))\\n\\n    stats = {\\n        \\\"trials\\\": 0,\\n        \\\"halts\\\": 0,\\n        \\\"loops\\\": 0,\\n        \\\"differentiation_trials\\\": 0,\\n        \\\"binding_trials\\\": 0,\\n        \\\"failures\\\": [],\\n    }\\n\\n    print(\\\"=\\\" * 78)\\n    print(\\\"FUZZING HECM HALTING ANALYSIS: LINGUAL BINDING + CORPUS DIFFERENTIATION\\\")\\n    print(\\\"=\\\" * 78)\\n    print(f\\\"Base corpus size: {len(base_tokens)} tokens, {len(base_vocab)} unique words\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"Trials requested: {num_trials}\\\" + (f\\\"  (seed={seed})\\\" if seed is not None else \\\"  (random seed)\\\"))\\n    print()\\n\\n    for trial in range(num_trials):\\n        use_differentiation = (trial % 2 == 0)\\n\\n        try:\\n            if use_differentiation:\\n                stats[\\\"differentiation_trials\\\"] += 1\\n                variant_tokens = differentiate_corpus(base_tokens, rng, mutation_rate=mutation_rate)\\n                mechanism = \\\"corpus_differentiation\\\"\\n            else:\\n                stats[\\\"binding_trials\\\"] += 1\\n                seed_word = rng.choice(base_vocab) if base_vocab else None\\n                variant_tokens = generate_bound_corpus(\\n                    bindings, base_vocab, corpus_length, rng, seed_word=seed_word\\n                )\\n                mechanism = \\\"lingual_binding\\\"\\n\\n            if len(variant_tokens) < 2:\\n                variant_tokens = list(base_tokens)  # degenerate mutation fallback\\n\\n            model = HECMModel(variant_tokens, order=order, min_count=1, boost_strength=0.0)\\n\\n            variant_vocab = [w for w in model.vocab if w not in (\\\"<unk>\\\", \\\"<bos>\\\")]\\n            if not variant_vocab:\\n                if verbose:\\n                    print(f\\\"  [trial {trial}] ({mechanism}) empty vocabulary after mutation -- skipped\\\")\\n                continue\\n\\n            prompt = rng.choice(variant_vocab)\\n            result = analyze_generation_halting(model, prompt, boost_strength=0.0)\\n            check_invariants(model, result)\\n\\n        except Exception as e:\\n            stats[\\\"failures\\\"].append({\\\"trial\\\": trial, \\\"mechanism\\\": mechanism, \\\"error\\\": repr(e)})\\n            print(f\\\"  [trial {trial}] ({mechanism}) FAILURE: {e!r}\\\")\\n            continue\\n\\n        stats[\\\"trials\\\"] += 1\\n        if result[\\\"result\\\"] == \\\"HALTS\\\":\\n            stats[\\\"halts\\\"] += 1\\n        else:\\n            stats[\\\"loops\\\"] += 1\\n\\n        if verbose:\\n            tag = result[\\\"result\\\"]\\n            extra = f\\\" cycle_length={result['cycle_length']}\\\" if tag == \\\"LOOPS\\\" else f\\\" steps={result['steps']}\\\"\\n            print(\\n                f\\\"  [trial {trial}] ({mechanism}) num_states={model.num_states()} \\\"\\n                f\\\"prompt={prompt!r} -> {tag}{extra}\\\"\\n            )\\n\\n    print()\\n    print(\\\"-\\\" * 78)\\n    print(\\\"FUZZING SUMMARY\\\")\\n    print(\\\"-\\\" * 78)\\n    print(f\\\"  Trials completed              : {stats['trials']}\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"  via corpus_differentiation     : {stats['differentiation_trials']}\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"  via lingual_binding            : {stats['binding_trials']}\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"  HALTS                          : {stats['halts']}\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"  LOOPS (proven)                 : {stats['loops']}\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"  Invariant failures             : {len(stats['failures'])}\\\")\\n\\n    if stats[\\\"failures\\\"]:\\n        print()\\n        print(\\\"FAILURES DETECTED:\\\")\\n        for f in stats[\\\"failures\\\"]:\\n            print(f\\\"  trial {f['trial']} ({f['mechanism']}): {f['error']}\\\")\\n    else:\\n        print()\\n        print(\\n            \\\"No invariant failures: every fuzzed corpus, whether generated by\\\\n\\\"\\n            \\\"corpus_differentiation (real mutations of the base text) or\\\\n\\\"\\n            \\\"lingual_binding (a real co-occurrence random walk), produced a\\\\n\\\"\\n            \\\"genuine HALTS-or-LOOPS proof within the finite state-space bound.\\\"\\n        )\\n\\n    return stats\\n\\n\\ndef main():\\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(\\n        description=\\\"Fuzz the HECM halting-analysis pipeline using lingual binding and corpus differentiation.\\\"\\n    )\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--data\\\", required=True, help=\\\"Path to a base corpus text file.\\\")\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--fuzz\\\", type=int, default=100, help=\\\"Number of fuzz trials.\\\")\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--seed\\\", type=int, default=None)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--corpus-length\\\", type=int, default=200, help=\\\"Length of lingual-binding-generated corpora.\\\")\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--mutation-rate\\\", type=float, default=0.15, help=\\\"Per-token mutation rate for corpus differentiation.\\\")\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--binding-window\\\", type=int, default=2, help=\\\"Co-occurrence window size for lingual binding.\\\")\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--order\\\", type=int, default=3)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--verbose\\\", action=\\\"store_true\\\")\\n    args = ap.parse_args()\\n\\n    with open(args.data, \\\"r\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\", errors=\\\"ignore\\\") as f:\\n        text = f.read().lower()\\n\\n    import re\\n    base_tokens = re.findall(r\\\"[a-z0-9']+|[.,!?;:]\\\", text)\\n\\n    fuzz_hecm_halting(\\n        base_tokens,\\n        num_trials=args.fuzz,\\n        seed=args.seed,\\n        corpus_length=args.corpus_length,\\n        mutation_rate=args.mutation_rate,\\n        binding_window=args.binding_window,\\n        order=args.order,\\n        verbose=args.verbose,\\n    )\\n\\n\\nif __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\":\\n    main()\\n\", \"filename\": \"/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm_fuzzer.py\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:18:46.857965Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:18:46.858008Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01SvjXMz5eegXDeeSvouSdgb", "name": "create_file", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "File created successfully: /mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm_fuzzer.py"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "File created successfully: /mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm_fuzzer.py"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:18:48.816047Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:18:49.292233Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Now let's actually run it and see if the invariants hold:", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:18:49.292271Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:18:50.457058Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01XEwUWJP5VhV2CLxzXeUZqA", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "cd /mnt/user-data/outputs && python3 hecm_fuzzer.py --data /tmp/small_corpus.txt --fuzz 100 --seed 42 --verbose 2>&1 | tail -60", "description": "Run the fuzzer with verbose output to check invariants across both lingual-binding and corpus-differentiation trials"}, "message": "Run the fuzzer with verbose output to check invariants across both lingual-binding and corpus-differentiation trials", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cd /mnt/user-data/outputs && python3 hecm_fuzzer.py --data /tmp/small_corpus.txt --fuzz 100 --seed 42 --verbose 2>&1 | tail -60\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:18:51.600561Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-26T13:18:51.600619Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01XEwUWJP5VhV2CLxzXeUZqA", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"  [trial 55] (lingual_binding) num_states=85 prompt='tree' -> LOOPS cycle_length=2\\n  [trial 56] (corpus_differentiation) num_states=39 prompt='the' -> LOOPS cycle_length=11\\n  [trial 57] (lingual_binding) num_states=74 prompt='and' -> LOOPS cycle_length=2\\n  [trial 58] (corpus_differentiation) num_states=36 prompt='played' -> LOOPS cycle_length=11\\n  [trial 59] (lingual_binding) num_states=85 prompt='and' -> LOOPS cycle_length=2\\n  [trial 60] (corpus_differentiation) num_states=42 prompt='the' -> LOOPS cycle_length=8\\n  [trial 61] (lingual_binding) num_states=87 prompt='every' -> LOOPS cycle_length=6\\n  [trial 62] (corpus_differentiation) num_states=44 prompt='old' -> HALTS steps=3\\n  [trial 63] (lingual_binding) num_states=91 prompt='hid' -> LOOPS cycle_length=2\\n  [trial 64] (corpus_differentiation) num_states=41 prompt='together' -> LOOPS cycle_length=13\\n  [trial 65] (lingual_binding) num_states=96 prompt='fox' -> LOOPS cycle_length=2\\n  [trial 66] (corpus_differentiation) num_states=41 prompt='tree' -> HALTS steps=1\\n  [trial 67] (lingual_binding) num_states=87 prompt='chased' -> LOOPS cycle_length=2\\n  [trial 68] (corpus_differentiation) num_states=44 prompt='friends' -> LOOPS cycle_length=9\\n  [trial 69] (lingual_binding) num_states=87 prompt='chased' -> LOOPS cycle_length=2\\n  [trial 70] (corpus_differentiation) num_states=42 prompt='near' -> HALTS steps=4\\n  [trial 71] (lingual_binding) num_states=91 prompt='around' -> LOOPS cycle_length=15\\n  [trial 72] (corpus_differentiation) num_states=45 prompt='old' -> HALTS steps=3\\n  [trial 73] (lingual_binding) num_states=88 prompt='ran' -> LOOPS cycle_length=2\\n  [trial 74] (corpus_differentiation) num_states=39 prompt='forest' -> LOOPS cycle_length=11\\n  [trial 75] (lingual_binding) num_states=83 prompt='chased' -> LOOPS cycle_length=4\\n  [trial 76] (corpus_differentiation) num_states=41 prompt='ran' -> LOOPS cycle_length=12\\n  [trial 77] (lingual_binding) num_states=79 prompt=