Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01F6esrQEsv2B4LFZb3dttPE", "name": "web_fetch", "input": {"url": "https://github.com/synthreason-source/OS/blob/main/kernel_parts/11_kernel_main.h"}, "message": "Fetching: https://github.com/synthreason-source/OS/blob/main/kernel_parts/11_kernel_main.h", "integration_name": "Web Fetch", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "globe", "context": null, "display_content": null, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:48:48.438966Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:48:48.439004Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01F6esrQEsv2B4LFZb3dttPE", "name": "web_fetch", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"error_type\": \"PERMISSIONS_ERROR\", \"error_message\": \"This URL was not in any prior search or fetch result. web_search for it first, then fetch the result link.\"}"}], "is_error": true, "structured_content": null, "meta": null, "message": "Failed to fetch: https://github.com/synthreason-source/OS/blob/main/kernel_parts/11_kernel_main.h", "integration_name": "Web Fetch", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": "https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?sz=64&domain=github.com", "icon_name": "globe", "display_content": null, "hidden_in_chat": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:48:54.022927Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:48:54.040789Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "I still can't open `11_kernel_main.h`. I just tried its GitHub URL directly and the fetch tool refused it, and the file hasn't come up in any search either. So I can't do all of it yet. I don't have the loop code, and a rewrite based on guesses would probably not compile against your window manager, framebuffer and PS/2 code.\n\nAny of these gets me the file:\n\n1. **Paste the file here.** This is the quickest. If it's too long for one message, send the `kernel_main()` function and the loop body.\n2. **Upload it as an attachment.** I can read it from disk and edit it directly.\n3. **Give me a link I can fetch,** such as the raw file URL (`raw.githubusercontent.com/synthreason-source/OS/main/kernel_parts/11_kernel_main.h`), pasted in your message. I can fetch any URL you put in a message.\n\nOnce I have it, I'll change the main loop and return the full replacement file. The changes will cover dirty-rect redraws, a back buffer with a single copy per frame, timer-paced frames with `hlt` instead of spinning, capped work per iteration for input, terminal and disk, and splitting long operations across frames. If a fix depends on code in another part (for example the back buffer in `02_boot_info_and_graphics_driver.h`), I'll say which file and what needs to change there.", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-28T08:48:54.066567Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-28T08:48:54.066567Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0e733-c6eb-7875-bab2-7122b36f8780"}, {"uuid": "01a0e734-3524-71ad-afd4-eeb4c03453ad", "text": "", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:49:15.493180Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:49:15.493180Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-28T08:49:15.493351Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-28T08:49:15.493351Z", "attachments": [{"file_name": "", "file_size": 65188, "file_type": "txt", "extracted_content": "#pragma once\r\n// 11_kernel_main.h\r\n// Hardware bring-up, the framebuffer probe, and kernel_main() --\r\n// the kernel's actual entry point.\r\n// Extracted from kernel.cpp (original lines 9360-10540) as part of\r\n// splitting the monolithic kernel into per-component files. Order matters:\r\n// this file relies on declarations from the kernel_parts files included\r\n// before it in kernel.cpp, and is itself included there in sequence --\r\n// it is NOT a standalone/independently-compilable translation unit.\r\n\r\n\r\n// =============================================================================\r\n// KERNEL MAIN - ATOMIC FRAME RENDERING\r\n// =============================================================================\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nstruct BochsCPURegs {\r\n    uint32_t eax, ebx, ecx, edx, esi, edi, esp, ebp;\r\n};\r\n\r\n// Forward decls for I/O callback adapters defined just below.\r\nstatic int  elf_io_read (int slot);\r\nstatic void elf_io_write(int slot, char c);\r\nstatic void elf_io_exit (int slot, int code);\r\n\r\n// Replace init_elf_system() call in kernel_main with:\r\nvoid init_elf_system() {\r\n    for (auto& p : elf_processes) {\r\n        p.active          = false;\r\n        p.cpu_initialized = false;\r\n        p.in_head  = p.in_tail  = 0;\r\n        p.out_head = p.out_tail = 0;\r\n    }\r\n    // Register IO callbacks for every slot. These are what make\r\n    // port-0xE9 writes from the guest reach the terminal: the chain is\r\n    //   guest: out 0xE9, al\r\n    //   -> bx_devices_c::outp (bochs_infra.cpp)\r\n    //   -> bochs_guest_putc   (bochs_glue.cpp)\r\n    //   -> write_cb = elf_io_write\r\n    //   -> push_output(slot, c)\r\n    //   -> tick_elf_processes drains it to terminal->console_print\r\n    for (int s = 0; s < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES; ++s) {\r\n        bochs_register_io_callbacks(s, elf_io_read, elf_io_write, elf_io_exit);\r\n    }\r\n    // NOTE: bochs_cpu_init() is NOT called here.\r\n    //\r\n    // We tried calling bochs_cpu_prewarm() (a guarded BX_CPU::initialize)\r\n    // here to surface init-time bugs early, but it triggered the VMware\r\n    // BAR1-unmap path documented at the top of vmware_svga_init(), causing\r\n    // VMware to die with \"execute an invalid part of memory\". The early\r\n    // call was unnecessary anyway: the host IDT installed in boot.S means\r\n    // a host fault inside BX_CPU::initialize() during lazy init now\r\n    // produces a visible \"!XX\" breadcrumb at VGA row 1 instead of a\r\n    // silent triple fault.\r\n    //\r\n    // Init runs lazily on the first ELF launch via x86_tick. The\r\n    // bochs_cpu_init() guard added in bochs_glue.cpp ensures\r\n    // BX_CPU::initialize() runs at most once per boot regardless of how\r\n    // many times the kernel calls bochs_cpu_init().\r\n}\r\n// I/O callback adapters\r\nstatic int  elf_io_read (int slot) { return pop_input(slot); }\r\nstatic void elf_io_write(int slot, char c) { push_output(slot, c); }\r\nstatic void elf_io_exit (int slot, int code) {\r\n    if (slot >= 0 && slot < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES) {\r\n        // DIAGNOSTIC: report the exit code (now the real fault vector\r\n        // number, per the per-vector stub change in inject_slab_tables).\r\n        // The buffer is zero-initialized this time, unlike the old\r\n        // disabled version this comment used to warn about.\r\n        char buf[64] = {0};\r\n        snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), \"\\n[guest exit, code=%d]\\n\", code);\r\n        console_print(buf);\r\n\r\n        elf_processes[slot].completed = true;\r\n        elf_processes[slot].active    = false;\r\n    }\r\n}static void dbg(const char* s) { /* write to VGA or terminal */ }\r\n// Minimal ELF execution path for Bochs-backed processes.\r\n// Assumes your existing kernel includes/types/helpers are already present.\r\n\r\n\r\nstatic inline unsigned int align_down(unsigned int v, unsigned int a) {\r\n    return v & ~(a - 1);\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic inline unsigned int align_up(unsigned int v, unsigned int a) {\r\n    return (v + a - 1) & ~(a - 1);\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic bool load_elf_image_to_slab(\r\n    int slot,\r\n    const unsigned char* elf,\r\n    unsigned int elf_size,\r\n    unsigned int& entry_out)\r\n{\r\n    if (elf_size < 52) return false;\r\n    if (!(elf[0] == 0x7f && elf[1] == 'E' && elf[2] == 'L' && elf[3] == 'F')) return false;\r\n    if (elf[4] != 1 || elf[5] != 1) return false;\r\n\r\n    auto rd16 = [&](unsigned int off) -> unsigned short {\r\n        return (unsigned short)(elf[off] | (elf[off + 1] << 8));\r\n    };\r\n    auto rd32 = [&](unsigned int off) -> unsigned int {\r\n        return (unsigned int)(elf[off] |\r\n                              (elf[off + 1] << 8) |\r\n                              (elf[off + 2] << 16) |\r\n                              (elf[off + 3] << 24));\r\n    };\r\n\r\n    unsigned short phoff = rd32(28);\r\n    unsigned short phentsize = rd16(42);\r\n    unsigned short phnum = rd16(44);\r\n    unsigned int entry = rd32(24);\r\n\r\n    if (phoff == 0 || phentsize < 32 || phnum == 0) return false;\r\n\r\n    unsigned int min_vaddr = 0xFFFFFFFFu;\r\n    unsigned int max_vaddr = 0;\r\n\r\n    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < phnum; ++i) {\r\n        unsigned int p = phoff + i * phentsize;\r\n        if (p + 32 > elf_size) return false;\r\n\r\n        unsigned int p_type = rd32(p + 0);\r\n        if (p_type != 1) continue; // PT_LOAD\r\n\r\n        unsigned int p_vaddr = rd32(p + 8);\r\n        unsigned int p_memsz = rd32(p + 20);\r\n\r\n        if (p_memsz == 0) continue;\r\n\r\n        if (p_vaddr < min_vaddr) min_vaddr = p_vaddr;\r\n        if (p_vaddr + p_memsz > max_vaddr) max_vaddr = p_vaddr + p_memsz;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    if (min_vaddr == 0xFFFFFFFFu || max_vaddr <= min_vaddr) return false;\r\n\r\n    unsigned int vaddr_base = align_down(min_vaddr, 0x1000);\r\n    unsigned int vaddr_top = align_up(max_vaddr + ELF_STACK_SIZE + ELF_HEAP_SIZE, 0x1000);\r\n    unsigned int slab_size = vaddr_top - vaddr_base;\r\n\r\n    unsigned char* slab = elf_alloc_bytes(slab_size);\r\n    if (!slab) return false;\r\n    memset(slab, 0, slab_size);\r\n\r\n    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < phnum; ++i) {\r\n        unsigned int p = phoff + i * phentsize;\r\n        if (p + 32 > elf_size) continue;\r\n\r\n        unsigned int p_type = rd32(p + 0);\r\n        if (p_type != 1) continue;\r\n\r\n        unsigned int p_offset = rd32(p + 4);\r\n        unsigned int p_vaddr  = rd32(p + 8);\r\n        unsigned int p_filesz  = rd32(p + 16);\r\n        unsigned int p_memsz   = rd32(p + 20);\r\n\r\n        if (p_offset + p_filesz > elf_size) {\r\n            elf_free_bytes(slab);\r\n            return false;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        unsigned int dest = p_vaddr - vaddr_base;\r\n        if (dest + p_memsz > slab_size) {\r\n            elf_free_bytes(slab);\r\n            return false;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        memcpy(slab + dest, elf + p_offset, p_filesz);\r\n        if (p_memsz > p_filesz) {\r\n            memset(slab + dest + p_filesz, 0, p_memsz - p_filesz);\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    entry_out = entry;\r\n    bochs_activate_slot(slot);\r\n    bochs_set_process_memory(slab, slab_size, vaddr_base);\r\n    bochs_finalize_process_memory();\r\n\r\n    // Populate ElfProcess so that start_elf_process (and kill_elf_process)\r\n    // can track and free this slab correctly.\r\n    ElfProcess& proc = elf_processes[slot];\r\n    proc.memory_base = slab;\r\n    proc.memory_size = slab_size;\r\n    proc.vaddr_base  = vaddr_base;\r\n    proc.vaddr_end   = vaddr_top;\r\n\r\n    return true;\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic bool start_elf_process(int slot, const unsigned char* elf, unsigned int elf_size) {\r\n    ElfProcess& proc = elf_processes[slot];\r\n    unsigned int entry = 0;\r\n\r\n    // load_elf_image_to_slab calls bochs_activate_slot + bochs_set_process_memory\r\n    // and populates proc.memory_base / proc.memory_size / proc.vaddr_base.\r\n    if (!load_elf_image_to_slab(slot, elf, elf_size, entry)) return false;\r\n\r\n    proc.active = true;\r\n    proc.completed = false;\r\n    proc.entry_point = entry;\r\n\r\n    // vaddr_base was set by load_elf_image_to_slab; don't clobber it with a\r\n    // page-aligned guess derived from the entry point (those can differ for\r\n    // PIE or non-zero-based binaries).\r\n    unsigned int stack_top = proc.vaddr_base + proc.memory_size;\r\n    proc.esp = stack_top - 16;\r\n    proc.brk_addr = proc.vaddr_base + proc.memory_size - ELF_HEAP_SIZE;\r\n\r\n    // Finish CPU wiring: init once, then point at entry.\r\n    bochs_cpu_init();\r\n    bochs_cpu_set_esp(proc.esp);\r\n    bochs_cpu_set_eip(proc.entry_point);\r\n    bochs_set_brk(slot, proc.brk_addr);\r\n\r\n    // Mark cpu_initialized so x86_tick skips the lazy-init path and goes\r\n    // straight to bochs_cpu_tick (we've done the full setup just above).\r\n    proc.cpu_initialized = true;\r\n\r\n    return true;\r\n}\r\n\r\nextern \"C\" volatile unsigned char bx_panic_breadcrumbs[64];\r\n// \u2500\u2500 Diagnostic breadcrumb at VGA row 2 \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\n// Each x86_tick lazy-init step writes a tag char to row 2 columns 0..N.\r\n// Tags are:\r\n//   col 0: 'L'  \u2014 entered lazy init\r\n//   col 1: 'M'  \u2014 set_process_memory returned\r\n//   col 2: 'I'  \u2014 bochs_cpu_init returned\r\n//   col 3: 'S'  \u2014 set_esp returned\r\n//   col 4: 'E'  \u2014 set_eip returned\r\n//   col 5: 'B'  \u2014 set_brk returned\r\n//   col 6: 'T'  \u2014 about to call bochs_cpu_tick\r\n//   col 7: 't'  \u2014 bochs_cpu_tick returned\r\n// If lazy init wedges, the last tag visible tells you which call did it.\r\n//\r\n// We write to BOTH the VGA text-mode plane (forensic for pmemsave) AND\r\n// directly to the live framebuffer (visible to the user even if the\r\n// kernel never reaches swap_buffers again). Direct framebuffer writes\r\n// bypass the back buffer and use fb_info.ptr; that means they survive\r\n// a hang inside x86_tick that prevents the next paint cycle.\r\nstatic inline void x86_breadcrumb(int col, char c) {\r\n    if (col < 0 || col >= 80) return;\r\n\r\n    // 1. VGA text-mode plane (forensic).\r\n    {\r\n        volatile unsigned short* p =\r\n            (volatile unsigned short*)(0xB8000 + 2 * 80 + 2 * col);\r\n        *p = (unsigned short)(0x0E00u | (unsigned char)c);\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    // 2. Live framebuffer (visible). 8x8 yellow glyph at row 2 of overlay.\r\n    if (!fb_info.ptr) return;\r\n    if ((unsigned char)c > 127) return;\r\n    const uint8_t* glyph = font + (int)c * 8;\r\n    int x0 = col * 8;\r\n    int y0 = 16;          // row 2 of overlay (rows 0,1,2 each 8px tall)\r\n    if (x0 + 8 > (int)fb_info.width)  return;\r\n    if (y0 + 8 > (int)fb_info.height) return;\r\n\r\n    uint32_t color = 0xFFFF55u;   // bright yellow\r\n    uint32_t bg    = 0x000000u;\r\n    for (int yy = 0; yy < 8; ++yy) {\r\n        uint32_t* row = &fb_info.ptr[(y0 + yy) * (fb_info.pitch / 4) + x0];\r\n        uint8_t bits = glyph[yy];\r\n        for (int xx = 0; xx < 8; ++xx) {\r\n            row[xx] = (bits & (0x80 >> xx)) ? color : bg;\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic bool x86_tick(int slot, int steps) {\r\n    ElfProcess& proc = elf_processes[slot];\r\n    // Tick a process that is alive: active and NOT yet completed.\r\n    // The previous form (`!proc.active || !proc.completed`) was inverted \u2014\r\n    // it returned for every live process, so the guest never advanced and\r\n    // its port-0xE9 output never reached the terminal display.\r\n    if (!proc.active || proc.completed) return false;\r\n\r\n    if (!proc.cpu_initialized) {\r\n        if (!proc.memory_base || proc.memory_size == 0) {\r\n            proc.active    = false;\r\n            proc.completed = true;\r\n            return false;\r\n        }\r\n        // Correct init order:\r\n        //   1. bochs_cpu_init()          -- global Bochs one-time init (idempotent)\r\n        //   2. bochs_activate_slot()     -- select which slot g_active_slot points at\r\n        //   3. bochs_set_process_memory  -- resets CPU to clean state, injects GDT/IDT/\r\n        //                                   stub tables, then restores to protected mode\r\n        //   4. set_esp / set_eip         -- point the protected-mode CPU at guest entry\r\n        //\r\n        // The previous order was: activate \u2192 set_memory \u2192 cpu_init.\r\n        // bochs_set_process_memory does BX_CPU::reset() + slot_restore_cpu() to\r\n        // arrive at protected mode. bochs_cpu_init() called *after* does another\r\n        // BX_CPU::reset() \u2014 wiping that protected mode state back to real mode.\r\n        // The guest then runs (or tries to) in real mode at a 32-bit EIP, which\r\n        // either executes garbage or hangs inside cpu_loop with no visible output.\r\n        x86_breadcrumb(0, 'L');\r\n        bochs_cpu_init();\r\n        x86_breadcrumb(1, 'I');\r\n        bochs_activate_slot(slot);\r\n        bochs_set_process_memory(proc.memory_base, proc.memory_size,\r\n                                 proc.vaddr_base);\r\n        x86_breadcrumb(2, 'M');\r\n        bochs_cpu_set_esp(proc.esp);\r\n        x86_breadcrumb(3, 'S');\r\n        bochs_cpu_set_eip(proc.entry_point);\r\n        x86_breadcrumb(4, 'E');\r\n        bochs_set_brk(slot, proc.vaddr_base + proc.memory_size - ELFHEAPSIZE);\r\n        x86_breadcrumb(5, 'B');\r\n        proc.cpu_initialized = true;\r\n    } else {\r\n        bochs_activate_slot(slot);\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    x86_breadcrumb(6, 'T');\r\n\r\n    // Diagnostic: snapshot EIP BEFORE tick so we can detect an unexpected\r\n    // jump back to entry after the tick. (Don't enable unconditionally \u2014\r\n    // gated on the very-recent-restart condition below so normal output\r\n    // isn't polluted.)\r\n    uint32_t eip_before = bochs_cpu_get_eip();\r\n\r\nbochs_cpu_tick(steps);\r\nx86_breadcrumb(7, 't');\r\n\r\nbool just_started_waiting =\r\n    bochs_process_wants_input(slot) && in_empty(slot);\r\n\r\nif (just_started_waiting) {\r\n    proc.waiting_for_input = true;\r\n    // The guest yielded mid-`IN` on port 0xE7. EIP isn't reliably\r\n    // retired across that abort, so don't trust it this tick \u2014\r\n    // skip the exit/EIP-range checks below entirely and pick back\r\n    // up cleanly next tick once real input has been pushed.\r\n    return true;\r\n}\r\n\r\n// \u2500\u2500\u2500 FIX: detect clean guest-exit signal \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\nif (!proc.active || proc.completed) return false;\r\n\r\nunsigned int eip = bochs_cpu_get_eip();\r\nif (eip == 0 || eip < proc.vaddr_base || eip >= proc.vaddr_base + proc.memory_size) {\r\n    proc.completed = true;\r\n    proc.active    = false;\r\n    return false;\r\n}\r\n\r\nreturn true;\r\n\r\n    return true;\r\n}\r\n// And definition without default:\r\nvoid tick_elf_processes(int steps) {\r\n    bool any_exited_this_frame = false;\r\n\r\n    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES; ++i) {\r\n        ElfProcess& proc = elf_processes[i];\r\n        // Drain output and step a process that is alive: active and not\r\n        // completed. Inverting this gate (the old `!active || !completed`)\r\n        // skipped every live process, leaving the terminal display silent\r\n        // even though the Bochs guest had written bytes to port 0xE9.\r\n        if (!proc.active || proc.completed) continue;\r\n\r\n        while (!out_empty(i)) {\r\n            char tmp[256];\r\n            int n = 0;\r\n            while (!out_empty(i) && n < 255) tmp[n++] = pop_output(i);\r\n            tmp[n] = 0;\r\n            if (proc.terminal && n) {\r\n                proc.terminal->console_print(tmp);\r\n                // Guest produced output. Flag the frame dirty so the main\r\n                // loop actually repaints. Without this the repaint is\r\n                // gated on g_evt_dirty / hasNewInput \u2014 both only set by\r\n                // user input \u2014 so guest output sat invisibly in the\r\n                // terminal buffer until the next keypress (\"need to press\r\n                // enter to print the last lot\").\r\n                g_evt_dirty = true;\r\n            }\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        if (proc.waiting_for_input && in_empty(i)) continue;\r\n\r\n        bool running = x86_tick(i, steps);\r\n\r\n        while (!out_empty(i)) {\r\n            char tmp[256];\r\n            int n = 0;\r\n            while (!out_empty(i) && n < 255) tmp[n++] = pop_output(i);\r\n            tmp[n] = 0;\r\n            if (proc.terminal && n) {\r\n                proc.terminal->console_print(tmp);\r\n                g_evt_dirty = true;   // see note above\r\n            }\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        if (!running) {\r\n            proc.active = false;\r\n            proc.completed = true;\r\n            if (proc.terminal) proc.terminal->captured_elf_slot = -1;\r\n            // A process just finished \u2014 the prompt needs to come back and\r\n            // any final output needs to show. Repaint this frame.\r\n            g_evt_dirty = true;\r\n\r\n            // \u2500\u2500 Per-slot kernel-side teardown \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\n            // Free the slab and stack we allocated in load_and_execute_elf,\r\n            // and clear cpu_initialized so the NEXT process that lands in\r\n            // this slot re-runs the lazy-init path from scratch.\r\n            //\r\n            // We do NOT touch the Bochs glue here. The glue side gets a\r\n            // single heavy `bochs_reset_all_slots()` call at the end of\r\n            // this function (see below) \u2014 that wipes every per-slot\r\n            // mapping AND hardware-resets BX_CPU(0), so launch N starts\r\n            // from the same state as launch 1. Trying to keep the glue\r\n            // \"incrementally consistent\" with surgical per-slot updates\r\n            // had a long history of subtle bugs (the second `bochs hello`\r\n            // looping \"HELLO WOHELLO WO...\" because some untracked CPU\r\n            // field survived across slot reuse). The big-hammer reset\r\n            // is cheap and unambiguous.\r\n            //\r\n            // EXCEPTION (added for concurrent processes): if any OTHER\r\n            // slot is still active, bochs_reset_all_slots() below will\r\n            // be SKIPPED (resetting the live CPU would clobber that\r\n            // peer's mid-execution state). In that case we still need\r\n            // to clear THIS slot's mapping and dangling mem_base pointer\r\n            // before freeing the slab \u2014 otherwise the glue carries\r\n            // forward a dangling pointer that could be dereferenced on\r\n            // a future activate_slot. bochs_release_slot does that\r\n            // surgically without touching peer slots or BX_CPU(0).\r\n            // It's safe to call unconditionally: when the all-slots\r\n            // reset path also fires below, release_slot's wipe is\r\n            // simply overwritten by the same values via reset_all_slots.\r\n            bochs_release_slot(i);\r\n            if (proc.memory_base) { elf_free_bytes(proc.memory_base); proc.memory_base = nullptr; }\r\n            if (proc.stack)       { elf_free_bytes(proc.stack);       proc.stack       = nullptr; }\r\n            proc.memory_size     = 0;\r\n            proc.cpu_initialized = false;\r\n\r\n            // \u2500\u2500 Wipe per-slot I/O ring buffers and transient flags \u2500\u2500\r\n            // Belt-and-braces companion to the scrub in\r\n            // load_and_execute_elf: cleared HERE on the exit edge so\r\n            // the slot is left in a strictly-empty state the instant\r\n            // the process becomes inactive. If any code path on the\r\n            // next frame reads from inbuf/outbuf before the next\r\n            // load_and_execute_elf runs (e.g. a stray\r\n            // tick_elf_processes pass on a slot whose `completed`\r\n            // flag flipped but whose `active` flag hasn't been\r\n            // re-set yet), it sees a clean empty queue rather than\r\n            // stale bytes from the run that just ended.\r\n            //\r\n            // Without this, the third in-place run of `hello` in the\r\n            // same emulator window produced \"HELLO WOHELLO WO...\"\r\n            // loops with leftover keystrokes from previous runs\r\n            // bleeding into the new guest's stdin and the previous\r\n            // guest's tail-end output bleeding into the new\r\n            // terminal display.\r\n            for (int _b = 0; _b < INBUFSIZE;  ++_b) proc.inbuf[_b]  = 0;\r\n            for (int _b = 0; _b < OUTBUFSIZE; ++_b) proc.outbuf[_b] = 0;\r\n            proc.in_head           = 0;\r\n            proc.in_tail           = 0;\r\n            proc.out_head          = 0;\r\n            proc.out_tail          = 0;\r\n            proc.waiting_for_input = false;\r\n            proc.exit_code         = 0;\r\n            proc.input_pos         = 0;\r\n\r\n            any_exited_this_frame = true;\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    // \u2500\u2500 Deferred glue-wide reset \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\n    // If any process exited this frame AND no other Bochs-emulated\r\n    // process is still running, wipe Bochs's glue state back to its\r\n    // post-boot baseline. The next launch will then start from the\r\n    // same state as launch #1.\r\n    //\r\n    // CRITICAL: do NOT reset while another slot is still running.\r\n    // bochs_reset_all_slots() unmaps every slab and hardware-resets\r\n    // BX_CPU(0); doing that to a live slot drops its EIP/CRs/segments\r\n    // back to the post-reset state. On the very next frame, x86_tick\r\n    // would re-enter the lazy-init path and bochs_cpu_set_eip would\r\n    // restart the guest from _start. The visible symptom is a runaway\r\n    // window spamming \"HELLO WOHELLO WO...\" for as long as another\r\n    // window is finishing \u2014 exactly because every frame's exit on\r\n    // window B triggered a reset that restarted window A from the top.\r\n    if (any_exited_this_frame) {\r\n        // Only wipe the Bochs glue when ALL slots are done AND all\r\n        // output has been drained. Use two separate loops: the old\r\n        // single-loop version broke out early on the first active slot,\r\n        // so any_output_pending was never checked for remaining slots.\r\n        bool any_still_active   = false;\r\n        bool any_output_pending = false;\r\n        for (int j = 0; j < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES; ++j) {\r\n            if (elf_processes[j].active) { any_still_active = true; break; }\r\n        }\r\n        if (!any_still_active) {\r\n            for (int j = 0; j < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES; ++j) {\r\n                if (!out_empty(j)) { any_output_pending = true; break; }\r\n            }\r\n        }\r\n        if (!any_still_active && !any_output_pending) {\r\n            bochs_reset_all_slots();\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n     \r\n}\r\n\r\nextern \"C\" void cmd_exec(const char* code_text) {\r\n    if (!code_text) return;\r\n    TCompiler C;\r\n    int ok = C.compile(code_text);\r\n    if (ok < 0) return;\r\n    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_EXEC_PROCESSES; i++) {\r\n        if (!exec_contexts[i].active) {\r\n            exec_contexts[i].active = true;\r\n            exec_contexts[i].exec_id = i;\r\n            exec_contexts[i].prog = C.pr;\r\n            const char* av[] = {\"exec\", nullptr};\r\n            exec_contexts[i].vm.start_execution(exec_contexts[i].prog,1,av,0,0,nullptr);\r\n            return;\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n// =============================================================================\r\n// Helper: write a short status string to VGA text mode row 1 (safe at any\r\n// point in kernel_main, before framebuffer is initialised).\r\n// =============================================================================\r\n// Write msg to VGA text row (0-based)\r\nstatic void vga_dbg_row(int row, const char* msg, uint8_t attr = 0x0F) {\r\n    volatile uint16_t* vga = (volatile uint16_t*)0xB8000 + row * 80;\r\n    int i = 0;\r\n    for (; i < 79 && msg[i]; i++)\r\n        vga[i] = (uint16_t)((uint16_t)(attr << 8) | (uint8_t)msg[i]);\r\n    for (; i < 79; i++)\r\n        vga[i] = (uint16_t)((uint16_t)(attr << 8) | ' ');\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic void vga_status(const char* msg, uint8_t attr = 0x0F) {\r\n    vga_dbg_row(1, msg, attr);\r\n}\r\n\r\n// Write a 32-bit hex value into buf[11] and return it\r\nstatic char* hex32(uint32_t v, char* buf) {\r\n    const char* h = \"0123456789ABCDEF\";\r\n    buf[0]='0'; buf[1]='x';\r\n    for (int i = 7; i >= 0; i--) { buf[2+i] = h[v & 0xF]; v >>= 4; }\r\n    buf[10] = 0;\r\n    return buf;\r\n}\r\n\r\n// Concatenate up to 6 strings into dst[128]\r\nstatic char* vga_cat(char* dst, const char* a, const char* b=\"\",\r\n                      const char* c=\"\", const char* d=\"\") {\r\n    char* p = dst;\r\n    auto app = [&](const char* s){ while(*s && p < dst+127) *p++=*s++; };\r\n    app(a); app(b); app(c); app(d);\r\n    *p = 0; return dst;\r\n}\r\n\r\n// =============================================================================\r\n// Framebuffer probe \u2014 four strategies, always returns something safe.\r\n// =============================================================================\r\nstatic void probe_framebuffer(multiboot_info* mbi,\r\n                              uint32_t& fb_phys,\r\n                              uint32_t& fb_w,\r\n                              uint32_t& fb_h,\r\n                              uint32_t& fb_pitch)\r\n{\r\n    fb_phys  = 0;\r\n    fb_w     = 1024;\r\n    fb_h     = 768;\r\n    fb_pitch = 1024 * 4;\r\n\r\n    // Diagnostic: show multiboot flags and framebuffer fields on VGA rows 2-4\r\n    {\r\n        char dbuf[128]; char hb[11];\r\n        vga_dbg_row(2, vga_cat(dbuf, \"MB flags=\", hex32(mbi->flags, hb)), 0x0E);\r\n        uint32_t raw_fb = (uint32_t)(uintptr_t)mbi->framebuffer_addr;\r\n        char hb2[11];\r\n        vga_dbg_row(3, vga_cat(dbuf, \"FB addr=\", hex32(raw_fb, hb),\r\n                           \" type=\", hex32(mbi->framebuffer_type, hb2)), 0x0E);\r\n        vga_dbg_row(4, vga_cat(dbuf, \"FB w=\", hex32(mbi->framebuffer_width, hb),\r\n                           \" h=\", hex32(mbi->framebuffer_height, hb2)), 0x0E);\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    // Strategy 1: GRUB filled framebuffer fields (our boot.S requests this\r\n    // via MB_FLAGS bit 2).  This is the normal path on QEMU + GRUB 2.\r\n    // framebuffer_type: 0=indexed, 1=RGB/direct, 2=EGA text \u2014 only accept 1.\r\n    if (mbi->flags & (1u << 12)) {\r\n        uint32_t addr = (uint32_t)(uintptr_t)mbi->framebuffer_addr;\r\n        char dbuf[128]; char hb[11];\r\n        vga_dbg_row(5, vga_cat(dbuf, \"S1: addr=\", hex32(addr, hb),\r\n                           \" type=\", hex32(mbi->framebuffer_type, hb)), 0x0A);\r\n        // Accept type 1 (RGB direct) or type 0 (indexed/paletted reported by\r\n        // some VMware GRUB configs). Reject type 2 (EGA text mode).\r\n        if (addr >= 0x1000000u && mbi->framebuffer_type != 2) {\r\n            fb_phys  = addr;\r\n            fb_w     = mbi->framebuffer_width;\r\n            fb_h     = mbi->framebuffer_height;\r\n            fb_pitch = mbi->framebuffer_pitch;\r\n            if (fb_w  > 1024) { fb_w  = 1024; fb_pitch = 1024*4; }\r\n            if (fb_h  > 768)  { fb_h  = 768; }\r\n            vga_dbg_row(5, \"S1: SUCCESS - using GRUB framebuffer\", 0x0A);\r\n            return;\r\n        }\r\n        vga_dbg_row(5, \"S1: SKIPPED (bad addr or type!=1)\", 0x0E);\r\n    } else {\r\n        vga_dbg_row(5, \"S1: SKIPPED (bit12 not set in flags)\", 0x0E);\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    // Strategy 2: Bochs VBE ports (QEMU -vga std).\r\n    {\r\n        auto vbe_out = [](uint16_t idx, uint16_t val) {\r\n            asm volatile(\"outw %0,%1\" :: \"a\"(idx), \"d\"((uint16_t)0x01CE));\r\n            asm volatile(\"outw %0,%1\" :: \"a\"(val), \"d\"((uint16_t)0x01CF));\r\n        };\r\n        auto vbe_in = [](uint16_t idx) -> uint16_t {\r\n            uint16_t v;\r\n            asm volatile(\"outw %0,%1\" :: \"a\"(idx), \"d\"((uint16_t)0x01CE));\r\n            asm volatile(\"inw %1,%0\"  : \"=a\"(v)   : \"d\"((uint16_t)0x01CF));\r\n            return v;\r\n        };\r\n        vbe_out(0x04, 0x00);\r\n        uint16_t id = vbe_in(0x00);\r\n        if (id >= 0xB0C0) {\r\n            vbe_out(0x01, 1024);\r\n            vbe_out(0x02, 768);\r\n            vbe_out(0x03, 32);\r\n            vbe_out(0x05, 1024);\r\n            vbe_out(0x06, 768);\r\n            vbe_out(0x07, 0);\r\n            vbe_out(0x08, 0);\r\n            vbe_out(0x04, 0x41); // ENABLE | LFB_ENABLED\r\n            for (uint16_t bus = 0; bus < 8 && !fb_phys; bus++) {\r\n                for (uint8_t dev = 0; dev < 32 && !fb_phys; dev++) {\r\n                    uint32_t vd = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x00);\r\n                    if ((vd & 0xFFFF) == 0xFFFF) continue;\r\n                    bool is_bochs   = (vd == 0x11111234u);\r\n                    uint32_t cc     = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x08) >> 16;\r\n                    bool is_display = (cc == 0x0300 || cc == 0x0380);\r\n                    if (!is_bochs && !is_display) continue;\r\n                    for (int b = 0; b < 3 && !fb_phys; b++) {\r\n                        uint32_t bar = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x10 + b*4);\r\n                        if (bar & 1) continue;\r\n                        uint32_t addr = bar & 0xFFFFFFF0u;\r\n                        if (addr >= 0x1000000u) fb_phys = addr;\r\n                    }\r\n                }\r\n            }\r\n            if (fb_phys) return;\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    // Strategy 3: VMware SVGA II (vendor 0x15AD, device 0x0405).\r\n    // The SVGA II adapter uses an I/O BAR (BAR0) for its index/value register\r\n    // pair and a memory BAR (BAR1) for the linear framebuffer.  It must be\r\n    // programmed via I/O ports to set the resolution and enable the FB;\r\n    // simply reading BAR1 is not enough \u2014 the FB is not live until ENABLE=1.\r\n    //\r\n    // SVGA II register map (index written to io_base+0, value at io_base+1):\r\n    //   SVGA_REG_ID       = 0   write SVGA_MAGIC|2 to negotiate version\r\n    //   SVGA_REG_ENABLE   = 1   write 1 to enable SVGA mode\r\n    //   SVGA_REG_WIDTH    = 2\r\n    //   SVGA_REG_HEIGHT   = 3\r\n    //   SVGA_REG_BPP      = 7   (bits per pixel)\r\n    //   SVGA_REG_FB_START = 13  returns the physical FB base address\r\n    //   SVGA_REG_PITCH    = 24  returns bytes per scan line\r\n    {\r\n        // Locate the SVGA II PCI device\r\n        uint16_t svga_io = 0;\r\n        uint32_t svga_fb_bar = 0;\r\n        for (uint16_t bus = 0; bus < 8 && !svga_io; bus++) {\r\n            for (uint8_t dev = 0; dev < 32 && !svga_io; dev++) {\r\n                uint32_t vd = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x00);\r\n                // VMware vendor 0x15AD, SVGA II device 0x0405\r\n                if (vd != 0x040515ADu) continue;\r\n                // BAR0 = I/O port base (bit 0 set = I/O space)\r\n                uint32_t bar0 = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x10);\r\n                if (bar0 & 1) svga_io = (uint16_t)(bar0 & 0xFFFE);\r\n                // BAR1 = framebuffer memory base\r\n                svga_fb_bar = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x14) & 0xFFFFFFF0u;\r\n\r\n                // Enable PCI memory + I/O decode (command register, offset 4)\r\n                uint32_t cmd = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x04);\r\n                cmd |= 0x03; // I/O + Memory enable\r\n                // pci_write_config_dword not available, use outl directly\r\n                uint32_t addr_reg = 0x80000000u | ((uint32_t)bus << 16) |\r\n                                    ((uint32_t)dev << 11) | 0x04u;\r\n                outl(0xCF8, addr_reg);\r\n                outl(0xCFC, cmd);\r\n            }\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        {\r\n            char dbuf[128]; char hb[11]; char hb2[11];\r\n            vga_dbg_row(6, vga_cat(dbuf, \"S3: io=\", hex32(svga_io, hb),\r\n                               \" bar1=\", hex32(svga_fb_bar, hb2)), 0x0A);\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        if (svga_io) {\r\n            // Helper lambdas for SVGA II register access\r\n            auto svga_write = [&](uint32_t reg, uint32_t val) {\r\n                outl((uint16_t)(svga_io + 0), reg); // index port\r\n                outl((uint16_t)(svga_io + 4), val); // value port\r\n            };\r\n            auto svga_read = [&](uint32_t reg) -> uint32_t {\r\n                outl((uint16_t)(svga_io + 0), reg);\r\n                return inl((uint16_t)(svga_io + 4));\r\n            };\r\n\r\n            // Negotiate SVGA II version (SVGA_ID_2 = 0x90000002)\r\n            svga_write(0 /*SVGA_REG_ID*/, 0x90000002u);\r\n            uint32_t id = svga_read(0);\r\n            { char dbuf[128]; char hb[11];\r\n              vga_dbg_row(7, vga_cat(dbuf, \"S3: SVGA_ID=\", hex32(id, hb)), 0x0A); }\r\n\r\n            if (id == 0x90000002u) {\r\n                // Set 1024x768x32\r\n                svga_write(2 /*SVGA_REG_WIDTH*/,  1024);\r\n                svga_write(3 /*SVGA_REG_HEIGHT*/,  768);\r\n                svga_write(7 /*SVGA_REG_BITS_PER_PIXEL*/, 32);\r\n                svga_write(1 /*SVGA_REG_ENABLE*/,  1);\r\n\r\n                // Read back actual FB address and pitch\r\n                uint32_t reported_fb = svga_read(13 /*SVGA_REG_FB_START*/);\r\n                uint32_t pitch       = svga_read(24 /*SVGA_REG_BYTES_PER_LINE*/);\r\n\r\n                { char dbuf[128]; char hb[11]; char hb2[11];\r\n                  vga_dbg_row(8, vga_cat(dbuf, \"S3: fb=\", hex32(reported_fb, hb),\r\n                                     \" pitch=\", hex32(pitch, hb2)), 0x0A); }\r\n\r\n                // Use the reported address if valid, fall back to BAR1\r\n                fb_phys  = (reported_fb >= 0x1000000u) ? reported_fb : svga_fb_bar;\r\n                fb_w     = 1024;\r\n                fb_h     = 768;\r\n                fb_pitch = (pitch >= 1024*4) ? pitch : 1024*4;\r\n                if (fb_phys >= 0x1000000u) {\r\n                    vga_dbg_row(9, \"S3: SUCCESS - VMware SVGA II programmed\", 0x0A);\r\n                    return;\r\n                }\r\n                vga_dbg_row(9, \"S3: FAILED - fb addr still bad\", 0x4F);\r\n            } else {\r\n                vga_dbg_row(7, \"S3: FAILED - wrong SVGA_ID (not VMware?)\", 0x4F);\r\n            }\r\n        } else {\r\n            vga_dbg_row(6, \"S3: SKIPPED - no SVGA II device found on PCI\", 0x0E);\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    // Strategy 4: hardcoded fallback.\r\n    // 0xFD000000 = Bochs/QEMU default.\r\n    // 0xE8000000 = VMware Workstation SVGA II BAR1 (confirmed from vmware.log).\r\n    // vmware_svga_init() runs before probe_framebuffer and will override this\r\n    // with the correct BAR1 address, so this fallback is only for QEMU/Bochs.\r\n    fb_phys = 0xFD000000u;\r\n}\r\n\r\n// =============================================================================\r\n// kernel_main\r\n// =============================================================================\r\n// \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\n// VMware SVGA II initialisation.\r\n// Must run BEFORE any framebuffer access.  Returns the live FB base address,\r\n// or 0 if no SVGA II device is found.\r\n//\r\n// The SVGA II I/O port pair lives at BAR0 (an I/O BAR):\r\n//   index port = BAR0 + 0\r\n//   value port = BAR0 + 4        (NOT +1 \u2014 the value port is 32-bit wide)\r\n// Register indices used here:\r\n//   0  SVGA_REG_ID              write 0x90000002 to negotiate SVGA2\r\n//   1  SVGA_REG_ENABLE          write 1 to enable linear framebuffer\r\n//   2  SVGA_REG_WIDTH\r\n//   3  SVGA_REG_HEIGHT\r\n//   7  SVGA_REG_BITS_PER_PIXEL\r\n//  13  SVGA_REG_FB_START        read to get physical FB address\r\n//  24  SVGA_REG_BYTES_PER_LINE  read to get pitch in bytes\r\n// \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\nstruct SVGAResult { uint32_t fb; uint32_t pitch; bool ok; };\r\n\r\n// \u2500\u2500 VMware SVGA II: full PCI scan + I/O programming \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\n// Scans ALL 256 PCI buses (some VMware configs place SVGA on bus > 7),\r\n// all 32 devices, all 8 functions.  Tries both device IDs 0x0405 and 0x0710.\r\n// The I/O BAR may be at BAR0 or BAR2 depending on SVGA revision.\r\nstatic SVGAResult vmware_svga_init(uint32_t w, uint32_t h) {\r\n    SVGAResult r = {0, w*4, false};\r\n\r\n    uint16_t io   = 0;\r\n    uint32_t bar1 = 0;\r\n\r\n    // Full PCI scan \u2014 VMware may put the SVGA on any bus\r\n    for (uint32_t bus = 0; bus < 256 && !io; bus++) {\r\n        for (uint32_t dev = 0; dev < 32 && !io; dev++) {\r\n            for (uint32_t fn = 0; fn < 8 && !io; fn++) {\r\n                uint32_t id = pci_read_config_dword(\r\n                    (uint16_t)bus, (uint8_t)dev, (uint8_t)fn, 0x00);\r\n                if ((id & 0xFFFFu) != 0x15ADu) continue; // not VMware vendor\r\n                uint32_t did = (id >> 16) & 0xFFFFu;\r\n                if (did != 0x0405u && did != 0x0710u) continue; // not SVGA\r\n\r\n                // DO NOT touch the PCI command register.\r\n                // The BIOS has already enabled I/O + Memory decode for SVGA.\r\n                // Re-writing the command register causes VMware to briefly\r\n                // unmap BAR1 (0xe8000000), creating a window where pixel\r\n                // writes crash with \"execute an invalid part of memory\".\r\n\r\n                // Read all 6 BARs \u2014 find the I/O BAR and the memory BAR\r\n                for (int b = 0; b < 6; b++) {\r\n                    uint32_t bar = pci_read_config_dword(\r\n                        (uint16_t)bus, (uint8_t)dev, (uint8_t)fn,\r\n                        (uint8_t)(0x10 + b*4));\r\n                    if ((bar & 1u) && !io) {\r\n                        io = (uint16_t)(bar & 0xFFFCu); // I/O BAR\r\n                    } else if (!(bar & 1u) && !bar1) {\r\n                        uint32_t addr = bar & 0xFFFFFFF0u;\r\n                        if (addr >= 0x1000000u) bar1 = addr; // memory BAR\r\n                    }\r\n                }\r\n            }\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    // Also try the fixed legacy SVGA I/O port (0x4560) used by very old VMware\r\n    if (!io) io = 0x4560u;\r\n\r\n    // I/O helpers\r\n    auto wr = [&](uint32_t reg, uint32_t val) {\r\n        outl(io,     reg);\r\n        outl(io + 4, val);\r\n    };\r\n    auto rd = [&](uint32_t reg) -> uint32_t {\r\n        outl(io, reg);\r\n        return inl(io + 4);\r\n    };\r\n\r\n    // Negotiate SVGA2 \u2014 try SVGA_ID_2, fall back to SVGA_ID_1\r\n    wr(0, 0x90000002u);\r\n    uint32_t svga_id = rd(0);\r\n    if (svga_id != 0x90000002u) {\r\n        wr(0, 0x90000001u);\r\n        svga_id = rd(0);\r\n        if (svga_id != 0x90000001u) return r; // not responding\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    // Program resolution\r\n    wr(2, w);\r\n    wr(3, h);\r\n    wr(7, 32);\r\n\r\n    // Enable\r\n    wr(1, 1);\r\n\r\n    // Read FB address and pitch.\r\n    // VMware Workstation 14+ returns 0 from SVGA_REG_FB_START (reg 13) \u2014\r\n    // the physical framebuffer is always at BAR1. Prefer BAR1 when valid.\r\n    uint32_t fb    = rd(13);\r\n    uint32_t pitch = rd(24);\r\n\r\n    if (bar1 >= 0x1000000u) fb = bar1;  // BAR1 is authoritative on VMware\r\n    else if (fb < 0x1000000u) fb = bar1;\r\n    if (pitch < w * 4) pitch = w * 4;\r\n\r\n    r.fb    = fb;\r\n    r.pitch = pitch;\r\n    r.ok    = (fb >= 0x1000000u);\r\n    return r;\r\n}\r\n\r\n\r\n// Sticky click-edge latches for the guest mouse ABI (bochs_drivers.h's\r\n// mouse_poll()). Set in the main loop below whenever mouse_left_down /\r\n// mouse_right_down transitions from up to down; cleared only once\r\n// kernel_gfx_mouse_poll() (defined further down, called from\r\n// bochs_glue.cpp's bochs_guest_mouse_poll()) has actually reported the\r\n// click to whichever guest program is the focused window's own ELF\r\n// process. File scope (not a kernel_main local) because\r\n// kernel_gfx_mouse_poll() needs to reach them from outside the loop.\r\nstatic bool g_gfx_click_left_pending  = false;\r\nstatic bool g_gfx_click_right_pending = false;\r\n\r\nextern \"C\" void kernel_main(uint32_t magic, uint32_t multiboot_addr) {\r\n\r\n    // \u2500\u2500 Verify Multiboot 1 magic FIRST, before any hardware probing \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\n    // If GRUB (or whatever bootloader) didn't pass 0x2BADB002, we are running\r\n    // under something that doesn't honour the contract \u2014 bail out cleanly\r\n    // instead of poking PCI / FB hardware on bad assumptions.\r\n    if (magic != 0x2BADB002) {\r\n        volatile uint16_t* vga = (volatile uint16_t*)0xB8000;\r\n        vga[0] = 0x4F45; // 'E' on red \u2014 \"bad magic\"\r\n        for (;;)\r\n            asm volatile(\"hlt\");\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    // \u2500\u2500 Initialise heap \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\n    g_allocator.init(kernel_heap, sizeof(kernel_heap));\r\n\r\n    // \u2500\u2500 Step 1: unconditionally try VMware SVGA II first. \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\n    // This MUST happen before reading any framebuffer address because the\r\n    // linear FB is not live until ENABLE=1 is written.\r\n    SVGAResult svga = vmware_svga_init(1024, 768);\r\n\r\n    // \u2500\u2500 Step 2: determine framebuffer address \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\n    multiboot_info* mbi = (multiboot_info*)multiboot_addr;\r\n\r\n    if (svga.ok) {\r\n        // VMware SVGA II programmed successfully \u2014 use its reported address.\r\n        // Wait for VMware to complete the MemSpace re-registration after ENABLE=1.\r\n        // The log shows VMware briefly unmaps/remaps 0xe8000000 during SVGA init;\r\n        // a short spin ensures the mapping is live before we write pixels.\r\n        for (volatile uint32_t i = 0; i < 5000000u; i++);\r\n\t\t\r\n        fb_info = { (uint32_t*)svga.fb, 1024, 768, svga.pitch };\r\n    } else {\r\n        // Not VMware (or SVGA II failed) \u2014 use GRUB multiboot info directly.\r\n        // Accept any type except 2 (EGA text). If bit 12 not set, fall back\r\n        // to probing Bochs VBE ports, then hardcoded candidates.\r\n        uint32_t fb_phys = 0, fb_w = 1024, fb_h = 768, fb_pitch = 1024*4;\r\n\r\n        if ((mbi->flags & (1u << 12)) && mbi->framebuffer_type != 2) {\r\n            // GRUB filled the framebuffer fields (because boot.S requested\r\n            // video mode via Multiboot1 FLAGS bit 2). Take its values\r\n            // verbatim; this is the normal path on real BIOS + VMware-BIOS\r\n            // and on UEFI through GRUB-EFI (which sets it up via GOP).\r\n            fb_phys  = (uint32_t)(uintptr_t)mbi->framebuffer_addr;\r\n            fb_w     = mbi->framebuffer_width  ? mbi->framebuffer_width  : 1024;\r\n            fb_h     = mbi->framebuffer_height ? mbi->framebuffer_height : 768;\r\n            fb_pitch = mbi->framebuffer_pitch  ? mbi->framebuffer_pitch  : fb_w*4;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        if (fb_phys < 0x1000000u) {\r\n            // GRUB gave nothing useful \u2014 try Bochs VBE then hardcoded\r\n            probe_framebuffer(mbi, fb_phys, fb_w, fb_h, fb_pitch);\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        fb_info = { (uint32_t*)(uintptr_t)fb_phys, fb_w, fb_h, fb_pitch };\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    if (fb_info.width  == 0 || fb_info.width  > 1920) fb_info.width  = 1024;\r\n    if (fb_info.height == 0 || fb_info.height > 1200) fb_info.height = 768;\r\n    if (fb_info.pitch  < fb_info.width * 4) fb_info.pitch = fb_info.width * 4;\r\n\r\n    // Real-hardware-only perf fix: mark the linear framebuffer's physical\r\n    // range Write-Combining via MTRR if at all possible (see the big\r\n    // comment above setup_framebuffer_write_combining() in\r\n    // 02_boot_info_and_graphics_driver.h for why this matters -- in short,\r\n    // it's what makes every-frame swap_buffers() blits, and therefore\r\n    // mouse tracking, not feel sluggish on real silicon). No-op under\r\n    // VMware/emulation, where this was never the bottleneck.\r\n    const char* g_fb_wc_status = setup_framebuffer_write_combining();\r\n\r\n    // \u2500\u2500 Step 3: commit and paint \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\n    backbuffer = backbuffer_storage;\r\n    g_gfx.init(false);\r\n\r\n    g_gfx.clear_screen(ColorPalette::DESKTOP_GRAY );\r\n    swap_buffers();\r\n\r\n    // \u2500\u2500 Open first terminal window \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\n    launch_new_terminal();\r\n\r\n    // Report whether the WC fix above actually took effect. This used to\r\n    // fail silently, so \"everything on real hardware is slow -- typing,\r\n    // opening windows, all of it\" had no visible cause: every one of\r\n    // those triggers a full swap_buffers() blit, and if the framebuffer\r\n    // is still stuck Uncacheable (no free MTRR slot is the common real-\r\n    // hardware case -- firmware often claims all of them), that blit\r\n    // stays 20-100x slower no matter what else gets optimized.\r\n    {\r\n        char wcmsg[128];\r\n        vga_cat(wcmsg, \"Framebuffer write-combining: \", g_fb_wc_status, \"\\n\");\r\n        wm.print_to_focused(wcmsg);\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    // \u2500\u2500 PS/2 mouse \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\n    ps2_flush_output_buffer();\r\n    if (initialize_universal_mouse()) {\r\n        wm.print_to_focused(\"Mouse: initialised.\\n\");\r\n    } else {\r\n        wm.print_to_focused(\"Mouse: init failed (keyboard-only mode).\\n\");\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    // \u2500\u2500 AHCI disk + FAT32 \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\n    disk_init();\r\n    // Don't hardcode a port selection here \u2014 disk_init() already\r\n    // auto-selected the first port with an attached device. Hardcoding\r\n    // \"1\" only worked on the QEMU command line in compile.md (which puts\r\n    // the hard disk on ahci.1); bare-metal and VMware almost always have\r\n    // the boot disk on port 0 and would have silently fallen back to no\r\n    // selection. Pass \"\" to just list the detected ports for the user.\r\n    cmd_list_and_select_disk(\"\");\r\n\r\n    if (ahci_base) {\r\n        fat32_init();\r\n        wm.print_to_focused(\"Disk: AHCI found.\\n\");\r\n    } else {\r\n        wm.print_to_focused(\"Disk: no AHCI controller.\\n\");\r\n    }\r\n    if (current_directory_cluster) {\r\n        wm.load_desktop_items();\r\n        if (extract_busybox_to_filesystem())\r\n            wm.print_to_focused(\"BusyBox: saved to FAT32.\\n\");\r\n        else\r\n            wm.print_to_focused(\"BusyBox: ramdisk empty or write failed.\\n\");\r\n        if (extract_hello_to_filesystem())\r\n            wm.print_to_focused(\"hello: saved to FAT32.\\n\");\r\n\r\n        // Write the TCC guest ABI header so user programs can #include \"tcc.h\"\r\n        // to get outb / kprint / kexit without redefining them.\r\n        {\r\n            fat_dir_entry_t tcc_hdr_entry;\r\n            uint32_t th_sec = 0, th_off = 0;\r\n            if (fat32_find_entry(\"tcc.h\", &tcc_hdr_entry, &th_sec, &th_off) != 0) {\r\n                static const char tcc_h[] =\r\n                    \"/* tcc.h \u2014 guest ABI for in-kernel TCC programs */\\n\"\r\n                    \"#ifndef TCC_H\\n#define TCC_H\\n\"\r\n                    \"static inline void outb(unsigned short p, unsigned char v) {\\n\"\r\n                    \"    __asm__ volatile(\\\"outb %0,%1\\\"::\\\"a\\\"(v),\\\"Nd\\\"(p)); }\\n\"\r\n                    \"static inline void kprint(const char* s) {\\n\"\r\n                    \"    while (*s) outb(0xE9, *s++); }\\n\"\r\n                    \"static inline void kexit(int c) {\\n\"\r\n                    \"    outb(0xE8, (unsigned char)c); }\\n\"\r\n                    \"#endif\\n\";\r\n                fat32_write_file(\"tcc.h\", tcc_h, (uint32_t)(sizeof(tcc_h) - 1));\r\n            }\r\n        }\r\n    } else {\r\n        wm.print_to_focused(\"FAT32: not initialised.\\n\");\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    // \u2500\u2500 Bochs CPU / ELF subsystem \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\n    //\r\n    // Run the file-scope C++ constructors NOW, before init_elf_system()\r\n    // and before any code can reach a Bochs entry point. boot.S does not\r\n    // walk __init_array, so without this the Bochs core objects (bx_cpu,\r\n    // bx_mem, the CPUID param objects, icache's pageWriteStampTable, ...)\r\n    // stay as zero-filled BSS with null vtables. The first ELF launched\r\n    // in the Bochs emulator window would then call bochs_cpu_init() ->\r\n    // BX_CPU(0)->initialize() against those null vtables and fault out on\r\n    // its very first tick \u2014 the \"first-time crash / autoclose\" symptom.\r\n    kernel_run_global_ctors_once();\r\n\r\n    // The constructors have now run exactly once. Tell the test module so\r\n    // its own run_init_array_once() inside test_module_run() becomes a\r\n    // no-op \u2014 otherwise the first `test` command would re-run every ctor\r\n    // a second time, re-constructing bx_cpu / bx_mem on top of live\r\n    // (already-initialised) Bochs state.\r\n    test_module_mark_ctors_done();\r\n\r\n    init_elf_system();\r\n\r\n    vga_status(\"Init complete - entering main loop\", 0x0A);\r\n\r\n    // \u2500\u2500 Main loop \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\r\n    const uint32_t TICKS_PER_FRAME = 1;\r\n    // Seeded so that (g_timer_ticks - last_tick_tick) >= TICKS_PER_FRAME is\r\n    // already true on the very first loop iteration (0 - (uint32_t)-1\r\n    // wraps around to 1). With last_tick_tick starting at 0 (matching\r\n    // g_timer_ticks' own starting value of 0), that gating check read\r\n    // (0 - 0) >= 1, i.e. false \u2014 so despite the g_evt_timer/g_evt_dirty\r\n    // flags being forced below, the paint block a few lines down was\r\n    // still skipped on frame one and the desktop (icons, taskbar, clock)\r\n    // stayed blank until the software timer's poll_counter first reached\r\n    // 500, rather than appearing immediately as the comment intended.\r\n    uint32_t last_tick_tick = (uint32_t)0 - TICKS_PER_FRAME;\r\n    int prev_mouse_x = mouse_x;\r\n    int prev_mouse_y = mouse_y;\r\n\r\n    // Force an immediate first render \u2014 don't wait 500 poll iterations\r\n    // for the software timer to tick before the desktop appears.\r\n    g_evt_timer = true;\r\n    g_evt_dirty = true;\r\n\r\n    // Spinning heartbeat at VGA column 79, row 0 (green)\r\n    volatile uint16_t* vga_hb = (volatile uint16_t*)(0xB8000 + 2*79);\r\n    uint32_t hb_counter = 0;\r\n    const char hb_chars[] = \"|/-\\\\\";\r\n    static uint32_t poll_counter = 0;\r\n\r\n    for (;;) {\r\n        if (++hb_counter % 10000 == 0) {\r\n            *vga_hb = (uint16_t)(0x0A00u | (uint8_t)hb_chars[(hb_counter/10000)%4]);\r\n\r\n\t\t}\r\n\r\n\r\n        bool prev_left  = mouse_left_down;\r\n        bool prev_right = mouse_right_down;\r\n        poll_input_universal();\r\n\r\n        bool leftClickedThisFrame  = (mouse_left_down  && !prev_left);\r\n        bool rightClickedThisFrame = (mouse_right_down && !prev_right);\r\n\r\n        // Latch click edges for the guest mouse ABI (bochs_drivers.h's\r\n        // mouse_poll(), via kernel_gfx_mouse_poll() below). This loop\r\n        // runs every iteration, but tick_elf_processes() -- where a\r\n        // guest actually gets to poll -- only runs once every\r\n        // TICKS_PER_FRAME iterations, so a plain \"clicked this frame\"\r\n        // bool would often be gone again before any guest ever saw it.\r\n        // Sticky-until-consumed fixes that: set here, cleared only by\r\n        // kernel_gfx_mouse_poll() once it's actually been reported.\r\n        if (leftClickedThisFrame)  g_gfx_click_left_pending  = true;\r\n        if (rightClickedThisFrame) g_gfx_click_right_pending = true;\r\n        bool mouse_moved = (mouse_x != prev_mouse_x || mouse_y != prev_mouse_y);\r\n        bool key_pressed = (last_key_press != 0);\r\n\r\n        // Anything that can actually change what's on screen beyond the\r\n        // cursor's own position: a key, a fresh click edge, or a button\r\n        // being held (drag/resize/paint-canvas in progress). A plain\r\n        // hover-move with nothing held is deliberately NOT included here\r\n        // -- see the cursor-only fast path further down for why forcing\r\n        // a full repaint for that case is what made mouse movement feel\r\n        // heavy.\r\n        bool input_needs_full_repaint = key_pressed || leftClickedThisFrame ||\r\n                                         rightClickedThisFrame || mouse_left_down ||\r\n                                         mouse_right_down;\r\n\r\n        if (key_pressed || mouse_moved || leftClickedThisFrame || rightClickedThisFrame) {\r\n            g_evt_input = true;\r\n            if (input_needs_full_repaint) g_input_state.hasNewInput = true;\r\n            prev_mouse_x = mouse_x;\r\n            prev_mouse_y = mouse_y;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        // Route keypresses to any active ELF guest process\r\n        //\r\n        // FIX (focus ignored on click): this used to scan every slot\r\n        // and hand the keystroke to the FIRST one that was active &&\r\n        // waiting_for_input, regardless of which terminal window was\r\n        // actually focused/clicked. With two terminals each running\r\n        // a program that reads stdin, typing while Terminal B was\r\n        // focused would silently feed Terminal A instead (whichever\r\n        // slot happened to be waiting), and last_key_press got\r\n        // zeroed here before wm.handle_input ever saw it \u2014 so B's\r\n        // own captured_elf_slot path (kernel.cpp's BUSYBOX CAPTURE\r\n        // block) never even ran.\r\n        //\r\n        // Fix: only steal the keystroke for the ELF slot owned by\r\n        // the currently FOCUSED window. If that slot isn't waiting\r\n        // for input (or no window is focused, or the focused window\r\n        // isn't capturing a slot), fall through and let the normal\r\n        // g_evt_input / wm.handle_input path below handle the key \u2014\r\n        // which is what already correctly threads input to whichever\r\n        // terminal's captured_elf_slot the user clicked into.\r\n        if (last_key_press != 0) {\r\n            int fs = wm.get_focused_elf_slot();\r\n            if (fs >= 0 && fs < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES &&\r\n                elf_processes[fs].active && elf_processes[fs].waiting_for_input) {\r\n                push_input(fs, last_key_press);\r\n                elf_processes[fs].waiting_for_input = false;\r\n                last_key_press = 0;\r\n            }\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        // Software timer (no PIT \u2014 IRQ0 would fire into an unhandled vector)\r\n        if (++poll_counter >= 500) {\r\n            poll_counter  = 0;\r\n            g_evt_timer   = true;\r\n\t\t\tg_evt_dirty = true;\r\n            g_timer_ticks++;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        if (g_evt_input) {\r\n            g_evt_input = false;\r\n            wm.handle_input(last_key_press, mouse_x, mouse_y,\r\n                            mouse_left_down,\r\n                            leftClickedThisFrame,\r\n                            rightClickedThisFrame);\r\n            if (last_key_press != 0) last_key_press = 0;\r\n            // Only force the expensive full-desktop repaint for input\r\n            // that can actually change what's drawn. handle_input()\r\n            // early-returns doing nothing at all for a plain hover-move\r\n            // (no button down, no click edge), so there's nothing here\r\n            // for a full repaint to pick up in that case anyway.\r\n            if (input_needs_full_repaint) g_evt_dirty = true;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        wm.cleanup_closed_windows();\r\n\r\n        // Guest-process ticking stays paced by the software timer (as\r\n        // before) \u2014 this is what governs how much CPU-emulation work a\r\n        // running ELF guest gets per iteration, and shouldn't speed up\r\n        // or slow down just because the mouse is being moved.\r\n        if (g_evt_timer && (g_timer_ticks - last_tick_tick) >= TICKS_PER_FRAME) {\r\n            // Tick ELF processes BEFORE the paint so any breadcrumbs they\r\n            // write (x86_breadcrumb at row 2, glue's tick markers at row 0\r\n            // col 72/73, panic tags at col 70) are reflected in the next\r\n            // swap_buffers. Otherwise a hang inside tick_elf_processes\r\n            // would leave the last painted frame without the breadcrumbs\r\n            // pointing at where the hang happened.\r\n            //\r\n            // A single tick_elf_processes() call can itself block for a\r\n            // perceptible chunk of wall time -- bochs_cpu_tick() (see\r\n            // bochs_glue.cpp) hands the guest a budget of up to tens of\r\n            // thousands of software-interpreted instructions per call,\r\n            // and a CPU-bound guest with little port I/O to yield on\r\n            // (its main way of handing control back early) can burn\r\n            // through that whole budget before returning. During that\r\n            // entire call the mouse can't be polled or redrawn at all --\r\n            // \"everything is smooth until a program is actually running,\r\n            // then the cursor stutters\" is exactly what that looks like.\r\n            // Splitting the interval's guest-execution budget into\r\n            // several smaller ticks and polling+redrawing the cursor\r\n            // between each one gives the pointer many more chances to\r\n            // stay current while a program runs, instead of only one at\r\n            // the very end of the interval. (A single sub-tick can still\r\n            // block for its own full budget if the guest never yields at\r\n            // all -- this doesn't fix that pathological case, but it's\r\n            // the common one: any guest doing the usual putc/getc-style\r\n            // I/O yields far more often than that.)\r\n            const int GUEST_SUBTICKS = 8;\r\n            for (int st = 0; st < GUEST_SUBTICKS; st++) {\r\n                tick_elf_processes(1);\r\n\r\n                poll_input_universal();\r\n                bool subtick_mouse_moved = (mouse_x != prev_mouse_x || mouse_y != prev_mouse_y);\r\n                if (subtick_mouse_moved) {\r\n                    prev_mouse_x = mouse_x;\r\n                    prev_mouse_y = mouse_y;\r\n                    // Only the cheap cursor-only redraw here -- if a\r\n                    // button is down (drag/resize/paint in progress) or\r\n                    // the backbuffer isn't in a known-clean state, leave\r\n                    // it for the normal full-repaint path below instead\r\n                    // of risking a partial/stale-looking mid-tick frame.\r\n                    if (g_backbuffer_is_clean_on_screen &&\r\n                        !mouse_left_down && !mouse_right_down) {\r\n                        erase_cursor_from_screen();\r\n                        draw_cursor_to_screen(mouse_x, mouse_y, ColorPalette::CURSOR_WHITE);\r\n                    }\r\n                }\r\n            }\r\n\r\n            last_tick_tick = g_timer_ticks;\r\n            g_evt_timer     = false;\r\n        }\r\n\r\n        // Repaint is intentionally NOT gated on g_evt_timer above \u2014 only\r\n        // on whether anything actually changed (g_evt_dirty /\r\n        // hasNewInput). It used to require BOTH the timer *and* a dirty\r\n        // flag, but the software timer here only fires once every 500\r\n        // raw loop iterations (poll_counter, further up \u2014 there's no\r\n        // real PIT/IRQ0 to drive it). Mouse movement/clicks and\r\n        // keystrokes are polled and flagged dirty on EVERY iteration\r\n        // (see poll_input_universal() + the g_evt_input block above),\r\n        // so gating the actual repaint behind that same slow timer made\r\n        // the on-screen cursor visibly lag ~500 iterations behind the\r\n        // real, continuously-updated mouse_x/mouse_y \u2014 i.e. the mouse\r\n        // felt \"slow\"/laggy even though input was being read promptly.\r\n        // Repainting as soon as something is dirty fixes that; the\r\n        // timer above still exists to pace guest ticking and to cover\r\n        // the \"nothing moved, but a guest changed its own frame\"\r\n        // periodic case via g_evt_timer's own g_evt_dirty = true (set\r\n        // where poll_counter reaches 500, further up).\r\n        if (g_evt_dirty || g_input_state.hasNewInput) {\r\n            g_evt_dirty               = false;\r\n            g_input_state.hasNewInput = false;\r\n            g_gfx.clear_screen(ColorPalette::DESKTOP_GRAY );\r\n            wm.update_all();\r\n            // Diagnostic overlay: paint VGA text-mode rows 0/1/2\r\n            // (boot/panic/tick breadcrumbs, host-IDT fault tags,\r\n            // x86_tick lazy-init progress) onto the framebuffer so\r\n            // they are visible in graphics mode. Drawn last so it\r\n            // overlays everything.\r\n            draw_vga_overlay();\r\n            swap_buffers();\r\n            // The backbuffer just pushed to the screen has no cursor in\r\n            // it (draw_cursor() is intentionally not called here any\r\n            // more -- see the cursor-only fast path comment below), so\r\n            // draw the cursor glyph straight onto the framebuffer now\r\n            // and remember where. That's what lets the *next* frame, if\r\n            // it's just a plain pointer move, skip the full repaint\r\n            // entirely.\r\n            erase_cursor_from_screen(); // no-op the first time through\r\n            draw_cursor_to_screen(mouse_x, mouse_y, ColorPalette::CURSOR_WHITE);\r\n            g_backbuffer_is_clean_on_screen = true;\r\n        } else if (mouse_moved && g_backbuffer_is_clean_on_screen) {\r\n            // Cursor-only fast path: nothing but the pointer position\r\n            // changed this frame (no key, no click, no button held --\r\n            // handle_input() already established there's nothing else\r\n            // to redraw). Move just the ~8x12px cursor glyph directly\r\n            // on the framebuffer instead of clearing and redrawing the\r\n            // entire desktop/every window/the taskbar clock and doing a\r\n            // full 1024x768 blit for a one-pixel pointer nudge.\r\n            erase_cursor_from_screen();\r\n            draw_cursor_to_screen(mouse_x, mouse_y, ColorPalette::CURSOR_WHITE);\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n// =============================================================================\r\n// extern \"C\" bridges for tcc_kernel.cpp\r\n// =============================================================================\r\n// tcc_kernel.cpp is compiled as freestanding C++ and cannot include the full\r\n// kernel headers. These thin wrappers expose the symbols it needs with plain\r\n// C linkage so the linker resolves them without name-mangling.\r\n\r\nextern \"C\" {\r\n\r\n// Bridge for bochs_glue.cpp's bochs_guest_mouse_poll() (guest port\r\n// 0xEF, see bochs_drivers.h's mouse_poll()). Reports the compositor's\r\n// cursor to ELF slot `slot` ONLY if that slot's window is BOTH the\r\n// currently focused window AND showing a live gfx canvas the cursor\r\n// happens to be over \u2014 everything else (titlebar drags, clicks on\r\n// other windows, the desktop, the taskbar) is handled entirely by\r\n// WindowManager::handle_input() above and never touches this path at\r\n// all, which is what keeps those clicks going to the compositor only.\r\n// On any \"no\" (wrong/no slot, not focused, no window, not in gfx\r\n// mode, cursor outside the canvas) this leaves *out_x/*out_y/\r\n// *out_buttons at their caller-supplied zero and returns false \u2014 a\r\n// clean \"cursor absent\" snapshot rather than stale or foreign data.\r\nbool kernel_gfx_mouse_poll(int slot, int* out_x, int* out_y,\r\n                            unsigned char* out_buttons) {\r\n    if (slot < 0 || wm.get_focused_elf_slot() != slot) return false;\r\n\r\n    Window* win = wm.find_window_by_elf_slot(slot);\r\n    if (!win) return false;\r\n\r\n    int lx = 0, ly = 0;\r\n    bool in_win = win->gfx_hit_test(mouse_x, mouse_y, &lx, &ly);\r\n    if (in_win) {\r\n        *out_x = lx;\r\n        *out_y = ly;\r\n        *out_buttons |= 0x10; // MOUSE_BIT_IN_WINDOW (bochs_drivers.h)\r\n    }\r\n    if (mouse_left_down)  *out_buttons |= 0x01; // MOUSE_BIT_LEFT_DOWN\r\n    if (mouse_right_down) *out_buttons |= 0x02; // MOUSE_BIT_RIGHT_DOWN\r\n    if (g_gfx_click_left_pending) {\r\n        *out_buttons |= 0x04; // MOUSE_BIT_LEFT_CLICKED\r\n        g_gfx_click_left_pending = false;\r\n    }\r\n    if (g_gfx_click_right_pending) {\r\n        *out_buttons |= 0x08; // MOUSE_BIT_RIGHT_CLICKED\r\n        g_gfx_click_right_pending = false;\r\n    }\r\n    return true;\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid tcc_bridge_console_print(const char* s) {\r\n    console_print(s);\r\n}\r\nstatic inline bool is_cc_safe_char(unsigned char c) {\r\n    return c == '\\n' || c == '\\r' || c == '\\t' || (c >= 32 && c != 127);\r\n}\r\n\r\nchar* tcc_bridge_fat32_read(const char* filename) {\r\n    // Path-aware: fat32_read_file_as_string() only ever looks in the\r\n    // current directory and treats any '/' as part of a garbage flat\r\n    // name. fat32_read_file_as_string_path() falls back to that exact\r\n    // behavior when there's no '/' in filename, and otherwise resolves\r\n    // the directory part first -- needed both for `cc sub/foo.c` and for\r\n    // #include \"sub/foo.h\" (this same function is what TCC's open()\r\n    // shim calls for every #include, via the fat32_read_file_as_string\r\n    // macro alias in tcc_kernel.cpp).\r\n    char *data = fat32_read_file_as_string_path(filename);\r\n\r\n    if (!data) {\r\n        return NULL;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    for (char *p = data; *p; ++p) {\r\n        if (!is_cc_safe_char((unsigned char)*p)) {\r\n            // FIX: this used to return NULL here without freeing `data`,\r\n            // leaking the whole file's buffer every time a source file\r\n            // or header contained a byte outside the \"safe\" set.\r\n            delete[] data;\r\n            return NULL;\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    return data;\r\n}\r\n\r\nint tcc_bridge_fat32_write(const char* filename, const void* data, unsigned int size) {\r\n    return fat32_write_file(filename, data, (uint32_t)size);\r\n}\r\n\r\nint tcc_bridge_exec_elf(void* terminal, const char* filename, const char* args) {\r\n    TerminalWindow* tw = (TerminalWindow*)terminal;\r\n    return tw->exec_elf(filename, args);\r\n}\r\n\r\n} // extern \"C\""}], "files": [{"file_uuid": "2c5f123f-fdd5-44c4-bd6e-c56bcab145bd", "file_name": ""}], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0e733-c6eb-7c54-8635-85c15afdb777"}, {"uuid": "01a0e734-3524-726e-85eb-ef7732b6909a", "text": "\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nI couldn't compile this against your tree, so treat it as untested. Two edits to `11_kernel_main.h` are below.\n\n## What was causing the lag\n\n1. **Pacing depended on loop speed.** The \"timer\" was `++poll_counter >= 500`, so frame rate and guest speed changed with CPU speed. Because that timer also set `g_evt_dirty`, the full desktop repaint (clear, `update_all`, 1024\u00d7768 blit) could fire many times a second even when nothing changed.\n2. **Input events were lost during guest ticks.** `poll_input_universal()` runs inside the guest sub-tick loop, but click edges and `prev_mouse_x/y` were only tracked at the top of the loop.\n   - A click that landed during a sub-tick was never seen, and neither was a mouse move.\n   - With a button held, `handle_input()` was skipped, so drags and resizes stalled while a program ran. That looks like lag.\n3. **The guest only got CPU when the slow timer fired.** It received 8 sub-ticks per 500 loop iterations, so guests ran slowly while the mouse polling still stuttered.\n4. **Nothing capped repaints.** Held buttons and chatty guest output could trigger a full repaint on every iteration.\n5. **`cleanup_closed_windows()` ran on every spin of the loop.**\n\n## Edit 1: helpers (above `extern \"C\" void kernel_main`, after the `g_gfx_click_*_pending` flags)\n\n```cpp\n// \u2500\u2500 Pacing helpers: TSC time base, calibrated against PIT channel 2 \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n// PIT ch.2 is read by polling port 0x61, so it needs no IRQ (IRQ0 is\n// unhandled in this kernel). All 64-bit math below avoids division so it\n// doesn't need __udivdi3 in a freestanding i386 build.\nstatic inline uint64_t ml_rdtsc() {\n    uint32_t lo, hi;\n    asm volatile(\"rdtsc\" : \"=a\"(lo), \"=d\"(hi));\n    return ((uint64_t)hi << 32) | lo;\n}\nstatic inline void    ml_outb(uint16_t p, uint8_t v) { asm volatile(\"outb %0,%1\" :: \"a\"(v), \"Nd\"(p)); }\nstatic inline uint8_t ml_inb (uint16_t p) { uint8_t v; asm volatile(\"inb %1,%0\" : \"=a\"(v) : \"Nd\"(p)); return v; }\n\nstatic uint32_t ml_tsc_per_ms = 2000000;   // fallback ~2 GHz if calibration fails\n\nstatic void ml_calibrate_tsc() {\n    uint8_t g = ml_inb(0x61);\n    ml_outb(0x61, g & 0xFC);                 // gate off, speaker off\n    ml_outb(0x43, 0xB0);                     // ch2, lo/hi byte, mode 0\n    ml_outb(0x42, 0x9C); ml_outb(0x42, 0x2E);// 0x2E9C = 11932 ticks \u2248 10 ms\n    ml_outb(0x61, (g & 0xFC) | 0x01);        // gate on: start counting\n    uint64_t t0 = ml_rdtsc();\n    uint32_t guard = 0;\n    bool ok = true;\n    while (!(ml_inb(0x61) & 0x20)) {         // OUT2 goes high at terminal count\n        if (++guard > 20000000u) { ok = false; break; }\n    }\n    uint64_t t1 = ml_rdtsc();\n    ml_outb(0x61, g);                        // restore port 0x61\n    uint64_t d = t1 - t0;\n    if (ok && d > 100000ull && d < 0xFFFFFFFFull) ml_tsc_per_ms = (uint32_t)d / 10;\n}\n```\n\n## Edit 2: replace everything from `// \u2500\u2500 Main loop \u2500\u2500` through the end of the `for (;;) { ... }` in `kernel_main`\n\n```cpp\n    // \u2500\u2500 Main loop \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n    ml_calibrate_tsc();\n\n    const uint64_t FRAME_TSC = (uint64_t)ml_tsc_per_ms * 16;    // ~60 Hz paint / tick cap\n    const uint64_t GUEST_TSC = (uint64_t)ml_tsc_per_ms * 6;     // max guest time per pass\n    const uint64_t CLOCK_TSC = (uint64_t)ml_tsc_per_ms * 1000;  // idle refresh (taskbar clock)\n\n    uint64_t last_paint = 0;\n    uint64_t last_tick  = ml_rdtsc();\n    uint64_t last_clock = last_tick;\n\n    // Input state: edges/motion are accumulated by poll_and_latch() no matter\n    // WHERE polling happens (main loop or inside the guest slice), then\n    // consumed once per pass, so nothing is lost while a guest is running.\n    bool prev_left = mouse_left_down, prev_right = mouse_right_down;\n    int  seen_x = mouse_x, seen_y = mouse_y;\n    bool acc_lclick = false, acc_rclick = false, acc_moved = false;\n\n    // Where the cursor glyph currently sits on the live framebuffer.\n    int  drawn_x = -1, drawn_y = -1;\n\n    auto poll_and_latch = [&]() {\n        poll_input_universal();\n        if (mouse_left_down  && !prev_left)  { acc_lclick = true; g_gfx_click_left_pending  = true; }\n        if (mouse_right_down && !prev_right) { acc_rclick = true; g_gfx_click_right_pending = true; }\n        prev_left  = mouse_left_down;\n        prev_right = mouse_right_down;\n        if (mouse_x != seen_x || mouse_y != seen_y) {\n            acc_moved = true; seen_x = mouse_x; seen_y = mouse_y;\n        }\n    };\n\n    // Cheap cursor-only update straight on the framebuffer.\n    auto fast_cursor = [&]() {\n        if (g_backbuffer_is_clean_on_screen && !mouse_left_down && !mouse_right_down &&\n            (mouse_x != drawn_x || mouse_y != drawn_y)) {\n            erase_cursor_from_screen();\n            draw_cursor_to_screen(mouse_x, mouse_y, ColorPalette::CURSOR_WHITE);\n            drawn_x = mouse_x; drawn_y = mouse_y;\n        }\n    };\n\n    // A guest is \"runnable\" if it's alive and not parked waiting on stdin.\n    auto any_runnable = [&]() -> bool {\n        for (int i = 0; i < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES; ++i) {\n            ElfProcess& p = elf_processes[i];\n            if (p.active && !p.completed && !(p.waiting_for_input && in_empty(i))) return true;\n        }\n        return false;\n    };\n\n    g_evt_timer = true;\n    g_evt_dirty = true;\n\n    volatile uint16_t* vga_hb = (volatile uint16_t*)(0xB8000 + 2*79);\n    uint32_t hb_counter = 0;\n    const char hb_chars[] = \"|/-\\\\\";\n\n    for (;;) {\n        if ((++hb_counter & 0x3FFF) == 0)\n            *vga_hb = (uint16_t)(0x0A00u | (uint8_t)hb_chars[(hb_counter >> 14) & 3]);\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 1. Input (edges + motion accumulated, then consumed) \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n        poll_and_latch();\n        bool leftClickedThisFrame  = acc_lclick;\n        bool rightClickedThisFrame = acc_rclick;\n        bool mouse_moved           = acc_moved;\n        acc_lclick = acc_rclick = acc_moved = false;\n        bool key_pressed = (last_key_press != 0);\n\n        bool input_needs_full_repaint = key_pressed || leftClickedThisFrame ||\n                                        rightClickedThisFrame || mouse_left_down ||\n                                        mouse_right_down;\n\n        if (key_pressed || mouse_moved || leftClickedThisFrame || rightClickedThisFrame) {\n            g_evt_input = true;\n            if (input_needs_full_repaint) g_input_state.hasNewInput = true;\n        }\n\n        // Route keypress to the FOCUSED window's ELF slot only (unchanged).\n        if (last_key_press != 0) {\n            int fs = wm.get_focused_elf_slot();\n            if (fs >= 0 && fs < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES &&\n                elf_processes[fs].active && elf_processes[fs].waiting_for_input) {\n                push_input(fs, last_key_press);\n                elf_processes[fs].waiting_for_input = false;\n                last_key_press = 0;\n            }\n        }\n\n        bool handled_input = false;\n        if (g_evt_input) {\n            g_evt_input = false;\n            handled_input = true;\n            wm.handle_input(last_key_press, mouse_x, mouse_y,\n                            mouse_left_down,\n                            leftClickedThisFrame,\n                            rightClickedThisFrame);\n            if (last_key_press != 0) last_key_press = 0;\n            if (input_needs_full_repaint) g_evt_dirty = true;\n        }\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 2. Real-time timebase (replaces the 500-iteration software timer) \u2500\n        uint64_t now = ml_rdtsc();\n        bool frame_due = false;\n        if (now - last_tick >= FRAME_TSC) {\n            last_tick = now;                 // = now, not += : no catch-up bursts\n            g_timer_ticks++;\n            g_evt_timer = true;\n            frame_due = true;\n        }\n        if (now - last_clock >= CLOCK_TSC) { // idle refresh: clock only, ~1 Hz\n            last_clock = now;\n            g_evt_dirty = true;\n        }\n\n        if (handled_input || frame_due) wm.cleanup_closed_windows();\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 3. Guest slice: time-boxed, polls input + moves cursor between ticks\n        bool ran_guest = false;\n        if (any_runnable()) {\n            ran_guest = true;\n            uint64_t slice_start = now;\n            do {\n                tick_elf_processes(1);\n                poll_and_latch();\n                fast_cursor();\n            } while (ml_rdtsc() - slice_start < GUEST_TSC && any_runnable());\n            if (frame_due) g_evt_dirty = true;   // guest may have redrawn its canvas\n        }\n        if (frame_due) g_evt_timer = false;\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 4. Paint: only when dirty, at most once per FRAME_TSC \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n        now = ml_rdtsc();\n        bool want_paint = g_evt_dirty || g_input_state.hasNewInput;\n        if (want_paint && (now - last_paint) >= FRAME_TSC) {\n            g_evt_dirty               = false;\n            g_input_state.hasNewInput = false;\n            g_gfx.clear_screen(ColorPalette::DESKTOP_GRAY);\n            wm.update_all();\n            draw_vga_overlay();\n            swap_buffers();\n            erase_cursor_from_screen();\n            draw_cursor_to_screen(mouse_x, mouse_y, ColorPalette::CURSOR_WHITE);\n            drawn_x = mouse_x; drawn_y = mouse_y;\n            g_backbuffer_is_clean_on_screen = true;\n            last_paint = ml_rdtsc();\n        } else {\n            // Nothing to paint (or paint deferred to hold 60 Hz): keep the\n            // pointer responsive with the cheap path.\n            fast_cursor();\n            if (!want_paint && !ran_guest && !handled_input)\n                asm volatile(\"pause\");   // idle: be kind to the (virtual) CPU\n        }\n    }\n```\n\n## What changed\n\n- **Real-time pacing.** A TSC clock calibrated from PIT channel 2 replaces the iteration counter. Paints are capped at about 60 Hz, and a deferred paint keeps its dirty flag and happens next frame. The desktop repaints about once a second when idle (clock only), and only at frame rate while a guest is running.\n- **No lost input.** `poll_and_latch()` is used everywhere, so clicks and motion that arrive during a guest tick still reach `handle_input()` and the guest mouse ABI latches.\n- **Guest slice.** A running guest now gets up to 6 ms per pass instead of waiting on the slow timer, with input polled and the cursor moved between ticks. Guests that are waiting for stdin no longer keep the loop busy.\n- **Less per-iteration work.** `cleanup_closed_windows()` only runs after input or on a frame tick, and the heartbeat uses a shift instead of modulo.\n- **No `hlt`.** Without a working timer IRQ, `hlt` would freeze the machine, so idle uses `pause` instead.\n\n## Things to check when you build it\n\n- **`g_timer_ticks` rate.** It now advances at about 60 Hz instead of once per 500 iterations. Grep for its other users (the taskbar clock, for example) in case any of them assumed the old rate.\n- **VMware and real hardware.** Calibration has a timeout and falls back to an assumed 2 GHz TSC. If the PIT ch.2 gate isn't emulated, pacing will run slightly fast or slow but won't hang.\n- **Remaining costs in other files.** These are out of scope here but are the next things to fix:\n  - Each repaint still clears the whole screen and redraws every window (`update_all`). Per-window dirty rects in `04_window_system.h` and `10_window_manager_impl.h` would help.\n  - `swap_buffers()` copies the full frame, so copying only changed rows would help.\n  - `draw_vga_overlay()` reads legacy VGA memory on every paint. That's slow on real hardware, so gate it behind a debug flag.\n  - The boot-time `for (volatile ... 5000000)` spin only affects startup.\n\nIf you send `04_window_system.h` or `10_window_manager_impl.h`, I can add dirty-rect redraw so full repaints stop being needed for most changes.", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:49:15.333338Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:49:47.424751Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Diagnosing causes of lag in the main loop."}, {"summary": "Redesigning timing and repaint pacing to smooth out CPU load."}, {"summary": "Tracing lost click and drag events during guest subticks."}, {"summary": "Avoiding costly 64-bit division while calibrating a timer source."}, {"summary": "Wiring up PIT-based timer calibration with a fallback rate."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:49:47.424819Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:49:48.298811Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01W7mk8QdWGwEWtPWe1v4dBs", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "ls -la /mnt/user-data/uploads; grep -n \"Main loop\\|^    for (;;)\\|^extern \\\"C\\\" void kernel_main\\|g_gfx_click_right_pending  = false\" /mnt/user-data/uploads/*11_kernel_main* 2>/dev/null", "description": "Check whether the uploaded file exists on disk and locate the loop"}, "message": "Check whether the uploaded file exists on disk and locate the loop", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"ls -la /mnt/user-data/uploads; grep -n \\\"Main loop\\\\|^    for (;;)\\\\|^extern \\\\\\\"C\\\\\\\" void kernel_main\\\\|g_gfx_click_right_pending  = false\\\" /mnt/user-data/uploads/*11_kernel_main* 2>/dev/null\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:49:49.497927Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:49:49.497962Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01W7mk8QdWGwEWtPWe1v4dBs", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":2,\"stdout\":\"total 4\\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 claude ubuntu    0 Sep 28 08:49 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 5 root   root   4096 Sep 28 08:49 ..\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"plaintext\", \"code\": \"total 4\\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 claude ubuntu    0 Sep 28 08:49 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 5 root   root   4096 Sep 28 08:49 ..\\n\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:49:50.994171Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:50:24.777573Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Drafting replacement code blocks for the kernel insertion point."}, {"summary": "Calibrating timing routines and setting frame-pacing constants."}, {"summary": "Wiring mouse and keyboard polling into the guest loop."}, {"summary": "Balancing guest CPU time against smooth compositor repaints."}, {"summary": "Handling idle guest processes and deferred paint edge cases."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:50:24.777681Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:50:46.308750Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "I couldn't compile this against your tree, so treat it as untested. Two edits to `11_kernel_main.h` are below.\n\n## What was causing the lag\n\n1. **Pacing depended on loop speed.** The \"timer\" was `++poll_counter >= 500`, so frame rate and guest speed changed with CPU speed. Because that timer also set `g_evt_dirty`, the full desktop repaint (clear, `update_all`, 1024\u00d7768 blit) could fire many times a second even when nothing changed.\n2. **Input events were lost during guest ticks.** `poll_input_universal()` runs inside the guest sub-tick loop, but click edges and `prev_mouse_x/y` were only tracked at the top of the loop.\n   - A click that landed during a sub-tick was never seen, and neither was a mouse move.\n   - With a button held, `handle_input()` was skipped, so drags and resizes stalled while a program ran. That looks like lag.\n3. **The guest only got CPU when the slow timer fired.** It received 8 sub-ticks per 500 loop iterations, so guests ran slowly while the mouse polling still stuttered.\n4. **Nothing capped repaints.** Held buttons and chatty guest output could trigger a full repaint on every iteration.\n5. **`cleanup_closed_windows()` ran on every spin of the loop.**\n\n## Edit 1: helpers (above `extern \"C\" void kernel_main`, after the `g_gfx_click_*_pending` flags)\n\n```cpp\n// \u2500\u2500 Pacing helpers: TSC time base, calibrated against PIT channel 2 \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n// PIT ch.2 is read by polling port 0x61, so it needs no IRQ (IRQ0 is\n// unhandled in this kernel). All 64-bit math below avoids division so it\n// doesn't need __udivdi3 in a freestanding i386 build.\nstatic inline uint64_t ml_rdtsc() {\n    uint32_t lo, hi;\n    asm volatile(\"rdtsc\" : \"=a\"(lo), \"=d\"(hi));\n    return ((uint64_t)hi << 32) | lo;\n}\nstatic inline void    ml_outb(uint16_t p, uint8_t v) { asm volatile(\"outb %0,%1\" :: \"a\"(v), \"Nd\"(p)); }\nstatic inline uint8_t ml_inb (uint16_t p) { uint8_t v; asm volatile(\"inb %1,%0\" : \"=a\"(v) : \"Nd\"(p)); return v; }\n\nstatic uint32_t ml_tsc_per_ms = 2000000;   // fallback ~2 GHz if calibration fails\n\nstatic void ml_calibrate_tsc() {\n    uint8_t g = ml_inb(0x61);\n    ml_outb(0x61, g & 0xFC);                 // gate off, speaker off\n    ml_outb(0x43, 0xB0);                     // ch2, lo/hi byte, mode 0\n    ml_outb(0x42, 0x9C); ml_outb(0x42, 0x2E);// 0x2E9C = 11932 ticks \u2248 10 ms\n    ml_outb(0x61, (g & 0xFC) | 0x01);        // gate on: start counting\n    uint64_t t0 = ml_rdtsc();\n    uint32_t guard = 0;\n    bool ok = true;\n    while (!(ml_inb(0x61) & 0x20)) {         // OUT2 goes high at terminal count\n        if (++guard > 20000000u) { ok = false; break; }\n    }\n    uint64_t t1 = ml_rdtsc();\n    ml_outb(0x61, g);                        // restore port 0x61\n    uint64_t d = t1 - t0;\n    if (ok && d > 100000ull && d < 0xFFFFFFFFull) ml_tsc_per_ms = (uint32_t)d / 10;\n}\n```\n\n## Edit 2: replace everything from `// \u2500\u2500 Main loop \u2500\u2500` through the end of the `for (;;) { ... }` in `kernel_main`\n\n```cpp\n    // \u2500\u2500 Main loop \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n    ml_calibrate_tsc();\n\n    const uint64_t FRAME_TSC = (uint64_t)ml_tsc_per_ms * 16;    // ~60 Hz paint / tick cap\n    const uint64_t GUEST_TSC = (uint64_t)ml_tsc_per_ms * 6;     // max guest time per pass\n    const uint64_t CLOCK_TSC = (uint64_t)ml_tsc_per_ms * 1000;  // idle refresh (taskbar clock)\n\n    uint64_t last_paint = 0;\n    uint64_t last_tick  = ml_rdtsc();\n    uint64_t last_clock = last_tick;\n\n    // Input state: edges/motion are accumulated by poll_and_latch() no matter\n    // WHERE polling happens (main loop or inside the guest slice), then\n    // consumed once per pass, so nothing is lost while a guest is running.\n    bool prev_left = mouse_left_down, prev_right = mouse_right_down;\n    int  seen_x = mouse_x, seen_y = mouse_y;\n    bool acc_lclick = false, acc_rclick = false, acc_moved = false;\n\n    // Where the cursor glyph currently sits on the live framebuffer.\n    int  drawn_x = -1, drawn_y = -1;\n\n    auto poll_and_latch = [&]() {\n        poll_input_universal();\n        if (mouse_left_down  && !prev_left)  { acc_lclick = true; g_gfx_click_left_pending  = true; }\n        if (mouse_right_down && !prev_right) { acc_rclick = true; g_gfx_click_right_pending = true; }\n        prev_left  = mouse_left_down;\n        prev_right = mouse_right_down;\n        if (mouse_x != seen_x || mouse_y != seen_y) {\n            acc_moved = true; seen_x = mouse_x; seen_y = mouse_y;\n        }\n    };\n\n    // Cheap cursor-only update straight on the framebuffer.\n    auto fast_cursor = [&]() {\n        if (g_backbuffer_is_clean_on_screen && !mouse_left_down && !mouse_right_down &&\n            (mouse_x != drawn_x || mouse_y != drawn_y)) {\n            erase_cursor_from_screen();\n            draw_cursor_to_screen(mouse_x, mouse_y, ColorPalette::CURSOR_WHITE);\n            drawn_x = mouse_x; drawn_y = mouse_y;\n        }\n    };\n\n    // A guest is \"runnable\" if it's alive and not parked waiting on stdin.\n    auto any_runnable = [&]() -> bool {\n        for (int i = 0; i < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES; ++i) {\n            ElfProcess& p = elf_processes[i];\n            if (p.active && !p.completed && !(p.waiting_for_input && in_empty(i))) return true;\n        }\n        return false;\n    };\n\n    g_evt_timer = true;\n    g_evt_dirty = true;\n\n    volatile uint16_t* vga_hb = (volatile uint16_t*)(0xB8000 + 2*79);\n    uint32_t hb_counter = 0;\n    const char hb_chars[] = \"|/-\\\\\";\n\n    for (;;) {\n        if ((++hb_counter & 0x3FFF) == 0)\n            *vga_hb = (uint16_t)(0x0A00u | (uint8_t)hb_chars[(hb_counter >> 14) & 3]);\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 1. Input (edges + motion accumulated, then consumed) \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n        poll_and_latch();\n        bool leftClickedThisFrame  = acc_lclick;\n        bool rightClickedThisFrame = acc_rclick;\n        bool mouse_moved           = acc_moved;\n        acc_lclick = acc_rclick = acc_moved = false;\n        bool key_pressed = (last_key_press != 0);\n\n        bool input_needs_full_repaint = key_pressed || leftClickedThisFrame ||\n                                        rightClickedThisFrame || mouse_left_down ||\n                                        mouse_right_down;\n\n        if (key_pressed || mouse_moved || leftClickedThisFrame || rightClickedThisFrame) {\n            g_evt_input = true;\n            if (input_needs_full_repaint) g_input_state.hasNewInput = true;\n        }\n\n        // Route keypress to the FOCUSED window's ELF slot only (unchanged).\n        if (last_key_press != 0) {\n            int fs = wm.get_focused_elf_slot();\n            if (fs >= 0 && fs < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES &&\n                elf_processes[fs].active && elf_processes[fs].waiting_for_input) {\n                push_input(fs, last_key_press);\n                elf_processes[fs].waiting_for_input = false;\n                last_key_press = 0;\n            }\n        }\n\n        bool handled_input = false;\n        if (g_evt_input) {\n            g_evt_input = false;\n            handled_input = true;\n            wm.handle_input(last_key_press, mouse_x, mouse_y,\n                            mouse_left_down,\n                            leftClickedThisFrame,\n                            rightClickedThisFrame);\n            if (last_key_press != 0) last_key_press = 0;\n            if (input_needs_full_repaint) g_evt_dirty = true;\n        }\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 2. Real-time timebase (replaces the 500-iteration software timer) \u2500\n        uint64_t now = ml_rdtsc();\n        bool frame_due = false;\n        if (now - last_tick >= FRAME_TSC) {\n            last_tick = now;                 // = now, not += : no catch-up bursts\n            g_timer_ticks++;\n            g_evt_timer = true;\n            frame_due = true;\n        }\n        if (now - last_clock >= CLOCK_TSC) { // idle refresh: clock only, ~1 Hz\n            last_clock = now;\n            g_evt_dirty = true;\n        }\n\n        if (handled_input || frame_due) wm.cleanup_closed_windows();\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 3. Guest slice: time-boxed, polls input + moves cursor between ticks\n        bool ran_guest = false;\n        if (any_runnable()) {\n            ran_guest = true;\n            uint64_t slice_start = now;\n            do {\n                tick_elf_processes(1);\n                poll_and_latch();\n                fast_cursor();\n            } while (ml_rdtsc() - slice_start < GUEST_TSC && any_runnable());\n            if (frame_due) g_evt_dirty = true;   // guest may have redrawn its canvas\n        }\n        if (frame_due) g_evt_timer = false;\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 4. Paint: only when dirty, at most once per FRAME_TSC \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n        now = ml_rdtsc();\n        bool want_paint = g_evt_dirty || g_input_state.hasNewInput;\n        if (want_paint && (now - last_paint) >= FRAME_TSC) {\n            g_evt_dirty               = false;\n            g_input_state.hasNewInput = false;\n            g_gfx.clear_screen(ColorPalette::DESKTOP_GRAY);\n            wm.update_all();\n            draw_vga_overlay();\n            swap_buffers();\n            erase_cursor_from_screen();\n            draw_cursor_to_screen(mouse_x, mouse_y, ColorPalette::CURSOR_WHITE);\n            drawn_x = mouse_x; drawn_y = mouse_y;\n            g_backbuffer_is_clean_on_screen = true;\n            last_paint = ml_rdtsc();\n        } else {\n            // Nothing to paint (or paint deferred to hold 60 Hz): keep the\n            // pointer responsive with the cheap path.\n            fast_cursor();\n            if (!want_paint && !ran_guest && !handled_input)\n                asm volatile(\"pause\");   // idle: be kind to the (virtual) CPU\n        }\n    }\n```\n\n## What changed\n\n- **Real-time pacing.** A TSC clock calibrated from PIT channel 2 replaces the iteration counter. Paints are capped at about 60 Hz, and a deferred paint keeps its dirty flag and happens next frame. The desktop repaints about once a second when idle (clock only), and only at frame rate while a guest is running.\n- **No lost input.** `poll_and_latch()` is used everywhere, so clicks and motion that arrive during a guest tick still reach `handle_input()` and the guest mouse ABI latches.\n- **Guest slice.** A running guest now gets up to 6 ms per pass instead of waiting on the slow timer, with input polled and the cursor moved between ticks. Guests that are waiting for stdin no longer keep the loop busy.\n- **Less per-iteration work.** `cleanup_closed_windows()` only runs after input or on a frame tick, and the heartbeat uses a shift instead of modulo.\n- **No `hlt`.** Without a working timer IRQ, `hlt` would freeze the machine, so idle uses `pause` instead.\n\n## Things to check when you build it\n\n- **`g_timer_ticks` rate.** It now advances at about 60 Hz instead of once per 500 iterations. Grep for its other users (the taskbar clock, for example) in case any of them assumed the old rate.\n- **VMware and real hardware.** Calibration has a timeout and falls back to an assumed 2 GHz TSC. If the PIT ch.2 gate isn't emulated, pacing will run slightly fast or slow but won't hang.\n- **Remaining costs in other files.** These are out of scope here but are the next things to fix:\n  - Each repaint still clears the whole screen and redraws every window (`update_all`). Per-window dirty rects in `04_window_system.h` and `10_window_manager_impl.h` would help.\n  - `swap_buffers()` copies the full frame, so copying only changed rows would help.\n  - `draw_vga_overlay()` reads legacy VGA memory on every paint. That's slow on real hardware, so gate it behind a debug flag.\n  - The boot-time `for (volatile ... 5000000)` spin only affects startup.\n\nIf you send `04_window_system.h` or `10_window_manager_impl.h`, I can add dirty-rect redraw so full repaints stop being needed for most changes.", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-28T08:50:46.415353Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-28T08:50:46.415353Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0e734-3524-71ad-afd4-eeb4c03453ad"}, {"uuid": "01a0e736-45be-7f61-80af-bc20435d3977", "text": "show the entire code", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:51:30.544302Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:51:30.544302Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "show the entire code", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-28T08:51:30.544542Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-28T08:51:30.544542Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0e734-3524-726e-85eb-ef7732b6909a"}, {"uuid": "01a0e736-45be-7d66-a309-9fd134a16390", "text": "\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nThe full `11_kernel_main.h` is in the file card below. It's a drop-in replacement for `kernel_parts/11_kernel_main.h`. I haven't compiled it against your tree.\n\nOnly two places differ from your original:\n\n1. **New pacing helpers** just above `kernel_main`: `ml_rdtsc`, `ml_outb`, `ml_inb`, `ml_calibrate_tsc`.\n2. **The main loop** in `kernel_main`, from `// \u2500\u2500 Main loop \u2500\u2500` to the end of the `for (;;)`.\n\nEverything else is unchanged, including the ELF loader, `x86_tick`, `tick_elf_processes`, the framebuffer probing, and the TCC bridges. I left the existing `ELFHEAPSIZE` versus `ELF_HEAP_SIZE` mismatch in `x86_tick` alone, since it's outside the loop. If it compiles now, both names are probably defined, but it's worth confirming.\n\nTwo things to check after building:\n- `g_timer_ticks` now advances at about 60 Hz instead of once per 500 loop iterations. Grep for its other uses (the taskbar clock, for example) in case any of them assumed the old rate.\n- If TSC calibration fails on some VM, it falls back to an assumed 2 GHz. Pacing would then be a bit off but won't hang.", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:51:30.538441Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:51:33.317163Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Preparing to output the full updated file."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:51:33.317249Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:53:27.267272Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01GV7WgFnd8of3rT7qXacS2q", "name": "create_file", "input": {"description": "Full 11_kernel_main.h with the lag-free main loop and pacing helpers applied", "path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/11_kernel_main.h", "file_text": "#pragma once\n// 11_kernel_main.h\n// Hardware bring-up, the framebuffer probe, and kernel_main() --\n// the kernel's actual entry point.\n// Extracted from kernel.cpp (original lines 9360-10540) as part of\n// splitting the monolithic kernel into per-component files. Order matters:\n// this file relies on declarations from the kernel_parts files included\n// before it in kernel.cpp, and is itself included there in sequence --\n// it is NOT a standalone/independently-compilable translation unit.\n\n\n// =============================================================================\n// KERNEL MAIN - ATOMIC FRAME RENDERING\n// =============================================================================\n\n\n\nstruct BochsCPURegs {\n    uint32_t eax, ebx, ecx, edx, esi, edi, esp, ebp;\n};\n\n// Forward decls for I/O callback adapters defined just below.\nstatic int  elf_io_read (int slot);\nstatic void elf_io_write(int slot, char c);\nstatic void elf_io_exit (int slot, int code);\n\n// Replace init_elf_system() call in kernel_main with:\nvoid init_elf_system() {\n    for (auto& p : elf_processes) {\n        p.active          = false;\n        p.cpu_initialized = false;\n        p.in_head  = p.in_tail  = 0;\n        p.out_head = p.out_tail = 0;\n    }\n    // Register IO callbacks for every slot. These are what make\n    // port-0xE9 writes from the guest reach the terminal: the chain is\n    //   guest: out 0xE9, al\n    //   -> bx_devices_c::outp (bochs_infra.cpp)\n    //   -> bochs_guest_putc   (bochs_glue.cpp)\n    //   -> write_cb = elf_io_write\n    //   -> push_output(slot, c)\n    //   -> tick_elf_processes drains it to terminal->console_print\n    for (int s = 0; s < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES; ++s) {\n        bochs_register_io_callbacks(s, elf_io_read, elf_io_write, elf_io_exit);\n    }\n    // NOTE: bochs_cpu_init() is NOT called here.\n    //\n    // We tried calling bochs_cpu_prewarm() (a guarded BX_CPU::initialize)\n    // here to surface init-time bugs early, but it triggered the VMware\n    // BAR1-unmap path documented at the top of vmware_svga_init(), causing\n    // VMware to die with \"execute an invalid part of memory\". The early\n    // call was unnecessary anyway: the host IDT installed in boot.S means\n    // a host fault inside BX_CPU::initialize() during lazy init now\n    // produces a visible \"!XX\" breadcrumb at VGA row 1 instead of a\n    // silent triple fault.\n    //\n    // Init runs lazily on the first ELF launch via x86_tick. The\n    // bochs_cpu_init() guard added in bochs_glue.cpp ensures\n    // BX_CPU::initialize() runs at most once per boot regardless of how\n    // many times the kernel calls bochs_cpu_init().\n}\n// I/O callback adapters\nstatic int  elf_io_read (int slot) { return pop_input(slot); }\nstatic void elf_io_write(int slot, char c) { push_output(slot, c); }\nstatic void elf_io_exit (int slot, int code) {\n    if (slot >= 0 && slot < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES) {\n        // DIAGNOSTIC: report the exit code (now the real fault vector\n        // number, per the per-vector stub change in inject_slab_tables).\n        // The buffer is zero-initialized this time, unlike the old\n        // disabled version this comment used to warn about.\n        char buf[64] = {0};\n        snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), \"\\n[guest exit, code=%d]\\n\", code);\n        console_print(buf);\n\n        elf_processes[slot].completed = true;\n        elf_processes[slot].active    = false;\n    }\n}static void dbg(const char* s) { /* write to VGA or terminal */ }\n// Minimal ELF execution path for Bochs-backed processes.\n// Assumes your existing kernel includes/types/helpers are already present.\n\n\nstatic inline unsigned int align_down(unsigned int v, unsigned int a) {\n    return v & ~(a - 1);\n}\n\nstatic inline unsigned int align_up(unsigned int v, unsigned int a) {\n    return (v + a - 1) & ~(a - 1);\n}\n\nstatic bool load_elf_image_to_slab(\n    int slot,\n    const unsigned char* elf,\n    unsigned int elf_size,\n    unsigned int& entry_out)\n{\n    if (elf_size < 52) return false;\n    if (!(elf[0] == 0x7f && elf[1] == 'E' && elf[2] == 'L' && elf[3] == 'F')) return false;\n    if (elf[4] != 1 || elf[5] != 1) return false;\n\n    auto rd16 = [&](unsigned int off) -> unsigned short {\n        return (unsigned short)(elf[off] | (elf[off + 1] << 8));\n    };\n    auto rd32 = [&](unsigned int off) -> unsigned int {\n        return (unsigned int)(elf[off] |\n                              (elf[off + 1] << 8) |\n                              (elf[off + 2] << 16) |\n                              (elf[off + 3] << 24));\n    };\n\n    unsigned short phoff = rd32(28);\n    unsigned short phentsize = rd16(42);\n    unsigned short phnum = rd16(44);\n    unsigned int entry = rd32(24);\n\n    if (phoff == 0 || phentsize < 32 || phnum == 0) return false;\n\n    unsigned int min_vaddr = 0xFFFFFFFFu;\n    unsigned int max_vaddr = 0;\n\n    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < phnum; ++i) {\n        unsigned int p = phoff + i * phentsize;\n        if (p + 32 > elf_size) return false;\n\n        unsigned int p_type = rd32(p + 0);\n        if (p_type != 1) continue; // PT_LOAD\n\n        unsigned int p_vaddr = rd32(p + 8);\n        unsigned int p_memsz = rd32(p + 20);\n\n        if (p_memsz == 0) continue;\n\n        if (p_vaddr < min_vaddr) min_vaddr = p_vaddr;\n        if (p_vaddr + p_memsz > max_vaddr) max_vaddr = p_vaddr + p_memsz;\n    }\n\n    if (min_vaddr == 0xFFFFFFFFu || max_vaddr <= min_vaddr) return false;\n\n    unsigned int vaddr_base = align_down(min_vaddr, 0x1000);\n    unsigned int vaddr_top = align_up(max_vaddr + ELF_STACK_SIZE + ELF_HEAP_SIZE, 0x1000);\n    unsigned int slab_size = vaddr_top - vaddr_base;\n\n    unsigned char* slab = elf_alloc_bytes(slab_size);\n    if (!slab) return false;\n    memset(slab, 0, slab_size);\n\n    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < phnum; ++i) {\n        unsigned int p = phoff + i * phentsize;\n        if (p + 32 > elf_size) continue;\n\n        unsigned int p_type = rd32(p + 0);\n        if (p_type != 1) continue;\n\n        unsigned int p_offset = rd32(p + 4);\n        unsigned int p_vaddr  = rd32(p + 8);\n        unsigned int p_filesz  = rd32(p + 16);\n        unsigned int p_memsz   = rd32(p + 20);\n\n        if (p_offset + p_filesz > elf_size) {\n            elf_free_bytes(slab);\n            return false;\n        }\n\n        unsigned int dest = p_vaddr - vaddr_base;\n        if (dest + p_memsz > slab_size) {\n            elf_free_bytes(slab);\n            return false;\n        }\n\n        memcpy(slab + dest, elf + p_offset, p_filesz);\n        if (p_memsz > p_filesz) {\n            memset(slab + dest + p_filesz, 0, p_memsz - p_filesz);\n        }\n    }\n\n    entry_out = entry;\n    bochs_activate_slot(slot);\n    bochs_set_process_memory(slab, slab_size, vaddr_base);\n    bochs_finalize_process_memory();\n\n    // Populate ElfProcess so that start_elf_process (and kill_elf_process)\n    // can track and free this slab correctly.\n    ElfProcess& proc = elf_processes[slot];\n    proc.memory_base = slab;\n    proc.memory_size = slab_size;\n    proc.vaddr_base  = vaddr_base;\n    proc.vaddr_end   = vaddr_top;\n\n    return true;\n}\n\nstatic bool start_elf_process(int slot, const unsigned char* elf, unsigned int elf_size) {\n    ElfProcess& proc = elf_processes[slot];\n    unsigned int entry = 0;\n\n    // load_elf_image_to_slab calls bochs_activate_slot + bochs_set_process_memory\n    // and populates proc.memory_base / proc.memory_size / proc.vaddr_base.\n    if (!load_elf_image_to_slab(slot, elf, elf_size, entry)) return false;\n\n    proc.active = true;\n    proc.completed = false;\n    proc.entry_point = entry;\n\n    // vaddr_base was set by load_elf_image_to_slab; don't clobber it with a\n    // page-aligned guess derived from the entry point (those can differ for\n    // PIE or non-zero-based binaries).\n    unsigned int stack_top = proc.vaddr_base + proc.memory_size;\n    proc.esp = stack_top - 16;\n    proc.brk_addr = proc.vaddr_base + proc.memory_size - ELF_HEAP_SIZE;\n\n    // Finish CPU wiring: init once, then point at entry.\n    bochs_cpu_init();\n    bochs_cpu_set_esp(proc.esp);\n    bochs_cpu_set_eip(proc.entry_point);\n    bochs_set_brk(slot, proc.brk_addr);\n\n    // Mark cpu_initialized so x86_tick skips the lazy-init path and goes\n    // straight to bochs_cpu_tick (we've done the full setup just above).\n    proc.cpu_initialized = true;\n\n    return true;\n}\n\nextern \"C\" volatile unsigned char bx_panic_breadcrumbs[64];\n// \u2500\u2500 Diagnostic breadcrumb at VGA row 2 \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n// Each x86_tick lazy-init step writes a tag char to row 2 columns 0..N.\n// Tags are:\n//   col 0: 'L'  \u2014 entered lazy init\n//   col 1: 'M'  \u2014 set_process_memory returned\n//   col 2: 'I'  \u2014 bochs_cpu_init returned\n//   col 3: 'S'  \u2014 set_esp returned\n//   col 4: 'E'  \u2014 set_eip returned\n//   col 5: 'B'  \u2014 set_brk returned\n//   col 6: 'T'  \u2014 about to call bochs_cpu_tick\n//   col 7: 't'  \u2014 bochs_cpu_tick returned\n// If lazy init wedges, the last tag visible tells you which call did it.\n//\n// We write to BOTH the VGA text-mode plane (forensic for pmemsave) AND\n// directly to the live framebuffer (visible to the user even if the\n// kernel never reaches swap_buffers again). Direct framebuffer writes\n// bypass the back buffer and use fb_info.ptr; that means they survive\n// a hang inside x86_tick that prevents the next paint cycle.\nstatic inline void x86_breadcrumb(int col, char c) {\n    if (col < 0 || col >= 80) return;\n\n    // 1. VGA text-mode plane (forensic).\n    {\n        volatile unsigned short* p =\n            (volatile unsigned short*)(0xB8000 + 2 * 80 + 2 * col);\n        *p = (unsigned short)(0x0E00u | (unsigned char)c);\n    }\n\n    // 2. Live framebuffer (visible). 8x8 yellow glyph at row 2 of overlay.\n    if (!fb_info.ptr) return;\n    if ((unsigned char)c > 127) return;\n    const uint8_t* glyph = font + (int)c * 8;\n    int x0 = col * 8;\n    int y0 = 16;          // row 2 of overlay (rows 0,1,2 each 8px tall)\n    if (x0 + 8 > (int)fb_info.width)  return;\n    if (y0 + 8 > (int)fb_info.height) return;\n\n    uint32_t color = 0xFFFF55u;   // bright yellow\n    uint32_t bg    = 0x000000u;\n    for (int yy = 0; yy < 8; ++yy) {\n        uint32_t* row = &fb_info.ptr[(y0 + yy) * (fb_info.pitch / 4) + x0];\n        uint8_t bits = glyph[yy];\n        for (int xx = 0; xx < 8; ++xx) {\n            row[xx] = (bits & (0x80 >> xx)) ? color : bg;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstatic bool x86_tick(int slot, int steps) {\n    ElfProcess& proc = elf_processes[slot];\n    // Tick a process that is alive: active and NOT yet completed.\n    // The previous form (`!proc.active || !proc.completed`) was inverted \u2014\n    // it returned for every live process, so the guest never advanced and\n    // its port-0xE9 output never reached the terminal display.\n    if (!proc.active || proc.completed) return false;\n\n    if (!proc.cpu_initialized) {\n        if (!proc.memory_base || proc.memory_size == 0) {\n            proc.active    = false;\n            proc.completed = true;\n            return false;\n        }\n        // Correct init order:\n        //   1. bochs_cpu_init()          -- global Bochs one-time init (idempotent)\n        //   2. bochs_activate_slot()     -- select which slot g_active_slot points at\n        //   3. bochs_set_process_memory  -- resets CPU to clean state, injects GDT/IDT/\n        //                                   stub tables, then restores to protected mode\n        //   4. set_esp / set_eip         -- point the protected-mode CPU at guest entry\n        //\n        // The previous order was: activate \u2192 set_memory \u2192 cpu_init.\n        // bochs_set_process_memory does BX_CPU::reset() + slot_restore_cpu() to\n        // arrive at protected mode. bochs_cpu_init() called *after* does another\n        // BX_CPU::reset() \u2014 wiping that protected mode state back to real mode.\n        // The guest then runs (or tries to) in real mode at a 32-bit EIP, which\n        // either executes garbage or hangs inside cpu_loop with no visible output.\n        x86_breadcrumb(0, 'L');\n        bochs_cpu_init();\n        x86_breadcrumb(1, 'I');\n        bochs_activate_slot(slot);\n        bochs_set_process_memory(proc.memory_base, proc.memory_size,\n                                 proc.vaddr_base);\n        x86_breadcrumb(2, 'M');\n        bochs_cpu_set_esp(proc.esp);\n        x86_breadcrumb(3, 'S');\n        bochs_cpu_set_eip(proc.entry_point);\n        x86_breadcrumb(4, 'E');\n        bochs_set_brk(slot, proc.vaddr_base + proc.memory_size - ELFHEAPSIZE);\n        x86_breadcrumb(5, 'B');\n        proc.cpu_initialized = true;\n    } else {\n        bochs_activate_slot(slot);\n    }\n\n    x86_breadcrumb(6, 'T');\n\n    // Diagnostic: snapshot EIP BEFORE tick so we can detect an unexpected\n    // jump back to entry after the tick. (Don't enable unconditionally \u2014\n    // gated on the very-recent-restart condition below so normal output\n    // isn't polluted.)\n    uint32_t eip_before = bochs_cpu_get_eip();\n\nbochs_cpu_tick(steps);\nx86_breadcrumb(7, 't');\n\nbool just_started_waiting =\n    bochs_process_wants_input(slot) && in_empty(slot);\n\nif (just_started_waiting) {\n    proc.waiting_for_input = true;\n    // The guest yielded mid-`IN` on port 0xE7. EIP isn't reliably\n    // retired across that abort, so don't trust it this tick \u2014\n    // skip the exit/EIP-range checks below entirely and pick back\n    // up cleanly next tick once real input has been pushed.\n    return true;\n}\n\n// \u2500\u2500\u2500 FIX: detect clean guest-exit signal \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nif (!proc.active || proc.completed) return false;\n\nunsigned int eip = bochs_cpu_get_eip();\nif (eip == 0 || eip < proc.vaddr_base || eip >= proc.vaddr_base + proc.memory_size) {\n    proc.completed = true;\n    proc.active    = false;\n    return false;\n}\n\nreturn true;\n\n    return true;\n}\n// And definition without default:\nvoid tick_elf_processes(int steps) {\n    bool any_exited_this_frame = false;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES; ++i) {\n        ElfProcess& proc = elf_processes[i];\n        // Drain output and step a process that is alive: active and not\n        // completed. Inverting this gate (the old `!active || !completed`)\n        // skipped every live process, leaving the terminal display silent\n        // even though the Bochs guest had written bytes to port 0xE9.\n        if (!proc.active || proc.completed) continue;\n\n        while (!out_empty(i)) {\n            char tmp[256];\n            int n = 0;\n            while (!out_empty(i) && n < 255) tmp[n++] = pop_output(i);\n            tmp[n] = 0;\n            if (proc.terminal && n) {\n                proc.terminal->console_print(tmp);\n                // Guest produced output. Flag the frame dirty so the main\n                // loop actually repaints. Without this the repaint is\n                // gated on g_evt_dirty / hasNewInput \u2014 both only set by\n                // user input \u2014 so guest output sat invisibly in the\n                // terminal buffer until the next keypress (\"need to press\n                // enter to print the last lot\").\n                g_evt_dirty = true;\n            }\n        }\n\n        if (proc.waiting_for_input && in_empty(i)) continue;\n\n        bool running = x86_tick(i, steps);\n\n        while (!out_empty(i)) {\n            char tmp[256];\n            int n = 0;\n            while (!out_empty(i) && n < 255) tmp[n++] = pop_output(i);\n            tmp[n] = 0;\n            if (proc.terminal && n) {\n                proc.terminal->console_print(tmp);\n                g_evt_dirty = true;   // see note above\n            }\n        }\n\n        if (!running) {\n            proc.active = false;\n            proc.completed = true;\n            if (proc.terminal) proc.terminal->captured_elf_slot = -1;\n            // A process just finished \u2014 the prompt needs to come back and\n            // any final output needs to show. Repaint this frame.\n            g_evt_dirty = true;\n\n            // \u2500\u2500 Per-slot kernel-side teardown \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n            // Free the slab and stack we allocated in load_and_execute_elf,\n            // and clear cpu_initialized so the NEXT process that lands in\n            // this slot re-runs the lazy-init path from scratch.\n            //\n            // We do NOT touch the Bochs glue here. The glue side gets a\n            // single heavy `bochs_reset_all_slots()` call at the end of\n            // this function (see below) \u2014 that wipes every per-slot\n            // mapping AND hardware-resets BX_CPU(0), so launch N starts\n            // from the same state as launch 1. Trying to keep the glue\n            // \"incrementally consistent\" with surgical per-slot updates\n            // had a long history of subtle bugs (the second `bochs hello`\n            // looping \"HELLO WOHELLO WO...\" because some untracked CPU\n            // field survived across slot reuse). The big-hammer reset\n            // is cheap and unambiguous.\n            //\n            // EXCEPTION (added for concurrent processes): if any OTHER\n            // slot is still active, bochs_reset_all_slots() below will\n            // be SKIPPED (resetting the live CPU would clobber that\n            // peer's mid-execution state). In that case we still need\n            // to clear THIS slot's mapping and dangling mem_base pointer\n            // before freeing the slab \u2014 otherwise the glue carries\n            // forward a dangling pointer that could be dereferenced on\n            // a future activate_slot. bochs_release_slot does that\n            // surgically without touching peer slots or BX_CPU(0).\n            // It's safe to call unconditionally: when the all-slots\n            // reset path also fires below, release_slot's wipe is\n            // simply overwritten by the same values via reset_all_slots.\n            bochs_release_slot(i);\n            if (proc.memory_base) { elf_free_bytes(proc.memory_base); proc.memory_base = nullptr; }\n            if (proc.stack)       { elf_free_bytes(proc.stack);       proc.stack       = nullptr; }\n            proc.memory_size     = 0;\n            proc.cpu_initialized = false;\n\n            // \u2500\u2500 Wipe per-slot I/O ring buffers and transient flags \u2500\u2500\n            // Belt-and-braces companion to the scrub in\n            // load_and_execute_elf: cleared HERE on the exit edge so\n            // the slot is left in a strictly-empty state the instant\n            // the process becomes inactive. If any code path on the\n            // next frame reads from inbuf/outbuf before the next\n            // load_and_execute_elf runs (e.g. a stray\n            // tick_elf_processes pass on a slot whose `completed`\n            // flag flipped but whose `active` flag hasn't been\n            // re-set yet), it sees a clean empty queue rather than\n            // stale bytes from the run that just ended.\n            //\n            // Without this, the third in-place run of `hello` in the\n            // same emulator window produced \"HELLO WOHELLO WO...\"\n            // loops with leftover keystrokes from previous runs\n            // bleeding into the new guest's stdin and the previous\n            // guest's tail-end output bleeding into the new\n            // terminal display.\n            for (int _b = 0; _b < INBUFSIZE;  ++_b) proc.inbuf[_b]  = 0;\n            for (int _b = 0; _b < OUTBUFSIZE; ++_b) proc.outbuf[_b] = 0;\n            proc.in_head           = 0;\n            proc.in_tail           = 0;\n            proc.out_head          = 0;\n            proc.out_tail          = 0;\n            proc.waiting_for_input = false;\n            proc.exit_code         = 0;\n            proc.input_pos         = 0;\n\n            any_exited_this_frame = true;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // \u2500\u2500 Deferred glue-wide reset \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n    // If any process exited this frame AND no other Bochs-emulated\n    // process is still running, wipe Bochs's glue state back to its\n    // post-boot baseline. The next launch will then start from the\n    // same state as launch #1.\n    //\n    // CRITICAL: do NOT reset while another slot is still running.\n    // bochs_reset_all_slots() unmaps every slab and hardware-resets\n    // BX_CPU(0); doing that to a live slot drops its EIP/CRs/segments\n    // back to the post-reset state. On the very next frame, x86_tick\n    // would re-enter the lazy-init path and bochs_cpu_set_eip would\n    // restart the guest from _start. The visible symptom is a runaway\n    // window spamming \"HELLO WOHELLO WO...\" for as long as another\n    // window is finishing \u2014 exactly because every frame's exit on\n    // window B triggered a reset that restarted window A from the top.\n    if (any_exited_this_frame) {\n        // Only wipe the Bochs glue when ALL slots are done AND all\n        // output has been drained. Use two separate loops: the old\n        // single-loop version broke out early on the first active slot,\n        // so any_output_pending was never checked for remaining slots.\n        bool any_still_active   = false;\n        bool any_output_pending = false;\n        for (int j = 0; j < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES; ++j) {\n            if (elf_processes[j].active) { any_still_active = true; break; }\n        }\n        if (!any_still_active) {\n            for (int j = 0; j < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES; ++j) {\n                if (!out_empty(j)) { any_output_pending = true; break; }\n            }\n        }\n        if (!any_still_active && !any_output_pending) {\n            bochs_reset_all_slots();\n        }\n    }\n     \n}\n\nextern \"C\" void cmd_exec(const char* code_text) {\n    if (!code_text) return;\n    TCompiler C;\n    int ok = C.compile(code_text);\n    if (ok < 0) return;\n    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_EXEC_PROCESSES; i++) {\n        if (!exec_contexts[i].active) {\n            exec_contexts[i].active = true;\n            exec_contexts[i].exec_id = i;\n            exec_contexts[i].prog = C.pr;\n            const char* av[] = {\"exec\", nullptr};\n            exec_contexts[i].vm.start_execution(exec_contexts[i].prog,1,av,0,0,nullptr);\n            return;\n        }\n    }\n}\n// =============================================================================\n// Helper: write a short status string to VGA text mode row 1 (safe at any\n// point in kernel_main, before framebuffer is initialised).\n// =============================================================================\n// Write msg to VGA text row (0-based)\nstatic void vga_dbg_row(int row, const char* msg, uint8_t attr = 0x0F) {\n    volatile uint16_t* vga = (volatile uint16_t*)0xB8000 + row * 80;\n    int i = 0;\n    for (; i < 79 && msg[i]; i++)\n        vga[i] = (uint16_t)((uint16_t)(attr << 8) | (uint8_t)msg[i]);\n    for (; i < 79; i++)\n        vga[i] = (uint16_t)((uint16_t)(attr << 8) | ' ');\n}\n\nstatic void vga_status(const char* msg, uint8_t attr = 0x0F) {\n    vga_dbg_row(1, msg, attr);\n}\n\n// Write a 32-bit hex value into buf[11] and return it\nstatic char* hex32(uint32_t v, char* buf) {\n    const char* h = \"0123456789ABCDEF\";\n    buf[0]='0'; buf[1]='x';\n    for (int i = 7; i >= 0; i--) { buf[2+i] = h[v & 0xF]; v >>= 4; }\n    buf[10] = 0;\n    return buf;\n}\n\n// Concatenate up to 6 strings into dst[128]\nstatic char* vga_cat(char* dst, const char* a, const char* b=\"\",\n                      const char* c=\"\", const char* d=\"\") {\n    char* p = dst;\n    auto app = [&](const char* s){ while(*s && p < dst+127) *p++=*s++; };\n    app(a); app(b); app(c); app(d);\n    *p = 0; return dst;\n}\n\n// =============================================================================\n// Framebuffer probe \u2014 four strategies, always returns something safe.\n// =============================================================================\nstatic void probe_framebuffer(multiboot_info* mbi,\n                              uint32_t& fb_phys,\n                              uint32_t& fb_w,\n                              uint32_t& fb_h,\n                              uint32_t& fb_pitch)\n{\n    fb_phys  = 0;\n    fb_w     = 1024;\n    fb_h     = 768;\n    fb_pitch = 1024 * 4;\n\n    // Diagnostic: show multiboot flags and framebuffer fields on VGA rows 2-4\n    {\n        char dbuf[128]; char hb[11];\n        vga_dbg_row(2, vga_cat(dbuf, \"MB flags=\", hex32(mbi->flags, hb)), 0x0E);\n        uint32_t raw_fb = (uint32_t)(uintptr_t)mbi->framebuffer_addr;\n        char hb2[11];\n        vga_dbg_row(3, vga_cat(dbuf, \"FB addr=\", hex32(raw_fb, hb),\n                           \" type=\", hex32(mbi->framebuffer_type, hb2)), 0x0E);\n        vga_dbg_row(4, vga_cat(dbuf, \"FB w=\", hex32(mbi->framebuffer_width, hb),\n                           \" h=\", hex32(mbi->framebuffer_height, hb2)), 0x0E);\n    }\n\n    // Strategy 1: GRUB filled framebuffer fields (our boot.S requests this\n    // via MB_FLAGS bit 2).  This is the normal path on QEMU + GRUB 2.\n    // framebuffer_type: 0=indexed, 1=RGB/direct, 2=EGA text \u2014 only accept 1.\n    if (mbi->flags & (1u << 12)) {\n        uint32_t addr = (uint32_t)(uintptr_t)mbi->framebuffer_addr;\n        char dbuf[128]; char hb[11];\n        vga_dbg_row(5, vga_cat(dbuf, \"S1: addr=\", hex32(addr, hb),\n                           \" type=\", hex32(mbi->framebuffer_type, hb)), 0x0A);\n        // Accept type 1 (RGB direct) or type 0 (indexed/paletted reported by\n        // some VMware GRUB configs). Reject type 2 (EGA text mode).\n        if (addr >= 0x1000000u && mbi->framebuffer_type != 2) {\n            fb_phys  = addr;\n            fb_w     = mbi->framebuffer_width;\n            fb_h     = mbi->framebuffer_height;\n            fb_pitch = mbi->framebuffer_pitch;\n            if (fb_w  > 1024) { fb_w  = 1024; fb_pitch = 1024*4; }\n            if (fb_h  > 768)  { fb_h  = 768; }\n            vga_dbg_row(5, \"S1: SUCCESS - using GRUB framebuffer\", 0x0A);\n            return;\n        }\n        vga_dbg_row(5, \"S1: SKIPPED (bad addr or type!=1)\", 0x0E);\n    } else {\n        vga_dbg_row(5, \"S1: SKIPPED (bit12 not set in flags)\", 0x0E);\n    }\n\n    // Strategy 2: Bochs VBE ports (QEMU -vga std).\n    {\n        auto vbe_out = [](uint16_t idx, uint16_t val) {\n            asm volatile(\"outw %0,%1\" :: \"a\"(idx), \"d\"((uint16_t)0x01CE));\n            asm volatile(\"outw %0,%1\" :: \"a\"(val), \"d\"((uint16_t)0x01CF));\n        };\n        auto vbe_in = [](uint16_t idx) -> uint16_t {\n            uint16_t v;\n            asm volatile(\"outw %0,%1\" :: \"a\"(idx), \"d\"((uint16_t)0x01CE));\n            asm volatile(\"inw %1,%0\"  : \"=a\"(v)   : \"d\"((uint16_t)0x01CF));\n            return v;\n        };\n        vbe_out(0x04, 0x00);\n        uint16_t id = vbe_in(0x00);\n        if (id >= 0xB0C0) {\n            vbe_out(0x01, 1024);\n            vbe_out(0x02, 768);\n            vbe_out(0x03, 32);\n            vbe_out(0x05, 1024);\n            vbe_out(0x06, 768);\n            vbe_out(0x07, 0);\n            vbe_out(0x08, 0);\n            vbe_out(0x04, 0x41); // ENABLE | LFB_ENABLED\n            for (uint16_t bus = 0; bus < 8 && !fb_phys; bus++) {\n                for (uint8_t dev = 0; dev < 32 && !fb_phys; dev++) {\n                    uint32_t vd = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x00);\n                    if ((vd & 0xFFFF) == 0xFFFF) continue;\n                    bool is_bochs   = (vd == 0x11111234u);\n                    uint32_t cc     = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x08) >> 16;\n                    bool is_display = (cc == 0x0300 || cc == 0x0380);\n                    if (!is_bochs && !is_display) continue;\n                    for (int b = 0; b < 3 && !fb_phys; b++) {\n                        uint32_t bar = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x10 + b*4);\n                        if (bar & 1) continue;\n                        uint32_t addr = bar & 0xFFFFFFF0u;\n                        if (addr >= 0x1000000u) fb_phys = addr;\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            if (fb_phys) return;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Strategy 3: VMware SVGA II (vendor 0x15AD, device 0x0405).\n    // The SVGA II adapter uses an I/O BAR (BAR0) for its index/value register\n    // pair and a memory BAR (BAR1) for the linear framebuffer.  It must be\n    // programmed via I/O ports to set the resolution and enable the FB;\n    // simply reading BAR1 is not enough \u2014 the FB is not live until ENABLE=1.\n    //\n    // SVGA II register map (index written to io_base+0, value at io_base+1):\n    //   SVGA_REG_ID       = 0   write SVGA_MAGIC|2 to negotiate version\n    //   SVGA_REG_ENABLE   = 1   write 1 to enable SVGA mode\n    //   SVGA_REG_WIDTH    = 2\n    //   SVGA_REG_HEIGHT   = 3\n    //   SVGA_REG_BPP      = 7   (bits per pixel)\n    //   SVGA_REG_FB_START = 13  returns the physical FB base address\n    //   SVGA_REG_PITCH    = 24  returns bytes per scan line\n    {\n        // Locate the SVGA II PCI device\n        uint16_t svga_io = 0;\n        uint32_t svga_fb_bar = 0;\n        for (uint16_t bus = 0; bus < 8 && !svga_io; bus++) {\n            for (uint8_t dev = 0; dev < 32 && !svga_io; dev++) {\n                uint32_t vd = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x00);\n                // VMware vendor 0x15AD, SVGA II device 0x0405\n                if (vd != 0x040515ADu) continue;\n                // BAR0 = I/O port base (bit 0 set = I/O space)\n                uint32_t bar0 = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x10);\n                if (bar0 & 1) svga_io = (uint16_t)(bar0 & 0xFFFE);\n                // BAR1 = framebuffer memory base\n                svga_fb_bar = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x14) & 0xFFFFFFF0u;\n\n                // Enable PCI memory + I/O decode (command register, offset 4)\n                uint32_t cmd = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x04);\n                cmd |= 0x03; // I/O + Memory enable\n                // pci_write_config_dword not available, use outl directly\n                uint32_t addr_reg = 0x80000000u | ((uint32_t)bus << 16) |\n                                    ((uint32_t)dev << 11) | 0x04u;\n                outl(0xCF8, addr_reg);\n                outl(0xCFC, cmd);\n            }\n        }\n\n        {\n            char dbuf[128]; char hb[11]; char hb2[11];\n            vga_dbg_row(6, vga_cat(dbuf, \"S3: io=\", hex32(svga_io, hb),\n                               \" bar1=\", hex32(svga_fb_bar, hb2)), 0x0A);\n        }\n\n        if (svga_io) {\n            // Helper lambdas for SVGA II register access\n            auto svga_write = [&](uint32_t reg, uint32_t val) {\n                outl((uint16_t)(svga_io + 0), reg); // index port\n                outl((uint16_t)(svga_io + 4), val); // value port\n            };\n            auto svga_read = [&](uint32_t reg) -> uint32_t {\n                outl((uint16_t)(svga_io + 0), reg);\n                return inl((uint16_t)(svga_io + 4));\n            };\n\n            // Negotiate SVGA II version (SVGA_ID_2 = 0x90000002)\n            svga_write(0 /*SVGA_REG_ID*/, 0x90000002u);\n            uint32_t id = svga_read(0);\n            { char dbuf[128]; char hb[11];\n              vga_dbg_row(7, vga_cat(dbuf, \"S3: SVGA_ID=\", hex32(id, hb)), 0x0A); }\n\n            if (id == 0x90000002u) {\n                // Set 1024x768x32\n                svga_write(2 /*SVGA_REG_WIDTH*/,  1024);\n                svga_write(3 /*SVGA_REG_HEIGHT*/,  768);\n                svga_write(7 /*SVGA_REG_BITS_PER_PIXEL*/, 32);\n                svga_write(1 /*SVGA_REG_ENABLE*/,  1);\n\n                // Read back actual FB address and pitch\n                uint32_t reported_fb = svga_read(13 /*SVGA_REG_FB_START*/);\n                uint32_t pitch       = svga_read(24 /*SVGA_REG_BYTES_PER_LINE*/);\n\n                { char dbuf[128]; char hb[11]; char hb2[11];\n                  vga_dbg_row(8, vga_cat(dbuf, \"S3: fb=\", hex32(reported_fb, hb),\n                                     \" pitch=\", hex32(pitch, hb2)), 0x0A); }\n\n                // Use the reported address if valid, fall back to BAR1\n                fb_phys  = (reported_fb >= 0x1000000u) ? reported_fb : svga_fb_bar;\n                fb_w     = 1024;\n                fb_h     = 768;\n                fb_pitch = (pitch >= 1024*4) ? pitch : 1024*4;\n                if (fb_phys >= 0x1000000u) {\n                    vga_dbg_row(9, \"S3: SUCCESS - VMware SVGA II programmed\", 0x0A);\n                    return;\n                }\n                vga_dbg_row(9, \"S3: FAILED - fb addr still bad\", 0x4F);\n            } else {\n                vga_dbg_row(7, \"S3: FAILED - wrong SVGA_ID (not VMware?)\", 0x4F);\n            }\n        } else {\n            vga_dbg_row(6, \"S3: SKIPPED - no SVGA II device found on PCI\", 0x0E);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Strategy 4: hardcoded fallback.\n    // 0xFD000000 = Bochs/QEMU default.\n    // 0xE8000000 = VMware Workstation SVGA II BAR1 (confirmed from vmware.log).\n    // vmware_svga_init() runs before probe_framebuffer and will override this\n    // with the correct BAR1 address, so this fallback is only for QEMU/Bochs.\n    fb_phys = 0xFD000000u;\n}\n\n// =============================================================================\n// kernel_main\n// =============================================================================\n// \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n// VMware SVGA II initialisation.\n// Must run BEFORE any framebuffer access.  Returns the live FB base address,\n// or 0 if no SVGA II device is found.\n//\n// The SVGA II I/O port pair lives at BAR0 (an I/O BAR):\n//   index port = BAR0 + 0\n//   value port = BAR0 + 4        (NOT +1 \u2014 the value port is 32-bit wide)\n// Register indices used here:\n//   0  SVGA_REG_ID              write 0x90000002 to negotiate SVGA2\n//   1  SVGA_REG_ENABLE          write 1 to enable linear framebuffer\n//   2  SVGA_REG_WIDTH\n//   3  SVGA_REG_HEIGHT\n//   7  SVGA_REG_BITS_PER_PIXEL\n//  13  SVGA_REG_FB_START        read to get physical FB address\n//  24  SVGA_REG_BYTES_PER_LINE  read to get pitch in bytes\n// \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nstruct SVGAResult { uint32_t fb; uint32_t pitch; bool ok; };\n\n// \u2500\u2500 VMware SVGA II: full PCI scan + I/O programming \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n// Scans ALL 256 PCI buses (some VMware configs place SVGA on bus > 7),\n// all 32 devices, all 8 functions.  Tries both device IDs 0x0405 and 0x0710.\n// The I/O BAR may be at BAR0 or BAR2 depending on SVGA revision.\nstatic SVGAResult vmware_svga_init(uint32_t w, uint32_t h) {\n    SVGAResult r = {0, w*4, false};\n\n    uint16_t io   = 0;\n    uint32_t bar1 = 0;\n\n    // Full PCI scan \u2014 VMware may put the SVGA on any bus\n    for (uint32_t bus = 0; bus < 256 && !io; bus++) {\n        for (uint32_t dev = 0; dev < 32 && !io; dev++) {\n            for (uint32_t fn = 0; fn < 8 && !io; fn++) {\n                uint32_t id = pci_read_config_dword(\n                    (uint16_t)bus, (uint8_t)dev, (uint8_t)fn, 0x00);\n                if ((id & 0xFFFFu) != 0x15ADu) continue; // not VMware vendor\n                uint32_t did = (id >> 16) & 0xFFFFu;\n                if (did != 0x0405u && did != 0x0710u) continue; // not SVGA\n\n                // DO NOT touch the PCI command register.\n                // The BIOS has already enabled I/O + Memory decode for SVGA.\n                // Re-writing the command register causes VMware to briefly\n                // unmap BAR1 (0xe8000000), creating a window where pixel\n                // writes crash with \"execute an invalid part of memory\".\n\n                // Read all 6 BARs \u2014 find the I/O BAR and the memory BAR\n                for (int b = 0; b < 6; b++) {\n                    uint32_t bar = pci_read_config_dword(\n                        (uint16_t)bus, (uint8_t)dev, (uint8_t)fn,\n                        (uint8_t)(0x10 + b*4));\n                    if ((bar & 1u) && !io) {\n                        io = (uint16_t)(bar & 0xFFFCu); // I/O BAR\n                    } else if (!(bar & 1u) && !bar1) {\n                        uint32_t addr = bar & 0xFFFFFFF0u;\n                        if (addr >= 0x1000000u) bar1 = addr; // memory BAR\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Also try the fixed legacy SVGA I/O port (0x4560) used by very old VMware\n    if (!io) io = 0x4560u;\n\n    // I/O helpers\n    auto wr = [&](uint32_t reg, uint32_t val) {\n        outl(io,     reg);\n        outl(io + 4, val);\n    };\n    auto rd = [&](uint32_t reg) -> uint32_t {\n        outl(io, reg);\n        return inl(io + 4);\n    };\n\n    // Negotiate SVGA2 \u2014 try SVGA_ID_2, fall back to SVGA_ID_1\n    wr(0, 0x90000002u);\n    uint32_t svga_id = rd(0);\n    if (svga_id != 0x90000002u) {\n        wr(0, 0x90000001u);\n        svga_id = rd(0);\n        if (svga_id != 0x90000001u) return r; // not responding\n    }\n\n    // Program resolution\n    wr(2, w);\n    wr(3, h);\n    wr(7, 32);\n\n    // Enable\n    wr(1, 1);\n\n    // Read FB address and pitch.\n    // VMware Workstation 14+ returns 0 from SVGA_REG_FB_START (reg 13) \u2014\n    // the physical framebuffer is always at BAR1. Prefer BAR1 when valid.\n    uint32_t fb    = rd(13);\n    uint32_t pitch = rd(24);\n\n    if (bar1 >= 0x1000000u) fb = bar1;  // BAR1 is authoritative on VMware\n    else if (fb < 0x1000000u) fb = bar1;\n    if (pitch < w * 4) pitch = w * 4;\n\n    r.fb    = fb;\n    r.pitch = pitch;\n    r.ok    = (fb >= 0x1000000u);\n    return r;\n}\n\n\n// Sticky click-edge latches for the guest mouse ABI (bochs_drivers.h's\n// mouse_poll()). Set in the main loop below whenever mouse_left_down /\n// mouse_right_down transitions from up to down; cleared only once\n// kernel_gfx_mouse_poll() (defined further down, called from\n// bochs_glue.cpp's bochs_guest_mouse_poll()) has actually reported the\n// click to whichever guest program is the focused window's own ELF\n// process. File scope (not a kernel_main local) because\n// kernel_gfx_mouse_poll() needs to reach them from outside the loop.\nstatic bool g_gfx_click_left_pending  = false;\nstatic bool g_gfx_click_right_pending = false;\n\n// =============================================================================\n// LAG FIX: pacing helpers -- TSC time base calibrated against PIT channel 2\n// =============================================================================\n// The old main loop derived its \"timer\" from a raw iteration counter\n// (++poll_counter >= 500), so frame rate, repaint rate and guest CPU share\n// all changed with host speed. There is no usable IRQ0 here, so we build a\n// real-time base instead: calibrate the TSC once against PIT channel 2 (which\n// is read by POLLING port 0x61, no interrupt needed) and pace everything off\n// rdtsc afterwards.\n//\n// All 64-bit math below avoids division so a freestanding i386 build does not\n// need __udivdi3 from libgcc.\nstatic inline uint64_t ml_rdtsc() {\n    uint32_t lo, hi;\n    asm volatile(\"rdtsc\" : \"=a\"(lo), \"=d\"(hi));\n    return ((uint64_t)hi << 32) | lo;\n}\nstatic inline void    ml_outb(uint16_t p, uint8_t v) { asm volatile(\"outb %0,%1\" :: \"a\"(v), \"Nd\"(p)); }\nstatic inline uint8_t ml_inb (uint16_t p) { uint8_t v; asm volatile(\"inb %1,%0\" : \"=a\"(v) : \"Nd\"(p)); return v; }\n\nstatic uint32_t ml_tsc_per_ms = 2000000;   // fallback ~2 GHz if calibration fails\n\nstatic void ml_calibrate_tsc() {\n    uint8_t g = ml_inb(0x61);\n    ml_outb(0x61, g & 0xFC);                  // gate off, speaker off\n    ml_outb(0x43, 0xB0);                      // ch2, lo/hi byte access, mode 0\n    ml_outb(0x42, 0x9C); ml_outb(0x42, 0x2E); // 0x2E9C = 11932 ticks ~= 10 ms\n    ml_outb(0x61, (g & 0xFC) | 0x01);         // gate on: start counting\n    uint64_t t0 = ml_rdtsc();\n    uint32_t guard = 0;\n    bool ok = true;\n    while (!(ml_inb(0x61) & 0x20)) {          // OUT2 goes high at terminal count\n        if (++guard > 20000000u) { ok = false; break; }\n    }\n    uint64_t t1 = ml_rdtsc();\n    ml_outb(0x61, g);                         // restore port 0x61\n    uint64_t d = t1 - t0;\n    if (ok && d > 100000ull && d < 0xFFFFFFFFull) ml_tsc_per_ms = (uint32_t)d / 10;\n}\n\nextern \"C\" void kernel_main(uint32_t magic, uint32_t multiboot_addr) {\n\n    // \u2500\u2500 Verify Multiboot 1 magic FIRST, before any hardware probing \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n    // If GRUB (or whatever bootloader) didn't pass 0x2BADB002, we are running\n    // under something that doesn't honour the contract \u2014 bail out cleanly\n    // instead of poking PCI / FB hardware on bad assumptions.\n    if (magic != 0x2BADB002) {\n        volatile uint16_t* vga = (volatile uint16_t*)0xB8000;\n        vga[0] = 0x4F45; // 'E' on red \u2014 \"bad magic\"\n        for (;;)\n            asm volatile(\"hlt\");\n    }\n\n    // \u2500\u2500 Initialise heap \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n    g_allocator.init(kernel_heap, sizeof(kernel_heap));\n\n    // \u2500\u2500 Step 1: unconditionally try VMware SVGA II first. \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n    // This MUST happen before reading any framebuffer address because the\n    // linear FB is not live until ENABLE=1 is written.\n    SVGAResult svga = vmware_svga_init(1024, 768);\n\n    // \u2500\u2500 Step 2: determine framebuffer address \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n    multiboot_info* mbi = (multiboot_info*)multiboot_addr;\n\n    if (svga.ok) {\n        // VMware SVGA II programmed successfully \u2014 use its reported address.\n        // Wait for VMware to complete the MemSpace re-registration after ENABLE=1.\n        // The log shows VMware briefly unmaps/remaps 0xe8000000 during SVGA init;\n        // a short spin ensures the mapping is live before we write pixels.\n        for (volatile uint32_t i = 0; i < 5000000u; i++);\n\t\t\n        fb_info = { (uint32_t*)svga.fb, 1024, 768, svga.pitch };\n    } else {\n        // Not VMware (or SVGA II failed) \u2014 use GRUB multiboot info directly.\n        // Accept any type except 2 (EGA text). If bit 12 not set, fall back\n        // to probing Bochs VBE ports, then hardcoded candidates.\n        uint32_t fb_phys = 0, fb_w = 1024, fb_h = 768, fb_pitch = 1024*4;\n\n        if ((mbi->flags & (1u << 12)) && mbi->framebuffer_type != 2) {\n            // GRUB filled the framebuffer fields (because boot.S requested\n            // video mode via Multiboot1 FLAGS bit 2). Take its values\n            // verbatim; this is the normal path on real BIOS + VMware-BIOS\n            // and on UEFI through GRUB-EFI (which sets it up via GOP).\n            fb_phys  = (uint32_t)(uintptr_t)mbi->framebuffer_addr;\n            fb_w     = mbi->framebuffer_width  ? mbi->framebuffer_width  : 1024;\n            fb_h     = mbi->framebuffer_height ? mbi->framebuffer_height : 768;\n            fb_pitch = mbi->framebuffer_pitch  ? mbi->framebuffer_pitch  : fb_w*4;\n        }\n\n        if (fb_phys < 0x1000000u) {\n            // GRUB gave nothing useful \u2014 try Bochs VBE then hardcoded\n            probe_framebuffer(mbi, fb_phys, fb_w, fb_h, fb_pitch);\n        }\n\n        fb_info = { (uint32_t*)(uintptr_t)fb_phys, fb_w, fb_h, fb_pitch };\n    }\n\n    if (fb_info.width  == 0 || fb_info.width  > 1920) fb_info.width  = 1024;\n    if (fb_info.height == 0 || fb_info.height > 1200) fb_info.height = 768;\n    if (fb_info.pitch  < fb_info.width * 4) fb_info.pitch = fb_info.width * 4;\n\n    // Real-hardware-only perf fix: mark the linear framebuffer's physical\n    // range Write-Combining via MTRR if at all possible (see the big\n    // comment above setup_framebuffer_write_combining() in\n    // 02_boot_info_and_graphics_driver.h for why this matters -- in short,\n    // it's what makes every-frame swap_buffers() blits, and therefore\n    // mouse tracking, not feel sluggish on real silicon). No-op under\n    // VMware/emulation, where this was never the bottleneck.\n    const char* g_fb_wc_status = setup_framebuffer_write_combining();\n\n    // \u2500\u2500 Step 3: commit and paint \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n    backbuffer = backbuffer_storage;\n    g_gfx.init(false);\n\n    g_gfx.clear_screen(ColorPalette::DESKTOP_GRAY );\n    swap_buffers();\n\n    // \u2500\u2500 Open first terminal window \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n    launch_new_terminal();\n\n    // Report whether the WC fix above actually took effect. This used to\n    // fail silently, so \"everything on real hardware is slow -- typing,\n    // opening windows, all of it\" had no visible cause: every one of\n    // those triggers a full swap_buffers() blit, and if the framebuffer\n    // is still stuck Uncacheable (no free MTRR slot is the common real-\n    // hardware case -- firmware often claims all of them), that blit\n    // stays 20-100x slower no matter what else gets optimized.\n    {\n        char wcmsg[128];\n        vga_cat(wcmsg, \"Framebuffer write-combining: \", g_fb_wc_status, \"\\n\");\n        wm.print_to_focused(wcmsg);\n    }\n\n    // \u2500\u2500 PS/2 mouse \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n    ps2_flush_output_buffer();\n    if (initialize_universal_mouse()) {\n        wm.print_to_focused(\"Mouse: initialised.\\n\");\n    } else {\n        wm.print_to_focused(\"Mouse: init failed (keyboard-only mode).\\n\");\n    }\n\n    // \u2500\u2500 AHCI disk + FAT32 \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n    disk_init();\n    // Don't hardcode a port selection here \u2014 disk_init() already\n    // auto-selected the first port with an attached device. Hardcoding\n    // \"1\" only worked on the QEMU command line in compile.md (which puts\n    // the hard disk on ahci.1); bare-metal and VMware almost always have\n    // the boot disk on port 0 and would have silently fallen back to no\n    // selection. Pass \"\" to just list the detected ports for the user.\n    cmd_list_and_select_disk(\"\");\n\n    if (ahci_base) {\n        fat32_init();\n        wm.print_to_focused(\"Disk: AHCI found.\\n\");\n    } else {\n        wm.print_to_focused(\"Disk: no AHCI controller.\\n\");\n    }\n    if (current_directory_cluster) {\n        wm.load_desktop_items();\n        if (extract_busybox_to_filesystem())\n            wm.print_to_focused(\"BusyBox: saved to FAT32.\\n\");\n        else\n            wm.print_to_focused(\"BusyBox: ramdisk empty or write failed.\\n\");\n        if (extract_hello_to_filesystem())\n            wm.print_to_focused(\"hello: saved to FAT32.\\n\");\n\n        // Write the TCC guest ABI header so user programs can #include \"tcc.h\"\n        // to get outb / kprint / kexit without redefining them.\n        {\n            fat_dir_entry_t tcc_hdr_entry;\n            uint32_t th_sec = 0, th_off = 0;\n            if (fat32_find_entry(\"tcc.h\", &tcc_hdr_entry, &th_sec, &th_off) != 0) {\n                static const char tcc_h[] =\n                    \"/* tcc.h \u2014 guest ABI for in-kernel TCC programs */\\n\"\n                    \"#ifndef TCC_H\\n#define TCC_H\\n\"\n                    \"static inline void outb(unsigned short p, unsigned char v) {\\n\"\n                    \"    __asm__ volatile(\\\"outb %0,%1\\\"::\\\"a\\\"(v),\\\"Nd\\\"(p)); }\\n\"\n                    \"static inline void kprint(const char* s) {\\n\"\n                    \"    while (*s) outb(0xE9, *s++); }\\n\"\n                    \"static inline void kexit(int c) {\\n\"\n                    \"    outb(0xE8, (unsigned char)c); }\\n\"\n                    \"#endif\\n\";\n                fat32_write_file(\"tcc.h\", tcc_h, (uint32_t)(sizeof(tcc_h) - 1));\n            }\n        }\n    } else {\n        wm.print_to_focused(\"FAT32: not initialised.\\n\");\n    }\n\n    // \u2500\u2500 Bochs CPU / ELF subsystem \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n    //\n    // Run the file-scope C++ constructors NOW, before init_elf_system()\n    // and before any code can reach a Bochs entry point. boot.S does not\n    // walk __init_array, so without this the Bochs core objects (bx_cpu,\n    // bx_mem, the CPUID param objects, icache's pageWriteStampTable, ...)\n    // stay as zero-filled BSS with null vtables. The first ELF launched\n    // in the Bochs emulator window would then call bochs_cpu_init() ->\n    // BX_CPU(0)->initialize() against those null vtables and fault out on\n    // its very first tick \u2014 the \"first-time crash / autoclose\" symptom.\n    kernel_run_global_ctors_once();\n\n    // The constructors have now run exactly once. Tell the test module so\n    // its own run_init_array_once() inside test_module_run() becomes a\n    // no-op \u2014 otherwise the first `test` command would re-run every ctor\n    // a second time, re-constructing bx_cpu / bx_mem on top of live\n    // (already-initialised) Bochs state.\n    test_module_mark_ctors_done();\n\n    init_elf_system();\n\n    vga_status(\"Init complete - entering main loop\", 0x0A);\n\n    // \u2500\u2500 Main loop \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n    // LAG FIX: fully rewritten. See the notes at each numbered stage.\n    //\n    // Root causes fixed here:\n    //  * Pacing came from a loop-iteration counter, not real time, so frame\n    //    rate / repaint rate / guest CPU share all varied with host speed and\n    //    the timer's g_evt_dirty forced full-desktop repaints far too often.\n    //  * Click edges and mouse motion seen by poll_input_universal() INSIDE\n    //    the guest sub-tick loop were never accumulated, so they were lost\n    //    (and prev_mouse_x/y were overwritten there) -> handle_input() was\n    //    skipped and drags / clicks stalled while a program ran.\n    //  * The guest only got CPU on the slow software timer.\n    //  * Nothing capped repaints (held buttons, chatty guest output).\n    //  * cleanup_closed_windows() ran on every spin of the loop.\n    ml_calibrate_tsc();\n\n    const uint64_t FRAME_TSC = (uint64_t)ml_tsc_per_ms * 16;    // ~60 Hz paint / tick cap\n    const uint64_t GUEST_TSC = (uint64_t)ml_tsc_per_ms * 6;     // max guest time per pass\n    const uint64_t CLOCK_TSC = (uint64_t)ml_tsc_per_ms * 1000;  // idle refresh (taskbar clock)\n\n    uint64_t last_paint = 0;\n    uint64_t last_tick  = ml_rdtsc();\n    uint64_t last_clock = last_tick;\n\n    // Input state: edges/motion are accumulated by poll_and_latch() no matter\n    // WHERE polling happens (main loop or inside the guest slice), then\n    // consumed once per pass, so nothing is lost while a guest is running.\n    bool prev_left = mouse_left_down, prev_right = mouse_right_down;\n    int  seen_x = mouse_x, seen_y = mouse_y;\n    bool acc_lclick = false, acc_rclick = false, acc_moved = false;\n\n    // Where the cursor glyph currently sits on the live framebuffer.\n    int  drawn_x = -1, drawn_y = -1;\n\n    auto poll_and_latch = [&]() {\n        poll_input_universal();\n        if (mouse_left_down  && !prev_left)  { acc_lclick = true; g_gfx_click_left_pending  = true; }\n        if (mouse_right_down && !prev_right) { acc_rclick = true; g_gfx_click_right_pending = true; }\n        prev_left  = mouse_left_down;\n        prev_right = mouse_right_down;\n        if (mouse_x != seen_x || mouse_y != seen_y) {\n            acc_moved = true; seen_x = mouse_x; seen_y = mouse_y;\n        }\n    };\n\n    // Cheap cursor-only update straight on the framebuffer.\n    auto fast_cursor = [&]() {\n        if (g_backbuffer_is_clean_on_screen && !mouse_left_down && !mouse_right_down &&\n            (mouse_x != drawn_x || mouse_y != drawn_y)) {\n            erase_cursor_from_screen();\n            draw_cursor_to_screen(mouse_x, mouse_y, ColorPalette::CURSOR_WHITE);\n            drawn_x = mouse_x; drawn_y = mouse_y;\n        }\n    };\n\n    // A guest is \"runnable\" if it's alive and not parked waiting on stdin.\n    auto any_runnable = [&]() -> bool {\n        for (int i = 0; i < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES; ++i) {\n            ElfProcess& p = elf_processes[i];\n            if (p.active && !p.completed && !(p.waiting_for_input && in_empty(i))) return true;\n        }\n        return false;\n    };\n\n    // Force an immediate first render.\n    g_evt_timer = true;\n    g_evt_dirty = true;\n\n    // Spinning heartbeat at VGA column 79, row 0 (green)\n    volatile uint16_t* vga_hb = (volatile uint16_t*)(0xB8000 + 2*79);\n    uint32_t hb_counter = 0;\n    const char hb_chars[] = \"|/-\\\\\";\n\n    for (;;) {\n        if ((++hb_counter & 0x3FFF) == 0)\n            *vga_hb = (uint16_t)(0x0A00u | (uint8_t)hb_chars[(hb_counter >> 14) & 3]);\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 1. Input (edges + motion accumulated, then consumed) \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n        poll_and_latch();\n        bool leftClickedThisFrame  = acc_lclick;\n        bool rightClickedThisFrame = acc_rclick;\n        bool mouse_moved           = acc_moved;\n        acc_lclick = acc_rclick = acc_moved = false;\n        bool key_pressed = (last_key_press != 0);\n\n        // Anything that can change what's on screen beyond the cursor's own\n        // position: a key, a fresh click edge, or a button being held\n        // (drag/resize/paint in progress). A plain hover-move is NOT included\n        // -- it takes the cursor-only fast path instead of a full repaint.\n        bool input_needs_full_repaint = key_pressed || leftClickedThisFrame ||\n                                        rightClickedThisFrame || mouse_left_down ||\n                                        mouse_right_down;\n\n        if (key_pressed || mouse_moved || leftClickedThisFrame || rightClickedThisFrame) {\n            g_evt_input = true;\n            if (input_needs_full_repaint) g_input_state.hasNewInput = true;\n        }\n\n        // Route keypress to the FOCUSED window's ELF slot only. If that slot\n        // isn't waiting for input, fall through to wm.handle_input, which\n        // threads it to whichever terminal's captured_elf_slot was clicked.\n        if (last_key_press != 0) {\n            int fs = wm.get_focused_elf_slot();\n            if (fs >= 0 && fs < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES &&\n                elf_processes[fs].active && elf_processes[fs].waiting_for_input) {\n                push_input(fs, last_key_press);\n                elf_processes[fs].waiting_for_input = false;\n                last_key_press = 0;\n            }\n        }\n\n        bool handled_input = false;\n        if (g_evt_input) {\n            g_evt_input = false;\n            handled_input = true;\n            wm.handle_input(last_key_press, mouse_x, mouse_y,\n                            mouse_left_down,\n                            leftClickedThisFrame,\n                            rightClickedThisFrame);\n            if (last_key_press != 0) last_key_press = 0;\n            if (input_needs_full_repaint) g_evt_dirty = true;\n        }\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 2. Real-time timebase (replaces the 500-iteration software timer) \u2500\n        uint64_t now = ml_rdtsc();\n        bool frame_due = false;\n        if (now - last_tick >= FRAME_TSC) {\n            last_tick = now;                 // = now, not += : no catch-up bursts\n            g_timer_ticks++;                 // now advances at ~60 Hz\n            g_evt_timer = true;\n            frame_due = true;\n        }\n        if (now - last_clock >= CLOCK_TSC) { // idle refresh: taskbar clock, ~1 Hz\n            last_clock = now;\n            g_evt_dirty = true;\n        }\n\n        if (handled_input || frame_due) wm.cleanup_closed_windows();\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 3. Guest slice: time-boxed; polls input + moves cursor between ticks\n        // Tick ELF processes BEFORE the paint so breadcrumbs they write are\n        // reflected in the next swap_buffers.\n        bool ran_guest = false;\n        if (any_runnable()) {\n            ran_guest = true;\n            uint64_t slice_start = now;\n            do {\n                tick_elf_processes(1);\n                poll_and_latch();\n                fast_cursor();\n            } while (ml_rdtsc() - slice_start < GUEST_TSC && any_runnable());\n            if (frame_due) g_evt_dirty = true;   // guest may have redrawn its canvas\n        }\n        if (frame_due) g_evt_timer = false;\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 4. Paint: only when dirty, at most once per FRAME_TSC \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n        // A deferred paint keeps its dirty flag and happens on a later pass.\n        now = ml_rdtsc();\n        bool want_paint = g_evt_dirty || g_input_state.hasNewInput;\n        if (want_paint && (now - last_paint) >= FRAME_TSC) {\n            g_evt_dirty               = false;\n            g_input_state.hasNewInput = false;\n            g_gfx.clear_screen(ColorPalette::DESKTOP_GRAY);\n            wm.update_all();\n            // Diagnostic overlay: paint VGA text-mode rows 0/1/2\n            // (boot/panic/tick breadcrumbs, host-IDT fault tags,\n            // x86_tick lazy-init progress) onto the framebuffer.\n            draw_vga_overlay();\n            swap_buffers();\n            // The pushed backbuffer has no cursor in it; draw the glyph\n            // straight onto the framebuffer so the next plain pointer move\n            // can skip the full repaint.\n            erase_cursor_from_screen(); // no-op the first time through\n            draw_cursor_to_screen(mouse_x, mouse_y, ColorPalette::CURSOR_WHITE);\n            drawn_x = mouse_x; drawn_y = mouse_y;\n            g_backbuffer_is_clean_on_screen = true;\n            last_paint = ml_rdtsc();\n        } else {\n            // Nothing to paint (or paint deferred to hold 60 Hz): keep the\n            // pointer responsive with the cheap path.\n            fast_cursor();\n            if (!want_paint && !ran_guest && !handled_input)\n                asm volatile(\"pause\");   // idle: be kind to the (virtual) CPU\n        }\n    }\n}\n// =============================================================================\n// extern \"C\" bridges for tcc_kernel.cpp\n// =============================================================================\n// tcc_kernel.cpp is compiled as freestanding C++ and cannot include the full\n// kernel headers. These thin wrappers expose the symbols it needs with plain\n// C linkage so the linker resolves them without name-mangling.\n\nextern \"C\" {\n\n// Bridge for bochs_glue.cpp's bochs_guest_mouse_poll() (guest port\n// 0xEF, see bochs_drivers.h's mouse_poll()). Reports the compositor's\n// cursor to ELF slot `slot` ONLY if that slot's window is BOTH the\n// currently focused window AND showing a live gfx canvas the cursor\n// happens to be over \u2014 everything else (titlebar drags, clicks on\n// other windows, the desktop, the taskbar) is handled entirely by\n// WindowManager::handle_input() above and never touches this path at\n// all, which is what keeps those clicks going to the compositor only.\n// On any \"no\" (wrong/no slot, not focused, no window, not in gfx\n// mode, cursor outside the canvas) this leaves *out_x/*out_y/\n// *out_buttons at their caller-supplied zero and returns false \u2014 a\n// clean \"cursor absent\" snapshot rather than stale or foreign data.\nbool kernel_gfx_mouse_poll(int slot, int* out_x, int* out_y,\n                            unsigned char* out_buttons) {\n    if (slot < 0 || wm.get_focused_elf_slot() != slot) return false;\n\n    Window* win = wm.find_window_by_elf_slot(slot);\n    if (!win) return false;\n\n    int lx = 0, ly = 0;\n    bool in_win = win->gfx_hit_test(mouse_x, mouse_y, &lx, &ly);\n    if (in_win) {\n        *out_x = lx;\n        *out_y = ly;\n        *out_buttons |= 0x10; // MOUSE_BIT_IN_WINDOW (bochs_drivers.h)\n    }\n    if (mouse_left_down)  *out_buttons |= 0x01; // MOUSE_BIT_LEFT_DOWN\n    if (mouse_right_down) *out_buttons |= 0x02; // MOUSE_BIT_RIGHT_DOWN\n    if (g_gfx_click_left_pending) {\n        *out_buttons |= 0x04; // MOUSE_BIT_LEFT_CLICKED\n        g_gfx_click_left_pending = false;\n    }\n    if (g_gfx_click_right_pending) {\n        *out_buttons |= 0x08; // MOUSE_BIT_RIGHT_CLICKED\n        g_gfx_click_right_pending = false;\n    }\n    return true;\n}\n\nvoid tcc_bridge_console_print(const char* s) {\n    console_print(s);\n}\nstatic inline bool is_cc_safe_char(unsigned char c) {\n    return c == '\\n' || c == '\\r' || c == '\\t' || (c >= 32 && c != 127);\n}\n\nchar* tcc_bridge_fat32_read(const char* filename) {\n    // Path-aware: fat32_read_file_as_string() only ever looks in the\n    // current directory and treats any '/' as part of a garbage flat\n    // name. fat32_read_file_as_string_path() falls back to that exact\n    // behavior when there's no '/' in filename, and otherwise resolves\n    // the directory part first -- needed both for `cc sub/foo.c` and for\n    // #include \"sub/foo.h\" (this same function is what TCC's open()\n    // shim calls for every #include, via the fat32_read_file_as_string\n    // macro alias in tcc_kernel.cpp).\n    char *data = fat32_read_file_as_string_path(filename);\n\n    if (!data) {\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    for (char *p = data; *p; ++p) {\n        if (!is_cc_safe_char((unsigned char)*p)) {\n            // FIX: this used to return NULL here without freeing `data`,\n            // leaking the whole file's buffer every time a source file\n            // or header contained a byte outside the \"safe\" set.\n            delete[] data;\n            return NULL;\n        }\n    }\n\n    return data;\n}\n\nint tcc_bridge_fat32_write(const char* filename, const void* data, unsigned int size) {\n    return fat32_write_file(filename, data, (uint32_t)size);\n}\n\nint tcc_bridge_exec_elf(void* terminal, const char* filename, const char* args) {\n    TerminalWindow* tw = (TerminalWindow*)terminal;\n    return tw->exec_elf(filename, args);\n}\n\n} // extern \"C\"\n"}, "message": "Full 11_kernel_main.h with the lag-free main loop and pacing helpers applied", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"c\", \"code\": \"#pragma once\\n// 11_kernel_main.h\\n// Hardware bring-up, the framebuffer probe, and kernel_main() --\\n// the kernel's actual entry point.\\n// Extracted from kernel.cpp (original lines 9360-10540) as part of\\n// splitting the monolithic kernel into per-component files. Order matters:\\n// this file relies on declarations from the kernel_parts files included\\n// before it in kernel.cpp, and is itself included there in sequence --\\n// it is NOT a standalone/independently-compilable translation unit.\\n\\n\\n// =============================================================================\\n// KERNEL MAIN - ATOMIC FRAME RENDERING\\n// =============================================================================\\n\\n\\n\\nstruct BochsCPURegs {\\n    uint32_t eax, ebx, ecx, edx, esi, edi, esp, ebp;\\n};\\n\\n// Forward decls for I/O callback adapters defined just below.\\nstatic int  elf_io_read (int slot);\\nstatic void elf_io_write(int slot, char c);\\nstatic void elf_io_exit (int slot, int code);\\n\\n// Replace init_elf_system() call in kernel_main with:\\nvoid init_elf_system() {\\n    for (auto& p : elf_processes) {\\n        p.active          = false;\\n        p.cpu_initialized = false;\\n        p.in_head  = p.in_tail  = 0;\\n        p.out_head = p.out_tail = 0;\\n    }\\n    // Register IO callbacks for every slot. These are what make\\n    // port-0xE9 writes from the guest reach the terminal: the chain is\\n    //   guest: out 0xE9, al\\n    //   -> bx_devices_c::outp (bochs_infra.cpp)\\n    //   -> bochs_guest_putc   (bochs_glue.cpp)\\n    //   -> write_cb = elf_io_write\\n    //   -> push_output(slot, c)\\n    //   -> tick_elf_processes drains it to terminal->console_print\\n    for (int s = 0; s < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES; ++s) {\\n        bochs_register_io_callbacks(s, elf_io_read, elf_io_write, elf_io_exit);\\n    }\\n    // NOTE: bochs_cpu_init() is NOT called here.\\n    //\\n    // We tried calling bochs_cpu_prewarm() (a guarded BX_CPU::initialize)\\n    // here to surface init-time bugs early, but it triggered the VMware\\n    // BAR1-unmap path documented at the top of vmware_svga_init(), causing\\n    // VMware to die with \\\"execute an invalid part of memory\\\". The early\\n    // call was unnecessary anyway: the host IDT installed in boot.S means\\n    // a host fault inside BX_CPU::initialize() during lazy init now\\n    // produces a visible \\\"!XX\\\" breadcrumb at VGA row 1 instead of a\\n    // silent triple fault.\\n    //\\n    // Init runs lazily on the first ELF launch via x86_tick. The\\n    // bochs_cpu_init() guard added in bochs_glue.cpp ensures\\n    // BX_CPU::initialize() runs at most once per boot regardless of how\\n    // many times the kernel calls bochs_cpu_init().\\n}\\n// I/O callback adapters\\nstatic int  elf_io_read (int slot) { return pop_input(slot); }\\nstatic void elf_io_write(int slot, char c) { push_output(slot, c); }\\nstatic void elf_io_exit (int slot, int code) {\\n    if (slot >= 0 && slot < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES) {\\n        // DIAGNOSTIC: report the exit code (now the real fault vector\\n        // number, per the per-vector stub change in inject_slab_tables).\\n        // The buffer is zero-initialized this time, unlike the old\\n        // disabled version this comment used to warn about.\\n        char buf[64] = {0};\\n        snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), \\\"\\\\n[guest exit, code=%d]\\\\n\\\", code);\\n        console_print(buf);\\n\\n        elf_processes[slot].completed = true;\\n        elf_processes[slot].active    = false;\\n    }\\n}static void dbg(const char* s) { /* write to VGA or terminal */ }\\n// Minimal ELF execution path for Bochs-backed processes.\\n// Assumes your existing kernel includes/types/helpers are already present.\\n\\n\\nstatic inline unsigned int align_down(unsigned int v, unsigned int a) {\\n    return v & ~(a - 1);\\n}\\n\\nstatic inline unsigned int align_up(unsigned int v, unsigned int a) {\\n    return (v + a - 1) & ~(a - 1);\\n}\\n\\nstatic bool load_elf_image_to_slab(\\n    int slot,\\n    const unsigned char* elf,\\n    unsigned int elf_size,\\n    unsigned int& entry_out)\\n{\\n    if (elf_size < 52) return false;\\n    if (!(elf[0] == 0x7f && elf[1] == 'E' && elf[2] == 'L' && elf[3] == 'F')) return false;\\n    if (elf[4] != 1 || elf[5] != 1) return false;\\n\\n    auto rd16 = [&](unsigned int off) -> unsigned short {\\n        return (unsigned short)(elf[off] | (elf[off + 1] << 8));\\n    };\\n    auto rd32 = [&](unsigned int off) -> unsigned int {\\n        return (unsigned int)(elf[off] |\\n                              (elf[off + 1] << 8) |\\n                              (elf[off + 2] << 16) |\\n                              (elf[off + 3] << 24));\\n    };\\n\\n    unsigned short phoff = rd32(28);\\n    unsigned short phentsize = rd16(42);\\n    unsigned short phnum = rd16(44);\\n    unsigned int entry = rd32(24);\\n\\n    if (phoff == 0 || phentsize < 32 || phnum == 0) return false;\\n\\n    unsigned int min_vaddr = 0xFFFFFFFFu;\\n    unsigned int max_vaddr = 0;\\n\\n    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < phnum; ++i) {\\n        unsigned int p = phoff + i * phentsize;\\n        if (p + 32 > elf_size) return false;\\n\\n        unsigned int p_type = rd32(p + 0);\\n        if (p_type != 1) continue; // PT_LOAD\\n\\n        unsigned int p_vaddr = rd32(p + 8);\\n        unsigned int p_memsz = rd32(p + 20);\\n\\n        if (p_memsz == 0) continue;\\n\\n        if (p_vaddr < min_vaddr) min_vaddr = p_vaddr;\\n        if (p_vaddr + p_memsz > max_vaddr) max_vaddr = p_vaddr + p_memsz;\\n    }\\n\\n    if (min_vaddr == 0xFFFFFFFFu || max_vaddr <= min_vaddr) return false;\\n\\n    unsigned int vaddr_base = align_down(min_vaddr, 0x1000);\\n    unsigned int vaddr_top = align_up(max_vaddr + ELF_STACK_SIZE + ELF_HEAP_SIZE, 0x1000);\\n    unsigned int slab_size = vaddr_top - vaddr_base;\\n\\n    unsigned char* slab = elf_alloc_bytes(slab_size);\\n    if (!slab) return false;\\n    memset(slab, 0, slab_size);\\n\\n    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < phnum; ++i) {\\n        unsigned int p = phoff + i * phentsize;\\n        if (p + 32 > elf_size) continue;\\n\\n        unsigned int p_type = rd32(p + 0);\\n        if (p_type != 1) continue;\\n\\n        unsigned int p_offset = rd32(p + 4);\\n        unsigned int p_vaddr  = rd32(p + 8);\\n        unsigned int p_filesz  = rd32(p + 16);\\n        unsigned int p_memsz   = rd32(p + 20);\\n\\n        if (p_offset + p_filesz > elf_size) {\\n            elf_free_bytes(slab);\\n            return false;\\n        }\\n\\n        unsigned int dest = p_vaddr - vaddr_base;\\n        if (dest + p_memsz > slab_size) {\\n            elf_free_bytes(slab);\\n            return false;\\n        }\\n\\n        memcpy(slab + dest, elf + p_offset, p_filesz);\\n        if (p_memsz > p_filesz) {\\n            memset(slab + dest + p_filesz, 0, p_memsz - p_filesz);\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    entry_out = entry;\\n    bochs_activate_slot(slot);\\n    bochs_set_process_memory(slab, slab_size, vaddr_base);\\n    bochs_finalize_process_memory();\\n\\n    // Populate ElfProcess so that start_elf_process (and kill_elf_process)\\n    // can track and free this slab correctly.\\n    ElfProcess& proc = elf_processes[slot];\\n    proc.memory_base = slab;\\n    proc.memory_size = slab_size;\\n    proc.vaddr_base  = vaddr_base;\\n    proc.vaddr_end   = vaddr_top;\\n\\n    return true;\\n}\\n\\nstatic bool start_elf_process(int slot, const unsigned char* elf, unsigned int elf_size) {\\n    ElfProcess& proc = elf_processes[slot];\\n    unsigned int entry = 0;\\n\\n    // load_elf_image_to_slab calls bochs_activate_slot + bochs_set_process_memory\\n    // and populates proc.memory_base / proc.memory_size / proc.vaddr_base.\\n    if (!load_elf_image_to_slab(slot, elf, elf_size, entry)) return false;\\n\\n    proc.active = true;\\n    proc.completed = false;\\n    proc.entry_point = entry;\\n\\n    // vaddr_base was set by load_elf_image_to_slab; don't clobber it with a\\n    // page-aligned guess derived from the entry point (those can differ for\\n    // PIE or non-zero-based binaries).\\n    unsigned int stack_top = proc.vaddr_base + proc.memory_size;\\n    proc.esp = stack_top - 16;\\n    proc.brk_addr = proc.vaddr_base + proc.memory_size - ELF_HEAP_SIZE;\\n\\n    // Finish CPU wiring: init once, then point at entry.\\n    bochs_cpu_init();\\n    bochs_cpu_set_esp(proc.esp);\\n    bochs_cpu_set_eip(proc.entry_point);\\n    bochs_set_brk(slot, proc.brk_addr);\\n\\n    // Mark cpu_initialized so x86_tick skips the lazy-init path and goes\\n    // straight to bochs_cpu_tick (we've done the full setup just above).\\n    proc.cpu_initialized = true;\\n\\n    return true;\\n}\\n\\nextern \\\"C\\\" volatile unsigned char bx_panic_breadcrumbs[64];\\n// \\u2500\\u2500 Diagnostic breadcrumb at VGA row 2 \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n// Each x86_tick lazy-init step writes a tag char to row 2 columns 0..N.\\n// Tags are:\\n//   col 0: 'L'  \\u2014 entered lazy init\\n//   col 1: 'M'  \\u2014 set_process_memory returned\\n//   col 2: 'I'  \\u2014 bochs_cpu_init returned\\n//   col 3: 'S'  \\u2014 set_esp returned\\n//   col 4: 'E'  \\u2014 set_eip returned\\n//   col 5: 'B'  \\u2014 set_brk returned\\n//   col 6: 'T'  \\u2014 about to call bochs_cpu_tick\\n//   col 7: 't'  \\u2014 bochs_cpu_tick returned\\n// If lazy init wedges, the last tag visible tells you which call did it.\\n//\\n// We write to BOTH the VGA text-mode plane (forensic for pmemsave) AND\\n// directly to the live framebuffer (visible to the user even if the\\n// kernel never reaches swap_buffers again). Direct framebuffer writes\\n// bypass the back buffer and use fb_info.ptr; that means they survive\\n// a hang inside x86_tick that prevents the next paint cycle.\\nstatic inline void x86_breadcrumb(int col, char c) {\\n    if (col < 0 || col >= 80) return;\\n\\n    // 1. VGA text-mode plane (forensic).\\n    {\\n        volatile unsigned short* p =\\n            (volatile unsigned short*)(0xB8000 + 2 * 80 + 2 * col);\\n        *p = (unsigned short)(0x0E00u | (unsigned char)c);\\n    }\\n\\n    // 2. Live framebuffer (visible). 8x8 yellow glyph at row 2 of overlay.\\n    if (!fb_info.ptr) return;\\n    if ((unsigned char)c > 127) return;\\n    const uint8_t* glyph = font + (int)c * 8;\\n    int x0 = col * 8;\\n    int y0 = 16;          // row 2 of overlay (rows 0,1,2 each 8px tall)\\n    if (x0 + 8 > (int)fb_info.width)  return;\\n    if (y0 + 8 > (int)fb_info.height) return;\\n\\n    uint32_t color = 0xFFFF55u;   // bright yellow\\n    uint32_t bg    = 0x000000u;\\n    for (int yy = 0; yy < 8; ++yy) {\\n        uint32_t* row = &fb_info.ptr[(y0 + yy) * (fb_info.pitch / 4) + x0];\\n        uint8_t bits = glyph[yy];\\n        for (int xx = 0; xx < 8; ++xx) {\\n            row[xx] = (bits & (0x80 >> xx)) ? color : bg;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nstatic bool x86_tick(int slot, int steps) {\\n    ElfProcess& proc = elf_processes[slot];\\n    // Tick a process that is alive: active and NOT yet completed.\\n    // The previous form (`!proc.active || !proc.completed`) was inverted \\u2014\\n    // it returned for every live process, so the guest never advanced and\\n    // its port-0xE9 output never reached the terminal display.\\n    if (!proc.active || proc.completed) return false;\\n\\n    if (!proc.cpu_initialized) {\\n        if (!proc.memory_base || proc.memory_size == 0) {\\n            proc.active    = false;\\n            proc.completed = true;\\n            return false;\\n        }\\n        // Correct init order:\\n        //   1. bochs_cpu_init()          -- global Bochs one-time init (idempotent)\\n        //   2. bochs_activate_slot()     -- select which slot g_active_slot points at\\n        //   3. bochs_set_process_memory  -- resets CPU to clean state, injects GDT/IDT/\\n        //                                   stub tables, then restores to protected mode\\n        //   4. set_esp / set_eip         -- point the protected-mode CPU at guest entry\\n        //\\n        // The previous order was: activate \\u2192 set_memory \\u2192 cpu_init.\\n        // bochs_set_process_memory does BX_CPU::reset() + slot_restore_cpu() to\\n        // arrive at protected mode. bochs_cpu_init() called *after* does another\\n        // BX_CPU::reset() \\u2014 wiping that protected mode state back to real mode.\\n        // The guest then runs (or tries to) in real mode at a 32-bit EIP, which\\n        // either executes garbage or hangs inside cpu_loop with no visible output.\\n        x86_breadcrumb(0, 'L');\\n        bochs_cpu_init();\\n        x86_breadcrumb(1, 'I');\\n        bochs_activate_slot(slot);\\n        bochs_set_process_memory(proc.memory_base, proc.memory_size,\\n                                 proc.vaddr_base);\\n        x86_breadcrumb(2, 'M');\\n        bochs_cpu_set_esp(proc.esp);\\n        x86_breadcrumb(3, 'S');\\n        bochs_cpu_set_eip(proc.entry_point);\\n        x86_breadcrumb(4, 'E');\\n        bochs_set_brk(slot, proc.vaddr_base + proc.memory_size - ELFHEAPSIZE);\\n        x86_breadcrumb(5, 'B');\\n        proc.cpu_initialized = true;\\n    } else {\\n        bochs_activate_slot(slot);\\n    }\\n\\n    x86_breadcrumb(6, 'T');\\n\\n    // Diagnostic: snapshot EIP BEFORE tick so we can detect an unexpected\\n    // jump back to entry after the tick. (Don't enable unconditionally \\u2014\\n    // gated on the very-recent-restart condition below so normal output\\n    // isn't polluted.)\\n    uint32_t eip_before = bochs_cpu_get_eip();\\n\\nbochs_cpu_tick(steps);\\nx86_breadcrumb(7, 't');\\n\\nbool just_started_waiting =\\n    bochs_process_wants_input(slot) && in_empty(slot);\\n\\nif (just_started_waiting) {\\n    proc.waiting_for_input = true;\\n    // The guest yielded mid-`IN` on port 0xE7. EIP isn't reliably\\n    // retired across that abort, so don't trust it this tick \\u2014\\n    // skip the exit/EIP-range checks below entirely and pick back\\n    // up cleanly next tick once real input has been pushed.\\n    return true;\\n}\\n\\n// \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500 FIX: detect clean guest-exit signal \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\nif (!proc.active || proc.completed) return false;\\n\\nunsigned int eip = bochs_cpu_get_eip();\\nif (eip == 0 || eip < proc.vaddr_base || eip >= proc.vaddr_base + proc.memory_size) {\\n    proc.completed = true;\\n    proc.active    = false;\\n    return false;\\n}\\n\\nreturn true;\\n\\n    return true;\\n}\\n// And definition without default:\\nvoid tick_elf_processes(int steps) {\\n    bool any_exited_this_frame = false;\\n\\n    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES; ++i) {\\n        ElfProcess& proc = elf_processes[i];\\n        // Drain output and step a process that is alive: active and not\\n        // completed. Inverting this gate (the old `!active || !completed`)\\n        // skipped every live process, leaving the terminal display silent\\n        // even though the Bochs guest had written bytes to port 0xE9.\\n        if (!proc.active || proc.completed) continue;\\n\\n        while (!out_empty(i)) {\\n            char tmp[256];\\n            int n = 0;\\n            while (!out_empty(i) && n < 255) tmp[n++] = pop_output(i);\\n            tmp[n] = 0;\\n            if (proc.terminal && n) {\\n                proc.terminal->console_print(tmp);\\n                // Guest produced output. Flag the frame dirty so the main\\n                // loop actually repaints. Without this the repaint is\\n                // gated on g_evt_dirty / hasNewInput \\u2014 both only set by\\n                // user input \\u2014 so guest output sat invisibly in the\\n                // terminal buffer until the next keypress (\\\"need to press\\n                // enter to print the last lot\\\").\\n                g_evt_dirty = true;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        if (proc.waiting_for_input && in_empty(i)) continue;\\n\\n        bool running = x86_tick(i, steps);\\n\\n        while (!out_empty(i)) {\\n            char tmp[256];\\n            int n = 0;\\n            while (!out_empty(i) && n < 255) tmp[n++] = pop_output(i);\\n            tmp[n] = 0;\\n            if (proc.terminal && n) {\\n                proc.terminal->console_print(tmp);\\n                g_evt_dirty = true;   // see note above\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        if (!running) {\\n            proc.active = false;\\n            proc.completed = true;\\n            if (proc.terminal) proc.terminal->captured_elf_slot = -1;\\n            // A process just finished \\u2014 the prompt needs to come back and\\n            // any final output needs to show. Repaint this frame.\\n            g_evt_dirty = true;\\n\\n            // \\u2500\\u2500 Per-slot kernel-side teardown \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n            // Free the slab and stack we allocated in load_and_execute_elf,\\n            // and clear cpu_initialized so the NEXT process that lands in\\n            // this slot re-runs the lazy-init path from scratch.\\n            //\\n            // We do NOT touch the Bochs glue here. The glue side gets a\\n            // single heavy `bochs_reset_all_slots()` call at the end of\\n            // this function (see below) \\u2014 that wipes every per-slot\\n            // mapping AND hardware-resets BX_CPU(0), so launch N starts\\n            // from the same state as launch 1. Trying to keep the glue\\n            // \\\"incrementally consistent\\\" with surgical per-slot updates\\n            // had a long history of subtle bugs (the second `bochs hello`\\n            // looping \\\"HELLO WOHELLO WO...\\\" because some untracked CPU\\n            // field survived across slot reuse). The big-hammer reset\\n            // is cheap and unambiguous.\\n            //\\n            // EXCEPTION (added for concurrent processes): if any OTHER\\n            // slot is still active, bochs_reset_all_slots() below will\\n            // be SKIPPED (resetting the live CPU would clobber that\\n            // peer's mid-execution state). In that case we still need\\n            // to clear THIS slot's mapping and dangling mem_base pointer\\n            // before freeing the slab \\u2014 otherwise the glue carries\\n            // forward a dangling pointer that could be dereferenced on\\n            // a future activate_slot. bochs_release_slot does that\\n            // surgically without touching peer slots or BX_CPU(0).\\n            // It's safe to call unconditionally: when the all-slots\\n            // reset path also fires below, release_slot's wipe is\\n            // simply overwritten by the same values via reset_all_slots.\\n            bochs_release_slot(i);\\n            if (proc.memory_base) { elf_free_bytes(proc.memory_base); proc.memory_base = nullptr; }\\n            if (proc.stack)       { elf_free_bytes(proc.stack);       proc.stack       = nullptr; }\\n            proc.memory_size     = 0;\\n            proc.cpu_initialized = false;\\n\\n            // \\u2500\\u2500 Wipe per-slot I/O ring buffers and transient flags \\u2500\\u2500\\n            // Belt-and-braces companion to the scrub in\\n            // load_and_execute_elf: cleared HERE on the exit edge so\\n            // the slot is left in a strictly-empty state the instant\\n            // the process becomes inactive. If any code path on the\\n            // next frame reads from inbuf/outbuf before the next\\n            // load_and_execute_elf runs (e.g. a stray\\n            // tick_elf_processes pass on a slot whose `completed`\\n            // flag flipped but whose `active` flag hasn't been\\n            // re-set yet), it sees a clean empty queue rather than\\n            // stale bytes from the run that just ended.\\n            //\\n            // Without this, the third in-place run of `hello` in the\\n            // same emulator window produced \\\"HELLO WOHELLO WO...\\\"\\n            // loops with leftover keystrokes from previous runs\\n            // bleeding into the new guest's stdin and the previous\\n            // guest's tail-end output bleeding into the new\\n            // terminal display.\\n            for (int _b = 0; _b < INBUFSIZE;  ++_b) proc.inbuf[_b]  = 0;\\n            for (int _b = 0; _b < OUTBUFSIZE; ++_b) proc.outbuf[_b] = 0;\\n            proc.in_head           = 0;\\n            proc.in_tail           = 0;\\n            proc.out_head          = 0;\\n            proc.out_tail          = 0;\\n            proc.waiting_for_input = false;\\n            proc.exit_code         = 0;\\n            proc.input_pos         = 0;\\n\\n            any_exited_this_frame = true;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // \\u2500\\u2500 Deferred glue-wide reset \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n    // If any process exited this frame AND no other Bochs-emulated\\n    // process is still running, wipe Bochs's glue state back to its\\n    // post-boot baseline. The next launch will then start from the\\n    // same state as launch #1.\\n    //\\n    // CRITICAL: do NOT reset while another slot is still running.\\n    // bochs_reset_all_slots() unmaps every slab and hardware-resets\\n    // BX_CPU(0); doing that to a live slot drops its EIP/CRs/segments\\n    // back to the post-reset state. On the very next frame, x86_tick\\n    // would re-enter the lazy-init path and bochs_cpu_set_eip would\\n    // restart the guest from _start. The visible symptom is a runaway\\n    // window spamming \\\"HELLO WOHELLO WO...\\\" for as long as another\\n    // window is finishing \\u2014 exactly because every frame's exit on\\n    // window B triggered a reset that restarted window A from the top.\\n    if (any_exited_this_frame) {\\n        // Only wipe the Bochs glue when ALL slots are done AND all\\n        // output has been drained. Use two separate loops: the old\\n        // single-loop version broke out early on the first active slot,\\n        // so any_output_pending was never checked for remaining slots.\\n        bool any_still_active   = false;\\n        bool any_output_pending = false;\\n        for (int j = 0; j < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES; ++j) {\\n            if (elf_processes[j].active) { any_still_active = true; break; }\\n        }\\n        if (!any_still_active) {\\n            for (int j = 0; j < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES; ++j) {\\n                if (!out_empty(j)) { any_output_pending = true; break; }\\n            }\\n        }\\n        if (!any_still_active && !any_output_pending) {\\n            bochs_reset_all_slots();\\n        }\\n    }\\n     \\n}\\n\\nextern \\\"C\\\" void cmd_exec(const char* code_text) {\\n    if (!code_text) return;\\n    TCompiler C;\\n    int ok = C.compile(code_text);\\n    if (ok < 0) return;\\n    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_EXEC_PROCESSES; i++) {\\n        if (!exec_contexts[i].active) {\\n            exec_contexts[i].active = true;\\n            exec_contexts[i].exec_id = i;\\n            exec_contexts[i].prog = C.pr;\\n            const char* av[] = {\\\"exec\\\", nullptr};\\n            exec_contexts[i].vm.start_execution(exec_contexts[i].prog,1,av,0,0,nullptr);\\n            return;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n// =============================================================================\\n// Helper: write a short status string to VGA text mode row 1 (safe at any\\n// point in kernel_main, before framebuffer is initialised).\\n// =============================================================================\\n// Write msg to VGA text row (0-based)\\nstatic void vga_dbg_row(int row, const char* msg, uint8_t attr = 0x0F) {\\n    volatile uint16_t* vga = (volatile uint16_t*)0xB8000 + row * 80;\\n    int i = 0;\\n    for (; i < 79 && msg[i]; i++)\\n        vga[i] = (uint16_t)((uint16_t)(attr << 8) | (uint8_t)msg[i]);\\n    for (; i < 79; i++)\\n        vga[i] = (uint16_t)((uint16_t)(attr << 8) | ' ');\\n}\\n\\nstatic void vga_status(const char* msg, uint8_t attr = 0x0F) {\\n    vga_dbg_row(1, msg, attr);\\n}\\n\\n// Write a 32-bit hex value into buf[11] and return it\\nstatic char* hex32(uint32_t v, char* buf) {\\n    const char* h = \\\"0123456789ABCDEF\\\";\\n    buf[0]='0'; buf[1]='x';\\n    for (int i = 7; i >= 0; i--) { buf[2+i] = h[v & 0xF]; v >>= 4; }\\n    buf[10] = 0;\\n    return buf;\\n}\\n\\n// Concatenate up to 6 strings into dst[128]\\nstatic char* vga_cat(char* dst, const char* a, const char* b=\\\"\\\",\\n                      const char* c=\\\"\\\", const char* d=\\\"\\\") {\\n    char* p = dst;\\n    auto app = [&](const char* s){ while(*s && p < dst+127) *p++=*s++; };\\n    app(a); app(b); app(c); app(d);\\n    *p = 0; return dst;\\n}\\n\\n// =============================================================================\\n// Framebuffer probe \\u2014 four strategies, always returns something safe.\\n// =============================================================================\\nstatic void probe_framebuffer(multiboot_info* mbi,\\n                              uint32_t& fb_phys,\\n                              uint32_t& fb_w,\\n                              uint32_t& fb_h,\\n                              uint32_t& fb_pitch)\\n{\\n    fb_phys  = 0;\\n    fb_w     = 1024;\\n    fb_h     = 768;\\n    fb_pitch = 1024 * 4;\\n\\n    // Diagnostic: show multiboot flags and framebuffer fields on VGA rows 2-4\\n    {\\n        char dbuf[128]; char hb[11];\\n        vga_dbg_row(2, vga_cat(dbuf, \\\"MB flags=\\\", hex32(mbi->flags, hb)), 0x0E);\\n        uint32_t raw_fb = (uint32_t)(uintptr_t)mbi->framebuffer_addr;\\n        char hb2[11];\\n        vga_dbg_row(3, vga_cat(dbuf, \\\"FB addr=\\\", hex32(raw_fb, hb),\\n                           \\\" type=\\\", hex32(mbi->framebuffer_type, hb2)), 0x0E);\\n        vga_dbg_row(4, vga_cat(dbuf, \\\"FB w=\\\", hex32(mbi->framebuffer_width, hb),\\n                           \\\" h=\\\", hex32(mbi->framebuffer_height, hb2)), 0x0E);\\n    }\\n\\n    // Strategy 1: GRUB filled framebuffer fields (our boot.S requests this\\n    // via MB_FLAGS bit 2).  This is the normal path on QEMU + GRUB 2.\\n    // framebuffer_type: 0=indexed, 1=RGB/direct, 2=EGA text \\u2014 only accept 1.\\n    if (mbi->flags & (1u << 12)) {\\n        uint32_t addr = (uint32_t)(uintptr_t)mbi->framebuffer_addr;\\n        char dbuf[128]; char hb[11];\\n        vga_dbg_row(5, vga_cat(dbuf, \\\"S1: addr=\\\", hex32(addr, hb),\\n                           \\\" type=\\\", hex32(mbi->framebuffer_type, hb)), 0x0A);\\n        // Accept type 1 (RGB direct) or type 0 (indexed/paletted reported by\\n        // some VMware GRUB configs). Reject type 2 (EGA text mode).\\n        if (addr >= 0x1000000u && mbi->framebuffer_type != 2) {\\n            fb_phys  = addr;\\n            fb_w     = mbi->framebuffer_width;\\n            fb_h     = mbi->framebuffer_height;\\n            fb_pitch = mbi->framebuffer_pitch;\\n            if (fb_w  > 1024) { fb_w  = 1024; fb_pitch = 1024*4; }\\n            if (fb_h  > 768)  { fb_h  = 768; }\\n            vga_dbg_row(5, \\\"S1: SUCCESS - using GRUB framebuffer\\\", 0x0A);\\n            return;\\n        }\\n        vga_dbg_row(5, \\\"S1: SKIPPED (bad addr or type!=1)\\\", 0x0E);\\n    } else {\\n        vga_dbg_row(5, \\\"S1: SKIPPED (bit12 not set in flags)\\\", 0x0E);\\n    }\\n\\n    // Strategy 2: Bochs VBE ports (QEMU -vga std).\\n    {\\n        auto vbe_out = [](uint16_t idx, uint16_t val) {\\n            asm volatile(\\\"outw %0,%1\\\" :: \\\"a\\\"(idx), \\\"d\\\"((uint16_t)0x01CE));\\n            asm volatile(\\\"outw %0,%1\\\" :: \\\"a\\\"(val), \\\"d\\\"((uint16_t)0x01CF));\\n        };\\n        auto vbe_in = [](uint16_t idx) -> uint16_t {\\n            uint16_t v;\\n            asm volatile(\\\"outw %0,%1\\\" :: \\\"a\\\"(idx), \\\"d\\\"((uint16_t)0x01CE));\\n            asm volatile(\\\"inw %1,%0\\\"  : \\\"=a\\\"(v)   : \\\"d\\\"((uint16_t)0x01CF));\\n            return v;\\n        };\\n        vbe_out(0x04, 0x00);\\n        uint16_t id = vbe_in(0x00);\\n        if (id >= 0xB0C0) {\\n            vbe_out(0x01, 1024);\\n            vbe_out(0x02, 768);\\n            vbe_out(0x03, 32);\\n            vbe_out(0x05, 1024);\\n            vbe_out(0x06, 768);\\n            vbe_out(0x07, 0);\\n            vbe_out(0x08, 0);\\n            vbe_out(0x04, 0x41); // ENABLE | LFB_ENABLED\\n            for (uint16_t bus = 0; bus < 8 && !fb_phys; bus++) {\\n                for (uint8_t dev = 0; dev < 32 && !fb_phys; dev++) {\\n                    uint32_t vd = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x00);\\n                    if ((vd & 0xFFFF) == 0xFFFF) continue;\\n                    bool is_bochs   = (vd == 0x11111234u);\\n                    uint32_t cc     = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x08) >> 16;\\n                    bool is_display = (cc == 0x0300 || cc == 0x0380);\\n                    if (!is_bochs && !is_display) continue;\\n                    for (int b = 0; b < 3 && !fb_phys; b++) {\\n                        uint32_t bar = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x10 + b*4);\\n                        if (bar & 1) continue;\\n                        uint32_t addr = bar & 0xFFFFFFF0u;\\n                        if (addr >= 0x1000000u) fb_phys = addr;\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n            if (fb_phys) return;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // Strategy 3: VMware SVGA II (vendor 0x15AD, device 0x0405).\\n    // The SVGA II adapter uses an I/O BAR (BAR0) for its index/value register\\n    // pair and a memory BAR (BAR1) for the linear framebuffer.  It must be\\n    // programmed via I/O ports to set the resolution and enable the FB;\\n    // simply reading BAR1 is not enough \\u2014 the FB is not live until ENABLE=1.\\n    //\\n    // SVGA II register map (index written to io_base+0, value at io_base+1):\\n    //   SVGA_REG_ID       = 0   write SVGA_MAGIC|2 to negotiate version\\n    //   SVGA_REG_ENABLE   = 1   write 1 to enable SVGA mode\\n    //   SVGA_REG_WIDTH    = 2\\n    //   SVGA_REG_HEIGHT   = 3\\n    //   SVGA_REG_BPP      = 7   (bits per pixel)\\n    //   SVGA_REG_FB_START = 13  returns the physical FB base address\\n    //   SVGA_REG_PITCH    = 24  returns bytes per scan line\\n    {\\n        // Locate the SVGA II PCI device\\n        uint16_t svga_io = 0;\\n        uint32_t svga_fb_bar = 0;\\n        for (uint16_t bus = 0; bus < 8 && !svga_io; bus++) {\\n            for (uint8_t dev = 0; dev < 32 && !svga_io; dev++) {\\n                uint32_t vd = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x00);\\n                // VMware vendor 0x15AD, SVGA II device 0x0405\\n                if (vd != 0x040515ADu) continue;\\n                // BAR0 = I/O port base (bit 0 set = I/O space)\\n                uint32_t bar0 = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x10);\\n                if (bar0 & 1) svga_io = (uint16_t)(bar0 & 0xFFFE);\\n                // BAR1 = framebuffer memory base\\n                svga_fb_bar = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x14) & 0xFFFFFFF0u;\\n\\n                // Enable PCI memory + I/O decode (command register, offset 4)\\n                uint32_t cmd = pci_read_config_dword(bus, dev, 0, 0x04);\\n                cmd |= 0x03; // I/O + Memory enable\\n                // pci_write_config_dword not available, use outl directly\\n                uint32_t addr_reg = 0x80000000u | ((uint32_t)bus << 16) |\\n                                    ((uint32_t)dev << 11) | 0x04u;\\n                outl(0xCF8, addr_reg);\\n                outl(0xCFC, cmd);\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        {\\n            char dbuf[128]; char hb[11]; char hb2[11];\\n            vga_dbg_row(6, vga_cat(dbuf, \\\"S3: io=\\\", hex32(svga_io, hb),\\n                               \\\" bar1=\\\", hex32(svga_fb_bar, hb2)), 0x0A);\\n        }\\n\\n        if (svga_io) {\\n            // Helper lambdas for SVGA II register access\\n            auto svga_write = [&](uint32_t reg, uint32_t val) {\\n                outl((uint16_t)(svga_io + 0), reg); // index port\\n                outl((uint16_t)(svga_io + 4), val); // value port\\n            };\\n            auto svga_read = [&](uint32_t reg) -> uint32_t {\\n                outl((uint16_t)(svga_io + 0), reg);\\n                return inl((uint16_t)(svga_io + 4));\\n            };\\n\\n            // Negotiate SVGA II version (SVGA_ID_2 = 0x90000002)\\n            svga_write(0 /*SVGA_REG_ID*/, 0x90000002u);\\n            uint32_t id = svga_read(0);\\n            { char dbuf[128]; char hb[11];\\n              vga_dbg_row(7, vga_cat(dbuf, \\\"S3: SVGA_ID=\\\", hex32(id, hb)), 0x0A); }\\n\\n            if (id == 0x90000002u) {\\n                // Set 1024x768x32\\n                svga_write(2 /*SVGA_REG_WIDTH*/,  1024);\\n                svga_write(3 /*SVGA_REG_HEIGHT*/,  768);\\n                svga_write(7 /*SVGA_REG_BITS_PER_PIXEL*/, 32);\\n                svga_write(1 /*SVGA_REG_ENABLE*/,  1);\\n\\n                // Read back actual FB address and pitch\\n                uint32_t reported_fb = svga_read(13 /*SVGA_REG_FB_START*/);\\n                uint32_t pitch       = svga_read(24 /*SVGA_REG_BYTES_PER_LINE*/);\\n\\n                { char dbuf[128]; char hb[11]; char hb2[11];\\n                  vga_dbg_row(8, vga_cat(dbuf, \\\"S3: fb=\\\", hex32(reported_fb, hb),\\n                                     \\\" pitch=\\\", hex32(pitch, hb2)), 0x0A); }\\n\\n                // Use the reported address if valid, fall back to BAR1\\n                fb_phys  = (reported_fb >= 0x1000000u) ? reported_fb : svga_fb_bar;\\n                fb_w     = 1024;\\n                fb_h     = 768;\\n                fb_pitch = (pitch >= 1024*4) ? pitch : 1024*4;\\n                if (fb_phys >= 0x1000000u) {\\n                    vga_dbg_row(9, \\\"S3: SUCCESS - VMware SVGA II programmed\\\", 0x0A);\\n                    return;\\n                }\\n                vga_dbg_row(9, \\\"S3: FAILED - fb addr still bad\\\", 0x4F);\\n            } else {\\n                vga_dbg_row(7, \\\"S3: FAILED - wrong SVGA_ID (not VMware?)\\\", 0x4F);\\n            }\\n        } else {\\n            vga_dbg_row(6, \\\"S3: SKIPPED - no SVGA II device found on PCI\\\", 0x0E);\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // Strategy 4: hardcoded fallback.\\n    // 0xFD000000 = Bochs/QEMU default.\\n    // 0xE8000000 = VMware Workstation SVGA II BAR1 (confirmed from vmware.log).\\n    // vmware_svga_init() runs before probe_framebuffer and will override this\\n    // with the correct BAR1 address, so this fallback is only for QEMU/Bochs.\\n    fb_phys = 0xFD000000u;\\n}\\n\\n// =============================================================================\\n// kernel_main\\n// =============================================================================\\n// \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n// VMware SVGA II initialisation.\\n// Must run BEFORE any framebuffer access.  Returns the live FB base address,\\n// or 0 if no SVGA II device is found.\\n//\\n// The SVGA II I/O port pair lives at BAR0 (an I/O BAR):\\n//   index port = BAR0 + 0\\n//   value port = BAR0 + 4        (NOT +1 \\u2014 the value port is 32-bit wide)\\n// Register indices used here:\\n//   0  SVGA_REG_ID              write 0x90000002 to negotiate SVGA2\\n//   1  SVGA_REG_ENABLE          write 1 to enable linear framebuffer\\n//   2  SVGA_REG_WIDTH\\n//   3  SVGA_REG_HEIGHT\\n//   7  SVGA_REG_BITS_PER_PIXEL\\n//  13  SVGA_REG_FB_START        read to get physical FB address\\n//  24  SVGA_REG_BYTES_PER_LINE  read to get pitch in bytes\\n// \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\nstruct SVGAResult { uint32_t fb; uint32_t pitch; bool ok; };\\n\\n// \\u2500\\u2500 VMware SVGA II: full PCI scan + I/O programming \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n// Scans ALL 256 PCI buses (some VMware configs place SVGA on bus > 7),\\n// all 32 devices, all 8 functions.  Tries both device IDs 0x0405 and 0x0710.\\n// The I/O BAR may be at BAR0 or BAR2 depending on SVGA revision.\\nstatic SVGAResult vmware_svga_init(uint32_t w, uint32_t h) {\\n    SVGAResult r = {0, w*4, false};\\n\\n    uint16_t io   = 0;\\n    uint32_t bar1 = 0;\\n\\n    // Full PCI scan \\u2014 VMware may put the SVGA on any bus\\n    for (uint32_t bus = 0; bus < 256 && !io; bus++) {\\n        for (uint32_t dev = 0; dev < 32 && !io; dev++) {\\n            for (uint32_t fn = 0; fn < 8 && !io; fn++) {\\n                uint32_t id = pci_read_config_dword(\\n                    (uint16_t)bus, (uint8_t)dev, (uint8_t)fn, 0x00);\\n                if ((id & 0xFFFFu) != 0x15ADu) continue; // not VMware vendor\\n                uint32_t did = (id >> 16) & 0xFFFFu;\\n                if (did != 0x0405u && did != 0x0710u) continue; // not SVGA\\n\\n                // DO NOT touch the PCI command register.\\n                // The BIOS has already enabled I/O + Memory decode for SVGA.\\n                // Re-writing the command register causes VMware to briefly\\n                // unmap BAR1 (0xe8000000), creating a window where pixel\\n                // writes crash with \\\"execute an invalid part of memory\\\".\\n\\n                // Read all 6 BARs \\u2014 find the I/O BAR and the memory BAR\\n                for (int b = 0; b < 6; b++) {\\n                    uint32_t bar = pci_read_config_dword(\\n                        (uint16_t)bus, (uint8_t)dev, (uint8_t)fn,\\n                        (uint8_t)(0x10 + b*4));\\n                    if ((bar & 1u) && !io) {\\n                        io = (uint16_t)(bar & 0xFFFCu); // I/O BAR\\n                    } else if (!(bar & 1u) && !bar1) {\\n                        uint32_t addr = bar & 0xFFFFFFF0u;\\n                        if (addr >= 0x1000000u) bar1 = addr; // memory BAR\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // Also try the fixed legacy SVGA I/O port (0x4560) used by very old VMware\\n    if (!io) io = 0x4560u;\\n\\n    // I/O helpers\\n    auto wr = [&](uint32_t reg, uint32_t val) {\\n        outl(io,     reg);\\n        outl(io + 4, val);\\n    };\\n    auto rd = [&](uint32_t reg) -> uint32_t {\\n        outl(io, reg);\\n        return inl(io + 4);\\n    };\\n\\n    // Negotiate SVGA2 \\u2014 try SVGA_ID_2, fall back to SVGA_ID_1\\n    wr(0, 0x90000002u);\\n    uint32_t svga_id = rd(0);\\n    if (svga_id != 0x90000002u) {\\n        wr(0, 0x90000001u);\\n        svga_id = rd(0);\\n        if (svga_id != 0x90000001u) return r; // not responding\\n    }\\n\\n    // Program resolution\\n    wr(2, w);\\n    wr(3, h);\\n    wr(7, 32);\\n\\n    // Enable\\n    wr(1, 1);\\n\\n    // Read FB address and pitch.\\n    // VMware Workstation 14+ returns 0 from SVGA_REG_FB_START (reg 13) \\u2014\\n    // the physical framebuffer is always at BAR1. Prefer BAR1 when valid.\\n    uint32_t fb    = rd(13);\\n    uint32_t pitch = rd(24);\\n\\n    if (bar1 >= 0x1000000u) fb = bar1;  // BAR1 is authoritative on VMware\\n    else if (fb < 0x1000000u) fb = bar1;\\n    if (pitch < w * 4) pitch = w * 4;\\n\\n    r.fb    = fb;\\n    r.pitch = pitch;\\n    r.ok    = (fb >= 0x1000000u);\\n    return r;\\n}\\n\\n\\n// Sticky click-edge latches for the guest mouse ABI (bochs_drivers.h's\\n// mouse_poll()). Set in the main loop below whenever mouse_left_down /\\n// mouse_right_down transitions from up to down; cleared only once\\n// kernel_gfx_mouse_poll() (defined further down, called from\\n// bochs_glue.cpp's bochs_guest_mouse_poll()) has actually reported the\\n// click to whichever guest program is the focused window's own ELF\\n// process. File scope (not a kernel_main local) because\\n// kernel_gfx_mouse_poll() needs to reach them from outside the loop.\\nstatic bool g_gfx_click_left_pending  = false;\\nstatic bool g_gfx_click_right_pending = false;\\n\\n// =============================================================================\\n// LAG FIX: pacing helpers -- TSC time base calibrated against PIT channel 2\\n// =============================================================================\\n// The old main loop derived its \\\"timer\\\" from a raw iteration counter\\n// (++poll_counter >= 500), so frame rate, repaint rate and guest CPU share\\n// all changed with host speed. There is no usable IRQ0 here, so we build a\\n// real-time base instead: calibrate the TSC once against PIT channel 2 (which\\n// is read by POLLING port 0x61, no interrupt needed) and pace everything off\\n// rdtsc afterwards.\\n//\\n// All 64-bit math below avoids division so a freestanding i386 build does not\\n// need __udivdi3 from libgcc.\\nstatic inline uint64_t ml_rdtsc() {\\n    uint32_t lo, hi;\\n    asm volatile(\\\"rdtsc\\\" : \\\"=a\\\"(lo), \\\"=d\\\"(hi));\\n    return ((uint64_t)hi << 32) | lo;\\n}\\nstatic inline void    ml_outb(uint16_t p, uint8_t v) { asm volatile(\\\"outb %0,%1\\\" :: \\\"a\\\"(v), \\\"Nd\\\"(p)); }\\nstatic inline uint8_t ml_inb (uint16_t p) { uint8_t v; asm volatile(\\\"inb %1,%0\\\" : \\\"=a\\\"(v) : \\\"Nd\\\"(p)); return v; }\\n\\nstatic uint32_t ml_tsc_per_ms = 2000000;   // fallback ~2 GHz if calibration fails\\n\\nstatic void ml_calibrate_tsc() {\\n    uint8_t g = ml_inb(0x61);\\n    ml_outb(0x61, g & 0xFC);                  // gate off, speaker off\\n    ml_outb(0x43, 0xB0);                      // ch2, lo/hi byte access, mode 0\\n    ml_outb(0x42, 0x9C); ml_outb(0x42, 0x2E); // 0x2E9C = 11932 ticks ~= 10 ms\\n    ml_outb(0x61, (g & 0xFC) | 0x01);         // gate on: start counting\\n    uint64_t t0 = ml_rdtsc();\\n    uint32_t guard = 0;\\n    bool ok = true;\\n    while (!(ml_inb(0x61) & 0x20)) {          // OUT2 goes high at terminal count\\n        if (++guard > 20000000u) { ok = false; break; }\\n    }\\n    uint64_t t1 = ml_rdtsc();\\n    ml_outb(0x61, g);                         // restore port 0x61\\n    uint64_t d = t1 - t0;\\n    if (ok && d > 100000ull && d < 0xFFFFFFFFull) ml_tsc_per_ms = (uint32_t)d / 10;\\n}\\n\\nextern \\\"C\\\" void kernel_main(uint32_t magic, uint32_t multiboot_addr) {\\n\\n    // \\u2500\\u2500 Verify Multiboot 1 magic FIRST, before any hardware probing \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n    // If GRUB (or whatever bootloader) didn't pass 0x2BADB002, we are running\\n    // under something that doesn't honour the contract \\u2014 bail out cleanly\\n    // instead of poking PCI / FB hardware on bad assumptions.\\n    if (magic != 0x2BADB002) {\\n        volatile uint16_t* vga = (volatile uint16_t*)0xB8000;\\n        vga[0] = 0x4F45; // 'E' on red \\u2014 \\\"bad magic\\\"\\n        for (;;)\\n            asm volatile(\\\"hlt\\\");\\n    }\\n\\n    // \\u2500\\u2500 Initialise heap \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n    g_allocator.init(kernel_heap, sizeof(kernel_heap));\\n\\n    // \\u2500\\u2500 Step 1: unconditionally try VMware SVGA II first. \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n    // This MUST happen before reading any framebuffer address because the\\n    // linear FB is not live until ENABLE=1 is written.\\n    SVGAResult svga = vmware_svga_init(1024, 768);\\n\\n    // \\u2500\\u2500 Step 2: determine framebuffer address \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n    multiboot_info* mbi = (multiboot_info*)multiboot_addr;\\n\\n    if (svga.ok) {\\n        // VMware SVGA II programmed successfully \\u2014 use its reported address.\\n        // Wait for VMware to complete the MemSpace re-registration after ENABLE=1.\\n        // The log shows VMware briefly unmaps/remaps 0xe8000000 during SVGA init;\\n        // a short spin ensures the mapping is live before we write pixels.\\n        for (volatile uint32_t i = 0; i < 5000000u; i++);\\n\\t\\t\\n        fb_info = { (uint32_t*)svga.fb, 1024, 768, svga.pitch };\\n    } else {\\n        // Not VMware (or SVGA II failed) \\u2014 use GRUB multiboot info directly.\\n        // Accept any type except 2 (EGA text). If bit 12 not set, fall back\\n        // to probing Bochs VBE ports, then hardcoded candidates.\\n        uint32_t fb_phys = 0, fb_w = 1024, fb_h = 768, fb_pitch = 1024*4;\\n\\n        if ((mbi->flags & (1u << 12)) && mbi->framebuffer_type != 2) {\\n            // GRUB filled the framebuffer fields (because boot.S requested\\n            // video mode via Multiboot1 FLAGS bit 2). Take its values\\n            // verbatim; this is the normal path on real BIOS + VMware-BIOS\\n            // and on UEFI through GRUB-EFI (which sets it up via GOP).\\n            fb_phys  = (uint32_t)(uintptr_t)mbi->framebuffer_addr;\\n            fb_w     = mbi->framebuffer_width  ? mbi->framebuffer_width  : 1024;\\n            fb_h     = mbi->framebuffer_height ? mbi->framebuffer_height : 768;\\n            fb_pitch = mbi->framebuffer_pitch  ? mbi->framebuffer_pitch  : fb_w*4;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (fb_phys < 0x1000000u) {\\n            // GRUB gave nothing useful \\u2014 try Bochs VBE then hardcoded\\n            probe_framebuffer(mbi, fb_phys, fb_w, fb_h, fb_pitch);\\n        }\\n\\n        fb_info = { (uint32_t*)(uintptr_t)fb_phys, fb_w, fb_h, fb_pitch };\\n    }\\n\\n    if (fb_info.width  == 0 || fb_info.width  > 1920) fb_info.width  = 1024;\\n    if (fb_info.height == 0 || fb_info.height > 1200) fb_info.height = 768;\\n    if (fb_info.pitch  < fb_info.width * 4) fb_info.pitch = fb_info.width * 4;\\n\\n    // Real-hardware-only perf fix: mark the linear framebuffer's physical\\n    // range Write-Combining via MTRR if at all possible (see the big\\n    // comment above setup_framebuffer_write_combining() in\\n    // 02_boot_info_and_graphics_driver.h for why this matters -- in short,\\n    // it's what makes every-frame swap_buffers() blits, and therefore\\n    // mouse tracking, not feel sluggish on real silicon). No-op under\\n    // VMware/emulation, where this was never the bottleneck.\\n    const char* g_fb_wc_status = setup_framebuffer_write_combining();\\n\\n    // \\u2500\\u2500 Step 3: commit and paint \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n    backbuffer = backbuffer_storage;\\n    g_gfx.init(false);\\n\\n    g_gfx.clear_screen(ColorPalette::DESKTOP_GRAY );\\n    swap_buffers();\\n\\n    // \\u2500\\u2500 Open first terminal window \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n    launch_new_terminal();\\n\\n    // Report whether the WC fix above actually took effect. This used to\\n    // fail silently, so \\\"everything on real hardware is slow -- typing,\\n    // opening windows, all of it\\\" had no visible cause: every one of\\n    // those triggers a full swap_buffers() blit, and if the framebuffer\\n    // is still stuck Uncacheable (no free MTRR slot is the common real-\\n    // hardware case -- firmware often claims all of them), that blit\\n    // stays 20-100x slower no matter what else gets optimized.\\n    {\\n        char wcmsg[128];\\n        vga_cat(wcmsg, \\\"Framebuffer write-combining: \\\", g_fb_wc_status, \\\"\\\\n\\\");\\n        wm.print_to_focused(wcmsg);\\n    }\\n\\n    // \\u2500\\u2500 PS/2 mouse \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n    ps2_flush_output_buffer();\\n    if (initialize_universal_mouse()) {\\n        wm.print_to_focused(\\\"Mouse: initialised.\\\\n\\\");\\n    } else {\\n        wm.print_to_focused(\\\"Mouse: init failed (keyboard-only mode).\\\\n\\\");\\n    }\\n\\n    // \\u2500\\u2500 AHCI disk + FAT32 \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n    disk_init();\\n    // Don't hardcode a port selection here \\u2014 disk_init() already\\n    // auto-selected the first port with an attached device. Hardcoding\\n    // \\\"1\\\" only worked on the QEMU command line in compile.md (which puts\\n    // the hard disk on ahci.1); bare-metal and VMware almost always have\\n    // the boot disk on port 0 and would have silently fallen back to no\\n    // selection. Pass \\\"\\\" to just list the detected ports for the user.\\n    cmd_list_and_select_disk(\\\"\\\");\\n\\n    if (ahci_base) {\\n        fat32_init();\\n        wm.print_to_focused(\\\"Disk: AHCI found.\\\\n\\\");\\n    } else {\\n        wm.print_to_focused(\\\"Disk: no AHCI controller.\\\\n\\\");\\n    }\\n    if (current_directory_cluster) {\\n        wm.load_desktop_items();\\n        if (extract_busybox_to_filesystem())\\n            wm.print_to_focused(\\\"BusyBox: saved to FAT32.\\\\n\\\");\\n        else\\n            wm.print_to_focused(\\\"BusyBox: ramdisk empty or write failed.\\\\n\\\");\\n        if (extract_hello_to_filesystem())\\n            wm.print_to_focused(\\\"hello: saved to FAT32.\\\\n\\\");\\n\\n        // Write the TCC guest ABI header so user programs can #include \\\"tcc.h\\\"\\n        // to get outb / kprint / kexit without redefining them.\\n        {\\n            fat_dir_entry_t tcc_hdr_entry;\\n            uint32_t th_sec = 0, th_off = 0;\\n            if (fat32_find_entry(\\\"tcc.h\\\", &tcc_hdr_entry, &th_sec, &th_off) != 0) {\\n                static const char tcc_h[] =\\n                    \\\"/* tcc.h \\u2014 guest ABI for in-kernel TCC programs */\\\\n\\\"\\n                    \\\"#ifndef TCC_H\\\\n#define TCC_H\\\\n\\\"\\n                    \\\"static inline void outb(unsigned short p, unsigned char v) {\\\\n\\\"\\n                    \\\"    __asm__ volatile(\\\\\\\"outb %0,%1\\\\\\\"::\\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\"(v),\\\\\\\"Nd\\\\\\\"(p)); }\\\\n\\\"\\n                    \\\"static inline void kprint(const char* s) {\\\\n\\\"\\n                    \\\"    while (*s) outb(0xE9, *s++); }\\\\n\\\"\\n                    \\\"static inline void kexit(int c) {\\\\n\\\"\\n                    \\\"    outb(0xE8, (unsigned char)c); }\\\\n\\\"\\n                    \\\"#endif\\\\n\\\";\\n                fat32_write_file(\\\"tcc.h\\\", tcc_h, (uint32_t)(sizeof(tcc_h) - 1));\\n            }\\n        }\\n    } else {\\n        wm.print_to_focused(\\\"FAT32: not initialised.\\\\n\\\");\\n    }\\n\\n    // \\u2500\\u2500 Bochs CPU / ELF subsystem \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n    //\\n    // Run the file-scope C++ constructors NOW, before init_elf_system()\\n    // and before any code can reach a Bochs entry point. boot.S does not\\n    // walk __init_array, so without this the Bochs core objects (bx_cpu,\\n    // bx_mem, the CPUID param objects, icache's pageWriteStampTable, ...)\\n    // stay as zero-filled BSS with null vtables. The first ELF launched\\n    // in the Bochs emulator window would then call bochs_cpu_init() ->\\n    // BX_CPU(0)->initialize() against those null vtables and fault out on\\n    // its very first tick \\u2014 the \\\"first-time crash / autoclose\\\" symptom.\\n    kernel_run_global_ctors_once();\\n\\n    // The constructors have now run exactly once. Tell the test module so\\n    // its own run_init_array_once() inside test_module_run() becomes a\\n    // no-op \\u2014 otherwise the first `test` command would re-run every ctor\\n    // a second time, re-constructing bx_cpu / bx_mem on top of live\\n    // (already-initialised) Bochs state.\\n    test_module_mark_ctors_done();\\n\\n    init_elf_system();\\n\\n    vga_status(\\\"Init complete - entering main loop\\\", 0x0A);\\n\\n    // \\u2500\\u2500 Main loop \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n    // LAG FIX: fully rewritten. See the notes at each numbered stage.\\n    //\\n    // Root causes fixed here:\\n    //  * Pacing came from a loop-iteration counter, not real time, so frame\\n    //    rate / repaint rate / guest CPU share all varied with host speed and\\n    //    the timer's g_evt_dirty forced full-desktop repaints far too often.\\n    //  * Click edges and mouse motion seen by poll_input_universal() INSIDE\\n    //    the guest sub-tick loop were never accumulated, so they were lost\\n    //    (and prev_mouse_x/y were overwritten there) -> handle_input() was\\n    //    skipped and drags / clicks stalled while a program ran.\\n    //  * The guest only got CPU on the slow software timer.\\n    //  * Nothing capped repaints (held buttons, chatty guest output).\\n    //  * cleanup_closed_windows() ran on every spin of the loop.\\n    ml_calibrate_tsc();\\n\\n    const uint64_t FRAME_TSC = (uint64_t)ml_tsc_per_ms * 16;    // ~60 Hz paint / tick cap\\n    const uint64_t GUEST_TSC = (uint64_t)ml_tsc_per_ms * 6;     // max guest time per pass\\n    const uint64_t CLOCK_TSC = (uint64_t)ml_tsc_per_ms * 1000;  // idle refresh (taskbar clock)\\n\\n    uint64_t last_paint = 0;\\n    uint64_t last_tick  = ml_rdtsc();\\n    uint64_t last_clock = last_tick;\\n\\n    // Input state: edges/motion are accumulated by poll_and_latch() no matter\\n    // WHERE polling happens (main loop or inside the guest slice), then\\n    // consumed once per pass, so nothing is lost while a guest is running.\\n    bool prev_left = mouse_left_down, prev_right = mouse_right_down;\\n    int  seen_x = mouse_x, seen_y = mouse_y;\\n    bool acc_lclick = false, acc_rclick = false, acc_moved = false;\\n\\n    // Where the cursor glyph currently sits on the live framebuffer.\\n    int  drawn_x = -1, drawn_y = -1;\\n\\n    auto poll_and_latch = [&]() {\\n        poll_input_universal();\\n        if (mouse_left_down  && !prev_left)  { acc_lclick = true; g_gfx_click_left_pending  = true; }\\n        if (mouse_right_down && !prev_right) { acc_rclick = true; g_gfx_click_right_pending = true; }\\n        prev_left  = mouse_left_down;\\n        prev_right = mouse_right_down;\\n        if (mouse_x != seen_x || mouse_y != seen_y) {\\n            acc_moved = true; seen_x = mouse_x; seen_y = mouse_y;\\n        }\\n    };\\n\\n    // Cheap cursor-only update straight on the framebuffer.\\n    auto fast_cursor = [&]() {\\n        if (g_backbuffer_is_clean_on_screen && !mouse_left_down && !mouse_right_down &&\\n            (mouse_x != drawn_x || mouse_y != drawn_y)) {\\n            erase_cursor_from_screen();\\n            draw_cursor_to_screen(mouse_x, mouse_y, ColorPalette::CURSOR_WHITE);\\n            drawn_x = mouse_x; drawn_y = mouse_y;\\n        }\\n    };\\n\\n    // A guest is \\\"runnable\\\" if it's alive and not parked waiting on stdin.\\n    auto any_runnable = [&]() -> bool {\\n        for (int i = 0; i < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES; ++i) {\\n            ElfProcess& p = elf_processes[i];\\n            if (p.active && !p.completed && !(p.waiting_for_input && in_empty(i))) return true;\\n        }\\n        return false;\\n    };\\n\\n    // Force an immediate first render.\\n    g_evt_timer = true;\\n    g_evt_dirty = true;\\n\\n    // Spinning heartbeat at VGA column 79, row 0 (green)\\n    volatile uint16_t* vga_hb = (volatile uint16_t*)(0xB8000 + 2*79);\\n    uint32_t hb_counter = 0;\\n    const char hb_chars[] = \\\"|/-\\\\\\\\\\\";\\n\\n    for (;;) {\\n        if ((++hb_counter & 0x3FFF) == 0)\\n            *vga_hb = (uint16_t)(0x0A00u | (uint8_t)hb_chars[(hb_counter >> 14) & 3]);\\n\\n        // \\u2500\\u2500 1. Input (edges + motion accumulated, then consumed) \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n        poll_and_latch();\\n        bool leftClickedThisFrame  = acc_lclick;\\n        bool rightClickedThisFrame = acc_rclick;\\n        bool mouse_moved           = acc_moved;\\n        acc_lclick = acc_rclick = acc_moved = false;\\n        bool key_pressed = (last_key_press != 0);\\n\\n        // Anything that can change what's on screen beyond the cursor's own\\n        // position: a key, a fresh click edge, or a button being held\\n        // (drag/resize/paint in progress). A plain hover-move is NOT included\\n        // -- it takes the cursor-only fast path instead of a full repaint.\\n        bool input_needs_full_repaint = key_pressed || leftClickedThisFrame ||\\n                                        rightClickedThisFrame || mouse_left_down ||\\n                                        mouse_right_down;\\n\\n        if (key_pressed || mouse_moved || leftClickedThisFrame || rightClickedThisFrame) {\\n            g_evt_input = true;\\n            if (input_needs_full_repaint) g_input_state.hasNewInput = true;\\n        }\\n\\n        // Route keypress to the FOCUSED window's ELF slot only. If that slot\\n        // isn't waiting for input, fall through to wm.handle_input, which\\n        // threads it to whichever terminal's captured_elf_slot was clicked.\\n        if (last_key_press != 0) {\\n            int fs = wm.get_focused_elf_slot();\\n            if (fs >= 0 && fs < MAX_ELF_PROCESSES &&\\n                elf_processes[fs].active && elf_processes[fs].waiting_for_input) {\\n                push_input(fs, last_key_press);\\n                elf_processes[fs].waiting_for_input = false;\\n                last_key_press = 0;\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        bool handled_input = false;\\n        if (g_evt_input) {\\n            g_evt_input = false;\\n            handled_input = true;\\n            wm.handle_input(last_key_press, mouse_x, mouse_y,\\n                            mouse_left_down,\\n                            leftClickedThisFrame,\\n                            rightClickedThisFrame);\\n            if (last_key_press != 0) last_key_press = 0;\\n            if (input_needs_full_repaint) g_evt_dirty = true;\\n        }\\n\\n        // \\u2500\\u2500 2. Real-time timebase (replaces the 500-iteration software timer) \\u2500\\n        uint64_t now = ml_rdtsc();\\n        bool frame_due = false;\\n        if (now - last_tick >= FRAME_TSC) {\\n            last_tick = now;                 // = now, not += : no catch-up bursts\\n            g_timer_ticks++;                 // now advances at ~60 Hz\\n            g_evt_timer = true;\\n            frame_due = true;\\n        }\\n        if (now - last_clock >= CLOCK_TSC) { // idle refresh: taskbar clock, ~1 Hz\\n            last_clock = now;\\n            g_evt_dirty = true;\\n        }\\n\\n        if (handled_input || frame_due) wm.cleanup_closed_windows();\\n\\n        // \\u2500\\u2500 3. Guest slice: time-boxed; polls input + moves cursor between ticks\\n        // Tick ELF processes BEFORE the paint so breadcrumbs they write are\\n        // reflected in the next swap_buffers.\\n        bool ran_guest = false;\\n        if (any_runnable()) {\\n            ran_guest = true;\\n            uint64_t slice_start = now;\\n            do {\\n                tick_elf_processes(1);\\n                poll_and_latch();\\n                fast_cursor();\\n            } while (ml_rdtsc() - slice_start < GUEST_TSC && any_runnable());\\n            if (frame_due) g_evt_dirty = true;   // guest may have redrawn its canvas\\n        }\\n        if (frame_due) g_evt_timer = false;\\n\\n        // \\u2500\\u2500 4. Paint: only when dirty, at most once per FRAME_TSC \\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\n        // A deferred paint keeps its dirty flag and happens on a later pass.\\n        now = ml_rdtsc();\\n        bool want_paint = g_evt_dirty || g_input_state.hasNewInput;\\n        if (want_paint && (now - last_paint) >= FRAME_TSC) {\\n            g_evt_dirty               = false;\\n            g_input_state.hasNewInput = false;\\n            g_gfx.clear_screen(ColorPalette::DESKTOP_GRAY);\\n            wm.update_all();\\n            // Diagnostic overlay: paint VGA text-mode rows 0/1/2\\n            // (boot/panic/tick breadcrumbs, host-IDT fault tags,\\n            // x86_tick lazy-init progress) onto the framebuffer.\\n            draw_vga_overlay();\\n            swap_buffers();\\n            // The pushed backbuffer has no cursor in it; draw the glyph\\n            // straight onto the framebuffer so the next plain pointer move\\n            // can skip the full repaint.\\n            erase_cursor_from_screen(); // no-op the first time through\\n            draw_cursor_to_screen(mouse_x, mouse_y, ColorPalette::CURSOR_WHITE);\\n            drawn_x = mouse_x; drawn_y = mouse_y;\\n            g_backbuffer_is_clean_on_screen = true;\\n            last_paint = ml_rdtsc();\\n        } else {\\n            // Nothing to paint (or paint deferred to hold 60 Hz): keep the\\n            // pointer responsive with the cheap path.\\n            fast_cursor();\\n            if (!want_paint && !ran_guest && !handled_input)\\n                asm volatile(\\\"pause\\\");   // idle: be kind to the (virtual) CPU\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n// =============================================================================\\n// extern \\\"C\\\" bridges for tcc_kernel.cpp\\n// =============================================================================\\n// tcc_kernel.cpp is compiled as freestanding C++ and cannot include the full\\n// kernel headers. These thin wrappers expose the symbols it needs with plain\\n// C linkage so the linker resolves them without name-mangling.\\n\\nextern \\\"C\\\" {\\n\\n// Bridge for bochs_glue.cpp's bochs_guest_mouse_poll() (guest port\\n// 0xEF, see bochs_drivers.h's mouse_poll()). Reports the compositor's\\n// cursor to ELF slot `slot` ONLY if that slot's window is BOTH the\\n// currently focused window AND showing a live gfx canvas the cursor\\n// happens to be over \\u2014 everything else (titlebar drags, clicks on\\n// other windows, the desktop, the taskbar) is handled entirely by\\n// WindowManager::handle_input() above and never touches this path at\\n// all, which is what keeps those clicks going to the compositor only.\\n// On any \\\"no\\\" (wrong/no slot, not focused, no window, not in gfx\\n// mode, cursor outside the canvas) this leaves *out_x/*out_y/\\n// *out_buttons at their caller-supplied zero and returns false \\u2014 a\\n// clean \\\"cursor absent\\\" snapshot rather than stale or foreign data.\\nbool kernel_gfx_mouse_poll(int slot, int* out_x, int* out_y,\\n                            unsigned char* out_buttons) {\\n    if (slot < 0 || wm.get_focused_elf_slot() != slot) return false;\\n\\n    Window* win = wm.find_window_by_elf_slot(slot);\\n    if (!win) return false;\\n\\n    int lx = 0, ly = 0;\\n    bool in_win = win->gfx_hit_test(mouse_x, mouse_y, &lx, &ly);\\n    if (in_win) {\\n        *out_x = lx;\\n        *out_y = ly;\\n        *out_buttons |= 0x10; // MOUSE_BIT_IN_WINDOW (bochs_drivers.h)\\n    }\\n    if (mouse_left_down)  *out_buttons |= 0x01; // MOUSE_BIT_LEFT_DOWN\\n    if (mouse_right_down) *out_buttons |= 0x02; // MOUSE_BIT_RIGHT_DOWN\\n    if (g_gfx_click_left_pending) {\\n        *out_buttons |= 0x04; // MOUSE_BIT_LEFT_CLICKED\\n        g_gfx_click_left_pending = false;\\n    }\\n    if (g_gfx_click_right_pending) {\\n        *out_buttons |= 0x08; // MOUSE_BIT_RIGHT_CLICKED\\n        g_gfx_click_right_pending = false;\\n    }\\n    return true;\\n}\\n\\nvoid tcc_bridge_console_print(const char* s) {\\n    console_print(s);\\n}\\nstatic inline bool is_cc_safe_char(unsigned char c) {\\n    return c == '\\\\n' || c == '\\\\r' || c == '\\\\t' || (c >= 32 && c != 127);\\n}\\n\\nchar* tcc_bridge_fat32_read(const char* filename) {\\n    // Path-aware: fat32_read_file_as_string() only ever looks in the\\n    // current directory and treats any '/' as part of a garbage flat\\n    // name. fat32_read_file_as_string_path() falls back to that exact\\n    // behavior when there's no '/' in filename, and otherwise resolves\\n    // the directory part first -- needed both for `cc sub/foo.c` and for\\n    // #include \\\"sub/foo.h\\\" (this same function is what TCC's open()\\n    // shim calls for every #include, via the fat32_read_file_as_string\\n    // macro alias in tcc_kernel.cpp).\\n    char *data = fat32_read_file_as_string_path(filename);\\n\\n    if (!data) {\\n        return NULL;\\n    }\\n\\n    for (char *p = data; *p; ++p) {\\n        if (!is_cc_safe_char((unsigned char)*p)) {\\n            // FIX: this used to return NULL here without freeing `data`,\\n            // leaking the whole file's buffer every time a source file\\n            // or\n\u2026[truncated \u2014 66067 chars total]"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:53:27.617418Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:53:27.617454Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01GV7WgFnd8of3rT7qXacS2q", "name": "create_file", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "File created successfully: /mnt/user-data/outputs/11_kernel_main.h"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "File created successfully: /mnt/user-data/outputs/11_kernel_main.h"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:53:30.167649Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:53:30.417501Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_019eKcugzQCjBnfhdG3X82un", "name": "present_files", "input": {"filepaths": ["/mnt/user-data/outputs/11_kernel_main.h"]}, "message": "Presenting file(s)...", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "table", "table": [["filepaths", "['/mnt/user-data/outputs/11_kernel_main.h']"]]}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:53:30.444362Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:53:30.444398Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_019eKcugzQCjBnfhdG3X82un", "name": "present_files", "content": [{"type": "local_resource", "file_path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/11_kernel_main.h", "name": "11 kernel main", "mime_type": "text/x-chdr", "artifact_publishable": false}, {"type": "text", "text": "The file cards appear at the end of your reply if it ends with text, otherwise under this step; say they are below only if you finish the reply with text."}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": "Presented file", "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:53:34.790221Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T08:53:34.808920Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "The full `11_kernel_main.h` is in the file card below. It's a drop-in replacement for `kernel_parts/11_kernel_main.h`. I haven't compiled it against your tree.\n\nOnly two places differ from your original:\n\n1. **New pacing helpers** just above `kernel_main`: `ml_rdtsc`, `ml_outb`, `ml_inb`, `ml_calibrate_tsc`.\n2. **The main loop** in `kernel_main`, from `// \u2500\u2500 Main loop \u2500\u2500` to the end of the `for (;;)`.\n\nEverything else is unchanged, including the ELF loader, `x86_tick`, `tick_elf_processes`, the framebuffer probing, and the TCC bridges. I left the existing `ELFHEAPSIZE` versus `ELF_HEAP_SIZE` mismatch in `x86_tick` alone, since it's outside the loop. If it compiles now, both names are probably defined, but it's worth confirming.\n\nTwo things to check after building:\n- `g_timer_ticks` now advances at about 60 Hz instead of once per 500 loop iterations. Grep for its other uses (the taskbar clock, for example) in case any of them assumed the old rate.\n- If TSC calibration fails on some VM, it falls back to an assumed 2 GHz. Pacing would then be a bit off but won't hang.", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-28T08:53:34.900500Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-28T08:53:34.900500Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0e736-45be-7f61-80af-bc20435d3977"}]},{"uuid": "3cc05e4f-18e5-4369-a6fd-0f513bb84789", "name": "N-gram language model with temporal index", "summary": "", "created_at": "2026-09-28T22:32:43.080677Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-29T09:27:40.074971Z", "account": {"uuid": "8d7882cd-3e98-47cc-a6e7-61494a1ee4ac"}, "chat_messages": [{"uuid": "01a0ea26-25ea-7746-864a-0feeb0bb7f9a", "text": "import argparse\nimport glob\nimport math\nimport re\nimport sys\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport scipy.sparse as sp\nfrom scipy.linalg import expm\n\nUNK = \"<unk>\"\nBOS = \"<bos>\"\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 1. Dataset loading (unchanged \u2014 no construct in the paper maps to this)\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef load_corpus(paths=None, raw_text=None, lowercase=True, strip_punct=False):\n    text_chunks = []\n    if paths:\n        found_any = False\n        for pattern in paths:\n            for fp in sorted(glob.glob(pattern)):\n                found_any = True\n                with open(fp, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\") as f:\n                    text_chunks.append(f.read())\n        if not found_any:\n            print(f\"[warn] no files matched {paths}\", file=sys.stderr)\n\n    if raw_text:\n        text_chunks.append(raw_text)\n    if not text_chunks:\n        text_chunks.append(_DEMO_CORPUS)\n\n    full_text = \"\\n\".join(text_chunks)\n    if lowercase:\n        full_text = full_text.lower()\n\n    tokens = re.findall(r\"[a-z0-9']+|[.,!?;:]\", full_text)\n    if strip_punct:\n        tokens = [t for t in tokens if re.match(r\"[a-z0-9']+\", t)]\n    return tokens\n\n\nwith open(input(\"Filename: \"), \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    _DEMO_CORPUS = file.read()\n\n\ndef build_vocab(tokens, min_count=1):\n    counts = Counter(tokens)\n    kept = sorted([w for w, c in counts.items() if c >= min_count])\n    vocab = [UNK, BOS] + kept\n    word_to_idx = {w: i for i, w in enumerate(vocab)}\n    return vocab, word_to_idx, counts\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 2. The membrane: context-state construction\n#\n#    Exchange: \"topological energy membrane separating internal state space\n#    from environment\" -> the boundary crossed when the Markov chain moves\n#    from one context-state to the next. d-Omega is not a spatial surface;\n#    it is the transition itself. Quanta N are tokens: one per crossing,\n#    genuinely indivisible (the tokenizer's vocabulary IS a discrete set),\n#    which is the one place in the whole exercise where \"quanta\" is not a\n#    metaphor borrowed from QM \u2014 it is just an accurate word for \"discrete\n#    countable unit,\" which is what a token already was.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef build_membrane_transition_graph(tokens, word_to_idx, order=3):\n    \"\"\"Build the square context -> context transition count matrix. This\n    IS the membrane: d-Omega = the set of (context_state -> context_state)\n    edges; a single generation step = one crossing of d-Omega; the emitted\n    word at that step = one quantum.\n\n    Renamed from build_ngram_contexts(). Same function, same sparse-matrix\n    justification: a dense (num_contexts x num_contexts) \"membrane\" at\n    real corpus scale is gigabytes to hundreds of gigabytes, which is\n    exactly the mass-density blowup discussed in build_mass_density_term()\n    below \u2014 the membrane and the mass term are two names for adjacent\n    facts about the same object, not two separate mechanisms.\n    \"\"\"\n    ctx_len = order - 1\n    if ctx_len < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"order must be >= 2\")\n\n    bos_idx = word_to_idx[BOS]\n    unk_idx = word_to_idx[UNK]\n    ids = [word_to_idx.get(t, unk_idx) for t in tokens]\n    padded = [bos_idx] * ctx_len + ids\n\n    context_to_idx = {}\n    contexts = []\n    context_counts = Counter()\n    edges = Counter()\n\n    def get_ctx_idx(ctx_tuple):\n        if ctx_tuple not in context_to_idx:\n            context_to_idx[ctx_tuple] = len(contexts)\n            contexts.append(ctx_tuple)\n        return context_to_idx[ctx_tuple]\n\n    n = len(padded)\n    for i in range(n - ctx_len):\n        ctx = tuple(padded[i:i + ctx_len])\n        next_ctx = tuple(padded[i + 1:i + 1 + ctx_len])\n        ci = get_ctx_idx(ctx)\n        cj = get_ctx_idx(next_ctx)\n        context_counts[ctx] += 1\n        edges[(ci, cj)] += 1.0\n\n    m = len(contexts)\n    if edges:\n        rows, cols, data = zip(*[(r, c, v) for (r, c), v in edges.items()])\n    else:\n        rows, cols, data = (), (), ()\n    membrane = sp.csr_matrix((data, (rows, cols)), shape=(m, m), dtype=np.float64)\n\n    return contexts, context_to_idx, membrane, context_counts\n\n\ndef log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts):\n    def key(ctx):\n        c = context_counts.get(ctx, 0)\n        return -math.log(c + 1.0)\n    return sorted(range(len(contexts)), key=lambda i: key(contexts[i]))\n\n\ndef permute_matrix(M, order):\n    order = np.asarray(order)\n    if sp.issparse(M):\n        return M[order, :][:, order].tocsr()\n    return M[np.ix_(order, order)]\n\n\ndef membrane_flux_normalize(M, eps=1e-9):\n    \"\"\"Row-normalize the membrane's transition weights into a proper\n    conservation law: total probability flux leaving any context-state\n    across d-Omega sums to 1. This is the literal, enforced version of the\n    paper's heuristic surface integral N = oint_dOmega sigma(x) dx \u2014 not\n    an analogy to it, an instance of it, since a row-stochastic matrix row\n    IS a discrete measure over the boundary that sums to a fixed quantum\n    budget per step.\n\n    Renamed from row_normalize(). Behavior unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    if sp.issparse(M):\n        row_sums = np.asarray(M.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n        row_sums[row_sums == 0] = 1.0\n        inv = sp.diags(1.0 / row_sums)\n        return (inv @ M).tocsr()\n    row_sums = M.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\n    row_sums[row_sums == 0] = 1.0\n    return M / (row_sums + eps)\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 3. Mass-density coupling term\n#\n#    Exchange: rho_eff (effective mass-density, coupled to retrospective\n#    orientation) -> the memory footprint and O(n^2)-to-O(n^3) compute cost\n#    of diffusing probability mass across the membrane. This is the ONE\n#    construct in the original paper that upgrades from metaphor to fact\n#    when ported to code: \"retrospection carries more computational mass\"\n#    stops being poetic and becomes a literal, measurable claim about\n#    bytes and FLOPs, which is exactly what the original file's own\n#    docstring already said before any relabeling (\"137,702 contexts ->\n#    ~141 GiB\").\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef build_mass_density_term(P, beta=0.15, remove_self_loops=True):\n    \"\"\"expm(beta * (P - I)): the diffusion/propagation step. In the paper's\n    vocabulary this is where mass-density is \"felt\" \u2014 it is the most\n    expensive operation in the pipeline (O(n^3) for a state space of size\n    n), and its cost is precisely what rho_eff = M(t)/|Omega_active(t)|\n    measures. A system leaning harder on this term (more diffusion, less\n    raw/verbatim retrospective lookup) really does carry more\n    computational mass: more memory, more latency, more bandwidth.\n\n    Renamed from diffusion_weighting(). Behavior unchanged, including the\n    self-loop caveat: exp(-beta) concentrates on the diagonal for small\n    beta, which would make generation stick to one context-state forever\n    (i.e., infinite effective mass, zero motion) unless removed.\n    \"\"\"\n    m = P.shape[0]\n    L = P - np.eye(m)\n    D = expm(beta * L)\n    D = np.clip(D, 0, None)\n\n    if remove_self_loops:\n        np.fill_diagonal(D, 0.0)\n        row_sums = D.sum(axis=1)\n        dead_rows = row_sums <= 1e-12\n        if np.any(dead_rows):\n            fallback = P.copy()\n            np.fill_diagonal(fallback, 0.0)\n            fb_sums = fallback.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\n            fb_sums[fb_sums == 0] = 1.0\n            fallback = fallback / fb_sums\n            D[dead_rows] = fallback[dead_rows]\n\n    D = membrane_flux_normalize(D)\n    return D\n\n\ndef blend_temporal_orientation(P_retrospective, P_diffused, alpha=0.5,\n                                remove_self_loops=True):\n    \"\"\"Blend the purely retrospective (raw n-gram, tau = -1) distribution\n    with the mass-density-diffused one, controlled by alpha. alpha is the\n    closest thing in this codebase to a dial on tau: alpha=0 is maximally\n    retrospective (pure lookup), alpha=1 is maximally diffused (mass-heavy,\n    still not \"anticipatory\" in any real sense \u2014 see the note in\n    compute_temporal_index() below about why diffusion isn't lookahead).\n\n    Renamed from blend(). Behavior unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    P = (1 - alpha) * P_retrospective * alpha * P_diffused\n    if remove_self_loops:\n        np.fill_diagonal(P, 0.0)\n        P = membrane_flux_normalize(P)\n    return P\n\n\ndef compute_mass_density(num_states, dtype_bytes=8):\n    \"\"\"rho_eff = M(t) / |Omega_active(t)|, made concrete: bytes required\n    for a DENSE membrane of this many context-states, divided by the\n    number of active states. This is the real, computable version of the\n    coupling constant lambda from the original speculative paper \u2014 no\n    free parameter, just arithmetic.\n    \"\"\"\n    dense_bytes = (num_states ** 2) * dtype_bytes\n    rho_eff = dense_bytes / max(num_states, 1)\n    return dense_bytes, rho_eff\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 4. Temporal index computation\n#\n#    Exchange: tau (subjective temporal orientation) -> fraction of\n#    per-step decision compute spent on the payload lookahead-rerank\n#    (prospective: scoring a word BEFORE it's committed) versus the raw\n#    context lookup (retrospective: reading what was already observed).\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef compute_temporal_index(boost_strength, lookahead_ops=1, retrospective_ops=1):\n    \"\"\"tau = (C_lookahead - C_retrospective) / (C_lookahead + C_retrospective),\n    gated by whether the payload customizer is active at all.\n\n    With boost_strength == 0, the payload rerank never executes (see\n    sample_at_boundary below), so tau = -1 exactly: this generator is\n    purely retrospective, same as any causal-masked autoregressive model.\n\n    With boost_strength > 0, the payload dot-product IS a one-step\n    lookahead \u2014 it scores the word that would be newly introduced by a\n    candidate transition before that transition is taken \u2014 so tau ticks\n    positive by an amount proportional to how much compute that rerank\n    consumes relative to the base lookup. This is a real, bounded,\n    non-metaphorical number, not a stand-in for felt anticipation.\n\n    Note on the diffusion term: build_mass_density_term() does NOT count\n    as lookahead, even though it's a \"future-looking\" propagator in the\n    continuous-time-Markov sense. It diffuses probability mass over\n    ALREADY-OBSERVED transitions; it doesn't evaluate not-yet-taken\n    candidate words the way the payload rerank does. Mass-density and\n    temporal index are independent axes here, exactly as the original\n    paper treated rho_eff and tau as coupled-but-distinct variables.\n    \"\"\"\n    if boost_strength == 0.0:\n        return -1.0\n    total = lookahead_ops + retrospective_ops\n    return (boost_strength * lookahead_ops - retrospective_ops) / (\n        boost_strength * lookahead_ops + retrospective_ops\n    )\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 5. Sinusoidal temperature \u2014 kept under its plain name\n#\n#    This is the one function this file will NOT rename into the paper's\n#    \"harmonic resonance\" vocabulary. See module docstring.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef sinusoidal_temperature(step, base_temp=0.8, amplitude=0.4, omega=0.5, phase=0.0, floor=0.05):\n    t = base_temp + amplitude * math.sin(omega * step + phase)\n    return max(floor, t)\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 6. Sampling: where the decision operator D acts on the rule R\n#\n#    Exchange: \"decisions precede the rule-set\" -> D narrows/reranks the\n#    ALREADY-LAWFUL candidate set (nonzero-probability transitions); it\n#    can never make a zero-probability transition eligible. R (the\n#    observed n-gram law, P_final) is never edited by D. This is the\n#    weak, defensible reading of the inversion, not the strong claim that\n#    decisions rewrite physics.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef sample_at_boundary(probs, temperature=1.0, top_k=10, rng=None,\n                        lookahead_bias=None, boost_strength=0.0):\n    \"\"\"Cross d-Omega once: pick the next context-state (i.e. emit one\n    quantum). `lookahead_bias` is the decision operator D's input;\n    `probs` (P_final's row) is the rule R. D can only rerank within\n    nonzero_idx \u2014 the domain R already permits \u2014 never expand it.\n\n    Renamed from sample_next(). Behavior unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    rng = rng or np.random.default_rng()\n    probs = np.asarray(probs, dtype=np.float64)\n\n    nonzero_idx = np.nonzero(probs > 0)[0]\n    if len(nonzero_idx) == 0:\n        return None\n\n    if top_k is not None and 0 < top_k < len(nonzero_idx):\n        sub = np.argpartition(probs[nonzero_idx], -top_k)[-top_k:]\n        top_idx = nonzero_idx[sub]\n    else:\n        top_idx = nonzero_idx\n\n    top_probs = probs[top_idx]\n    logits = np.log(top_probs) / max(temperature, 1e-6)\n    if lookahead_bias is not None and boost_strength != 0.0:\n        logits = logits + boost_strength * np.asarray(lookahead_bias)[top_idx]\n    logits -= logits.max()\n    weights = np.exp(logits)\n    weights /= weights.sum()\n\n    choice = rng.choice(len(top_idx), p=weights)\n    return top_idx[choice]\n\n\ndef parse_payload(payload_str, word_to_idx):\n    vocab_size = len(word_to_idx)\n    payload_vector = np.zeros(vocab_size, dtype=np.float64)\n    if not payload_str:\n        return payload_vector\n    for item in payload_str.split(\",\"):\n        item = item.strip()\n        if not item:\n            continue\n        if \":\" in item:\n            word, weight_str = item.rsplit(\":\", 1)\n            try:\n                weight = float(weight_str)\n            except ValueError:\n                print(f\"[warn] payload entry '{item}' has a non-numeric weight; skipping\", file=sys.stderr)\n                continue\n        else:\n            word, weight = item, 1.0\n        word = word.strip().lower()\n        idx = word_to_idx.get(word)\n        if idx is None:\n            print(f\"[warn] payload word '{word}' is not in the vocabulary; skipping\", file=sys.stderr)\n            continue\n        payload_vector[idx] = weight\n    return payload_vector\n\n\nclass HECMModel:\n    \"\"\"Same model as before. Internally, the membrane, mass-density term,\n    and temporal index are now named as such and exposed as attributes\n    (self.rho_eff, self.tau) so they can be inspected directly rather than\n    inferred from comments.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, tokens, order=3, min_count=1, beta=0.0, alpha=0.0,\n                 max_diffusion_states=1500, payload=None, boost_strength=0.0):\n        self.order = order\n        vocab, word_to_idx, counts = build_vocab(tokens, min_count=min_count)\n        self.vocab = vocab\n        self.word_to_idx = word_to_idx\n\n        contexts, context_to_idx, membrane, context_counts = build_membrane_transition_graph(\n            tokens, word_to_idx, order=order\n        )\n\n        perm = log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts)\n        contexts = [contexts[i] for i in perm]\n        membrane = permute_matrix(membrane, perm)\n        context_to_idx = {ctx: i for i, ctx in enumerate(contexts)}\n\n        self.contexts = contexts\n        self.context_to_idx = context_to_idx\n        self.context_counts = context_counts\n\n        self.P_retrospective = membrane_flux_normalize(membrane)\n\n        num_states = len(contexts)\n        self.dense_bytes, self.rho_eff = compute_mass_density(num_states)\n\n        self.P_diffused = self.P_retrospective\n        self.P_final = self.P_retrospective\n        \n\n        self.boost_strength = boost_strength\n        self.tau = compute_temporal_index(boost_strength)\n\n        if payload is None:\n            payload = np.zeros(len(vocab), dtype=np.float64)\n        self.payload = payload\n        self.context_word_score = np.array(\n            [sum(payload[w] for w in ctx) for ctx in contexts], dtype=np.float64\n        )\n        self.introduced_word_score = np.array(\n            [payload[ctx[-1]] for ctx in contexts], dtype=np.float64\n        )\n        self.context_total_score = self.context_word_score + 0.0\n\n    def vocab_size(self):\n        return len(self.vocab)\n\n    def num_states(self):\n        return len(self.contexts)\n\n    def _get_row(self, ctx_idx):\n        row = self.P_final[ctx_idx]\n        if sp.issparse(self.P_final):\n            return np.asarray(row.todense()).ravel()\n        return np.asarray(row)\n\n    def _tokenize_prompt(self, prompt):\n        toks = re.findall(r\"[a-z0-9']+|[.,!?;:]\", prompt.lower())\n        unk = self.word_to_idx[UNK]\n        return [self.word_to_idx.get(t, unk) for t in toks]\n\n    def _weighted_pick(self, candidates, boost_strength, rng):\n        if boost_strength == 0.0 or len(candidates) == 1:\n            return candidates[rng.integers(len(candidates))] if len(candidates) > 1 else candidates[0]\n        scores = boost_strength * self.context_total_score[candidates]\n        scores = scores - scores.max()\n        weights = np.exp(scores)\n        weights /= weights.sum()\n        return candidates[rng.choice(len(candidates), p=weights)]\n\n    def _initial_context(self, prompt_ids, rng, boost_strength=0.0):\n        ctx_len = self.order - 1\n        bos = self.word_to_idx[BOS]\n        padded = [bos] * ctx_len + prompt_ids\n        desired = tuple(padded[-ctx_len:])\n\n        if desired in self.context_to_idx:\n            return self.context_to_idx[desired]\n\n        for k in range(ctx_len - 1, 0, -1):\n            suffix = desired[-k:]\n            candidates = [i for i, ctx in enumerate(self.contexts) if ctx[-k:] == suffix]\n            if candidates:\n                return self._weighted_pick(candidates, boost_strength, rng)\n\n        counts_arr = np.array([self.context_counts.get(ctx, 0) for ctx in self.contexts])\n        top_candidates = list(np.argsort(counts_arr)[-10:])\n        return self._weighted_pick(top_candidates, boost_strength, rng)\n\n    def generate(self, prompt, max_new_tokens=40, top_k=10, base_temp=0.8,\n                 amplitude=0.4, omega=0.5, phase=0.0, seed=None,\n                 boost_strength=0.0):\n        rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n        prompt_ids = self._tokenize_prompt(prompt)\n\n        ctx_idx = self._initial_context(prompt_ids, rng, boost_strength=boost_strength)\n        out_ids = list(prompt_ids)\n        quanta_crossed = 0  # N: count of boundary crossings this run\n\n        for step in range(max_new_tokens):\n            temp = sinusoidal_temperature(step, base_temp=base_temp, amplitude=amplitude,\n                                           omega=omega, phase=phase)\n            probs = self._get_row(ctx_idx)\n            next_ctx_idx = sample_at_boundary(\n                probs, temperature=temp, top_k=top_k, rng=rng,\n                lookahead_bias=self.introduced_word_score, boost_strength=boost_strength,\n            )\n\n            if next_ctx_idx is None:\n                ctx_idx = self._initial_context(out_ids, rng, boost_strength=boost_strength)\n                continue\n\n            next_word_id = self.contexts[next_ctx_idx][-1]\n            out_ids.append(next_word_id)\n            ctx_idx = next_ctx_idx\n            quanta_crossed += 1\n\n        self.last_quanta_crossed = quanta_crossed\n        words = [self.vocab[i] for i in out_ids]\n        return _detokenize(words)\n\n\ndef _detokenize(tokens):\n    out = []\n    for tok in tokens:\n        if tok in (BOS,):\n            continue\n        if out and re.match(r\"[.,!?;:]\", tok):\n            out[-1] = out[-1] + tok\n        else:\n            out.append(tok)\n    text = \" \".join(out)\n    if text:\n        text = text[0].upper() + text[1:]\n    return text\n\n\ndef main():\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"HECM model, relabeled to the temporal-index paper's vocabulary\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--data\", nargs=\"*\", default=None)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--order\", type=int, default=3)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--min-count\", type=int, default=1)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--beta\", type=float, default=0.3, help=\"mass-density diffusion strength\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--alpha\", type=float, default=0.1, help=\"temporal-orientation blend factor\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--max-diffusion-states\", type=int, default=5)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--prompt\", type=str, default=\"the fox and\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--length\", type=int, default=400)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--top-k\", type=int, default=10)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--base-temp\", type=float, default=0.8)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--amplitude\", type=float, default=0.4)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--omega\", type=float, default=0.5)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--phase\", type=float, default=0.0)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--seed\", type=int, default=None)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--boost\", type=str, default=None)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--boost-strength\", type=float, default=1.0)\n    args = ap.parse_args()\n\n    tokens = load_corpus(paths=args.data)\n    print(f\"[info] loaded {len(tokens)} tokens\", file=sys.stderr)\n\n    boost_strength = args.boost_strength if args.boost else 0.0\n\n    model = HECMModel(\n        tokens, order=args.order, min_count=args.min_count,\n        beta=args.beta, alpha=args.alpha,\n        max_diffusion_states=args.max_diffusion_states,\n        boost_strength=boost_strength,\n    )\n\n    print(\n        f\"[info] order={args.order} | vocab={model.vocab_size()} | \"\n        f\"membrane states={model.num_states()} | \"\n        f\"rho_eff={model.rho_eff:.1f} bytes/state | \"\n        f\"tau={model.tau:.3f}\",\n        file=sys.stderr,\n    )\n\n    if args.boost:\n        payload = parse_payload(args.boost, model.word_to_idx)\n        model.payload = payload\n        model.context_word_score = np.array(\n            [sum(payload[w] for w in ctx) for ctx in model.contexts], dtype=np.float64\n        )\n        model.introduced_word_score = np.array(\n            [payload[ctx[-1]] for ctx in model.contexts], dtype=np.float64\n        )\n        model.context_total_score = model.context_word_score\n\n    def run_once(prompt):\n        result = model.generate(\n            prompt, max_new_tokens=args.length, top_k=args.top_k,\n            base_temp=args.base_temp, amplitude=args.amplitude,\n            omega=args.omega, phase=args.phase, seed=args.seed,\n            boost_strength=boost_strength,\n        )\n        print(result)\n        print(f\"[info] quanta crossed this run (N): {model.last_quanta_crossed}\", file=sys.stderr)\n\n    print(\"[info] interactive mode -- type a prompt, or 'quit' to exit\", file=sys.stderr)\n    while True:\n        try:\n            prompt = input(\"prompt> \")\n        except EOFError:\n            break\n        if prompt.strip().lower() in (\"quit\", \"exit\"):\n            break\n        run_once(prompt)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n\nadd sorted(exp(log(x)*sine(x)))", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T22:32:44.631858Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T22:32:44.631858Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "import argparse\nimport glob\nimport math\nimport re\nimport sys\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport scipy.sparse as sp\nfrom scipy.linalg import expm\n\nUNK = \"<unk>\"\nBOS = \"<bos>\"\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 1. Dataset loading (unchanged \u2014 no construct in the paper maps to this)\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef load_corpus(paths=None, raw_text=None, lowercase=True, strip_punct=False):\n    text_chunks = []\n    if paths:\n        found_any = False\n        for pattern in paths:\n            for fp in sorted(glob.glob(pattern)):\n                found_any = True\n                with open(fp, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\") as f:\n                    text_chunks.append(f.read())\n        if not found_any:\n            print(f\"[warn] no files matched {paths}\", file=sys.stderr)\n\n    if raw_text:\n        text_chunks.append(raw_text)\n    if not text_chunks:\n        text_chunks.append(_DEMO_CORPUS)\n\n    full_text = \"\\n\".join(text_chunks)\n    if lowercase:\n        full_text = full_text.lower()\n\n    tokens = re.findall(r\"[a-z0-9']+|[.,!?;:]\", full_text)\n    if strip_punct:\n        tokens = [t for t in tokens if re.match(r\"[a-z0-9']+\", t)]\n    return tokens\n\n\nwith open(input(\"Filename: \"), \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    _DEMO_CORPUS = file.read()\n\n\ndef build_vocab(tokens, min_count=1):\n    counts = Counter(tokens)\n    kept = sorted([w for w, c in counts.items() if c >= min_count])\n    vocab = [UNK, BOS] + kept\n    word_to_idx = {w: i for i, w in enumerate(vocab)}\n    return vocab, word_to_idx, counts\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 2. The membrane: context-state construction\n#\n#    Exchange: \"topological energy membrane separating internal state space\n#    from environment\" -> the boundary crossed when the Markov chain moves\n#    from one context-state to the next. d-Omega is not a spatial surface;\n#    it is the transition itself. Quanta N are tokens: one per crossing,\n#    genuinely indivisible (the tokenizer's vocabulary IS a discrete set),\n#    which is the one place in the whole exercise where \"quanta\" is not a\n#    metaphor borrowed from QM \u2014 it is just an accurate word for \"discrete\n#    countable unit,\" which is what a token already was.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef build_membrane_transition_graph(tokens, word_to_idx, order=3):\n    \"\"\"Build the square context -> context transition count matrix. This\n    IS the membrane: d-Omega = the set of (context_state -> context_state)\n    edges; a single generation step = one crossing of d-Omega; the emitted\n    word at that step = one quantum.\n\n    Renamed from build_ngram_contexts(). Same function, same sparse-matrix\n    justification: a dense (num_contexts x num_contexts) \"membrane\" at\n    real corpus scale is gigabytes to hundreds of gigabytes, which is\n    exactly the mass-density blowup discussed in build_mass_density_term()\n    below \u2014 the membrane and the mass term are two names for adjacent\n    facts about the same object, not two separate mechanisms.\n    \"\"\"\n    ctx_len = order - 1\n    if ctx_len < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"order must be >= 2\")\n\n    bos_idx = word_to_idx[BOS]\n    unk_idx = word_to_idx[UNK]\n    ids = [word_to_idx.get(t, unk_idx) for t in tokens]\n    padded = [bos_idx] * ctx_len + ids\n\n    context_to_idx = {}\n    contexts = []\n    context_counts = Counter()\n    edges = Counter()\n\n    def get_ctx_idx(ctx_tuple):\n        if ctx_tuple not in context_to_idx:\n            context_to_idx[ctx_tuple] = len(contexts)\n            contexts.append(ctx_tuple)\n        return context_to_idx[ctx_tuple]\n\n    n = len(padded)\n    for i in range(n - ctx_len):\n        ctx = tuple(padded[i:i + ctx_len])\n        next_ctx = tuple(padded[i + 1:i + 1 + ctx_len])\n        ci = get_ctx_idx(ctx)\n        cj = get_ctx_idx(next_ctx)\n        context_counts[ctx] += 1\n        edges[(ci, cj)] += 1.0\n\n    m = len(contexts)\n    if edges:\n        rows, cols, data = zip(*[(r, c, v) for (r, c), v in edges.items()])\n    else:\n        rows, cols, data = (), (), ()\n    membrane = sp.csr_matrix((data, (rows, cols)), shape=(m, m), dtype=np.float64)\n\n    return contexts, context_to_idx, membrane, context_counts\n\n\ndef log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts):\n    def key(ctx):\n        c = context_counts.get(ctx, 0)\n        return -math.log(c + 1.0)\n    return sorted(range(len(contexts)), key=lambda i: key(contexts[i]))\n\n\ndef permute_matrix(M, order):\n    order = np.asarray(order)\n    if sp.issparse(M):\n        return M[order, :][:, order].tocsr()\n    return M[np.ix_(order, order)]\n\n\ndef membrane_flux_normalize(M, eps=1e-9):\n    \"\"\"Row-normalize the membrane's transition weights into a proper\n    conservation law: total probability flux leaving any context-state\n    across d-Omega sums to 1. This is the literal, enforced version of the\n    paper's heuristic surface integral N = oint_dOmega sigma(x) dx \u2014 not\n    an analogy to it, an instance of it, since a row-stochastic matrix row\n    IS a discrete measure over the boundary that sums to a fixed quantum\n    budget per step.\n\n    Renamed from row_normalize(). Behavior unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    if sp.issparse(M):\n        row_sums = np.asarray(M.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n        row_sums[row_sums == 0] = 1.0\n        inv = sp.diags(1.0 / row_sums)\n        return (inv @ M).tocsr()\n    row_sums = M.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\n    row_sums[row_sums == 0] = 1.0\n    return M / (row_sums + eps)\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 3. Mass-density coupling term\n#\n#    Exchange: rho_eff (effective mass-density, coupled to retrospective\n#    orientation) -> the memory footprint and O(n^2)-to-O(n^3) compute cost\n#    of diffusing probability mass across the membrane. This is the ONE\n#    construct in the original paper that upgrades from metaphor to fact\n#    when ported to code: \"retrospection carries more computational mass\"\n#    stops being poetic and becomes a literal, measurable claim about\n#    bytes and FLOPs, which is exactly what the original file's own\n#    docstring already said before any relabeling (\"137,702 contexts ->\n#    ~141 GiB\").\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef build_mass_density_term(P, beta=0.15, remove_self_loops=True):\n    \"\"\"expm(beta * (P - I)): the diffusion/propagation step. In the paper's\n    vocabulary this is where mass-density is \"felt\" \u2014 it is the most\n    expensive operation in the pipeline (O(n^3) for a state space of size\n    n), and its cost is precisely what rho_eff = M(t)/|Omega_active(t)|\n    measures. A system leaning harder on this term (more diffusion, less\n    raw/verbatim retrospective lookup) really does carry more\n    computational mass: more memory, more latency, more bandwidth.\n\n    Renamed from diffusion_weighting(). Behavior unchanged, including the\n    self-loop caveat: exp(-beta) concentrates on the diagonal for small\n    beta, which would make generation stick to one context-state forever\n    (i.e., infinite effective mass, zero motion) unless removed.\n    \"\"\"\n    m = P.shape[0]\n    L = P - np.eye(m)\n    D = expm(beta * L)\n    D = np.clip(D, 0, None)\n\n    if remove_self_loops:\n        np.fill_diagonal(D, 0.0)\n        row_sums = D.sum(axis=1)\n        dead_rows = row_sums <= 1e-12\n        if np.any(dead_rows):\n            fallback = P.copy()\n            np.fill_diagonal(fallback, 0.0)\n            fb_sums = fallback.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\n            fb_sums[fb_sums == 0] = 1.0\n            fallback = fallback / fb_sums\n            D[dead_rows] = fallback[dead_rows]\n\n    D = membrane_flux_normalize(D)\n    return D\n\n\ndef blend_temporal_orientation(P_retrospective, P_diffused, alpha=0.5,\n                                remove_self_loops=True):\n    \"\"\"Blend the purely retrospective (raw n-gram, tau = -1) distribution\n    with the mass-density-diffused one, controlled by alpha. alpha is the\n    closest thing in this codebase to a dial on tau: alpha=0 is maximally\n    retrospective (pure lookup), alpha=1 is maximally diffused (mass-heavy,\n    still not \"anticipatory\" in any real sense \u2014 see the note in\n    compute_temporal_index() below about why diffusion isn't lookahead).\n\n    Renamed from blend(). Behavior unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    P = (1 - alpha) * P_retrospective * alpha * P_diffused\n    if remove_self_loops:\n        np.fill_diagonal(P, 0.0)\n        P = membrane_flux_normalize(P)\n    return P\n\n\ndef compute_mass_density(num_states, dtype_bytes=8):\n    \"\"\"rho_eff = M(t) / |Omega_active(t)|, made concrete: bytes required\n    for a DENSE membrane of this many context-states, divided by the\n    number of active states. This is the real, computable version of the\n    coupling constant lambda from the original speculative paper \u2014 no\n    free parameter, just arithmetic.\n    \"\"\"\n    dense_bytes = (num_states ** 2) * dtype_bytes\n    rho_eff = dense_bytes / max(num_states, 1)\n    return dense_bytes, rho_eff\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 4. Temporal index computation\n#\n#    Exchange: tau (subjective temporal orientation) -> fraction of\n#    per-step decision compute spent on the payload lookahead-rerank\n#    (prospective: scoring a word BEFORE it's committed) versus the raw\n#    context lookup (retrospective: reading what was already observed).\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef compute_temporal_index(boost_strength, lookahead_ops=1, retrospective_ops=1):\n    \"\"\"tau = (C_lookahead - C_retrospective) / (C_lookahead + C_retrospective),\n    gated by whether the payload customizer is active at all.\n\n    With boost_strength == 0, the payload rerank never executes (see\n    sample_at_boundary below), so tau = -1 exactly: this generator is\n    purely retrospective, same as any causal-masked autoregressive model.\n\n    With boost_strength > 0, the payload dot-product IS a one-step\n    lookahead \u2014 it scores the word that would be newly introduced by a\n    candidate transition before that transition is taken \u2014 so tau ticks\n    positive by an amount proportional to how much compute that rerank\n    consumes relative to the base lookup. This is a real, bounded,\n    non-metaphorical number, not a stand-in for felt anticipation.\n\n    Note on the diffusion term: build_mass_density_term() does NOT count\n    as lookahead, even though it's a \"future-looking\" propagator in the\n    continuous-time-Markov sense. It diffuses probability mass over\n    ALREADY-OBSERVED transitions; it doesn't evaluate not-yet-taken\n    candidate words the way the payload rerank does. Mass-density and\n    temporal index are independent axes here, exactly as the original\n    paper treated rho_eff and tau as coupled-but-distinct variables.\n    \"\"\"\n    if boost_strength == 0.0:\n        return -1.0\n    total = lookahead_ops + retrospective_ops\n    return (boost_strength * lookahead_ops - retrospective_ops) / (\n        boost_strength * lookahead_ops + retrospective_ops\n    )\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 5. Sinusoidal temperature \u2014 kept under its plain name\n#\n#    This is the one function this file will NOT rename into the paper's\n#    \"harmonic resonance\" vocabulary. See module docstring.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef sinusoidal_temperature(step, base_temp=0.8, amplitude=0.4, omega=0.5, phase=0.0, floor=0.05):\n    t = base_temp + amplitude * math.sin(omega * step + phase)\n    return max(floor, t)\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 6. Sampling: where the decision operator D acts on the rule R\n#\n#    Exchange: \"decisions precede the rule-set\" -> D narrows/reranks the\n#    ALREADY-LAWFUL candidate set (nonzero-probability transitions); it\n#    can never make a zero-probability transition eligible. R (the\n#    observed n-gram law, P_final) is never edited by D. This is the\n#    weak, defensible reading of the inversion, not the strong claim that\n#    decisions rewrite physics.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef sample_at_boundary(probs, temperature=1.0, top_k=10, rng=None,\n                        lookahead_bias=None, boost_strength=0.0):\n    \"\"\"Cross d-Omega once: pick the next context-state (i.e. emit one\n    quantum). `lookahead_bias` is the decision operator D's input;\n    `probs` (P_final's row) is the rule R. D can only rerank within\n    nonzero_idx \u2014 the domain R already permits \u2014 never expand it.\n\n    Renamed from sample_next(). Behavior unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    rng = rng or np.random.default_rng()\n    probs = np.asarray(probs, dtype=np.float64)\n\n    nonzero_idx = np.nonzero(probs > 0)[0]\n    if len(nonzero_idx) == 0:\n        return None\n\n    if top_k is not None and 0 < top_k < len(nonzero_idx):\n        sub = np.argpartition(probs[nonzero_idx], -top_k)[-top_k:]\n        top_idx = nonzero_idx[sub]\n    else:\n        top_idx = nonzero_idx\n\n    top_probs = probs[top_idx]\n    logits = np.log(top_probs) / max(temperature, 1e-6)\n    if lookahead_bias is not None and boost_strength != 0.0:\n        logits = logits + boost_strength * np.asarray(lookahead_bias)[top_idx]\n    logits -= logits.max()\n    weights = np.exp(logits)\n    weights /= weights.sum()\n\n    choice = rng.choice(len(top_idx), p=weights)\n    return top_idx[choice]\n\n\ndef parse_payload(payload_str, word_to_idx):\n    vocab_size = len(word_to_idx)\n    payload_vector = np.zeros(vocab_size, dtype=np.float64)\n    if not payload_str:\n        return payload_vector\n    for item in payload_str.split(\",\"):\n        item = item.strip()\n        if not item:\n            continue\n        if \":\" in item:\n            word, weight_str = item.rsplit(\":\", 1)\n            try:\n                weight = float(weight_str)\n            except ValueError:\n                print(f\"[warn] payload entry '{item}' has a non-numeric weight; skipping\", file=sys.stderr)\n                continue\n        else:\n            word, weight = item, 1.0\n        word = word.strip().lower()\n        idx = word_to_idx.get(word)\n        if idx is None:\n            print(f\"[warn] payload word '{word}' is not in the vocabulary; skipping\", file=sys.stderr)\n            continue\n        payload_vector[idx] = weight\n    return payload_vector\n\n\nclass HECMModel:\n    \"\"\"Same model as before. Internally, the membrane, mass-density term,\n    and temporal index are now named as such and exposed as attributes\n    (self.rho_eff, self.tau) so they can be inspected directly rather than\n    inferred from comments.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, tokens, order=3, min_count=1, beta=0.0, alpha=0.0,\n                 max_diffusion_states=1500, payload=None, boost_strength=0.0):\n        self.order = order\n        vocab, word_to_idx, counts = build_vocab(tokens, min_count=min_count)\n        self.vocab = vocab\n        self.word_to_idx = word_to_idx\n\n        contexts, context_to_idx, membrane, context_counts = build_membrane_transition_graph(\n            tokens, word_to_idx, order=order\n        )\n\n        perm = log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts)\n        contexts = [contexts[i] for i in perm]\n        membrane = permute_matrix(membrane, perm)\n        context_to_idx = {ctx: i for i, ctx in enumerate(contexts)}\n\n        self.contexts = contexts\n        self.context_to_idx = context_to_idx\n        self.context_counts = context_counts\n\n        self.P_retrospective = membrane_flux_normalize(membrane)\n\n        num_states = len(contexts)\n        self.dense_bytes, self.rho_eff = compute_mass_density(num_states)\n\n        self.P_diffused = self.P_retrospective\n        self.P_final = self.P_retrospective\n        \n\n        self.boost_strength = boost_strength\n        self.tau = compute_temporal_index(boost_strength)\n\n        if payload is None:\n            payload = np.zeros(len(vocab), dtype=np.float64)\n        self.payload = payload\n        self.context_word_score = np.array(\n            [sum(payload[w] for w in ctx) for ctx in contexts], dtype=np.float64\n        )\n        self.introduced_word_score = np.array(\n            [payload[ctx[-1]] for ctx in contexts], dtype=np.float64\n        )\n        self.context_total_score = self.context_word_score + 0.0\n\n    def vocab_size(self):\n        return len(self.vocab)\n\n    def num_states(self):\n        return len(self.contexts)\n\n    def _get_row(self, ctx_idx):\n        row = self.P_final[ctx_idx]\n        if sp.issparse(self.P_final):\n            return np.asarray(row.todense()).ravel()\n        return np.asarray(row)\n\n    def _tokenize_prompt(self, prompt):\n        toks = re.findall(r\"[a-z0-9']+|[.,!?;:]\", prompt.lower())\n        unk = self.word_to_idx[UNK]\n        return [self.word_to_idx.get(t, unk) for t in toks]\n\n    def _weighted_pick(self, candidates, boost_strength, rng):\n        if boost_strength == 0.0 or len(candidates) == 1:\n            return candidates[rng.integers(len(candidates))] if len(candidates) > 1 else candidates[0]\n        scores = boost_strength * self.context_total_score[candidates]\n        scores = scores - scores.max()\n        weights = np.exp(scores)\n        weights /= weights.sum()\n        return candidates[rng.choice(len(candidates), p=weights)]\n\n    def _initial_context(self, prompt_ids, rng, boost_strength=0.0):\n        ctx_len = self.order - 1\n        bos = self.word_to_idx[BOS]\n        padded = [bos] * ctx_len + prompt_ids\n        desired = tuple(padded[-ctx_len:])\n\n        if desired in self.context_to_idx:\n            return self.context_to_idx[desired]\n\n        for k in range(ctx_len - 1, 0, -1):\n            suffix = desired[-k:]\n            candidates = [i for i, ctx in enumerate(self.contexts) if ctx[-k:] == suffix]\n            if candidates:\n                return self._weighted_pick(candidates, boost_strength, rng)\n\n        counts_arr = np.array([self.context_counts.get(ctx, 0) for ctx in self.contexts])\n        top_candidates = list(np.argsort(counts_arr)[-10:])\n        return self._weighted_pick(top_candidates, boost_strength, rng)\n\n    def generate(self, prompt, max_new_tokens=40, top_k=10, base_temp=0.8,\n                 amplitude=0.4, omega=0.5, phase=0.0, seed=None,\n                 boost_strength=0.0):\n        rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n        prompt_ids = self._tokenize_prompt(prompt)\n\n        ctx_idx = self._initial_context(prompt_ids, rng, boost_strength=boost_strength)\n        out_ids = list(prompt_ids)\n        quanta_crossed = 0  # N: count of boundary crossings this run\n\n        for step in range(max_new_tokens):\n            temp = sinusoidal_temperature(step, base_temp=base_temp, amplitude=amplitude,\n                                           omega=omega, phase=phase)\n            probs = self._get_row(ctx_idx)\n            next_ctx_idx = sample_at_boundary(\n                probs, temperature=temp, top_k=top_k, rng=rng,\n                lookahead_bias=self.introduced_word_score, boost_strength=boost_strength,\n            )\n\n            if next_ctx_idx is None:\n                ctx_idx = self._initial_context(out_ids, rng, boost_strength=boost_strength)\n                continue\n\n            next_word_id = self.contexts[next_ctx_idx][-1]\n            out_ids.append(next_word_id)\n            ctx_idx = next_ctx_idx\n            quanta_crossed += 1\n\n        self.last_quanta_crossed = quanta_crossed\n        words = [self.vocab[i] for i in out_ids]\n        return _detokenize(words)\n\n\ndef _detokenize(tokens):\n    out = []\n    for tok in tokens:\n        if tok in (BOS,):\n            continue\n        if out and re.match(r\"[.,!?;:]\", tok):\n            out[-1] = out[-1] + tok\n        else:\n            out.append(tok)\n    text = \" \".join(out)\n    if text:\n        text = text[0].upper() + text[1:]\n    return text\n\n\ndef main():\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"HECM model, relabeled to the temporal-index paper's vocabulary\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--data\", nargs=\"*\", default=None)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--order\", type=int, default=3)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--min-count\", type=int, default=1)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--beta\", type=float, default=0.3, help=\"mass-density diffusion strength\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--alpha\", type=float, default=0.1, help=\"temporal-orientation blend factor\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--max-diffusion-states\", type=int, default=5)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--prompt\", type=str, default=\"the fox and\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--length\", type=int, default=400)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--top-k\", type=int, default=10)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--base-temp\", type=float, default=0.8)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--amplitude\", type=float, default=0.4)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--omega\", type=float, default=0.5)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--phase\", type=float, default=0.0)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--seed\", type=int, default=None)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--boost\", type=str, default=None)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--boost-strength\", type=float, default=1.0)\n    args = ap.parse_args()\n\n    tokens = load_corpus(paths=args.data)\n    print(f\"[info] loaded {len(tokens)} tokens\", file=sys.stderr)\n\n    boost_strength = args.boost_strength if args.boost else 0.0\n\n    model = HECMModel(\n        tokens, order=args.order, min_count=args.min_count,\n        beta=args.beta, alpha=args.alpha,\n        max_diffusion_states=args.max_diffusion_states,\n        boost_strength=boost_strength,\n    )\n\n    print(\n        f\"[info] order={args.order} | vocab={model.vocab_size()} | \"\n        f\"membrane states={model.num_states()} | \"\n        f\"rho_eff={model.rho_eff:.1f} bytes/state | \"\n        f\"tau={model.tau:.3f}\",\n        file=sys.stderr,\n    )\n\n    if args.boost:\n        payload = parse_payload(args.boost, model.word_to_idx)\n        model.payload = payload\n        model.context_word_score = np.array(\n            [sum(payload[w] for w in ctx) for ctx in model.contexts], dtype=np.float64\n        )\n        model.introduced_word_score = np.array(\n            [payload[ctx[-1]] for ctx in model.contexts], dtype=np.float64\n        )\n        model.context_total_score = model.context_word_score\n\n    def run_once(prompt):\n        result = model.generate(\n            prompt, max_new_tokens=args.length, top_k=args.top_k,\n            base_temp=args.base_temp, amplitude=args.amplitude,\n            omega=args.omega, phase=args.phase, seed=args.seed,\n            boost_strength=boost_strength,\n        )\n        print(result)\n        print(f\"[info] quanta crossed this run (N): {model.last_quanta_crossed}\", file=sys.stderr)\n\n    print(\"[info] interactive mode -- type a prompt, or 'quit' to exit\", file=sys.stderr)\n    while True:\n        try:\n            prompt = input(\"prompt> \")\n        except EOFError:\n            break\n        if prompt.strip().lower() in (\"quit\", \"exit\"):\n            break\n        run_once(prompt)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n\nadd sorted(exp(log(x)*sine(x)))", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-28T22:32:44.690908Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-28T22:32:44.690908Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000000"}, {"uuid": "01a0ea26-25eb-7e64-b4db-7b471bb2c730", "text": "I read this as a new sort key for the contexts, applied to each context's count, `x = count + 1` (the +1 keeps `log` defined for contexts with zero count). Here's `log_sort_contexts` with it added, keeping the original ordering available:\n\n```python\ndef log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts, mode=\"sine\"):\n    \"\"\"Return context indices ordered by a key on each context's count.\n\n    mode=\"log\":  original ordering, key = -log(c + 1)\n    mode=\"sine\": key = exp(log(x) * sin(x)) with x = c + 1\n                 (mathematically x ** sin(x))\n    \"\"\"\n    def key(ctx):\n        x = context_counts.get(ctx, 0) + 1.0\n        if mode == \"sine\":\n            return math.exp(math.log(x) * math.sin(x))\n        return -math.log(x)\n\n    return sorted(range(len(contexts)), key=lambda i: key(contexts[i]))\n```\n\n`HECMModel.__init__` already calls this function, so the new ordering takes effect with no other changes. To pick the mode from the command line, add `--sort-mode` and pass it through:\n\n```python\n# in main()\nap.add_argument(\"--sort-mode\", choices=[\"log\", \"sine\"], default=\"sine\")\n\n# HECMModel.__init__ signature\ndef __init__(self, tokens, order=3, min_count=1, beta=0.0, alpha=0.0,\n             max_diffusion_states=1500, payload=None, boost_strength=0.0,\n             sort_mode=\"sine\"):\n    ...\n    perm = log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts, mode=sort_mode)\n\n# HECMModel(...) call in main()\nmodel = HECMModel(..., sort_mode=args.sort_mode)\n```\n\n- **The key isn't monotonic.** `exp(log(x)\u00b7sin(x)) = x^sin(x)` oscillates between about `1/x` and `x` as the count grows, so the sort scrambles contexts by frequency rather than ordering them by it. That is fine if you want a pseudo-shuffle, but it no longer gives the frequency-descending layout the original `-log(c+1)` did.\n- **Sorting the contexts only relabels the matrix.** It permutes rows and columns without changing the model's behavior, unless something downstream depends on index order (for example, `_initial_context` takes the last 10 by count with `argsort`, which doesn't depend on the sort).\n\nUnrelated, but I noticed two bugs while reading:\n\n1. In `blend_temporal_orientation`, `(1 - alpha) * P_retrospective * alpha * P_diffused` should almost certainly be `+`, not `*`. Also, `HECMModel` never calls the blend or the diffusion, so `--beta` and `--alpha` currently do nothing.\n2. The module-level `with open(input(\"Filename: \")...)` runs at import time and ignores `--data`. It also prompts before argparse runs, so `--help` will hang waiting on input.\n\nI can fix either if you'd like.", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T22:32:44.625073Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T22:32:48.869296Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Untangling how a sorting expression should be applied."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-28T22:32:48.869326Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-28T22:32:55.855896Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "I read this as a new sort key for the contexts, applied to each context's count, `x = count + 1` (the +1 keeps `log` defined for contexts with zero count). Here's `log_sort_contexts` with it added, keeping the original ordering available:\n\n```python\ndef log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts, mode=\"sine\"):\n    \"\"\"Return context indices ordered by a key on each context's count.\n\n    mode=\"log\":  original ordering, key = -log(c + 1)\n    mode=\"sine\": key = exp(log(x) * sin(x)) with x = c + 1\n                 (mathematically x ** sin(x))\n    \"\"\"\n    def key(ctx):\n        x = context_counts.get(ctx, 0) + 1.0\n        if mode == \"sine\":\n            return math.exp(math.log(x) * math.sin(x))\n        return -math.log(x)\n\n    return sorted(range(len(contexts)), key=lambda i: key(contexts[i]))\n```\n\n`HECMModel.__init__` already calls this function, so the new ordering takes effect with no other changes. To pick the mode from the command line, add `--sort-mode` and pass it through:\n\n```python\n# in main()\nap.add_argument(\"--sort-mode\", choices=[\"log\", \"sine\"], default=\"sine\")\n\n# HECMModel.__init__ signature\ndef __init__(self, tokens, order=3, min_count=1, beta=0.0, alpha=0.0,\n             max_diffusion_states=1500, payload=None, boost_strength=0.0,\n             sort_mode=\"sine\"):\n    ...\n    perm = log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts, mode=sort_mode)\n\n# HECMModel(...) call in main()\nmodel = HECMModel(..., sort_mode=args.sort_mode)\n```\n\n- **The key isn't monotonic.** `exp(log(x)\u00b7sin(x)) = x^sin(x)` oscillates between about `1/x` and `x` as the count grows, so the sort scrambles contexts by frequency rather than ordering them by it. That is fine if you want a pseudo-shuffle, but it no longer gives the frequency-descending layout the original `-log(c+1)` did.\n- **Sorting the contexts only relabels the matrix.** It permutes rows and columns without changing the model's behavior, unless something downstream depends on index order (for example, `_initial_context` takes the last 10 by count with `argsort`, which doesn't depend on the sort).\n\nUnrelated, but I noticed two bugs while reading:\n\n1. In `blend_temporal_orientation`, `(1 - alpha) * P_retrospective * alpha * P_diffused` should almost certainly be `+`, not `*`. Also, `HECMModel` never calls the blend or the diffusion, so `--beta` and `--alpha` currently do nothing.\n2. The module-level `with open(input(\"Filename: \")...)` runs at import time and ignores `--data`. It also prompts before argparse runs, so `--help` will hang waiting on input.\n\nI can fix either if you'd like.", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-28T22:32:55.868490Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-28T22:32:55.868490Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ea26-25ea-7746-864a-0feeb0bb7f9a"}, {"uuid": "01a0ec74-a950-7aea-886b-8ff599bb53de", "text": "apply Drying the local minimum of the probs, to prevent illogical prob selection in generate", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:17:43.952133Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:17:43.952133Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "apply Drying the local minimum of the probs, to prevent illogical prob selection in generate", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-29T09:17:43.952383Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-29T09:17:43.952383Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ea26-25eb-7e64-b4db-7b471bb2c730"}, {"uuid": "01a0ec74-a950-7632-a04f-f3cc2b9a0354", "text": "I'm reading \"drying\" as pruning: cut the low-probability tail at the \"valley\" of the candidate distribution so it can never be sampled. A sorted distribution has no true local minimum, so I use the elbow, the largest multiplicative drop between neighbouring sorted probabilities, and discard everything after it. Because this runs before the temperature and the lookahead boost, the boost can't revive a pruned candidate.\n\n```python\ndef dry_local_minimum(probs, idx, min_keep=2, gap_ratio=4.0):\n    \"\"\"Prune the tail below the steepest drop in the sorted candidate probs.\n\n    probs: the full probability row; idx: candidate indices (nonzero, after top_k).\n    Returns the subset of idx to keep. The cut only happens when the biggest\n    drop is at least `gap_ratio`x, so flat distributions are left untouched.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(idx) <= min_keep:\n        return idx\n    order = np.argsort(probs[idx])[::-1]          # descending by prob\n    sorted_p = probs[idx][order]\n    drops = sorted_p[:-1] / sorted_p[1:]           # p_i / p_{i+1}\n    # only consider cut points that keep at least min_keep candidates\n    drops[: min_keep - 1] = 0.0\n    cut = int(np.argmax(drops))\n    if drops[cut] < gap_ratio:\n        return idx\n    return idx[order[: cut + 1]]\n```\n\nIn `sample_at_boundary`, add the parameters and call it right after the `top_k` step:\n\n```python\ndef sample_at_boundary(probs, temperature=1.0, top_k=10, rng=None,\n                       lookahead_bias=None, boost_strength=0.0,\n                       dry=True, dry_gap=4.0, dry_min_keep=2):\n    ...\n    else:\n        top_idx = nonzero_idx\n\n    if dry:\n        top_idx = dry_local_minimum(probs, top_idx,\n                                    min_keep=dry_min_keep, gap_ratio=dry_gap)\n\n    top_probs = probs[top_idx]\n    ...\n```\n\nPass the settings through `generate` and the CLI:\n\n```python\n# generate signature\ndef generate(self, prompt, ..., boost_strength=0.0, dry=True, dry_gap=4.0):\n    ...\n    next_ctx_idx = sample_at_boundary(\n        probs, temperature=temp, top_k=top_k, rng=rng,\n        lookahead_bias=self.introduced_word_score, boost_strength=boost_strength,\n        dry=dry, dry_gap=dry_gap,\n    )\n\n# main()\nap.add_argument(\"--no-dry\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"disable tail pruning\")\nap.add_argument(\"--dry-gap\", type=float, default=4.0,\n                help=\"prune tail when the steepest drop between sorted probs is >= this ratio\")\n\n# in run_once\nresult = model.generate(..., dry=not args.no_dry, dry_gap=args.dry_gap)\n```\n\n- **`gap_ratio` is the main dial.** Lower values (around 2) prune aggressively, higher values (10 or more) only remove extreme outliers, and `inf` turns pruning off.\n- **The effect is strongest at high temperature.** The sinusoidal temperature peaks at `base_temp + amplitude` (1.2 by default), where unlikely transitions are otherwise most likely to slip through.\n- **Sparse rows are mostly unaffected.** With an order-3 n-gram model, many contexts have only one or two successors, and `min_keep=2` protects those rows from being cut down to one.\n\nIf you meant something different by \"drying\", such as a min-p style cutoff relative to the top probability, tell me and I'll swap the pruning rule.", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:17:43.946934Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:17:49.854328Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Figuring out how to prevent illogical probability selection in generation."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:17:49.854363Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:17:58.580617Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "I'm reading \"drying\" as pruning: cut the low-probability tail at the \"valley\" of the candidate distribution so it can never be sampled. A sorted distribution has no true local minimum, so I use the elbow, the largest multiplicative drop between neighbouring sorted probabilities, and discard everything after it. Because this runs before the temperature and the lookahead boost, the boost can't revive a pruned candidate.\n\n```python\ndef dry_local_minimum(probs, idx, min_keep=2, gap_ratio=4.0):\n    \"\"\"Prune the tail below the steepest drop in the sorted candidate probs.\n\n    probs: the full probability row; idx: candidate indices (nonzero, after top_k).\n    Returns the subset of idx to keep. The cut only happens when the biggest\n    drop is at least `gap_ratio`x, so flat distributions are left untouched.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(idx) <= min_keep:\n        return idx\n    order = np.argsort(probs[idx])[::-1]          # descending by prob\n    sorted_p = probs[idx][order]\n    drops = sorted_p[:-1] / sorted_p[1:]           # p_i / p_{i+1}\n    # only consider cut points that keep at least min_keep candidates\n    drops[: min_keep - 1] = 0.0\n    cut = int(np.argmax(drops))\n    if drops[cut] < gap_ratio:\n        return idx\n    return idx[order[: cut + 1]]\n```\n\nIn `sample_at_boundary`, add the parameters and call it right after the `top_k` step:\n\n```python\ndef sample_at_boundary(probs, temperature=1.0, top_k=10, rng=None,\n                       lookahead_bias=None, boost_strength=0.0,\n                       dry=True, dry_gap=4.0, dry_min_keep=2):\n    ...\n    else:\n        top_idx = nonzero_idx\n\n    if dry:\n        top_idx = dry_local_minimum(probs, top_idx,\n                                    min_keep=dry_min_keep, gap_ratio=dry_gap)\n\n    top_probs = probs[top_idx]\n    ...\n```\n\nPass the settings through `generate` and the CLI:\n\n```python\n# generate signature\ndef generate(self, prompt, ..., boost_strength=0.0, dry=True, dry_gap=4.0):\n    ...\n    next_ctx_idx = sample_at_boundary(\n        probs, temperature=temp, top_k=top_k, rng=rng,\n        lookahead_bias=self.introduced_word_score, boost_strength=boost_strength,\n        dry=dry, dry_gap=dry_gap,\n    )\n\n# main()\nap.add_argument(\"--no-dry\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"disable tail pruning\")\nap.add_argument(\"--dry-gap\", type=float, default=4.0,\n                help=\"prune tail when the steepest drop between sorted probs is >= this ratio\")\n\n# in run_once\nresult = model.generate(..., dry=not args.no_dry, dry_gap=args.dry_gap)\n```\n\n- **`gap_ratio` is the main dial.** Lower values (around 2) prune aggressively, higher values (10 or more) only remove extreme outliers, and `inf` turns pruning off.\n- **The effect is strongest at high temperature.** The sinusoidal temperature peaks at `base_temp + amplitude` (1.2 by default), where unlikely transitions are otherwise most likely to slip through.\n- **Sparse rows are mostly unaffected.** With an order-3 n-gram model, many contexts have only one or two successors, and `min_keep=2` protects those rows from being cut down to one.\n\nIf you meant something different by \"drying\", such as a min-p style cutoff relative to the top probability, tell me and I'll swap the pruning rule.", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-29T09:17:58.600079Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-29T09:17:58.600079Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ec74-a950-7aea-886b-8ff599bb53de"}, {"uuid": "01a0ec75-2cf6-7a4c-97cc-098df4fae75e", "text": "do the full code", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:18:16.901170Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:18:16.901170Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "do the full code", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-29T09:18:16.901385Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-29T09:18:16.901385Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ec74-a950-7632-a04f-f3cc2b9a0354"}, {"uuid": "01a0ec75-2cf6-705b-9a56-8185568f1461", "text": "\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nThe full script is in `hecm.py`, with both changes applied:\n\n- **Sine sort key:** `log_sort_contexts` takes a `mode` argument, and `--sort-mode {log,sine}` (default `sine`) is passed through `HECMModel`.\n- **Drying:** `dry_local_minimum` runs inside `sample_at_boundary` after the top-k step, controlled by `--dry-gap` (default 4.0) and `--no-dry`.\n\nI left everything else as you had it, including the two bugs I mentioned earlier. `blend_temporal_orientation` still multiplies where it should add, and `--beta` and `--alpha` still have no effect. The top-level `input(\"Filename: \")` still runs at import time.\n\nI haven't run the script, since it needs your corpus file.", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:18:16.894964Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:18:21.928894Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Preparing the complete updated code file."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:18:21.928952Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:19:07.640288Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01NbD5suC9BjPQUqmrSUt5nM", "name": "create_file", "input": {"description": "Full HECM script with sine sort key and local-minimum drying applied", "path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py", "file_text": "import argparse\nimport glob\nimport math\nimport re\nimport sys\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport scipy.sparse as sp\nfrom scipy.linalg import expm\n\nUNK = \"<unk>\"\nBOS = \"<bos>\"\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 1. Dataset loading (unchanged \u2014 no construct in the paper maps to this)\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef load_corpus(paths=None, raw_text=None, lowercase=True, strip_punct=False):\n    text_chunks = []\n    if paths:\n        found_any = False\n        for pattern in paths:\n            for fp in sorted(glob.glob(pattern)):\n                found_any = True\n                with open(fp, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\") as f:\n                    text_chunks.append(f.read())\n        if not found_any:\n            print(f\"[warn] no files matched {paths}\", file=sys.stderr)\n\n    if raw_text:\n        text_chunks.append(raw_text)\n    if not text_chunks:\n        text_chunks.append(_DEMO_CORPUS)\n\n    full_text = \"\\n\".join(text_chunks)\n    if lowercase:\n        full_text = full_text.lower()\n\n    tokens = re.findall(r\"[a-z0-9']+|[.,!?;:]\", full_text)\n    if strip_punct:\n        tokens = [t for t in tokens if re.match(r\"[a-z0-9']+\", t)]\n    return tokens\n\n\nwith open(input(\"Filename: \"), \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    _DEMO_CORPUS = file.read()\n\n\ndef build_vocab(tokens, min_count=1):\n    counts = Counter(tokens)\n    kept = sorted([w for w, c in counts.items() if c >= min_count])\n    vocab = [UNK, BOS] + kept\n    word_to_idx = {w: i for i, w in enumerate(vocab)}\n    return vocab, word_to_idx, counts\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 2. The membrane: context-state construction\n#\n#    Exchange: \"topological energy membrane separating internal state space\n#    from environment\" -> the boundary crossed when the Markov chain moves\n#    from one context-state to the next. d-Omega is not a spatial surface;\n#    it is the transition itself. Quanta N are tokens: one per crossing,\n#    genuinely indivisible (the tokenizer's vocabulary IS a discrete set),\n#    which is the one place in the whole exercise where \"quanta\" is not a\n#    metaphor borrowed from QM \u2014 it is just an accurate word for \"discrete\n#    countable unit,\" which is what a token already was.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef build_membrane_transition_graph(tokens, word_to_idx, order=3):\n    \"\"\"Build the square context -> context transition count matrix. This\n    IS the membrane: d-Omega = the set of (context_state -> context_state)\n    edges; a single generation step = one crossing of d-Omega; the emitted\n    word at that step = one quantum.\n\n    Renamed from build_ngram_contexts(). Same function, same sparse-matrix\n    justification: a dense (num_contexts x num_contexts) \"membrane\" at\n    real corpus scale is gigabytes to hundreds of gigabytes, which is\n    exactly the mass-density blowup discussed in build_mass_density_term()\n    below \u2014 the membrane and the mass term are two names for adjacent\n    facts about the same object, not two separate mechanisms.\n    \"\"\"\n    ctx_len = order - 1\n    if ctx_len < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"order must be >= 2\")\n\n    bos_idx = word_to_idx[BOS]\n    unk_idx = word_to_idx[UNK]\n    ids = [word_to_idx.get(t, unk_idx) for t in tokens]\n    padded = [bos_idx] * ctx_len + ids\n\n    context_to_idx = {}\n    contexts = []\n    context_counts = Counter()\n    edges = Counter()\n\n    def get_ctx_idx(ctx_tuple):\n        if ctx_tuple not in context_to_idx:\n            context_to_idx[ctx_tuple] = len(contexts)\n            contexts.append(ctx_tuple)\n        return context_to_idx[ctx_tuple]\n\n    n = len(padded)\n    for i in range(n - ctx_len):\n        ctx = tuple(padded[i:i + ctx_len])\n        next_ctx = tuple(padded[i + 1:i + 1 + ctx_len])\n        ci = get_ctx_idx(ctx)\n        cj = get_ctx_idx(next_ctx)\n        context_counts[ctx] += 1\n        edges[(ci, cj)] += 1.0\n\n    m = len(contexts)\n    if edges:\n        rows, cols, data = zip(*[(r, c, v) for (r, c), v in edges.items()])\n    else:\n        rows, cols, data = (), (), ()\n    membrane = sp.csr_matrix((data, (rows, cols)), shape=(m, m), dtype=np.float64)\n\n    return contexts, context_to_idx, membrane, context_counts\n\n\ndef log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts, mode=\"sine\"):\n    \"\"\"Return context indices ordered by a key on each context's count.\n\n    mode=\"log\":  original ordering, key = -log(c + 1)\n    mode=\"sine\": key = exp(log(x) * sin(x)) with x = c + 1\n                 (mathematically x ** sin(x); not monotonic in x)\n    \"\"\"\n    def key(ctx):\n        x = context_counts.get(ctx, 0) + 1.0\n        if mode == \"sine\":\n            return math.exp(math.log(x) * math.sin(x))\n        return -math.log(x)\n\n    return sorted(range(len(contexts)), key=lambda i: key(contexts[i]))\n\n\ndef permute_matrix(M, order):\n    order = np.asarray(order)\n    if sp.issparse(M):\n        return M[order, :][:, order].tocsr()\n    return M[np.ix_(order, order)]\n\n\ndef membrane_flux_normalize(M, eps=1e-9):\n    \"\"\"Row-normalize the membrane's transition weights into a proper\n    conservation law: total probability flux leaving any context-state\n    across d-Omega sums to 1. This is the literal, enforced version of the\n    paper's heuristic surface integral N = oint_dOmega sigma(x) dx \u2014 not\n    an analogy to it, an instance of it, since a row-stochastic matrix row\n    IS a discrete measure over the boundary that sums to a fixed quantum\n    budget per step.\n\n    Renamed from row_normalize(). Behavior unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    if sp.issparse(M):\n        row_sums = np.asarray(M.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n        row_sums[row_sums == 0] = 1.0\n        inv = sp.diags(1.0 / row_sums)\n        return (inv @ M).tocsr()\n    row_sums = M.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\n    row_sums[row_sums == 0] = 1.0\n    return M / (row_sums + eps)\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 3. Mass-density coupling term\n#\n#    Exchange: rho_eff (effective mass-density, coupled to retrospective\n#    orientation) -> the memory footprint and O(n^2)-to-O(n^3) compute cost\n#    of diffusing probability mass across the membrane. This is the ONE\n#    construct in the original paper that upgrades from metaphor to fact\n#    when ported to code: \"retrospection carries more computational mass\"\n#    stops being poetic and becomes a literal, measurable claim about\n#    bytes and FLOPs, which is exactly what the original file's own\n#    docstring already said before any relabeling (\"137,702 contexts ->\n#    ~141 GiB\").\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef build_mass_density_term(P, beta=0.15, remove_self_loops=True):\n    \"\"\"expm(beta * (P - I)): the diffusion/propagation step. In the paper's\n    vocabulary this is where mass-density is \"felt\" \u2014 it is the most\n    expensive operation in the pipeline (O(n^3) for a state space of size\n    n), and its cost is precisely what rho_eff = M(t)/|Omega_active(t)|\n    measures. A system leaning harder on this term (more diffusion, less\n    raw/verbatim retrospective lookup) really does carry more\n    computational mass: more memory, more latency, more bandwidth.\n\n    Renamed from diffusion_weighting(). Behavior unchanged, including the\n    self-loop caveat: exp(-beta) concentrates on the diagonal for small\n    beta, which would make generation stick to one context-state forever\n    (i.e., infinite effective mass, zero motion) unless removed.\n    \"\"\"\n    m = P.shape[0]\n    L = P - np.eye(m)\n    D = expm(beta * L)\n    D = np.clip(D, 0, None)\n\n    if remove_self_loops:\n        np.fill_diagonal(D, 0.0)\n        row_sums = D.sum(axis=1)\n        dead_rows = row_sums <= 1e-12\n        if np.any(dead_rows):\n            fallback = P.copy()\n            np.fill_diagonal(fallback, 0.0)\n            fb_sums = fallback.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\n            fb_sums[fb_sums == 0] = 1.0\n            fallback = fallback / fb_sums\n            D[dead_rows] = fallback[dead_rows]\n\n    D = membrane_flux_normalize(D)\n    return D\n\n\ndef blend_temporal_orientation(P_retrospective, P_diffused, alpha=0.5,\n                                remove_self_loops=True):\n    \"\"\"Blend the purely retrospective (raw n-gram, tau = -1) distribution\n    with the mass-density-diffused one, controlled by alpha. alpha is the\n    closest thing in this codebase to a dial on tau: alpha=0 is maximally\n    retrospective (pure lookup), alpha=1 is maximally diffused (mass-heavy,\n    still not \"anticipatory\" in any real sense \u2014 see the note in\n    compute_temporal_index() below about why diffusion isn't lookahead).\n\n    Renamed from blend(). Behavior unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    P = (1 - alpha) * P_retrospective * alpha * P_diffused\n    if remove_self_loops:\n        np.fill_diagonal(P, 0.0)\n        P = membrane_flux_normalize(P)\n    return P\n\n\ndef compute_mass_density(num_states, dtype_bytes=8):\n    \"\"\"rho_eff = M(t) / |Omega_active(t)|, made concrete: bytes required\n    for a DENSE membrane of this many context-states, divided by the\n    number of active states. This is the real, computable version of the\n    coupling constant lambda from the original speculative paper \u2014 no\n    free parameter, just arithmetic.\n    \"\"\"\n    dense_bytes = (num_states ** 2) * dtype_bytes\n    rho_eff = dense_bytes / max(num_states, 1)\n    return dense_bytes, rho_eff\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 4. Temporal index computation\n#\n#    Exchange: tau (subjective temporal orientation) -> fraction of\n#    per-step decision compute spent on the payload lookahead-rerank\n#    (prospective: scoring a word BEFORE it's committed) versus the raw\n#    context lookup (retrospective: reading what was already observed).\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef compute_temporal_index(boost_strength, lookahead_ops=1, retrospective_ops=1):\n    \"\"\"tau = (C_lookahead - C_retrospective) / (C_lookahead + C_retrospective),\n    gated by whether the payload customizer is active at all.\n\n    With boost_strength == 0, the payload rerank never executes (see\n    sample_at_boundary below), so tau = -1 exactly: this generator is\n    purely retrospective, same as any causal-masked autoregressive model.\n\n    With boost_strength > 0, the payload dot-product IS a one-step\n    lookahead \u2014 it scores the word that would be newly introduced by a\n    candidate transition before that transition is taken \u2014 so tau ticks\n    positive by an amount proportional to how much compute that rerank\n    consumes relative to the base lookup. This is a real, bounded,\n    non-metaphorical number, not a stand-in for felt anticipation.\n\n    Note on the diffusion term: build_mass_density_term() does NOT count\n    as lookahead, even though it's a \"future-looking\" propagator in the\n    continuous-time-Markov sense. It diffuses probability mass over\n    ALREADY-OBSERVED transitions; it doesn't evaluate not-yet-taken\n    candidate words the way the payload rerank does. Mass-density and\n    temporal index are independent axes here, exactly as the original\n    paper treated rho_eff and tau as coupled-but-distinct variables.\n    \"\"\"\n    if boost_strength == 0.0:\n        return -1.0\n    total = lookahead_ops + retrospective_ops\n    return (boost_strength * lookahead_ops - retrospective_ops) / (\n        boost_strength * lookahead_ops + retrospective_ops\n    )\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 5. Sinusoidal temperature \u2014 kept under its plain name\n#\n#    This is the one function this file will NOT rename into the paper's\n#    \"harmonic resonance\" vocabulary. See module docstring.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef sinusoidal_temperature(step, base_temp=0.8, amplitude=0.4, omega=0.5, phase=0.0, floor=0.05):\n    t = base_temp + amplitude * math.sin(omega * step + phase)\n    return max(floor, t)\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 6. Sampling: where the decision operator D acts on the rule R\n#\n#    Exchange: \"decisions precede the rule-set\" -> D narrows/reranks the\n#    ALREADY-LAWFUL candidate set (nonzero-probability transitions); it\n#    can never make a zero-probability transition eligible. R (the\n#    observed n-gram law, P_final) is never edited by D. This is the\n#    weak, defensible reading of the inversion, not the strong claim that\n#    decisions rewrite physics.\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef dry_local_minimum(probs, idx, min_keep=2, gap_ratio=4.0):\n    \"\"\"Prune the tail below the steepest drop in the sorted candidate probs.\n\n    probs: the full probability row; idx: candidate indices (nonzero, after top_k).\n    Returns the subset of idx to keep. The cut only happens when the biggest\n    drop is at least `gap_ratio`x, so flat distributions are left untouched.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(idx) <= min_keep:\n        return idx\n    order = np.argsort(probs[idx])[::-1]          # descending by prob\n    sorted_p = probs[idx][order]\n    drops = sorted_p[:-1] / sorted_p[1:]           # p_i / p_{i+1}\n    # only consider cut points that keep at least min_keep candidates\n    drops[: min_keep - 1] = 0.0\n    cut = int(np.argmax(drops))\n    if drops[cut] < gap_ratio:\n        return idx\n    return idx[order[: cut + 1]]\n\n\ndef sample_at_boundary(probs, temperature=1.0, top_k=10, rng=None,\n                        lookahead_bias=None, boost_strength=0.0,\n                        dry=True, dry_gap=4.0, dry_min_keep=2):\n    \"\"\"Cross d-Omega once: pick the next context-state (i.e. emit one\n    quantum). `lookahead_bias` is the decision operator D's input;\n    `probs` (P_final's row) is the rule R. D can only rerank within\n    nonzero_idx \u2014 the domain R already permits \u2014 never expand it.\n\n    Drying: after top-k, the tail below the steepest drop in the sorted\n    candidate probabilities is pruned, before temperature and the\n    lookahead boost, so the boost cannot revive a pruned candidate.\n\n    Renamed from sample_next(). Behavior unchanged apart from drying.\n    \"\"\"\n    rng = rng or np.random.default_rng()\n    probs = np.asarray(probs, dtype=np.float64)\n\n    nonzero_idx = np.nonzero(probs > 0)[0]\n    if len(nonzero_idx) == 0:\n        return None\n\n    if top_k is not None and 0 < top_k < len(nonzero_idx):\n        sub = np.argpartition(probs[nonzero_idx], -top_k)[-top_k:]\n        top_idx = nonzero_idx[sub]\n    else:\n        top_idx = nonzero_idx\n\n    if dry:\n        top_idx = dry_local_minimum(probs, top_idx,\n                                    min_keep=dry_min_keep, gap_ratio=dry_gap)\n\n    top_probs = probs[top_idx]\n    logits = np.log(top_probs) / max(temperature, 1e-6)\n    if lookahead_bias is not None and boost_strength != 0.0:\n        logits = logits + boost_strength * np.asarray(lookahead_bias)[top_idx]\n    logits -= logits.max()\n    weights = np.exp(logits)\n    weights /= weights.sum()\n\n    choice = rng.choice(len(top_idx), p=weights)\n    return top_idx[choice]\n\n\ndef parse_payload(payload_str, word_to_idx):\n    vocab_size = len(word_to_idx)\n    payload_vector = np.zeros(vocab_size, dtype=np.float64)\n    if not payload_str:\n        return payload_vector\n    for item in payload_str.split(\",\"):\n        item = item.strip()\n        if not item:\n            continue\n        if \":\" in item:\n            word, weight_str = item.rsplit(\":\", 1)\n            try:\n                weight = float(weight_str)\n            except ValueError:\n                print(f\"[warn] payload entry '{item}' has a non-numeric weight; skipping\", file=sys.stderr)\n                continue\n        else:\n            word, weight = item, 1.0\n        word = word.strip().lower()\n        idx = word_to_idx.get(word)\n        if idx is None:\n            print(f\"[warn] payload word '{word}' is not in the vocabulary; skipping\", file=sys.stderr)\n            continue\n        payload_vector[idx] = weight\n    return payload_vector\n\n\nclass HECMModel:\n    \"\"\"Same model as before. Internally, the membrane, mass-density term,\n    and temporal index are now named as such and exposed as attributes\n    (self.rho_eff, self.tau) so they can be inspected directly rather than\n    inferred from comments.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, tokens, order=3, min_count=1, beta=0.0, alpha=0.0,\n                 max_diffusion_states=1500, payload=None, boost_strength=0.0,\n                 sort_mode=\"sine\"):\n        self.order = order\n        vocab, word_to_idx, counts = build_vocab(tokens, min_count=min_count)\n        self.vocab = vocab\n        self.word_to_idx = word_to_idx\n\n        contexts, context_to_idx, membrane, context_counts = build_membrane_transition_graph(\n            tokens, word_to_idx, order=order\n        )\n\n        perm = log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts, mode=sort_mode)\n        contexts = [contexts[i] for i in perm]\n        membrane = permute_matrix(membrane, perm)\n        context_to_idx = {ctx: i for i, ctx in enumerate(contexts)}\n\n        self.contexts = contexts\n        self.context_to_idx = context_to_idx\n        self.context_counts = context_counts\n\n        self.P_retrospective = membrane_flux_normalize(membrane)\n\n        num_states = len(contexts)\n        self.dense_bytes, self.rho_eff = compute_mass_density(num_states)\n\n        self.P_diffused = self.P_retrospective\n        self.P_final = self.P_retrospective\n\n        self.boost_strength = boost_strength\n        self.tau = compute_temporal_index(boost_strength)\n\n        if payload is None:\n            payload = np.zeros(len(vocab), dtype=np.float64)\n        self.payload = payload\n        self.context_word_score = np.array(\n            [sum(payload[w] for w in ctx) for ctx in contexts], dtype=np.float64\n        )\n        self.introduced_word_score = np.array(\n            [payload[ctx[-1]] for ctx in contexts], dtype=np.float64\n        )\n        self.context_total_score = self.context_word_score + 0.0\n\n    def vocab_size(self):\n        return len(self.vocab)\n\n    def num_states(self):\n        return len(self.contexts)\n\n    def _get_row(self, ctx_idx):\n        row = self.P_final[ctx_idx]\n        if sp.issparse(self.P_final):\n            return np.asarray(row.todense()).ravel()\n        return np.asarray(row)\n\n    def _tokenize_prompt(self, prompt):\n        toks = re.findall(r\"[a-z0-9']+|[.,!?;:]\", prompt.lower())\n        unk = self.word_to_idx[UNK]\n        return [self.word_to_idx.get(t, unk) for t in toks]\n\n    def _weighted_pick(self, candidates, boost_strength, rng):\n        if boost_strength == 0.0 or len(candidates) == 1:\n            return candidates[rng.integers(len(candidates))] if len(candidates) > 1 else candidates[0]\n        scores = boost_strength * self.context_total_score[candidates]\n        scores = scores - scores.max()\n        weights = np.exp(scores)\n        weights /= weights.sum()\n        return candidates[rng.choice(len(candidates), p=weights)]\n\n    def _initial_context(self, prompt_ids, rng, boost_strength=0.0):\n        ctx_len = self.order - 1\n        bos = self.word_to_idx[BOS]\n        padded = [bos] * ctx_len + prompt_ids\n        desired = tuple(padded[-ctx_len:])\n\n        if desired in self.context_to_idx:\n            return self.context_to_idx[desired]\n\n        for k in range(ctx_len - 1, 0, -1):\n            suffix = desired[-k:]\n            candidates = [i for i, ctx in enumerate(self.contexts) if ctx[-k:] == suffix]\n            if candidates:\n                return self._weighted_pick(candidates, boost_strength, rng)\n\n        counts_arr = np.array([self.context_counts.get(ctx, 0) for ctx in self.contexts])\n        top_candidates = list(np.argsort(counts_arr)[-10:])\n        return self._weighted_pick(top_candidates, boost_strength, rng)\n\n    def generate(self, prompt, max_new_tokens=40, top_k=10, base_temp=0.8,\n                 amplitude=0.4, omega=0.5, phase=0.0, seed=None,\n                 boost_strength=0.0, dry=True, dry_gap=4.0):\n        rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n        prompt_ids = self._tokenize_prompt(prompt)\n\n        ctx_idx = self._initial_context(prompt_ids, rng, boost_strength=boost_strength)\n        out_ids = list(prompt_ids)\n        quanta_crossed = 0  # N: count of boundary crossings this run\n\n        for step in range(max_new_tokens):\n            temp = sinusoidal_temperature(step, base_temp=base_temp, amplitude=amplitude,\n                                           omega=omega, phase=phase)\n            probs = self._get_row(ctx_idx)\n            next_ctx_idx = sample_at_boundary(\n                probs, temperature=temp, top_k=top_k, rng=rng,\n                lookahead_bias=self.introduced_word_score, boost_strength=boost_strength,\n                dry=dry, dry_gap=dry_gap,\n            )\n\n            if next_ctx_idx is None:\n                ctx_idx = self._initial_context(out_ids, rng, boost_strength=boost_strength)\n                continue\n\n            next_word_id = self.contexts[next_ctx_idx][-1]\n            out_ids.append(next_word_id)\n            ctx_idx = next_ctx_idx\n            quanta_crossed += 1\n\n        self.last_quanta_crossed = quanta_crossed\n        words = [self.vocab[i] for i in out_ids]\n        return _detokenize(words)\n\n\ndef _detokenize(tokens):\n    out = []\n    for tok in tokens:\n        if tok in (BOS,):\n            continue\n        if out and re.match(r\"[.,!?;:]\", tok):\n            out[-1] = out[-1] + tok\n        else:\n            out.append(tok)\n    text = \" \".join(out)\n    if text:\n        text = text[0].upper() + text[1:]\n    return text\n\n\ndef main():\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"HECM model, relabeled to the temporal-index paper's vocabulary\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--data\", nargs=\"*\", default=None)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--order\", type=int, default=3)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--min-count\", type=int, default=1)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--beta\", type=float, default=0.3, help=\"mass-density diffusion strength\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--alpha\", type=float, default=0.1, help=\"temporal-orientation blend factor\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--max-diffusion-states\", type=int, default=5)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--prompt\", type=str, default=\"the fox and\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--length\", type=int, default=400)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--top-k\", type=int, default=10)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--base-temp\", type=float, default=0.8)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--amplitude\", type=float, default=0.4)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--omega\", type=float, default=0.5)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--phase\", type=float, default=0.0)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--seed\", type=int, default=None)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--boost\", type=str, default=None)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--boost-strength\", type=float, default=1.0)\n    ap.add_argument(\"--sort-mode\", choices=[\"log\", \"sine\"], default=\"sine\",\n                    help=\"context ordering key: original -log(c+1) or exp(log(x)*sin(x))\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--no-dry\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"disable tail pruning\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--dry-gap\", type=float, default=4.0,\n                    help=\"prune tail when the steepest drop between sorted probs is >= this ratio\")\n    args = ap.parse_args()\n\n    tokens = load_corpus(paths=args.data)\n    print(f\"[info] loaded {len(tokens)} tokens\", file=sys.stderr)\n\n    boost_strength = args.boost_strength if args.boost else 0.0\n\n    model = HECMModel(\n        tokens, order=args.order, min_count=args.min_count,\n        beta=args.beta, alpha=args.alpha,\n        max_diffusion_states=args.max_diffusion_states,\n        boost_strength=boost_strength,\n        sort_mode=args.sort_mode,\n    )\n\n    print(\n        f\"[info] order={args.order} | vocab={model.vocab_size()} | \"\n        f\"membrane states={model.num_states()} | \"\n        f\"rho_eff={model.rho_eff:.1f} bytes/state | \"\n        f\"tau={model.tau:.3f}\",\n        file=sys.stderr,\n    )\n\n    if args.boost:\n        payload = parse_payload(args.boost, model.word_to_idx)\n        model.payload = payload\n        model.context_word_score = np.array(\n            [sum(payload[w] for w in ctx) for ctx in model.contexts], dtype=np.float64\n        )\n        model.introduced_word_score = np.array(\n            [payload[ctx[-1]] for ctx in model.contexts], dtype=np.float64\n        )\n        model.context_total_score = model.context_word_score\n\n    def run_once(prompt):\n        result = model.generate(\n            prompt, max_new_tokens=args.length, top_k=args.top_k,\n            base_temp=args.base_temp, amplitude=args.amplitude,\n            omega=args.omega, phase=args.phase, seed=args.seed,\n            boost_strength=boost_strength,\n            dry=not args.no_dry, dry_gap=args.dry_gap,\n        )\n        print(result)\n        print(f\"[info] quanta crossed this run (N): {model.last_quanta_crossed}\", file=sys.stderr)\n\n    print(\"[info] interactive mode -- type a prompt, or 'quit' to exit\", file=sys.stderr)\n    while True:\n        try:\n            prompt = input(\"prompt> \")\n        except EOFError:\n            break\n        if prompt.strip().lower() in (\"quit\", \"exit\"):\n            break\n        run_once(prompt)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n"}, "message": "Full HECM script with sine sort key and local-minimum drying applied", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"python\", \"code\": \"import argparse\\nimport glob\\nimport math\\nimport re\\nimport sys\\nfrom collections import Counter\\n\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport scipy.sparse as sp\\nfrom scipy.linalg import expm\\n\\nUNK = \\\"<unk>\\\"\\nBOS = \\\"<bos>\\\"\\n\\n\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 1. Dataset loading (unchanged \\u2014 no construct in the paper maps to this)\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\ndef load_corpus(paths=None, raw_text=None, lowercase=True, strip_punct=False):\\n    text_chunks = []\\n    if paths:\\n        found_any = False\\n        for pattern in paths:\\n            for fp in sorted(glob.glob(pattern)):\\n                found_any = True\\n                with open(fp, \\\"r\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\", errors=\\\"ignore\\\") as f:\\n                    text_chunks.append(f.read())\\n        if not found_any:\\n            print(f\\\"[warn] no files matched {paths}\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n\\n    if raw_text:\\n        text_chunks.append(raw_text)\\n    if not text_chunks:\\n        text_chunks.append(_DEMO_CORPUS)\\n\\n    full_text = \\\"\\\\n\\\".join(text_chunks)\\n    if lowercase:\\n        full_text = full_text.lower()\\n\\n    tokens = re.findall(r\\\"[a-z0-9']+|[.,!?;:]\\\", full_text)\\n    if strip_punct:\\n        tokens = [t for t in tokens if re.match(r\\\"[a-z0-9']+\\\", t)]\\n    return tokens\\n\\n\\nwith open(input(\\\"Filename: \\\"), \\\"r\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") as file:\\n    _DEMO_CORPUS = file.read()\\n\\n\\ndef build_vocab(tokens, min_count=1):\\n    counts = Counter(tokens)\\n    kept = sorted([w for w, c in counts.items() if c >= min_count])\\n    vocab = [UNK, BOS] + kept\\n    word_to_idx = {w: i for i, w in enumerate(vocab)}\\n    return vocab, word_to_idx, counts\\n\\n\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 2. The membrane: context-state construction\\n#\\n#    Exchange: \\\"topological energy membrane separating internal state space\\n#    from environment\\\" -> the boundary crossed when the Markov chain moves\\n#    from one context-state to the next. d-Omega is not a spatial surface;\\n#    it is the transition itself. Quanta N are tokens: one per crossing,\\n#    genuinely indivisible (the tokenizer's vocabulary IS a discrete set),\\n#    which is the one place in the whole exercise where \\\"quanta\\\" is not a\\n#    metaphor borrowed from QM \\u2014 it is just an accurate word for \\\"discrete\\n#    countable unit,\\\" which is what a token already was.\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\ndef build_membrane_transition_graph(tokens, word_to_idx, order=3):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Build the square context -> context transition count matrix. This\\n    IS the membrane: d-Omega = the set of (context_state -> context_state)\\n    edges; a single generation step = one crossing of d-Omega; the emitted\\n    word at that step = one quantum.\\n\\n    Renamed from build_ngram_contexts(). Same function, same sparse-matrix\\n    justification: a dense (num_contexts x num_contexts) \\\"membrane\\\" at\\n    real corpus scale is gigabytes to hundreds of gigabytes, which is\\n    exactly the mass-density blowup discussed in build_mass_density_term()\\n    below \\u2014 the membrane and the mass term are two names for adjacent\\n    facts about the same object, not two separate mechanisms.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    ctx_len = order - 1\\n    if ctx_len < 1:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"order must be >= 2\\\")\\n\\n    bos_idx = word_to_idx[BOS]\\n    unk_idx = word_to_idx[UNK]\\n    ids = [word_to_idx.get(t, unk_idx) for t in tokens]\\n    padded = [bos_idx] * ctx_len + ids\\n\\n    context_to_idx = {}\\n    contexts = []\\n    context_counts = Counter()\\n    edges = Counter()\\n\\n    def get_ctx_idx(ctx_tuple):\\n        if ctx_tuple not in context_to_idx:\\n            context_to_idx[ctx_tuple] = len(contexts)\\n            contexts.append(ctx_tuple)\\n        return context_to_idx[ctx_tuple]\\n\\n    n = len(padded)\\n    for i in range(n - ctx_len):\\n        ctx = tuple(padded[i:i + ctx_len])\\n        next_ctx = tuple(padded[i + 1:i + 1 + ctx_len])\\n        ci = get_ctx_idx(ctx)\\n        cj = get_ctx_idx(next_ctx)\\n        context_counts[ctx] += 1\\n        edges[(ci, cj)] += 1.0\\n\\n    m = len(contexts)\\n    if edges:\\n        rows, cols, data = zip(*[(r, c, v) for (r, c), v in edges.items()])\\n    else:\\n        rows, cols, data = (), (), ()\\n    membrane = sp.csr_matrix((data, (rows, cols)), shape=(m, m), dtype=np.float64)\\n\\n    return contexts, context_to_idx, membrane, context_counts\\n\\n\\ndef log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts, mode=\\\"sine\\\"):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return context indices ordered by a key on each context's count.\\n\\n    mode=\\\"log\\\":  original ordering, key = -log(c + 1)\\n    mode=\\\"sine\\\": key = exp(log(x) * sin(x)) with x = c + 1\\n                 (mathematically x ** sin(x); not monotonic in x)\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    def key(ctx):\\n        x = context_counts.get(ctx, 0) + 1.0\\n        if mode == \\\"sine\\\":\\n            return math.exp(math.log(x) * math.sin(x))\\n        return -math.log(x)\\n\\n    return sorted(range(len(contexts)), key=lambda i: key(contexts[i]))\\n\\n\\ndef permute_matrix(M, order):\\n    order = np.asarray(order)\\n    if sp.issparse(M):\\n        return M[order, :][:, order].tocsr()\\n    return M[np.ix_(order, order)]\\n\\n\\ndef membrane_flux_normalize(M, eps=1e-9):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Row-normalize the membrane's transition weights into a proper\\n    conservation law: total probability flux leaving any context-state\\n    across d-Omega sums to 1. This is the literal, enforced version of the\\n    paper's heuristic surface integral N = oint_dOmega sigma(x) dx \\u2014 not\\n    an analogy to it, an instance of it, since a row-stochastic matrix row\\n    IS a discrete measure over the boundary that sums to a fixed quantum\\n    budget per step.\\n\\n    Renamed from row_normalize(). Behavior unchanged.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    if sp.issparse(M):\\n        row_sums = np.asarray(M.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\\n        row_sums[row_sums == 0] = 1.0\\n        inv = sp.diags(1.0 / row_sums)\\n        return (inv @ M).tocsr()\\n    row_sums = M.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\\n    row_sums[row_sums == 0] = 1.0\\n    return M / (row_sums + eps)\\n\\n\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 3. Mass-density coupling term\\n#\\n#    Exchange: rho_eff (effective mass-density, coupled to retrospective\\n#    orientation) -> the memory footprint and O(n^2)-to-O(n^3) compute cost\\n#    of diffusing probability mass across the membrane. This is the ONE\\n#    construct in the original paper that upgrades from metaphor to fact\\n#    when ported to code: \\\"retrospection carries more computational mass\\\"\\n#    stops being poetic and becomes a literal, measurable claim about\\n#    bytes and FLOPs, which is exactly what the original file's own\\n#    docstring already said before any relabeling (\\\"137,702 contexts ->\\n#    ~141 GiB\\\").\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\ndef build_mass_density_term(P, beta=0.15, remove_self_loops=True):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"expm(beta * (P - I)): the diffusion/propagation step. In the paper's\\n    vocabulary this is where mass-density is \\\"felt\\\" \\u2014 it is the most\\n    expensive operation in the pipeline (O(n^3) for a state space of size\\n    n), and its cost is precisely what rho_eff = M(t)/|Omega_active(t)|\\n    measures. A system leaning harder on this term (more diffusion, less\\n    raw/verbatim retrospective lookup) really does carry more\\n    computational mass: more memory, more latency, more bandwidth.\\n\\n    Renamed from diffusion_weighting(). Behavior unchanged, including the\\n    self-loop caveat: exp(-beta) concentrates on the diagonal for small\\n    beta, which would make generation stick to one context-state forever\\n    (i.e., infinite effective mass, zero motion) unless removed.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    m = P.shape[0]\\n    L = P - np.eye(m)\\n    D = expm(beta * L)\\n    D = np.clip(D, 0, None)\\n\\n    if remove_self_loops:\\n        np.fill_diagonal(D, 0.0)\\n        row_sums = D.sum(axis=1)\\n        dead_rows = row_sums <= 1e-12\\n        if np.any(dead_rows):\\n            fallback = P.copy()\\n            np.fill_diagonal(fallback, 0.0)\\n            fb_sums = fallback.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\\n            fb_sums[fb_sums == 0] = 1.0\\n            fallback = fallback / fb_sums\\n            D[dead_rows] = fallback[dead_rows]\\n\\n    D = membrane_flux_normalize(D)\\n    return D\\n\\n\\ndef blend_temporal_orientation(P_retrospective, P_diffused, alpha=0.5,\\n                                remove_self_loops=True):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Blend the purely retrospective (raw n-gram, tau = -1) distribution\\n    with the mass-density-diffused one, controlled by alpha. alpha is the\\n    closest thing in this codebase to a dial on tau: alpha=0 is maximally\\n    retrospective (pure lookup), alpha=1 is maximally diffused (mass-heavy,\\n    still not \\\"anticipatory\\\" in any real sense \\u2014 see the note in\\n    compute_temporal_index() below about why diffusion isn't lookahead).\\n\\n    Renamed from blend(). Behavior unchanged.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    P = (1 - alpha) * P_retrospective * alpha * P_diffused\\n    if remove_self_loops:\\n        np.fill_diagonal(P, 0.0)\\n        P = membrane_flux_normalize(P)\\n    return P\\n\\n\\ndef compute_mass_density(num_states, dtype_bytes=8):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"rho_eff = M(t) / |Omega_active(t)|, made concrete: bytes required\\n    for a DENSE membrane of this many context-states, divided by the\\n    number of active states. This is the real, computable version of the\\n    coupling constant lambda from the original speculative paper \\u2014 no\\n    free parameter, just arithmetic.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    dense_bytes = (num_states ** 2) * dtype_bytes\\n    rho_eff = dense_bytes / max(num_states, 1)\\n    return dense_bytes, rho_eff\\n\\n\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 4. Temporal index computation\\n#\\n#    Exchange: tau (subjective temporal orientation) -> fraction of\\n#    per-step decision compute spent on the payload lookahead-rerank\\n#    (prospective: scoring a word BEFORE it's committed) versus the raw\\n#    context lookup (retrospective: reading what was already observed).\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\ndef compute_temporal_index(boost_strength, lookahead_ops=1, retrospective_ops=1):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"tau = (C_lookahead - C_retrospective) / (C_lookahead + C_retrospective),\\n    gated by whether the payload customizer is active at all.\\n\\n    With boost_strength == 0, the payload rerank never executes (see\\n    sample_at_boundary below), so tau = -1 exactly: this generator is\\n    purely retrospective, same as any causal-masked autoregressive model.\\n\\n    With boost_strength > 0, the payload dot-product IS a one-step\\n    lookahead \\u2014 it scores the word that would be newly introduced by a\\n    candidate transition before that transition is taken \\u2014 so tau ticks\\n    positive by an amount proportional to how much compute that rerank\\n    consumes relative to the base lookup. This is a real, bounded,\\n    non-metaphorical number, not a stand-in for felt anticipation.\\n\\n    Note on the diffusion term: build_mass_density_term() does NOT count\\n    as lookahead, even though it's a \\\"future-looking\\\" propagator in the\\n    continuous-time-Markov sense. It diffuses probability mass over\\n    ALREADY-OBSERVED transitions; it doesn't evaluate not-yet-taken\\n    candidate words the way the payload rerank does. Mass-density and\\n    temporal index are independent axes here, exactly as the original\\n    paper treated rho_eff and tau as coupled-but-distinct variables.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    if boost_strength == 0.0:\\n        return -1.0\\n    total = lookahead_ops + retrospective_ops\\n    return (boost_strength * lookahead_ops - retrospective_ops) / (\\n        boost_strength * lookahead_ops + retrospective_ops\\n    )\\n\\n\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 5. Sinusoidal temperature \\u2014 kept under its plain name\\n#\\n#    This is the one function this file will NOT rename into the paper's\\n#    \\\"harmonic resonance\\\" vocabulary. See module docstring.\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\ndef sinusoidal_temperature(step, base_temp=0.8, amplitude=0.4, omega=0.5, phase=0.0, floor=0.05):\\n    t = base_temp + amplitude * math.sin(omega * step + phase)\\n    return max(floor, t)\\n\\n\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# 6. Sampling: where the decision operator D acts on the rule R\\n#\\n#    Exchange: \\\"decisions precede the rule-set\\\" -> D narrows/reranks the\\n#    ALREADY-LAWFUL candidate set (nonzero-probability transitions); it\\n#    can never make a zero-probability transition eligible. R (the\\n#    observed n-gram law, P_final) is never edited by D. This is the\\n#    weak, defensible reading of the inversion, not the strong claim that\\n#    decisions rewrite physics.\\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\ndef dry_local_minimum(probs, idx, min_keep=2, gap_ratio=4.0):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Prune the tail below the steepest drop in the sorted candidate probs.\\n\\n    probs: the full probability row; idx: candidate indices (nonzero, after top_k).\\n    Returns the subset of idx to keep. The cut only happens when the biggest\\n    drop is at least `gap_ratio`x, so flat distributions are left untouched.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    if len(idx) <= min_keep:\\n        return idx\\n    order = np.argsort(probs[idx])[::-1]          # descending by prob\\n    sorted_p = probs[idx][order]\\n    drops = sorted_p[:-1] / sorted_p[1:]           # p_i / p_{i+1}\\n    # only consider cut points that keep at least min_keep candidates\\n    drops[: min_keep - 1] = 0.0\\n    cut = int(np.argmax(drops))\\n    if drops[cut] < gap_ratio:\\n        return idx\\n    return idx[order[: cut + 1]]\\n\\n\\ndef sample_at_boundary(probs, temperature=1.0, top_k=10, rng=None,\\n                        lookahead_bias=None, boost_strength=0.0,\\n                        dry=True, dry_gap=4.0, dry_min_keep=2):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Cross d-Omega once: pick the next context-state (i.e. emit one\\n    quantum). `lookahead_bias` is the decision operator D's input;\\n    `probs` (P_final's row) is the rule R. D can only rerank within\\n    nonzero_idx \\u2014 the domain R already permits \\u2014 never expand it.\\n\\n    Drying: after top-k, the tail below the steepest drop in the sorted\\n    candidate probabilities is pruned, before temperature and the\\n    lookahead boost, so the boost cannot revive a pruned candidate.\\n\\n    Renamed from sample_next(). Behavior unchanged apart from drying.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    rng = rng or np.random.default_rng()\\n    probs = np.asarray(probs, dtype=np.float64)\\n\\n    nonzero_idx = np.nonzero(probs > 0)[0]\\n    if len(nonzero_idx) == 0:\\n        return None\\n\\n    if top_k is not None and 0 < top_k < len(nonzero_idx):\\n        sub = np.argpartition(probs[nonzero_idx], -top_k)[-top_k:]\\n        top_idx = nonzero_idx[sub]\\n    else:\\n        top_idx = nonzero_idx\\n\\n    if dry:\\n        top_idx = dry_local_minimum(probs, top_idx,\\n                                    min_keep=dry_min_keep, gap_ratio=dry_gap)\\n\\n    top_probs = probs[top_idx]\\n    logits = np.log(top_probs) / max(temperature, 1e-6)\\n    if lookahead_bias is not None and boost_strength != 0.0:\\n        logits = logits + boost_strength * np.asarray(lookahead_bias)[top_idx]\\n    logits -= logits.max()\\n    weights = np.exp(logits)\\n    weights /= weights.sum()\\n\\n    choice = rng.choice(len(top_idx), p=weights)\\n    return top_idx[choice]\\n\\n\\ndef parse_payload(payload_str, word_to_idx):\\n    vocab_size = len(word_to_idx)\\n    payload_vector = np.zeros(vocab_size, dtype=np.float64)\\n    if not payload_str:\\n        return payload_vector\\n    for item in payload_str.split(\\\",\\\"):\\n        item = item.strip()\\n        if not item:\\n            continue\\n        if \\\":\\\" in item:\\n            word, weight_str = item.rsplit(\\\":\\\", 1)\\n            try:\\n                weight = float(weight_str)\\n            except ValueError:\\n                print(f\\\"[warn] payload entry '{item}' has a non-numeric weight; skipping\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n                continue\\n        else:\\n            word, weight = item, 1.0\\n        word = word.strip().lower()\\n        idx = word_to_idx.get(word)\\n        if idx is None:\\n            print(f\\\"[warn] payload word '{word}' is not in the vocabulary; skipping\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n            continue\\n        payload_vector[idx] = weight\\n    return payload_vector\\n\\n\\nclass HECMModel:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Same model as before. Internally, the membrane, mass-density term,\\n    and temporal index are now named as such and exposed as attributes\\n    (self.rho_eff, self.tau) so they can be inspected directly rather than\\n    inferred from comments.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\n    def __init__(self, tokens, order=3, min_count=1, beta=0.0, alpha=0.0,\\n                 max_diffusion_states=1500, payload=None, boost_strength=0.0,\\n                 sort_mode=\\\"sine\\\"):\\n        self.order = order\\n        vocab, word_to_idx, counts = build_vocab(tokens, min_count=min_count)\\n        self.vocab = vocab\\n        self.word_to_idx = word_to_idx\\n\\n        contexts, context_to_idx, membrane, context_counts = build_membrane_transition_graph(\\n            tokens, word_to_idx, order=order\\n        )\\n\\n        perm = log_sort_contexts(contexts, context_counts, mode=sort_mode)\\n        contexts = [contexts[i] for i in perm]\\n        membrane = permute_matrix(membrane, perm)\\n        context_to_idx = {ctx: i for i, ctx in enumerate(contexts)}\\n\\n        self.contexts = contexts\\n        self.context_to_idx = context_to_idx\\n        self.context_counts = context_counts\\n\\n        self.P_retrospective = membrane_flux_normalize(membrane)\\n\\n        num_states = len(contexts)\\n        self.dense_bytes, self.rho_eff = compute_mass_density(num_states)\\n\\n        self.P_diffused = self.P_retrospective\\n        self.P_final = self.P_retrospective\\n\\n        self.boost_strength = boost_strength\\n        self.tau = compute_temporal_index(boost_strength)\\n\\n        if payload is None:\\n            payload = np.zeros(len(vocab), dtype=np.float64)\\n        self.payload = payload\\n        self.context_word_score = np.array(\\n            [sum(payload[w] for w in ctx) for ctx in contexts], dtype=np.float64\\n        )\\n        self.introduced_word_score = np.array(\\n            [payload[ctx[-1]] for ctx in contexts], dtype=np.float64\\n        )\\n        self.context_total_score = self.context_word_score + 0.0\\n\\n    def vocab_size(self):\\n        return len(self.vocab)\\n\\n    def num_states(self):\\n        return len(self.contexts)\\n\\n    def _get_row(self, ctx_idx):\\n        row = self.P_final[ctx_idx]\\n        if sp.issparse(self.P_final):\\n            return np.asarray(row.todense()).ravel()\\n        return np.asarray(row)\\n\\n    def _tokenize_prompt(self, prompt):\\n        toks = re.findall(r\\\"[a-z0-9']+|[.,!?;:]\\\", prompt.lower())\\n        unk = self.word_to_idx[UNK]\\n        return [self.word_to_idx.get(t, unk) for t in toks]\\n\\n    def _weighted_pick(self, candidates, boost_strength, rng):\\n        if boost_strength == 0.0 or len(candidates) == 1:\\n            return candidates[rng.integers(len(candidates))] if len(candidates) > 1 else candidates[0]\\n        scores = boost_strength * self.context_total_score[candidates]\\n        scores = scores - scores.max()\\n        weights = np.exp(scores)\\n        weights /= weights.sum()\\n        return candidates[rng.choice(len(candidates), p=weights)]\\n\\n    def _initial_context(self, prompt_ids, rng, boost_strength=0.0):\\n        ctx_len = self.order - 1\\n        bos = self.word_to_idx[BOS]\\n        padded = [bos] * ctx_len + prompt_ids\\n        desired = tuple(padded[-ctx_len:])\\n\\n        if desired in self.context_to_idx:\\n            return self.context_to_idx[desired]\\n\\n        for k in range(ctx_len - 1, 0, -1):\\n            suffix = desired[-k:]\\n            candidates = [i for i, ctx in enumerate(self.contexts) if ctx[-k:] == suffix]\\n            if candidates:\\n                return self._weighted_pick(candidates, boost_strength, rng)\\n\\n        counts_arr = np.array([self.context_counts.get(ctx, 0) for ctx in self.contexts])\\n        top_candidates = list(np.argsort(counts_arr)[-10:])\\n        return self._weighted_pick(top_candidates, boost_strength, rng)\\n\\n    def generate(self, prompt, max_new_tokens=40, top_k=10, base_temp=0.8,\\n                 amplitude=0.4, omega=0.5, phase=0.0, seed=None,\\n                 boost_strength=0.0, dry=True, dry_gap=4.0):\\n        rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\\n        prompt_ids = self._tokenize_prompt(prompt)\\n\\n        ctx_idx = self._initial_context(prompt_ids, rng, boost_strength=boost_strength)\\n        out_ids = list(prompt_ids)\\n        quanta_crossed = 0  # N: count of boundary crossings this run\\n\\n        for step in range(max_new_tokens):\\n            temp = sinusoidal_temperature(step, base_temp=base_temp, amplitude=amplitude,\\n                                           omega=omega, phase=phase)\\n            probs = self._get_row(ctx_idx)\\n            next_ctx_idx = sample_at_boundary(\\n                probs, temperature=temp, top_k=top_k, rng=rng,\\n                lookahead_bias=self.introduced_word_score, boost_strength=boost_strength,\\n                dry=dry, dry_gap=dry_gap,\\n            )\\n\\n            if next_ctx_idx is None:\\n                ctx_idx = self._initial_context(out_ids, rng, boost_strength=boost_strength)\\n                continue\\n\\n            next_word_id = self.contexts[next_ctx_idx][-1]\\n            out_ids.append(next_word_id)\\n            ctx_idx = next_ctx_idx\\n            quanta_crossed += 1\\n\\n        self.last_quanta_crossed = quanta_crossed\\n        words = [self.vocab[i] for i in out_ids]\\n        return _detokenize(words)\\n\\n\\ndef _detokenize(tokens):\\n    out = []\\n    for tok in tokens:\\n        if tok in (BOS,):\\n            continue\\n        if out and re.match(r\\\"[.,!?;:]\\\", tok):\\n            out[-1] = out[-1] + tok\\n        else:\\n            out.append(tok)\\n    text = \\\" \\\".join(out)\\n    if text:\\n        text = text[0].upper() + text[1:]\\n    return text\\n\\n\\ndef main():\\n    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\\\"HECM model, relabeled to the temporal-index paper's vocabulary\\\")\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--data\\\", nargs=\\\"*\\\", default=None)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--order\\\", type=int, default=3)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--min-count\\\", type=int, default=1)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--beta\\\", type=float, default=0.3, help=\\\"mass-density diffusion strength\\\")\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--alpha\\\", type=float, default=0.1, help=\\\"temporal-orientation blend factor\\\")\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--max-diffusion-states\\\", type=int, default=5)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--prompt\\\", type=str, default=\\\"the fox and\\\")\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--length\\\", type=int, default=400)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--top-k\\\", type=int, default=10)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--base-temp\\\", type=float, default=0.8)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--amplitude\\\", type=float, default=0.4)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--omega\\\", type=float, default=0.5)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--phase\\\", type=float, default=0.0)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--seed\\\", type=int, default=None)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--boost\\\", type=str, default=None)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--boost-strength\\\", type=float, default=1.0)\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--sort-mode\\\", choices=[\\\"log\\\", \\\"sine\\\"], default=\\\"sine\\\",\\n                    help=\\\"context ordering key: original -log(c+1) or exp(log(x)*sin(x))\\\")\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--no-dry\\\", action=\\\"store_true\\\", help=\\\"disable tail pruning\\\")\\n    ap.add_argument(\\\"--dry-gap\\\", type=float, default=4.0,\\n                    help=\\\"prune tail when the steepest drop between sorted probs is >= this ratio\\\")\\n    args = ap.parse_args()\\n\\n    tokens = load_corpus(paths=args.data)\\n    print(f\\\"[info] loaded {len(tokens)} tokens\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n\\n    boost_strength = args.boost_strength if args.boost else 0.0\\n\\n    model = HECMModel(\\n        tokens, order=args.order, min_count=args.min_count,\\n        beta=args.beta, alpha=args.alpha,\\n        max_diffusion_states=args.max_diffusion_states,\\n        boost_strength=boost_strength,\\n        sort_mode=args.sort_mode,\\n    )\\n\\n    print(\\n        f\\\"[info] order={args.order} | vocab={model.vocab_size()} | \\\"\\n        f\\\"membrane states={model.num_states()} | \\\"\\n        f\\\"rho_eff={model.rho_eff:.1f} bytes/state | \\\"\\n        f\\\"tau={model.tau:.3f}\\\",\\n        file=sys.stderr,\\n    )\\n\\n    if args.boost:\\n        payload = parse_payload(args.boost, model.word_to_idx)\\n        model.payload = payload\\n        model.context_word_score = np.array(\\n            [sum(payload[w] for w in ctx) for ctx in model.contexts], dtype=np.float64\\n        )\\n        model.introduced_word_score = np.array(\\n            [payload[ctx[-1]] for ctx in model.contexts], dtype=np.float64\\n        )\\n        model.context_total_score = model.context_word_score\\n\\n    def run_once(prompt):\\n        result = model.generate(\\n            prompt, max_new_tokens=args.length, top_k=args.top_k,\\n            base_temp=args.base_temp, amplitude=args.amplitude,\\n            omega=args.omega, phase=args.phase, seed=args.seed,\\n            boost_strength=boost_strength,\\n            dry=not args.no_dry, dry_gap=args.dry_gap,\\n        )\\n        print(result)\\n        print(f\\\"[info] quanta crossed this run (N): {model.last_quanta_crossed}\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n\\n    print(\\\"[info] interactive mode -- type a prompt, or 'quit' to exit\\\", file=sys.stderr)\\n    while True:\\n        try:\\n            prompt = input(\\\"prompt> \\\")\\n        except EOFError:\\n            break\\n        if prompt.strip().lower() in (\\\"quit\\\", \\\"exit\\\"):\\n            break\\n        run_once(prompt)\\n\\n\\nif __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\":\\n    main()\\n\", \"filename\": \"/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:19:08.950160Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:19:08.950197Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01NbD5suC9BjPQUqmrSUt5nM", "name": "create_file", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "File created successfully: /mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "File created successfully: /mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:19:10.620124Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:19:10.837674Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01C4N6KkmFjB3ZjTFEpeuGMr", "name": "present_files", "input": {"filepaths": ["/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py"]}, "message": "Presenting file(s)...", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "table", "table": [["filepaths", "['/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py']"]]}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:19:10.858961Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:19:10.858987Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01C4N6KkmFjB3ZjTFEpeuGMr", "name": "present_files", "content": [{"type": "local_resource", "file_path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py", "name": "hecm", "mime_type": "text/x-python", "artifact_publishable": false}, {"type": "text", "text": "The file cards appear at the end of your reply if it ends with text, otherwise under this step; say they are below only if you finish the reply with text."}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": "Presented file", "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:19:14.001660Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:19:14.015141Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "The full script is in `hecm.py`, with both changes applied:\n\n- **Sine sort key:** `log_sort_contexts` takes a `mode` argument, and `--sort-mode {log,sine}` (default `sine`) is passed through `HECMModel`.\n- **Drying:** `dry_local_minimum` runs inside `sample_at_boundary` after the top-k step, controlled by `--dry-gap` (default 4.0) and `--no-dry`.\n\nI left everything else as you had it, including the two bugs I mentioned earlier. `blend_temporal_orientation` still multiplies where it should add, and `--beta` and `--alpha` still have no effect. The top-level `input(\"Filename: \")` still runs at import time.\n\nI haven't run the script, since it needs your corpus file.", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-09-29T09:19:14.142393Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-29T09:19:14.142393Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ec75-2cf6-7a4c-97cc-098df4fae75e"}, {"uuid": "01a0ec7c-9edc-7db2-b432-a0888de169a2", "text": "use 2D prob mirrors to geometrically distort the area the probs influence, resulting in shattered glass style wordage", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:26:24.896400Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:26:24.896400Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "use 2D prob mirrors to geometrically distort the area the probs influence, resulting in shattered glass style wordage", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-09-29T09:26:24.896570Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-29T09:26:24.896570Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a0ec75-2cf6-705b-9a56-8185568f1461"}, {"uuid": "01a0ec7c-9edc-77cd-bdf6-0aee32dc0211", "text": "\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nI'm wiring the shattered-mirror distortion into the generation loop and the CLI, and after that I'll present the updated file.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n`hecm.py` now has the shattered-glass mirrors, on by default at `--shatter 0.6`.\n\n**How it works**\n- **The plane:** each context-state gets a 2D position from the 2nd and 3rd singular vectors of the transition matrix, so states with similar successors sit close together. Tiny or degenerate matrices fall back to a random layout.\n- **The glass:** the plane is split into Voronoi shards, and each shard has its own mirror line.\n- **The reflection:** each surviving candidate's probability is emitted from its reflected position as a Gaussian bump, and it lands on whichever candidates sit near that reflected image. Neighbours in different shards get thrown in unrelated directions, which gives the fragmented, non-local word choices.\n- **The order:** distortion runs after top-k and drying and before temperature and the lookahead boost, so it only reweights candidates that were already allowed. It is blended with the original distribution, so it can't make a zero-probability word eligible.\n\n**New flags**\n- `--shatter` is the strength from 0 to 1, where 0 turns it off. Higher values give more broken output.\n- `--shards` is the number of mirrors. More shards mean smaller, more chaotic fragments.\n- `--shatter-sigma` is the width of each reflected bump. Small values scatter mass onto a few far-off candidates, and large values smear it back toward the original.\n- `--shatter-drift` is how far the mirrors rotate each step, so the fracture pattern changes as the text goes on.\n- `--shatter-seed` fixes the crack layout.\n\nI haven't run it, since it needs your corpus file. One thing to expect: the effect depends on how many candidates each context has. Rows with only one or two successors, which are common at order 3, have nothing to shatter, so the glass mostly shows up in high-branching contexts. Lowering `--dry-gap` or raising `--top-k` keeps more candidates alive for the mirrors to act on.", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:26:24.890555Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:26:57.047284Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Designing a shattered-glass text effect using distorted probability mirrors."}, {"summary": "Mapping candidate positions and coding mirror-based reflection math."}, {"summary": "Blending mirrored influence with original probabilities and planning code edits."}, {"summary": "Planning targeted code edits to integrate the mirror-distortion feature."}, {"summary": "Computing shard geometry and distance-based distortion strength."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:26:57.047345Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:26:57.384045Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_017jJqSktNekGtQ6Y31j7mGU", "name": "str_replace", "input": {"path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py", "description": "Import svds for the 2D embedding", "old_str": "from scipy.linalg import expm\n", "new_str": "from scipy.linalg import expm\nfrom scipy.sparse.linalg import svds\n"}, "message": "Import svds for the 2D embedding", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "edit", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Import svds for the 2D embedding"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:26:58.710756Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:26:58.710788Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_017jJqSktNekGtQ6Y31j7mGU", "name": "str_replace", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "edit", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:27:00.602552Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:27:09.575886Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_0199X9nbWqxdZYLvnqz1Qpyj", "name": "str_replace", "input": {"path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py", "description": "Add the shattered-mirror class and new sample_at_boundary signature", "old_str": "def sample_at_boundary(probs, temperature=1.0, top_k=10, rng=None,\n                        lookahead_bias=None, boost_strength=0.0,\n                        dry=True, dry_gap=4.0, dry_min_keep=2):", "new_str": "def build_mirror_coords(P, seed=0):\n    \"\"\"Place every context-state on a 2D plane. Uses the 2nd and 3rd singular\n    vectors of the transition matrix (the 1st is the trivial stationary mode),\n    so states that transition to similar places sit near each other. Falls\n    back to random positions for tiny or degenerate matrices.\"\"\"\n    m = P.shape[0]\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    coords = None\n    if m > 4:\n        try:\n            u, s, _ = svds(P.astype(np.float64), k=3, v0=rng.standard_normal(m))\n            order = np.argsort(-s)[1:3]\n            coords = u[:, order] * s[order]\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(f\"[warn] svds embedding failed ({e}); using random 2D layout\", file=sys.stderr)\n    if coords is None or not np.all(np.isfinite(coords)) or np.ptp(coords) < 1e-12:\n        coords = rng.standard_normal((m, 2))\n    coords = coords - coords.mean(axis=0)\n    coords = coords / max(np.abs(coords).max(), 1e-12)\n    return coords\n\n\nclass ShatterMirrors:\n    \"\"\"A pane of broken glass laid over the candidate plane.\n\n    The plane is split into Voronoi shards. Each shard has its own mirror\n    line. A candidate's probability mass is not spent on the candidate\n    itself: it is emitted from the candidate's REFLECTED position (reflected\n    in its shard's mirror), as a Gaussian bump of width `sigma`, and lands\n    on whichever candidates sit near that reflected image. Shard borders\n    are discontinuities, so neighbouring candidates in different shards get\n    thrown in unrelated directions -- that is the shattering. `drift`\n    slowly rotates the mirrors each step so the fracture pattern evolves.\n\n    The distortion only re-weights candidates that already survived\n    top-k/drying, and it is blended with the original distribution via\n    `strength`, so it can never make a zero-probability transition eligible.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, coords, n_shards=7, strength=0.6, sigma=0.35,\n                 drift=0.05, seed=0):\n        rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n        self.coords = coords\n        self.strength = strength\n        self.sigma = max(sigma, 1e-3)\n        self.drift = drift\n        self.seeds = rng.uniform(-1, 1, (n_shards, 2))\n        self.angles = rng.uniform(0, math.pi, n_shards)\n        self.offsets = rng.uniform(-0.3, 0.3, (n_shards, 2))\n\n    def distort(self, idx, p, step=0):\n        if self.strength <= 0 or len(idx) < 2:\n            return p\n        p = p / p.sum()\n        pts = self.coords[idx]                                   # (n, 2)\n\n        # which shard each candidate lives in\n        d2 = ((pts[:, None, :] - self.seeds[None, :, :]) ** 2).sum(-1)\n        shard = d2.argmin(axis=1)\n\n        # reflect each candidate across its shard's mirror line\n        ang = self.angles[shard] + self.drift * step\n        dirs = np.stack([np.cos(ang), np.sin(ang)], axis=1)\n        a = self.seeds[shard] + self.offsets[shard]\n        rel = pts - a\n        proj = (rel * dirs).sum(axis=1, keepdims=True) * dirs\n        refl = a + 2.0 * proj - rel\n\n        # mass emitted from reflected images lands on nearby candidates\n        d2 = ((pts[:, None, :] - refl[None, :, :]) ** 2).sum(-1)  # [target, source]\n        kernel = np.exp(-d2 / (2.0 * self.sigma ** 2))\n        landed = kernel @ p\n        total = landed.sum()\n        if total <= 1e-300:\n            return p\n        landed = landed / total\n\n        mixed = (1.0 - self.strength) * p + self.strength * landed\n        return mixed / mixed.sum()\n\n\ndef sample_at_boundary(probs, temperature=1.0, top_k=10, rng=None,\n                        lookahead_bias=None, boost_strength=0.0,\n                        dry=True, dry_gap=4.0, dry_min_keep=2,\n                        mirror=None, step=0):"}, "message": "Add the shattered-mirror class and new sample_at_boundary signature", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "edit", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Add the shattered-mirror class and new sample_at_boundary signature"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:27:10.137387Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:27:10.137426Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_0199X9nbWqxdZYLvnqz1Qpyj", "name": "str_replace", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "edit", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:27:11.657597Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:27:12.876866Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01JYhbj7EkvewE6nna19njD9", "name": "str_replace", "input": {"path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py", "description": "Apply mirror distortion to candidate probs before temperature/boost", "old_str": "    top_probs = probs[top_idx]\n    logits = np.log(top_probs) / max(temperature, 1e-6)", "new_str": "    top_probs = probs[top_idx]\n    if mirror is not None:\n        top_probs = mirror.distort(top_idx, top_probs, step=step)\n    logits = np.log(top_probs) / max(temperature, 1e-6)"}, "message": "Apply mirror distortion to candidate probs before temperature/boost", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "edit", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Apply mirror distortion to candidate probs before temperature/boost"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:27:13.177611Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:27:13.177642Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01JYhbj7EkvewE6nna19njD9", "name": "str_replace", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "edit", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:27:15.085594Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:27:16.009782Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_0166p15MxJsXkCmyY8f9tFBv", "name": "str_replace", "input": {"path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py", "description": "Add shatter params to HECMModel constructor", "old_str": "                 sort_mode=\"sine\"):\n        self.order = order", "new_str": "                 sort_mode=\"sine\", shatter=0.0, shards=7, shatter_sigma=0.35,\n                 shatter_drift=0.05, shatter_seed=0):\n        self.order = order"}, "message": "Add shatter params to HECMModel constructor", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "edit", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Add shatter params to HECMModel constructor"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:27:16.285807Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:27:16.285836Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_0166p15MxJsXkCmyY8f9tFBv", "name": "str_replace", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "edit", "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:27:17.773759Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:27:19.030261Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_011G9rg6K8Z8e9f4nkdyB1Gt", "name": "str_replace", "input": {"path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/hecm.py", "description": "Build the mirror plane after P_final is set", "old_str": "        self.P_final = self.P_retrospective\n", "new_str": "        self.P_final = self.P_retrospective\n\n        self.mirror = None\n        if shatter > 0.0:\n            coords = build_mirror_coords(self.P_final, seed=shatter_seed)\n            self.mirror = ShatterMirrors(\n                coords, n_shards=shards, strength=shatter, sigma=shatter_sigma,\n                drift=shatter_drift, seed=shatter_seed,\n            )\n"}, "message": "Build the mirror plane after P_final is set", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "edit", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Build the mirror plane after P_final is set"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:27:19.293846Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-29T09:27:19.293879Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_011G9rg6K8Z8e9f4nkdyB1Gt", "name": "str_replace", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Successfully replaced string in /mnt/use