mestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:08.843958Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:09.442060Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01FxgJZkuuyboY4Hsha9UE6v", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "cd /home/claude/OS && grep -rn \"out of memory\\|Out of memory\\|OOM\\|oom\\|alloc failed\\|bump\" --include=*.cpp --include=*.h --include=*.c . 2>/dev/null | grep -v \"bochs-2\\|tcc-mob\\|tinycc\\|^./build\" | head -30", "description": "Find out-of-memory handling and allocator code"}, "message": "Find out-of-memory handling and allocator code", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cd /home/claude/OS && grep -rn \\\"out of memory\\\\|Out of memory\\\\|OOM\\\\|oom\\\\|alloc failed\\\\|bump\\\" --include=*.cpp --include=*.h --include=*.c . 2>/dev/null | grep -v \\\"bochs-2\\\\|tcc-mob\\\\|tinycc\\\\|^./build\\\" | head -30\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:10.294543Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:10.294612Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01FxgJZkuuyboY4Hsha9UE6v", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"./bochs_cstubs.c:11: *   M1. The bump allocator below backs every malloc/new in the kernel\\n./bochs_cstubs.c:32: *       size to a multiple of 8 before bumping the pointer. This\\n./bochs_cstubs.c:62: * Total \u2248 22.5 MiB. 48 MiB is roughly 2\u00d7 to leave room for the kernel\\n./bochs_cstubs.c:91:void free(void* p) { (void)p; }   /* bump allocator: no-op free */\\n./bochs_cstubs.c:98: * and the kernel OOM-halts. This 48 MiB pool was sized specifically for\\n./bochs_cstubs.c:104: *                              bump allocator never frees individually). */\\n./bochs_glue .cpp:296:// Reserve room for 256 tiny per-vector stubs + one shared tail.\\n./bochs_glue .cpp:1099:    // tables out of the bump pool) and were the explicit reason\\n./matrix_array.h:61:    uint32_t ver_num;      // bumped on every successful W\\n./test_module.cpp:298: * in BSS so it costs nothing on disk and no bump-pool allocation. */\\n./test_module.cpp:455:    /* Leave room above the image for a stack; ESP starts at the very\\n./test_module.cpp:457:     * guest. Require at least 64KB of headroom for the stack. */\\n./editf.c:38: *     smaller mid-session and the editor just fills whatever room\\n./editf.c:105:    s_edit_h = GFX_HEIGHT - EDIT_Y - 16;   /* room left for the status line below */\\n./editf.c:396:         * room, otherwise it drops to its own row instead \u2014 on a\\n./test_main.cpp:11://   - Operator new/delete that go to the bump allocator in\\n./test_main.cpp:34:    // From bochs_cstubs.c bump allocator\\n./test_main.cpp:282:// allocate from the bump pool (which is now committed to Bochs).\\n./tcc_glue.h:72: *   TCC_ERR_NOMEM      (-5)  \u2014 kernel allocator OOM\\n./bochs_glue.cpp:296:// Reserve room for 256 tiny per-vector stubs + one shared tail.\\n./bochs_glue.cpp:1128:    // tables out of the bump pool) and were the explicit reason\\n./bochs_glue.cpp:1801://   g_bochs_gfx_serial  -- bumped whenever a guest's visible canvas changes\\n./gui_demo.c:11: * Click the button to bump a counter, drag the scrollbar to change\\n./kernel_parts/02_boot_info_and_graphics_driver.h:56:// oom_halt). Here we just define the single instance.\\n./kernel_parts/02_boot_info_and_graphics_driver.h:499:    // so we have room for the EIP + breadcrumb trail.\\n./kernel_parts/06_fat32_filesystem_and_explorer.h:1261:// If (b) doesn't leave enough contiguous room before the end of that\\n./kernel_parts/06_fat32_filesystem_and_explorer.h:1356:    int room_in_cluster = entries_per_cluster - end_entry_in_cluster;\\n./kernel_parts/06_fat32_filesystem_and_explorer.h:1357:    if (room_in_cluster >= total_slots) {\\n./kernel_parts/06_fat32_filesystem_and_explorer.h:1397:    int fits_in_cbuf1 = room_in_cluster; // < total_slots, guaranteed by the branch above\\n./kernel_parts/06_fat32_filesystem_and_explorer.h:2073:        return -2; // Out of memory\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"./bochs_cstubs.c:11: *   M1. The bump allocator below backs every malloc/new in the kernel\\n./bochs_cstubs.c:32: *       size to a multiple of 8 before bumping the pointer. This\\n./bochs_cstubs.c:62: * Total \u2248 22.5 MiB. 48 MiB is roughly 2\u00d7 to leave room for the kernel\\n./bochs_cstubs.c:91:void free(void* p) { (void)p; }   /* bump allocator: no-op free */\\n./bochs_cstubs.c:98: * and the kernel OOM-halts. This 48 MiB pool was sized specifically for\\n./bochs_cstubs.c:104: *                              bump allocator never frees individually). */\\n./bochs_glue .cpp:296:// Reserve room for 256 tiny per-vector stubs + one shared tail.\\n./bochs_glue .cpp:1099:    // tables out of the bump pool) and were the explicit reason\\n./matrix_array.h:61:    uint32_t ver_num;      // bumped on every successful W\\n./test_module.cpp:298: * in BSS so it costs nothing on disk and no bump-pool allocation. */\\n./test_module.cpp:455:    /* Leave room above the image for a stack; ESP starts at the very\\n./test_module.cpp:457:     * guest. Require at least 64KB of headroom for the stack. */\\n./editf.c:38: *     smaller mid-session and the editor just fills whatever room\\n./editf.c:105:    s_edit_h = GFX_HEIGHT - EDIT_Y - 16;   /* room left for the status line below */\\n./editf.c:396:         * room, otherwise it drops to its own row instead \u2014 on a\\n./test_main.cpp:11://   - Operator new/delete that go to the bump allocator in\\n./test_main.cpp:34:    // From bochs_cstubs.c bump allocator\\n./test_main.cpp:282:// allocate from the bump pool (which is now committed to Bochs).\\n./tcc_glue.h:72: *   TCC_ERR_NOMEM      (-5)  \u2014 kernel allocator OOM\\n./bochs_glue.cpp:296:// Reserve room for 256 tiny per-vector stubs + one shared tail.\\n./bochs_glue.cpp:1128:    // tables out of the bump pool) and were the explicit reason\\n./bochs_glue.cpp:1801://   g_bochs_gfx_serial  -- bumped whenever a guest's visible canvas changes\\n./gui_demo.c:11: * Click the button to bump a counter, drag the scrollbar to change\\n./kernel_parts/02_boot_info_and_graphics_driver.h:56:// oom_halt). Here we just define the single instance.\\n./kernel_parts/02_boot_info_and_graphics_driver.h:499:    // so we have room for the EIP + breadcrumb trail.\\n./kernel_parts/06_fat32_filesystem_and_explorer.h:1261:// If (b) doesn't leave enough contiguous room before the end of that\\n./kernel_parts/06_fat32_filesystem_and_explorer.h:1356:    int room_in_cluster = entries_per_cluster - end_entry_in_cluster;\\n./kernel_parts/06_fat32_filesystem_and_explorer.h:1357:    if (room_in_cluster >= total_slots) {\\n./kernel_parts/06_fat32_filesystem_and_explorer.h:1397:    int fits_in_cbuf1 = room_in_cluster; // < total_slots, guaranteed by the branch above\\n./kernel_parts/06_fat32_filesystem_and_explorer.h:2073:        return -2; // Out of memory\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:13.282488Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:13.779283Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_018Hz5kJAm39kSQMEm8tE2p1", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "cd /home/claude/OS && sed -n 1,140p bochs_cstubs.c", "description": "Read the kernel bump allocator design and pool sizing"}, "message": "Read the kernel bump allocator design and pool sizing", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cd /home/claude/OS && sed -n 1,140p bochs_cstubs.c\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:14.072008Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:14.072100Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_018Hz5kJAm39kSQMEm8tE2p1", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"/* ====================================================================\\n * bochs_cstubs.c \u2014 freestanding C stdlib shims for the kernel build.\\n *\\n * Compiled with -ffreestanding and NO system headers. Provides\\n * minimal implementations of every libc symbol that libcpu.a,\\n * libmemory.a, libcpudb.a, libfpu.a, libgcc_eh.a, or our bochs_infra\\n * code references.\\n *\\n * Memory invariants enforced here:\\n *\\n *   M1. The bump allocator below backs every malloc/new in the kernel\\n *       Bochs path. Capacity is 1 MiB \u2014 enough for:\\n *\\n *         - BX_MEM_C::init_memory() vector[]      (~1 MiB at our\\n *           configured request size \u2014 but vector goes through\\n *           alloc_vector_aligned which uses operator new in\\n *           libmemory.a; that ends up here)\\n *         - BX_MEM_C::init_memory() blocks[]      (8 entries x 4 B\\n *           = 32 B at our 1 MiB guest size, but grows with guest size)\\n *         - BX_MEM_C::init_memory() memory_handlers[] (up to ~1 M\\n *           entries x 4 B = ~4 MiB on a 40-bit phys-addr config)\\n *         - libgcc_eh's per-thread exception-handling globals\\n *           (~256 B once)\\n *         - bx_param_string_c dummies' backing buffers\\n *\\n *       The biggest single allocation is the memory_handlers table.\\n *       For BX_PHY_ADDRESS_WIDTH = 32 (the common 32-bit-host case)\\n *       it's (1<<32 >> 20) = 4096 entries = 16 KiB. For 40-bit phys\\n *       addrs it's 4 MiB. The pool below is sized accordingly.\\n *\\n *   M2. Allocation is 8-byte aligned. malloc rounds up the requested\\n *       size to a multiple of 8 before bumping the pointer. This\\n *       matches the alignment requirements of any standard C/C++\\n *       object on a 32-bit i386 build.\\n *\\n *   M3. Out-of-pool returns NULL (the previous version halted the\\n *       CPU; that prevents the caller from gracefully degrading or\\n *       reporting). Callers that need a hard failure can check.\\n * ==================================================================== */\\n\\ntypedef unsigned long  size_t;\\ntypedef long           ssize_t;\\ntypedef long           time_t;\\ntypedef long long      off64_t;\\ntypedef int            pthread_mutex_t;\\n\\n/* \u2500\u2500 Bump allocator \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 */\\n/* 8 MiB is enough for the worst-case memory_handlers table (4 MiB at\\n * 40-bit phys addr config) plus a few MiB of slack for blocks, vector,\\n * and miscellaneous small allocations. The pool lives in BSS so it\\n * doesn't bloat the kernel ELF on disk. */\\n\\n/* 48 MiB. Sized for the worst-case BX_MEM_C::init_memory request with\\n * BX_PHY_ADDRESS_WIDTH = 40 (the 64-bit Bochs build):\\n *\\n *   vector[]          = host + 2 MiB BIOSROMSZ + 128 KiB EXROMSIZE + 4 KiB\\n *                       = 16 MiB + ~2.1 MiB = ~18.2 MiB\\n *   memory_handlers[] = (1 << 40) >> 20 entries * 4 B = 4 MiB\\n *   blocks[]          = (16 MiB / 128 KiB) entries * 4 B = 512 B (negligible)\\n *   misc small allocs (param strings, libgcc_eh) = a few KiB\\n *\\n * Total \u2248 22.5 MiB. 48 MiB is roughly 2\u00d7 to leave room for the kernel\\n * to also use this pool for its own allocations after Bochs init. The\\n * BSS allocation cost is paid by GRUB zeroing it during multiboot\\n * load; the on-disk kernel ELF is unaffected.\\n *\\n * Critical: previously 8 MiB. With that, vector[] allocation failed\\n * silently (compiler optimised out the NULL check in\\n * alloc_vector_aligned, since operator new in standard C++ doesn't\\n * return NULL), causing rom = vector + host to dereference a NULL\\n * base + 16 MiB offset and write 2.1 MiB of 0xFF garbage to physical\\n * address 0x1000000 \u2014 sometimes wedging the boot inside the bogus\\n * memset depending on what BSS was placed at that range.\\n */\\n#define BHEAP_BYTES (48 * 1024 * 1024)\\nstatic char  _bheap[BHEAP_BYTES] __attribute__((aligned(16)));\\nstatic unsigned long _bptr = 0;\\n\\nvoid* malloc(size_t n) {\\n    /* Round up to 8-byte alignment for any standard alignment\\n       requirement on i386. */\\n    n = (n + 7u) & ~(size_t)7u;\\n    if (_bptr + n > (unsigned long)BHEAP_BYTES) {\\n        return (void*)0;\\n    }\\n    void* p = _bheap + _bptr;\\n    _bptr += n;\\n    return p;\\n}\\n\\nvoid free(void* p) { (void)p; }   /* bump allocator: no-op free */\\n\\n/* \u2500\u2500 Bump-pool fallback for the kernel's operator new \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\\n * The Bochs constructors (e.g. icache.o's pageWriteStampTable ctor, which\\n * allocates 4 MiB) call the global C++ operator new, which kernel.cpp\\n * routes to its own FreeListAllocator. By the time `test` runs, that heap\\n * is mostly consumed by the running desktop, so the 4 MiB request fails\\n * and the kernel OOM-halts. This 48 MiB pool was sized specifically for\\n * Bochs (see the header comment). These two helpers let kernel.cpp's\\n * operator new fall back here instead of halting:\\n *   bochs_pool_alloc(n)      \u2014 allocate n bytes from the pool (NULL if full)\\n *   bochs_pool_owns(p)       \u2014 nonzero if p points inside the pool, so the\\n *                              kernel's operator delete can skip it (the\\n *                              bump allocator never frees individually). */\\nvoid* bochs_pool_alloc(size_t n) { return malloc(n); }\\n\\nint bochs_pool_owns(const void* p) {\\n    const char* c = (const char*)p;\\n    return (c >= _bheap) && (c < _bheap + BHEAP_BYTES);\\n}\\n\\nvoid abort(void) { __asm__(\\\"cli; hlt\\\"); __builtin_unreachable(); }\\n\\n/* \u2500\u2500 String / memory functions \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 */\\nsize_t strlen(const char* s)         { size_t n=0; while(s[n]) n++; return n; }\\nint    strcmp(const char* a, const char* b) {\\n    while(*a && *a==*b){a++;b++;} return (unsigned char)*a-(unsigned char)*b;\\n}\\nint    strncmp(const char* a, const char* b, size_t n) {\\n    while(n && *a && *a==*b){a++;b++;n--;}\\n    return n ? (unsigned char)*a-(unsigned char)*b : 0;\\n}\\nchar*  strncpy(char* d, const char* s, size_t n) {\\n    size_t i=0;\\n    for(; i<n && s[i]; i++) d[i] = s[i];\\n    for(; i<n; i++) d[i] = 0;\\n    return d;\\n}\\nchar*  strcat(char* d, const char* s) {\\n    char* r = d;\\n    while(*d) d++;\\n    while((*d++ = *s++));\\n    return r;\\n}\\nchar*  strncat(char* d, const char* s, size_t n) {\\n    char* r = d;\\n    while(*d) d++;\\n    while(n-- && *s) *d++ = *s++;\\n    *d = 0;\\n    return r;\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"/* ====================================================================\\n * bochs_cstubs.c \u2014 freestanding C stdlib shims for the kernel build.\\n *\\n * Compiled with -ffreestanding and NO system headers. Provides\\n * minimal implementations of every libc symbol that libcpu.a,\\n * libmemory.a, libcpudb.a, libfpu.a, libgcc_eh.a, or our bochs_infra\\n * code references.\\n *\\n * Memory invariants enforced here:\\n *\\n *   M1. The bump allocator below backs every malloc/new in the kernel\\n *       Bochs path. Capacity is 1 MiB \u2014 enough for:\\n *\\n *         - BX_MEM_C::init_memory() vector[]      (~1 MiB at our\\n *           configured request size \u2014 but vector goes through\\n *           alloc_vector_aligned which uses operator new in\\n *           libmemory.a; that ends up here)\\n *         - BX_MEM_C::init_memory() blocks[]      (8 entries x 4 B\\n *           = 32 B at our 1 MiB guest size, but grows with guest size)\\n *         - BX_MEM_C::init_memory() memory_handlers[] (up to ~1 M\\n *           entries x 4 B = ~4 MiB on a 40-bit phys-addr config)\\n *         - libgcc_eh's per-thread exception-handling globals\\n *           (~256 B once)\\n *         - bx_param_string_c dummies' backing buffers\\n *\\n *       The biggest single allocation is the memory_handlers table.\\n *       For BX_PHY_ADDRESS_WIDTH = 32 (the common 32-bit-host case)\\n *       it's (1<<32 >> 20) = 4096 entries = 16 KiB. For 40-bit phys\\n *       addrs it's 4 MiB. The pool below is sized accordingly.\\n *\\n *   M2. Allocation is 8-byte aligned. malloc rounds up the requested\\n *       size to a multiple of 8 before bumping the pointer. This\\n *       matches the alignment requirements of any standard C/C++\\n *       object on a 32-bit i386 build.\\n *\\n *   M3. Out-of-pool returns NULL (the previous version halted the\\n *       CPU; that prevents the caller from gracefully degrading or\\n *       reporting). Callers that need a hard failure can check.\\n * ==================================================================== */\\n\\ntypedef unsigned long  size_t;\\ntypedef long           ssize_t;\\ntypedef long           time_t;\\ntypedef long long      off64_t;\\ntypedef int            pthread_mutex_t;\\n\\n/* \u2500\u2500 Bump allocator \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 */\\n/* 8 MiB is enough for the worst-case memory_handlers table (4 MiB at\\n * 40-bit phys addr config) plus a few MiB of slack for blocks, vector,\\n * and miscellaneous small allocations. The pool lives in BSS so it\\n * doesn't bloat the kernel ELF on disk. */\\n\\n/* 48 MiB. Sized for the worst-case BX_MEM_C::init_memory request with\\n * BX_PHY_ADDRESS_WIDTH = 40 (the 64-bit Bochs build):\\n *\\n *   vector[]          = host + 2 MiB BIOSROMSZ + 128 KiB EXROMSIZE + 4 KiB\\n *                       = 16 MiB + ~2.1 MiB = ~18.2 MiB\\n *   memory_handlers[] = (1 << 40) >> 20 entries * 4 B = 4 MiB\\n *   blocks[]          = (16 MiB / 128 KiB) entries * 4 B = 512 B (negligible)\\n *   misc small allocs (param strings, libgcc_eh) = a few KiB\\n *\\n * Total \u2248 22.5 MiB. 48 MiB is roughly 2\u00d7 to leave room for the kernel\\n * to also use this pool for its own allocations after Bochs init. The\\n * BSS allocation cost is paid by GRUB zeroing it during multiboot\\n * load; the on-disk kernel ELF is unaffected.\\n *\\n * Critical: previously 8 MiB. With that, vector[] allocation failed\\n * silently (compiler optimised out the NULL check in\\n * alloc_vector_aligned, since operator new in standard C++ doesn't\\n * return NULL), causing rom = vector + host to dereference a NULL\\n * base + 16 MiB offset and write 2.1 MiB of 0xFF garbage to physical\\n * address 0x1000000 \u2014 sometimes wedging the boot inside the bogus\\n * memset depending on what BSS was placed at that range.\\n */\\n#define BHEAP_BYTES (48 * 1024 * 1024)\\nstatic char  _bheap[BHEAP_BYTES] __attribute__((aligned(16)));\\nstatic unsigned long _bptr = 0;\\n\\nvoid* malloc(size_t n) {\\n    /* Round up to 8-byte alignment for any standard alignment\\n       requirement on i386. */\\n    n = (n + 7u) & ~(size_t)7u;\\n    if (_bptr + n > (unsigned long)BHEAP_BYTES) {\\n        return (void*)0;\\n    }\\n    void* p = _bheap + _bptr;\\n    _bptr += n;\\n    return p;\\n}\\n\\nvoid free(void* p) { (void)p; }   /* bump allocator: no-op free */\\n\\n/* \u2500\u2500 Bump-pool fallback for the kernel's operator new \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\\n * The Bochs constructors (e.g. icache.o's pageWriteStampTable ctor, which\\n * allocates 4 MiB) call the global C++ operator new, which kernel.cpp\\n * routes to its own FreeListAllocator. By the time `test` runs, that heap\\n * is mostly consumed by the running desktop, so the 4 MiB request fails\\n * and the kernel OOM-halts. This 48 MiB pool was sized specifically for\\n * Bochs (see the header comment). These two helpers let kernel.cpp's\\n * operator new fall back here instead of halting:\\n *   bochs_pool_alloc(n)      \u2014 allocate n bytes from the pool (NULL if full)\\n *   bochs_pool_owns(p)       \u2014 nonzero if p points inside the pool, so the\\n *                              kernel's operator delete can skip it (the\\n *                              bump allocator never frees individually). */\\nvoid* bochs_pool_alloc(size_t n) { return malloc(n); }\\n\\nint bochs_pool_owns(const void* p) {\\n    const char* c = (const char*)p;\\n    return (c >= _bheap) && (c < _bheap + BHEAP_BYTES);\\n}\\n\\nvoid abort(void) { __asm__(\\\"cli; hlt\\\"); __builtin_unreachable(); }\\n\\n/* \u2500\u2500 String / memory functions \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 */\\nsize_t strlen(const char* s)         { size_t n=0; while(s[n]) n++; return n; }\\nint    strcmp(const char* a, const char* b) {\\n    while(*a && *a==*b){a++;b++;} return (unsigned char)*a-(unsigned char)*b;\\n}\\nint    strncmp(const char* a, const char* b, size_t n) {\\n    while(n && *a && *a==*b){a++;b++;n--;}\\n    return n ? (unsigned char)*a-(unsigned char)*b : 0;\\n}\\nchar*  strncpy(char* d, const char* s, size_t n) {\\n    size_t i=0;\\n    for(; i<n && s[i]; i++) d[i] = s[i];\\n    for(; i<n; i++) d[i] = 0;\\n    return d;\\n}\\nchar*  strcat(char* d, const char* s) {\\n    char* r = d;\\n    while(*d) d++;\\n    while((*d++ = *s++));\\n    return r;\\n}\\nchar*  strncat(char* d, const char* s, size_t n) {\\n    char* r = d;\\n    while(*d) d++;\\n    while(n-- && *s) *d++ = *s++;\\n    *d = 0;\\n    return r;\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:16.784291Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:23.298464Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Designing a memory allocator for a kernel environment."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:23.298614Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:23.298899Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Testing whether repeated compiles exhaust the bump allocator's heap"}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:23.298972Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:23.672489Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01JrGhKRESnDiogQVvdcUnif", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "cd /home/claude/OS && ls tcc_glue* tcc_kernel* libtcc* 2>/dev/null; sed -n 55,100p tcc_glue.h; grep -n \"malloc\\|realloc\\|free\\|tcc_set_realloc\\|bochs_pool\" tcc_glue.cpp 2>/dev/null | head -30", "description": "Find how the in-kernel TCC gets memory"}, "message": "Find how the in-kernel TCC gets memory", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cd /home/claude/OS && ls tcc_glue* tcc_kernel* libtcc* 2>/dev/null; sed -n 55,100p tcc_glue.h; grep -n \\\"malloc\\\\|realloc\\\\|free\\\\|tcc_set_realloc\\\\|bochs_pool\\\" tcc_glue.cpp 2>/dev/null | head -30\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:23.892182Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:23.892241Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01JrGhKRESnDiogQVvdcUnif", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"tcc_glue.cpp\\ntcc_glue.h\\ntcc_kernel.cpp\\n/* \u2500\u2500 Compiler entry point \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\\n * Compile the C source file `src_filename` (read from FAT32) and write\\n * the resulting i386 ELF to `out_filename` (written to FAT32).\\n *\\n * Parameters\\n *   src_filename  \u2014 FAT32 filename of the C source (e.g. \\\"hello.c\\\")\\n *   out_filename  \u2014 FAT32 filename for the ELF output (e.g. \\\"hello\\\")\\n *                   May be NULL; the glue then derives it by stripping\\n *                   the extension from src_filename.\\n *   extra_flags   \u2014 NULL-terminated list of extra -D/-I/-W flags,\\n *                   or NULL for none.\\n *\\n * Returns 0 on success, negative errno-style on failure:\\n *   TCC_ERR_NOT_INIT   (-1)  \u2014 tcc_module_init() not called / failed\\n *   TCC_ERR_NO_SRC     (-2)  \u2014 source file not found on FAT32\\n *   TCC_ERR_COMPILE    (-3)  \u2014 TCC reported a compilation error\\n *   TCC_ERR_OUTPUT     (-4)  \u2014 could not write ELF to FAT32\\n *   TCC_ERR_NOMEM      (-5)  \u2014 kernel allocator OOM\\n */\\nint  tcc_compile_file(const char* src_filename,\\n                      const char* out_filename,\\n                      const char* const* extra_flags);\\n\\n#define TCC_ERR_NOT_INIT  (-1)\\n#define TCC_ERR_NO_SRC    (-2)\\n#define TCC_ERR_COMPILE   (-3)\\n#define TCC_ERR_OUTPUT    (-4)\\n#define TCC_ERR_NOMEM     (-5)\\n\\n/* \u2500\u2500 Status / diagnostics \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500*/\\nint         tcc_module_available(void);\\nconst char* tcc_last_error(void);\\n\\n#ifdef __cplusplus\\n}\\n#endif\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"tcc_glue.cpp\\ntcc_glue.h\\ntcc_kernel.cpp\\n/* \u2500\u2500 Compiler entry point \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\\n * Compile the C source file `src_filename` (read from FAT32) and write\\n * the resulting i386 ELF to `out_filename` (written to FAT32).\\n *\\n * Parameters\\n *   src_filename  \u2014 FAT32 filename of the C source (e.g. \\\"hello.c\\\")\\n *   out_filename  \u2014 FAT32 filename for the ELF output (e.g. \\\"hello\\\")\\n *                   May be NULL; the glue then derives it by stripping\\n *                   the extension from src_filename.\\n *   extra_flags   \u2014 NULL-terminated list of extra -D/-I/-W flags,\\n *                   or NULL for none.\\n *\\n * Returns 0 on success, negative errno-style on failure:\\n *   TCC_ERR_NOT_INIT   (-1)  \u2014 tcc_module_init() not called / failed\\n *   TCC_ERR_NO_SRC     (-2)  \u2014 source file not found on FAT32\\n *   TCC_ERR_COMPILE    (-3)  \u2014 TCC reported a compilation error\\n *   TCC_ERR_OUTPUT     (-4)  \u2014 could not write ELF to FAT32\\n *   TCC_ERR_NOMEM      (-5)  \u2014 kernel allocator OOM\\n */\\nint  tcc_compile_file(const char* src_filename,\\n                      const char* out_filename,\\n                      const char* const* extra_flags);\\n\\n#define TCC_ERR_NOT_INIT  (-1)\\n#define TCC_ERR_NO_SRC    (-2)\\n#define TCC_ERR_COMPILE   (-3)\\n#define TCC_ERR_OUTPUT    (-4)\\n#define TCC_ERR_NOMEM     (-5)\\n\\n/* \u2500\u2500 Status / diagnostics \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500*/\\nint         tcc_module_available(void);\\nconst char* tcc_last_error(void);\\n\\n#ifdef __cplusplus\\n}\\n#endif\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:25.134793Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:26.201690Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01LBmqwmQKHGjxXtjAPWvaUX", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "cd /home/claude/OS && wc -l tcc_glue.cpp tcc_kernel.cpp; grep -n \"alloc\\|free\\|heap\\|pool\\|arena\\|NOMEM\\|malloc\" tcc_glue.cpp | head -40; echo ----; grep -n \"alloc\\|free\\|heap\\|pool\\|arena\\|malloc\" tcc_kernel.cpp | head -40", "description": "Search the TCC glue for allocation behaviour"}, "message": "Search the TCC glue for allocation behaviour", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cd /home/claude/OS && wc -l tcc_glue.cpp tcc_kernel.cpp; grep -n \\\"alloc\\\\|free\\\\|heap\\\\|pool\\\\|arena\\\\|NOMEM\\\\|malloc\\\" tcc_glue.cpp | head -40; echo ----; grep -n \\\"alloc\\\\|free\\\\|heap\\\\|pool\\\\|arena\\\\|malloc\\\" tcc_kernel.cpp | head -40\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:26.488241Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-09T22:00:26.488304Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01LBmqwmQKHGjxXtjAPWvaUX", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"  324 tcc_glue.cpp\\n  981 tcc_kernel.cpp\\n 1305 total\\n----\\n7:// via kernel-heap-backed stubs, and implements tcc_kernel_cmd_cc() which the\\n14://      (open/fdopen/fwrite intercept write into kernel-heap buffer)\\n19://   heap:    malloc, realloc, free, calloc\\n51:// \u2500\u2500 Kernel heap via operator new/delete (defined in kernel.cpp) \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\\n52:// We provide malloc/free/realloc in extern \\\"C\\\" below.\\n55:// GCC's __builtin_va_* work in freestanding mode.\\n156:// \u2500\u2500 Memory allocation \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\\n157:// IMPORTANT: this calls kernel_alloc_nofail()/kernel_free() (defined in\\n162:// memory\\\" and return to the shell, not freeze the OS.\\n163:extern void* kernel_alloc_nofail(unsigned long size);\\n164:extern void  kernel_free(void* ptr);\\n166:// Set the moment any malloc() call fails during this compile. TCC's own\\n168:// the real cause was a failed allocation deep inside libtcc \u2014 checking\\n173:void* malloc(unsigned long size) {\\n175:    void* p = kernel_alloc_nofail(size);\\n179:void free(void* ptr) {\\n180:    if (ptr) kernel_free(ptr);\\n182:extern unsigned long kernel_alloc_usable_size(void* ptr);\\n184:void* realloc(void* ptr, unsigned long size) {\\n185:    if (!ptr)  return malloc(size);\\n186:    if (!size) { free(ptr); return nullptr; }\\n187:    void* n = malloc(size);\\n189:        // Copy only as many bytes as the OLD allocation actually has.\\n192:        // out-of-bounds read that silently corrupts whatever heap memory\\n193:        // follows it. kernel_alloc_usable_size() gives a safe upper bound;\\n194:        // fall back to `size` only if it's unavailable (e.g. bump-pool\\n197:        unsigned long old_usable = kernel_alloc_usable_size(ptr);\\n201:        free(ptr);\\n205:void* calloc(unsigned long n, unsigned long size) {\\n208:    void* p = malloc(t);\\n217:    char* p = (char*)malloc(n);\\n298:    char* tmp = (char*)malloc(sz);\\n309:    free(tmp);\\n429:    if (!resolved) resolved=(char*)malloc(256);\\n475:// it had eaten the entire 64MB kernel heap and tripped the global\\n476:// allocator's OOM halt, freezing the whole OS over one bad `cc`.\\n490:static int kfd_alloc() {\\n496:            // buf alive for harvest (write) or explicit free (read)\\\"),\\n498:            // free it. But kfd_alloc() reuses that same (now inactive)\\n502:            // ever freeing what it pointed to. One header leaked per\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"  324 tcc_glue.cpp\\n  981 tcc_kernel.cpp\\n 1305 total\\n----\\n7:// via kernel-heap-backed stubs, and implements tcc_kernel_cmd_cc() which the\\n14://      (open/fdopen/fwrite intercept write into kernel-heap buffer)\\n19://   heap:    malloc, realloc, free, calloc\\n51:// \u2500\u2500 Kernel heap via operator new/delete (defined in kernel.cpp) \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\\n52:// We provide malloc/free/realloc in extern \\\"C\\\" below.\\n55:// GCC's __builtin_va_* work in freestanding mode.\\n156:// \u2500\u2500 Memory allocation \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\\n157:// IMPORTANT: this calls kernel_alloc_nofail()/kernel_free() (defined in\\n162:// memory\\\" and return to the shell, not freeze the OS.\\n163:extern void* kernel_alloc_nofail(unsigned long size);\\n164:extern void  kernel_free(void* ptr);\\n166:// Set the moment any malloc() call fails during this compile. TCC's own\\n168:// the real cause was a failed allocation deep inside libtcc \u2014 checking\\n173:void* malloc(unsigned long size) {\\n175:    void* p = kernel_alloc_nofail(size);\\n179:void free(void* ptr) {\\n180:    if (ptr) kernel_free(ptr);\\n182:extern unsigned long kernel_alloc_usable_size(void* ptr);\\n184:void* realloc(void* ptr, unsigned long size) {\\n185:    if (!ptr)  return malloc(size);\\n186:    if (!size) { free(ptr); return nullptr; }\\n187:    void* n = malloc(size);\\n189:        // Copy only as many bytes as the OLD allocation actually has.\\n192:        // out-of-bounds read that silently corrupts whatever heap memory\\n193:        // follows it. kernel_alloc_usable_size() gives a safe upper bound;\\n194:        // fall back to `size` only if it's unavailable (e.g. bump-pool\\n197:        unsigned long old_usable = kernel_alloc_usable_size(ptr);\\n201:        free(ptr);\\n205:void* calloc(unsigned long n, unsigned long size) {\\n208:    void* p = malloc(t);\\n217:    char* p = (char*)malloc(n);\\n298:    char* tmp = (char*)malloc(sz);\\n309:    free(tmp);\\n429:    if (!resolved) resolved=(char*)malloc(256);\\n475:// it had eaten the entire 64MB kernel heap and tripped the global\\n476:// allocator's OOM halt, freezing the whole OS over one bad `cc`.\\n490:static int kfd_alloc() {\\n496:            // buf alive for harvest (write) or explicit free (read)\\\"),\\n498:            // free it. But kfd_alloc() reuses that same (now inactive)\\n502:            // ever freeing what it pointed to. One header leaked per\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-10-09T22:00:26.874055Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-09T22:00:26.874055Z", "attachments": [], "files": [{"file_uuid": "b7dc4919-e08a-5e43-8873-00fec4fce490", "file_name": "h1.png"}, {"file_uuid": "53513867-9dfa-512f-8835-ee99da987cb7", "file_name": "c1.png"}, {"file_uuid": "2022c1c7-c923-5f87-87bd-ff933ab45e8d", "file_name": "c3.png"}], "parent_message_uuid": "01a122ab-4bee-7ac8-9100-a40493c1a87d"}, {"uuid": "01a123a4-25f5-799d-8eba-878dd1cb9246", "text": "Continue", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-10-10T02:28:57.443933Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-10T02:28:57.443933Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "Continue", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-10-10T02:28:57.444248Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-10T02:28:57.444248Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a122ab-4bee-77d2-95e6-eb9f0a73bbdc"}, {"uuid": "01a123a4-25f5-7dc6-a257-f9bf8195ec15", "text": "The compiler uses the kernel's free-list heap (which does free), not the bump pool, so my first theory is partly wrong. Let me read the allocator wrappers and the limits the compile path enforces.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nThe compile path already has OOM reporting and leak fixes, so something else is eating the heap. I need measurements, not guesses. Does the kernel expose a way to see heap usage?\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nI'll add `meminfo` (and the allocator accessors it needs), and also look at how `hello` is dispatched since it's a built-in shortcut.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nThis `snprintf` only knows `%d`, `%s` and `%c`. Switching the command to `%d` with a plain `snprintf`, then looking at how `hello` and its output ordering are handled.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nNow rebuild and use `meminfo` to see what actually happens to memory across compiles and program runs.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-10-10T02:28:57.443706Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-10T02:28:59.235529Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Investigating how the compiler's memory allocation is managed."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-10T02:28:59.235701Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-10T02:28:59.235904Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "The compiler uses the kernel's free-list heap (which does free), not the bump pool, so my first theory is partly wrong. Let me read the allocator wrappers and the limits the compile path enforces.", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-10T02:28:59.235909Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-10T02:28:59.657064Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_011UfmMQKF2sJ3DGogFFfoGH", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "cd /home/claude/OS && sed -n 156,235p tcc_kernel.cpp; sed -n 466,530p tcc_kernel.cpp", "description": "Read the TCC allocation wrappers and compile memory limits"}, "message": "Read the TCC allocation wrappers and compile memory limits", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cd /home/claude/OS && sed -n 156,235p tcc_kernel.cpp; sed -n 466,530p tcc_kernel.cpp\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-10T02:29:02.502004Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-10T02:29:02.502109Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_011UfmMQKF2sJ3DGogFFfoGH", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"// \u2500\u2500 Memory allocation \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\\n// IMPORTANT: this calls kernel_alloc_nofail()/kernel_free() (defined in\\n// kernel.cpp), NOT ::operator new/delete directly. The global operator new\\n// halts the entire kernel on exhaustion (oom_halt()) \u2014 appropriate for\\n// kernel-internal structures, but not for memory driven by an arbitrary\\n// user-supplied C source file. A bad `cc foo.c` should report \\\"out of\\n// memory\\\" and return to the shell, not freeze the OS.\\nextern void* kernel_alloc_nofail(unsigned long size);\\nextern void  kernel_free(void* ptr);\\n\\n// Set the moment any malloc() call fails during this compile. TCC's own\\n// error path may report a confusing/unrelated parse or codegen error when\\n// the real cause was a failed allocation deep inside libtcc \u2014 checking\\n// this latch lets tcc_kernel_cmd_cc() print an unambiguous \\\"out of memory\\\"\\n// message instead. Reset at the top of every tcc_kernel_cmd_cc() call.\\nstatic bool g_oom_hit = false;\\n\\nvoid* malloc(unsigned long size) {\\n    if (!size) return nullptr;\\n    void* p = kernel_alloc_nofail(size);\\n    if (!p) g_oom_hit = true;\\n    return p;\\n}\\nvoid free(void* ptr) {\\n    if (ptr) kernel_free(ptr);\\n}\\nextern unsigned long kernel_alloc_usable_size(void* ptr);\\n\\nvoid* realloc(void* ptr, unsigned long size) {\\n    if (!ptr)  return malloc(size);\\n    if (!size) { free(ptr); return nullptr; }\\n    void* n = malloc(size);\\n    if (n) {\\n        // Copy only as many bytes as the OLD allocation actually has.\\n        // Copying `size` (the NEW, larger request) unconditionally would\\n        // read past the end of the original block on every grow \u2014 an\\n        // out-of-bounds read that silently corrupts whatever heap memory\\n        // follows it. kernel_alloc_usable_size() gives a safe upper bound;\\n        // fall back to `size` only if it's unavailable (e.g. bump-pool\\n        // pointer), which preserves the old (unsafe but pre-existing)\\n        // behavior solely for that rare path.\\n        unsigned long old_usable = kernel_alloc_usable_size(ptr);\\n        unsigned long copy_n = old_usable ? old_usable : size;\\n        if (copy_n > size) copy_n = size;\\n        memcpy(n, ptr, copy_n);\\n        free(ptr);\\n    }\\n    return n;\\n}\\nvoid* calloc(unsigned long n, unsigned long size) {\\n    if (n != 0 && size > (~0UL) / n) return nullptr;  // overflow guard\\n    unsigned long t = n * size;\\n    void* p = malloc(t);\\n    if (p) memset(p, 0, t);\\n    return p;\\n}\\n\\n// \u2500\u2500 String utilities \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\\nchar* strdup(const char* s) {\\n    if (!s) return nullptr;\\n    unsigned long n = strlen(s) + 1;\\n    char* p = (char*)malloc(n);\\n    if (p) memcpy(p, s, n);\\n    return p;\\n}\\n\\nstatic long long kern_strtoll(const char* s, char** end, int base) {\\n    while (*s == ' ' || *s == '\\\\t') s++;\\n    int neg = 0;\\n    if (*s == '-')      { neg = 1; s++; }\\n    else if (*s == '+') { s++; }\\n    if (base == 0) {\\n        if (s[0]=='0' && (s[1]=='x'||s[1]=='X')) { base=16; s+=2; }\\n        else if (s[0]=='0')                        { base=8;  s++;  }\\n        else                                        { base=10;       }\\n    } else if (base==16 && s[0]=='0' && (s[1]=='x'||s[1]=='X')) { s+=2; }\\n    long long v = 0;\\n    while (1) {\\n        int d;\\n        if      (*s>='0'&&*s<='9') d = *s-'0';\\n#define FAKE_FD_MAX   32   // TCC opens several internal fds during compilation\\n                           // -- bumped from 16 to give headroom for source\\n                           // files that #include several headers (each one\\n                           // briefly holds its own fake fd during preprocessing)\\n\\n// Hard ceiling on any single fake-fd output buffer. A 32-bit i386 ELF\\n// produced by a single `cc` invocation has no legitimate reason to exceed\\n// this. Without a cap, a runaway/corrupt compile would let\\n// kfd_write_bytes() double its buffer (65536 -> 128K -> 256K -> ...) until\\n// it had eaten the entire 64MB kernel heap and tripped the global\\n// allocator's OOM halt, freezing the whole OS over one bad `cc`.\\n#define TCC_FD_MAX_BYTES (16u * 1024u * 1024u)  // 16MB per fake-fd buffer\\n\\nstruct KFd {\\n    bool          active;\\n    bool          is_write;\\n    unsigned char* buf;\\n    unsigned long  cap;\\n    unsigned long  size;\\n    unsigned long  pos;\\n};\\n\\nstatic KFd g_kfds[FAKE_FD_MAX];\\n\\nstatic int kfd_alloc() {\\n    for (int i=0;i<FAKE_FD_MAX;i++)\\n        if (!g_kfds[i].active) {\\n            KFd* f = &g_kfds[i];\\n            // FIX (multi-include leak): close() intentionally leaves a\\n            // just-closed read fd's buffer alive (comment above it: \\\"keep\\n            // buf alive for harvest (write) or explicit free (read)\\\"),\\n            // relying on kfd_reset_all() at the top of the NEXT `cc` to\\n            // free it. But kfd_alloc() reuses that same (now inactive)\\n            // slot for the very next open() within THIS compile -- e.g.\\n            // the second #include in a source file that has more than\\n            // one -- and used to overwrite f->buf with `*f = {}` without\\n            // ever freeing what it pointed to. One header leaked per\\n            // additional #include, silently, for the whole compile.\\n            if (f->buf) { free(f->buf); }\\n            *f = {};\\n            f->active=true; return FAKE_FD_BASE+i;\\n        }\\n    return -1;\\n}\\nstatic KFd* kfd_get(int fd) {\\n    int i=fd-FAKE_FD_BASE;\\n    if (i<0||i>=FAKE_FD_MAX||!g_kfds[i].active) return nullptr;\\n    return &g_kfds[i];\\n}\\nstatic void kfd_release(int fd) {\\n    KFd* f=kfd_get(fd);\\n    if (f) { if(f->buf) free(f->buf); *f={}; }\\n}\\n\\n// Free every fake-fd slot's buffer, active or not. Must be called at the\\n// START of every `cc` invocation (before tcc_new()) so that any buffer\\n// left behind by a previous failed/aborted compile \u2014 e.g. a compile that\\n// errored out after open() but before harvest_elf() ran \u2014 is released\\n// instead of silently accumulating. Without this sweep, repeated `cc`\\n// attempts on a broken source file leak ~64KB+ of kernel heap per attempt\\n// and never get it back: a slow, easy-to-trigger memory loop.\\nstatic void kfd_reset_all() {\\n    for (int i=0;i<FAKE_FD_MAX;i++) {\\n        KFd* f=&g_kfds[i];\\n        if (f->buf) { free(f->buf); }\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"// \u2500\u2500 Memory allocation \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\\n// IMPORTANT: this calls kernel_alloc_nofail()/kernel_free() (defined in\\n// kernel.cpp), NOT ::operator new/delete directly. The global operator new\\n// halts the entire kernel on exhaustion (oom_halt()) \u2014 appropriate for\\n// kernel-internal structures, but not for memory driven by an arbitrary\\n// user-supplied C source file. A bad `cc foo.c` should report \\\"out of\\n// memory\\\" and return to the shell, not freeze the OS.\\nextern void* kernel_alloc_nofail(unsigned long size);\\nextern void  kernel_free(void* ptr);\\n\\n// Set the moment any malloc() call fails during this compile. TCC's own\\n// error path may report a confusing/unrelated parse or codegen error when\\n// the real cause was a failed allocation deep inside libtcc \u2014 checking\\n// this latch lets tcc_kernel_cmd_cc() print an unambiguous \\\"out of memory\\\"\\n// message instead. Reset at the top of every tcc_kernel_cmd_cc() call.\\nstatic bool g_oom_hit = false;\\n\\nvoid* malloc(unsigned long size) {\\n    if (!size) return nullptr;\\n    void* p = kernel_alloc_nofail(size);\\n    if (!p) g_oom_hit = true;\\n    return p;\\n}\\nvoid free(void* ptr) {\\n    if (ptr) kernel_free(ptr);\\n}\\nextern unsigned long kernel_alloc_usable_size(void* ptr);\\n\\nvoid* realloc(void* ptr, unsigned long size) {\\n    if (!ptr)  return malloc(size);\\n    if (!size) { free(ptr); return nullptr; }\\n    void* n = malloc(size);\\n    if (n) {\\n        // Copy only as many bytes as the OLD allocation actually has.\\n        // Copying `size` (the NEW, larger request) unconditionally would\\n        // read past the end of the original block on every grow \u2014 an\\n        // out-of-bounds read that silently corrupts whatever heap memory\\n        // follows it. kernel_alloc_usable_size() gives a safe upper bound;\\n        // fall back to `size` only if it's unavailable (e.g. bump-pool\\n        // pointer), which preserves the old (unsafe but pre-existing)\\n        // behavior solely for that rare path.\\n        unsigned long old_usable = kernel_alloc_usable_size(ptr);\\n        unsigned long copy_n = old_usable ? old_usable : size;\\n        if (copy_n > size) copy_n = size;\\n        memcpy(n, ptr, copy_n);\\n        free(ptr);\\n    }\\n    return n;\\n}\\nvoid* calloc(unsigned long n, unsigned long size) {\\n    if (n != 0 && size > (~0UL) / n) return nullptr;  // overflow guard\\n    unsigned long t = n * size;\\n    void* p = malloc(t);\\n    if (p) memset(p, 0, t);\\n    return p;\\n}\\n\\n// \u2500\u2500 String utilities \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\\nchar* strdup(const char* s) {\\n    if (!s) return nullptr;\\n    unsigned long n = strlen(s) + 1;\\n    char* p = (char*)malloc(n);\\n    if (p) memcpy(p, s, n);\\n    return p;\\n}\\n\\nstatic long long kern_strtoll(const char* s, char** end, int base) {\\n    while (*s == ' ' || *s == '\\\\t') s++;\\n    int neg = 0;\\n    if (*s == '-')      { neg = 1; s++; }\\n    else if (*s == '+') { s++; }\\n    if (base == 0) {\\n        if (s[0]=='0' && (s[1]=='x'||s[1]=='X')) { base=16; s+=2; }\\n        else if (s[0]=='0')                        { base=8;  s++;  }\\n        else                                        { base=10;       }\\n    } else if (base==16 && s[0]=='0' && (s[1]=='x'||s[1]=='X')) { s+=2; }\\n    long long v = 0;\\n    while (1) {\\n        int d;\\n        if      (*s>='0'&&*s<='9') d = *s-'0';\\n#define FAKE_FD_MAX   32   // TCC opens several internal fds during compilation\\n                           // -- bumped from 16 to give headroom for source\\n                           // files that #include several headers (each one\\n                           // briefly holds its own fake fd during preprocessing)\\n\\n// Hard ceiling on any single fake-fd output buffer. A 32-bit i386 ELF\\n// produced by a single `cc` invocation has no legitimate reason to exceed\\n// this. Without a cap, a runaway/corrupt compile would let\\n// kfd_write_bytes() double its buffer (65536 -> 128K -> 256K -> ...) until\\n// it had eaten the entire 64MB kernel heap and tripped the global\\n// allocator's OOM halt, freezing the whole OS over one bad `cc`.\\n#define TCC_FD_MAX_BYTES (16u * 1024u * 1024u)  // 16MB per fake-fd buffer\\n\\nstruct KFd {\\n    bool          active;\\n    bool          is_write;\\n    unsigned char* buf;\\n    unsigned long  cap;\\n    unsigned long  size;\\n    unsigned long  pos;\\n};\\n\\nstatic KFd g_kfds[FAKE_FD_MAX];\\n\\nstatic int kfd_alloc() {\\n    for (int i=0;i<FAKE_FD_MAX;i++)\\n        if (!g_kfds[i].active) {\\n            KFd* f = &g_kfds[i];\\n            // FIX (multi-include leak): close() intentionally leaves a\\n            // just-closed read fd's buffer alive (comment above it: \\\"keep\\n            // buf alive for harvest (write) or explicit free (read)\\\"),\\n            // relying on kfd_reset_all() at the top of the NEXT `cc` to\\n            // free it. But kfd_alloc() reuses that same (now inactive)\\n            // slot for the very next open() within THIS compile -- e.g.\\n            // the second #include in a source file that has more than\\n            // one -- and used to overwrite f->buf with `*f = {}` without\\n            // ever freeing what it pointed to. One header leaked per\\n            // additional #include, silently, for the whole compile.\\n            if (f->buf) { free(f->buf); }\\n            *f = {};\\n            f->active=true; return FAKE_FD_BASE+i;\\n        }\\n    return -1;\\n}\\nstatic KFd* kfd_get(int fd) {\\n    int i=fd-FAKE_FD_BASE;\\n    if (i<0||i>=FAKE_FD_MAX||!g_kfds[i].active) return nullptr;\\n    return &g_kfds[i];\\n}\\nstatic void kfd_release(int fd) {\\n    KFd* f=kfd_get(fd);\\n    if (f) { if(f->buf) free(f->buf); *f={}; }\\n}\\n\\n// Free every fake-fd slot's buffer, active or not. Must be called at the\\n// START of every `cc` invocation (before tcc_new()) so that any buffer\\n// left behind by a previous failed/aborted compile \u2014 e.g. a compile that\\n// errored out after open() but before harvest_elf() ran \u2014 is released\\n// instead of silently accumulating. Without this sweep, repeated `cc`\\n// attempts on a broken source file leak ~64KB+ of kernel heap per attempt\\n// and never get it back: a slow, easy-to-trigger memory loop.\\nstatic void kfd_reset_all() {\\n    for (int i=0;i<FAKE_FD_MAX;i++) {\\n        KFd* f=&g_kfds[i];\\n        if (f->buf) { free(f->buf); }\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-10T02:29:03.962870Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-10T02:29:08.746799Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Investigating kernel heap size and memory consumption."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-10T02:29:08.746969Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-10T02:29:08.747129Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "The compile path already has OOM reporting and leak fixes, so something else is eating the heap. I need measurements, not guesses. Does the kernel expose a way to see heap usage?", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-10T02:29:08.747135Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-10T02:29:09.667021Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01NTZY4RJrttKyY9YqpHkHSx", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "cd /home/claude/OS && grep -n \"\\\"meminfo\\\"\\|\\\"mem\\\"\\|\\\"free\\\"\\|\\\"heap\\\"\\|\\\"memstat\\\"\\|\\\"sysinfo\\\"\\|\\\"stat\\\"\" kernel_parts/*.h kernel.cpp | head; grep -n \"kernel_alloc_nofail\" -B3 -A40 kernel.cpp | sed -n 1,90p", "description": "Look for a memory-inspection command and the kernel allocator"}, "message": "Look for a memory-inspection command and the kernel allocator", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cd /home/claude/OS && grep -n \\\"\\\\\\\"meminfo\\\\\\\"\\\\|\\\\\\\"mem\\\\\\\"\\\\|\\\\\\\"free\\\\\\\"\\\\|\\\\\\\"heap\\\\\\\"\\\\|\\\\\\\"memstat\\\\\\\"\\\\|\\\\\\\"sysinfo\\\\\\\"\\\\|\\\\\\\"stat\\\\\\\"\\\" kernel_parts/*.h kernel.cpp | head; grep -n \\\"kernel_alloc_nofail\\\" -B3 -A40 kernel.cpp | sed -n 1,90p\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": 