Replace image in brochure and render preview", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cd /home/claude && mkdir -p /mnt/user-data/outputs && python3 -W ignore -c \\\"\\nimport pymupdf as fitz\\nd=fitz.open('/mnt/user-data/uploads/hadronic_matter_brochure.pdf')\\np=d[0]\\np.delete_image(4)\\nw=383.85; h=w*965/1258\\nx0=105.71; y0=157.38\\np.insert_image(fitz.Rect(x0,y0,x0+w,y0+h), filename='/mnt/user-data/uploads/1792487494673_image.png')\\nd.save('/mnt/user-data/outputs/hadronic_matter_brochure_updated.pdf', garbage=4, deflate=True)\\nd2=fitz.open('/mnt/user-data/outputs/hadronic_matter_brochure_updated.pdf')\\nd2[0].get_pixmap(dpi=110).save('p1.png')\\nprint(len(d2), d2[0].get_image_info()[0]['bbox'])\\n\\\"\"}"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:12:50.414053Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:12:50.414087Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_016oSC2w52kLVW77xrhNxCnx", "name": "bash_tool", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"2 (105.71179962158203, 157.37796020507812, 489.5636901855469, 440.8425598144531)\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "none"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"returncode\":0,\"stdout\":\"2 (105.71179962158203, 157.37796020507812, 489.5636901855469, 440.8425598144531)\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\"}"}, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:12:52.400903Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:12:52.597997Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01GA3atNhtMLo7PZM2puNQrF", "name": "view", "input": {"description": "Check rendered page 1", "path": "/home/claude/p1.png"}, "message": "Check rendered page 1", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "bookText", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Check rendered page 1"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:12:53.138262Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:12:53.138313Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01GA3atNhtMLo7PZM2puNQrF", "name": "view", "content": [{"type": "image", "file_uuid": "c37a3f6e-83ec-5b61-9e2a-553cc8a461e8"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "other"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "bookText", "display_content": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:12:55.998285Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:12:55.998400Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "I've swapped the new image into page 1 and checked the render. It looks right, so I'm handing you the file now.", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:12:55.998404Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:12:56.289558Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_011G1sz5f5AiHhzvtnKbVJgw", "name": "present_files", "input": {"filepaths": ["/mnt/user-data/outputs/hadronic_matter_brochure_updated.pdf"]}, "message": "Presenting file(s)...", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "file", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "table", "table": [["filepaths", "['/mnt/user-data/outputs/hadronic_matter_brochure_updated.pdf']"]]}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:12:56.310352Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:12:56.310385Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_011G1sz5f5AiHhzvtnKbVJgw", "name": "present_files", "content": [{"type": "local_resource", "file_path": "/mnt/user-data/outputs/hadronic_matter_brochure_updated.pdf", "name": "hadronic matter brochure updated", "mime_type": "application/pdf", "artifact_publishable": false}, {"type": "text", "text": "\n\nThe file cards appear with your reply, and where they sit relative to its text depends on the app the user is reading in; finish the reply with text, and refer to a file by its name, never as above or below."}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "pdf"}, "message": "Presented file", "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "file", "display_content": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:13:00.287014Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:13:00.306095Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "I replaced the old three-step figure and tank picture on page 1 with your new image, and left the text, layout and page 2 unchanged. The updated brochure is in `hadronic_matter_brochure_updated.pdf`.\n\nThe new image fills the same space and is slightly taller, which fits easily above the first section heading.\n\nThe new image still has the errors I listed earlier. The crossover band runs along the whole first-order line, the second \"\u03bcB = 0\" label should be a high value, and the neutron-star path is wrong. The nucleation curve also starts below zero, and its bubble inset looks like heterogeneous nucleation. The tank caption is unchanged, so the brochure's own disclaimer is still the only thing separating it from the physics. If you'd like, I can correct the diagram and rebuild the brochure with it.", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-10-20T09:13:00.369641Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-20T09:13:00.369641Z", "attachments": [], "files": [{"file_uuid": "c37a3f6e-83ec-5b61-9e2a-553cc8a461e8", "file_name": "p1.png"}], "parent_message_uuid": "01a15895-3d05-744c-a025-b234baa0f977"}]},{"uuid": "1c8d35d1-422e-4393-b4c7-fe7a60d54650", "name": "Hadronic polycrystalline material synthesis", "summary": "", "created_at": "2026-10-20T09:15:08.160376Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-20T10:02:07.712227Z", "account": {"uuid": "8d7882cd-3e98-47cc-a6e7-61494a1ee4ac"}, "chat_messages": [{"uuid": "01a15897-d5cf-702f-84a0-8cf6ec180bce", "text": "how to make hadronic polycrystaline material?", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:15:10.221297Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:15:10.221297Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "how to make hadronic polycrystaline material?", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-10-20T09:15:10.221518Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-20T09:15:10.221518Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000000"}, {"uuid": "01a15897-d5cf-7eb8-b04c-ee580d8234f7", "text": "\"Hadronic polycrystalline material\" isn't an established term in materials science or physics, so I'll cover the readings I think you might mean.\n\n**1. Ordinary polycrystalline solids.** Technically, all everyday matter is made of hadrons (protons and neutrons in the nuclei), so any polycrystalline material qualifies. These are made by:\n- **Solidification:** cooling a melt so many crystal grains nucleate and grow until they meet (metals, ceramics, ice).\n- **Sintering:** pressing and heating powders just below the melting point so grains fuse (ceramics, tungsten, many oxides).\n- **Deposition or annealing:** vapor or sputter deposition, or crystallizing an amorphous film by heating (silicon, thin films).\n\n**2. Exotic hadronic matter in neutron stars.** Physicists do discuss crystalline hadronic matter here:\n- The inner crust of a neutron star is a lattice of neutron-rich nuclei in a neutron fluid, and deeper down are \"nuclear pasta\" phases.\n- At extreme densities, quark matter may form a \"crystalline color superconductor.\"\n- Matter this dense exists only under stellar gravity, at around 10^14 g/cm\u00b3. It can't be made in a lab, though heavy-ion colliders briefly create quark-gluon plasma and hot hadronic matter, and simulations (molecular dynamics, quantum molecular dynamics) model pasta structures.\n\n**3. A term from fiction, a game, or a speculative paper.** In that case, the \"recipe\" depends on the source's own rules.\n\nWhich did you mean? If you saw the term somewhere specific, tell me where and I can work out what it refers to.", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:15:10.221171Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:15:12.662396Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Clarifying what hadronic polycrystalline material means."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:15:12.662431Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T09:15:16.939907Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "\"Hadronic polycrystalline material\" isn't an established term in materials science or physics, so I'll cover the readings I think you might mean.\n\n**1. Ordinary polycrystalline solids.** Technically, all everyday matter is made of hadrons (protons and neutrons in the nuclei), so any polycrystalline material qualifies. These are made by:\n- **Solidification:** cooling a melt so many crystal grains nucleate and grow until they meet (metals, ceramics, ice).\n- **Sintering:** pressing and heating powders just below the melting point so grains fuse (ceramics, tungsten, many oxides).\n- **Deposition or annealing:** vapor or sputter deposition, or crystallizing an amorphous film by heating (silicon, thin films).\n\n**2. Exotic hadronic matter in neutron stars.** Physicists do discuss crystalline hadronic matter here:\n- The inner crust of a neutron star is a lattice of neutron-rich nuclei in a neutron fluid, and deeper down are \"nuclear pasta\" phases.\n- At extreme densities, quark matter may form a \"crystalline color superconductor.\"\n- Matter this dense exists only under stellar gravity, at around 10^14 g/cm\u00b3. It can't be made in a lab, though heavy-ion colliders briefly create quark-gluon plasma and hot hadronic matter, and simulations (molecular dynamics, quantum molecular dynamics) model pasta structures.\n\n**3. A term from fiction, a game, or a speculative paper.** In that case, the \"recipe\" depends on the source's own rules.\n\nWhich did you mean? If you saw the term somewhere specific, tell me where and I can work out what it refers to.", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-10-20T09:15:16.949806Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-20T09:15:16.949806Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a15897-d5cf-702f-84a0-8cf6ec180bce"}, {"uuid": "01a158c1-067f-76c3-b0d1-a007bae1caec", "text": "do this with this image", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T10:00:10.046512Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T10:00:10.046512Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "do this with this image", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-10-20T10:00:10.046985Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-20T10:00:10.046985Z", "attachments": [], "files": [{"file_uuid": "408e0c11-33c2-45cb-a09c-c6ed8da3e270", "file_name": "hadronic_matter_brochure.pdf"}, {"file_uuid": "14ed3235-fb6c-48ec-bb99-3cd0fed83143", "file_name": "1792490404572_image.png"}], "parent_message_uuid": "01a15897-d5cf-7eb8-b04c-ee580d8234f7"}, {"uuid": "01a158c1-067f-7e7e-8f83-05af7b7cf612", "text": "I read this as applying the brochure's concept to the endoskeleton in your image: how you'd make a \"polycrystalline hadronic matter\" robot frame. The honest answer has two layers.\n\n**The physics in the diagram is real, but it can't build a skeleton.** The three steps show supercooled quark-gluon plasma nucleating hadron bubbles. That transition happens at roughly 150 MeV (about 10\u00b9\u00b2 K) and lasts microseconds in colliders or the early universe. The product is a hot hadron gas, not a solid structure, and no known process stabilizes it into a frame. As the brochure's own disclaimer says, the chassis application is hypothetical.\n\n**The same three steps do work as a real analogy for forging a metal frame.** Solidification of ordinary metals follows the same logic, which is why the brochure's \"polycrystalline\" ending isn't arbitrary:\n\n1. **Supercooling:** a molten alloy (titanium, steel or nickel-based) is cooled slightly below its melting point without solidifying, which is the same metastable state as in Step 1.\n2. **Nucleation barrier:** tiny solid clusters below a critical radius redissolve, and larger ones survive. This is classical nucleation theory, the same free-energy-barrier picture as your middle panel.\n3. **Grain growth:** nuclei grow until they collide, giving a polycrystalline solid. Latent heat release (recalescence) reheats the metal, like the \"reheating to T_transition\" box in your diagram.\n\n**Applying it to the endoskeleton, a realistic build would look like this:**\n- **Structure:** cast or forge each bone-like part (skull, ribs, limb links, joints) from a high-strength alloy, controlling cooling rate and adding grain refiners to get fine, uniform grains. Fine grains give higher strength and better crack resistance.\n- **Heat treatment:** anneal, quench and temper to tune hardness versus toughness, and machine the final shapes.\n- **Chrome look:** polish and finish with electroplating or PVD coating. The mirror finish in your image is cosmetic, not structural.\n- **Joints:** use hardened steel bearings and pins, with actuators and wiring added separately.\n- **Design validation:** run finite-element and phase-field grain simulations for stress, heat and fracture, the \"multiphysics simulation\" the brochure mentions, before building.\n\nThe figure itself is a movie-style endoskeleton. A fully functional version would be limited by actuators, power and control rather than the frame material.\n\nI can turn this into a brochure-style page matching your PDF, with the endoskeleton in place of the tank chassis, or write it up as a materials spec. Which would be more useful?", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T10:00:10.037522Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T10:00:17.618381Z", "flags": null, "type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "summaries": [{"summary": "Turning an endoskeleton image into requested new artwork."}], "cut_off": false, "truncated": true, "hidden": false, "thinking_hidden": true, "alternative_display_type": null, "signature": null}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T10:00:17.618429Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T10:00:24.943284Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "I read this as applying the brochure's concept to the endoskeleton in your image: how you'd make a \"polycrystalline hadronic matter\" robot frame. The honest answer has two layers.\n\n**The physics in the diagram is real, but it can't build a skeleton.** The three steps show supercooled quark-gluon plasma nucleating hadron bubbles. That transition happens at roughly 150 MeV (about 10\u00b9\u00b2 K) and lasts microseconds in colliders or the early universe. The product is a hot hadron gas, not a solid structure, and no known process stabilizes it into a frame. As the brochure's own disclaimer says, the chassis application is hypothetical.\n\n**The same three steps do work as a real analogy for forging a metal frame.** Solidification of ordinary metals follows the same logic, which is why the brochure's \"polycrystalline\" ending isn't arbitrary:\n\n1. **Supercooling:** a molten alloy (titanium, steel or nickel-based) is cooled slightly below its melting point without solidifying, which is the same metastable state as in Step 1.\n2. **Nucleation barrier:** tiny solid clusters below a critical radius redissolve, and larger ones survive. This is classical nucleation theory, the same free-energy-barrier picture as your middle panel.\n3. **Grain growth:** nuclei grow until they collide, giving a polycrystalline solid. Latent heat release (recalescence) reheats the metal, like the \"reheating to T_transition\" box in your diagram.\n\n**Applying it to the endoskeleton, a realistic build would look like this:**\n- **Structure:** cast or forge each bone-like part (skull, ribs, limb links, joints) from a high-strength alloy, controlling cooling rate and adding grain refiners to get fine, uniform grains. Fine grains give higher strength and better crack resistance.\n- **Heat treatment:** anneal, quench and temper to tune hardness versus toughness, and machine the final shapes.\n- **Chrome look:** polish and finish with electroplating or PVD coating. The mirror finish in your image is cosmetic, not structural.\n- **Joints:** use hardened steel bearings and pins, with actuators and wiring added separately.\n- **Design validation:** run finite-element and phase-field grain simulations for stress, heat and fracture, the \"multiphysics simulation\" the brochure mentions, before building.\n\nThe figure itself is a movie-style endoskeleton. A fully functional version would be limited by actuators, power and control rather than the frame material.\n\nI can turn this into a brochure-style page matching your PDF, with the endoskeleton in place of the tank chassis, or write it up as a materials spec. Which would be more useful?", "citations": []}], "sender": "assistant", "created_at": "2026-10-20T10:00:24.955215Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-20T10:00:24.955215Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a158c1-067f-76c3-b0d1-a007bae1caec"}, {"uuid": "01a158c1-890a-73be-91a0-bf06c807b58e", "text": "just switch the image", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T10:00:42.540314Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T10:00:42.540314Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "just switch the image", "citations": []}], "sender": "human", "created_at": "2026-10-20T10:00:42.540516Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-20T10:00:42.540516Z", "attachments": [], "files": [], "parent_message_uuid": "01a158c1-067f-7e7e-8f83-05af7b7cf612"}, {"uuid": "01a158c1-890a-74dc-ab51-979a2ef5a865", "text": "I'll swap the tank image in the brochure for the endoskeleton. First I'll check the PDF skill and the file.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nI'm swapping the tank for the endoskeleton. The tank is baked into the same embedded image as the diagram, so I'm rebuilding that image with the new figure and shifting the text below it down to fit.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nThe box is too wide and tall for a portrait figure. I'll make it narrower and tighter.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\n\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nPage 1 looks right, and page 2 is untouched. Here's the file.\n```\nThis block is not supported on your current device yet.\n```\n\nI swapped the tank for the endoskeleton on page 1 of the brochure and saved it as hadronic_matter_brochure_endoskeleton.pdf.\n\nBecause the endoskeleton is a tall image and the tank was wide, I made its frame narrower and taller. The caption \"Polycrystalline hadronic matter chassis\" stays under it, and the text below moved down about 69 pt to make room. The rest of the layout and page 2 are unchanged.\n\nThe brochure's text still says \"chassis\" and \"vehicle platform\" in places. I can reword those for a robot frame if you'd like.", "content": [{"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T10:00:43.358331Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T10:00:43.792608Z", "flags": null, "type": "text", "text": "I'll swap the tank image in the brochure for the endoskeleton. First I'll check the PDF skill and the file.", "citations": []}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T10:00:43.792615Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T10:00:43.916992Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_011Ujkai2GTyk2FBwUWr7WSV", "name": "view", "input": {"description": "Read the PDF skill before editing the brochure", "path": "/mnt/skills/public/pdf/SKILL.md"}, "message": "Read the PDF skill before editing the brochure", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "bookText", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "text", "text": "Read the PDF skill before editing the brochure"}, "approval_options": null, "approval_key": null, "approval_key_legacy": null, "is_mcp_app": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "mcp_server_url": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T10:00:43.933062Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T10:00:43.933159Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": "toolu_011Ujkai2GTyk2FBwUWr7WSV", "name": "view", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "     1\t---\n     2\tname: pdf\n     3\tdescription: Use this skill whenever the user wants to do anything with PDF files. This includes reading or extracting text/tables from PDFs, combining or merging multiple PDFs into one, splitting PDFs apart, rotating pages, adding watermarks, creating new PDFs, filling PDF forms, encrypting/decrypting PDFs, extracting images, and OCR on scanned PDFs to make them searchable. If the user mentions a .pdf file or asks to produce one, use this skill.\n     4\tlicense: Proprietary. LICENSE.txt has complete terms\n     5\t---\n     6\t\n     7\t# PDF Processing Guide\n     8\t\n     9\t## Overview\n    10\t\n    11\tThis guide covers essential PDF processing operations using Python libraries and command-line tools. For advanced features, JavaScript libraries, and detailed examples, see REFERENCE.md. If you need to fill out a PDF form, read FORMS.md and follow its instructions.\n    12\t\n    13\t## Quick Start\n    14\t\n    15\t```python\n    16\tfrom pypdf import PdfReader, PdfWriter\n    17\t\n    18\t# Read a PDF\n    19\treader = PdfReader(\"document.pdf\")\n    20\tprint(f\"Pages: {len(reader.pages)}\")\n    21\t\n    22\t# Extract text\n    23\ttext = \"\"\n    24\tfor page in reader.pages:\n    25\t    text += page.extract_text()\n    26\t```\n    27\t\n    28\t## Python Libraries\n    29\t\n    30\t### pypdf - Basic Operations\n    31\t\n    32\t#### Merge PDFs\n    33\t```python\n    34\tfrom pypdf import PdfWriter, PdfReader\n    35\t\n    36\twriter = PdfWriter()\n    37\tfor pdf_file in [\"doc1.pdf\", \"doc2.pdf\", \"doc3.pdf\"]:\n    38\t    reader = PdfReader(pdf_file)\n    39\t    for page in reader.pages:\n    40\t        writer.add_page(page)\n    41\t\n    42\twith open(\"merged.pdf\", \"wb\") as output:\n    43\t    writer.write(output)\n    44\t```\n    45\t\n    46\t#### Split PDF\n    47\t```python\n    48\treader = PdfReader(\"input.pdf\")\n    49\tfor i, page in enumerate(reader.pages):\n    50\t    writer = PdfWriter()\n    51\t    writer.add_page(page)\n    52\t    with open(f\"page_{i+1}.pdf\", \"wb\") as output:\n    53\t        writer.write(output)\n    54\t```\n    55\t\n    56\t#### Extract Metadata\n    57\t```python\n    58\treader = PdfReader(\"document.pdf\")\n    59\tmeta = reader.metadata\n    60\tprint(f\"Title: {meta.title}\")\n    61\tprint(f\"Author: {meta.author}\")\n    62\tprint(f\"Subject: {meta.subject}\")\n    63\tprint(f\"Creator: {meta.creator}\")\n    64\t```\n    65\t\n    66\t#### Rotate Pages\n    67\t```python\n    68\treader = PdfReader(\"input.pdf\")\n    69\twriter = PdfWriter()\n    70\t\n    71\tpage = reader.pages[0]\n    72\tpage.rotate(90)  # Rotate 90 degrees clockwise\n    73\twriter.add_page(page)\n    74\t\n    75\twith open(\"rotated.pdf\", \"wb\") as output:\n    76\t    writer.write(output)\n    77\t```\n    78\t\n    79\t### pdfplumber - Text and Table Extraction\n    80\t\n    81\t#### Extract Text with Layout\n    82\t```python\n    83\timport pdfplumber\n    84\t\n    85\twith pdfplumber.open(\"document.pdf\") as pdf:\n    86\t    for page in pdf.pages:\n    87\t        text = page.extract_text()\n    88\t        print(text)\n    89\t```\n    90\t\n    91\t#### Extract Tables\n    92\t```python\n    93\twith pdfplumber.open(\"document.pdf\") as pdf:\n    94\t    for i, page in enumerate(pdf.pages):\n    95\t        tables = page.extract_tables()\n    96\t        for j, table in enumerate(tables):\n    97\t            print(f\"Table {j+1} on page {i+1}:\")\n    98\t            for row in table:\n    99\t                print(row)\n   100\t```\n   101\t\n   102\t#### Advanced Table Extraction\n   103\t```python\n   104\timport pandas as pd\n   105\t\n   106\twith pdfplumber.open(\"document.pdf\") as pdf:\n   107\t    all_tables = []\n   108\t    for page in pdf.pages:\n   109\t        tables = page.extract_tables()\n   110\t        for table in tables:\n   111\t            if table:  # Check if table is not empty\n   112\t                df = pd.DataFrame(table[1:], columns=table[0])\n   113\t                all_tables.append(df)\n   114\t\n   115\t# Combine all tables\n   116\tif all_tables:\n   117\t    combined_df = pd.concat(all_tables, ignore_index=True)\n   118\t    combined_df.to_excel(\"extracted_tables.xlsx\", index=False)\n   119\t```\n   120\t\n   121\t### reportlab - Create PDFs\n   122\t\n   123\t#### Basic PDF Creation\n   124\t```python\n   125\tfrom reportlab.lib.pagesizes import letter\n   126\tfrom reportlab.pdfgen import canvas\n   127\t\n   128\tc = canvas.Canvas(\"hello.pdf\", pagesize=letter)\n   129\twidth, height = letter\n   130\t\n   131\t# Add text\n   132\tc.drawString(100, height - 100, \"Hello World!\")\n   133\tc.drawString(100, height - 120, \"This is a PDF created with reportlab\")\n   134\t\n   135\t# Add a line\n   136\tc.line(100, height - 140, 400, height - 140)\n   137\t\n   138\t# Save\n   139\tc.save()\n   140\t```\n   141\t\n   142\t#### Create PDF with Multiple Pages\n   143\t```python\n   144\tfrom reportlab.lib.pagesizes import letter\n   145\tfrom reportlab.platypus import SimpleDocTemplate, Paragraph, Spacer, PageBreak\n   146\tfrom reportlab.lib.styles import getSampleStyleSheet\n   147\t\n   148\tdoc = SimpleDocTemplate(\"report.pdf\", pagesize=letter)\n   149\tstyles = getSampleStyleSheet()\n   150\tstory = []\n   151\t\n   152\t# Add content\n   153\ttitle = Paragraph(\"Report Title\", styles['Title'])\n   154\tstory.append(title)\n   155\tstory.append(Spacer(1, 12))\n   156\t\n   157\tbody = Paragraph(\"This is the body of the report. \" * 20, styles['Normal'])\n   158\tstory.append(body)\n   159\tstory.append(PageBreak())\n   160\t\n   161\t# Page 2\n   162\tstory.append(Paragraph(\"Page 2\", styles['Heading1']))\n   163\tstory.append(Paragraph(\"Content for page 2\", styles['Normal']))\n   164\t\n   165\t# Build PDF\n   166\tdoc.build(story)\n   167\t```\n   168\t\n   169\t#### Subscripts and Superscripts\n   170\t\n   171\t**IMPORTANT**: Never use Unicode subscript/superscript characters (\u2080\u2081\u2082\u2083\u2084\u2085\u2086\u2087\u2088\u2089, \u2070\u00b9\u00b2\u00b3\u2074\u2075\u2076\u2077\u2078\u2079) in ReportLab PDFs. The built-in fonts do not include these glyphs, causing them to render as solid black boxes.\n   172\t\n   173\tInstead, use ReportLab's XML markup tags in Paragraph objects:\n   174\t```python\n   175\tfrom reportlab.platypus import Paragraph\n   176\tfrom reportlab.lib.styles import getSampleStyleSheet\n   177\t\n   178\tstyles = getSampleStyleSheet()\n   179\t\n   180\t# Subscripts: use <sub> tag\n   181\tchemical = Paragraph(\"H<sub>2</sub>O\", styles['Normal'])\n   182\t\n   183\t# Superscripts: use <super> tag\n   184\tsquared = Paragraph(\"x<super>2</super> + y<super>2</super>\", styles['Normal'])\n   185\t```\n   186\t\n   187\tFor canvas-drawn text (not Paragraph objects), manually adjust font the size and position rather than using Unicode subscripts/superscripts.\n   188\t\n   189\t## Command-Line Tools\n   190\t\n   191\t### pdftotext (poppler-utils)\n   192\t```bash\n   193\t# Extract text\n   194\tpdftotext input.pdf output.txt\n   195\t\n   196\t# Extract text preserving layout\n   197\tpdftotext -layout input.pdf output.txt\n   198\t\n   199\t# Extract specific pages\n   200\tpdftotext -f 1 -l 5 input.pdf output.txt  # Pages 1-5\n   201\t```\n   202\t\n   203\t### qpdf\n   204\t```bash\n   205\t# Merge PDFs\n   206\tqpdf --empty --pages file1.pdf file2.pdf -- merged.pdf\n   207\t\n   208\t# Split pages\n   209\tqpdf input.pdf --pages . 1-5 -- pages1-5.pdf\n   210\tqpdf input.pdf --pages . 6-10 -- pages6-10.pdf\n   211\t\n   212\t# Rotate pages\n   213\tqpdf input.pdf output.pdf --rotate=+90:1  # Rotate page 1 by 90 degrees\n   214\t\n   215\t# Remove password\n   216\tqpdf --password=mypassword --decrypt encrypted.pdf decrypted.pdf\n   217\t```\n   218\t\n   219\t### pdftk (if available)\n   220\t```bash\n   221\t# Merge\n   222\tpdftk file1.pdf file2.pdf cat output merged.pdf\n   223\t\n   224\t# Split\n   225\tpdftk input.pdf burst\n   226\t\n   227\t# Rotate\n   228\tpdftk input.pdf rotate 1east output rotated.pdf\n   229\t```\n   230\t\n   231\t## Common Tasks\n   232\t\n   233\t### Extract Text from Scanned PDFs\n   234\t```python\n   235\t# Requires: pip install pytesseract pdf2image\n   236\timport pytesseract\n   237\tfrom pdf2image import convert_from_path\n   238\t\n   239\t# Convert PDF to images\n   240\timages = convert_from_path('scanned.pdf')\n   241\t\n   242\t# OCR each page\n   243\ttext = \"\"\n   244\tfor i, image in enumerate(images):\n   245\t    text += f\"Page {i+1}:\\n\"\n   246\t    text += pytesseract.image_to_string(image)\n   247\t    text += \"\\n\\n\"\n   248\t\n   249\tprint(text)\n   250\t```\n   251\t\n   252\t### Add Watermark\n   253\t```python\n   254\tfrom pypdf import PdfReader, PdfWriter\n   255\t\n   256\t# Create watermark (or load existing)\n   257\twatermark = PdfReader(\"watermark.pdf\").pages[0]\n   258\t\n   259\t# Apply to all pages\n   260\treader = PdfReader(\"document.pdf\")\n   261\twriter = PdfWriter()\n   262\t\n   263\tfor page in reader.pages:\n   264\t    page.merge_page(watermark)\n   265\t    writer.add_page(page)\n   266\t\n   267\twith open(\"watermarked.pdf\", \"wb\") as output:\n   268\t    writer.write(output)\n   269\t```\n   270\t\n   271\t### Extract Images\n   272\t```bash\n   273\t# Using pdfimages (poppler-utils)\n   274\tpdfimages -j input.pdf output_prefix\n   275\t\n   276\t# This extracts all images as output_prefix-000.jpg, output_prefix-001.jpg, etc.\n   277\t```\n   278\t\n   279\t### Password Protection\n   280\t```python\n   281\tfrom pypdf import PdfReader, PdfWriter\n   282\t\n   283\treader = PdfReader(\"input.pdf\")\n   284\twriter = PdfWriter()\n   285\t\n   286\tfor page in reader.pages:\n   287\t    writer.add_page(page)\n   288\t\n   289\t# Add password\n   290\twriter.encrypt(\"userpassword\", \"ownerpassword\")\n   291\t\n   292\twith open(\"encrypted.pdf\", \"wb\") as output:\n   293\t    writer.write(output)\n   294\t```\n   295\t\n   296\t## Quick Reference\n   297\t\n   298\t| Task | Best Tool | Command/Code |\n   299\t|------|-----------|--------------|\n   300\t| Merge PDFs | pypdf | `writer.add_page(page)` |\n   301\t| Split PDFs | pypdf | One page per file |\n   302\t| Extract text | pdfplumber | `page.extract_text()` |\n   303\t| Extract tables | pdfplumber | `page.extract_tables()` |\n   304\t| Create PDFs | reportlab | Canvas or Platypus |\n   305\t| Command line merge | qpdf | `qpdf --empty --pages ...` |\n   306\t| OCR scanned PDFs | pytesseract | Convert to image first |\n   307\t| Fill PDF forms | pdf-lib or pypdf (see FORMS.md) | See FORMS.md |\n   308\t\n   309\t## Next Steps\n   310\t\n   311\t- For advanced pypdfium2 usage, see REFERENCE.md\n   312\t- For JavaScript libraries (pdf-lib), see REFERENCE.md\n   313\t- If you need to fill out a PDF form, follow the instructions in FORMS.md\n   314\t- For troubleshooting guides, see REFERENCE.md\n   315\t"}], "is_error": false, "structured_content": null, "meta": {"output_format_category": "md"}, "message": null, "integration_name": "File Creation", "mcp_server_url": null, "integration_icon_url": null, "icon_name": "bookText", "display_content": null, "hidden_in_chat": null, "tool_origin": "first_party"}, {"start_timestamp": "2026-10-20T10:00:45.530057Z", "stop_timestamp": "2026-10-20T10:00:46.295031Z", "flags": null, "type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01JYPvWZWBgDwvYyDxx1yrA4", "name": "bash_tool", "input": {"command": "cd /mnt/user-data/uploads && ls -la && pdfimages -list hadronic_matter_brochure.pdf && python3 -c \"\nfrom PIL import Image\nim=Image.open('1792490404572_image.png'); print(im.size, im.mode)\"", "description": "List uploads, PDF embedded images, and uploaded image size"}, "message": "List uploads, PDF embedded images, and uploaded image size", "integration_name": "File Creation", "integration_icon_url": null, "tool_identifier": null, "icon_name": "commandLine", "context": null, "display_content": {"type": "json_block", "json_block": "{\"language\": \"bash\", \"code\": \"cd /mnt/user-data/uploads && ls -l